/* The Flagships tab of the Wine Atlas: built by .scripts/flagships/build-flagships.cjs from .scripts/flagships/fragments, never edited by hand.
   FLAGSHIPS holds one record per producer, keyed by its id (fl- and eight base36 digits, minted once from the plan's
   key and frozen in .scripts/flagships/keys.v1.json), in the plan's order: the house (producer, its WINE_PRODUCERS id
   or "", how to say it, its place, the year it was founded or "" where nobody is sure), its flagship wine (name,
   appellation, classification, colour, grapes, sites, making, the glass in named things, ageing, service and the
   words on its label), the line to say at the table, the story, its other wines, the traps, two cards and two
   questions (ids minted from the record's id and the text; the answer positions dealt by the build) and the
   sources. FLAGSHIP_GROUPS lists the groups in the plan's order with the ids of their records; FLAGSHIP_META states
   the edition and the counts. Every string is British and dash free, and names nobody living. */
var FLAGSHIPS = {
"fl-4rgb90w0": {"id":"fl-4rgb90w0","key":"krug","group":"champagne","region":"champagne","producer":"Krug","producerId":"p-krug","say":"KROOG","place":"Reims, Champagne","founded":"1843","wine":{"name":"Krug Grande Cuvée","appellation":"Champagne","classification":"","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Pinot Noir, Chardonnay and Meunier, in proportions that change with each Édition.","sites":"Plots across the villages of Champagne, each kept apart as its own wine, and a library of reserve wines from many years.","making":"Each plot is fermented on its own in small, old oak casks rather than steel, so that every wine can be judged apart before blending. More than a hundred and twenty wines from more than ten years are blended, reserve wines among them, and the bottles rest about seven years on their lees in the Reims cellars.","glass":"Toasted brioche, roasted hazelnut and candied lemon peel over ripe pear and honeyed pastry, with a fine, persistent mousse, a broad, almost vinous weight and a long, dry, faintly smoky finish.","ageing":"Drinks well on release and keeps developing for a decade or two after it, gaining toffee, coffee and dried fig.","serve":"9 to 11 degrees Celsius, never ice cold; no need to decant; a wide white wine glass shows it better than a narrow flute.","label":[{"term":"Édition","means":"The release's place in the line of yearly blends; each Édition is built around one harvest."},{"term":"Krug iD","means":"A code on the back label that opens, on the house's site, the story of that bottle."},{"term":"Brut","means":"Dry: less than twelve grams of sugar a litre after disgorging."}]},"table":"Krug Grande Cuvée is the house's whole idea in one bottle: more than a hundred and twenty wines from over ten years, fermented in small old casks and aged about seven years. It drinks like a mature white Burgundy with bubbles, so pour it in a wine glass.","story":"Joseph Krug (1800 to 1866), born in Mainz, learned Champagne at the house of Jacquesson before founding his own in Reims in 1843. In 1848, as the house tells it, he set down his creed in a personal notebook: to make the fullest expression of Champagne every year, whatever the weather, by blending the wines of many plots and many years. His son took over at his death in 1866, the year the house moved to the Rue Coquebert in Reims. Until 1978 the blend was sold as Private Cuvée; that year the house renamed it Grande Cuvée. Beside it the house made vintage wines, and in the 1970s it added a rosé and, from the walled Clos du Mesnil bought in 1971, a Chardonnay of one vineyard first made from the 1979 vintage, but the blend remained the house's purpose. Krug kept fermenting in small oak casks while most houses turned to steel tanks, and it built a library of reserve wines from many years for the cellar master to draw on. In the 2010s the house began to print an Édition number on the front label, so that a guest can tell one year's Grande Cuvée from the next and read the harvest it was built around. A sommelier sells it as a wine to be tasted, not a toast to be drunk.","others":[{"name":"Krug Vintage","note":"Declared years only, made with the same small casks and long cellar ageing as the Grande Cuvée."},{"name":"Krug Clos du Mesnil","note":"Chardonnay alone from one walled vineyard in Le Mesnil-sur-Oger, first made from the 1979 vintage."},{"name":"Krug Clos d'Ambonnay","note":"The Pinot Noir counterpart from a tiny walled plot in Ambonnay, first made in 1995."}],"traps":["Grande Cuvée carries no vintage, yet it spends longer in the cellar than many vintage Champagnes.","Clos du Mesnil is Chardonnay from Le Mesnil-sur-Oger; Clos d'Ambonnay is Pinot Noir from Ambonnay.","An Édition number counts the yearly creations of the blend; it is not a vintage year."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-fgwjrsaz","front":"Krug Grande Cuvée: what is fermented in small old oak casks, and why?","back":"The wine of every plot, kept apart so that each can be judged on its own before blending; the casks are old ones, not new oak."},{"id":"fl-it5hrcts","front":"What does the Édition number on Krug Grande Cuvée tell a guest?","back":"Which yearly creation of the blend it is, printed on the front label since the 163rd Édition; each is built around one harvest with reserve wines from many years."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-r5ltytkl","q":"A guest notices that Krug Grande Cuvée carries no vintage on its label. Which explanation is right?","opts":["It comes only from the walled Clos du Mesnil","It blends over a hundred wines from more than ten years","It is a Blanc de Blancs from the Côte des Blancs","It is a single vintage held back for seven years"],"a":1,"exp":"Grande Cuvée is a multi vintage blend of well over a hundred wines, reserve wines among them; a single vintage would carry its year, and Clos du Mesnil is a separate Chardonnay.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rfungqvg","q":"Joseph Krug learned his trade at Jacquesson. In which city did he found his own house in 1843?","opts":["Reims","Épernay","Aÿ","Châlons-sur-Marne"],"a":0,"exp":"Krug was founded in Reims in 1843 and moved to the Rue Coquebert there in 1866; Épernay and Aÿ are the towns of other houses, and Châlons-sur-Marne is where Jacquesson began.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Krug: Our Story","url":"https://www.krug.com/en-us/our-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Krug: Individuality, the craftsmanship plot by plot","url":"https://www.krug.com/en-int/craftsmanship/individuality","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Krug: Grande Cuvée 172nd Édition field notes","url":"https://www.krug.com/sites/default/files/node/champagne/field_notes/Champagne-Notes_Krug-Grande-Cuvee-172eme-Edition.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Champagne Krug","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Champagne_Krug","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinepair: the story of Krug Grande Cuvée","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/krug-champagne-cuvee/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-l7ru7uy8": {"id":"fl-l7ru7uy8","key":"moet-chandon","group":"champagne","region":"champagne","producer":"Moët & Chandon","producerId":"p-moet-chandon","say":"mo-ET eh shahn-DOHN, dom pay-ree-NYOHN","place":"Épernay, Champagne","founded":"1743","wine":{"name":"Dom Pérignon","appellation":"Champagne","classification":"","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, in close to equal parts that shift with the year.","sites":"Chiefly Grand Cru villages of the Côte des Blancs, the Montagne de Reims and Aÿ, with fruit from the abbey's own Premier Cru vineyard at Hautvillers.","making":"Made only in declared vintages. Since the vintages after 1964 its base wines have been fermented in stainless steel with no oak. The wine then ages on its lees for the better part of a decade before the first release, and the same vintage returns later as Plénitude 2 after far longer on its lees.","glass":"Toasted almond, white peach and fresh brioche, with a smoky note of struck flint and a fine, creamy mousse; with age it moves towards roasted coffee, candied ginger and acacia honey.","ageing":"Approachable on release, it gains depth for fifteen to twenty years and more, which is why the house releases each vintage again as Plénitudes.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a tulip or a white wine glass rather than a narrow flute.","label":[{"term":"Vintage","means":"Dom Pérignon is always the wine of one year, and the label prints it."},{"term":"P2","means":"The second Plénitude: the same vintage released again after far longer on its lees."},{"term":"Brut","means":"Dry: less than twelve grams of sugar a litre after disgorging."}]},"table":"Dom Pérignon is Moët & Chandon's prestige cuvée, named for the Benedictine monk who kept the cellars of Hautvillers. It is always a single vintage of Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, aged for years on its lees before release.","story":"An Épernay wine merchant of the Moët family founded the house in 1743, and his grandson Jean-Rémy Moët (1758 to 1841) carried its wines across Europe; the Emperor Napoleon (1769 to 1821) visited him at Épernay and awarded him the Légion d'honneur. In 1833 the house passed to Jean-Rémy's son and his son-in-law, a Chandon, and took the name Moët & Chandon. Its prestige cuvée honours Dom Pierre Pérignon, cellarer of the Benedictine abbey of Hautvillers from 1668 until his death in 1715. He did not invent sparkling Champagne, a tale first told in 1821, long after his death: he blended grapes from different vineyards and, by most accounts, refined the making of white wine from black grapes, while the wines his abbey sold did not sparkle. The name Dom Pérignon belonged to the house of Mercier, which passed it to Moët after a marriage between the two families in 1927. The first Dom Pérignon, from the 1921 vintage, was released in 1936. It has since been made only in declared years, and the house releases the same vintage a second and a third time, as Plénitudes, after far longer ageing on the lees.","others":[{"name":"Moët Impérial","note":"The house's non vintage Brut of Pinot Noir, Meunier and Chardonnay, its everyday style."},{"name":"Moët & Chandon Grand Vintage","note":"The vintage wine under the Moët name, made apart from Dom Pérignon."},{"name":"Dom Pérignon Rosé","note":"The rosé of a declared year, made in far smaller quantity."}],"traps":["Dom Pérignon did not invent sparkling Champagne: he blended grapes from many vineyards, and the tale of his bubbles was first told in 1821.","Dom Pérignon is always vintage dated; Moët Impérial is the house's non vintage Brut.","P2 is not a different blend: it is the same vintage disgorged after a far longer stay on its lees."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-m74ur2ci","front":"Moët & Chandon names its prestige cuvée for a monk. Who was he, and what did he truly do?","back":"Dom Pierre Pérignon, cellarer of the abbey of Hautvillers from 1668 to his death in 1715; he blended grapes from many vineyards, but he did not invent the bubbles."},{"id":"fl-w1og7f4a","front":"Moët & Chandon's Dom Pérignon: which grapes, and is there a non vintage version?","back":"Chardonnay and Pinot Noir in close to equal parts; it is always the wine of a single declared year."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-zvzdy7nz","q":"A guest ordering Dom Pérignon asks which grapes go into it. What do you tell them?","opts":["Pinot Noir, Meunier and Chardonnay together","Chardonnay alone, as a Blanc de Blancs","Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, in close to equal parts","Pinot Noir alone, as a Blanc de Noirs"],"a":2,"exp":"Dom Pérignon blends Chardonnay and Pinot Noir in close to equal parts in each declared year; Meunier goes into Moët Impérial, the house's non vintage Brut.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ecix87dx","q":"How did the name Dom Pérignon come to Moët & Chandon before the first release of 1936?","opts":["The abbey of Hautvillers sold it at auction","Napoleon granted it to the Moët family in 1804","The Comité Champagne granted it to the house","The house of Mercier passed it on after a family marriage"],"a":3,"exp":"The name was an unused mark of the house of Mercier, given to Moët after a Mercier married a Chandon in 1927; the 1921 vintage was the first released.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Union des Maisons de Champagne: Maison Moët & Chandon","url":"https://maisons-champagne.com/en/houses/heritage/epernay-and-surrounding-area/article/maison-moet-chandon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Moët & Chandon: the house","url":"https://www.moet.com/en-int/the-house-moet-chandon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Union des Maisons de Champagne: Dom Pérignon, moine bénédictin de Hautvillers","url":"https://maisons-champagne.com/fr/encyclopedies/personnalites-du-champagne/hommes-de-dieu/article/dom-perignon-moine-benedictin-de-l-abbaye-d","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Dom Pérignon producer page","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/producer/612ec28006000000000022f0/dom-perignon","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Justerinis: Dom Pérignon vintages back to 1970","url":"https://www.justerinis.com/en-primeur/vintage-reports/dom-perignon-vintages-back-to-1970","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-cae69lso": {"id":"fl-cae69lso","key":"louis-roederer","group":"champagne","region":"champagne","producer":"Louis Roederer","producerId":"p-louis-roederer","say":"loo-EE roh-deh-RAIR, kree-STAHL","place":"Reims, Champagne","founded":"1776","wine":{"name":"Cristal","appellation":"Champagne","classification":"","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, in shares that change with the year.","sites":"Only the house's own Grand Cru vineyards: Verzenay, Verzy and Beaumont-sur-Vesle on the Montagne de Reims, Aÿ in the Marne valley, and Avize, Cramant and Le Mesnil-sur-Oger in the Côte des Blancs.","making":"Made only in years the house judges ripe enough, from estate parcels vinified apart, a share of them in oak casks, with the malolactic conversion usually blocked to keep its tension. It rests about six years in the cellars and eight months more after disgorging.","glass":"Meyer lemon, white peach and fresh almond over crushed chalk, with a delicate, creamy mousse; time brings toasted brioche, hazelnut and honeyed pastry while the line stays taut and saline.","ageing":"Taut on release, it opens after eight to ten years and keeps for twenty or more in a cool cellar.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a tulip or a white wine glass, the cellophane left on until the bottle is opened.","label":[{"term":"Cristal","means":"The cuvée's name, from the clear glass of the first bottles made for the Tsar."},{"term":"Louis Roederer","means":"The house that makes it; Cristal is its prestige cuvée, not a separate producer."},{"term":"Brut","means":"Dry: less than twelve grams of sugar a litre after disgorging."}]},"table":"Cristal is Louis Roederer's prestige cuvée, first made in 1876 for Tsar Alexander II in the clear bottle he asked for. It comes only from the house's own Grand Cru vines, Pinot Noir with Chardonnay, and only in years ripe enough.","story":"The house that became Louis Roederer was a Reims wine business recorded in 1776. A nephew of its owner inherited it in 1833 and gave it his own name, Louis Roederer, and under him and his son the house sold widely in Russia. In 1876 Tsar Alexander II (1818 to 1881) asked the house to set aside a cuvée for his court each year, and it was bottled in clear glass with a flat base so that, as the story is told, nothing could be hidden in the bottle or its punt. The wine took its name, Cristal, from that glass. The revolution of 1917 ended the imperial orders and much of the house's Russian trade, and Cristal later became a wine any guest could order; a rosé followed with the 1974 harvest. Unlike most houses, Roederer built its prestige wine on its own vineyards rather than on bought grapes, and Cristal has been made only in the years the house judges ripe enough. The coloured cellophane round each bottle is there to keep light from a wine in clear glass.","others":[{"name":"Cristal Rosé","note":"The rosé of a declared year, first made in 1974, in far smaller quantity."},{"name":"Louis Roederer Collection","note":"The house's numbered multi vintage Brut, which replaced Brut Premier."},{"name":"Louis Roederer Blanc de Blancs","note":"A vintage Chardonnay from the Côte des Blancs."}],"traps":["Cristal is always vintage: there is no non vintage Cristal.","Cristal is Louis Roederer's cuvée from Reims, not the wine of Roederer Estate in California.","The clear bottle dates from the Tsar's order of 1876; the cellophane wrap is there to keep the light out."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ekeoqv09","front":"Louis Roederer Cristal: for whom was it first made, and when?","back":"For Tsar Alexander II of Russia, in 1876, in a clear glass bottle with a flat base."},{"id":"fl-kkb7mupd","front":"Where do the grapes for Cristal come from?","back":"Only from Louis Roederer's own Grand Cru vineyards, Pinot Noir with Chardonnay, and only in declared years."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-tkte32ds","q":"A guest asks whether Cristal is a blend of bought grapes, like many prestige cuvées. What is true?","opts":["It comes only from the house's own vineyards","It is a non vintage blend of bought grapes","It is a single vineyard Blanc de Blancs","It is made largely from bought Meunier"],"a":0,"exp":"Cristal is drawn only from Louis Roederer's own Grand Cru vines, Pinot Noir with Chardonnay, in declared years; it is never non vintage and holds no Meunier.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-d1jysdrf","q":"Why was the first Cristal of 1876 bottled in clear glass with a flat base?","opts":["French law of the time required clear bottles","The Tsar wished to see that nothing was hidden in it","Clear glass let the cellar watch the second fermentation","Flat bases stood better on the decks of ships"],"a":1,"exp":"As the house tells it, the Tsar wanted a bottle in which nothing could be hidden; the coloured cellophane round it is there only to keep light off the clear glass.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Louis Roederer: Cristal","url":"https://www.louis-roederer.com/en/wine/cristal","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Louis Roederer: Cristal 2014 technical sheet","url":"https://www.louis-roederer.com/sites/default/files/pdf/lr_tech_sheet_cristal_blanc_2014_en.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Louis Roederer: the fiftieth anniversary of Cristal Rosé","url":"https://www.louis-roederer.com/sites/all/themes/roederer/files/cristal-rose-50-ans-dp-digital_EN.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Union des Maisons de Champagne: chronology of Louis Roederer","url":"https://maisons-champagne.com/fr/encyclopedies/chronologie-des-evenements/?id_maison=30&s_senstri=1","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Louis Roederer","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Roederer","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2apwr2hr": {"id":"fl-2apwr2hr","key":"bollinger","group":"champagne","region":"champagne","producer":"Bollinger","producerId":"p-bollinger","say":"BOLL-in-jer, or bo-lan-ZHAY","place":"Aÿ, Champagne","founded":"1829","wine":{"name":"La Grande Année","appellation":"Champagne","classification":"","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Pinot Noir, from about three fifths to two thirds, with Chardonnay for the rest.","sites":"About twenty Grand and Premier Cru villages, the Pinot Noir chiefly from Aÿ and Verzenay, the Chardonnay largely from Chouilly.","making":"Every base wine is fermented in oak barrels, old ones and never new. The wine is then aged under natural cork rather than a crown cap, on its lees for more than twice the three years the appellation asks of a vintage, and every bottle is riddled and disgorged by hand.","glass":"Baked apple, ripe pear and roasted hazelnut over toasted brioche, with a savoury, almost mushroom depth from the Pinot Noir and the barrels, a firm, vinous body and a fine, steady mousse.","ageing":"Rich enough on release, it grows more savoury and nutty over ten to twenty years.","serve":"9 to 11 degrees Celsius; no need to decant, though a few minutes in the glass help; a tulip or a white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"La Grande Année","means":"The great year: Bollinger's name for its vintage wine, made only in years the house declares."},{"term":"Champagne","means":"The appellation, which requires a vintage wine to rest at least three years before release."},{"term":"Brut","means":"Dry: less than twelve grams of sugar a litre after disgorging."}]},"table":"La Grande Année is Bollinger's vintage Champagne: Pinot Noir led, fermented entirely in oak barrels and aged under cork for years before it is disgorged by hand. It has the weight to stand beside a main course.","story":"Bollinger was founded at Aÿ in 1829 by three partners, one of them a young German from Württemberg whose name the house carries. It received a Royal Warrant from Queen Victoria (1819 to 1901) in 1884. After her husband's death in 1941, Elisabeth Bollinger, known as Lily (1899 to 1977), ran the house through the German occupation and on until 1971. She cycled through the vineyards to inspect them, released R.D. in 1967, a 1952 vintage disgorged late after a long rest on its lees, and in 1969 made the first Vieilles Vignes Françaises. The house kept old ways that others dropped: fermenting in old barrels, holding part of its reserve wines in magnum under cork and tending two small plots of ungrafted Pinot Noir inside Aÿ, the source of Vieilles Vignes Françaises. La Grande Année is the wine in which all of that shows: a vintage of Pinot Noir with Chardonnay, fermented in barrel, aged under cork for years and riddled by hand, and made only in the years the house declares.","others":[{"name":"Bollinger Special Cuvée","note":"The non vintage Brut, Pinot Noir led, with part of its reserve wines kept in magnum."},{"name":"Bollinger R.D.","note":"La Grande Année held far longer on its lees and disgorged late; R.D. means recently disgorged."},{"name":"Bollinger Vieilles Vignes Françaises","note":"A Blanc de Noirs from two small plots of ungrafted Pinot Noir in Aÿ, first made in 1969."}],"traps":["R.D. is not a vineyard or a separate blend: it is La Grande Année disgorged years later.","Vieilles Vignes Françaises is ungrafted Pinot Noir, not old vine Chardonnay.","La Grande Année is fermented in oak, yet it is not meant to taste of new wood: the barrels are old."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ylio6q76","front":"Bollinger La Grande Année: how is it fermented and aged?","back":"Entirely in old oak barrels, then aged under natural cork rather than a crown cap, and riddled and disgorged by hand."},{"id":"fl-6ykzqssr","front":"What does R.D. mean on a Bollinger label?","back":"Recently disgorged: La Grande Année left far longer on its lees and disgorged late, first released in 1967, in Lily Bollinger's time."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-zaa71fm7","q":"Which grape leads the blend of Bollinger La Grande Année, as it leads the house style?","opts":["Pinot Blanc","Chardonnay","Meunier","Pinot Noir"],"a":3,"exp":"Pinot Noir, about three fifths to two thirds of the blend and largely from Aÿ and Verzenay, leads La Grande Année, with Chardonnay for the rest; Meunier plays no part in it.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-xwowsxmg","q":"Who ran Bollinger from 1941 to 1971 and is remembered for inspecting the vineyards by bicycle?","opts":["Louise Pommery","Camille Olry-Roederer","Lily Bollinger","Barbe-Nicole Clicquot"],"a":2,"exp":"Elisabeth, known as Lily, Bollinger led the house after her husband's death in 1941; the widows Clicquot and Pommery ran their houses in the nineteenth century.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bollinger: La Grande Année, ten years of patience","url":"https://www.champagne-bollinger.com/en/the-collection/grande-annee/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bollinger: The history of Madame Bollinger","url":"https://www.champagne-bollinger.com/en/news/history-madame-bollinger/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Champagne Bollinger producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/champagne-bollinger-producer-profile-461814","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bollinger","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bollinger","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Lily Bollinger","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lily_Bollinger","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-q9kepfw6": {"id":"fl-q9kepfw6","key":"jacques-selosse","group":"champagne","region":"champagne","producer":"Jacques Selosse","producerId":"p-jacques-selosse","say":"zhahk suh-LOHSS","place":"Avize, Côte des Blancs, Champagne","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Substance","appellation":"Champagne","classification":"Grand Cru","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Chardonnay alone: a Blanc de Blancs.","sites":"The domaine's own Grand Cru Chardonnay in Avize, in the Côte des Blancs.","making":"Each year's wine, fermented in barrels with native yeasts, is added to a solera begun in 1986 by most accounts, and a part of the whole is drawn off to bottle, so every bottle carries a little of every year since. It is bottled with little dosage after long lees ageing, and the label gives the date of disgorging.","glass":"Bruised apple, walnut and salted caramel with a note of fino Sherry, over crushed chalk and lemon peel; dry, broad and vinous, with a fine mousse and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Ready on release, it gains nuttier, deeper notes for another decade, and many cellar it as they would white Burgundy.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, since colder it closes; decant it briefly if you like, or let it open in a white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Grand Cru","means":"The grapes come from Avize, one of the seventeen Grand Cru villages of Champagne."},{"term":"Blanc de Blancs","means":"A white Champagne made from white grapes alone, here Chardonnay."},{"term":"Récoltant-Manipulant","means":"A grower making Champagne from its own grapes; printed as RM before the registration number."}]},"table":"Jacques Selosse is the Avize grower held up as the model for grower Champagne, and Substance is its Chardonnay drawn from a solera, as in a Sherry bodega. It tastes of walnut, bruised apple and chalk: pour it in a wine glass, a little warmer than most Champagne.","story":"Jacques Selosse is a small domaine in Avize, in the Côte des Blancs, set up by its founder after the Second World War, in 1949 by several accounts. The family first made and bottled Champagne of its own in the years around 1960, rather than selling all its grapes to the houses. The founder's son, who had studied at the wine school in Beaune, joined him in 1974 and took charge in 1980. He brought Burgundy's ways back to Champagne: low yields, native yeasts, fermentation in barrels and a wine made first as a wine of place and only then as a sparkling wine. In 1986, by most accounts, he began a perpetual reserve of Avize Chardonnay on the model of a Sherry solera: each year the new wine goes into the reserve and a part of the whole is drawn off to bottle, so the blend carries a little of every year since it began. That wine is Substance. Its oxidative, nutty depth divided drinkers, but it made Selosse the model for the grower movement of the 1990s and 2000s, when small estates across Champagne began to bottle their own wines and to name their villages and plots on the label.","others":[{"name":"Selosse Initial","note":"The Grand Cru Blanc de Blancs that introduces the house style."},{"name":"Selosse V.O. Version Originale","note":"An Extra Brut Blanc de Blancs from the Grand Cru villages of the Côte des Blancs."},{"name":"Selosse Lieux-dits","note":"Single parcels bottled under their own names, the grower's clearest statement of place."}],"traps":["Substance carries no vintage: it is drawn from a solera of many years.","Récoltant-Manipulant is a label code for a grower, not a mark of quality.","Its nutty, Sherry like notes come from age and air in the solera, not from any fortification."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-vfhobcnt","front":"Jacques Selosse Substance: how is it made, and why does it carry no vintage?","back":"It is drawn from a solera of Avize Chardonnay begun in 1986 by most accounts: each year's wine is added and a part bottled, so every bottle blends many years."},{"id":"fl-6u2chyqo","front":"What did Jacques Selosse change in Champagne from 1980?","back":"It made Champagne as a wine of place, from low yields, native yeasts and barrels, and gave the grower movement its model."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-d8h2aqmt","q":"A guest is surprised that Selosse Substance shows no vintage. Which method explains it?","opts":["A solera, topped up each year with new wine","Carbon dioxide pumped into a Chardonnay before release","Saignée, bleeding juice from the Pinot Noir vats","A blend of declared vintages from bought grapes"],"a":0,"exp":"Substance is drawn from a solera of Avize Chardonnay begun in 1986 by most accounts; each year's wine refreshes it, so no single vintage applies. It uses no bought grapes and no saignée.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-8dpre7cy","q":"Where did the heir who took charge of Jacques Selosse in 1980 train, before bringing barrel fermentation and low yields to Avize?","opts":["Jerez","Burgundy","Alsace","Bordeaux"],"a":1,"exp":"He studied in Burgundy, at the wine school in Beaune, and brought its handling of wine to Avize; Sherry gave only the idea of the solera behind Substance, not his training.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Jacques Selosse","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacques_Selosse","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinepair: how Selosse changed grower Champagne","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/selosse-became-cult-champagne/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vins et Millésimes: the Selosse estate","url":"https://www.vinsetmillesimes.com/en/blog/post/the-selosse-estate-an-exceptional-growers-champagne.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Berry Bros and Rudd: Selosse Substance, Blanc de Blancs, Grand Cru","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-3453-champagne-jacques-selosse-substance-nv-brut-cuvee","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Comité Champagne: how to read a Champagne label","url":"https://www.champagne.fr/en/from-vine-to-wine/labelling/the-label","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-70biqggy": {"id":"fl-70biqggy","key":"raveneau","group":"burgundy-chablis","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine Raveneau","producerId":"p-dom-raveneau","say":"rahv-NOH","place":"Chablis, Chablis Grand Cru, Burgundy","founded":"1948","wine":{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Les Clos","appellation":"Chablis Grand Cru","classification":"Grand Cru, one of the seven climats of the single Chablis Grand Cru appellation","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, as every Chablis must be.","sites":"Parcels of Les Clos, the largest of the seven grand cru climats, on the hill that faces south west across the Serein from the town; stony limestone high on the slope, deeper clay lower down.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel tank, then moved to used oak casks, chiefly the traditional Chablis feuillette of 132 litres, with little or no new wood. The wine rests on its fine lees for a year to eighteen months before it is bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Oyster shell, lemon pith and green apple over wet chalk, with a note of iodine; tight and dense, with high acidity and a long, saline finish that turns towards honey, hazelnut and dried mushroom with age.","ageing":"Closed for its first five to ten years, it opens slowly and keeps for twenty years and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius, cool rather than cold; decant a young bottle for an hour; a white Burgundy glass with a wide bowl.","label":[{"term":"Chablis Grand Cru","means":"The one appellation for the seven climats on the hill across the Serein from the town."},{"term":"Les Clos","means":"The climat, the largest of the seven, its name recalling the dry stone walls that once enclosed its vines."}]},"table":"From the largest of the seven Chablis grand cru climats, Raveneau's Les Clos is fermented in tank and raised in old casks with little or no new wood. Tight and saline when young, it needs years in bottle, so decant it and pour it cool rather than cold.","story":"Chablis grew rich in the Middle Ages on wine shipped down the Yonne to Paris, and the Cistercian monks of Pontigny worked its slopes from the twelfth century; Les Clos may be the first kernel of their vineyard, and its name recalls the dry stone walls that once enclosed the vines. Phylloxera, war and frost shrank the district, and in 1938 the law gave the hill's seven climats a single appellation, Chablis Grand Cru. Ten years later the founder, whose name the domaine carries, married into the Dauvissat family, and the vines he had bought and the plots his wife's family held became one domaine. He was the first of his family to bottle his own wine, from the 1960s, and through the 1960s and 1970s, as grand cru land came up for sale, he bought parcels in Les Clos, Valmur and Blanchot. One son joined him in 1978 and a second followed, and they kept his method: fermentation in tank, then a year and more in old casks. In their cellar Les Clos shows that Chablis can be raised in wood and keep the taste of its limestone. On a list it belongs with the wines to cellar, not with the crisp aperitifs.","others":[{"name":"Chablis Premier Cru Montée de Tonnerre","note":"The premier cru that adjoins the grand cru hill, raised in old casks like the grands crus."},{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Valmur","note":"The broadest of the domaine's grands crus, from the climat between Grenouilles and Les Clos."},{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Blanchot","note":"From the climat at the south eastern end of the hill, beside Les Clos."}],"traps":["Chablis Grand Cru is one appellation of seven climats on a single hill, not seven appellations or seven villages.","Raveneau is Chablis raised in old wood: call it neither steel fresh nor new oaked, since the old casks give texture rather than a taste of new oak.","Domaine Raveneau and Domaine Vincent Dauvissat are linked by marriage but are separate estates."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-pl3exkg5","front":"Raveneau Les Clos: in what is it fermented, and in what is it then raised?","back":"Fermented in tank, then raised for a year and more in old oak casks, the Chablis feuillette among them."},{"id":"fl-fde4b0c6","front":"When was Domaine Raveneau founded, and how did its first vines come together?","back":"In 1948, when the founder married into the Dauvissat family and joined the vines he had bought to plots his wife's family held."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-j067udtx","q":"A guest ordering Raveneau's Les Clos asks how it differs from the other Chablis grand cru climats. Which fact fits?","opts":["It is the smallest of the seven, between Vaudésir and Valmur","It is a premier cru that adjoins the grand cru hill","It is the largest of the seven, facing south west","It is a walled monopole on the left bank of the Serein"],"a":2,"exp":"Les Clos is the largest of the seven climats, on the south west facing hill above the Serein; Grenouilles is the smallest, and Montée de Tonnerre is the premier cru beside the hill.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-tnlyk9dc","q":"Domaine Raveneau began in 1948 with family vines. How did it come to hold Les Clos, Valmur and Blanchot?","opts":["The monks of Pontigny left them to the family","The appellation decree of 1938 granted them","The Chablis cooperative passed them on in 1923","Its founder bought parcels in the 1960s and 1970s"],"a":3,"exp":"The founder bought grand cru parcels in the 1960s and 1970s, as land came up for sale; 1923 is the cooperative's founding, and the decree of 1938 drew the appellation, not anyone's holdings.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Domaine Raveneau","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domaine_Raveneau","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Raveneau, Chablis","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/producer/raveneau-chablis","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Domaine François Raveneau","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/producer/612ec280060000000000060f/domaine-raveneau","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The wines of Chablis: the climat Les Clos","url":"https://www.chablis-wines.com/explore/the-terroir/the-climats-of-chablis-micro-terroirs/les-clos,3251,15606.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The wines of Chablis: Thousands of years of history","url":"https://www.chablis-wines.com/discover/a-unique-territory-and-terroir/thousands-of-years-of-history/thousands-of-years-of-history,1808,7645.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-2krlpddn": {"id":"fl-2krlpddn","key":"vincent-dauvissat","group":"burgundy-chablis","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine Vincent Dauvissat","producerId":"p-dom-vincent-dauvissat","say":"doh-vee-SAH","place":"Chablis, Chablis Grand Cru, Burgundy","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Les Preuses","appellation":"Chablis Grand Cru","classification":"Grand Cru, one of the seven climats of the single Chablis Grand Cru appellation","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, as every Chablis must be.","sites":"Les Preuses, between Bougros and Vaudésir towards the north western end of the grand cru hill on the right bank of the Serein, facing south and south west over the valley, on clay and limestone over Kimmeridgian marl; the vines are farmed organically.","making":"Raised in old oak, in barrels and the traditional Chablis feuillette of 132 litres, with little or no new wood, and left long on its lees, so the cask shapes the texture more than the flavour. It is bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Lemon rind, crushed shell and white peach over chalk, rounder and more open than the house's Les Clos, with a quiet, creamy texture, bright acidity and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"More giving young than the house's Les Clos, it rewards ten years in bottle and keeps well beyond that in a strong vintage.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle briefly and an old one not at all; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Chablis Grand Cru","means":"One appellation shared by the seven climats on the hill across the Serein from the town."},{"term":"Les Preuses","means":"The climat, between Bougros and Vaudésir towards the north western end of the hill."},{"term":"René et Vincent Dauvissat","means":"The name long printed on the domaine's labels, for the founder's son and grandson; the domaine is also known as Vincent Dauvissat."}]},"table":"Dauvissat's Les Preuses is a Chablis grand cru from the north western reach of the hill, raised in old casks and feuillettes with little or no new wood. It is rounder and more open than the house's Les Clos, yet stony and saline, and it rewards ten years in bottle.","story":"Few Chablis growers bottled their own wine in 1931, when most sold it in cask to the merchants, but that year the founder of this domaine became one of the first to do so. In 1938 Chablis Grand Cru became an appellation, and the seven climats of the hill took one name on the label with their own beneath it. The founder's son was at work in the domaine from 1950, and his sister's marriage to the founder of Raveneau made the two families kin, so the domaines named together grew up side by side. The founder's grandson joined his father in 1976 and in time took charge. He kept the cellar as he found it: old barrels, the small Chablis feuillette, long lees ageing and little or no new oak, and in the early 2000s he turned the vineyards to organic farming. Les Preuses and Les Clos are the domaine's two grands crus, the first rounder and more open, the second firmer and slower. When a guest asks for Chablis raised in wood that keeps the taste of its limestone, Dauvissat is the other name beside Raveneau, and Les Preuses is the easier of its grands crus to open young.","others":[{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Les Clos","note":"The domaine's other grand cru, firmer and slower to open than Les Preuses."},{"name":"Chablis Premier Cru La Forest","note":"The premier cru that is the house's calling card, spelled La Forest without an accent."}],"traps":["Domaine Vincent Dauvissat is distinct from the other Dauvissat estates of Chablis, so read the first name on the label.","La Forest is spelled without an accent and is a premier cru, not a grand cru.","Les Preuses is a grand cru climat; Montée de Tonnerre and La Forest, often poured beside it, are premiers crus."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-y3eg3jhk","front":"Domaine Vincent Dauvissat: in what year did the family first bottle its own Chablis?","back":"1931, when bottling at the domaine was rare in Chablis."},{"id":"fl-bpw63lf2","front":"Which two grands crus does Dauvissat make, and which is the more open young?","back":"Les Clos and Les Preuses; Les Preuses is rounder and more open, Les Clos firmer and slower."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-gnqa9wdt","q":"A guest who knows only steel fermented Chablis asks what sets Dauvissat's Les Preuses apart. Which answer is right?","opts":["It is raised in old oak casks and feuillettes","It is aged three years in new barriques","It is a blend of several grand cru climats","It is a premier cru from the left bank"],"a":0,"exp":"Dauvissat raises its grands crus in old barrels and feuillettes with little or no new wood; Les Preuses is a single grand cru climat on the right bank, not a premier cru or a blend.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-in2v99yi","q":"Dauvissat and Raveneau are named together as the Chablis domaines that raise grand cru in wood. What links them?","opts":["Membership of the Chablis cooperative","Kinship by marriage and an old cask method","A single owner who bought both in 1989","A shared lease on the climat Les Preuses"],"a":1,"exp":"The families are linked by marriage and both raise their grands crus in old casks; each is a separate family estate, and neither is a member of La Chablisienne, the cooperative.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Decanter: Dauvissat, the wizard of Chablis","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/dauvissat-the-wizard-of-chablis-560535/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Domaine François Raveneau, taking the pulse of Chablis","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/domaine-francois-raveneau-taking-the-pulse-of-chablis-greatest-wine-538472/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Domaine René et Vincent Dauvissat","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/producer/612ec280060000000000032d/domaine-rene-vincent-dauvissat","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Alliance Wine: Vincent Dauvissat, producer page","url":"https://www.alliancewine.com/our-producers/vincent-dauvissat","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The wines of Chablis: the climat Preuses","url":"https://www.chablis-wines.com/explore/the-terroir/the-climats-of-chablis-micro-terroirs/preuses,3251,15608.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-ecg6pkuh": {"id":"fl-ecg6pkuh","key":"william-fevre","group":"burgundy-chablis","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine William Fèvre","producerId":"p-dom-william-fevre","say":"FEV-ruh","place":"Chablis, Chablis Grand Cru, Burgundy","founded":"1959","wine":{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Bougros Côte Bouguerots","appellation":"Chablis Grand Cru","classification":"Grand Cru: a named parcel within Bougros, one of the seven climats of Chablis Grand Cru","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, as every Chablis must be.","sites":"Côte Bouguerots, the steep, south facing lower slope of Bougros, the climat at the western end of the grand cru hill on the right bank of the Serein; poor soil over Kimmeridgian limestone and marl.","making":"Pressed gently and moved by gravity rather than pumped, it is fermented in stainless steel vats; a share of the wine that varies with the vintage then rests on its fine lees in French oak barrels several years old, never new, and the whole is brought together in steel before it is bottled at the domaine, well over a year after the harvest.","glass":"Lemon curd, ripe pear and sea spray over chalk, broad and ripe for a Chablis grand cru, with firm acidity, a supple middle and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Open within five years, it gains honey and hazelnut over the next ten and keeps longer in a strong vintage.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; no need to decant, though a young bottle opens with air in the glass; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Chablis Grand Cru","means":"The one appellation shared by the seven climats on the hill across the Serein."},{"term":"Bougros","means":"The climat at the western end of the hill, one of the seven."},{"term":"Côte Bouguerots","means":"The steep lower slope within Bougros: a named part of the climat, not an eighth grand cru."}]},"table":"Steep even by the measure of the Chablis hill, Côte Bouguerots is the lower slope of Bougros that William Fèvre bottles under its own name. Fermented in steel and partly raised in old oak, never new, it is broad and ripe for Chablis, yet saline.","story":"When the law drew the Chablis appellations in 1938, it gave the seven climats of the hill above the Serein one name, Chablis Grand Cru, but the district was in decline: phylloxera, war and frost had left only a fraction of its old vineyard planted, and the frost of 1957 took a whole crop. The domaine was founded in 1959 as the Domaine de la Maladière, by a grower whose family had long worked vines in Chablis, on the eve of the district's recovery, and over the decades that followed it came to hold one of the largest shares of grand cru in the district, in Les Clos, Bougros, Les Preuses, Valmur and Vaudésir. Its founder favoured new oak, which divided opinion in a place that prizes the taste of its limestone. In 1998 the domaine passed to the Henriot family of Champagne, who had taken the helm of Bouchard Père et Fils in Beaune in 1995. The new team set the new barrels aside and raised the grands crus in steel and older oak. Côte Bouguerots is the steep lower slope of Bougros, and the house bottles it under its own name, so that a guest can taste one slope within a climat. Here a large domaine's grand cru tastes of the hill, not of the cellar.","others":[{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Les Clos","note":"From the domaine's largest grand cru holding, in the largest of the seven climats."},{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Les Preuses","note":"A grand cru of rounder, more open character, from the north western reach of the hill."}],"traps":["Côte Bouguerots is a named parcel inside Bougros, not an eighth grand cru: Chablis Grand Cru has seven climats.","A large holding is no sign of blending: Fèvre bottles each grand cru climat apart, and Côte Bouguerots apart within Bougros.","Heavy new oak marked the domaine's wines before 1998; since then it has raised its grands crus in steel and older barrels, never new."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-uwsblvwb","front":"William Fèvre: what is Côte Bouguerots, and is it an eighth grand cru?","back":"The steep lower slope of the grand cru Bougros, bottled under its own name; a parcel within a climat, not an eighth grand cru."},{"id":"fl-eprrllql","front":"What changed at Domaine William Fèvre after it passed to new owners in 1998?","back":"The Henriot family of Champagne set the new barrels aside, raising the grands crus in steel and older oak."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-crvkeqrg","q":"A guest reads Bougros Côte Bouguerots on a William Fèvre label and asks what the second name means. What do you say?","opts":["An eighth grand cru added to Chablis in 1959","A premier cru that lies beside Bougros","The steep lower slope of Bougros, bottled apart","A blend of Bougros with its neighbour Les Preuses"],"a":2,"exp":"Côte Bouguerots is a named parcel within Bougros, one of the seven climats of Chablis Grand Cru; there is no eighth grand cru, and it is neither a premier cru nor a blend.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-3zpq6a8j","q":"In 1998 Domaine William Fèvre passed to new owners, three years after they had bought a Beaune house. Who were they?","opts":["La Chablisienne, the growers' cooperative","The Moët Hennessy group of Épernay","The Rothschild family of Lafite","The Henriot family of Champagne"],"a":3,"exp":"The Henriot family bought Bouchard Père et Fils in Beaune in 1995 and took over Domaine William Fèvre in 1998; La Chablisienne is the Chablis cooperative of 1923.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaines Barons de Rothschild wine directory: Bougros Côte Bouguerots","url":"https://winedirectory.lafite.com/en/the-wine-directory/product-N1QWPE-grand-cru-bougros-cote-bouguerots/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Domaine William Fèvre producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/producer-profiles/burgundy-producers/domaine-william-fevre-249296/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bouchard Père et Fils: Our heritage","url":"https://bouchard-pereetfils.com/en/the-domaine","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The wines of Chablis: the climat Bougros","url":"https://www.chablis-wines.com/explore/the-terroir/the-climats-of-chablis-micro-terroirs/bougros,3251,15605.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The wines of Chablis: Thousands of years of history","url":"https://www.chablis-wines.com/discover/a-unique-territory-and-terroir/thousands-of-years-of-history/thousands-of-years-of-history,1808,7645.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-aqe4wyv2": {"id":"fl-aqe4wyv2","key":"louis-michel","group":"burgundy-chablis","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine Louis Michel","producerId":"p-dom-louis-michel","say":"loo-EE mee-SHELL","place":"Chablis, Chablis Premier Cru, Burgundy","founded":"1850","wine":{"name":"Chablis Premier Cru Montée de Tonnerre","appellation":"Chablis Premier Cru","classification":"Premier Cru","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, as every Chablis must be.","sites":"Montée de Tonnerre, the premier cru on the right bank of the Serein just set back from the grand cru hill, across from Blanchot; west facing slopes of shallow soil over Kimmeridgian marly limestone.","making":"Fermented with its own yeasts in stainless steel tanks and aged in steel on its fine lees for a year or more, with no oak at any stage, as every wine of the domaine is, then bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Green apple, lemon zest and wet chalk with a note of crushed shell, taut and pure with no trace of wood, high acidity and a long, saline finish; age brings honey and toasted hazelnut.","ageing":"Bright and ready after three or four years, it gains depth over ten years and more in a good vintage.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass of moderate size.","label":[{"term":"Chablis Premier Cru","means":"The tier between Chablis and Chablis Grand Cru, with the climat named after it."},{"term":"Montée de Tonnerre","means":"The climat, across from the grand cru Blanchot on the right bank of the Serein."},{"term":"Louis Michel & Fils","means":"The name on the labels: Louis Michel and sons, the family domaine."}]},"table":"Not a stave of oak touches Louis Michel's Montée de Tonnerre: this Chablis premier cru from beside the grand cru hill is fermented and aged in steel alone. It is pure, taut and saline, with nothing between the fruit and the limestone, so pour it with oysters or white fish.","story":"The family counts its beginnings from 1850, when it planted its first vines in Chablis, then a busy vineyard sending its wine to Paris by river. The decades that followed were hard: phylloxera, wars and spring frosts left only a fraction of the old vineyard standing, and when the law drew its appellations in 1938 the district was at a low ebb. Recovery came after the frost of 1957, when heaters and sprinklers let growers plant again, and through it the Michel family took the opposite road from the old barrel domaines. Until then their wines had been made in old oak; from the end of the 1960s they turned to steel, and by about 1980 the last barrels were gone, so that every wine of the house, grand cru included, is fermented and aged in stainless steel. Their argument is that Chablis needs nothing from wood: the cold climate and the Kimmeridgian marl give the acidity, the salt and the shell, and steel keeps them clear. Montée de Tonnerre, the premier cru across from the grand cru hill, is where that argument is heard at its fullest. Poured beside a Raveneau or a Dauvissat from the same ground, it shows a guest what the cellar changes and what the vineyard does not.","others":[{"name":"Chablis Premier Cru Montmains","note":"A left bank premier cru from plots the family replanted in the 1980s, made in steel."},{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Grenouilles","note":"From the top of the smallest grand cru climat, made in steel like everything else."}],"traps":["Unoaked here means steel for every wine, grand cru included, not only for the village Chablis.","Montée de Tonnerre is a premier cru, though it adjoins the grand cru hill, and the labels read Louis Michel & Fils."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-i9sd31xm","front":"Louis Michel: what vessel ferments and ages every wine of the domaine?","back":"Stainless steel, with no oak at any stage, grand cru included."},{"id":"fl-s92vs99s","front":"Where does Montée de Tonnerre lie, and what is its rank?","back":"On the right bank of the Serein, across from the grand cru Blanchot; it is a premier cru."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-5p16fysq","q":"A guest wants a Chablis premier cru that has never touched wood. Why does Louis Michel's Montée de Tonnerre fit?","opts":["Chablis premier cru may not legally see oak","The domaine ferments and ages every wine in steel","It is blended from several left bank climats","It is raised in old feuillettes that give no flavour"],"a":1,"exp":"Louis Michel uses stainless steel for every wine; old feuillettes are the method of Raveneau and Dauvissat, and the law allows oak at every tier of Chablis.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-c3sbdxld","q":"Louis Michel took the opposite road from the old barrel domaines of Chablis. When did the family begin to give up oak?","opts":["At the end of the 1960s","When the domaine was founded in 1850","In the decade after phylloxera reached Chablis","When the appellation was drawn in 1938"],"a":0,"exp":"The first vintages in steel came at the end of the 1960s, and by about 1980 no barrel was left; before then the domaine's wines, like most Chablis, were made in old oak.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Berry Bros and Rudd: Chablis Montée de Tonnerre, Louis Michel et Fils","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-30215-2013-chablis-montee-de-tonnerre-domaine-louis-michel","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bourgogne Info: Domaine Louis Michel","url":"https://bourgogne-info.eu/html/domaine_louis_michel.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"iDealwine: Domaine Louis Michel","url":"https://www.idealwine.net/?p=47538","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Enotria and Coe: Louis Michel Chablis Premier Cru Montée de Tonnerre","url":"https://www.enotria.com/products/louis-michel-chablis-1er-cru-montee-de-tonnerre-2022","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The wines of Chablis: the climat Montée de Tonnerre","url":"https://www.chablis-wines.com/explore/the-terroir/the-climats-of-chablis-micro-terroirs/montee-de-tonnerre-chapelot-cote-de-brechain-pied-d-aloup,3251,15612.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-ioiph9mu": {"id":"fl-ioiph9mu","key":"la-chablisienne","group":"burgundy-chablis","region":"burgundy","producer":"La Chablisienne","producerId":"p-la-chablisienne","say":"lah shab-lee-ZYEN, shah-TOH gruh-NOO-yuh","place":"Chablis, Chablis Grand Cru, Burgundy","founded":"1923","wine":{"name":"Chablis Grand Cru Château Grenouilles","appellation":"Chablis Grand Cru","classification":"Grand Cru, from Grenouilles, one of the seven climats of Chablis Grand Cru","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, as every Chablis must be.","sites":"Château Grenouilles, a single block of vines covering most of Grenouilles, the smallest of the seven climats, between Vaudésir and Valmur on the right bank of the Serein, on clay and limestone over Kimmeridgian marl.","making":"The must is settled cold, then fermented and taken through its malolactic partly in oak barrels and partly in tank; the wine rests on its fine lees, in barrel and tank, for well over a year before the cooperative bottles it under the château's name.","glass":"Green apple, lemon peel and white blossom over chalk dust, with a cool sea breeze note, a supple, rounded middle and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Open after four or five years, it gains honey and hazelnut over ten and keeps longer in a strong vintage.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Château Grenouilles","means":"The estate in Grenouilles whose wine the cooperative bottles apart; a cooperative's wine, not a private grower's."},{"term":"Chablis Grand Cru","means":"The one appellation shared by the seven climats on the hill."},{"term":"La Chablisienne","means":"The Chablis growers' cooperative, founded in 1923, which makes and bottles the wine."}]},"table":"Château Grenouilles is La Chablisienne's grand cru, from most of Grenouilles, the smallest of the seven Chablis climats. Fermented in barrel and tank and long on its lees, it is supple and saline, and it shows what a cooperative of growers can bottle at grand cru level.","story":"In the hard years after the First World War, when Chablis wine was difficult to sell, a group of growers led by a priest joined together in 1923 to make and sell their wine as one, and La Chablisienne was born. Fifteen years later, in 1938, the law gave the seven climats of the grand cru hill a single appellation, Chablis Grand Cru. The cooperative grew with the district's recovery after the frosts of the 1950s, until its members farmed a large share of the appellation. Grenouilles is the smallest of the seven climats, set between Vaudésir and Valmur beside the Serein; its name, written as early as 1321, means frogs. Most of it lies in one block, Château Grenouilles, an estate the cooperative took on in 2003, by its own account, and whose wine it bottles apart under the château's name. That a grand cru should come from a cooperative surprises many guests, and it is a question a candidate meets. Château Grenouilles is the proof that Chablis Grand Cru is not the wine of small domaines alone.","others":[{"name":"Chablis Les Vénérables","note":"The cooperative's village Chablis under a name of its own."},{"name":"Petit Chablis Pas si Petit","note":"The cooperative's Petit Chablis, from Portlandian limestone, raised in tank; its name means not so small."}],"traps":["Grenouilles is the smallest of the seven grand cru climats of Chablis; it is not a premier cru.","Château Grenouilles is a cooperative wine, not a private grower's estate bottling.","Louis Michel also holds vines at the top of Grenouilles; Château Grenouilles is La Chablisienne's wine from most of the climat."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-to36eftd","front":"La Chablisienne: when was it founded, and what kind of house is it?","back":"1923; a cooperative of Chablis growers who make and sell their wine together."},{"id":"fl-s9ecqvot","front":"What sets Grenouilles apart among the seven Chablis grand cru climats, and who bottles most of it?","back":"It is the smallest of the seven; La Chablisienne bottles most of it as Château Grenouilles."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-48elwjvb","q":"A guest orders La Chablisienne's grand cru and asks where it stands among the seven climats. What do you tell them?","opts":["It is a blend of all seven climats","It is a premier cru below the hill","It is the smallest of the seven climats","It is the largest, beside Blanchot"],"a":2,"exp":"Grenouilles, between Vaudésir and Valmur, is the smallest of the seven climats; Les Clos is the largest, and Château Grenouilles comes from one climat, not a blend.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-jty1obj4","q":"A guest is surprised that a Chablis grand cru comes from a cooperative. When did the growers of La Chablisienne first join together?","opts":["In 1885, as phylloxera arrived","In 1938, with the appellation decree","In 1959, as frost protection spread","In 1923, after the First World War"],"a":3,"exp":"La Chablisienne was founded in 1923, in the hard years after the First World War; 1938 is the year the Chablis Grand Cru appellation was drawn, and frost protection spread later.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"La Chablisienne: Château Grenouilles","url":"https://chablisienne.com/chateau-grenouilles.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"La Chablisienne: Château Grenouilles 2022 technical sheet","url":"https://chablisienne.com/media/wysiwyg/fiches_techniques/Chablis_grand_cru_Ch_teau_Grenouilles_2022.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: La Chablisienne","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Chablisienne","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The wines of Chablis: the climat Grenouilles","url":"https://www.chablis-wines.com/explore/the-terroir/the-climats-of-chablis-micro-terroirs/grenouilles,3251,15607.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bourgogne wines: the Chablis Grand Cru appellation","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/bourgogne-and-its-appellations/chablis-grand-cru,2458,9253.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-v5r5wf8w": {"id":"fl-v5r5wf8w","key":"romanee-conti","group":"burgundy-nuits","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine de la Romanée-Conti","producerId":"p-dom-romanee-conti","say":"roh-mah-NAY kon-TEE","place":"Vosne-Romanée, Côte de Nuits, Burgundy","founded":"1869","wine":{"name":"Romanée-Conti","appellation":"Romanée-Conti","classification":"Grand Cru of the Côte de Nuits, held whole by the domaine as a monopole","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"The single small vineyard of Romanée-Conti in the middle of the Vosne-Romanée slope, with La Romanée above it, Richebourg to the north, La Grande Rue to the south and Romanée-Saint-Vivant below.","making":"Fermented in wooden vats over a long cuvaison of up to a month, in most vintages wholly as whole bunches with the stems kept. The wine is aged in new oak barrels for sixteen to twenty months and bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Rose petal, red cherry and wild strawberry over Chinese five spice, orange peel and forest floor; light in body yet deep, with silken, fine tannin and a long, perfumed finish.","ageing":"Supple enough to taste young but at its fullest after fifteen to twenty years, and a strong vintage lasts for decades.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant only to take an old bottle off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Romanée-Conti","means":"The grand cru, its own appellation; the vineyard's name stands alone, with no village."},{"term":"Société Civile","means":"The label names the owner, the Société Civile du Domaine de la Romanée-Conti, which has held the domaine since 1942."},{"term":"Bouteilles récoltées","means":"The count of bottles the vintage gave, printed with this bottle's own number."}]},"table":"Named for the Prince de Conti, who bought it in 1760, Romanée-Conti is the monopole of the Domaine de la Romanée-Conti, a single small grand cru in the middle of the Vosne-Romanée slope. Light in body yet long and perfumed, it wants a wide glass and time.","story":"The priory of Saint-Vivant held this vineyard in the Middle Ages, and by the middle of the seventeenth century it was known as La Romanée, for a reason nobody has found. In 1760 it was bought by Louis François de Bourbon, Prince de Conti (1717 to 1776), and the vineyard came to carry his name; the tale that he outbid the King's favourite for it is doubted. The Revolution seized it from his son, and in 1794 it was sold at auction. In 1869 it was bought by a wine merchant whose heirs, the de Villaine family among them, built the domaine around it, and in 1933 the domaine bought the rest of La Tâche at the auction of the Liger-Belair estate, which made it a second monopole. In 1942 the domaine became a société civile, and the Leroy family bought half of it. Romanée-Conti kept its ungrafted vines through phylloxera until the harvest of 1945; they were then pulled up, the vineyard was replanted in 1947, and the next Romanée-Conti was the 1952 vintage. For a sommelier it is the name every guest knows and few have tasted: a grand cru held whole by one domaine, light and endless rather than powerful.","others":[{"name":"La Tâche","note":"The domaine's other monopole, a little to the south beyond La Grande Rue, darker and firmer than Romanée-Conti."},{"name":"Richebourg","note":"A grand cru the domaine shares with other owners, broader and more muscular than its monopoles."},{"name":"Romanée-Saint-Vivant","note":"A shared grand cru below Romanée-Conti on the slope, delicate rather than broad."}],"traps":["Romanée-Conti and La Tâche are the domaine's monopoles; Richebourg and Romanée-Saint-Vivant are shared with other owners.","Romanée-Conti is one of six grands crus of Vosne-Romanée; Échezeaux and Grands Échezeaux lie in Flagey-Échezeaux.","Domaine de la Romanée-Conti is the house; Romanée-Conti is one vineyard and its one wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-v23ymhpz","front":"Romanée-Conti: who bought the vineyard in 1760, and what did it keep of him?","back":"Louis François de Bourbon, Prince de Conti; the vineyard came to carry his name."},{"id":"fl-5gep155x","front":"Until what harvest did Romanée-Conti keep its ungrafted vines, and when was it replanted?","back":"Until the harvest of 1945; it was replanted in 1947, and the next Romanée-Conti was the 1952 vintage."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ks7k9x82","q":"A guest asks whether any domaine besides the Domaine de la Romanée-Conti bottles a Romanée-Conti. What is the answer?","opts":["No: it is a premier cru of Vosne","No: the vineyard is the domaine's monopole","Yes: it is shared like Richebourg","Yes: merchants may buy its grapes"],"a":1,"exp":"Romanée-Conti is held whole by the domaine, as La Tâche is; Richebourg and Romanée-Saint-Vivant are shared, and Romanée-Conti is a grand cru, not a premier cru.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2hlrrw5v","q":"In 1760 the vineyard then called La Romanée changed hands, and it came to carry the buyer's name. Who bought it?","opts":["Louis François de Bourbon, Prince de Conti","The King's favourite, after a bidding war","The Liger-Belair family of Vosne","The monks of the priory of Saint-Vivant"],"a":0,"exp":"The Prince de Conti bought it in 1760 and it came to carry his name; the tale that he outbid the King's favourite is doubted, and the priory of Saint-Vivant held it centuries earlier.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Romanée-Conti","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman%C3%A9e-Conti","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Domaine de la Romanée-Conti producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/domaine-de-la-romanee-conti-profile-wine-ratings-384876/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Sotheby's: Domaine de la Romanée-Conti, the essential guide","url":"https://www.sothebys.com/en/articles/domaine-de-la-romanee-conti-a-collectors-guide-to-the-renowned-burgundy-wines","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Domaine de la Romanée-Conti: Romanée-Conti","url":"https://romanee-conti.fr/en/9-grand-crus/7/romanee-conti","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Justia Trademarks: the domaine's filed label for La Tâche","url":"https://trademarks.justia.com/786/18/societe-civile-du-domaine-de-la-romanee-conti-proprietaire-a-vosne-romanee-cote-d-or-france-la-tache-appellation-la-tache-78618498.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-b1odpwt6": {"id":"fl-b1odpwt6","key":"armand-rousseau","group":"burgundy-nuits","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine Armand Rousseau","producerId":"p-dom-armand-rousseau","say":"shahm-bair-TAN","place":"Gevrey-Chambertin, Côte de Nuits, Burgundy","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Chambertin","appellation":"Chambertin","classification":"Grand Cru of the Côte de Nuits","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"Parcels of Chambertin, the grand cru of the Gevrey slope between Chambertin-Clos de Bèze to the north and Latricières-Chambertin to the south, with Griotte-Chambertin and Charmes-Chambertin below it to the east.","making":"The grapes are sorted as they reach the cellar and fermented at the domaine. The wine is then aged for well over a year in oak barrels that are all new, as only the Chambertin and the Clos de Bèze are each year, the house's other wines taking less new wood, and is bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Black cherry, liquorice and violet over iron, game and a savoury, earthy depth; firm, fine tannin and a long, deep finish, more power than charm when young.","ageing":"Tight and tannic for ten years or more, it opens slowly and lasts for decades in a strong vintage.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one only off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Chambertin","means":"The grand cru, its own appellation; the vineyard's name stands alone, with no village."},{"term":"Grand Cru","means":"The rank above Premier Cru, printed with the vineyard's name."},{"term":"Domaine Armand Rousseau","means":"The house, named for the founder who began to bottle his own wine in the 1930s."}]},"table":"From the Gevrey grand cru that lies between Clos de Bèze and Latricières, Domaine Armand Rousseau's Chambertin is raised wholly in new oak, as its Clos de Bèze is. Dense, firm and savoury, and slower to open than that neighbour, it repays a decanter and a long wait.","story":"Gevrey's great vineyard began with its neighbour: in 640, as the Bourgogne wine body records it, a duke gave a clos here to the monks of the abbey of Bèze, and Chambertin, the story goes, is the field of a man named Bertin who planted beside them, the champ de Bertin, a name in the records by the thirteenth century. Napoleon, as the legend tells it, drank its wine with water at his meals. In 1847 the village of Gevrey added the vineyard's name to its own. Armand Rousseau (1884 to 1959) came into a house and his first vines in Gevrey with his marriage in 1909, and he bought parcels of the grands crus, Mazis-Chambertin in 1937 among them. In the 1930s, urged on by the editor of the Revue du Vin de France, he began to bottle and sell his own wine, among the first growers of the Côte d'Or to do so, when most sold their wine in cask to the merchants. His son Charles, who died in 2016, took charge in 1959 and widened the holdings. Chambertin, firmer and slower than the house's Clos de Bèze, shows the domaine's method plainest: every barrel new, and patience.","others":[{"name":"Chambertin-Clos de Bèze","note":"The neighbouring grand cru to the north, the older vineyard of the two, which may also be sold as Chambertin."},{"name":"Gevrey-Chambertin Premier Cru Clos Saint-Jacques","note":"A premier cru often argued deserving of grand cru, of which the domaine holds the largest share."},{"name":"Ruchottes-Chambertin Clos des Ruchottes","note":"A walled monopole of the house within the grand cru Ruchottes-Chambertin."}],"traps":["Clos de Bèze may be sold as Chambertin; Chambertin may never be sold as Clos de Bèze.","Clos Saint-Jacques is a premier cru, however highly it is prized, and the domaine is one of its five owners.","Gevrey-Chambertin is the village appellation; Chambertin alone, with no village name, is the grand cru."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-rrgn6jrw","front":"Domaine Armand Rousseau: which neighbouring grand cru may legally be sold as Chambertin?","back":"Chambertin-Clos de Bèze; Chambertin itself may never be sold as Clos de Bèze."},{"id":"fl-qevie1f6","front":"In what decade did Armand Rousseau begin to bottle his own wine, and who urged him on?","back":"The 1930s, urged by the editor of the Revue du Vin de France, when most growers sold in cask to the merchants."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-z276wodt","q":"A guest asks how Domaine Armand Rousseau raises its Chambertin. Which answer is right?","opts":["In stainless steel tanks, with no wood at all","In barrels a year old, to keep the oak in check","In large old oak casks, with no new wood","In barrels that are all new, as its Clos de Bèze is"],"a":3,"exp":"Rousseau ages its Chambertin and its Clos de Bèze in barrels that are all new each year, while its other wines take less new wood; it raises neither in large old casks nor in steel.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-syo7r2kj","q":"Gevrey's grand cru takes its name, the story goes, from a man who planted beside the monks' vineyard. What was Chambertin at first?","opts":["A vineyard given to a marshal of Napoleon","The chapel of the abbey of Bèze","The field of a peasant named Bertin","A hunting ground of the Dukes of Burgundy"],"a":2,"exp":"Champ de Bertin, Bertin's field, lay beside the clos given to the abbey of Bèze in the seventh century; Napoleon drank the wine but gave it no name.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Domaine Armand Rousseau","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domaine_Armand_Rousseau","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Sotheby's: A collector's guide to Domaine Armand Rousseau","url":"https://www.sothebys.com/en/articles/a-collectors-guide-to-domaine-armand-rousseau","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Frederick Wildman: Rousseau Chambertin-Clos de Bèze Grand Cru 2015","url":"https://frederickwildman.com/producers/domaine-armand-rousseau/chambertin-clos-de-beze-grand-cru-2015-2","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bourgogne wines: Gevrey-Chambertin","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/bourgogne-and-its-appellations/gevrey-chambertin,2458,9253.html?args=Y29tcF9pZD0yMjc4JmFjdGlvbj12aWV3RmljaGUmaWQ9MzA5Jnw%3D","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Chambertin","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chambertin","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-1x84mmsh": {"id":"fl-1x84mmsh","key":"comte-georges-de-vogue","group":"burgundy-nuits","region":"burgundy","producer":"Comte Georges de Vogüé","producerId":"p-comte-georges-de-vogue","say":"duh voh-GWAY, mew-zee-NYEE","place":"Chambolle-Musigny, Côte de Nuits, Burgundy","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Musigny Grand Cru Vieilles Vignes","appellation":"Musigny","classification":"Grand Cru of the Côte de Nuits","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"The domaine's larger part of Musigny, Les Petits Musigny entire among it, at the southern end of Chambolle above the Clos de Vougeot; only vines of twenty five years and more go into this wine.","making":"Fermented in vats and aged in oak barrels, a third or a little more of them new, for well over a year before bottling at the domaine. Musigny vines younger than twenty five years go into the domaine's Chambolle-Musigny Premier Cru instead.","glass":"Red and black cherry, violet and rose over iron, liquorice and forest floor; silken and perfumed yet firmly built, with fine tannin and a long, slow finish.","ageing":"Reserved and tightly wound young, it shows its perfume only after a decade in bottle and lasts for decades.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one gently off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Musigny","means":"The grand cru, its own appellation, at the southern end of Chambolle-Musigny."},{"term":"Vieilles Vignes","means":"Old vines: here Musigny vines of twenty five years and more, though the law does not define the term."},{"term":"Comte Georges de Vogüé","means":"The domaine's name, after the count who ran it from 1925 until his death in 1987."}]},"table":"Only the older vines of de Vogüé's large share of Musigny, the grand cru at the southern end of Chambolle, go into its Musigny Vieilles Vignes. Reserved and tightly wound young, silken and perfumed with age, it rewards a decade's patience.","story":"The family's vines in Chambolle go back to 1450, and the estate passed down by descent and by marriage until, in 1766, a marriage brought it into the de Vogüé family. Comte Georges de Vogüé inherited it from his father in 1925, gave it the name it bears and ran it until his death in 1987. The domaine holds the larger part of the grand cru Musigny, Les Petits Musigny entire. Musigny is also the one grand cru of the Côte de Nuits with a white wine, and de Vogüé is its maker. After a replanting in the late 1980s and the 1990s, the domaine sold that white as Bourgogne Blanc from the 1994 vintage, and the Musigny name returned to its label with the 2015 vintage. Only vines of twenty five years and more go into Musigny Vieilles Vignes; the younger make the domaine's Chambolle premier cru, so the grand cru carries the depth of old roots. Slow and structured, the face of Chambolle's perfume that opens only after ten years, it shows beside the house's Bonnes-Mares how one village can give two such different grands crus.","others":[{"name":"Bonnes-Mares","note":"The domaine's other grand cru, firmer and earthier, from the Chambolle side of the vineyard."},{"name":"Chambolle-Musigny Premier Cru Les Amoureuses","note":"The premier cru below Musigny, often argued for promotion to grand cru."},{"name":"Musigny Blanc","note":"The white of Musigny, sold as Bourgogne Blanc from 1994 until the name returned with 2015."}],"traps":["Musigny Blanc was sold as Bourgogne Blanc from 1994 while replanted vines matured; it carries the Musigny name again from the 2015 vintage.","Bonnes-Mares straddles Chambolle-Musigny and Morey-Saint-Denis; Musigny lies wholly in Chambolle.","Young Musigny vines go into the domaine's Chambolle-Musigny Premier Cru, not into the Vieilles Vignes."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-rpfndq2d","front":"Comte Georges de Vogüé: which vines make the Musigny Vieilles Vignes, and where do the younger ones go?","back":"Only Musigny vines of twenty five years and more; the younger go into the domaine's Chambolle-Musigny Premier Cru."},{"id":"fl-zyenbqli","front":"Which grand cru of the Côte de Nuits gives a white wine as well as red, and from which domaine?","back":"Musigny; Comte Georges de Vogüé, whose white was sold as Bourgogne Blanc from the 1994 vintage until the name returned with 2015."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-mutl6z0c","q":"A guest asks why de Vogüé's Musigny is called Vieilles Vignes when the law does not define the term. What do you say?","opts":["The domaine keeps it for its older Musigny vines","It means the vines are ungrafted","It is a legal grade above Grand Cru","It marks the domaine's white Musigny"],"a":0,"exp":"The domaine keeps the name for Musigny vines of twenty five years and more and sends the younger to its Chambolle premier cru; the term is unregulated and is not a grade.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-dftkqxis","q":"After replanting, de Vogüé's white Musigny was sold under a humbler name for two decades. Which name?","opts":["Chambolle-Musigny Blanc","Bourgogne Blanc","Vin de France","Côte de Nuits-Villages"],"a":1,"exp":"From the 1994 vintage the white was declassified to Bourgogne Blanc while the young vines matured; Musigny returned to its label with the 2015 vintage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Decanter: Comte Georges de Vogüé, ever en vogue","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/ever-en-vogue-249036/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Domaine Comte Georges de Vogüé producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/producer-profiles/producer-profile-comte-georges-de-vogue-245996/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: de Vogüé brings back Musigny Blanc","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2017/03/de-vogue-brings-back-musigny-blanc/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bourgogne wines: Musigny","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/bourgogne-and-its-appellations/musigny,2458,9253.html?args=Y29tcF9pZD0yMjc4JmFjdGlvbj12aWV3RmljaGUmaWQ9MzYwJnw%3D","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Berry Bros and Rudd: 2015 Musigny Blanc, Comte Georges de Vogüé","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-48647-2015-musigny-blanc-de-vogue","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-vnltv9o5": {"id":"fl-vnltv9o5","key":"georges-roumier","group":"burgundy-nuits","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine Georges Roumier","producerId":"p-dom-georges-roumier","say":"roo-MYAY, bon-MAR","place":"Chambolle-Musigny, Côte de Nuits, Burgundy","founded":"1924","wine":{"name":"Bonnes-Mares","appellation":"Bonnes-Mares","classification":"Grand Cru of the Côte de Nuits","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"Two parcels of Bonnes-Mares, one in the terres blanches of pale, chalky marl high on the slope and one in the terres rouges of reddish clay lower down, in the grand cru that runs from Chambolle-Musigny into Morey-Saint-Denis.","making":"Partly destemmed, with a share of whole bunches that changes with the vintage, often a third or more. The two parcels are fermented and aged apart, in oak barrels a third to a half of them new, for fourteen to eighteen months, then blended and bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Black cherry, red currant and rose stem over crushed stone and a savoury earthiness; dense and firm for Chambolle, with fine, grippy tannin and a long, cool finish.","ageing":"Firm and closed young, it needs ten to fifteen years in bottle and lasts well beyond.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one gently off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Bonnes-Mares","means":"The grand cru, its own appellation, shared between Chambolle-Musigny and Morey-Saint-Denis."},{"term":"Grand Cru","means":"The rank above Premier Cru, printed with the vineyard's name."}]},"table":"Drawn from two parcels of Bonnes-Mares, one on pale marl high on the slope and one on red clay below, Domaine Georges Roumier's wine is firmer and darker than most Chambolle. It is slow to open, so keep it ten years and pour it with game.","story":"Bonnes-Mares runs along the slope from Chambolle-Musigny into Morey-Saint-Denis, and its name is read either as the good mothers, the Cistercian nuns of Tart who held vines here from the twelfth century, or as marer, an old word for tilling the ground. The domaine dates from 1924, when its founder married into a Chambolle family and his wife's dowry of vines came with her. In 1945 he began to bottle part of his wine at the domaine, when most growers sold theirs in cask to the merchants. The founder's son took over in the 1960s, and the founder's grandson joined him in the early 1980s. Under the family the style has stayed fine and cool: a share of whole bunches, some new oak and no more. The domaine also holds Clos de la Bussière, a premier cru in Morey-Saint-Denis, whole. Bonnes-Mares shows the house plainly, because its two parcels lie on two soils, pale marl high on the slope and red clay below, and the domaine raises them apart before it blends them. Explain those two soils to a guest and you have explained why one vineyard can give two textures in a single wine.","others":[{"name":"Chambolle-Musigny Premier Cru Les Amoureuses","note":"The premier cru below Musigny, often argued for promotion to grand cru."},{"name":"Musigny","note":"From a sliver of the grand cru, made in very small quantity."},{"name":"Morey-Saint-Denis Premier Cru Clos de la Bussière","note":"The domaine's premier cru monopole in Morey-Saint-Denis."}],"traps":["Clos de la Bussière is in Morey-Saint-Denis, not Chambolle-Musigny.","Bonnes-Mares straddles Chambolle-Musigny and Morey-Saint-Denis; its terres rouges and terres blanches are soils within one cru, not two wines.","Musigny is not a de Vogüé monopole: Roumier and others hold small parts of it."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-2pv33vpp","front":"Domaine Georges Roumier: which two soils of Bonnes-Mares go into its wine, and how are they made?","back":"The terres blanches of pale marl and the terres rouges of reddish clay, one parcel in each, fermented and aged apart, then blended."},{"id":"fl-p3il3roi","front":"Which monopole does Roumier hold, and in which village does it lie?","back":"Clos de la Bussière, a premier cru in Morey-Saint-Denis."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-n75xvj70","q":"A guest asks which village Roumier's Bonnes-Mares comes from. What is the precise answer?","opts":["A village wine of Vougeot","A premier cru of Chambolle alone","A grand cru shared by Chambolle and Morey","A grand cru lying wholly within Morey"],"a":2,"exp":"Bonnes-Mares is a grand cru that runs from Chambolle-Musigny into Morey-Saint-Denis, with the smaller part in Morey; it is no premier cru and no village wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-sob7n528","q":"The domaine that became Georges Roumier took shape in Chambolle in 1924. How did its founder come by his first vines?","opts":["By leasing them from a Beaune merchant","By inheriting them from the de Vogüé family","By buying them from the nuns of Tart","By marrying into a Chambolle family"],"a":3,"exp":"His marriage in 1924 brought his wife's dowry of Chambolle vines; the nuns of Tart lost their vineyards at the Revolution.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Decanter: Domaine Georges Roumier producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/domaine-georges-roumier-profile-383561/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Christie's: Domaine Georges Roumier, a Burgundy wine guide","url":"https://www.christies.com.cn/en/stories/collecting-guide-domaine-roumier-71dd0aeab90c45a888337a0e8d06b8e8","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bonnes Mares","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonnes_Mares","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Domaine de la Vougeraie: Bonnes Mares Grand Cru sheet","url":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/boissetfamilyestates-assets/pdfs/tech-sheets/Bonnes_Mares_Grand_Cru_GB19.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Leon and Son: 2023 Domaine Roumier Bonnes Mares Grand Cru","url":"https://leonandsonwine.com/products/2023-domaine-roumier-bonnes-mares-grand-cru-burgundy-france","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-umoxkvwe": {"id":"fl-umoxkvwe","key":"clos-de-tart","group":"burgundy-nuits","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine du Clos de Tart","producerId":"p-dom-clos-de-tart","say":"kloh-duh-TAR","place":"Morey-Saint-Denis, Côte de Nuits, Burgundy","founded":"1141","wine":{"name":"Clos de Tart","appellation":"Clos de Tart","classification":"Grand Cru of the Côte de Nuits since 1939, held whole as a monopole","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"The walled clos itself, on the Morey-Saint-Denis slope between Clos des Lambrays to the north and Bonnes-Mares to the south, its rows planted across the slope from north to south rather than down it and picked in separate parcels.","making":"The parcels of the clos are picked and fermented apart and gathered into pre-blends before they are aged in oak barrels and blended. Wine the domaine judges below the grand cru, from the younger vines among others, is declassified, and the grand cru is bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Black cherry, plum and crushed violet over iron, liquorice and a savoury, stony depth; dark and firmly built, with dense, fine tannin and a long, cool finish.","ageing":"Dark and closed young, it opens after about ten years and lasts for decades.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one gently off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Clos de Tart","means":"The grand cru, its own appellation, a walled vineyard of Morey-Saint-Denis."},{"term":"Grand Cru","means":"The rank above Premier Cru, printed with the vineyard's name; Clos de Tart has held it since 1939."}]},"table":"A walled grand cru held whole since Cistercian nuns acquired it in 1141, Clos de Tart is made entirely by the Domaine du Clos de Tart. Its rows run across the slope, and its wine is dark, firm and slow, so decant it an hour ahead.","story":"In 1141 the Cistercian nuns of Notre-Dame de Tart acquired a vineyard on the slope of Morey from a hospital house at Brochon; the name Clos de Tart came later. They held it for six and a half centuries, and in all that time it was never divided. The Revolution took it from them, and in 1791 it was sold at auction as national property to the Marey-Monge family, who kept it whole until 1932, when it passed to the Mommessin family, merchants of Mâcon. By a decree of 4 January 1939 it became a grand cru appellation of its own. The Mommessin heirs sold it in 2017 to Artémis Domaines, so that in almost nine centuries the clos has known only a handful of owners. Its vines run across the slope, from north to south, against the usual Burgundian rows, a layout said to hold the soil against erosion, and the domaine picks and ferments its parcels apart before it blends the wine; the younger vines go to a second wine, La Forge de Tart. Candidates learn it as the textbook monopole, with a founding year and a short line of owners; guests meet the dark, firm voice of Morey.","others":[{"name":"La Forge de Tart","note":"The second wine, labelled Morey-Saint-Denis Premier Cru, from the younger vines of the clos."}],"traps":["Clos de Tart is a full monopole; Clos des Lambrays, next door, is a near monopole.","Morey-Saint-Denis holds four grands crus wholly its own, Clos de Tart among them, plus the Morey slice of Bonnes-Mares.","Clos de Tart is not Clos de Vougeot: both are walled Cistercian vineyards, but Vougeot is split among many owners."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xbwdemro","front":"Clos de Tart: who acquired the vineyard, and in what year?","back":"The Cistercian nuns of Notre-Dame de Tart, in 1141."},{"id":"fl-4mm6jeso","front":"How are the rows of Clos de Tart planted, and why is that unusual?","back":"Across the slope rather than up and down it, the reverse of the usual Burgundian layout."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-fjevcd0g","q":"A guest notices that the rows of Clos de Tart run across the slope. What sets that layout apart?","opts":["It copies the rows of Clos de Vougeot","It is the reverse of the usual Burgundian rows","It is required of every monopole in Burgundy","It is required of every Morey grand cru"],"a":1,"exp":"Most Burgundy vines run up and down the slope; Clos de Tart's rows run across it, north to south, to hold the soil, and no rule requires it of a monopole or a grand cru.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-hgt4hluk","q":"Clos de Tart has changed hands very rarely since 1141. Which family held it from 1932 until 2017?","opts":["The Mommessin family","The Leroy family","The Marey-Monge family","The de Vogüé family"],"a":0,"exp":"The Mommessin family bought it in 1932 and sold it to Artémis Domaines in 2017; the Marey-Monge family held it from the Revolution until 1932.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Clos de Tart","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clos_de_Tart","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Corney and Barrow: Clos de Tart, the times they are a changin","url":"https://www.corneyandbarrow.com/clos-de-tart-the-times-they-are-a-changin","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Clos de Tart 1947 to 2019 vertical tasting","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/clos-de-tart-1947-2019-vertical-tasting-483674","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"French Ministry of Agriculture: Clos de Tart specification","url":"https://info.agriculture.gouv.fr/boagri/document_administratif-c984dfd1-de81-4722-a9f6-9ace397bb3e4/telechargement","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Thienpont Wine: Clos de Tart","url":"https://www.thienpontwine.com/en/appellation/bourgogne/clos-de-tart","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-i679e293": {"id":"fl-i679e293","key":"leflaive","group":"burgundy-beaune","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine Leflaive","producerId":"p-dom-leflaive","say":"luh-FLEV, shuh-vahl-YAY mon-rah-SHAY","place":"Puligny-Montrachet, Côte de Beaune, Burgundy","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Chevalier-Montrachet","appellation":"Chevalier-Montrachet","classification":"Grand Cru of the Côte de Beaune","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone.","sites":"Chevalier-Montrachet, the grand cru on the upper slope above Le Montrachet, at about 265 to 290 metres and wholly within Puligny-Montrachet, on thin, stony soil over marl and marly limestone; the vines are farmed biodynamically.","making":"Fermented in oak barrels, about a quarter of them new, and aged in them for about twelve months, then moved to stainless steel tank for about six months more on its lees before it is bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Acacia and lime blossom, white peach and lemon zest over hazelnut and crushed chalk; taut and slow to open, a fine, stony line rather than richness, with a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Closed for its first five to eight years, it opens slowly and keeps for twenty years and more.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a wide white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Chevalier-Montrachet","means":"The grand cru, its own appellation, on the slope above Le Montrachet in Puligny."},{"term":"Grand Cru","means":"The rank above Premier Cru, printed beneath the vineyard's name."},{"term":"Domaine Leflaive","means":"The family domaine of Puligny, distinct from Olivier Leflaive, a négociant house of the village."}]},"table":"Just above Le Montrachet, on thin and stony soil, Leflaive farms its Chevalier-Montrachet biodynamically and raises it in barrel, then tank. Taut, chalky and slow to open, more line than richness, it is a white that gains from air when young.","story":"The Leflaive family has been rooted in Puligny since 1717, by the domaine's own account, but the domaine took its present shape in the early twentieth century, when Joseph Leflaive (1870 to 1953), a naval engineer, came home to a village whose vines phylloxera had laid low. He bought vineyards one by one and replanted them on grafted rootstocks, parcel by parcel. After his death in 1953 two of his sons ran the domaine together until the early 1990s. In 1990 the daughter of one of them, Anne-Claude Leflaive (1956 to 2015), came to share the direction with a cousin and began biodynamic trials on a small plot; from 1994 she led the domaine alone, and in 1996, by the domaine's own account, she put the whole estate under biodynamic farming. In the early 1990s the domaine also added a small parcel of Le Montrachet. Chevalier-Montrachet is the wine it leads with: its parcels sit on the stony upper slope above Le Montrachet, well above Bâtard lower down, and give a narrower, more chiselled wine than Bâtard. Les Pucelles, the premier cru beside Bienvenues, is the one argued for promotion; the Chevalier is the house's grand cru measure. Its virtues are line and patience, and it asks the same of whoever opens it.","others":[{"name":"Puligny-Montrachet Premier Cru Les Pucelles","note":"The premier cru beside Bienvenues-Bâtard-Montrachet, often argued for promotion."},{"name":"Bâtard-Montrachet","note":"The grand cru below Le Montrachet, broader and heavier than the Chevalier."},{"name":"Bienvenues-Bâtard-Montrachet","note":"A grand cru lying wholly within Puligny, beside Les Pucelles."}],"traps":["Domaine Leflaive is not Olivier Leflaive, a separate négociant house of the same village.","Chevalier-Montrachet and Bienvenues-Bâtard lie wholly in Puligny; Le Montrachet and Bâtard-Montrachet straddle Puligny and Chassagne.","Les Pucelles is a premier cru, however close it lies to the grands crus."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-36v1y8qj","front":"Domaine Leflaive: where does Chevalier-Montrachet lie in relation to Le Montrachet?","back":"Above it, higher on the slope on thin, stony soil, wholly within Puligny."},{"id":"fl-o2j2bc6g","front":"Who led Domaine Leflaive's turn to biodynamic farming, and in what decade?","back":"Anne-Claude Leflaive, in the 1990s."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-7vm1zgs6","q":"A guest compares Leflaive's Chevalier-Montrachet with its Bâtard-Montrachet. Which description fits the Chevalier?","opts":["Broad and heavy, from lower on the slope","Soft and early, from young replanted vines","Taut and stony, from the upper slope","Rich and toasty, from new barrels alone"],"a":2,"exp":"Chevalier-Montrachet lies above Le Montrachet on thin, stony soil and gives a taut, narrow wine; Bâtard lies lower on the slope and is the broader, heavier of the two.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ouekmvbd","q":"Which member of the Leflaive family led the domaine's conversion to biodynamic farming in the 1990s?","opts":["Her father, who ran it from 1953","A cousin who shared the direction until 1994","Joseph, the naval engineer who founded it","Anne-Claude, the founder's granddaughter"],"a":3,"exp":"Anne-Claude Leflaive shared the direction from 1990, led it alone from 1994 and put the whole domaine under biodynamic farming in 1996; her grandfather founded it and her father ran it before her.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine Leflaive: An emotion, for 4 generations","url":"https://leflaive.fr/en_US/a-emotion-for-4-generations","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bourgogne wines: Chevalier-Montrachet","url":"https://www.bourgogne-wines.com/wine-and-terroir/bourgogne-and-its-appellations/chevalier-montrachet,2458,9253.html?args=Y29tcF9pZD0yMjc4JmFjdGlvbj12aWV3RmljaGUmaWQ9Mjc4Jnw%3D","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Domaine Leflaive","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domaine_Leflaive","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Anne-Claude Leflaive","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne-Claude_Leflaive","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wilson Daniels: Domaine Leflaive Chevalier-Montrachet Grand Cru","url":"https://wilsondaniels.com/wine/chevalier-montrachet-grand-cru","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-aakqfdtx": {"id":"fl-aakqfdtx","key":"comtes-lafon","group":"burgundy-beaune","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine des Comtes Lafon","producerId":"p-dom-comtes-lafon","say":"lah-FOHN, mer-SOH peh-RYAIR","place":"Meursault, Côte de Beaune, Burgundy","founded":"1894","wine":{"name":"Meursault Premier Cru Les Perrières","appellation":"Meursault Premier Cru","classification":"Premier Cru","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone.","sites":"Les Perrières, on the southern side of Meursault towards Puligny, on thin, stony soil over limestone; its name recalls the stone quarries once worked there, many of them since filled and planted.","making":"Fermented with its own yeasts in oak barrels, about a third of them new by the domaine's account, with the malolactic coming late, in spring; the wine then rests on its lees for well over a year, close to two by the domaine's sheets, before bottling at the domaine. The vines have been farmed biodynamically since 1998.","glass":"Lemon, ripe pear and hazelnut over crushed stone and a flinty, smoky note; broad yet taut, with brown butter in the middle and a long line of firm acidity.","ageing":"Generous young, it closes for a few years and then keeps for fifteen to twenty.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle briefly; a wide white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Meursault Premier Cru","means":"Meursault at its premier cru tier; the village has no grand cru."},{"term":"Les Perrières","means":"The climat, named for the stone quarries once worked on it."},{"term":"Domaine des Comtes Lafon","means":"The family domaine of Meursault, named for the papal title of count its founder received in 1918."}]},"table":"Meursault has no grand cru, and Lafon's Meursault Perrières comes from the stony premier cru towards Puligny most often argued for one. Raised in barrel for well over a year, it is broad yet taut, with hazelnut, ripe pear and a flinty edge, and keeps for twenty years.","story":"The founder was no Burgundian: born in the Tarn-et-Garonne in 1864, he was an inspector of the public registry, and in 1894 he married into the Boch family, merchants and growers of Meursault. The domaine grew from his wife's inheritance and from later purchases and exchanges, and came to hold Perrières, Genevrières and Charmes, the walled Clos de la Barre around the family house and a parcel of Le Montrachet. In 1918 the Holy See made him a papal count, in thanks for his refusal, as an inspector, to make the clergy declare their wealth, and the domaine's name carries the title. In 1923, as mayor of Meursault, he revived the Paulée, the feast that closes the harvest, inviting thirty five friends to dine in his vat room. After his death most of the vines were let to sharecroppers, and in 1956 his grandson stepped in to stop the domaine being sold. In the mid 1980s the founder's great grandson took charge, ended the last sharecropping leases by 1993, farmed biodynamically from 1998 and in 1999 bought a second estate in the Mâconnais, the Héritiers du Comte Lafon. Les Perrières, stony ground towards Puligny, is the premier cru most often argued for promotion, and here it gains breadth without losing its line.","others":[{"name":"Meursault Clos de la Barre","note":"The village monopole around the family house, the domaine's own walled clos."},{"name":"Volnay Premier Cru Santenots-du-Milieu","note":"A red from vineyard land inside Meursault, sold under the Volnay name."},{"name":"Le Montrachet","note":"A small parcel of the grand cru, made in small quantity."}],"traps":["Les Santenots lies in Meursault, but its reds are sold as Volnay Santenots and its whites as Meursault.","Héritiers du Comte Lafon is the family's separate Mâconnais estate, not the Meursault domaine.","Meursault has no grand cru: Les Perrières, however highly prized, is a premier cru."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-r9g5hlcs","front":"Domaine des Comtes Lafon: what feast of Meursault did its founder revive in 1923?","back":"The Paulée de Meursault, the feast that closes the harvest."},{"id":"fl-3imyqlmv","front":"Where does Les Perrières lie in Meursault, and where does its name come from?","back":"On the southern side of the village towards Puligny; it is named for the stone once quarried there."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-hd924z9j","q":"A guest asks whether Lafon's Meursault Perrières is a grand cru. What is the precise answer?","opts":["No: Meursault has no grand cru at all","Yes: Perrières was promoted in 1984","Yes: it shares the rank of Le Montrachet","No: it is a village wine of Puligny"],"a":0,"exp":"Meursault has no grand cru, so Les Perrières is a premier cru, often argued for promotion; Le Montrachet is a grand cru of Puligny and Chassagne, and Perrières lies in Meursault.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qjrfnog1","q":"Lafon's vines were long farmed by sharecroppers. What changed after the founder's great grandson took charge in the 1980s?","opts":["The domaine joined the Meursault cooperative","The family took its vineyards back into its own hands","The vines were grubbed up and replanted with red grapes","The domaine was sold to a Beaune merchant"],"a":1,"exp":"From the mid 1980s the family took its vineyards back from sharecropping, the last leases ending by 1993, and later farmed biodynamically; it neither sold the domaine nor joined a cooperative.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine des Comtes Lafon: History","url":"https://www.comtes-lafon.fr/domaine_eng/histo.htm","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine des Comtes Lafon: Vinification","url":"https://www.comtes-lafon.fr/domaine_eng/vinification.htm","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Domaine des Comtes Lafon producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/producer-profiles/domaine-des-comtes-lafon-producer-profile-245913/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinatis: Domaine des Comtes Lafon","url":"https://www.vinatis.com/maisons/domaine-des-comtes-lafon","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Climats of Burgundy: the trail of the Climats and the stone","url":"https://www.climats-bourgogne.com/fichiers/parcours-climats-et-la-pierre-nord-sud_1443111345.pdf","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-v6aqzvus": {"id":"fl-v6aqzvus","key":"coche-dury","group":"burgundy-beaune","region":"burgundy","producer":"Coche-Dury","producerId":"p-coche-dury","say":"kosh dew-REE","place":"Meursault, Côte de Beaune, Burgundy","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Corton-Charlemagne","appellation":"Corton-Charlemagne","classification":"Grand Cru of the Côte de Beaune","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone.","sites":"A small parcel of Corton-Charlemagne, the white grand cru on the high, cool upper slopes of the hill of Corton, which spans Aloxe-Corton, Pernand-Vergelesses and Ladoix-Serrigny.","making":"Fermented and aged in oak barrels, a share of them new, for fifteen months to two years by the merchants' accounts, and left long on its lees, which keeps the wine in the reductive, struck match register the domaine is known for; it is bottled at the domaine without filtration.","glass":"Struck match and flint over lemon oil, white peach and hazelnut skin; tight and cool, with a firm, chalky line and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Austere for its first five to ten years, it then opens towards hazelnut and honey and keeps for twenty years and more.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle so the struck match note can settle; a wide white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Corton-Charlemagne","means":"The white grand cru of the hill of Corton, its own appellation across three communes."},{"term":"Grand Cru","means":"The rank above Premier Cru, which the rules require directly beneath the appellation's name."}]},"table":"Struck match is the mark of Coche-Dury, a Meursault family domaine, and its Corton-Charlemagne carries it from a small parcel of the white grand cru on the hill of Corton. Tight and saline when young, it opens slowly over many years.","story":"The hill of Corton carries the name of Charlemagne (about 747 to 814), who held land there and in 775 gave much of the hill to the abbey of Saint-Andoche at Saulieu. A later legend says white grapes were planted on his part of the slope so that red wine would not stain his beard. The hill's white wine kept his name, and on 31 July 1937 a decree made Corton-Charlemagne a white grand cru spread over three communes, with Corton, the red grand cru, sharing the hill. Coche-Dury goes back to a grower who worked vines in Meursault in the 1920s; his son began to sell wine under the family's own label in 1964, and in the 1970s a marriage in the next generation joined the names Coche and Dury. Its wines, from Bourgogne Blanc to the grand cru, rest long on their lees in barrel, with a deliberate reduction that gives a struck match note over citrus and salt, an approach that spread far beyond Burgundy. The domaine passed to the next generation in 2010. The Corton-Charlemagne comes from a small parcel and is made in very small quantity. Keep it, and when its time comes, keep a decanter beside it.","others":[{"name":"Meursault Premier Cru Les Perrières","note":"The domaine's premier cru from Meursault's stony climat, often argued deserving of grand cru."},{"name":"Meursault Les Rougeots","note":"A village lieu-dit handled with premier cru care."},{"name":"Bourgogne Blanc","note":"The regional white, labelled Bourgogne rather than by village or vineyard."}],"traps":["Meursault has no grand cru; the domaine's grand cru comes from the hill of Corton, not from its own village.","Corton-Charlemagne is the white grand cru of the hill and spans three communes; Corton is the red.","The struck match note of Coche-Dury is a choice of the cellar, reduction, not a fault of the cork."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-impxas5n","front":"Coche-Dury: where is its grand cru white grown, and in which communes?","back":"On the upper slopes of the hill of Corton, in Aloxe-Corton, Pernand-Vergelesses and Ladoix-Serrigny."},{"id":"fl-w0aq69jh","front":"What gives Coche-Dury's whites their struck match note?","back":"A deliberate reduction during long lees ageing in barrel, a choice of the cellar."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-841irk6w","q":"A guest smells struck match on a young Coche-Dury Corton-Charlemagne and asks if the bottle is faulty. What do you say?","opts":["It is volatile acidity from a warm cellar","It is cork taint, so open another","It is new oak toast from heavy charring","It is reduction, the domaine's chosen style"],"a":3,"exp":"Coche-Dury keeps its whites in a reductive, struck match register through long lees ageing; cork taint would smell instead of damp cardboard and flatten the fruit.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-oz2b08uc","q":"In 775 Charlemagne gave much of the hill of Corton away, and the white grand cru carries his name. Who received it?","opts":["The Cistercian monks of Cîteaux","The Hospices de Beaune","The abbey of Saint-Andoche at Saulieu","The Dukes of Burgundy"],"a":2,"exp":"Charlemagne gave the land to the abbey of Saint-Andoche at Saulieu in 775; the Hospices de Beaune date from 1443 and Cîteaux from 1098, centuries later.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Vins de Bourgogne: the rules of the appellation Corton-Charlemagne","url":"https://www.vins-bourgogne.fr/nos-vins-nos-terroirs/la-bourgogne-et-ses-appellations/gallery_files/site/321/19990/20217.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Corton-Charlemagne","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corton-Charlemagne","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: a complete guide to Corton","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/corton-wine-guide-ratings-burgundy-455938/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Domaine Coche-Dury","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domaine_Coche-Dury","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Coche-Dury","url":"https://frw.co.uk/producer/612ec280060000000000066d/coche-dury","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-s5vy71fj": {"id":"fl-s5vy71fj","key":"marquis-dangerville","group":"burgundy-beaune","region":"burgundy","producer":"Domaine Marquis d'Angerville","producerId":"p-dom-marquis-dangerville","say":"dahn-zher-VEEL, kloh day DEWK","place":"Volnay, Côte de Beaune, Burgundy","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Volnay Premier Cru Clos des Ducs","appellation":"Volnay Premier Cru","classification":"Premier Cru, held whole by the domaine as a monopole","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"The Clos des Ducs, a premier cru clos beside the village of Volnay, on very calcareous, stony soil over a marly, clay rich subsoil; the vines are farmed biodynamically.","making":"Fermented with gentle extraction, pressed gently and run by gravity into oak barrels in the cellars beneath the winery, where it ages for about eighteen months; about a fifth of the barrels are new on average across the domaine's Volnay premiers crus, and the malolactic comes in spring.","glass":"Red cherry, raspberry and violet over a cool, stony note; pale, fine and perfumed, with fine, dry tannin and a long, lifted finish.","ageing":"Slow to evolve, it reaches its full richness from about ten years, as the domaine tells it, and keeps well beyond that in a strong vintage.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant only an old bottle, off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Volnay Premier Cru","means":"Volnay at its premier cru tier; the village has no grand cru."},{"term":"Clos des Ducs","means":"The climat, held by the Dukes of Burgundy from the twelfth to the fifteenth century, by the domaine's account."}]},"table":"Held whole by d'Angerville and named for the Dukes of Burgundy who once owned it, Clos des Ducs is a Volnay premier cru beside the village. Pale, fine and perfumed, Volnay in its silken register, it is served a touch below room temperature.","story":"From the twelfth to the fifteenth century, by the domaine's account, the Dukes of Burgundy held the vineyard beside Volnay that carries their name. In 1804 a forebear of the family, then sub-prefect of Autun, bought the Clos des Ducs estate, and in 1888 a later heir left it to his nephew and godson, then fifteen, the future Marquis d'Angerville. Phylloxera had ruined the vines, and from 1906 the Marquis, trained at the École des Beaux-Arts rather than in a cellar, replanted them with fine Pinot Noir. In the 1920s he spoke out against the blending of some Beaune merchants; they stopped buying his crop, and he answered by bottling and selling his own wine at the domaine, among the early growers of the Côte d'Or to do so. His stand fed the appellation laws of the 1930s. His son ran the domaine from his father's death in 1952 until his own in 2003, and the next generation turned the vines to biodynamic farming from 2006 and in 2012 founded Domaine du Pélican in the Jura. Clos des Ducs, which the domaine calls its grand vin, is a Volnay premier cru argued for promotion. In the glass it makes Volnay's case: perfume and line rather than weight.","others":[{"name":"Volnay Premier Cru Taillepieds","note":"The stoniest of the domaine's crus, often argued for promotion."},{"name":"Volnay Premier Cru Champans","note":"A broader premier cru from the middle of the slope."}],"traps":["Volnay has no grand cru: Clos des Ducs is a premier cru monopole.","Domaine du Pélican, the family's Jura estate, is a separate property in Arbois.","Volnay Santenots is grown in Meursault; Clos des Ducs lies in Volnay itself, beside the village."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8ziqkb8b","front":"Domaine Marquis d'Angerville: what did the Marquis of the 1920s fight, and how?","back":"The blending of some Beaune merchants; when they stopped buying his crop, he bottled and sold his own wine at the domaine."},{"id":"fl-6tw9lv6l","front":"Clos des Ducs: what is its rank, and who makes it?","back":"A Volnay premier cru held whole as a monopole by Domaine Marquis d'Angerville."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-fwxrlhgq","q":"A guest asks why d'Angerville's Clos des Ducs carries Premier Cru rather than Grand Cru. What is the answer?","opts":["Monopoles may not be grands crus","Volnay has no grand cru","It lies partly in Meursault","The domaine declassifies it each year"],"a":1,"exp":"Volnay has no grand cru, so its leading climats are premiers crus; Clos de Tart and La Tâche show that a monopole may be a grand cru elsewhere.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-yygf3ps9","q":"In the 1920s the Beaune merchants stopped buying a Marquis d'Angerville's crop. What had he done?","opts":["Spoken out against their blending","Sold the Clos des Ducs to a négociant","Planted Gamay in the Clos des Ducs","Refused to join the Hospices auction"],"a":0,"exp":"He denounced the blending of some Beaune merchants, and when they stopped buying his crop he bottled and sold his own wine, a stand that fed the appellation laws of the 1930s.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine Marquis d'Angerville: Our history","url":"https://www.domainedangerville.fr/notre-histoire/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Marquis d'Angerville: Volnay Premier Cru Clos des Ducs","url":"https://www.domainedangerville.fr/nos-vins-volnay-1er-cru-clos-des-ducs/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Marquis d'Angerville: Our philosophy","url":"https://www.domainedangerville.fr/notre-philosophie/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Domaine Marquis d'Angerville","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/producer/612ec2800600000000000656/domaine-marquis-d-angerville","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Corney and Barrow: Domaine Marquis d'Angerville","url":"https://www.corneyandbarrow.com/domaine-marquis-d-angerville","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9fs2e5xr": {"id":"fl-9fs2e5xr","key":"bouchard","group":"burgundy-beaune","region":"burgundy","producer":"Bouchard Père et Fils","producerId":"p-maison-bouchard","say":"boo-SHAR, veen duh lon-FON zhay-ZEW","place":"Beaune, Côte de Beaune, Burgundy","founded":"1731","wine":{"name":"Beaune Premier Cru Grèves Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus","appellation":"Beaune Premier Cru","classification":"Premier Cru: a parcel of the climat Les Grèves held whole by the house","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus, a parcel in the heart of Les Grèves, the Beaune premier cru on the middle of the slope facing east to south east; the name Grèves recalls its gravelly, almost sandy topsoil over broken limestone, which drains well.","making":"Picked by hand into small crates and sorted by hand, among the house's first plots each year. After a few days of cold soak it ferments with punching down, stays on its skins for eighteen to twenty days and goes to a vertical press, then ages for a year or more in French oak barrels, a share of them new.","glass":"Red cherry, raspberry and wild strawberry over clove, cinnamon and a soft, earthy note; supple and round, with fine tannin and a gentle, perfumed finish.","ageing":"Open and supple young, it ages well for seven to ten years and more by the house's own sheet, and a strong vintage can last for decades.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; no need to decant a young bottle; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Beaune Premier Cru","means":"Beaune at its premier cru tier; the town has no grand cru."},{"term":"Grèves","means":"The climat, a premier cru on the middle of the slope, named for its gravelly soil."},{"term":"Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus","means":"The house's parcel within Grèves, named, the story goes, for a Carmelite nun's devotion to the Infant Jesus."}]},"table":"Once the vineyard of Beaune's Carmelite nuns, Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus is a parcel in the heart of Beaune Grèves that Bouchard has held whole since 1791. Supple and round, with red cherry and a touch of clove, it is a Beaune premier cru that pours well young.","story":"Michel Bouchard (1692 to 1755), a cloth merchant from the Dauphiné, crossed Burgundy on his way to Flanders and, by the house's account, settled in Beaune in 1731; his son became his partner in 1746, and in 1775 the house bought its first vines, in Volnay Les Caillerets. Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus belonged then to the Carmelite nuns of Beaune. One of them, Sister Marguerite du Saint-Sacrement (1619 to 1648), was devoted to the Infant Jesus and, the story goes, foretold that the long childless queen, Anne of Austria (1601 to 1666), would bear a son; when the future Louis XIV was born in 1638, the vineyard took its name from her devotion. The Revolution took it from the convent, and in 1791 the house bought it at the sale of national property. In 1820 the family bought the Château de Beaune, the fifteenth century royal fortress of Louis XI (1423 to 1483), and its bastions became the house's cellars. In 1995 the Henriot family of Champagne took the helm of the house, and three years later took over Domaine William Fèvre in Chablis. The story of the nun and the King is the one to tell as it is poured.","others":[{"name":"Volnay Premier Cru Caillerets Ancienne Cuvée Carnot","note":"From the house's first vines, bought in 1775, and named for the Carnot family who once owned them."},{"name":"Beaune Premier Cru Clos de la Mousse","note":"A Beaune premier cru held whole by the house, its last parcels gathered in 1872."},{"name":"Chevalier-Montrachet La Cabotte","note":"A named parcel within the grand cru Chevalier-Montrachet."}],"traps":["Bouchard Père et Fils is not Bouchard Aîné et Fils, a separate Beaune house.","Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus is a parcel within the premier cru Grèves, not a climat of its own.","Beaune has no grand cru; Grèves is a premier cru, however famous the parcel."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-j8n7w05j","front":"Bouchard Père et Fils: in what year was the house founded, and where are its cellars?","back":"1731; in the bastions of the fifteenth century Château de Beaune, bought in 1820."},{"id":"fl-88hma8rt","front":"Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus: whose devotion gave the parcel its name?","back":"A Carmelite nun of Beaune, Sister Marguerite du Saint-Sacrement, devoted to the Infant Jesus."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-rn0poax3","q":"A guest asks what the word Grèves on Bouchard's Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus tells them. What do you say?","opts":["A grand cru of the hill of Corton","A style of light, early drinking red","The Beaune premier cru the parcel lies in","The cellar where the wine is aged"],"a":2,"exp":"Grèves is the Beaune premier cru, named for its gravel, and Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus is the house's parcel within it; Beaune has no grand cru.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ci0act6d","q":"Bouchard Père et Fils bought Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus at the Revolution. From whom had the land been taken?","opts":["The Hospices de Beaune","The Dukes of Burgundy","The monks of Cîteaux","The Carmelite nuns of Beaune"],"a":3,"exp":"The vineyard was the Carmelite convent's until the Revolution, when church land was sold and the house bought it in 1791; its name recalls a Carmelite's devotion, not the Dukes or Cîteaux.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bouchard Père et Fils: The domaine","url":"https://bouchard-pereetfils.com/en/le-domaine","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bouchard Père et Fils: The Château de Beaune","url":"https://www.bouchard-pereetfils.com/en/nearly-3-centuries-of-history/the-chateau-de-beaune/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bouchard Père et Fils: the climat of Vigne de l'Enfant Jésus","url":"https://www.bouchard-pereetfils.com/jeu-chasse-aux-climats/bienvenue-dans-le-climat-beaune-greves-vignes-de-lenfant-jesus-premier-cru/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bouchard Père et Fils: Beaune Les Grèves","url":"https://bouchard-pereetfils.com/en/iconic/beaune-les-greves","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Encyclopedia.com: Margaret of the Blessed Sacrament","url":"https://encyclopedia.com/religion/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/margaret-blessed-sacrament-ven","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-uily2kyv": {"id":"fl-uily2kyv","key":"lafite-rothschild","group":"bordeaux-left","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Lafite Rothschild","producerId":"p-ch-lafite-rothschild","say":"lah-FEET rot-SHEELD","place":"Pauillac, Haut-Médoc, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Lafite Rothschild","appellation":"Pauillac","classification":"Premier Cru Classé in the 1855 classification of the Médoc","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with Merlot and small parts of Cabernet Franc and Petit Verdot.","sites":"Deep gravel slopes in the north of Pauillac, running to the border with Saint-Estèphe, and the Carruades plateau beside them.","making":"Fermented in vats for about a fortnight at a controlled temperature. Each barrel is then tasted on its own before the blend is made, and the wine is aged in new oak barrels for about fifteen months, by the estate's own account, before it is bottled at the château.","glass":"Cedar, pencil shavings and cassis over violet and graphite, more perfume than power, with fine, firm tannin, fresh acidity and a long, dry finish of cigar box.","ageing":"Closed for its first ten to fifteen years in a strong vintage, it can then hold and develop for half a century.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, and an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Pauillac","means":"The commune appellation, red wine only, between Saint-Julien and Saint-Estèphe."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant in Bordeaux."},{"term":"Rothschild","means":"The family name the estate took after Baron James de Rothschild bought it in 1868."}]},"table":"Château Lafite Rothschild stands first in the 1855 list of the Médoc's first growths: Pauillac, mostly Cabernet Sauvignon, from gravel by the Saint-Estèphe border. Expect cedar, pencil shavings and cassis, more perfume than power, built to age for decades.","story":"Vines were planted at Lafite in the late seventeenth century by the Ségur family, as the estate tells it, and in the eighteenth century Nicolas-Alexandre de Ségur (1697 to 1755), who also held Latour, was remembered as the Prince of the Vines. The story goes that the military governor of Guyenne, a friend of the king, made Lafite the wine of the court at Versailles. Heavy debts forced a later Ségur to sell in 1784, to a kinsman who was guillotined in 1794, and the estate was seized and sold as national property. When the Bordeaux brokers drew up their classification for the Universal Exhibition in Paris in 1855, Lafite stood first among the four first growths. On 8 August 1868 Baron James de Rothschild (1792 to 1868), head of the family's Paris bank, bought it at auction in Paris, against a coalition of Bordeaux merchants as the estate tells it, and he died that November, three months later. The estate took the family's name, and the label as it is known first appeared on the next vintage. The Carruades plateau that adjoins the vineyard gives its name to the second wine. For a sommelier, Lafite is the perfumed, restrained side of Pauillac, the first growth that whispers rather than shouts.","others":[{"name":"Carruades de Lafite","note":"The second wine, named for the Carruades plateau, a group of plots that adjoins the ridge of the grand vin's vineyard."}],"traps":["Carruades de Lafite is the second wine, not the grand vin.","Lafite is in Pauillac, not Saint-Estèphe, though its vines run to the border.","Lafite and Mouton were bought by different branches of the Rothschild family, Mouton in 1853 and Lafite in 1868."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-tlk56si5","front":"Château Lafite Rothschild: who bought it in 1868, and what happened next?","back":"Baron James de Rothschild (1792 to 1868) of the Paris bank, at auction; he died three months later and the estate took the family's name."},{"id":"fl-fnumohvv","front":"Which grape leads Château Lafite Rothschild, and what is its second wine?","back":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority; the second wine is Carruades de Lafite."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-4y0o8uua","q":"A guest wants the second wine of Château Lafite Rothschild. Which bottle do you bring?","opts":["Les Forts de Latour","Carruades de Lafite","Pavillon Rouge","Le Petit Mouton"],"a":1,"exp":"Carruades de Lafite, named for a plateau beside the vineyard, is Lafite's second wine; Les Forts is Latour's, Pavillon Rouge is Margaux's and Le Petit Mouton is Mouton's.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1js7atrp","q":"Which first growth did Baron James de Rothschild buy at auction in August 1868, months before his death?","opts":["Château Lafite","Château Latour","Château Brane-Mouton","Château Margaux"],"a":0,"exp":"He bought Lafite on 8 August 1868 and died that November; Brane-Mouton had been bought by Nathaniel de Rothschild in 1853 and renamed Mouton.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaines Barons de Rothschild: History","url":"https://www.lafite.com/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaines Barons de Rothschild: Read Château Lafite Rothschild","url":"https://www.lafite.com/domaines/chateau-lafite-rothschild/read/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Rothschild Archive: Château Lafite, Pauillac","url":"https://family.rothschildarchive.org/estates/64-chateau-lafite","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: The Prince of Vines","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/the-prince-of-vines-46838","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Your guide to the 1855 classification","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/editorial/your-guide-to-the-1855-classification","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-5h5uaoik": {"id":"fl-5h5uaoik","key":"latour","group":"bordeaux-left","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Latour","producerId":"p-ch-latour","say":"lah-TOOR","place":"Pauillac, Haut-Médoc, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Latour","appellation":"Pauillac","classification":"Premier Cru Classé in the 1855 classification of the Médoc","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, by far the larger part, with Merlot and touches of Cabernet Franc and Petit Verdot.","sites":"L'Enclos, the walled heart of the estate on deep gravel above the Gironde at the southern edge of Pauillac, by the Saint-Julien border; fruit from outside it goes to the second and third wines.","making":"Fermented in temperature controlled stainless steel vats, first installed in 1964, and aged for eighteen months or more in new oak barrels before bottling at the château; the wines are then held at the estate until it judges them ready.","glass":"Cassis, blackberry and cigar box over graphite and an iron, almost bloody note; dense and grippy, with high, firm tannin, fresh acidity and a long, tight finish.","ageing":"Austere for fifteen to twenty years in a strong vintage, it then lasts for decades, and it holds its shape even in weak years.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for two hours or more, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Grand Vin de Château Latour","means":"The first wine, as the label prints it, drawn from L'Enclos."},{"term":"Pauillac","means":"The commune appellation, red wine only, between Saint-Julien and Saint-Estèphe."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant in Bordeaux."}]},"table":"Château Latour is the Pauillac first growth named for the tower that guarded the Gironde in the Hundred Years War. Mostly Cabernet Sauvignon from the walled Enclos, it is dense and firm and needs decades, so decant it well.","story":"A fortified tower stood here in the fourteenth century, guarding the estuary during the Hundred Years War, and it gave the estate its name; the round tower shown on every label was built later, in the seventeenth century. By the early eighteenth century Latour had joined Lafite in the hands of the Ségur family, and in 1855 the Bordeaux brokers ranked it among the four first growths. The Ségur heirs kept it until 1963, when they sold a majority to the British Pearson group, with Harveys of Bristol beside it. The new owners modernised the cellar and in 1964 replaced the old wooden vats with temperature controlled stainless steel, three years after Haut-Brion. Les Forts de Latour, the second wine, followed with the 1966 vintage, and the estate returned to French hands in 1993. Latour is famous for consistency: its deep gravel by the river gives a wine that holds its shape in weak years, and its 1961 is a vintage of history. With the 2012 vintage it left the en primeur system, the sale of wine still in barrel, and has since released its wines when it judges them ready to drink.","others":[{"name":"Les Forts de Latour","note":"The second wine in its modern form, from the 1966 vintage, mostly from parcels outside L'Enclos."},{"name":"Pauillac de Latour","note":"The third wine, labelled simply by its commune."}],"traps":["Les Forts de Latour is the second wine, not the grand vin.","Château Latour in Pauillac is a different estate from Latour-Martillac in Pessac-Léognan and La Tour Carnet in the Haut-Médoc.","Latour left the en primeur system with the 2012 vintage, so its wines reach the market only when the estate releases them."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-jspyhb7a","front":"Château Latour: what is L'Enclos, and what wine does it give?","back":"The walled heart of the estate on deep gravel above the Gironde; it gives the grand vin, while other parcels go to the second and third wines."},{"id":"fl-fd84qv1g","front":"What did Château Latour change in its cellar in 1964?","back":"It replaced its wooden vats with temperature controlled stainless steel, three years after Haut-Brion."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-oothvkd8","q":"A guest asks why Château Latour is known for holding up even in weak vintages. Which reason fits?","opts":["Grapes bought from across the Haut-Médoc","Long ageing in large old casks","Deep gravel by the Gironde in the walled Enclos","A high share of Merlot on cool clay"],"a":2,"exp":"L'Enclos lies on deep, warm gravel beside the estuary, which drains well and ripens Cabernet Sauvignon even in hard years; the blend is mostly Cabernet, not Merlot.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-xdp7bnua","q":"In 1963 a majority of Château Latour was sold to new owners who then modernised its cellar. Who were they?","opts":["The Paris bank of the Rothschild family","A cooperative of Pauillac growers","The Bordeaux merchant house of Ginestet","The British Pearson group, with Harveys of Bristol"],"a":3,"exp":"The Ségur heirs sold a majority to Pearson, with Harveys of Bristol, in 1963; the new owners put in stainless steel vats in 1964.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Latour","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_Latour","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein.plus: Château Latour","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/chateau-latour","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Christie's: An expert guide to Château Latour","url":"https://www.christies.com.cn/features/An-expert-guide-to-Chateau-Latour-9584-1.aspx","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Latour producer page","url":"https://frw.co.uk/producer/612ec2800600000000000f64/latour","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Château Latour 2012 released as first new vintage in eight years","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/chateau-latour-2012-vintage-release-433063","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ak3fnhuo": {"id":"fl-ak3fnhuo","key":"margaux","group":"bordeaux-left","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Margaux","producerId":"p-ch-margaux","say":"mar-GOH","place":"Margaux, Haut-Médoc, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Margaux","appellation":"Margaux","classification":"Premier Cru Classé in the 1855 classification of the Médoc","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the larger part, with Merlot and a little Petit Verdot and Cabernet Franc.","sites":"Gravel croupes, low rises of deep gravel, in the commune of Margaux around the château; a separate parcel of Sauvignon Blanc gives the white.","making":"Fermented in vats, then racked off its lees after the malolactic and put into new French oak barrels in late October or November. It ages there for eighteen months to two years, about twenty-one months on average, on an estate that keeps its own cooperage beside the cellars, before bottling at the château.","glass":"Violet, rose petal and cassis over cedar and cool graphite, with blackberry and a little liquorice; fine grained, silky tannin, bright acidity and a long, lifted finish.","ageing":"Charming earlier than Latour, it still needs ten to fifteen years in a strong vintage and can last half a century.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Premier Grand Cru Classé","means":"The words the estate uses for its first growth rank of 1855."},{"term":"Margaux","means":"Both the estate and the commune appellation, red only; the estate's white is sold as Bordeaux."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant in Bordeaux."}]},"table":"Château Margaux is the first growth of the Margaux appellation, a Cabernet Sauvignon led wine of violets, cassis and silk, made behind a neoclassical château of the 1810s. Its white, Pavillon Blanc, is sold as plain Bordeaux.","story":"In the 1570s the Lestonnac family reshaped the old domain at Margaux around its vineyards, as the estate tells it. In 1787 Thomas Jefferson (1743 to 1826), then the American minister in Paris, toured Bordeaux and named Château Margaux among four wines of the first quality, with Latour, Haut-Brion and Lafite. After the Revolution the estate was sold as national property, and its new owner of 1801 had a Bordeaux architect build the neoclassical château, begun in 1810, whose front appears on the label. In 1855 the brokers ranked Château Margaux a first growth, one of only four reds. The twentieth century was harder: the Ginestet family, who had come to own the whole estate by about 1950, were left in difficulty by the slump of the 1970s and sold it in 1977 to André Mentzelopoulos (1915 to 1980). He drained and replanted the vineyard, with the oenologist Émile Peynaud (1912 to 2004) as adviser, and by his death in 1980 the estate was held to have its old standing back. Its white, Pavillon Blanc, is all Sauvignon Blanc, and since the Margaux appellation is for red wine alone it must be sold as Bordeaux Blanc.","others":[{"name":"Pavillon Rouge du Château Margaux","note":"The second wine, Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot from the estate."},{"name":"Pavillon Blanc du Château Margaux","note":"All Sauvignon Blanc, sold as Bordeaux Blanc because the Margaux appellation is red only."}],"traps":["Pavillon Blanc is appellation Bordeaux Blanc, never appellation Margaux.","Pavillon Rouge is the second wine; Pavillon Blanc is a different wine altogether.","A Margaux on a list is not necessarily the first growth: the appellation holds many estates."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-tnfjliru","front":"Château Margaux's white wine: what is it, and how is it labelled?","back":"Pavillon Blanc du Château Margaux, all Sauvignon Blanc, labelled Bordeaux Blanc because the Margaux appellation admits only red."},{"id":"fl-3r98qi97","front":"Who bought Château Margaux in 1977, and what followed?","back":"André Mentzelopoulos, who drained and replanted the vineyard with Émile Peynaud's advice; by his death in 1980 the estate had its old standing back."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-xjw2kdg1","q":"A guest orders Château Margaux's white wine and asks why the label says Bordeaux rather than Margaux. What do you explain?","opts":["The Margaux appellation covers red wine only","The white is a blend of bought grapes","The 1855 rank forbids a white Margaux","The white grows in the Entre-Deux-Mers"],"a":0,"exp":"Pavillon Blanc grows on the estate, but the Margaux appellation, like every commune appellation of the Médoc, is for red wine, so the white is sold as Bordeaux Blanc.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-a4e9vbct","q":"In 1787 an American diplomat touring Bordeaux named Château Margaux among four wines of the first quality. Who was he?","opts":["John Adams","Thomas Jefferson","Benjamin Franklin","James Monroe"],"a":1,"exp":"Thomas Jefferson, then the American minister in Paris, named Margaux, Latour, Haut-Brion and Lafite in his notes of 1787, the same four estates the brokers ranked first growths in 1855.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château Margaux: The story of Château Margaux","url":"https://chateau-margaux.com/en/le-domaine/histoire","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Margaux: Ageing in barrels","url":"https://www.chateau-margaux.com/en/savoir-faire/le-travail-des-chais/elevage-en-barriques","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Margaux: Grand Vin du Château Margaux","url":"https://chateau-margaux.com/vins/grand-vin-du-chateau-margaux","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Margaux","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_Margaux","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Emile Peynaud, who influenced winemaking around the world, dies at 92","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/emile-peynaud-who-influenced-winemaking-around-the-world-dies-at-92-22076","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2o9nekr8": {"id":"fl-2o9nekr8","key":"haut-brion","group":"bordeaux-left","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Haut-Brion","producerId":"p-ch-haut-brion","say":"oh-bree-OHN","place":"Pessac, Pessac-Léognan, Bordeaux","founded":"1533","wine":{"name":"Château Haut-Brion","appellation":"Pessac-Léognan","classification":"Premier Cru Classé in 1855, and a Cru Classé de Graves for its red in 1959","colour":"red","grapes":"Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon, the lead passing between them with the year, with Cabernet Franc for the rest.","sites":"Gravel mounds in Pessac around the château, long since surrounded by the suburbs of Bordeaux.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel vats, first installed in 1961, the first of the first growths to use them, then aged for eighteen to twenty months in oak barrels, the share of new oak set each year by the vintage, before bottling at the château.","glass":"Tobacco leaf, cigar box and wood smoke over cassis and ripe black cherry, with warm gravel, a little truffle and fine, sweet tannin; savoury, supple and long.","ageing":"Open sooner than the Pauillac first growths, it still rewards fifteen years and can last for more than half a century.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Pessac-Léognan","means":"The appellation of the northern Graves, created in 1987, where Haut-Brion lies."},{"term":"Graves","means":"The older name of the district, printed on bottles from before Pessac-Léognan was carved out of it."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant in Bordeaux."}]},"table":"Château Haut-Brion is the one first growth of 1855 outside the Médoc, from gravel in Pessac long since surrounded by the suburbs of Bordeaux. Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon give tobacco, wood smoke and cassis, more savoury than the Pauillacs.","story":"In 1525 a marriage brought part of the Haut-Brion land to the Pontac family as a bride's dowry; in 1533 the bridegroom bought its manorial rights from a Basque merchant, and in 1549 he began the château. By the 1660s the wine was known in London by the estate's own name: on 10 April 1663 Samuel Pepys (1633 to 1703) wrote in his diary of drinking, at the Royal Oak tavern in Lombard Street, a French wine called Ho Bryan, and in 1666 the family opened a London tavern of its own, Pontack's Head. Thomas Jefferson (1743 to 1826) came on 25 May 1787 and bought its wine, and the statesman Talleyrand (1754 to 1838) owned it briefly in the early nineteenth century. In 1855 the brokers ranked it a first growth beside three Médoc estates, though it lies in the Graves. In 1935 a New York banker bought it, and the second wine, Le Clarence, carries his first name. In 1959 it was classified again, among the red Crus Classés de Graves, and in 1961 it became the first of the first growths to ferment in stainless steel vats. Its white, Haut-Brion Blanc, carries no classification of its own.","others":[{"name":"Le Clarence de Haut-Brion","note":"The second wine, called Bahans Haut-Brion until the 2007 vintage and named for the banker who bought the estate in 1935."},{"name":"Château Haut-Brion Blanc","note":"The dry white of Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc; unlike the red, it carries no Graves classification."}],"traps":["Haut-Brion is in Pessac-Léognan, in the Graves, not the Médoc, despite its rank of 1855.","It is classified twice: as a first growth in 1855 and among the Graves Crus Classés in 1959.","La Mission Haut-Brion, across the road, is a separate estate."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-finvftgr","front":"Château Haut-Brion: why is it the odd one out among the first growths?","back":"It lies in Pessac, in the Graves, the one estate of the 1855 list outside the Médoc."},{"id":"fl-aeix6tkj","front":"Château Haut-Brion appears in a London diary of 1663: whose, and under what name?","back":"Samuel Pepys, on 10 April 1663, who wrote of a French wine called Ho Bryan."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-rrxhn8tm","q":"Blind, a first growth shows tobacco, wood smoke and soft cassis, from a blend that gives Merlot a far larger part than the Médoc first growths do. Which appellation fits?","opts":["Pauillac","Saint-Estèphe","Margaux","Pessac-Léognan"],"a":3,"exp":"Haut-Brion, in Pessac-Léognan, blends Merlot with Cabernet Sauvignon and Cabernet Franc on warm gravel, giving smoke and tobacco; the Pauillac and Margaux first growths are Cabernet led.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-i3u07skk","q":"Which family came to the Haut-Brion land by a marriage of 1525 and opened a tavern in London in 1666?","opts":["The Ségur family","The Lestonnac family","The Pontac family","The Fumel family"],"a":2,"exp":"The Pontacs gained the land by a marriage of 1525, bought its rights in 1533 and opened Pontack's Head in London in 1666; the Fumels held Haut-Brion when Jefferson came in 1787, the Ségurs Lafite and Latour.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Haut-Brion: History","url":"https://www.haut-brion.com/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Haut-Brion: Savoir-faire","url":"https://www.haut-brion.com/en/savoir-faire/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Diary of Samuel Pepys: Friday 10 April 1663","url":"https://pepysdiary.com/diary/1663/04/10","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Haut-Brion","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_Haut-Brion","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Britannica: Charles-Maurice de Talleyrand, prince de Bénévent","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Charles-Maurice-de-Talleyrand-prince-de-Benevent","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-tco8gwds": {"id":"fl-tco8gwds","key":"mouton-rothschild","group":"bordeaux-left","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Mouton Rothschild","producerId":"p-ch-mouton-rothschild","say":"moo-TOHN rot-SHEELD","place":"Pauillac, Haut-Médoc, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Mouton Rothschild","appellation":"Pauillac","classification":"Premier Cru Classé since 1973; a second growth in the 1855 classification","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with Merlot and small parts of Cabernet Franc and Petit Verdot.","sites":"The Mouton plateau of deep gravel in the north of Pauillac, beside Lafite.","making":"Fermented parcel by parcel, mostly in oak vats with some of steel, in a vat room fed by gravity, then aged for about twenty months in new oak barrels before bottling at the château, where the whole crop has been bottled since the 1924 vintage.","glass":"Cassis, blackcurrant leaf and mint over cigar box, roasted coffee and sweet cedar; opulent and dense, with firm, ripe tannin, fresh acidity and a long finish of clove and graphite.","ageing":"Opulent enough to tempt early, it needs fifteen years in a strong vintage and can last for decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for two hours, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"The artist's label","means":"Since 1945 a different artist has made the work at the head of each year's label."},{"term":"Pauillac","means":"The commune appellation, red wine only, between Saint-Julien and Saint-Estèphe."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate, which Mouton did with its whole crop from 1924."}]},"table":"Château Mouton Rothschild is the Pauillac first growth that had to fight for its rank: promoted in 1973, the one promotion in the 1855 list. Cabernet Sauvignon gives cassis, cigar box and coffee, and every vintage since 1945 carries an artist's label.","story":"Baron Nathaniel de Rothschild (1812 to 1870), of the English branch of the family, bought Château Brane-Mouton in 1853, and the estate took the family's name. Two years later the brokers' classification of 1855 placed it among the second growths, a rank the family would not accept. His great grandson, Baron Philippe de Rothschild (1902 to 1988), took charge in 1922, barely twenty, and from the 1924 vintage bottled the whole crop at the château, where until then the wine had been sold in barrel to the Bordeaux merchants; that year's label was drawn by a young poster artist in the Cubist manner. In 1945 he marked the year of victory with a label bearing a V for victory, by a then unknown illustrator, and since then a different artist has made the work at the head of each year's label. The old motto ran: first I cannot be, second I do not deign, Mouton I am. After some twenty years of campaigning, a decree of 1973 made Mouton a first growth, and the motto became: first I am, second I was, Mouton does not change. In 1978 Philippe de Rothschild agreed with a Napa winemaker to make one wine together, Opus One, first made from the 1979 vintage.","others":[{"name":"Le Petit Mouton de Mouton Rothschild","note":"The second wine, largely from younger vines picked earlier and made apart in the estate's oak vats."},{"name":"Aile d'Argent","note":"The dry white of Sauvignon Blanc and Sémillon, sold as Bordeaux Blanc."}],"traps":["The 1973 promotion is the one change of rank in the 1855 red list; no estate has been demoted.","Mouton Cadet is a merchant's brand from the same family, not the estate's second wine.","Mouton was bought in 1853 by the English branch of the Rothschilds; Lafite came to the French branch in 1868."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xduxd19e","front":"Château Mouton Rothschild: what changed in 1973?","back":"A decree promoted it from second growth to first growth, the one promotion in the 1855 list, and its motto changed to first I am, second I was, Mouton does not change."},{"id":"fl-a6zhvjxo","front":"What has Château Mouton Rothschild's label carried each year since 1945?","back":"A work by a different artist, a series begun with the V for victory label marking 1945, the year of victory."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ufh163zx","q":"A guest at a Bordeaux dinner wants the dry white made at Château Mouton Rothschild. Which wine is it?","opts":["Pavillon Blanc","Aile d'Argent","Mouton Cadet Blanc","Le Petit Mouton"],"a":1,"exp":"Aile d'Argent is the estate's dry white, sold as Bordeaux Blanc; Pavillon Blanc is Margaux's, Le Petit Mouton is the second red, and Mouton Cadet is a merchant's brand.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-97ad9e6d","q":"In which year was Château Mouton Rothschild promoted from second growth to first growth by decree?","opts":["1973","1855","1945","1924"],"a":0,"exp":"A decree of 1973 made Mouton a first growth, the one promotion in the 1855 list; 1924 was its first whole château bottling and 1945 the first label of the artist series.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château Mouton Rothschild: History, from Baron Philippe","url":"https://www.chateau-mouton-rothschild.com/the-history/the-history-of-mouton/from-baron-philippe","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Mouton Rothschild: From vine to wine","url":"https://www.chateau-mouton-rothschild.com/the-vineyard/from-vine-to-wine","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Rothschild Archive: Nathaniel de Rothschild","url":"https://family.rothschildarchive.org/people/37-nathaniel-nat-de-rothschild-1812-1870","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Britannica: Baron Philippe de Rothschild","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Baron-Philippe-de-Rothschild","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Château Mouton Rothschild 1945","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-chateau-mouton-rothschild-1945-374280/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-lveaj3oc": {"id":"fl-lveaj3oc","key":"petrus","group":"bordeaux-right","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Pétrus","producerId":"p-petrus","say":"pay-TREWSS","place":"Pomerol, the Right Bank, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Pétrus","appellation":"Pomerol","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Merlot, effectively the whole blend; older vintages could carry a small share of Cabernet Franc.","sites":"One vineyard at the high point of the Pomerol plateau, on a buttonhole of dense, iron rich blue clay, a smectite that swells when wet, holds back water and makes the roots work hard.","making":"The grapes are picked in the afternoons, once the morning dew has dried. The wine is fermented in concrete vats and aged for eighteen to twenty months in oak barrels, about half of them new in most accounts, before it is bottled at the estate.","glass":"Black plum, black cherry and truffle over cocoa and a note of iron, with liquorice and violet; dense and velvety, with ripe, fine tannin and a long, warm, savoury finish.","ageing":"Supple enough to taste young, it then closes and needs fifteen to twenty years in a strong vintage; the great years last half a century.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two and an old one gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Pétrus","means":"The estate's name alone, with no Château before it, under an image of Saint Peter holding the keys of heaven."},{"term":"Pomerol","means":"The appellation of the clay and gravel plateau beside Libourne, which has never been classified."}]},"table":"Almost pure Merlot from a buttonhole of blue clay at the top of the Pomerol plateau, Pétrus carries no classification at all. It is plum, truffle and velvet, and needs years in bottle before it opens.","story":"At the Universal Exhibition in Paris in 1878 a Pomerol wine won a gold medal, an early sign of fame for a commune the brokers of 1855 had passed over: Pétrus, then the property of the Arnaud family; its name is the Latin for Peter. From the mid 1920s Madame Loubat, who kept a hotel in Libourne and died in 1961, bought shares in the estate, and by the 1940s she held all of it and had given it the label that shows Saint Peter holding the keys of heaven. In the same decade she gave the Libourne merchant Jean-Pierre Moueix (1913 to 2003) the sole right to sell the wine, and the 1947 is a vintage of history. When the frost of 1956 killed vines across the Right Bank, she had the estate's vines cut back to grow again from their old roots rather than pulled up and replanted, and so kept their age. In 1964 the Moueix family bought a share from her heirs, and around 1970 a parcel bought from neighbouring Château Gazin joined the vineyard. Pomerol never drew up a ranking, and Pétrus has never needed one: its standing rests on the clay, the Merlot and the care of one family of merchants.","others":[{"name":"La Fleur-Pétrus","note":"A separate estate on the Pomerol plateau, long run by the Moueix house of Libourne; a different wine, not a second wine of Pétrus."}],"traps":["Pétrus holds no classification: Pomerol has never been ranked, so a list that calls it a first growth is wrong.","The label reads Pétrus alone; there is no Château before the name.","Pétrus makes no second wine: La Fleur-Pétrus and Trotanoy are separate Pomerol estates, not lesser bottlings of it."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-lj9enkfg","front":"Pétrus: what is the soil under its vineyard, and why does it matter?","back":"A buttonhole of dense, iron rich blue clay at the top of the Pomerol plateau, a smectite that swells when wet and makes the roots work hard."},{"id":"fl-mwi09b59","front":"Which Libourne merchant gained the sole right to sell Pétrus in the 1940s?","back":"Jean-Pierre Moueix (1913 to 2003), whose family bought a share of the estate from Madame Loubat's heirs in 1964."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-sncvh8q5","q":"A guest asks which grape makes Pétrus. Which answer fits?","opts":["Cabernet Sauvignon first, with Merlot behind","Cabernet Franc and Merlot in near equal parts","About a third each of three grapes","Merlot, effectively the whole blend"],"a":3,"exp":"Pétrus is effectively all Merlot from blue clay; near equal Cabernet Franc and Merlot is Cheval Blanc, and a third each of three grapes is Figeac.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2i005qvh","q":"From the mid 1920s a Libourne hotel keeper bought shares in Pétrus, and by the 1940s she held the whole estate. Who was she?","opts":["Madame Clicquot","Madame Pommery","Madame Loubat","Madame Bollinger"],"a":2,"exp":"Madame Loubat bought her first shares in the 1920s and held Pétrus until her death in 1961; the other three widows ran Champagne houses.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Pétrus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_P%C3%A9trus","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Pétrus wine, behind the legend","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/petrus-wine-behind-the-legend-248076/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Jean-Pierre Moueix dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/jean-pierre-moueix-dies-104188/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Folly Bordeaux guide: Pétrus","url":"https://bordeaux.guides.winefolly.com/wineries/chateau-petrus/wines/petrus/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Pétrus producer page","url":"https://frw.co.uk/producer/612ec280060000000000103b/petrus","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-b3o1q4ag": {"id":"fl-b3o1q4ag","key":"le-pin","group":"bordeaux-right","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Le Pin","producerId":"w-le-pin","say":"luh-PAN","place":"Pomerol, the Right Bank, Bordeaux","founded":"1979","wine":{"name":"Le Pin","appellation":"Pomerol","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Merlot, all or nearly all of it, with a trace of Cabernet Franc in some years.","sites":"A small vineyard of gravel and clay on the Pomerol plateau, beside Vieux Château Certan, around the house where a pine tree gave the estate its name.","making":"Fermented in small stainless steel vats, the wine is run into oak barrels, most or all of them new, for its malolactic fermentation, and stays in them for fourteen to eighteen months before it is bottled at the estate.","glass":"Ripe plum, black cherry and mocha over cream, vanilla and a little truffle; plush and round, with soft, sweet tannin, gentle acidity and a long, rich finish.","ageing":"Open and generous within a few years of the vintage, it keeps its charm for twenty years and more in a strong year.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one only to take it off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Le Pin","means":"The estate's name, from a pine tree that stood beside the house on the plot."},{"term":"Pomerol","means":"The appellation of the plateau north east of Libourne, which has no classification of any kind."}]},"table":"Begun by a Belgian family in 1979, Le Pin is Pomerol Merlot from a few rows of gravel and clay, raised mostly in new oak. Plush and openly ripe, with plum, mocha and cream, it charms far younger than its neighbours.","story":"A pine tree beside a modest house gave Le Pin its name. The Thienpont family, wine merchants from Belgium, had owned Vieux Château Certan on the Pomerol plateau since 1924, and in 1979 members of the family bought this small plot nearby, with its house and a small cellar. The first vintage was made that autumn in those rough surroundings and raised in old barrels from Vieux Château Certan; malolactic fermentation in barrel, begun when there were too few vats, became a habit of the house. The early vintages sold slowly, but the 1982 was praised by critics and made the estate's name. From 1991, when Valandraud in Saint-Émilion made its first vintage, a wave of tiny, dense, new oaked wines from sheds and garages followed; their makers were called garagistes, and Le Pin was named as their forerunner. The estate stayed small and never sought a rank: Pomerol has no classification. In 2011 a new cellar designed by Belgian architects opened on the site. Le Pin stands for the opulent side of Pomerol, the wine to pour when a guest wants pleasure young rather than in twenty years.","others":[{"name":"Vieux Château Certan","note":"The neighbouring estate of the wider Thienpont family since 1924, made apart from Le Pin; a different wine, not its second."}],"traps":["Le Pin has no classification: Pomerol has never been ranked, so its fame rests on the wine alone.","Le Pin and Vieux Château Certan share a family name but are separate estates with separate wines.","Le Pin is Pomerol, not Saint-Émilion, though most of the garage wines it inspired were made in Saint-Émilion."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-kmmsorpt","front":"Le Pin: where does the estate's name come from?","back":"A pine tree beside the small house on the plot the Thienpont family bought in 1979."},{"id":"fl-qrmpppsr","front":"From the 1990s, what name was given to the tiny, dense Right Bank wines that followed Le Pin's lead?","back":"Garage wines, made by garagistes, mostly in Saint-Émilion from Valandraud's first vintage of 1991; Le Pin is named as their forerunner."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-kz9xn4er","q":"A guest asks how Le Pin is raised to taste of mocha and cream. Which answer fits?","opts":["Mostly new oak barrels, malolactic included","Large old casks for three years","Concrete vats, with no oak at all","Clay amphorae buried in the cellar"],"a":0,"exp":"Le Pin goes into oak barrels, most or all of them new, for its malolactic fermentation and ageing; large old casks or bare concrete would give none of that mocha and cream.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rjo0zz49","q":"In what year did members of the Thienpont family buy the small Pomerol plot that became Le Pin?","opts":["1924","1979","1947","1991"],"a":1,"exp":"They bought it in 1979 and made the first vintage that autumn; 1924 is when the family bought Vieux Château Certan nearby, and 1991 is Valandraud's first vintage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Fine and Rare: Le Pin, from the beginning","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/editorial/le-pin-from-the-beginning","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Le Pin 1982","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-le-pin-1982-375061/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein.plus: Château Le Pin","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/chateau-le-pin","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Vieux Château Certan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vieux_Ch%C3%A2teau_Certan","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Bordeaux, calling time on garage wine","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/bordeaux-calling-time-on-garage-wine-247228/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-7k1wmunv": {"id":"fl-7k1wmunv","key":"cheval-blanc","group":"bordeaux-right","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Cheval Blanc","producerId":"p-ch-cheval-blanc","say":"shuh-VAHL BLAHN","place":"Saint-Émilion, on the border with Pomerol, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Cheval Blanc","appellation":"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru","classification":"Premier Grand Cru Classé A from 1955 until it withdrew in 2021","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Franc and Merlot in near equal parts, the Cabernet Franc a little more of the vineyard, with a touch of Cabernet Sauvignon in some years.","sites":"Gravel, clay and sand side by side in the far north west of Saint-Émilion, hard against the Pomerol border, on land bought out of Figeac from 1832.","making":"Each parcel is fermented apart in a concrete vat sized to it, in the cellar that opened among the vines in June 2011. The wine is then aged for sixteen to eighteen months in oak barrels, which the estate replaces every year, and bottled at the château.","glass":"Violet, red plum and raspberry over sweet tobacco, mint and cedar, with truffle as it ages; silky and perfumed, with fine, satin tannin and a long, fresh finish.","ageing":"Charming in its first years, it then closes and opens again after fifteen or so; the great vintages last half a century and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru","means":"The appellation, the stricter of Saint-Émilion's two; it is not a rank in the classification."},{"term":"Premier Grand Cru Classé","means":"The rank printed on older vintages, before the estate left the classification in 2021."}]},"table":"Château Cheval Blanc is the Saint-Émilion estate on the Pomerol border where Cabernet Franc stands beside Merlot in near equal parts on gravel, clay and sand. Expect violets, red plum and silken tannin, perfume before power; its 1947 is a vintage of history.","story":"The land of Cheval Blanc was once part of Château Figeac. In 1832 a judge of Libourne bought the heart of it, and more Figeac land followed over the next two decades. Through his daughter's marriage of 1852 it passed to the Laussac-Fourcaud family, later the Fourcaud-Laussac family, who held it until 1998; how it came by its name is not known for certain. It won a medal at the Universal Exhibition in London in 1862, which its label has carried ever since, and a gold medal at Paris in 1878. The 1947, made after an abnormally hot, dry summer, came out so ripe and dense that it is a vintage of history, talked of for its weight as a wine almost like Port. When Saint-Émilion drew up its first classification in 1955, Cheval Blanc and Ausone were the two estates ranked Premier Grand Cru Classé A, a rank they held alone until 2012. In June 2011 a new cellar of concrete vats, sized to the parcels, opened among the vines. In 2021 Cheval Blanc withdrew from the classification, as Ausone did, objecting to the weight its criteria gave to matters beyond the wine in the glass. When a guest doubts that Cabernet Franc can lead on the Right Bank, Cheval Blanc is the bottle to pour.","others":[{"name":"Le Petit Cheval","note":"The second wine, Cabernet Franc and Merlot from the same vineyard."},{"name":"Cheval des Andes","note":"A Malbec led red of Mendoza, made with Terrazas de los Andes since the 1999 vintage."}],"traps":["Cheval Blanc and Figeac share gravel and a history but are separate estates: Cheval Blanc's land was carved from Figeac.","Cheval Blanc left the Saint-Émilion classification in 2021 and holds no rank in the classification of 2022, though older bottles print Premier Grand Cru Classé.","Cheval Blanc is Saint-Émilion, not Pomerol, though its vines run to the Pomerol border."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-58ttxt97","front":"Château Cheval Blanc: which grape fills about half its vineyard, unusually for the Right Bank?","back":"Cabernet Franc, with Merlot close behind and a little Cabernet Sauvignon."},{"id":"fl-hs857fa9","front":"Which Saint-Émilion estate grew from land carved from Figeac from 1832, and made a wine of history in 1947?","back":"Château Cheval Blanc."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-f5lu74xo","q":"A guest asks why Château Cheval Blanc tastes more perfumed than most Saint-Émilion. Which reason fits?","opts":["Long ageing in large old casks","Merlot alone, on a rise of blue clay","Much Cabernet Franc, on gravel by Pomerol","Cabernet Sauvignon leads, on the limestone côte"],"a":2,"exp":"Cheval Blanc's Cabernet Franc, about half the vineyard, gives violets and silk on gravel; Merlot on blue clay is Pétrus, and the limestone côte below the town is Ausone's ground.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-luqwdpc5","q":"In 2021 Château Cheval Blanc left the Saint-Émilion classification. Which estate also withdrew that year?","opts":["Château Figeac","Pétrus","Château Pavie","Château Ausone"],"a":3,"exp":"Ausone also withdrew in 2021, by its own decision, and Angélus followed early in 2022; Figeac and Pavie became the two A estates of 2022, and Pétrus, in Pomerol, was never classified.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château Cheval Blanc: History","url":"https://www.chateau-cheval-blanc.com/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Cheval Blanc: Ageing and storing","url":"https://www.chateau-cheval-blanc.com/en/ageing-storing/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Cheval Blanc: The vineyard","url":"https://www.chateau-cheval-blanc.com/en/the-vineyard/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Château Cheval Blanc 1947","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-chateau-cheval-blanc-1947-369917/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Cheval Blanc and Ausone to leave the St-Emilion classification","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/cheval-blanc-and-ausone-to-leave-st-emilion-classification-461827/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-y6t2h708": {"id":"fl-y6t2h708","key":"ausone","group":"bordeaux-right","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Ausone","producerId":"p-ch-ausone","say":"oh-ZOHN","place":"Saint-Émilion, on the côte below the town, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Ausone","appellation":"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru","classification":"Premier Grand Cru Classé A from 1955 until it withdrew in 2021","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Franc and Merlot, the Cabernet Franc usually the larger share, from an even split to about two thirds of the blend.","sites":"A south east facing amphitheatre on the limestone côte directly below the town, with cellars in old quarry galleries beneath the château.","making":"Each block is fermented apart in oak vats after a cold soak. The wine then goes into barrels, new or nearly all new, for its malolactic fermentation and some twenty months of ageing in the cellars cut into the rock, before it is bottled at the château.","glass":"Crushed stone, blood orange and red cherry over violet, black tea and clove, cool and tightly wound; firm, fine grained tannin, high acidity and a long, chalky finish.","ageing":"Austere and closed when young, it needs twenty years in a strong vintage and can go on for many decades after.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for two hours, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Château Ausone","means":"Named for the Latin poet Ausonius, whose villa, by local belief, stood on this slope."},{"term":"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru","means":"The appellation on the label, which is not the same thing as a rank in the classification."}]},"table":"Named for the Roman poet Ausonius, Château Ausone grows on the limestone slope beneath the town of Saint-Émilion. Cabernet Franc with Merlot, it is cool, stony and tightly wound, a wine for the long cellar.","story":"Ausonius (about 310 to about 395), the Latin poet of Bordeaux, tutor to the future emperor Gratian and the poet of the vines of the Moselle, gave the estate its name: local belief long held that his villa stood on this slope below the town, though no one has proved it. The vines grow on the limestone côte in an amphitheatre beneath the château, and the cellars run back into old quarry galleries cut into the rock. When Saint-Émilion drew up its first classification in 1955, Ausone and Cheval Blanc were the two estates ranked Premier Grand Cru Classé A, a pair that held that rank alone for more than half a century. For much of that time two families shared the estate, and a long dispute between them, which went to the courts, ended in the mid 1990s, when the Vauthier family bought the other half and took sole charge of vineyard and cellar. Ausone kept its A rank in 2012, when Angélus and Pavie were raised to join it, but in 2021 it withdrew from the classification, as Cheval Blanc did. Ausone is the voice of the limestone: cool, slow and built for the cellar, the opposite pole to Cheval Blanc's gravel.","others":[{"name":"Chapelle d'Ausone","note":"The second wine, Cabernet Franc and Merlot from the same slope."}],"traps":["Ausone is limestone côte and Cheval Blanc is gravel beside Pomerol: the two A estates of 1955 differ in soil and in style.","Ausone, Cheval Blanc and Angélus all withdrew before the 2022 revision, so Ausone holds no rank in the classification of 2022."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-4piiwwn2","front":"Château Ausone: whose name does it carry, and why?","back":"The Latin poet Ausonius of Bordeaux; local belief held that his villa stood on the slope below Saint-Émilion."},{"id":"fl-ncxghd2j","front":"On what ground does Château Ausone grow, and where are its cellars?","back":"The limestone côte directly below the town, with cellars in old quarry galleries cut into the rock."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-zv3scpei","q":"A guest wants a Saint-Émilion grown on the limestone côte below the town rather than on gravel. Which estate fits?","opts":["Le Pin","Château Ausone","Château Figeac","Château Cheval Blanc"],"a":1,"exp":"Ausone's slope is limestone directly below the town; Cheval Blanc and Figeac grow on the gravel by Pomerol, and Le Pin is in Pomerol itself.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-jvkazt40","q":"Saint-Émilion's first classification, of 1955, placed two estates alone in its A rank. Which two?","opts":["Ausone and Cheval Blanc","Pétrus and Le Pin","Figeac and Pavie","Angélus and Pavie"],"a":0,"exp":"Ausone and Cheval Blanc held the A rank alone until 2012, when Angélus and Pavie joined; Figeac and Pavie are the A estates of 2022, and Pomerol is never classified.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Ausone","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_Ausone","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Château Ausone, pillar of society","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/chateau-ausone-pillar-of-society-248692/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Wine Cellar Insider: Château Ausone guide","url":"https://www.thewinecellarinsider.com/bordeaux-wine-producer-profiles/bordeaux/st-emilion/ausone","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Britannica: Decimus Magnus Ausonius","url":"https://www.britannica.com/print/article/43540","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Cheval Blanc and Ausone quit the St Emilion classification","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2021/07/cheval-blanc-and-ausone-quit-st-emilion-classification-system/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-lk53ev8k": {"id":"fl-lk53ev8k","key":"figeac","group":"bordeaux-right","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Figeac","producerId":"p-ch-figeac","say":"fee-ZHAHK","place":"Saint-Émilion, on the gravel by the Pomerol border, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Figeac","appellation":"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru","classification":"Premier Grand Cru Classé A in the 2022 Saint-Émilion classification","colour":"red","grapes":"Roughly a third each of Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc and Merlot, a share of Cabernet Sauvignon rare on the Right Bank.","sites":"Three rises of deep, ancient gravel with flint and quartz over blue clay, in the north west of Saint-Émilion beside Cheval Blanc and the Pomerol border; the gravel ripens both Cabernets.","making":"Each parcel is fermented apart in steel or oak vats, and the malolactic fermentation takes place in barrel. The wine is then aged for fifteen to eighteen months in new oak barrels before it is bottled at the château.","glass":"Blackcurrant leaf, cedar and graphite over red cherry, tobacco and a little mint, cooler and more structured than most Saint-Émilion; firm, fine tannin, fresh acidity and a long, dry finish.","ageing":"Reserved in youth, it opens after ten to fifteen years, and the strong vintages hold for decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two and an old one off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Premier Grand Cru Classé","means":"The rank on the label; in 2022 Figeac was placed in its upper group, A."},{"term":"Saint-Émilion Grand Cru","means":"The appellation, a stricter tier than plain Saint-Émilion and separate from the classification."}]},"table":"With about a third of Cabernet Sauvignon on gravel beside Cheval Blanc, Château Figeac tastes closer to the Médoc than its neighbours do: blackcurrant leaf, cedar and graphite. In 2022 it was raised to Premier Grand Cru Classé A.","story":"The estate carries, the story goes, the name of a Gallo-Roman landowner, Figeacus, whose villa stood here in the second century. In the eighteenth century Figeac reached across much of the north west of Saint-Émilion, and in the nineteenth its owners sold land, among it, from 1832, the parcels that became Cheval Blanc. In 1892 the grandparents of Thierry Manoncourt (1917 to 2010) bought the estate, and he took charge of it in 1947, as the estate tells it. He widened the plantings of Cabernet Sauvignon and Cabernet Franc on the warm gravel and set the blend at about a third of each grape, so that Figeac grew into the Right Bank's Cabernet estate, closer in taste to the Médoc than to its Merlot neighbours. In 1955 it was ranked Premier Grand Cru Classé in Saint-Émilion's first classification, in the group below Ausone and Cheval Blanc, and it stayed in that group through every revision for more than sixty years. In 2022, with Ausone, Cheval Blanc and Angélus gone from the list, Figeac was promoted to A beside Pavie. Offer it to a guest who loves Pauillac: three grapes in near equal parts, cedar before plum.","others":[{"name":"Petit-Figeac","note":"The second wine since the 2012 vintage; until then it was called La Grange Neuve de Figeac."}],"traps":["Figeac and Cheval Blanc share gravel and a history but are different estates: Cheval Blanc's land was carved from Figeac.","Figeac's blend carries about a third of Cabernet Sauvignon, so do not sell it as a Merlot wine.","Figeac was raised to A only in 2022, when Pavie, A since 2012, was the one other estate in the rank."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-bc13guwk","front":"Château Figeac: what sets its blend apart on the Right Bank?","back":"Roughly a third each of Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc and Merlot, an unusual share of Cabernet Sauvignon for Saint-Émilion."},{"id":"fl-b5ekcl9q","front":"Which Saint-Émilion estate was promoted to Premier Grand Cru Classé A in 2022, alongside Pavie?","back":"Château Figeac."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-rvzqcq4u","q":"A guest who drinks Pauillac wants a Saint-Émilion in a similar vein. Why does Château Figeac suit?","opts":["It is all Merlot from a rise of blue clay","It grows on the limestone côte below the town","It is aged for years in concrete vats","About a third of its blend is Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":3,"exp":"Figeac's gravel carries about a third of Cabernet Sauvignon, giving cedar and graphite; blue clay Merlot is Pétrus, and the côte below the town is Ausone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-y71og7oa","q":"Château Figeac once covered far more ground. Which neighbour grew from land carved from it from 1832?","opts":["Château Angélus","Château Pavie","Château Cheval Blanc","Château Ausone"],"a":2,"exp":"Figeac's owners sold the vineyard that became Cheval Blanc from 1832; Ausone and Pavie lie on the limestone côte below the town.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château Figeac: The Builders","url":"https://www.chateau-figeac.com/en/the-builders/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Figeac: The Place","url":"https://www.chateau-figeac.com/en/the-place/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Thierry Manoncourt","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thierry_Manoncourt","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: St-Emilion 2022 classification promotes Château Figeac","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/st-emilion-2022-classification-promotes-chateau-figeac-to-premier-grand-cru-classe-a-487144/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Berry Bros and Rudd: The St-Emilion classification","url":"https://www.bbr.com/wine-knowledge/st-emilion-classification","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-1xfeu2na": {"id":"fl-1xfeu2na","key":"yquem","group":"bordeaux-sauternes","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château d'Yquem","producerId":"p-ch-dyquem","say":"shah-TOH dee-KEM","place":"Sauternes, on the left bank of the Garonne, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château d'Yquem","appellation":"Sauternes","classification":"Premier Cru Supérieur in the 1855 classification, a rank held by no other estate","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Sémillon, about three quarters, with Sauvignon Blanc for the rest.","sites":"One hill above the village of Sauternes, its soils varying from plot to plot, sandy in some and clay in others, near where the cold Ciron meets the warmer Garonne and autumn mists bring the noble rot.","making":"Pickers pass through the vines in successive rounds over several weeks, taking only berries shrivelled by noble rot. Each day's pick is pressed and fermented in new oak barrels; the barrels kept for Yquem then age for about twenty months in the estate's cellar before the blend is bottled at the château. In a year judged too poor, nothing is released under the name.","glass":"Apricot, saffron, barley sugar and orange marmalade over pineapple, vanilla and crème brûlée; luscious and deep, yet carried by fresh acidity to a long finish of honey and bitter orange peel.","ageing":"Delicious from release, it deepens to amber over decades and can last a century in a strong vintage.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, an old bottle a degree or two warmer; no need to decant a young bottle; a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Sauternes","means":"The appellation of five communes on the Ciron; by law its wine is sweet."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant in Bordeaux."}]},"table":"Ranked alone above every other Sauternes and Barsac in 1855, Château d'Yquem is mostly Sémillon, picked berry by berry over many passes as noble rot takes hold. It tastes of apricot, saffron and marmalade, and can last a century.","story":"In 1593 a member of the Sauvage family, local nobles, was given the feudal tenure of Yquem, as the estate tells it, and in 1711 the family became its full owners. In 1785 the Sauvage heiress married into the Lur-Saluces family; widowed three years later, she ran the estate herself. In December 1787 Thomas Jefferson (1743 to 1826), the American minister in Paris, ordered Yquem for himself, and in 1790 he ordered thirty dozen bottles more from the estate for President George Washington (1732 to 1799). In 1855 the Bordeaux brokers set Yquem alone as Premier Cru Supérieur, above the Premiers Crus of Sauternes and Barsac, a rank made for it and never shared. The family kept its discipline: many passes through the vines at each harvest, and no wine at all under the name in years judged too poor, nine of them in the twentieth century, from 1910 to 1992. In 1959 it added a dry white, Y, sold as Bordeaux. In 1996 relatives sold a majority to the luxury group LVMH, which took control in 1999 after a court battle with the head of the family, and in 2012 Yquem again declined a vintage. For a sommelier it is the clearest lesson in what Sauternes asks: noble rot, patience and the nerve to sell nothing in a poor year.","others":[{"name":"Y du Château d'Yquem","note":"The dry white of Sauvignon Blanc and Sémillon, first made in 1959 and sold as Bordeaux, never as Sauternes; a different wine, not a second wine."}],"traps":["Premier Cru Supérieur is Yquem's alone; the eleven estates below it are Premiers Crus.","Y is a dry wine sold as Bordeaux, not Sauternes, and it is not a second wine of Yquem.","Yquem releases nothing under its name in a year it judges too poor, so a missing vintage is a decision, not a lost bottle."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-unh5xnuh","front":"Château d'Yquem: what rank did the 1855 classification give it?","back":"Premier Cru Supérieur, a rank made for it alone, above the Premiers Crus of Sauternes and Barsac."},{"id":"fl-k37ezldd","front":"In which years did Château d'Yquem release no wine under its name?","back":"Years judged too poor, among them 1964, 1972, 1974, 1992 and 2012."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-fuyoixdk","q":"A guest asks how Château d'Yquem gathers grapes for its sweet wine. Which answer fits?","opts":["One early pick of sound, ripe bunches","Many passes through the vines for nobly rotten berries","Grapes dried on straw mats after harvest","Frozen grapes picked on a winter night"],"a":1,"exp":"Pickers pass through Yquem's vines many times, taking only berries shrivelled by noble rot; frozen grapes make ice wine and straw mats make vin de paille.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qoaoapx8","q":"From a marriage of 1785 until LVMH took control in the 1990s, which family held Château d'Yquem?","opts":["The Lur-Saluces family","The Sauvage family","The Rothschild family","The Ségur family"],"a":0,"exp":"The Sauvage heiress married into the Lur-Saluces family in 1785, and they held Yquem until relatives sold a majority to LVMH in 1996; the Sauvages held it before them.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château d'Yquem: The history of Yquem","url":"https://yquem.fr/fr-en/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château d'Yquem: the 2012 vintage","url":"https://www.yquem.fr/int-en/millesimes/yquem/2012","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Founders Online: Thomas Jefferson to Joseph Fenwick, 6 September 1790","url":"https://founders.archives.gov/documents/Jefferson/01-17-02-0155","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Château d'Yquem","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_d%27Yquem","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Battle over Château d'Yquem ends with LVMH as new owner","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/battle-over-chateau-dyquem-ends-with-lvmh-as-new-owner-8197","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9k54c7xq": {"id":"fl-9k54c7xq","key":"climens","group":"bordeaux-sauternes","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Climens","producerId":"p-ch-climens","say":"klee-MAHNSS","place":"Barsac, in the Sauternes district, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Climens","appellation":"Barsac","classification":"Premier Cru in the 1855 classification of Sauternes and Barsac","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Sémillon alone, with no Sauvignon Blanc at all.","sites":"One block on the Barsac plateau: red, iron rich clay and sand over a shelf of fissured limestone.","making":"Picked in several passes for nobly rotten berries, each pass and each parcel is fermented apart in oak barrels, about a third of them new. The lots are raised in barrel for twenty months to two years and kept apart until a late blend, before bottling at the château.","glass":"Lemon curd, quince and candied ginger over acacia honey, white peach and a little saffron; sweet but airy, with bright acidity running through it and a long, clean finish.","ageing":"Open and fresh in youth, it gains honey and dried apricot over twenty years and keeps for decades after.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Barsac","means":"The commune's own appellation; a Barsac estate may print Barsac or Sauternes, and Climens prints Barsac."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant."}]},"table":"Made from Sémillon alone on red sand and clay over limestone, Château Climens is the Barsac Premier Cru its admirers call the Lord of Barsac. Sweet yet light on its feet, it tastes of lemon curd, quince and candied ginger.","story":"Climens lies on the Barsac plateau, where the cold Ciron meets the warmer Garonne and the autumn mists that follow bring noble rot to the grapes. Barsac's soils are thin over limestone, and its wines are known for tension rather than weight. In 1855 the Bordeaux brokers ranked Climens among the Premiers Crus of Sauternes and Barsac, beneath Yquem alone. The Gounouilhou family of Bordeaux held it for decades after, until 1971, when it was bought by another Bordeaux family that already held growths of the Médoc. From 1992 a daughter of that family ran it. She kept the vineyard to Sémillon alone, picked in many passes, and kept each pass and each parcel apart in barrel until a late blend, and in the 2010s she turned the farming to biodynamics. In 2022 the family sold a majority of the estate to new owners. Through each change of hands Climens has held to one idea: that Barsac's limestone and Sémillon alone can make a sweet wine that feels weightless. Its lesson for a candidate is simple: Sauvignon Blanc is allowed in Sauternes and Barsac, never required.","others":[{"name":"Cyprès de Climens","note":"The second wine, Sémillon alone from the same vineyard."}],"traps":["A Barsac estate may label its wine Barsac or Sauternes; an estate elsewhere in Sauternes may not use Barsac.","Climens is Sémillon alone: do not describe it as a Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc blend."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-gjrv62p3","front":"Château Climens: what grape, and on what soil?","back":"Sémillon alone, on red, iron rich clay and sand over the fissured limestone of the Barsac plateau."},{"id":"fl-5m7adswj","front":"What two names may a Barsac estate such as Climens print on its label?","back":"Barsac or Sauternes; an estate in the other four communes may use only Sauternes."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-nhm5uqxh","q":"A guest asks how much Sauvignon Blanc goes into Château Climens. What do you answer?","opts":["Half, with Sémillon for the rest","About a quarter, as at Yquem","Most of it, with a little Muscadelle","None: it is Sémillon alone"],"a":3,"exp":"Climens is planted to Sémillon alone, which shows that Sauvignon Blanc is allowed in Sauternes and Barsac but never required; a quarter is close to Yquem's share.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-vfigxy5b","q":"In 1971 Château Climens was bought by owners who held it for half a century. Who were they?","opts":["The Médeville family of Preignac","The luxury group LVMH","The Lurton family of Bordeaux","The insurance group AXA"],"a":2,"exp":"The Lurton family, owners of several Médoc growths, bought Climens in 1971 and sold a majority in 2022; AXA bought Suduiraut in 1992, and LVMH took Yquem in the 1990s.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Union des Grands Crus de Bordeaux: Château Climens","url":"https://www.ugcb.net/en/node/362","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Climens","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_Climens","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine and Spirits Magazine: Two iconic wineries change hands","url":"https://www.wineandspiritsmagazine.com/?p=44422","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Bordeaux profile, Château Climens","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2021/02/bordeaux-profile-chateau-climens/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Berry Bros. and Rudd: 2009 Château Climens, Barsac","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-952342-2009-ch-climens-barsac","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3kaq7k2j": {"id":"fl-3kaq7k2j","key":"coutet","group":"bordeaux-sauternes","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Coutet","producerId":"p-ch-coutet","say":"koo-TAY","place":"Barsac, in the Sauternes district, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Coutet","appellation":"Barsac","classification":"Premier Cru in the 1855 classification of Sauternes and Barsac","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Sémillon, the great majority, with Sauvignon Blanc and a little Muscadelle.","sites":"One block around the château and its medieval tower on the Barsac plateau, over the limestone that runs close beneath the commune.","making":"Picked in several passes for nobly rotten berries, it is raised for about eighteen months in oak barrels, a share of them new, before bottling at the château. In rare years the most concentrated Sémillon of the two oldest parcels is picked on a day of its own and bottled apart as Cuvée Madame.","glass":"Lime, grapefruit and acacia over apricot, honeysuckle and a little beeswax; sweet but taut, with lively acidity and a long, clean finish of candied lemon.","ageing":"Bright and appealing in its first five years, it gains honey and marmalade with age and keeps for decades in a strong vintage.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; serve it straight from the bottle, with no decanting; a small tulip glass.","label":[{"term":"Barsac","means":"The commune appellation Coutet uses; Barsac wine may also be sold as Sauternes."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant."}]},"table":"Around a medieval tower on the Barsac plateau grows Château Coutet, mostly Sémillon with Sauvignon Blanc and a little Muscadelle. Sweet but taut, lime and apricot over limestone, it bottles a richer selection, Cuvée Madame, only in rare years.","story":"Coutet began as a fortress while Aquitaine belonged to the English crown, in the thirteenth century by the estate's account, and its square, battlemented tower still stands in the courtyard. Confiscated by the state in 1794, it later passed by inheritance to a Lur-Saluces, of the family that held Yquem. In 1855 the Bordeaux brokers ranked Coutet a Premier Cru of Sauternes and Barsac, beside Climens, the two Barsac estates of that rank. The Lur-Saluces kept it until the early 1920s, when it passed to the family of a Lyon industrialist, and a lady of that family owned it until 1977. In her honour, as the estate tells it, the vineyard workers gave her a day's work at harvest, picking the most concentrated grapes of the two oldest Sémillon parcels, and from 1943 that pick became Cuvée Madame, made only in rare years. In 1977 the Baly family bought Coutet and kept the tradition. Coutet makes the case for Barsac's own appellation: sweet wine with the tension of limestone, less broad than the Sauternes of the hills, and the right of a Barsac estate to print Sauternes instead if it wishes.","others":[{"name":"Cuvée Madame","note":"A rare selection of the most concentrated Sémillon of the two oldest parcels, first made in 1943 and only in rare years since."},{"name":"La Chartreuse de Coutet","note":"The second wine, sweet like the grand vin."},{"name":"Opalie de Château Coutet","note":"A dry white of Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc, sold as Bordeaux."}],"traps":["Barsac is one of the five communes entitled to the Sauternes appellation, and it also has its own.","Cuvée Madame is not made every year, and it is a separate selection, not the estate's standard wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-wjm3mzib","front":"Château Coutet: what is Cuvée Madame, and whom does it honour?","back":"The most concentrated Sémillon of its two oldest parcels, first made in 1943 and only in rare years, named for the lady of the house who owned Coutet until 1977."},{"id":"fl-6vcxp0x6","front":"Which Barsac Premier Cru began as a medieval fortress whose square tower still stands in its courtyard?","back":"Château Coutet."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-9prhoakl","q":"A guest wants a Barsac Premier Cru whose blend holds Sauvignon Blanc and a little Muscadelle beside Sémillon. Which do you pour?","opts":["Château Coutet","Château Gilette","Château d'Yquem","Château Climens"],"a":0,"exp":"Coutet blends Sémillon with Sauvignon Blanc and a little Muscadelle; Climens is Sémillon alone, and Yquem and Gilette are not in Barsac.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-3h0al4hd","q":"In 1977 a new family bought the Barsac Premier Cru Château Coutet. Which family?","opts":["The Lurton family","The Baly family","The Moueix family","The Médeville family"],"a":1,"exp":"The Baly family bought Coutet in 1977; the Lurtons bought Climens in 1971, the Médevilles are Gilette's family and the Moueix are Libourne merchants.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château Coutet: History","url":"https://www.chateaucoutet.com/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Coutet: Cuvée Madame de Château Coutet 1995","url":"https://www.chateaucoutet.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/1995_Cuvee_Madame_EN.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Coutet","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_Coutet","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Hachette Vins: Château Coutet","url":"https://www.hachette-vins.com/guide-vins/producteurs/2709/ch-coutet/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Millésima: Château Coutet 2025","url":"https://www.millesima-usa.com/chateau-coutet-2025.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-tvp17qb7": {"id":"fl-tvp17qb7","key":"suduiraut","group":"bordeaux-sauternes","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Suduiraut","producerId":"p-ch-suduiraut","say":"sew-dwee-ROH","place":"Preignac, in the Sauternes district, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Suduiraut","appellation":"Sauternes","classification":"Premier Cru in the 1855 classification of Sauternes and Barsac","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Sémillon, the great majority, with Sauvignon for the rest; the estate grows Sauvignon Blanc and a little Sauvignon Gris.","sites":"A broad estate in Preignac whose vines run to the edge of Yquem's, on gravelly, sandy clay where the Ciron's mists bring noble rot in most autumns.","making":"Picked in many passes for nobly rotten berries, the must is fermented for two to three weeks in French oak barrels, half of them new. The wine is then raised for sixteen to twenty months in barrels half new and half used once, as the estate's sheets give it, before bottling at the château.","glass":"Candied apricot, roasted pineapple and honey over saffron, vanilla and orange peel; rich and broad, with an oily texture held up by acidity and a long, sweet finish of marmalade.","ageing":"Rich enough to enjoy young, it turns to amber, marmalade and toffee over twenty to thirty years and lasts far longer in a strong vintage.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; decanting is not needed; a white wine glass, filled no more than a third.","label":[{"term":"Sauternes","means":"The appellation of five communes on the Ciron, Preignac among them; its wine is sweet by law."},{"term":"Mis en bouteille au château","means":"Bottled at the estate rather than by a merchant."}]},"table":"Pour Château Suduiraut for the rich side of Sauternes: Sémillon led, from Preignac vines that run to the edge of Yquem's. Expect candied apricot, roasted pineapple and honey on an oily texture, beside foie gras or a blue cheese.","story":"In 1580 a marriage brought this Preignac estate to the Suduiraut family, whose name it carries. During the Fronde, the civil wars of 1648 to 1653, the old manor was plundered and burnt, and in 1670 a grandson of that marriage built a new château, framed by formal gardens in the French manner attributed to André Le Nôtre (1613 to 1700), the gardener of Versailles, or to his workshop. In the eighteenth century it passed to a nephew, and for a time, by the estate's account, its wine was sold as Cru du Roy. The vineyard runs to the border of Yquem, and the Ciron's mists that settle there bring noble rot in most autumns. In 1855 the Bordeaux brokers ranked Suduiraut a Premier Cru of Sauternes and Barsac. It stayed in private hands until 1992, when it was bought by AXA Millésimes, the wine estates of the French insurance group, whose first purchase had been Pichon Baron in Pauillac in 1987. The new owners kept the house style, broad Sémillon picked late in many passes, and from 2004 made a dry white of the old vines, S de Suduiraut, sold as Bordeaux. Set beside Climens or Coutet, Suduiraut gives the other face of the district: Preignac's breadth against the tension of Barsac across the Ciron.","others":[{"name":"Castelnau de Suduiraut","note":"The second wine, Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc from the same estate."},{"name":"S de Suduiraut","note":"A dry white of Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc from the estate's old vines, made since 2004 by its own account and sold as Bordeaux."}],"traps":["The five Sauternes communes are Sauternes, Barsac, Bommes, Fargues and Preignac; Suduiraut is in Preignac.","Suduiraut is a Premier Cru, not a Premier Cru Supérieur: that rank is Yquem's alone.","S de Suduiraut is dry and sold as Bordeaux; it is not the estate's sweet wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-psvhl8i7","front":"Château Suduiraut: which famous neighbour do its vines border?","back":"Château d'Yquem."},{"id":"fl-39cz2nq3","front":"What did a grandson of the Suduiraut family build at Preignac in 1670, after the Fronde?","back":"A new château, framed by formal gardens attributed to André Le Nôtre or his workshop."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-5prrwfd0","q":"A guest wants the rich, broad side of Sauternes rather than the tension of Barsac. Which Premier Cru fits?","opts":["Château Climens","Y du Château d'Yquem","Château Coutet","Château Suduiraut"],"a":3,"exp":"Suduiraut, in Preignac, is rich and oily, apricot and pineapple; Climens and Coutet are the taut Barsac pair, and Y is a dry wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-jmr83201","q":"In 1992 Château Suduiraut was bought by the wine estates of a French group. Which group?","opts":["The luxury group LVMH","The bank of the Rothschild family","The insurance group AXA","The Libourne house of Moueix"],"a":2,"exp":"AXA Millésimes bought Suduiraut in 1992, five years after Pichon Baron; LVMH took Yquem in the 1990s.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château Suduiraut: History","url":"https://www.suduiraut.com/en/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Suduiraut: Historical timeline","url":"https://www.suduiraut.com/f/fichier_attache/historical-timeline.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Suduiraut: An exceptional terroir","url":"https://www.suduiraut.com/en/terroir/an-exceptional-terroir","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Suduiraut: 2024 technical sheet","url":"https://www.bordeaux-tradition.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/02/ft_chateau-suduiraut-2024_ft-en-chateau-suduiraut-2024-premier-cru-classe-en-1855-sauternes.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"BKWine: Wines from AXA Millésimes in France, Portugal and Hungary","url":"https://www.bkwine.com/features/top-wines-axa-millesime-france-portugal-hungary-britt-forbes/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-cz4ied47": {"id":"fl-cz4ied47","key":"gilette","group":"bordeaux-sauternes","region":"bordeaux","producer":"Château Gilette","producerId":"p-ch-gilette","say":"zhee-LET","place":"Preignac, in the Sauternes district, Bordeaux","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Château Gilette Crème de Tête","appellation":"Sauternes","classification":"","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Mainly Sémillon, with a little Sauvignon Blanc and Muscadelle.","sites":"A small vineyard in the commune of Preignac, picked in passes for nobly rotten berries like the classed growths around it.","making":"Fermented without oak, the wine is moved into concrete vats and kept there for about fifteen to twenty years, sometimes longer, before it is bottled, so that it reaches the market already mature.","glass":"Crème brûlée, dried apricot and orange marmalade over toffee, quince paste and a little coffee; deep, mellow and long, with a freshness that belies its years in vat.","ageing":"Released already mature, decades after the harvest, it is ready to drink and holds for many years more in bottle.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, an old bottle a degree or two warmer; decant gently only if a crust has formed; a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Crème de Tête","means":"The name the estate gives its leading selection of sweet wine."},{"term":"Sauternes","means":"The appellation; Gilette was not ranked in 1855 and prints no classification."}]},"table":"Kept for many years in concrete vats and never in oak, Château Gilette's Crème de Tête reaches the list already mature. The small Preignac estate stood outside the 1855 list, and its wine tastes of crème brûlée and dried apricot, fresh even after decades.","story":"When the Bordeaux brokers ranked the sweet wines in 1855, Gilette, in Preignac, one of the five communes of Sauternes, was not among the estates listed, and since that list has never been reopened it has stayed outside it. The decree of 30 September 1936 that first recognised the Sauternes appellation gave it the same name as Yquem, and what has made Gilette known is not rank but time. For generations the estate has been in the hands of the Médeville family, growers of Preignac. In the 1930s, as the family tells it, the Depression left Bordeaux in a slump, and the family built concrete vats in place of oak barrels. The wine aged there more slowly than in wood, and the family has kept its sweet wine in concrete ever since, often for fifteen to twenty years before bottling, so that a Gilette reaches a list already decades old. Its leading selection is bottled as Crème de Tête. The result is a Sauternes aged without oak: deep and mellow, yet with a freshness that surprises a guest who reads the vintage. For a sommelier it is the rare chance to offer mature Sauternes without a cellar of one's own.","others":[{"name":"Château Les Justices","note":"The Médeville family's neighbouring Sauternes estate in Preignac; a separate wine, not a second wine of Gilette."}],"traps":["Gilette was not ranked in 1855; it is not a Premier Cru, whatever its reputation.","A Gilette on release is already decades old, so an old vintage on the list is no sign of a forgotten bottle.","Crème de Tête is aged in concrete vats, not in oak barrels."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-6d89dxx3","front":"Château Gilette Crème de Tête: in what vessels is it aged, and for how long?","back":"Concrete vats, never oak, for about fifteen to twenty years before it is bottled and released."},{"id":"fl-fvw88rr1","front":"Was Château Gilette ranked in the 1855 classification of Sauternes and Barsac?","back":"No: it stood outside the list in 1855 and has stayed outside it, since the list has never been reopened."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-0doyjox9","q":"A guest is surprised that a Gilette Crème de Tête on the list is already decades old. What do you explain?","opts":["It is held for years in concrete vats before release","It is a solera blend of many vintages","It was declassified and sold off late","It was a forgotten bottle found in an old cellar"],"a":0,"exp":"Gilette ages its Crème de Tête for fifteen to twenty years in concrete vats and releases it mature; it is a single vintage, not a solera.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rqyp14dk","q":"Château Gilette was left unranked in 1855. What gave it the Sauternes name it shares with Yquem?","opts":["A royal charter of 1593","The appellation decree of 1936","The classification of 1855","The Saint-Émilion list of 1955"],"a":1,"exp":"The decree of 30 September 1936 first recognised the Sauternes appellation; the list of 1855 ranked estates but left Gilette out.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"INAO: Cahier des charges de l'appellation Sauternes","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/2.1-CDC-Sauternes.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinfolio: Château Gilette editorial","url":"https://www.vinfolio.com/editorial/chateau-gilette-the-worlds-most-unique-sauternes","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Martine's Wines: Château Gilette","url":"https://www.mwines.com/?p=5229","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Château Gilette, the Sauternes aged twenty years before bottling","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/chateau-gilette-sauternes-wines-418842/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein.plus glossary: Château Gilette","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/chateau-gilette","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-rl3y2p3k": {"id":"fl-rl3y2p3k","key":"guigal","group":"rhone-north","region":"rhone","producer":"E. Guigal","producerId":"p-guigal","say":"gee-GAHL, lah moo-LEEN","place":"Ampuis, Côte-Rôtie, Rhône","founded":"1946","wine":{"name":"Côte-Rôtie La Mouline","appellation":"Côte-Rôtie","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Syrah, the great majority, with about a tenth of Viognier grown among the vines and fermented with it.","sites":"La Mouline, a lieu-dit on the Côte Blonde above Ampuis: very old vines on narrow terraces curved round the slope like a Roman amphitheatre, on pale gneiss with silica and a little limestone loess.","making":"The Viognier grown among the Syrah goes into the vat with it, and the two ferment together. The wine then spends forty to forty two months in new oak barrels by the house's own sheets, more than three years, before it is bottled at Ampuis.","glass":"Violet, raspberry and blackberry over smoked bacon and black pepper, with a lift of apricot skin from the Viognier; silky and perfumed rather than heavy, the new oak folded in as vanilla and toast, on fine, ripe tannin.","ageing":"Open sooner than the house's La Landonne, it wants eight to ten years to absorb its oak, and a strong vintage keeps for decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a large Burgundy glass for the perfume.","label":[{"term":"Côte-Rôtie","means":"The roasted slope above Ampuis, the northernmost cru of the Rhône: Syrah with up to a fifth Viognier in the vat."},{"term":"La Mouline","means":"The lieu-dit on the Côte Blonde, first bottled on its own with the 1966 vintage."},{"term":"E. Guigal","means":"The house of Ampuis that grows, makes and bottles the wine."}]},"table":"From one lieu-dit on the Côte Blonde, La Mouline is E. Guigal's Côte-Rôtie of Syrah fermented with about a tenth of Viognier, then raised more than three years in new oak. Violets and smoked bacon over silk, it wants a decanter and a large glass.","story":"The founder of this house entered the cellars of the Ampuis merchant Vidal-Fleury in 1924, at fourteen, and set up on his own in 1946. In 1961, by the house's account, a sudden blindness, total though it proved temporary, struck him, and his son, then in his teens, took charge. The slope above the village was already told as a story: a lord of Maugiron, it is said, left one hillside to a fair daughter and one to a dark one, and the names Côte Blonde and Côte Brune stuck to rocks that truly differ. With the 1966 vintage the house bottled the old vines of La Mouline, on the Blonde, as a wine of their own. La Landonne followed in 1978 and La Turque in 1985, the trio the trade calls the La La wines, and in 1984 Guigal bought Vidal-Fleury, the house where its founder had begun. In 1995 it bought the Château d'Ampuis by the river, and in 2003 it set up its own cooperage there. The three single vineyards carried the name of Côte-Rôtie far beyond the Rhône. La Mouline remains the lesson in co-fermented Viognier: the white grape lends the perfume, the Syrah gives the frame.","others":[{"name":"Côte-Rôtie La Landonne","note":"From the Côte Brune, Syrah alone, first made in 1978: the firmest and slowest of the three."},{"name":"Côte-Rôtie La Turque","note":"From the Côte Brune with a little Viognier, first made in 1985, the third of the La La wines."},{"name":"Côte-Rôtie Château d'Ampuis","note":"A blend of several lieux-dits, the step below the La La wines, named for the château the house bought in 1995."}],"traps":["La Mouline is on the Côte Blonde; La Landonne and La Turque are on the Côte Brune.","La Landonne is Syrah alone; La Mouline and La Turque are fermented with a little Viognier, which goes into the vat with the Syrah, never into the finished wine.","Vidal-Fleury has been Guigal's since 1984 yet keeps its own label, so a Vidal-Fleury Côte-Rôtie is not a Guigal wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-sc8qhii1","front":"E. Guigal's La Mouline: which side of Côte-Rôtie, and what grows among its Syrah?","back":"The Côte Blonde; about a tenth of Viognier, grown among the vines and fermented with the Syrah."},{"id":"fl-n4g46cs8","front":"Guigal's three La La Côte-Rôties: their first vintages, in order?","back":"La Mouline 1966, La Landonne 1978, La Turque 1985."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-okxkdu0x","q":"A guest finds apricot and violet in a glass of Guigal's La Mouline and asks where they come from. What do you say?","opts":["A late harvest of ripe Syrah","Viognier blended in after fermenting","Viognier fermented with the Syrah","A share of Marsanne in the blend"],"a":2,"exp":"La Mouline carries about a tenth of Viognier grown among its vines and fermented with the Syrah; Côte-Rôtie lets the white grape into the vat, never into the finished wine, and Marsanne is a grape of Hermitage.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-k0gip6hk","q":"In 1984 E. Guigal bought the Ampuis merchant house where its founder had worked as a boy. Which house was it?","opts":["M. Chapoutier","Paul Jaboulet Aîné","Delas Frères","Vidal-Fleury"],"a":3,"exp":"The founder worked at Vidal-Fleury before he set up in 1946, and Guigal bought it in 1984 while keeping its label; Jaboulet and Chapoutier are houses of Tain-l'Hermitage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Maison Guigal: History of the house since 1946","url":"https://www.guigal.com/en/maison-guigal/our-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maison Guigal: Côte-Rôtie La Mouline","url":"https://www.guigal.com/en/wine/cote-rotie-la-mouline/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Guigal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guigal","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, E Guigal La Mouline 1969","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/grape-varieties/shiraz-syrah/wine-legend-e-guigal-la-mouline-1969-331893/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fells: E. Guigal producer page","url":"https://fells.co.uk/our-producers/e-guigal/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-g2m3zfjj": {"id":"fl-g2m3zfjj","key":"jaboulet","group":"rhone-north","region":"rhone","producer":"Paul Jaboulet Aîné","producerId":"p-jaboulet","say":"zhah-boo-LAY, air-mee-TAHZH lah shah-PEL","place":"Tain-l'Hermitage, Hermitage, Rhône","founded":"1834","wine":{"name":"Hermitage La Chapelle","appellation":"Hermitage","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Syrah alone, though the appellation would allow a little Marsanne and Roussanne in the vat.","sites":"Chiefly Le Méal, on stony terraces in the middle of the south facing hill, and Les Bessards, granite at its western end, with smaller parts from Les Greffieux and Les Rocoules.","making":"The grapes are destemmed and each parcel is fermented apart, with three weeks or more on the skins. The blend is then raised for twelve to eighteen months in French oak barrels, a minority of them new, with a share in concrete eggs since the late 2010s.","glass":"Blackberry, black olive and smoked bacon over violet, iron and cracked pepper; dense and closed when young, with firm tannin, turning with age to leather, truffle and dried fig.","ageing":"Closed for ten to fifteen years after a strong vintage, it then opens slowly; its 1961 and 1978 have lasted for decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for two hours or more, an old one gently off its heavy sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Hermitage","means":"The single hill above Tain and its appellation: red from Syrah, white from Marsanne and Roussanne."},{"term":"La Chapelle","means":"The house's name for its red, after the chapel of Saint-Christophe at the summit; a name, not a vineyard."},{"term":"Paul Jaboulet Aîné","means":"The house name on the label until the 2020 vintage; aîné means the elder, for the founder's son Paul."},{"term":"Domaine de La Chapelle","means":"The name on the label from the 2021 vintage, when the wine moved to a winery of its own."}]},"table":"Named for the chapel of Saint-Christophe at the summit of the hill, Hermitage La Chapelle is Paul Jaboulet Aîné's red from Le Méal and Les Bessards, labelled Domaine de La Chapelle since 2021. All Syrah, black olive and smoked meat, it stays closed for ten years or more.","story":"The hill takes its name from a legend: in the 1220s, the story goes, the knight Gaspard de Stérimberg came home from the crusade against the Albigensians, was granted the slope by Blanche of Castile (1188 to 1252) and lived out his days there as a hermit, at the summit where a chapel now stands. In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries Bordeaux merchants strengthened their clarets with its wine, for colour and body. The house was founded at Tain in 1834 by Antoine Jaboulet (1807 to 1864) and grew under his sons Paul and Henry; it took its name from Paul, the elder. In 1919 it bought the chapel of Saint-Christophe, and its red Hermitage carries the chapel's name. Through the twentieth century La Chapelle was the Hermitage that drinkers abroad knew by name, and its 1961, with the 1978 beside it, became the measure of what northern Syrah can do with age. After a lean spell in the 1990s the family sold the house in 2006 to the Frey family, and the recovery of La Chapelle dates from that sale. With the 2021 vintage the wine moved to a label and a winery of its own, Domaine de La Chapelle. When a guest names Hermitage, La Chapelle is often the wine they mean.","others":[{"name":"Hermitage Le Chevalier de Stérimberg","note":"The white Hermitage of Marsanne and Roussanne, named for the knight of the legend; from 2021 it too carries the Domaine de La Chapelle label."},{"name":"Crozes-Hermitage Domaine de Thalabert","note":"The house's estate Crozes-Hermitage, Syrah from the larger appellation around the hill."}],"traps":["The chapel names the wine and is a landmark of the hill; La Chapelle is a blend of climats, not a vineyard of that name.","La Chapelle is the red; Le Chevalier de Stérimberg is the white Hermitage.","From the 2021 vintage La Chapelle is labelled Domaine de La Chapelle, not Paul Jaboulet Aîné: the same wine of the hill under a new name."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ktdfj4wq","front":"Paul Jaboulet Aîné's La Chapelle: what is the chapel, and is it a vineyard?","back":"The chapel of Saint-Christophe at the summit of the hill of Hermitage, which the house bought in 1919; it names the wine and is no vineyard."},{"id":"fl-6ur8g905","front":"Name the two reference vintages of Hermitage La Chapelle.","back":"1961 and 1978."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-xviqeb2y","q":"A guest asks which climats of the hill give most of Hermitage La Chapelle. What do you answer?","opts":["Les Bessards and Le Méal","Reynard and Sabarotte","La Crau and Pignan","Côte Blonde and Côte Brune"],"a":0,"exp":"La Chapelle is drawn chiefly from Les Bessards, granite at the hill's west end, and Le Méal; the Blonde and the Brune are slopes of Côte-Rôtie, and Reynard and Sabarotte are in Cornas.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-xwdojc7o","q":"Hermitage La Chapelle went through a lean spell in the 1990s. What happened to Paul Jaboulet Aîné in 2006?","opts":["It moved its cellars to Valence","The family sold it to the Frey family","The Tain cooperative took it over","It merged with M. Chapoutier of Tain"],"a":1,"exp":"The Jaboulet family sold the house to the Frey family in 2006, and the recovery of La Chapelle dates from that sale; Chapoutier is a separate house of Tain.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Paul Jaboulet Aîné: Our history","url":"https://jaboulet.com/our-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Inter Rhône: the Hermitage appellation","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/cotes-du-rhone-cru-aoc-hermitage","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Berry Bros. & Rudd: 2021 Hermitage La Chapelle, Domaine de La Chapelle","url":"https://us.bbr.com/collections/region-france-rhone-wine/products/2021-hermitage-la-chapelle-domaine-de-la-chapelle-rhone","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Battle of the Banks","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/battle-of-the-banks-101414/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Hachette Vins: Domaine Paul Jaboulet Aîné","url":"https://www.hachette-vins.com/guide-vins/producteurs/1280/dom-paul-jaboulet-aa-r-na-c","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-r4n74dyq": {"id":"fl-r4n74dyq","key":"chave","group":"rhone-north","region":"rhone","producer":"Domaine Jean-Louis Chave","producerId":"p-chave","say":"SHAHV, air-mee-TAHZH","place":"Mauves and the hill of Hermitage, Rhône","founded":"1481","wine":{"name":"Hermitage","appellation":"Hermitage","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Syrah alone; the family keeps its Marsanne and Roussanne for the white Hermitage.","sites":"Parcels in several of the hill's climats: Les Bessards on granite at the western end, L'Ermite and Péléat, and Le Méal and Les Rocoules on stonier ground further east.","making":"Each parcel is picked, fermented and raised apart, in oak barrels with only a small share of new wood. After some eighteen months the lots are assembled into the one wine, which rests a little longer before it is bottled at Mauves.","glass":"Blackberry, black olive and violet over smoked meat, cracked pepper and graphite, with a note of iron and thyme; layered rather than loud, with dense, fine tannin and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Reserved for its first decade, it opens slowly and keeps for thirty years and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for two hours, and pour an old one gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Hermitage","means":"The appellation of the hill above Tain, on the east bank of the Rhône."},{"term":"J.L. Chave Sélection","means":"The family's merchant label, on wines it buys in; the domaine's own Hermitage does not carry it."}]},"table":"Rather than bottle each climat, Domaine Jean-Louis Chave blends Les Bessards, L'Ermite, Le Méal and others into a single Hermitage, raising each parcel apart for a year and a half before the blend is made. Black olive, pepper and smoked meat, it is built to last thirty years.","story":"By the family's own account its growers can be traced to 1481, and the vines have passed from father to son ever since. They worked the granite terraces of the west bank around Mauves, in what became the Saint-Joseph appellation, and in 1865 bought their first parcels across the river on the hill of Hermitage. When the Hermitage appellation was fixed by decree in 1937, the hill was already known by its climats, each with its own ground, from the granite of Les Bessards in the west to the stonier soils further east. Where some houses of the hill came to bottle their climats one by one, the Chaves held to the older idea of Hermitage as a blend of the whole slope, each parcel adding something the others lack. The family also makes a white Hermitage that can outlast the red, a rare vin de paille of grapes dried on straw and, from 1990 and only in chosen years, Cuvée Cathelin, a selection of the red. In the 1990s the son who had taken over the domaine began to reclaim abandoned Saint-Joseph terraces around Mauves. Chave's Hermitage makes the case for blending a hill rather than dividing it.","others":[{"name":"Hermitage Blanc","note":"Marsanne with a little Roussanne from the hill, a white that can outlast the red."},{"name":"Ermitage Cuvée Cathelin","note":"A selection of the red, first made in 1990 and only in chosen vintages, named for a painter friend of the family who drew its label."},{"name":"Saint-Joseph","note":"Syrah from the family's terraces at Mauves and Tournon, across the river from the hill."}],"traps":["Chave blends its climats into one Hermitage; Chapoutier bottles many of its climats separately.","J.L. Chave Sélection is the family's merchant label, not the domaine's own wine.","Cuvée Cathelin is not made every year, so the domaine's Hermitage is the wine of record."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-x7n7s3w9","front":"Domaine Jean-Louis Chave: how is its red Hermitage put together?","back":"Each parcel is picked, fermented and raised apart for some eighteen months, then the lots are blended into one wine."},{"id":"fl-m2iv2dvh","front":"Which rare sweet Hermitage does Domaine Jean-Louis Chave make?","back":"A vin de paille, from white grapes dried on straw before pressing."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-4k774sgd","q":"A guest asks why Chave makes one red Hermitage rather than a wine from each climat. Which answer fits?","opts":["Its climats are too young to bottle apart","Its vines lie in Les Bessards alone","Each climat adds a part to one wine","The law forbids climat names on Hermitage labels"],"a":2,"exp":"Chave treats the climats as parts of one wine, each adding something the others lack; climat names may appear on labels, as Chapoutier's show.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-0d9heddm","q":"By its own account, from which year has the Chave family grown vines in the northern Rhône?","opts":["1946","1937","1834","1481"],"a":3,"exp":"The family dates its line to 1481; 1834 is Jaboulet's founding, 1937 the year of the Hermitage appellation, and 1946 Guigal's founding.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"INAO: specification of the Hermitage appellation","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/PNO2024AOPHermitage.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, JL Chave Cathelin Ermitage 1990","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-jl-chave-cathelin-ermitage-1990-297936/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Grand Cru Selections: the domaine's producer page","url":"https://grandcruselections.com/domaine-jeanlouis-chave","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Scholar Guild: Domaine J.L. Chave producer guide","url":"https://www.winescholarguild.com/producer-guide/france/rhone/493-domaine-jl-chave","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-0t1n2i8g": {"id":"fl-0t1n2i8g","key":"clape","group":"rhone-north","region":"rhone","producer":"Domaine Auguste Clape","producerId":"p-clape","say":"KLAHP, kor-NAHSS","place":"Cornas, Rhône","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Cornas","appellation":"Cornas","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Syrah alone, as the appellation requires: Cornas admits no white grape at all.","sites":"Old vines on steep granite terraces above the village, chiefly Reynard, La Côte, Patou and Sabarotte, blended into this one wine; younger vines go to Renaissance.","making":"Whole bunches, stems and all, ferment in concrete vats. The wine is then raised for some twenty months in old oval foudres, with no new oak at all, and bottled unfiltered at the domaine.","glass":"Blackberry, tar and black olive over smoked bacon, violet and iron; dark, rugged and tannic in youth, turning with age to smoke, leather and dried thyme.","ageing":"Hard and closed for its first eight to ten years, it then softens and keeps for twenty years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for two hours or more, an old one gently; a large glass with room for the aromas.","label":[{"term":"Cornas","means":"The appellation facing Valence across the river, red only and Syrah only, on granite."},{"term":"Appellation Cornas Contrôlée","means":"The legal guarantee that the wine was grown and made under the rules of Cornas."}]},"table":"Syrah in its old, unpolished form is what Domaine Auguste Clape bottles as Cornas: whole bunches from granite terraces above the village, fermented in concrete and raised in big old foudres without new oak. Black olive, tar and smoke when young, it softens only after eight to ten years.","story":"Cornas faces Valence across the Rhône, and its name, it is said, comes from a Celtic word for burnt earth, for the sun on its granite terraces. The appellation was drawn in 1938, for red wine alone and Syrah alone. For much of the twentieth century most of the village's harvest was sold in bulk to merchants, and the steep slopes, worked by hand, lost their workers to the factories of the valley. The grower whose name the domaine carries, from a family of Languedoc vignerons, came to Cornas when he married into a family of growers there in 1949. For some years he too sold his wine in bulk; then, in the 1950s, he began to bottle it himself, by most accounts the first grower of the village to do so. He held to the old ways: whole bunches, concrete vats, no new wood and long months in old oval casks. He died in 2018, aged 93, and his son and grandson kept to the same methods. Growers who destem and buy new oak are measured against it, and Clape's Cornas is the wine to taste first to learn the traditional school of northern Syrah.","others":[{"name":"Cornas Renaissance","note":"From the domaine's younger vines: a different bottling, not a selection above the estate Cornas."},{"name":"Saint-Péray","note":"White from Marsanne and Roussanne, from the neighbouring appellation that makes no red."},{"name":"Côtes du Rhône","note":"Syrah from around Cornas sold under the regional name."}],"traps":["Cornas is Syrah only and admits no white grape, unlike Côte-Rôtie, which allows Viognier.","Renaissance is the younger vine Cornas, not a reserve above the estate wine.","Auguste Clape of Cornas has nothing to do with La Clape, the limestone massif of the Languedoc."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ocp2ys4t","front":"Domaine Auguste Clape's Cornas: destemmed or whole bunch, and in what wood?","back":"Whole bunch, stems and all, raised in large old foudres with no new oak."},{"id":"fl-biap9xcd","front":"What may grow beside Syrah in a Cornas, and when was the appellation drawn?","back":"Nothing: Cornas is Syrah alone, under an appellation of 1938."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-tehclv2a","q":"A guest compares a young Clape Cornas with a polished, new oak Cornas from another grower. What sets the Clape apart in the cellar?","opts":["Co-fermented Viognier for perfume","Whole bunches and large old foudres","Destemmed fruit and new barrels","Carbonic maceration in sealed vats"],"a":1,"exp":"Clape ferments whole bunches and raises the wine in big old foudres with no new oak; Cornas allows no Viognier, and carbonic maceration is the method of Beaujolais.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2exbfmzs","q":"Before growers such as the founder of Domaine Auguste Clape bottled their own, how was most Cornas sold?","opts":["In bulk to merchants","Only to the village's own cafés","As white wine to Saint-Péray","As Hermitage, across the river"],"a":0,"exp":"For much of the twentieth century most Cornas went in bulk to merchants, so a grower's own bottling gave the village a name; Cornas has always been red, and Hermitage is a separate appellation across the river.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"INAO: specification of the Cornas appellation","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/PNO2023AOPCornas.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Cornas pioneer Pierre-Auguste Clape dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/opinion/cornas-pierre-auguste-clape-has-died-397786","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: In memoriam, Auguste Clape","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/in-memoriam-auguste-clape-legendary-rhone-vintner-56876","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Saveur: A Rugged Blackstrap Masterpiece","url":"https://saveur.com/article/Wine-and-Drink/A-Rugged-Blackstrap-Masterpiece","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Auguste Clape obituary","url":"https://thedrinksbusiness.com/?p=419223","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9043cjli": {"id":"fl-9043cjli","key":"vernay","group":"rhone-north","region":"rhone","producer":"Domaine Georges Vernay","producerId":"p-vernay","say":"vair-NAY, kohn-dree-UH, koh-TOH duh vair-NOHN","place":"Condrieu, Rhône","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Condrieu Coteau de Vernon","appellation":"Condrieu","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"white","grapes":"Viognier alone, as the appellation requires.","sites":"Coteau de Vernon, the hillside above the town of Condrieu: old vines on terraces of biotite granite facing south and south east.","making":"The grapes are pressed whole and the juice is settled cold, then fermented in oak, a minority of it new. The wine is raised on its fine lees for twelve to eighteen months before it is bottled.","glass":"Apricot skin, white peach and honeysuckle over violet, white pepper and wet granite; full and silky, yet held in line by a stony, faintly bitter finish rather than any sweetness.","ageing":"Pleasing young, it gains honey, dried apricot and a waxy depth over eight to ten years, where most Condrieu is drunk young.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius, cool rather than cold; no need to decant, though air helps a young bottle; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Condrieu","means":"The appellation of Viognier alone on the granite of the northern Rhône; most is dry, though the rules allow a sweet style."},{"term":"Coteau de Vernon","means":"A lieu-dit, the hillside above the town, not an appellation of its own."}]},"table":"Coteau de Vernon is Domaine Georges Vernay's Condrieu from old Viognier on granite terraces above the town, fermented in oak and raised on its lees for a year and more. Dry, with apricot, violet and a stony, slightly bitter finish, it is a Condrieu for the cellar.","story":"Sweet Condrieu was long the custom of the hill, and its steep granite terraces were hard to work. The appellation was drawn in 1940, beside the single estate of Château-Grillet, which had been given an appellation of its own in 1936. After the war the terraces emptied, and by the 1950s, as Inter Rhône tells it, the vineyard had all but vanished. The grower whose name the domaine carries held on to his vines through those lean years, and when he died in 2017 the obituaries credited him with saving Viognier from extinction. Inter Rhône dates the revival of the appellation to the 1980s and to the work of a handful of growers, and Viognier was later planted from California to the Languedoc. Coteau de Vernon, the old vines on the hill above the town, became the domaine's flagship, made to age rather than to drink at once. His daughter took charge in 1996. Most Condrieu is drunk within a few years; Coteau de Vernon was made to be kept, and it shows what the grape his family kept alive can become.","others":[{"name":"Condrieu Les Chaillées de l'Enfer","note":"From steep, south facing terraces of the appellation, bottled apart."},{"name":"Condrieu Les Terrasses de l'Empire","note":"The domaine's Condrieu for drinking young."},{"name":"Côte-Rôtie Maison Rouge","note":"The domaine's red, Syrah from the slope just north of Condrieu."}],"traps":["Coteau de Vernon is dry: Viognier's perfume reads as sweetness and misleads candidates.","Coteau de Vernon is a lieu-dit within Condrieu, not an appellation of its own; Château-Grillet is the single estate that has one."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-v0d2hwaw","front":"Domaine Georges Vernay's Coteau de Vernon: which grape, and what is the name on the label?","back":"Viognier alone; Coteau de Vernon is a lieu-dit, the granite hillside above the town of Condrieu."},{"id":"fl-zw9fa2ja","front":"Why is the name Vernay tied to the survival of Condrieu?","back":"When Condrieu had all but vanished, the grower behind the domaine held on to its slopes; his obituaries credit him with saving Viognier."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-1t71nc5r","q":"A guest tastes Coteau de Vernon, finds apricot and honeysuckle, and asks if it is sweet. What do you tell them?","opts":["It is a late picked sweet Condrieu","It is a sweet white from Château-Grillet","It is dry: Viognier's perfume reads as sweetness","It is dry but sweetened before bottling"],"a":2,"exp":"Coteau de Vernon is a dry Condrieu, and the apricot perfume of Viognier often reads as sweetness; sweet Condrieu exists, but this is not one.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-t4tmhoym","q":"By the 1950s the Condrieu vineyard had all but vanished. What did the obituaries of 2017 credit the grower behind Domaine Georges Vernay with?","opts":["Founding the Château-Grillet estate","Replanting the slopes with Syrah","Bringing Viognier to California","Saving Viognier from extinction"],"a":3,"exp":"Decanter and The Drinks Business credited him with saving Viognier when Condrieu had almost vanished; Château-Grillet is a separate single estate with its own appellation.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: the Condrieu appellation","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/cotes-du-rhone-cru-aoc-condrieu","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Viognier saviour Georges Vernay dies in the Rhône","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/viognier-saviour-georges-vernay-dies-in-the-rhone-369451/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Georges Vernay obituary","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/?p=374893","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Broadbent: Coteau de Vernon 2020 fact sheet","url":"https://broadbent.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/2020-Vernay-Coteau-de-Vernon-Condrieu-FactSheet.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vente à la propriété: Coteau de Vernon 2021, a return from the domaine","url":"https://www.ventealapropriete.com/en/vente/ficheproduitprint/DGV0033","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3shqihpc": {"id":"fl-3shqihpc","key":"beaucastel","group":"rhone-south","region":"rhone","producer":"Château de Beaucastel","producerId":"p-beaucastel","say":"boh-kah-STEL, shah-toh-NUHF dew PAHP","place":"Courthézon, Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Rhône","founded":"1909","wine":{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","appellation":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Grenache and Mourvèdre in roughly equal leading parts, with Syrah and Counoise next and smaller parts of the other permitted varieties: the estate grows all thirteen.","sites":"One large block around the château at Courthézon, on the northern edge of the appellation and open to the Mistral: rolled stones over sand, clay and limestone, planted to all thirteen varieties, with much old Mourvèdre.","making":"Each variety is picked by hand and fermented apart, Mourvèdre and Syrah in conical wooden vats and the others in concrete and steel. The grapes are destemmed and, by long habit of the estate, heated briefly and cooled again before about two weeks of maceration. The blend is then raised for about a year in large old oak foudres and bottled at the château.","glass":"Kirsch and blackberry over leather, game and dried thyme, with black olive, liquorice and a note of truffle as it ages; firm and savoury rather than sweet, the Mourvèdre giving grip and a long, meaty finish.","ageing":"Generous on release, it often closes between about five and ten years, then opens again and keeps for two or three decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a large Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","means":"The popes' new castle: the cru between Orange and Avignon that permits thirteen named varieties."},{"term":"Château de Beaucastel","means":"The estate at Courthézon, on the northern edge of the appellation."}]},"table":"All thirteen permitted varieties grow at Château de Beaucastel, and its Châteauneuf-du-Pape carries far more Mourvèdre than most. Savoury rather than sweet, leather, game and kirsch, it can close up in its middle years, so decant it and be patient.","story":"Courthézon, on the northern edge of Châteauneuf, has known the name Beaucastel since 1549, when, by the estate's account, a man of that name bought a barn and land at Coudoulet. In 1909 the property was bought by a forebear of the Perrin family, who rebuilt its vineyard; it passed to his son-in-law, the first Perrin there, who enlarged it, and then to that son-in-law's son, Jacques Perrin, who ran it until his death in 1978. By the estate's account Jacques Perrin was a forerunner of organic farming from the 1960s, and he brought back Mourvèdre, a variety that had almost vanished from the appellation; it gives the wine its savoury frame of leather and game. Châteauneuf-du-Pape, decreed in 1936 among the first appellations of France, allows thirteen varieties, and Beaucastel grows every one of them. In 1989 his sons first made Hommage à Jacques Perrin, a Mourvèdre led selection kept for strong years, and that same year the family and their American importer bought land at Paso Robles in California, the start of Tablas Creek. Mourvèdre belongs in any talk of Châteauneuf because of Beaucastel, the counterweight to Grenache alone.","others":[{"name":"Hommage à Jacques Perrin","note":"A Mourvèdre led selection built on the estate's oldest Mourvèdre, first made in 1989 and only in strong vintages."},{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape Roussanne Vieilles Vignes","note":"The white of old Roussanne vines, a Châteauneuf blanc with a long life."},{"name":"Coudoulet de Beaucastel","note":"Côtes du Rhône from vines just outside the appellation, across the road from the estate."}],"traps":["Coudoulet de Beaucastel is Côtes du Rhône, not Châteauneuf-du-Pape.","Thirteen varieties by name, eighteen when the colour forms of Grenache, Clairette and Piquepoul are counted apart.","Hommage à Jacques Perrin is Mourvèdre led and not made every year; the estate's Châteauneuf is the annual wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-hol1nv1z","front":"Château de Beaucastel: how many of Châteauneuf's permitted varieties does it grow, and which one sets it apart?","back":"All thirteen; an unusually large share of Mourvèdre."},{"id":"fl-ju3d7mqa","front":"What does Château de Beaucastel do to its harvest before fermentation?","back":"It heats the destemmed grapes briefly, then cools them again, before they macerate and ferment."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-tzqqg49v","q":"A guest finds Château de Beaucastel's Châteauneuf-du-Pape more savoury and gamey than the one before it. Which grape do you credit?","opts":["Mourvèdre","Grenache Blanc","Clairette","Cinsault"],"a":0,"exp":"Beaucastel grows far more Mourvèdre than most of the appellation, and it gives leather, game and grip; Cinsault is light and perfumed, and Grenache Blanc and Clairette are white.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-c8w3dasp","q":"In 1989 the family of Château de Beaucastel first made a Mourvèdre led selection. Whom does its name honour?","opts":["The popes of Avignon, builders of the castle","Jacques Perrin, who ran the estate until 1978","Claude Chappe, inventor of the telegraph","The leader of the growers' union of 1923"],"a":1,"exp":"Hommage à Jacques Perrin honours the man who ran Beaucastel until his death in 1978 and brought back its Mourvèdre; the growers' union of 1923 was led by Baron Le Roy.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château de Beaucastel: Our history","url":"https://www.beaucastel.com/en/pages/notre-histoire","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Famille Perrin: Château de Beaucastel Châteauneuf-du-Pape Rouge technical sheet","url":"https://m.familleperrin.com/GNZ16E","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tablas Creek Vineyard: History","url":"https://tablascreek.com/story/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Château de Beaucastel Châteauneuf-du-Pape 2010","url":"https://top100.winespectator.com/2013/wine/8-chateau-de-beaucastel/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"iDealwine: Château de Beaucastel, a historic estate of Châteauneuf","url":"https://www.idealwine.net/le-chateau-de-beaucastel-a-chateauneuf-un-domaine-historique/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-tczf9ae9": {"id":"fl-tczf9ae9","key":"rayas","group":"rhone-south","region":"rhone","producer":"Château Rayas","producerId":"p-rayas","say":"rah-YAHSS","place":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Rhône","founded":"1880","wine":{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","appellation":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Grenache alone, from old vines.","sites":"Parcels of sand with some clay, ringed by woods north east of the village, most of them facing north, so the Grenache ripens slowly in shade and cool air; none of the rolled stones of La Crau.","making":"Grenache from old vines, cropped at very low yields, is fermented and then raised in old foudres and demi-muids, none of them new and some of them decades old, before bottling at the estate.","glass":"Kirsch, raspberry and wild strawberry over incense, rose petal and dried orange peel; pale in the glass, silky and light on its feet for its strength, with fine tannin and a long, perfumed finish.","ageing":"Charming within a few years in a ripe vintage, it grows more savoury over fifteen to twenty years and more.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant gently if at all, so as to keep the perfume; a large Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Château Rayas","means":"The estate's name; on a French label château names a wine estate, whatever its buildings."},{"term":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","means":"The cru between Orange and Avignon, which allows a blend of varieties yet does not require one."}]},"table":"Pale enough to surprise a guest, Château Rayas's Châteauneuf-du-Pape is Grenache alone, grown on cool, north facing sand among the woods rather than on the famous stones. Kirsch, incense and rose, it drinks more like a red Burgundy than a southern blend, so pour it into a Burgundy glass.","story":"Rayas lies among woods north east of the village of Châteauneuf, on sand rather than stone. It was founded in 1880 by a notary of the Reynaud family who had lost his hearing and turned to the vine. His son, who had studied agriculture, bottled the estate's first wine in 1920 and printed Premier Grand Cru on its labels, a rank of his own making that exasperated the local authorities, since Châteauneuf has no official classification. He added Château des Tours at Sarrians in the 1930s and Château de Fonsalette in the Côtes du Rhône in 1945. On his death in 1978 one of his sons took charge of Rayas and Fonsalette, until his own sudden death, without children, in 1997. A nephew who had been running Château des Tours then took Rayas on, keeping its Grenache alone, its very low yields and its old wood until his death in 2025. The appellation allows thirteen varieties, yet Rayas showed that a Châteauneuf could be pale, perfumed and of one grape, and that cool sand could matter as much as the rolled stones. Colour and weight, it teaches, are not the measure of a southern red.","others":[{"name":"Pignan","note":"A named parcel of the same estate, bottled apart as a second Châteauneuf, not a separate property."},{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape Blanc","note":"Grenache Blanc and Clairette, a white that ages for years."},{"name":"Château de Fonsalette","note":"The family's Côtes du Rhône estate, bought in 1945 and made under the same hand."}],"traps":["The red is Grenache alone, not a blend of the permitted varieties.","Pignan is a Rayas cuvée, not a separate estate; Fonsalette is Côtes du Rhône, not Châteauneuf.","The Premier Grand Cru the founder's son printed on Rayas labels was his own wording, not a rank: Châteauneuf has no official classification."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-jm2lux8k","front":"Château Rayas: which grape, and on what soil?","back":"Grenache alone, on cool, north facing sand among the woods north east of the village, not on rolled stones."},{"id":"fl-5xl9men4","front":"What is Pignan, beside Château Rayas on a list?","back":"A named parcel of the same estate, bottled as a second Châteauneuf, not another property."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-9yu10j28","q":"A guest is surprised that a glass of Château Rayas is so pale for a Châteauneuf-du-Pape. Which explanation fits?","opts":["A short ferment in new oak barrels","Young vines on the rolled stones of La Crau","A large share of white grapes in the vat","Old Grenache alone, grown on cool sand"],"a":3,"exp":"Rayas is pure Grenache from cool, sandy soil, which gives a pale, perfumed wine; it carries no white grapes, uses no new oak and has no vines on La Crau.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-atn4vgve","q":"Which family has held Château Rayas since 1880?","opts":["The Avril family","The Perrin family","The Reynaud family","The Brunier family"],"a":2,"exp":"A notary of the Reynaud family founded Rayas in 1880; the Perrins are Beaucastel's family, the Bruniers Vieux Télégraphe's and the Avrils those of Clos des Papes.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Château Rayas","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_Rayas","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"FINE+RARE: Guide to Rayas, Fonsalette and Des Tours","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/editorial/everything-you-need-to-know-about-rayas","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Château Rayas, the enduring winemaking mysteries","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/chateau-rayas-the-enduring-winemaking-mysteries-behind-this-rhone-icon-573770/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"iDealwine: the Reynaud family's estates, a long read","url":"https://www.idealwine.info/?p=17941","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Berry Bros. and Rudd: Château Rayas producer page","url":"https://www.bbr.com/producer-3590-chateau-rayas","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-js1n5np6": {"id":"fl-js1n5np6","key":"vieux-telegraphe","group":"rhone-south","region":"rhone","producer":"Domaine du Vieux Télégraphe","producerId":"p-vieux-telegraphe","say":"vyuh tay-lay-GRAHF, lah KROH","place":"Bédarrides, Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Rhône","founded":"1891","wine":{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape La Crau","appellation":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Grenache, about two thirds, with Mourvèdre and Syrah in near equal smaller parts and a little Cinsault, Clairette and other permitted varieties.","sites":"The plateau of La Crau in the south east of the appellation near Bédarrides, at about 130 metres: rolled Alpine pebbles, the galets roulés, over Miocene molasse and a thick bed of stony clay that holds water through the summer.","making":"Picked by hand and sorted three times, at the vine and in the cellar. The grapes are destemmed, in some years with a share of whole bunches kept, and fermented in vat; the blend is then raised for fifteen to sixteen months in large French oak foudres of thirty to sixty hectolitres and bottled at the domaine.","glass":"Kirsch and blackberry over dried thyme, lavender and warm stone, with leather and liquorice from the Mourvèdre; full and firm rather than sweet, with fine, dusty tannin and a long finish.","ageing":"Generous for a few years after release, it then closes for a while and keeps for twenty years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"La Crau","means":"The stony plateau of Châteauneuf-du-Pape where the vines grow, not the Crau plain of Provence."},{"term":"Vieux Télégraphe","means":"The old telegraph: the optical signal tower that once stood on the plateau."}]},"table":"On the stony plateau of La Crau, where rolled stones store the day's heat, Domaine du Vieux Télégraphe grows its Châteauneuf-du-Pape La Crau. Grenache led, with Mourvèdre and Syrah, it tastes of kirsch, thyme and warm stone, and ages for twenty years.","story":"The domaine is named for a tower. Claude Chappe (1763 to 1805) devised the optical telegraph, a chain of towers with movable arms that passed messages across France from hilltop to hilltop, and its first line opened in 1794. By the domaine's account a tower of his system was raised in 1821 on La Crau, the stony plateau south east of the village. In 1891, the year the family dates its work from, the Bruniers began to plant vines on the plateau, among stones so thick that the soil beneath them can barely be seen, and a later generation gave the estate the old tower's name. When the appellation was decreed in 1936 the plateau lay within it. The galets roulés, pebbles carried down from the Alps by the ancient Rhône, store the day's heat and give it back at night, while the clay beneath holds water through the summer, so the old Grenache ripens fully without drying out. The family made La Crau its flagship, with Télégramme, from younger vines, beside it as a second wine. Ask what the stones of Châteauneuf actually do, and Vieux Télégraphe is the glass that answers.","others":[{"name":"Télégramme","note":"The second wine, Châteauneuf-du-Pape from the domaine's younger vines."},{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape Blanc","note":"The white of Clairette, Grenache Blanc, Bourboulenc and Roussanne from the same domaine."}],"traps":["The name comes from an optical telegraph tower on the plateau, not from a vineyard.","La Crau here is a plateau of Châteauneuf-du-Pape, not the Crau plain of Provence.","Télégramme is the second wine, from younger vines, not a lighter style of La Crau."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-law7fp4p","front":"Domaine du Vieux Télégraphe: where does its name come from?","back":"A tower of the optical telegraph Claude Chappe devised, raised on the plateau of La Crau in 1821 by the domaine's account."},{"id":"fl-ex865rjz","front":"What do the galets roulés of La Crau do for the vines of Vieux Télégraphe?","back":"They store the day's heat and give it back at night, while the clay below holds water through the summer."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-0z7i8egx","q":"A guest asks why Vieux Télégraphe's La Crau ripens its Grenache so fully. Which reason do you give?","opts":["Rolled stones that store the day's heat","Limestone crags that shade the vines","North facing terraces of granite","Cool sand under pine and oak woods"],"a":0,"exp":"The galets roulés of La Crau hold the day's heat and release it at night; cool sand in the woods is Rayas, granite terraces the northern Rhône and limestone crags Gigondas.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-0ofilqev","q":"The Brunier family dates its vines on La Crau to 1891. What gave Domaine du Vieux Télégraphe its name?","opts":["A wine merchant named Télégraphe","A signal tower of the optical telegraph","A railway telegraph line below the plateau","A post office in the village"],"a":1,"exp":"A tower of the optical telegraph Claude Chappe devised stood on the plateau of La Crau; the domaine kept the name, and Télégramme, the second wine, plays on it.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine du Vieux Télégraphe: the domaine's brochure","url":"https://www.vieux-telegraphe.fr/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/brochure_vieux_telegraphe.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine du Vieux Télégraphe: technical sheets","url":"https://www.vieux-telegraphe.fr/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/fiches_techniques_vieux_telegraphe.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine du Vieux Télégraphe: Geography","url":"https://www.vieux-telegraphe.fr/en/geographie/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Primal Wine: Famille Brunier, Vieux Télégraphe and the La Crau plateau","url":"https://primalwine.com/blogs/natural-wine-producers-primal-wine/famille-brunier-vieux-telegraphe-and-the-la-crau-plateau","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Encyclopaedia Britannica: Claude Chappe","url":"https://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/106172/Claude-Chappe","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ff8j0vnd": {"id":"fl-ff8j0vnd","key":"clos-des-papes","group":"rhone-south","region":"rhone","producer":"Clos des Papes","producerId":"p-clos-des-papes","say":"kloh day PAHP, shah-toh-NUHF dew PAHP","place":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape, Rhône","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","appellation":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Grenache, the larger part, with an unusually high share of Mourvèdre, from a fifth to nearly a third, some Syrah and small parts of other permitted varieties.","sites":"Around two dozen parcels scattered across the appellation's varied soils, blended so that no single soil leads; among them the old vine plot beside the ruins of the popes' castle that gave the estate its name.","making":"The grapes are destemmed and fermented in vat; the wine is raised in vat and in large old oak foudres, never new, before the blend is bottled at the domaine, and no special cuvée is drawn off.","glass":"Raspberry, kirsch and black cherry over dried thyme, lavender and liquorice, with a savoury edge of leather from the Mourvèdre; fresh and structured for Châteauneuf, with firm, fine tannin.","ageing":"Fruity on release, it closes after a few years and opens again towards its tenth year, keeping for two decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two; a large Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Clos des Papes","means":"The estate's name, from one old vine plot by the popes' castle; the wine blends many parcels."},{"term":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape","means":"The cru named for the castle the popes of Avignon built above the village in the fourteenth century."}]},"table":"One red a year and no special cuvée above it: Clos des Papes blends its Châteauneuf-du-Pape from parcels all over the appellation, with more Mourvèdre than most. Fresh and firm, raspberry, thyme and leather, it opens again towards its tenth year.","story":"Records of the Avril family in Châteauneuf go back, as the family tells it, to 1600, in a village named for the castle the popes of Avignon built above it between 1317 and 1333. In 1896 an Avril first bottled the estate's wine under the name Clos des Papes, taken from an old vine plot beside the ruins of that castle. In 1923 the growers of the village formed a union under Baron Pierre Le Roy de Boiseaumarié (1890 to 1967) to win strict rules on where their wine grew, from which grapes and at what ripeness, and on 15 May 1936 Châteauneuf-du-Pape became one of the first appellations of France. The family's holding grew to around two dozen parcels in every corner of the appellation. Where other houses set their old vines apart in prestige cuvées, Clos des Papes kept to one red and one white, and its red carries more Mourvèdre than most. That refusal to divide is the estate's signature. On a list it stands for the whole appellation in one bottle, and its white, from six varieties, deserves a place beside it.","others":[{"name":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape Blanc","note":"The white, from six varieties in roughly equal parts, a wine that ages for years."},{"name":"Le Petit Vin d'Avril","note":"The family's Vin de France, sold without the appellation's name."}],"traps":["The name comes from one old vine plot by the popes' castle, but the wine is a blend of parcels from across the appellation, not a single clos.","The estate makes one red and no prestige cuvée, so there is no grander Clos des Papes to trade up to.","Mourvèdre matters more here than in most Châteauneuf, which leans on Grenache."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-pl8zf3er","front":"Clos des Papes: how many red Châteauneufs does it make each year, and from where?","back":"One, blended from around two dozen parcels across the appellation, with no prestige cuvée."},{"id":"fl-rtxw6dro","front":"In the village of Clos des Papes, who led the growers' union of 1923 that fought for strict rules?","back":"Baron Pierre Le Roy de Boiseaumarié (1890 to 1967), later the chairman of the INAO from 1947."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-0ufrq0ji","q":"A guest asks for the prestige cuvée of Clos des Papes. What do you tell them?","opts":["It is a bottling of the plot by the castle","It is the Cuvée Vieilles Vignes","It is sold only as a Vin de France","There is none: one red is made each year"],"a":3,"exp":"Clos des Papes blends its parcels into a single red with no prestige cuvée; the plot by the popes' castle gave the estate its name but goes into that one red, and Le Petit Vin d'Avril is a simpler Vin de France.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-642py0bn","q":"Clos des Papes stands in the village whose growers formed a union in 1923. In which year did Châteauneuf-du-Pape become an appellation?","opts":["1971","1923","1936","1909"],"a":2,"exp":"The decree of 15 May 1936 made law of what the growers' union of 1923 had fought for; 1909 is the year Beaucastel was bought, and 1971 the year Gigondas became a cru.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Clos des Papes: the estate's home page","url":"https://www.clos-des-papes.fr/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"AOC Châteauneuf-du-Pape: the growers' association and its history","url":"https://en.chateauneuf.com/appellation-chateauneuf-du-pape/history-chateauneuf-du-pape/association-chateauneuf-du-pape/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Producer profile, Clos des Papes","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/producer-profile-clos-des-pape-420365/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Clos des Papes producer page","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/clos-des-papes-2/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Scholar Guild: Clos des Papes producer guide","url":"https://www.winescholarguild.com/producer-guide/france/rhone/536-clos-des-papes","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-k4tlru2w": {"id":"fl-k4tlru2w","key":"saint-cosme","group":"rhone-south","region":"rhone","producer":"Château de Saint Cosme","producerId":"p-saint-cosme","say":"san KOHM, zhee-gohn-DAHSS","place":"Gigondas, Rhône","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Gigondas","appellation":"Gigondas","classification":"A cru of the Rhône Valley, above Côtes du Rhône and its Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Grenache, about seven tenths, with Mourvèdre and Syrah in near equal smaller parts and a touch of Cinsault.","sites":"The estate's bush vines around Gigondas below the limestone crags of the Dentelles de Montmirail, on limestone soils in one of the cooler corners of the cru, where cool nights keep the wine fresh.","making":"Fermented as whole bunches on native yeasts; the wine is then raised for about a year, part in concrete vats and part in barrels, a small share of them new and the rest used for a few wines, and bottled at the estate without fining or filtration.","glass":"Black cherry and blackberry over smoke, dried thyme and black pepper, with a cool, stony freshness from the limestone; firm but fine tannin and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Open within a few years of the vintage, it is at its fullest between five and fifteen years.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant for an hour when young; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Gigondas","means":"A cru under the Dentelles, red and rosé since 1971 and white too from the 2023 vintage."},{"term":"Château de Saint Cosme","means":"The family estate's own wine; the house's merchant range, from bought grapes, is labelled Saint Cosme alone."}]},"table":"At the foot of the Dentelles, limestone and cool nights keep Château de Saint Cosme's Gigondas fresh: Grenache led, with Mourvèdre and Syrah, fermented as whole bunches. Black cherry, smoke and pepper, it is a firmer, cooler neighbour to Châteauneuf, made for game or lamb.","story":"Under the jagged limestone of the Dentelles de Montmirail, Saint Cosme stands on ground worked since Roman times: vats cut into the limestone beneath the château are held to be Gallo-Roman. The Barruol family has been there since 1490 by most accounts, with 1570 in others. Gigondas, long a commune of the Côtes du Rhône Villages, became on 6 January 1971 the first of them raised to a cru of its own, for red and rosé. In 1992 a son of the family took charge after his father fell ill, and later in that decade, by the family's account, set up a merchant business beside the estate for wines from bought grapes, labelled Saint Cosme alone. Beside its Gigondas the estate bottles lieux-dits apart, Le Claux, Hominis Fides and Le Poste, and an old vine Valbelle. From the 2023 vintage the appellation added white wine to its red and rosé. The estate's Gigondas, whole bunches of Grenache with Mourvèdre and Syrah from its own vines, introduces the house and shows that Gigondas is not simply a Châteauneuf from the next valley.","others":[{"name":"Gigondas Valbelle","note":"The estate's old vine selection, Grenache with some Syrah, raised partly in new oak."},{"name":"Gigondas Le Claux","note":"Old Grenache from one lieu-dit on yellowish limestone marl, bottled apart."},{"name":"Gigondas Hominis Fides","note":"Grenache of great age from a lieu-dit of Miocene sand and clay, the perfumed one."}],"traps":["Gigondas has been a cru since 1971; before that it was a commune of the Côtes du Rhône Villages.","The Saint Cosme merchant wines are made from bought grapes; Château de Saint Cosme is the estate.","Gigondas is mostly Grenache, like Châteauneuf, but its limestone and height give a cooler, firmer wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-l2f1mnr2","front":"Château de Saint Cosme's Gigondas: which grapes, and how are they fermented?","back":"Grenache, about seven tenths, with Mourvèdre, Syrah and a touch of Cinsault; as whole bunches, on native yeasts."},{"id":"fl-mpeqw0qa","front":"Château de Saint Cosme's Gigondas: what did the appellation become in 1971, and what did it add from 2023?","back":"A cru of its own, the first of the Villages promoted; white wine beside its red and rosé."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-2zdqyux9","q":"A guest wants a southern Rhône red fresher and firmer than Châteauneuf. Why might Château de Saint Cosme's Gigondas suit?","opts":["Limestone and cool nights under the Dentelles","Sea breezes off the Mediterranean coast","A large share of white grapes in the blend","Rolled stones that store the summer heat"],"a":0,"exp":"Gigondas sits high under the limestone Dentelles, and cooler nights keep it fresh; rolled stones that hold the heat are the mark of La Crau in Châteauneuf.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-45ka7eel","q":"Château de Saint Cosme's village was promoted from Côtes du Rhône Villages to a cru of its own. In which year?","opts":["2023","1971","1936","1990"],"a":1,"exp":"Gigondas became a cru on 6 January 1971, the first of the Villages; 1936 was the decree of Châteauneuf-du-Pape, and 2023 the first vintage of white Gigondas.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Rhône: Côtes du Rhône Cru AOC Gigondas","url":"https://www.vins-rhone.com/en/cotes-du-rhone-cru-aoc-gigondas","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Winebow: Château de Saint Cosme Gigondas","url":"https://www.winebow.com/our-brands/chateau-de-saint-cosme/chateau-de-saint-cosme-gigondas/2024","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Grape Collective: Château de Saint Cosme, wines that reflect Gigondas' soils","url":"https://www.grapecollective.com/chateau-de-saint-cosme-wines-that-reflect-gigondas-distinctive-soils/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Genius of Gigondas","url":"https://top100.winespectator.com/2017/article/genius-of-gigondas/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"iDealwine: Château de Saint Cosme, in one family since 1490","url":"https://www.idealwine.net/chateau-de-saint-cosme-lame-historique-de-gigondas-dans-la-meme-famille-depuis-1490/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-o75spbav": {"id":"fl-o75spbav","key":"huet","group":"loire","region":"loire","producer":"Domaine Huet","producerId":"p-huet","say":"oo-ETT, luh oh-LYUH","place":"Vouvray, Touraine, the Loire Valley","founded":"1928","wine":{"name":"Vouvray Le Haut-Lieu","appellation":"Vouvray","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Chenin Blanc alone.","sites":"Le Haut-Lieu, the domaine's first vineyard and its home, bought in 1928: deep clay and limestone, richer than the soils of its other two vineyards, which gives a softer, more open wine than theirs.","making":"Each of the three vineyards is made apart, and in a rich autumn the pickers pass through the rows more than once. Whole bunches go to the press uncrushed, and the fermentation runs as long as the richness of the juice demands. The year's ripeness, not a recipe, sets the style: sec, demi-sec or moelleux.","glass":"Quince, pear and green apple over lime blossom, beeswax and wool, with a chalky edge; taut as a sec, rounder with a touch of honey as a demi-sec, and always driven by high acidity.","ageing":"Bright and open young, it may close for a few years in its middle age and then keeps for decades; the sweet years last half a century.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius for the sec and demi-sec, a little cooler for a moelleux; decant a young sec for half an hour; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Le Haut-Lieu","means":"The vineyard, a lieu-dit of Vouvray, not an appellation of its own."},{"term":"Sec","means":"Dry: the style the year gave this bottling; another year the same vineyard may be demi-sec."},{"term":"Demi-Sec","means":"Medium dry, its sugar balanced by the high acidity of Chenin Blanc."},{"term":"Moelleux","means":"Sweet, made in rich years from late passes, some berries touched by noble rot."}]},"table":"Le Haut-Lieu is the first vineyard of Domaine Huet, bought in 1928: Vouvray Chenin Blanc from deep clay and limestone, made dry, medium dry or sweet as the year allows. Quince, pear and beeswax ride on high acidity, so read the label for the style.","story":"Domaine Huet began in 1928, when a Paris bistro owner whose health had been broken in the First World War came to Vouvray with his son, Gaston Huet (1910 to 2002), and bought Le Haut-Lieu, the first of its vineyards. Taken prisoner in the Second World War, Gaston spent five years in Oflag IV-D, a prison camp in Germany. In 1947 he was elected mayor of Vouvray, an office he held until 1989, and that autumn he made a Haut-Lieu Moelleux that became a Loire wine of history. The domaine took on the walled Clos du Bourg in 1953 and bought Le Mont in 1957, and from then on made each of its three vineyards apart, dry, medium dry or sweet as the year allowed. His son in law joined in 1971, took charge of the cellar in 1976 and turned the vineyards to biodynamic farming at the end of the 1980s. Gaston Huet died in 2002, and in 2003 the domaine passed to the Hwang family, the son in law staying in the cellar until 2012. Le Haut-Lieu, the first of the three, is the plainest lesson in Chenin: one vineyard, one grape, and the year deciding whether the bottle is dry or sweet.","others":[{"name":"Vouvray Le Mont","note":"Bought in 1957, on stony green clay; the most tightly wound of the three vineyards."},{"name":"Vouvray Clos du Bourg","note":"The walled vineyard the domaine took on in 1953, on thin soil over the rock, giving its most concentrated sweet wines."},{"name":"Vouvray Pétillant","note":"Traditional method sparkling Vouvray, aged long on its lees."}],"traps":["Sec, demi-sec and moelleux are styles the year decides, not tiers of quality or of vineyard.","Huet's Pétillant is traditional method sparkling Vouvray, not a pétillant naturel.","Le Haut-Lieu, Le Mont and Clos du Bourg are lieux-dits of Vouvray, not separate appellations."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-0a868h16","front":"Domaine Huet: what are its three vineyards, and which came first?","back":"Le Haut-Lieu, bought in 1928, then the walled Clos du Bourg, taken on in 1953, and Le Mont, bought in 1957."},{"id":"fl-odukutpj","front":"What decides whether a bottle of Huet's Le Haut-Lieu is sec, demi-sec or moelleux?","back":"The year and the picking: the ripeness and the share of noble rot in each harvest set the style."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-2iwy7xip","q":"A guest sees Le Haut-Lieu Sec on the list and, beside it, Le Haut-Lieu Moelleux of another year. What do you explain?","opts":["Two vineyards of the same name in Vouvray","A dry wine and its sweetened second wine","One vineyard, its style set each year by the harvest","A Vouvray and a Montlouis from facing banks"],"a":2,"exp":"Huet makes Le Haut-Lieu sec, demi-sec or moelleux as the ripeness and noble rot of each year allow; it is one lieu-dit, and Montlouis faces Vouvray across the river.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-nfqah3eu","q":"Gaston Huet came home from five years in a German prison camp. In which year was he elected mayor of Vouvray and made a Haut-Lieu Moelleux of history?","opts":["1928","1957","1989","1947"],"a":3,"exp":"He was elected mayor in 1947, and that year's Haut-Lieu Moelleux is a Loire wine of history; 1928 is the founding, 1957 the purchase of Le Mont and 1989 the year he left the mayor's office.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Domaine Huet","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domaine_Huet","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Domaine Huet Le Haut Lieu 1947","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-domaine-huet-le-haut-lieu-1947-374618","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Dreyfus Ashby: Domaine Huet producer page","url":"https://dreyfus-ashby.co.uk/?p=2741","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Domaine Huet article","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/media/183161/huet-article.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Alliance Wine: Domaine Huet producer page","url":"https://www.alliancewine.com/our-producers/domaine-huet","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-gh654iaa": {"id":"fl-gh654iaa","key":"coulee-de-serrant","group":"loire","region":"loire","producer":"Coulée de Serrant","producerId":"p-nicolas-joly","say":"koo-LAY duh seh-RAHN","place":"Savennières, Anjou, the Loire Valley","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Coulée de Serrant","appellation":"Savennières Coulée de Serrant","classification":"An appellation of its own within Savennières, held whole by one estate","colour":"white","grapes":"Chenin Blanc alone.","sites":"A steep, walled slope above the north bank of the Loire near Angers, thin soil over tilted red schist and quartz facing south and south east, under vine since monks planted it in 1130.","making":"Hard pruning keeps yields low, and the grapes are picked by hand in three to five passes over three to four weeks. They ferment slowly with the yeasts of the place, for two to four months or longer, never chaptalised. The wine is raised on its lees, stirred and never racked off, in barrels of 500 litres with almost no new oak, and bottled dry with little or no filtration.","glass":"Bruised quince, camomile and beeswax over dried apricot, honey and wet stone, dry despite its honeyed scent; broad and powerful, with high acidity, a gentle bitterness and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Closed in its first five years, when the estate asks for a bottle to be opened a day ahead, it opens with age and is made for long keeping; an opened bottle often tastes better on its third or fourth day.","serve":"13 to 15 degrees Celsius, as the estate asks, never fridge cold; open a young bottle a day ahead or decant it more than once; a wide white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Savennières Coulée de Serrant","means":"The appellation of this one walled vineyard, inside Savennières, held whole by a single estate."},{"term":"Savennières","means":"The wider appellation of Chenin on schist near Angers, with two named crus inside it, each an appellation of its own."}]},"table":"Coulée de Serrant is one walled slope of Chenin Blanc above the Loire, planted by monks in 1130 and an appellation held whole by a single estate. Farmed biodynamically since 1981, it is dry yet tastes of honey and quince; open a young bottle a day ahead.","story":"Cistercian monks planted the slope in 1130, as the estate tells it, on schist above the north bank of the Loire near Angers, and it has been under vine ever since. The writer Curnonsky (1872 to 1956), born in Angers and elected prince of gastronomes in 1927, counted its wine, by the estate's account, among the five great white wines of France. In 1962 a Paris surgeon bought the estate with the Clos de la Coulée de Serrant. His son came home in 1977 after six years as an investment banker in New York and London and, having read Rudolf Steiner (1861 to 1925), whose lectures of 1924 began biodynamic farming, farmed the Coulée biodynamically from 1981 and the whole estate from 1984, an early convert in France. He became a voice for the method, writing books on it that were published in several languages. Because one estate holds the whole appellation, Coulée de Serrant is one of very few French appellations that is also a monopole. On a list it is the wine to explain Savennières: dry Chenin of great power, with an argument about farming in every bottle.","others":[{"name":"Savennières Roche aux Moines Clos de la Bergerie","note":"From the neighbouring named cru, Roche aux Moines, a separate appellation next door."},{"name":"Savennières Les Vieux Clos","note":"The estate's Savennières, the introduction to its style."}],"traps":["Savennières Coulée de Serrant is an appellation of its own, held whole by one estate, not a lieu-dit printed on a Savennières label.","Savennières is dry Chenin; Coteaux du Layon, across the river, is sweet.","Roche aux Moines is a separate named cru next door, not a part of Coulée de Serrant."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8cdqmyms","front":"Coulée de Serrant: who planted the vineyard, and when?","back":"Cistercian monks, in 1130 by the estate's account, on the schist above the Loire near Angers."},{"id":"fl-51mvf5r1","front":"What farming did Coulée de Serrant take up in 1981, the whole estate following by 1984?","back":"Biodynamics, the farming that grew from Rudolf Steiner's lectures of 1924."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-dwuoa4bn","q":"A guest orders Coulée de Serrant expecting a sweet wine from its honeyed scent. What do you tell them?","opts":["It is dry, as Savennières is","It is a sparkling wine of Anjou","It is medium dry, like a demi-sec Vouvray","It is sweet, like Coteaux du Layon"],"a":0,"exp":"Coulée de Serrant is dry Chenin, its honey coming from ripeness and age, not sugar; sweet Chenin is Coteaux du Layon across the river.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ioggghhs","q":"Which family bought Coulée de Serrant in 1962 and later made it a pulpit for biodynamics?","opts":["The Mellot family","The Joly family","The Foucault family","The Huet family"],"a":1,"exp":"The Joly family bought it in 1962 and farmed it biodynamically from 1981; the Huets are Vouvray, the Foucaults Saumur-Champigny and the Mellots Sancerre.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"La Coulée de Serrant: History of the Coulée of Serrant","url":"https://coulee-de-serrant.com/en/history-of-the-coulee-of-serrant/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"La Coulée de Serrant: The charter of quality","url":"https://coulee-de-serrant.com/en/charter/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vins de Loire: All about the Coulée de Serrant","url":"https://www.vinsdeloire.fr/en/loire-wines/coulee-serrant/all-about-coulee-serrant","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Clos de la Coulée de Serrant","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clos_de_la_Coul%C3%A9e_de_Serrant","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bibliothèque nationale de France: Curnonsky, authority record","url":"https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/cb11898316p","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-wu2i3mzg": {"id":"fl-wu2i3mzg","key":"dagueneau","group":"loire","region":"loire","producer":"Didier Dagueneau","producerId":"p-didier-dagueneau","say":"dee-DYAY dah-guh-NOH, see-LEX","place":"Saint-Andelain, Pouilly-Fumé, the Loire Valley","founded":"1982","wine":{"name":"Pouilly-Fumé Silex","appellation":"Pouilly-Fumé; Vin de France since the late 2010s","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc alone.","sites":"The domaine's old, low yielding parcels on silex, the flint stones in clay found mostly around Saint-Andelain, which give the wine its name and much of its smoke.","making":"Yields are kept low. The juice is fermented and raised in oak barrels of several sizes, among them the long, narrow casks called cigares, of the founder's design, which give the wine more contact with its lees. New oak, a large share in the early years, was cut back over time, to about a quarter by 2002, and the wine rests on its lees for months before bottling.","glass":"Gunflint, struck match and smoke over grapefruit, lemon zest and white peach, with a little acacia; dense and long, with high, ripe acidity and a stony, saline finish.","ageing":"Tight for its first five years, it then opens and keeps for fifteen to twenty, far longer than most Sauvignon Blanc.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a white Burgundy glass rather than a narrow one.","label":[{"term":"Pouilly-Fumé","means":"The appellation for Sauvignon Blanc around Pouilly-sur-Loire; never Pouilly-Fuissé, the Chardonnay of the Mâconnais."},{"term":"Silex","means":"The flint clay soil the vines grow on, not the name of a vineyard."},{"term":"Vin de France","means":"Printed in place of Pouilly-Fumé since the late 2010s, when the domaine began to bottle Silex outside the appellation."}]},"table":"Silex is Didier Dagueneau's Pouilly-Fumé from flint soils around Saint-Andelain: Sauvignon Blanc from low yields, fermented in barrel and rested on its lees. Gunflint and grapefruit lead, and it ages like a white Burgundy rather than a crisp Sauvignon.","story":"Didier Dagueneau (1956 to 2008) was born at Saint-Andelain into a family of growers, but before wine he raced motorcycle sidecars and drove sled dogs. In 1982 he set up an estate of his own rather than join the family's. Pouilly-Fumé was then often a thin, grassy white, made in steel and bottled early, and he set out to show what the place could give. He cut yields hard, spoke out against neighbours who overcropped, and raised his wines in oak, a large share of it new at first, with long, narrow casks made to his own design. The flint parcels gave Silex, the bottle that made his name, and beside it came Pur Sang and Buisson Renard, and in the far south west a sweet Jurançon, Les Jardins de Babylone. On 17 September 2008 he died when his microlight crashed near Cognac, and his son took the domaine on. Since the late 2010s the domaine has bottled Silex as Vin de France, outside the appellation, though it comes from the same flint. Silex is the bottle that proves Sauvignon Blanc can age: a Loire white to set beside a grand cru Chablis, never a crisp glass for the terrace.","others":[{"name":"Pouilly-Fumé Pur Sang","note":"Another Sauvignon Blanc of the domaine, its name French for thoroughbred, rounder and more open than Silex."},{"name":"Pouilly-Fumé Buisson Renard","note":"From a parcel of its own at Saint-Andelain, the most exotic wine of the range, ripe and exuberant."},{"name":"Les Jardins de Babylone","note":"Sweet Jurançon of Petit Manseng from the domaine's vines in the far south west of France."}],"traps":["Pouilly-Fumé is Loire Sauvignon Blanc; Pouilly-Fuissé is Mâconnais Chardonnay.","Pouilly-sur-Loire is a separate appellation on the same ground, made from Chasselas.","Since the late 2010s Silex has been labelled Vin de France, not Pouilly-Fumé: the domaine bottles it outside the appellation, from the same flint."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-pqys6hf8","front":"Didier Dagueneau's Silex: what does the name mean?","back":"Flint: the clay with flint stones in which the vines grow at Saint-Andelain, not a vineyard's name."},{"id":"fl-c53tngft","front":"When did Didier Dagueneau found his Pouilly-Fumé domaine, and when did he die?","back":"He founded it in 1982 and died in a microlight crash in 2008; his son took it on."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-j3vedwq4","q":"A guest asks for Pouilly-Fuissé and points to Dagueneau's Silex on the list. What do you explain?","opts":["Silex is a Mâconnais Chardonnay","Silex is a Sancerre from Chavignol","Silex is Pouilly-Fumé, Loire Sauvignon Blanc","Silex is Chasselas from Pouilly-sur-Loire"],"a":2,"exp":"Silex is Pouilly-Fumé, Sauvignon Blanc on flint at Saint-Andelain; Pouilly-Fuissé is Chardonnay of the Mâconnais, and Pouilly-sur-Loire is Chasselas.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-fgfzca60","q":"Didier Dagueneau founded his domaine in 1982. What set his Pouilly-Fumé apart from most of the appellation then?","opts":["Steel tanks and early bottling for freshness","Grapes bought from across the Centre-Loire","A share of Chardonnay in the blend","Low yields and ageing in oak, much of it new"],"a":3,"exp":"He cut yields and raised his wines in oak, much of it new, when almost all Pouilly-Fumé was made in steel and bottled early; the appellation allows Sauvignon Blanc alone.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Didier Dagueneau","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Didier_Dagueneau","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Didier Dagueneau Silex Pouilly-Fumé 2002","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-didier-dagueneau-silex-pouilly-fume-2002-441382/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Didier Dagueneau dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/didier-dagueneau-dies-77821/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"SommSelect: Behind the wine, Didier Dagueneau","url":"https://sommselect.com/blogs/tasting-notes/behind-the-wine-didier-dagueneau","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Alliance Wine: Domaine Didier Dagueneau, Silex Vin de France 2023","url":"https://www.alliancewine.com/domaine-didier-dagueneau/silex-vin-de-france-2023-6405","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-y78yxzbu": {"id":"fl-y78yxzbu","key":"clos-rougeard","group":"loire","region":"loire","producer":"Clos Rougeard","producerId":"p-clos-rougeard","say":"kloh roo-ZHAR, luh BOOR","place":"Chacé, Saumur-Champigny, the Loire Valley","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Saumur-Champigny Le Bourg","appellation":"Saumur-Champigny","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Franc alone.","sites":"A small parcel of old vines near the village of Chacé, on limestone over the tuffeau, the soft white chalk of Saumur.","making":"Picked by hand, the grapes are made in the estate's old cellars cut into the tuffeau. Le Bourg is raised for eighteen months to two years in new oak barriques, where Les Poyeux goes to barrels a year old from Bordeaux châteaux and Le Clos to older wood, and it is bottled without fining or filtering.","glass":"Raspberry, black cherry and violet over graphite, tobacco leaf and a little cedar, the green pepper of Cabernet Franc held in check; fine, firm tannin and a long, silky finish.","ageing":"Firm when young, it needs ten years and keeps for twenty and more; old vintages take on the silk of mature Burgundy.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Saumur-Champigny","means":"The appellation for red wine, mainly Cabernet Franc, from the villages south east of Saumur."},{"term":"Le Bourg","means":"The name of the parcel, a single old vineyard, not a village or a rank."}]},"table":"Le Bourg is Clos Rougeard's old vine Cabernet Franc from Chacé in Saumur-Champigny, the one red of the estate raised in new oak, for up to two years. It shows raspberry, graphite and silk, and wants ten years in the cellar before it is poured.","story":"The Foucault family worked vines at Chacé, south east of Saumur, for generations, a line usually traced to 1664, on limestone over the tuffeau whose cellars run beneath the village. While much of Saumur-Champigny was a light red to drink young, the family kept slower ways: picking by hand, long ageing in barrel and bottling without fining or filtration. Two brothers of the family ran the estate for decades and gave its three reds their shape: Le Clos, the estate wine, raised in older barrels; Les Poyeux, the perfumed parcel on sandier soil, in barrels a year old bought from Bordeaux châteaux; and Le Bourg, the old vines, in new oak for eighteen months to two years. One of the brothers died in December 2015, and in 2017, after months of reports, the family sold the estate to the Bouygues family, who already owned Château Montrose in Saint-Estèphe. Le Bourg is the bottle that showed Loire Cabernet Franc could be laid down beside Bordeaux and Burgundy, and the reference point for the Loire reds that followed.","others":[{"name":"Saumur-Champigny Les Poyeux","note":"The perfumed parcel, on sandier soil over clay and limestone, raised in barrels a year old."},{"name":"Saumur-Champigny Le Clos","note":"The estate wine from several parcels, raised in older barrels; the Clos Rougeard most guests will meet."},{"name":"Saumur Blanc Brézé","note":"Chenin Blanc from the limestone hill of Brézé, fermented in barrel."}],"traps":["Le Bourg and Les Poyeux are separate parcels, not a village and a cru above it.","Saumur-Champigny is a Cabernet Franc appellation, not a Cabernet Sauvignon one.","Brézé is a hill and a commune of Saumur, and its wine is a white Saumur, not a Saumur-Champigny red."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-t38znzz6","front":"Where is Clos Rougeard, and in which appellation?","back":"At Chacé, south east of Saumur, in Saumur-Champigny, on limestone over the tuffeau."},{"id":"fl-wbugja1p","front":"Clos Rougeard's Le Bourg: how is it raised differently from the estate's other reds?","back":"In new oak barriques, where Les Poyeux goes to barrels a year old and Le Clos to older wood."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-4x57bvgf","q":"A guest asks what grape makes Clos Rougeard's Le Bourg. Which answer fits?","opts":["Cabernet Franc alone","Cabernet Franc with Merlot","Pinot Noir, as in Sancerre","Cabernet Sauvignon alone"],"a":0,"exp":"Saumur-Champigny is a Cabernet Franc appellation, and Le Bourg is Cabernet Franc alone; Pinot Noir is the red grape of Sancerre.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-gvc3wdi5","q":"Which family farmed Clos Rougeard at Chacé for generations before the estate's sale in 2017?","opts":["The Huet family","The Foucault family","The Dagueneau family","The Joly family"],"a":1,"exp":"The Foucaults farmed Clos Rougeard until the sale of 2017; the Jolys are Savennières, the Huets Vouvray and the Dagueneaus Pouilly-Fumé.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Decanter: Clos Rougeard, a profile of the estate","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/clos-rougeard-410437/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Clos Rougeard sold to the Bouygues brothers","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/clos-rougeard-sold-loire-bouygues-brothers-371476/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"iDealwine: Clos Rougeard officially purchased by the Bouygues brothers","url":"https://www.idealwine.info/clos-rougeard-has-officially-been-purchased-by-the-bouygues-brothers/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vins et Millésimes: how Clos Rougeard's wines are made and aged","url":"https://www.vinsetmillesimes.com/en/faq/faqs/comment-les-vins-du-clos-rougeard-sont-ils-elabores-et-combien-de-temps-sont-ils-eleves-en-fut-","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"SommSelect: Behind the wine, Clos Rougeard","url":"https://sommselect.com/blogs/tasting-notes/behind-the-wine-clos-rougeard","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-4w0urz33": {"id":"fl-4w0urz33","key":"alphonse-mellot","group":"loire","region":"loire","producer":"Alphonse Mellot","producerId":"p-alphonse-mellot","say":"al-FONSS meh-LOH, lah moo-SYAIR","place":"Sancerre, Centre-Loire, the Loire Valley","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Sancerre La Moussière","appellation":"Sancerre","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc alone.","sites":"Domaine La Moussière, the family's home vineyard on the slopes just west of the hilltop town of Sancerre, facing south and south west, on marl over Kimmeridgian limestone.","making":"The biodynamic grapes come from low yields and are picked by hand. The wine is fermented partly in oak barrels, new ones among them, and partly in vats, and kept on its fine lees for several months, which gives it more weight than most Sancerre, before it is bottled.","glass":"Grapefruit, white peach and lemon zest over flint, chalk and a little blackcurrant leaf; fuller than most Sancerre, with fresh acidity and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Bright and open within two years of the vintage, it keeps well for five to eight.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass with a tulip bowl.","label":[{"term":"Sancerre","means":"The appellation of the hills around the town: white from Sauvignon Blanc, red and rosé from Pinot Noir."},{"term":"La Moussière","means":"The family's home domaine beside the town, a vineyard name and not a classified cru."},{"term":"Alphonse Mellot","means":"The name of the house, carried by the eldest son of each generation since the nineteenth century."}]},"table":"Sancerre with more weight than most, La Moussière is Alphonse Mellot's home domaine beside the town: Sauvignon Blanc farmed biodynamically, fermented partly in oak and given months on its lees. It has grapefruit, white peach and flint; serve it cool, not icy.","story":"The Mellot family traces its records as growers and merchants at Sancerre to 1513, and its history tells of an ancestor whom Louis XIV (1638 to 1715) chose in 1698 as an adviser on wine. Early in the nineteenth century an Alphonse Mellot opened an inn in the town, where he poured the local wines, and by family custom the eldest son of each generation has carried his first name since, so the name on the label has passed from father to son for two centuries. The heart of the estate is Domaine La Moussière, the vineyard on marl over Kimmeridgian limestone just west of the town, and its Sauvignon Blanc carries the name. Where most Sancerre is made in steel to be drunk young, the family farms biodynamically and ferments part of its white in oak before months on the lees. Beside it the family bottles an old vine white, Cuvée Edmond, a selection named Génération XIX for the nineteenth generation, and Pinot Noir from single parcels, which shows Sancerre as a red appellation too. La Moussière is the family's signature on a list: a Sancerre with weight and length, a corrective to the steel bright picture of the appellation.","others":[{"name":"Sancerre Cuvée Edmond","note":"Old vine Sauvignon Blanc, from vines of forty to eighty years and more, the estate's fuller white."},{"name":"Sancerre Génération XIX","note":"A selection named for the nineteenth generation of the family."},{"name":"Sancerre En Grands Champs","note":"Single parcel Pinot Noir, a Sancerre rouge made with Burgundian care."}],"traps":["The eldest son of each generation is called Alphonse, so several Alphonse Mellots appear across the vintages.","La Moussière is the domaine's vineyard name, not a classified cru: Sancerre has none.","Sancerre makes red and rosé from Pinot Noir too, so ask which colour a guest means."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-0dgmvldm","front":"Alphonse Mellot's La Moussière: what is it?","back":"The family's home domaine just west of the hilltop town of Sancerre, and its Sauvignon Blanc of that name."},{"id":"fl-khiqktwu","front":"Why do so many generations of this Sancerre family carry the name Alphonse Mellot?","back":"By family custom the eldest son of each generation takes the first name of the Alphonse who opened an inn in Sancerre early in the nineteenth century."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-tcr53v79","q":"A guest likes Alphonse Mellot's La Moussière and asks for the red of the same appellation. Which grape will it be?","opts":["Gamay","Malbec, there called Côt","Pinot Noir","Cabernet Franc"],"a":2,"exp":"Red Sancerre is Pinot Noir; Cabernet Franc makes the reds of Saumur, Chinon and Bourgueil, and Côt is the Touraine name for Malbec.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-bnieuzzk","q":"By its own account, since which century has the Mellot family made wine at Sancerre?","opts":["The eighteenth","The twelfth","The twentieth","The sixteenth"],"a":3,"exp":"The family dates its records to 1513, in the sixteenth century; an ancestor is said to have been chosen by Louis XIV as an adviser on wine in 1698.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"iDealwine: Sancerre, Alphonse Mellot in its nineteenth generation","url":"https://www.idealwine.info/?p=5601","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinatis: Domaine Alphonse Mellot","url":"https://www.vinatis.com/maisons/domaine-alphonse-mellot","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vins et Millésimes: Alphonse Mellot","url":"https://vinsetmillesimes.com/en/6304-alphonse-mellot-","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Hachette Vins: Alphonse Mellot","url":"https://www.hachette-vins.com/guide-vins/producteurs/16085/alphonse-mellot/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Millesima: Alphonse Mellot producer page","url":"https://www.millesima-usa.com/producer-alphonse-mellot.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-u2r9nhxn": {"id":"fl-u2r9nhxn","key":"trimbach","group":"alsace","region":"alsace","producer":"Trimbach","producerId":"p-trimbach","say":"TRIM-bahk, kloh sant OON","place":"Ribeauvillé, with the clos at Hunawihr, Alsace","founded":"1626","wine":{"name":"Riesling Clos Sainte Hune","appellation":"Alsace","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone, as Alsace law asks of any wine labelled with one grape.","sites":"Clos Sainte Hune, a parcel of the Rosacker Grand Cru on the slope above Hunawihr, held by the family for more than two centuries: marly limestone over Muschelkalk and Lettenkohle bedrock.","making":"Fermented dry over three or four weeks in temperature controlled stainless steel, or in old casks when the year and the volume of juice call for it, with no malolactic conversion, and bottled in the spring after the harvest. The bottles then rest in the house's cellars at Ribeauvillé for some five years before release.","glass":"Lime peel, green apple and white peach over crushed limestone and a smoky, saline edge; bone dry, taut and long. Age brings petrol, beeswax, dried lemon and toast.","ageing":"Closed and severe for its first decade, it opens slowly after ten years and keeps for thirty and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, never an old one; a wide white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Clos Ste Hune","means":"The parcel's name as the label has printed it since the 1919 vintage, Ste for Sainte, after the saint of Hunawihr."},{"term":"Alsace","means":"The regional appellation: the label names no Grand Cru, though the parcel lies inside the Rosacker."},{"term":"Riesling","means":"The grape, and the whole of the wine, as an Alsace varietal label requires."}]},"table":"From a family parcel inside the Rosacker Grand Cru at Hunawihr comes Trimbach's Clos Sainte Hune, a Riesling whose label names only Alsace. Bone dry and held five years before release, it is taut and stony young, so offer it with ten years behind it.","story":"Riquewihr is where the Trimbach story starts: in 1626, by the house's own account, a Trimbach from Sainte-Marie-aux-Mines was received as a citizen of the town and took up the vine there. The family moved its house to Ribeauvillé, a few kilometres north, in 1920. Between the two towns lies Hunawihr, named, as the clos is, for Saint Hune, a noblewoman of the seventh century who, the legend goes, washed the clothes of the poor; she was made a saint in 1520. The family has held its parcel above the village for more than two centuries; the first label to bear the clos's name, of a design unchanged since, dates from the 1919 vintage. The appellation Alsace came in 1962 and the Grand Cru tier in 1975, and the hill around the clos was later classified as Grand Cru Rosacker. Trimbach chose to keep that name off the label and sell the wine as Alsace under the clos's own name, dry, with no malolactic conversion, and only after some five years in its cellars. The family bought vines on the Schlossberg in 2012, and that wine has carried the Grand Cru name since the 2014 vintage; Clos Sainte Hune never has. Its label shows plainly that a parcel's own name can say more than its appellation.","others":[{"name":"Riesling Cuvée Frédéric Emile","note":"Riesling from the Geisberg and Osterberg Grand Cru slopes above Ribeauvillé, labelled simply Alsace."},{"name":"Gewurztraminer Cuvée des Seigneurs de Ribeaupierre","note":"A dry Gewurztraminer named for the old lords of Ribeauvillé, drier than the grape's reputation suggests."},{"name":"Riesling Réserve","note":"The house Riesling, and the measure of its dry style."}],"traps":["Clos Sainte Hune lies inside the Rosacker Grand Cru, yet its label names only the appellation Alsace.","The house stands at Ribeauvillé, while Clos Sainte Hune lies at Hunawihr, the next village to the south.","Cuvée Frédéric Emile is a different Trimbach Riesling, from Grand Cru slopes above Ribeauvillé, also labelled simply Alsace."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-m2x0bye3","front":"Trimbach's Clos Sainte Hune lies inside which Grand Cru, and does its label say so?","back":"The Rosacker at Hunawihr; the label names only the appellation Alsace."},{"id":"fl-t814w5gc","front":"How is Trimbach's Clos Sainte Hune made, and when is it released?","back":"Fermented dry with no malolactic conversion and bottled the spring after the harvest; it then rests some five years in the cellar before release."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-58worj4g","q":"A guest reads Alsace, Riesling and Clos Ste Hune on a Trimbach label and asks why no Grand Cru appears. What do you say?","opts":["The house leaves the Rosacker name off by choice","The parcel lies on the plain outside every Grand Cru","The wine is a blend, which a Grand Cru forbids","A Grand Cru label may not name Riesling"],"a":0,"exp":"Clos Sainte Hune lies inside Grand Cru Rosacker, but Trimbach labels it Alsace by choice; Riesling is one of the four noble grapes every Grand Cru admits.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-mndy8yh1","q":"Trimbach dates its family's record in wine to the year a Trimbach was received as a citizen of Riquewihr. Which year?","opts":["1639","1626","1947","1898"],"a":1,"exp":"Trimbach counts from 1626; 1639 is Hugel's arrival at Riquewihr, 1898 the Faller family's purchase of Weinbach and 1947 the founding of Marcel Deiss.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Trimbach: Our history","url":"https://www.trimbach.fr/en/notre-histoire","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Trimbach: All about Clos Sainte Hune","url":"https://www.trimbach.fr/all-about-clos-sainte-hune/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Trimbach Clos Ste-Hune 1990","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-trimbach-clos-ste-hune-1990-297948","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Palm Bay International: a hundred years of Clos Ste. Hune","url":"https://www.palmbay.com/pbi-journal/celebrating-100-years-of-the-most-wanted-riesling-in-the-world/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Hunna","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunna","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9ymqs7jc": {"id":"fl-9ymqs7jc","key":"hugel","group":"alsace","region":"alsace","producer":"Hugel","producerId":"p-hugel","say":"HOO-gul, geh-VURTS-trah-mee-ner","place":"Riquewihr, Haut-Rhin, Alsace","founded":"1639","wine":{"name":"Gewurztraminer Vendange Tardive","appellation":"Alsace","classification":"Vendanges Tardives, a legal late harvest mention of the Alsace appellation, defined in 1984","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Gewurztraminer alone, picked late and very ripe.","sites":"The house's oldest vines, in its parcels of the Sporen Grand Cru just outside Riquewihr, on clay and marl of the Lias, decalcified at the surface; the label names no cru.","making":"Picked by hand in a late pass at or above the legal minimum ripeness, with no sugar added, the harvest declared in advance and checked by the authorities. The juice settles for a few hours and ferments in temperature controlled casks or vats; racked once before winter, the wine clears naturally and is bottled in the spring.","glass":"Lychee, rose petal and candied ginger over quince paste, orange peel and acacia honey; rich and unctuous, its sweetness lifted by a faint bitter grip at the finish.","ageing":"Open and perfumed on release, it deepens for ten to twenty years in a good vintage, turning towards dried apricot and saffron.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a small white wine glass and a modest pour, with foie gras, Munster or a fruit tart.","label":[{"term":"Vendange Tardive","means":"Late harvest of a noble grape at a set minimum sugar, with no chaptalisation; rich, or at times nearly dry."},{"term":"Gewurztraminer","means":"The grape, written in Alsace without the German umlaut; Gewürz is German for spice."},{"term":"Alsace","means":"The appellation; the late harvest words are a mention added to it, never an appellation of their own."}]},"table":"Late picked Gewurztraminer from the house's oldest vines in the Sporen, made with no added sugar, is what Vendange Tardive means at Hugel, the family that drafted the rules made law in 1984. Pour it for its lychee, rose and ginger, rich enough to meet foie gras.","story":"The Thirty Years War had not yet ended when a Swiss-born Hugel settled at Riquewihr in 1639, by the family's own account, and was made a freeman of the town; his descendants have made wine there ever since. Their cellar keeps a cask of 1715, the Sainte Catherine, which the house says Guinness has recognised as the oldest working cask in the world. After the hot summer of 1976, Jean Hugel (1924 to 2009), known as Johnny, is credited with first putting the words Vendange Tardive on late picked wines, words that then had no meaning in law, and he drafted rules to give them one. In 1984 French law defined both late harvest terms: the four noble grapes alone, a minimum sugar for each, the picking declared in advance and checked, and no sugar added. Gewurztraminer and Pinot Gris must reach a higher minimum than Riesling and Muscat. Hugel long left its Grand Cru names off its labels, so its late harvest Gewurztraminer speaks of Riquewihr and the year, though it grows in the Sporen. When the 2021 vintage brought a compulsory sweetness mention to Alsace whites, the two late harvest terms were spared it. Every bottle is the law of 1984 at work: late picked, nothing added, its sweetness the grape's own.","others":[{"name":"Gewurztraminer Sélection de Grains Nobles","note":"From berries touched by noble rot; always sweet, and made only in years that bring it, which 1990, for one, did not."},{"name":"Riesling Schoelhammer","note":"A single parcel of the Schoenenbourg slope, released only in selected years."}],"traps":["A Vendange Tardive can finish nearly dry, while a Sélection de Grains Nobles is always sweet.","Vendange Tardive and Sélection de Grains Nobles are mentions on the Alsace appellation, limited to the four noble grapes, not appellations of their own.","Hugel has long left the Grand Cru names of the Sporen and the Schoenenbourg off its labels, so a Hugel late harvest names no cru."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-gr2xydbd","front":"What did the French law of 1984, from rules a Hugel drafted, define?","back":"Vendange Tardive and Sélection de Grains Nobles: noble grapes only, a minimum sugar for each, picking declared in advance and no added sugar."},{"id":"fl-7w2qe2mr","front":"Hugel's Gewurztraminer Vendange Tardive: may the grower add sugar to the juice?","back":"No: chaptalisation is forbidden for both late harvest mentions, so the sweetness is the grape's own."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-slctuwmk","q":"A guest compares Hugel's Gewurztraminer Vendange Tardive with its Sélection de Grains Nobles. Which statement holds?","opts":["The Sélection de Grains Nobles may be made from Pinot Blanc","Both must be made from botrytised berries alone","The Vendange Tardive may be chaptalised in a cool year","Only the Sélection de Grains Nobles must always be sweet"],"a":3,"exp":"A Sélection de Grains Nobles is always sweet, while a Vendange Tardive may finish nearly dry; neither may be chaptalised, and both are limited to the four noble grapes.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-t2jmkeh1","q":"In which year did French law, after rules drafted by a son of the Hugel family, define the Alsace late harvest terms?","opts":["2007","1975","1984","1962"],"a":2,"exp":"Both late harvest terms were defined in 1984; 1962 is the Alsace appellation, 1975 the first Grand Cru and 2007 the admission of Kaefferkopf as the fifty-first.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Famille Hugel: since 1639 in Riquewihr","url":"https://m.hugel.com/598DGMB3C4","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Famille Hugel: Gewurztraminer Vendange Tardive 2010","url":"https://m.hugel.com/PWUIDE","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Vendange tardive","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vendange_tardive","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Johnny Hugel dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/johnny-hugel-dies-69759/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Hugel Riesling SGN 1976","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-hugel-riesling-sgn-1976-alsace-france-334805","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-tjpt1wjd": {"id":"fl-tjpt1wjd","key":"zind-humbrecht","group":"alsace","region":"alsace","producer":"Domaine Zind-Humbrecht","producerId":"p-zind-humbrecht","say":"tsint HOOM-brekt, RAHNG-en duh TAHN","place":"Turckheim, with the Rangen at Thann, Alsace","founded":"1959","wine":{"name":"Riesling Rangen de Thann Clos Saint Urbain","appellation":"Alsace Grand Cru Rangen","classification":"Alsace Grand Cru, from the southernmost of the fifty-one sites","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"Clos Saint Urbain, the domaine's monopole within the Rangen above Thann, around a chapel of Saint Urbain built in the fifteenth century: volcanic greywacke and tuff on a very steep slope above the river Thur.","making":"Whole bunches are pressed slowly and the juice ferments with its own yeasts in old oak foudres, often for months, and it may stop before all its sugar is gone, leaving the touch of sweetness that the index on the label reports.","glass":"Smoke, struck flint and crushed dark rock over lime, quince and candied ginger; dense and powerful, at times with a touch of sweetness, and a long, almost salty finish.","ageing":"Smoky and closed for its first five to eight years, it then opens and lasts twenty years and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Rangen de Thann","means":"The Grand Cru above Thann, volcanic and very steep, the southernmost in Alsace."},{"term":"Clos Saint Urbain","means":"The domaine's monopole inside the Rangen, named for the fifteenth century chapel of Saint Urbain among its vines."},{"term":"Indice","means":"The domaine's own sweetness scale on the label, from 1 for dry to 5 for sweet; not a legal category."}]},"table":"On the volcanic Rangen above Thann, the southernmost Grand Cru of Alsace, Zind-Humbrecht farms Clos Saint Urbain, and its Riesling smells of smoke and struck flint. It can keep a touch of sugar, and the domaine's own sweetness index on the label says how much.","story":"Two families' vines became one domaine in 1959, when a Humbrecht grower of Gueberschwihr, from a family that had tended vines there since 1620 by its own account, married a daughter of the Zind family of Wintzenheim, and the domaine took both names. Far to the south, above Thann, the Rangen had almost fallen out of use: by the end of the 1960s phylloxera, two world wars and the toil of its slopes had left only a small part of the hill in vine. In 1977 the family bought Clos Saint Urbain there, named for the chapel among its vines, and replanted the neglected slope. The Rangen was classified Grand Cru in 1983, and in 1989 the founders' son became France's first Master of Wine. The domaine came to be known for very low yields, slow ferments with the grapes' own yeasts in old casks and, from 1997, biodynamic farming, certified in 2002. With so many of those ferments stopping short of dryness, it began printing its own sweetness index, from 1 to 5, on its labels, years before Alsace law made a sweetness mention compulsory with the 2021 vintage. Clos Saint Urbain puts the Rangen in one bottle: volcanic rock, a hand worked slope and a label that warns the guest how sweet the glass will be.","others":[{"name":"Riesling Clos Windsbuhl","note":"A high, cool limestone clos at Hunawihr, slow to ripen and slow to open."},{"name":"Pinot Gris Clos Jebsal","note":"A warm gypsum amphitheatre that regularly gives Vendange Tardive."},{"name":"Gewurztraminer Grand Cru Hengst","note":"Marl and limestone at Wintzenheim, the domaine's most powerful Gewurztraminer."}],"traps":["The sweetness index on the label is the domaine's own scale, not a legal category.","The Rangen is at Thann, volcanic and the southernmost Grand Cru, not a site near Colmar.","A Grand Cru wine can finish sweet: the cru names the place and says nothing about residual sugar."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-hi11x39b","front":"On what rock does Zind-Humbrecht's Clos Saint Urbain grow, and above which town?","back":"Volcanic greywacke and tuff on the steep Rangen, above Thann on the river Thur."},{"id":"fl-5b04e3lf","front":"What does the index on a Zind-Humbrecht label tell a guest?","back":"How sweet the wine is, on the domaine's own scale from 1 to 5; it is not a legal category."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-oce22lyp","q":"A guest asks why Zind-Humbrecht's Rangen de Thann Clos Saint Urbain smells of smoke and struck flint. Which reason fits?","opts":["Botrytis from the river mists of the plain","Volcanic rock on the steep Rangen above Thann","Granite at the mouth of the Kaysersberg valley","New oak barrels charred before filling"],"a":1,"exp":"The Rangen is the volcanic Grand Cru, and its rock gives the smoky, flinty note; the domaine uses old foudres, not new oak, and granite is the Schlossberg's.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-9dbntiur","q":"In 1959 a marriage joined two Alsace families' vines into one domaine that took both their names. Which domaine?","opts":["Zind-Humbrecht","Weinbach","Marcel Deiss","Trimbach"],"a":0,"exp":"Zind-Humbrecht took both family names in 1959; the Faller family bought Weinbach in 1898, Marcel Deiss was founded in 1947 and Trimbach counts from 1626.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Domaine Zind-Humbrecht","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domaine_Zind-Humbrecht","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinous: Zind-Humbrecht's Pinot Gris Clos Saint Urbain Rangen de Thann, 1983 to 2013","url":"https://vinous.com/articles/zind-humbrecht-s-pinot-gris-clos-saint-urbain-rangen-de-thann-1983-2013-jun-2016","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The World of Fine Wine: Rangen de Thann, Alsace's explosive Grand Cru","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/homepage-featured-articles/rangen-de-thann-alsace-explosive-grand-cru","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: The Decanter interview","url":"https://www.decanter.com/interviews/the-decanter-interview-1-41472/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Biodyvin: Domaine Zind-Humbrecht member page","url":"https://www.biodyvin.com/en/our-members/98-domaine-zind-humbrecht.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-3ojg9rsw": {"id":"fl-3ojg9rsw","key":"weinbach","group":"alsace","region":"alsace","producer":"Domaine Weinbach","producerId":"p-weinbach","say":"VINE-bahk, SHLOSS-bairg","place":"Kaysersberg, below the Schlossberg, Alsace","founded":"1898","wine":{"name":"Riesling Grand Cru Schlossberg Cuvée Sainte Catherine","appellation":"Alsace Grand Cru Schlossberg","classification":"Alsace Grand Cru, from the Schlossberg, the first site classified, in 1975","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"Old vines on the lower part of the Schlossberg, the steep granite hill above Kientzheim and Kaysersberg, where the wines come out fuller than higher up; the walled Clos des Capucins lies at its foot.","making":"A selection of old vines from the domaine's Schlossberg parcels. Pressed gently and gradually, the juice ferments slowly with its own yeasts alone in the old oak foudres of the domaine's cellar, by the domaine's own account of its method.","glass":"Ripe peach, lemon curd and orange zest over crushed granite and white pepper, with a hint of rose; dry, broad and rich, yet driven by firm acidity.","ageing":"Charming young, it gains depth for ten to fifteen years, and a strong year keeps for twenty and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant only a young bottle, and briefly; a wide white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Grand Cru Schlossberg","means":"The granite Grand Cru of Kaysersberg and Kientzheim, the first classified, in 1975."},{"term":"Cuvée Sainte Catherine","means":"A house name, not a vineyard: the domaine's selection of old vines on the lower Schlossberg."},{"term":"Clos des Capucins","means":"The walled vineyard around the domaine's house, recorded since 890, where Capuchin friars built their house in 1612."}]},"table":"Domaine Weinbach's Cuvée Sainte Catherine is a house name, not a site: Riesling from old vines low on the granite Schlossberg, the first Grand Cru of Alsace. It is fuller than the domaine's other Schlossberg wines, with ripe peach and lemon held by firm acidity.","story":"The walled vineyard below the Schlossberg is recorded as early as 890, and in 1612, as the domaine tells it, Capuchin friars built their house there; the little stream that runs through it, the Weinbach, or wine stream, later gave the estate its name. The Revolution took the friars' clos and sold it as national property, and in 1898 two Faller brothers bought it and passed it on to the next generation of the family. The granite hill above became the first Grand Cru of Alsace in 1975. After the head of the family died in 1979, his widow and their two daughters carried the domaine on, and they built its name for ripe, perfumed wines called after the family rather than after sites: Cuvée Théo for the late husband, Cuvée Laurence and Cuvée Sainte Catherine, an old vine selection from the lower slope of the Schlossberg. Each cuvée is a selection of parcels within a site, fermented with its own yeasts in old foudres. The vineyards came to be farmed biodynamically, and the women of the family made the domaine a house that a guest knows by its first names. Hence the rule its labels teach, that in Alsace a cuvée may honour a person while only the cru tells you where the grapes grew.","others":[{"name":"Riesling Grand Cru Schlossberg","note":"The domaine's Schlossberg Riesling from higher on the hill, livelier than the Sainte Catherine."},{"name":"Gewurztraminer Grand Cru Furstentum","note":"Limestone at Kientzheim, the domaine's most aromatic Grand Cru wine."},{"name":"Pinot Gris Altenbourg","note":"A lieu dit rather than a Grand Cru, often bottled as Vendange Tardive."}],"traps":["Cuvée Théo, Cuvée Laurence and Cuvée Sainte Catherine are house names, not vineyards.","The Schlossberg was the first Alsace Grand Cru, in 1975; the walled Clos des Capucins at its foot is not Grand Cru.","Altenbourg is a lieu dit, not a Grand Cru."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-zaxm9ult","front":"Domaine Weinbach's Cuvée Sainte Catherine: from which Grand Cru, and on what rock?","back":"The Schlossberg above Kaysersberg and Kientzheim, on granite."},{"id":"fl-4fe2o9nb","front":"Which friars built their house in Domaine Weinbach's walled clos in 1612, and who bought it in 1898?","back":"The Capuchins; two Faller brothers bought it in 1898, after the Revolution had sold it as national property."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-dbdm3izr","q":"A guest sees Cuvée Sainte Catherine on a Domaine Weinbach Riesling and asks which vineyard that is. What do you say?","opts":["A walled vineyard beside the Capuchin friary","A Grand Cru named for a chapel of the saint","A house name for old vines low on the Schlossberg","A lieu dit on the limestone of Kientzheim"],"a":2,"exp":"Sainte Catherine is a house name for the domaine's old vines on the lower Schlossberg, not a site; the Clos des Capucins is the walled vineyard at the domaine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-6rnsizpa","q":"Which order of friars built its house in the walled vineyard that Domaine Weinbach calls home, long before the Faller family bought it?","opts":["The Carthusians","The Benedictines","The Cistercians","The Capuchins"],"a":3,"exp":"Capuchin friars built their house there in 1612, as the domaine tells it; the Cistercians walled Clos de Vougeot and the Steinberg, and the Benedictines held Johannisberg.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine Weinbach: Un domaine d'exception","url":"https://www.domaineweinbach.com/fr/le-domaine","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Weinbach: Le Clos des Capucins","url":"https://www.domaineweinbach.com/fr/les-terroirs/le-clos-des-capucins","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Weinbach: Cuvées, terroirs et grands crus","url":"https://www.domaineweinbach.com/fr/les-vins/cuvees-terroirs-grandscrus","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Weinbach: La cave","url":"https://www.domaineweinbach.com/fr/vinification","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Alsace wine matriarch Colette Faller dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/alsace-wine-matriarch-colette-faller-dies-2474","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-uuefcd50": {"id":"fl-uuefcd50","key":"marcel-deiss","group":"alsace","region":"alsace","producer":"Domaine Marcel Deiss","producerId":"p-marcel-deiss","say":"DICE, AHL-ten-bairg duh BAIRG-hime","place":"Bergheim, Haut-Rhin, Alsace","founded":"1947","wine":{"name":"Altenberg de Bergheim Grand Cru","appellation":"Alsace Grand Cru Altenberg de Bergheim","classification":"Alsace Grand Cru, one of the two whose rules admit a blend of grapes","colour":"white","grapes":"A field blend of many varieties planted together in the same rows, Riesling, Pinot Gris and Gewurztraminer chief among them, picked and fermented as one.","sites":"The domaine's parcels of the Altenberg above Bergheim, a south facing slope of hard Jurassic limestone over marl, with seams of pink sandstone and iron rich clay.","making":"The varieties of a parcel are picked together, pressed slowly as whole bunches, for as long as twelve hours, and fermented with their own yeasts in large old foudres, where the wine is raised for about a year. It often keeps some natural sugar.","glass":"Candied orange, quince and dried apricot over ginger, honeycomb and a salty, chalky grip; broad and textured, often a little sweet, with a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Rich and closed in its youth, it opens after five to eight years and lasts twenty years and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Altenberg de Bergheim","means":"The Grand Cru of Bergheim whose rules admit a blend, so the label names no grape."},{"term":"Grand Cru","means":"One of the fifty-one Alsace Grand Cru appellations, each an appellation of its own since 2011."}]},"table":"No grape appears on Domaine Marcel Deiss's Altenberg de Bergheim, because the Grand Cru admits a field blend: several varieties grown side by side, picked and fermented together. Rich, textured and often a touch sweet, it tastes of the hill before any one grape.","story":"The Deiss family had grown vines at Bergheim since 1744, but the domaine that carries the founder's name dates from 1947, when he came home from the Second World War and set up on his own, selling wine by grape, as Alsace did. His grandson took charge in 1973, after studying oenology, and came to argue that the grape was hiding the place: the old vineyards of Alsace, he held, had been planted with many varieties together, and the great hills were known by name long before anyone sold their wine by grape. He replanted parcels with mixed varieties, farmed them biodynamically and labelled them by site alone, at first against the rules, which asked a Grand Cru for a single noble grape. The argument won ground. In the 2000s the rules of the Altenberg de Bergheim were rewritten to admit a blend of the hill, and Kaefferkopf, admitted in 2007 as the fifty-first Grand Cru, took blends as well. When each Grand Cru became an appellation of its own in 2011, the Altenberg kept that right in its own rules. The domaine bottles the Altenberg, the Schoenenbourg and the Mambourg as field blends, each named for its hill. The Altenberg answers an old Alsace question: a Grand Cru may name its place and no grape at all.","others":[{"name":"Schoenenbourg","note":"The Riquewihr Grand Cru as a field blend, on marl and gypsum."},{"name":"Mambourg","note":"A field blend from the warm marl and limestone of Sigolsheim."},{"name":"Burg","note":"A lieu dit field blend that brings the domaine's argument to a wine below Grand Cru."}],"traps":["Alsace usually labels by grape, the whole wine of the named variety; Deiss names the place instead, by design.","Altenberg de Bergheim and Kaefferkopf admit blends and Zotzenberg admits Sylvaner; the other Grand Crus ask for a single noble grape.","Complantation means co-planting in the vineyard, not blending finished wines in the cellar."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-cjfq1y2y","front":"Marcel Deiss bottles a Grand Cru with no grape on its label. Which other Grand Cru admits a blend?","back":"Kaefferkopf, admitted in 2007 as the fifty-first."},{"id":"fl-d4lnzkcz","front":"What is complantation, the method behind Deiss's Altenberg de Bergheim?","back":"Several varieties planted together in the same rows, picked and fermented as one wine."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-iw9sqomt","q":"A guest notices that Marcel Deiss's Altenberg de Bergheim names no grape on its label. What do you explain?","opts":["Its Grand Cru admits a field blend of grapes grown together","It is declassified wine sold outside the appellation","It is Riesling, which an Alsace label may leave unnamed","It is a Crémant d'Alsace, which names no grape"],"a":0,"exp":"Altenberg de Bergheim is one of two Grand Crus whose rules admit a blend; an Alsace varietal label must name its grape, and a Crémant is sparkling.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qwc7bql1","q":"Domaine Marcel Deiss was set up at Bergheim after the Second World War. In which year?","opts":["1975","1947","1984","1959"],"a":1,"exp":"Deiss was founded in 1947; 1959 is the marriage that made Zind-Humbrecht, 1975 the first Grand Cru and 1984 the late harvest law.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Marcel Deiss","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcel_Deiss","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Hameau Albert: Domaine Marcel Deiss technical sheet","url":"https://www.hameaualbert.fr/sites/default/files/2019-09/FT%20Domaine%20Marcel%20Deiss.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Domaine Marcel Deiss, Altenberg de Bergheim Grand Cru","url":"https://skurnik.com/sku/alsace-grand-cru-blanc-altenberg-de-bergheim-domaine-marcel-deiss-3-2-2","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Les Grappes: Domaine Marcel Deiss, Alsace Grand Cru Altenberg de Bergheim","url":"https://www.lesgrappes.com/en/products/alsace-domainemarceldeiss-alsacegrandcrualtenbergdebergheim-blanc-2018","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Millesima: History of the Domaine Marcel Deiss","url":"https://www.millesima-usa.com/producer-domaine-marcel-deiss.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-4y4jwto3": {"id":"fl-4y4jwto3","key":"lapierre","group":"beaujolais","region":"beaujolais","producer":"Marcel Lapierre","producerId":"p-marcel-lapierre","say":"mar-SEL lah-PYAIR, mor-GOHN","place":"Villié-Morgon, Morgon, Beaujolais","founded":"1909","wine":{"name":"Morgon","appellation":"Morgon","classification":"One of the ten crus of Beaujolais, above Beaujolais and Beaujolais-Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Gamay alone, from old vines.","sites":"Old Gamay vines around Villié-Morgon, on the granite and schist of the cru, among them parcels on the Côte du Py, farmed organically and without herbicides.","making":"Picked by hand and carefully sorted, the grapes go into vat as whole bunches for the traditional semi carbonic maceration of the Beaujolais, ten to twenty one days on their own yeasts, with no sugar added and no sulphur in the vat. The wine is raised for about nine months in old 216 litre barrels and bottled unfiltered, some lots with a light dose of sulphur and the cuvée N with none.","glass":"Crushed raspberry, black cherry and violet over iron and a cool, stony note; juicy and silky, with light tannin, bright acidity and a pure, clean finish.","ageing":"Open and juicy within a year or two of the vintage, it keeps for about ten years, as the domaine gives it; keep a bottle without sulphur in a cool cellar.","serve":"13 to 15 degrees Celsius, lightly cool; no need to decant; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Morgon","means":"One of the ten crus, around Villié-Morgon; as a cru it need not print the word Beaujolais."},{"term":"Marcel Lapierre","means":"The domaine's name, kept by his son and daughter after his death in 2010."}]},"table":"Marcel Lapierre's Morgon is the wine of the domaine at the root of natural Beaujolais: old vine Gamay fermented as whole bunches on its own yeasts, with no sugar added and little or no sulphur. Pour it lightly cool for its crushed raspberry and violet.","story":"The Lapierre family's estate at Villié-Morgon dates, by its own account, from 1909, and Marcel Lapierre (1950 to 2010), born in the village, took it over from his father in 1973. In 1981, under the guidance of Jules Chauvet (1907 to 1989), a négociant and chemist of the Beaujolais whose research on yeasts and fermentation argued that sound grapes, carefully handled, needed little added, he changed course: he farmed organically, picked by hand and sorted hard, put whole bunches into vat, left the fermentation to the native yeasts, stopped adding sugar and used little or no sulphur. Neighbouring growers in Morgon worked the same way, and in America, where their importer gave them the name, the four became known as the Gang of Four, a nickname rather than a society. His Morgon found drinkers far beyond the Beaujolais, and the natural wine movement took it as a model. He died in October 2010, just after the harvest, and his son and daughter carried the domaine on as he had made it, bottling the Morgon in some years both with and without sulphur. The movement's argument fits in this one bottle: nothing added, and everything decided in the vineyard.","others":[{"name":"Cuvée Marcel Lapierre","note":"Morgon from century old vines on the Côte du Py and Le Douby, made only in good years."},{"name":"Raisins Gaulois","note":"A young vine red sold as Vin de France, made for drinking soon."}],"traps":["The Gang of Four was their American importer's nickname for four Morgon growers, not a body or a label.","Morgon is a cru, so the word Beaujolais need not appear on the label.","Raisins Gaulois is Vin de France from young vines, not the domaine's Morgon."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-fuibqb1b","front":"Marcel Lapierre's Morgon: what is left out in the cellar?","back":"Cultured yeast, added sugar and most or all of the sulphur; the whole bunches ferment on their own yeasts."},{"id":"fl-t01zh7ab","front":"Which négociant and chemist guided Marcel Lapierre from 1981 towards wine made with nothing added?","back":"Jules Chauvet, 1907 to 1989."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-lsq0ykfx","q":"A guest asks how Marcel Lapierre's Morgon is fermented. What do you say?","opts":["Destemmed fruit in new oak barrels","Cultured yeast and added sugar for strength","Whole bunches, semi carbonic, on native yeasts","Pressed at once, like a white wine"],"a":2,"exp":"Lapierre ferments whole bunches in the semi carbonic way on the grapes' own yeasts, with no sugar added; destemming and long maceration before barrel is the Burgundian way of Château des Jacques.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-sylz5l18","q":"Marcel Lapierre's way of making Morgon owed much to one man's teaching. Who was he?","opts":["Philip the Bold, Duke of Burgundy","Émile Peynaud, Bordeaux oenologist","Georges Duboeuf, the Nouveau merchant","Jules Chauvet, chemist and négociant"],"a":3,"exp":"Jules Chauvet's work on native yeasts and wine made without additions guided Lapierre from 1981; Duboeuf was a merchant of Nouveau, not a teacher of natural wine.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine Marcel Lapierre: The domaine","url":"https://www.marcel-lapierre.com/en/the-domaine/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Marcel Lapierre: Morgon technical sheet","url":"https://www.marcel-lapierre.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/Fiche-Tech-morgon-2020.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinous: Cellar Favorite, 2009 Marcel Lapierre Morgon Cuvée Marcel Lapierre","url":"https://vinous.com/articles/cellar-favorite-2009-marcel-lapierre-morgon-cuvee-marcel-lapierre-jan-2022","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Jules Chauvet","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jules_Chauvet","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Scholar Guild: The Beaujolais Gang of Four","url":"https://www.winescholarguild.com/blog/regional-spotlight/beaujolais-gang-of-four","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-n6t90zwa": {"id":"fl-n6t90zwa","key":"foillard","group":"beaujolais","region":"beaujolais","producer":"Jean Foillard","producerId":"p-jean-foillard","say":"fwah-YAR, koht dew PEE","place":"Villié-Morgon, Morgon, Beaujolais","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Morgon Côte du Py","appellation":"Morgon","classification":"One of the ten crus of Beaujolais, above Beaujolais and Beaujolais-Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Gamay, from old vines on the hill.","sites":"The Côte du Py, the hill at the heart of Morgon: old schist rich in iron oxide and manganese, weathered to the crumbly roche pourrie, rotten rock, of the growers, where the domaine's old vines grow.","making":"Whole bunches go into vat for a long, cool semi carbonic maceration of some three to four weeks on their own yeasts, with no sugar added and little or no sulphur; the wine is then raised for six to nine months in old barrels that once held Burgundy before bottling.","glass":"Dark cherry, blackberry and violet over wet slate, iron and a hint of kirsch; deeper and more savoury than most Gamay, with fine, firm tannin and a long, stony finish.","ageing":"Generous young, it keeps for ten years and more in a good vintage, taking on the kirsch and savour of aged Morgon.","serve":"14 to 16 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for half an hour; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Côte du Py","means":"The hill of decomposed schist at the heart of Morgon, printed as a climat after the cru's name."},{"term":"Morgon","means":"The cru around Villié-Morgon, with Moulin-à-Vent the one most often kept for years."}]},"table":"Old vines on Morgon's hill of rotten schist give Jean Foillard's Côte du Py its depth: whole bunches fermented cool with almost no sulphur, then raised in old Burgundy barrels. Darker and firmer than most Gamay, it rewards a decade in the cellar.","story":"Morgon's growers have an old verb for what their wine does with age: it morgonne, taking on a kirsch and a savour that set it apart from the lighter crus. At the heart of the cru rises the Côte du Py, a hill of old schist rich in manganese, weathered to a crumbly rock whose wines are slow to open, and Morgon became an appellation of its own by the decree of 11 September 1936. The grower whose name the domaine carries took over his father's vines at Villié-Morgon with his wife in 1980, by most accounts. Close to Marcel Lapierre (1950 to 2010) and to the teaching of the chemist Jules Chauvet (1907 to 1989), he farmed organically, gave up cultured yeast and added sugar, kept sulphur to a minimum and let whole bunches ferment slowly in the cool. In America, where their importer gave them the name, the four Morgon growers who worked this way became known as the Gang of Four. Foillard's Côte du Py, raised in old barrels that once held Burgundy, became for many drinkers the bottle that showed how deep and long lived Gamay made with little added could be. Open a ten year old bottle for the guest who says Beaujolais never ages: the hill, as much as the cellar, is what makes it last.","others":[{"name":"Morgon Corcelette","note":"From sandier granite within the cru, a lighter and more floral Morgon."},{"name":"Fleurie","note":"From the domaine's parcels in the neighbouring cru, perfumed and silky."}],"traps":["Côte du Py is decomposed schist, not the granite of most Beaujolais crus.","Côte du Py is a climat within Morgon, printed after the cru's name, not an appellation of its own.","Morgonner is the growers' verb for a Morgon that has taken on the kirsch and savour of age, not a way of making it."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-zfrctxxm","front":"Jean Foillard's Côte du Py: what rock lies under the vines?","back":"Old schist rich in iron oxide and manganese, weathered to the roche pourrie, rotten rock, of the growers."},{"id":"fl-m1vglqst","front":"What do Morgon growers mean when they say a wine morgonne?","back":"That with age it has taken on the kirsch and savour of an old Morgon; Foillard's Côte du Py is built for it."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-o72oic6s","q":"A guest doubts that Beaujolais can age, and you suggest Jean Foillard's Morgon Côte du Py. Which reason fits?","opts":["Old vines on weathered, manganese rich schist","Grapes left to raisin on straw mats","Long ageing in new oak barrels","Fruit picked early for sharp acidity"],"a":0,"exp":"The weathered schist of the Côte du Py gives deep, firm Gamay that ages for a decade; the domaine raises it in old barrels, not new, and picks ripe fruit, not early.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-kpdees6z","q":"The grower behind Jean Foillard's Côte du Py worked like three Morgon neighbours who gave up added sugar and most sulphur. What were the four called abroad?","opts":["The Chauvet Brothers","The Gang of Four","The Morgon Syndicate","The Côte du Py Society"],"a":1,"exp":"The Gang of Four was their American importer's nickname for four Morgon growers, Marcel Lapierre among them, not a body; Jules Chauvet was their teacher, not a relative.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"INAO: specification of the Morgon appellation","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/PNO-CDCMorgon-221130.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Inter Beaujolais: Morgon","url":"https://www.beaujolais.com/en/appellation/morgon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Mommessin: Morgon Côte du Py technical sheet","url":"https://ft.boisset.fr/en/mommessin.m-131/morgon-cote-du-py.v-9139.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Leon and Son: Domaine Jean Foillard Morgon Côte du Py 2024","url":"https://leonandsonwine.com/products/2024-domaine-jean-foillard-morgon-cote-du-py-beaujolais-france","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Infinivin: Domaine Jean Foillard","url":"https://www.infinivin.com/en/domaines/domaine-jean-foillard-80","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-qms8mm4d": {"id":"fl-qms8mm4d","key":"thivin","group":"beaujolais","region":"beaujolais","producer":"Château Thivin","producerId":"p-chateau-thivin","say":"tee-VAN, koht duh broo-YEE","place":"Odenas, Côte de Brouilly, Beaujolais","founded":"1877","wine":{"name":"Côte de Brouilly Les Sept Vignes","appellation":"Côte de Brouilly","classification":"One of the ten crus of Beaujolais, above Beaujolais and Beaujolais-Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Mostly Gamay, with a small share of Chardonnay for lightness, by the estate's own account.","sites":"Seven parcels on the east, south and west faces of Mont Brouilly, among them Clos Bertrand, La Chapelle and Godefroy, on the hard blue stone the growers call pierre bleue: stonier high on the hill, more clayey near its foot.","making":"Each of the seven parcels is fermented apart in the estate's hillside cellars, partly as whole bunches in the semi carbonic way, the fermentation finishing in large oak foudres; the wine is then raised in foudre for some seven months, and the parcels are blended in the spring.","glass":"Cherry, raspberry and violet over crushed stone and a peppery note; firm, fine tannin, bright acidity and a saline, slightly bitter finish.","ageing":"Open it from its second year, as the estate advises; it keeps and gains depth for five to ten years.","serve":"13 to 15 degrees Celsius, cool for a red; no need to decant; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Côte de Brouilly","means":"The cru of the slopes of Mont Brouilly itself, a different cru from Brouilly around its foot."},{"term":"Les Sept Vignes","means":"The seven vineyards: the estate's parcels around the hill, fermented apart and blended into this one wine."}]},"table":"Château Thivin blends seven parcels from the slopes of Mont Brouilly into its Côte de Brouilly Les Sept Vignes, mostly Gamay with a touch of Chardonnay, raised in large old casks. Pour it cool for its cherry, violet and crushed stone, with charcuterie or roast chicken.","story":"Mont Brouilly stands on its own at the southern end of the crus, a hill of hard blue stone among the granite. Shaken by the vine plagues of the nineteenth century, the growers raised a chapel on its summit in 1857, Notre-Dame du Raisin, to ask protection from the oidium that was ruining their vines. Twenty years later, on 8 June 1877, a grower from the nearby hamlet of Durette and his wife bought Château Thivin at a public sale, with a small holding of vines on the flank of the hill, and the Geoffray family's story on the hill begins there. The family worked vines on several faces of the hill, each ripening at its own pace. The growers of the slopes formed their own association in 1934, and the decree of 19 October 1938 gave them an appellation of their own, Côte de Brouilly, apart from the larger Brouilly that rings the foot of the hill. Thivin, fermenting parcel by parcel in its hillside cellars, showed what the hill gives Gamay: a firmer frame and a saline bite. Where Brouilly and Côte de Brouilly are confused, Thivin settles it: the hill and the ring around it taste different, and this is the hill.","others":[{"name":"Côte de Brouilly Cuvée Zaccharie","note":"The old vine bottling, raised in barrel, sharing its name with the buyer of 1877."},{"name":"Côte de Brouilly La Chapelle","note":"From high on the hill, near the chapel at the summit."}],"traps":["Brouilly and Côte de Brouilly are two crus: Côte de Brouilly is the hill, Brouilly the larger ring around its foot.","Côte de Brouilly lies chiefly on hard blue stone, the pierre bleue, where most crus lie on pink granite.","Les Sept Vignes is not Gamay alone: the estate blends a small share of Chardonnay into it."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-dnahzrpc","front":"Château Thivin's Côte de Brouilly: what rock lies under most of its vines?","back":"The hard blue stone of Mont Brouilly, the pierre bleue, of ancient volcanic origin, among the granite."},{"id":"fl-axuysm9d","front":"Where Château Thivin farms, which cru is the hill and which the ring around it?","back":"Côte de Brouilly is the hill of Mont Brouilly; Brouilly is the larger cru around its foot."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-gq42cdrz","q":"A guest orders Château Thivin's Côte de Brouilly expecting the Brouilly he drank last week. What do you tell him?","opts":["A Beaujolais-Villages from the hill's foot","The same cru under an older name","A separate cru, from the slopes of the hill","A Nouveau released before the Brouilly"],"a":2,"exp":"Côte de Brouilly is its own cru on the steep slopes of Mont Brouilly, given its appellation in 1938; Brouilly is the larger cru around the hill's foot.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-3t4z2dqk","q":"How did Château Thivin pass to the Geoffray family in 1877?","opts":["Inherited from the lords of Beaujeu","Given as a dowry by a Lyon merchant","Won from a neighbour in a wager","Bought at a public sale by a local grower"],"a":3,"exp":"On 8 June 1877 a grower from the hamlet of Durette bought Thivin at a public sale; it came by purchase, not by inheritance or marriage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château Thivin: Côte de Brouilly Les Sept Vignes","url":"https://www.chateau-thivin.com/en/wine/cote-brouilly-sept-vignes-8.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château Thivin: The quest for quality wines","url":"https://www.chateau-thivin.com/en/quest-bribes-quality.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"INAO: specification of the Côte de Brouilly appellation","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/PNO-CDCC%C3%B4tedeBrouilly-CN221130.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Inter Beaujolais: Côte de Brouilly","url":"https://www.beaujolais.com/en/appellation/cote-de-brouilly/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"iDealwine: Château Thivin, au sommet du Beaujolais","url":"https://www.idealwine.net/chateau-thivin-au-sommet-du-beaujolais/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-vgzerisa": {"id":"fl-vgzerisa","key":"chateau-des-jacques","group":"beaujolais","region":"beaujolais","producer":"Château des Jacques","producerId":"p-chateau-des-jacques","say":"shah-TOH day ZHAHK, moo-LAN ah VAHN","place":"Romanèche-Thorins, Moulin-à-Vent, Beaujolais","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Moulin-à-Vent Clos de Rochegrès","appellation":"Moulin-à-Vent","classification":"One of the ten crus of Beaujolais, above Beaujolais and Beaujolais-Villages","colour":"red","grapes":"Gamay, the black grape with white juice.","sites":"Clos de Rochegrès, at some 360 metres the highest ground of Moulin-à-Vent by the estate's account, a steep slope facing south east, on shallow pink granite run through with quartz, a little more clayey than the estate's other clos.","making":"Picked and sorted by hand, the grapes are usually destemmed and left to macerate slowly for three to four weeks, as red Burgundy is made, rather than by carbonic maceration; the wine is then raised for about ten months in oak barrels, old and new, in the estate's old cellar.","glass":"Black cherry and plum over violet, liquorice and a stony, peppery note; dense and structured for Gamay, with firm, fine tannin, turning with age to dried cherry and leather.","ageing":"Firm and closed in youth, it is built for a decade and more, and with age its Gamay can recall a red Burgundy.","serve":"14 to 16 degrees Celsius; decant for an hour when young; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Moulin-à-Vent","means":"A cru named for the windmill on its hill, not for a village; the word Beaujolais need not appear."},{"term":"Clos de Rochegrès","means":"The clos high in Moulin-à-Vent from which the wine comes, bottled on its own."}]},"table":"Château des Jacques treats its Moulin-à-Vent Clos de Rochegrès the way Burgundy treats Pinot Noir: destemmed, macerated for weeks and raised in barrel, from one clos high on the granite of the cru. Dark and firm for Gamay, give it years in the cellar.","story":"On 31 July 1395 Philip the Bold (1342 to 1404), Duke of Burgundy, ordered Gamay torn out of the Côte d'Or as a very bad and disloyal plant, and the grape held its ground further south, on the granite hills of the Beaujolais, beyond his lands. Moulin-à-Vent takes its name from the old windmill above Romanèche-Thorins, not from any village, and it became an appellation by the decree of 11 September 1936, its vines in Romanèche-Thorins and Chénas. Château des Jacques, an estate at Romanèche-Thorins with vines in several clos of the cru, belonged to the Thorin family until 1996, when it was bought by Maison Louis Jadot, the Beaune house founded in 1859. Under Jadot the estate replanted part of its vineyard, added vines in Morgon in 2001 and renovated its winery in 2017. It destems its grapes and ages its wines in barrel, as a domaine of the Côte d'Or would, and bottles its clos apart, Clos de Rochegrès, Clos du Grand Carquelin, Champ de Cour and La Roche among them. Rochegrès, from the highest ground of the cru, shows that one clos of Gamay can carry its own name and age like Burgundy, bringing the Burgundian idea of the climat back to the grape Burgundy once banished.","others":[{"name":"Moulin-à-Vent Clos du Grand Carquelin","note":"Another clos of the estate in Moulin-à-Vent, bottled on its own."},{"name":"Moulin-à-Vent Champ de Cour","note":"From deeper soils, the estate's densest Moulin-à-Vent."},{"name":"Morgon Côte du Py","note":"From the estate's vines in the neighbouring cru, on the hill of decomposed schist."}],"traps":["The wines are usually destemmed and macerated for weeks as in Burgundy, not made by carbonic maceration.","Moulin-à-Vent is named for a windmill, not for a village: no commune bears the name.","Clos de Rochegrès is a climat of Moulin-à-Vent, not an appellation of its own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-npbifs6t","front":"Château des Jacques's Clos de Rochegrès: how is it made, and why does that surprise?","back":"Usually destemmed, macerated for three to four weeks and raised in barrel, as red Burgundy is, where most Beaujolais is made by carbonic maceration."},{"id":"fl-8wefo0vn","front":"Château des Jacques's Clos de Rochegrès: where in Moulin-à-Vent does it lie?","back":"At the highest point of the cru, some 360 metres up, on a steep south east slope of pink granite veined with quartz."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-va7jczgd","q":"A guest asks why Château des Jacques's Moulin-à-Vent tastes more like Burgundy than like Nouveau. Which reason do you give?","opts":["Destemmed, macerated for weeks and aged in barrel","Whole bunches fermented under carbon dioxide","Grapes bought from across the Beaujolais","Grapes dried on mats before pressing"],"a":0,"exp":"The estate usually destems, macerates for three to four weeks and ages in barrel, as Burgundy does; whole bunches under carbon dioxide is the carbonic method of Nouveau.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1ldlfbwh","q":"In 1996 the Thorin family sold Château des Jacques in Moulin-à-Vent. Who bought it?","opts":["Georges Duboeuf of Romanèche-Thorins","Maison Louis Jadot of Beaune","Bouchard Père et Fils of Beaune","Joseph Drouhin of Beaune"],"a":1,"exp":"Maison Louis Jadot, founded in Beaune in 1859, bought the estate in 1996; Duboeuf is a négociant of the same village, not the buyer.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Château des Jacques: Clos de Rochegrès","url":"https://chateau-des-jacques.fr/fr/vin/22","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Château des Jacques: Presentation","url":"https://www.chateau-des-jacques.fr/en/presentation","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maison Louis Jadot: Our story","url":"https://www.louisjadot.com/en/maison/our-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"INAO: specification of the Moulin-à-Vent appellation","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/PNO-CDCMoulin-%C3%A0-Vent-CN221130.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: 31 July 1395, the banning of Gamay","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2016/07/31-july-1395-the-banning-of-gamay/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-wgw8slpd": {"id":"fl-wgw8slpd","key":"duboeuf","group":"beaujolais","region":"beaujolais","producer":"Georges Duboeuf","producerId":"p-georges-duboeuf","say":"zhorzh dew-BUHF, boh-zhoh-LAY noo-VOH","place":"Romanèche-Thorins, Beaujolais","founded":"1964","wine":{"name":"Beaujolais Nouveau","appellation":"Beaujolais","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Gamay, the black grape with white juice.","sites":"Fruit and wine bought from a great many growers and cooperatives across the region's Beaujolais appellation, rather than from vines of the house's own.","making":"Whole bunches go into closed vats, where the carbon dioxide of the first fermentation blankets them and many berries begin to ferment inside their skins, the semi carbonic way of the region. After a few days they are pressed, the juice finishes fermenting off the skins in steel, and the wine is bottled within weeks for release on the third Thursday of November.","glass":"Banana, bubblegum and kirsch over crushed raspberry and red cherry, with a touch of violet; light, juicy and soft, with almost no tannin and bright acidity.","ageing":"Made to be drunk in its first months, by the spring after the vintage; it gains nothing in bottle.","serve":"12 to 13 degrees Celsius, cool for a red; no need to decant; a plain red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Nouveau","means":"Wine of the year's harvest, released on the third Thursday of November and made to drink young."},{"term":"Beaujolais","means":"The regional appellation; Nouveau may also be Beaujolais-Villages, but never one of the ten crus."},{"term":"Georges Duboeuf","means":"The négociant house of Romanèche-Thorins that buys, blends and bottles the wine."}]},"table":"Picked only weeks before and fermented as whole bunches under carbon dioxide, Georges Duboeuf's Beaujolais Nouveau is released on the third Thursday of November. Banana, bubblegum and red cherry, light and juicy, it is a toast to the new harvest, so pour it cool and soon.","story":"In 1951 the growers' union of the Beaujolais asked to sell its new wine before the general release of 15 December, and a note of the tax authorities of 13 November that year allowed it: Beaujolais Nouveau went on sale from 15 November. Georges Duboeuf (1933 to 2020), born to a family of growers in the Mâconnais, began at eighteen, as the story goes, by delivering wine by bicycle to the restaurants and chefs of the region, Lyon among them. In 1964 he set up his own business at Romanèche-Thorins, buying wine from growers across the region and bottling it under his own name, and from the 1970s, by his own account, he dressed his crus in flower labels. He made the November release an event, and by the house's account the new wine reached North America, Australia and Japan in the 1980s, where he went each year to open the first bottles. In 1985 the release was fixed on the third Thursday of November. The fashion carried the name Beaujolais round the world. He died at his home in Romanèche-Thorins in January 2020. For a sommelier the bottle is a date on the calendar and a lesson in carbonic maceration.","others":[{"name":"Moulin-à-Vent","note":"One of the house's cru wines, sold under its flower labels."},{"name":"Beaujolais-Villages Nouveau","note":"The same November release from the Villages appellation, the hamlets around the crus."}],"traps":["Beaujolais Nouveau is released on the third Thursday of November, a date fixed in 1985.","Nouveau may be Beaujolais or Beaujolais-Villages, never a cru.","Duboeuf is a négociant, buying from growers across the region, not a domaine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-7qlnqwmu","front":"Georges Duboeuf's Beaujolais Nouveau: how is it fermented?","back":"Whole bunches under carbon dioxide, many berries fermenting inside their skins, then pressed after a few days."},{"id":"fl-z8o6h0f2","front":"On what day is Beaujolais Nouveau released, and since when?","back":"The third Thursday of November, a date fixed in 1985."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-w8zg8c12","q":"A guest smells banana and bubblegum in a glass of Duboeuf's Beaujolais Nouveau and asks why. What do you say?","opts":["A share of Muscat in the blend","A banana liqueur added before bottling","Whole bunch fermentation under carbon dioxide","Long ageing in new oak barrels"],"a":2,"exp":"Carbonic maceration, whole bunches fermenting inside their skins under carbon dioxide, gives Nouveau its banana and bubblegum notes; nothing is added and the wine is raised in steel, not oak.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-7fa01bya","q":"Georges Duboeuf founded his négociant house at Romanèche-Thorins in 1964. In which year was the third Thursday of November fixed for Nouveau?","opts":["1951","1964","1936","1985"],"a":3,"exp":"The release day was fixed on the third Thursday of November in 1985; 1951 is the year the early sale of new wine was first allowed, and 1936 the year Morgon and Moulin-à-Vent became appellations.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Inter Beaujolais: Beaujolais Nouveaux press kit","url":"https://www.beaujolais.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2022/09/Dossier-de-presse-Beaujolais-Nouveaux-2022.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"INAO: specification of the Beaujolais appellation","url":"https://extranet.inao.gouv.fr/fichier/PNO-cdcBeaujolais-cn220210.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Les Vins Georges Duboeuf: Beaujolais Nouveau","url":"https://www.duboeuf.com/en/beaujolais-nouveau/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Beaujolais wine Pope Georges Duboeuf dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/obituaries/beaujolais-wine-pope-georges-duboeuf-dies-429939","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Georges Duboeuf","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georges_Duboeuf","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-u5olwuzs": {"id":"fl-u5olwuzs","key":"gaja","group":"piedmont","region":"piedmont","producer":"Gaja","producerId":"p-gaja","say":"GUY-ah, bar-bah-RESS-koh","place":"Barbaresco, Barbaresco DOCG, Langhe, Piedmont","founded":"1859","wine":{"name":"Barbaresco","appellation":"Barbaresco DOCG","classification":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita, granted to Barbaresco in 1980","colour":"red","grapes":"Nebbiolo alone, as the appellation requires.","sites":"Fourteen of the family's own vineyards around the village of Barbaresco, each picked and fermented on its own before the blend.","making":"Each vineyard is fermented apart in stainless steel. The wines then spend a year in barriques before they are blended, and a second year in large oak casks before bottling.","glass":"Red cherry, rose petal and liquorice with a touch of clove and tar; pale garnet, polished and fresh, with firm, fine grained tannin and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Opens from about eight years after the vintage and keeps developing for two decades or more in a strong year.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a large, wide bowled glass.","label":[{"term":"Gaja","means":"The family name, printed large across the top of the label."},{"term":"Barbaresco","means":"The DOCG for Nebbiolo from the Barbaresco zone, aged at least twenty six months, nine of them in wood."}]},"table":"Gaja's Barbaresco is the house's village wine: Nebbiolo from fourteen family vineyards around Barbaresco, each fermented apart, then a year in barriques and a year in large casks. Polished and fresh over firm tannin, it wants an hour or two in a decanter and a large glass.","story":"In 1859 the founder of the house began to make and sell wine in the village of Barbaresco, and his family has made wine there ever since. The change came when the founder's great grandson joined in 1961. He brought French barriques into the cellar and, from the 1967 vintage, bottled single vineyards on their own: Sorì San Lorenzo first, then Sorì Tildìn in 1970 and Costa Russi in 1978. That same year he planted Cabernet Sauvignon on a Nebbiolo site in the village, and his father's verdict, as the family tells it, darmagi, what a pity, gave the wine its name. From the 1996 vintage the three single vineyard wines left the Barbaresco DOCG and were sold as Langhe Nebbiolo, so that a little Barbera could join the blend; with the 2013 vintage, under the next generation, they returned to Barbaresco as Nebbiolo alone. Through all of it the village wine, a blend of the family's parcels raised first in barrique and then in large casks, stayed the house's calling card. Know it before the single vineyards: the family name printed large across the label, and Barbaresco beneath it.","others":[{"name":"Sorì San Lorenzo","note":"The single vineyard wine that came first, from the 1967 vintage; Langhe Nebbiolo from 1996 to 2012 and Barbaresco again from 2013."},{"name":"Sorì Tildìn","note":"Single vineyard Barbaresco first made in 1970, a separate site from Sorì San Lorenzo."},{"name":"Darmagi","note":"Cabernet Sauvignon planted in Barbaresco in 1978; the name is the Piedmontese for what a pity."}],"traps":["Gaja's single vineyard wines were labelled Langhe Nebbiolo from the 1996 to the 2012 vintage, with a little Barbera; from 2013 they are Barbaresco again, and Nebbiolo alone.","Sorì Tildìn and Sorì San Lorenzo are two different vineyards, not two bottlings of one site.","The village Barbaresco blends fourteen family vineyards; it is not the declassified fruit of the single vineyard wines."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-gdycz5zn","front":"Gaja's Barbaresco: how is it raised after fermentation?","back":"A year in barriques, then blending, then a second year in large oak casks before bottling."},{"id":"fl-etmhvvhf","front":"Which Gaja wine takes its name from a father's verdict, and what does the word mean?","back":"Darmagi, Cabernet Sauvignon planted in Barbaresco in 1978: Piedmontese for what a pity."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-jx29zlpq","q":"A guest asks how Gaja's village Barbaresco differs from its single vineyard wines. What sets it apart?","opts":["It is Nebbiolo with a share of Cabernet Sauvignon","It blends fourteen family vineyards around Barbaresco","It is declassified fruit from Sorì San Lorenzo","It is made from grapes bought across the Langhe"],"a":1,"exp":"The Barbaresco blends fourteen of the family's own vineyards, each fermented apart; it is not declassified single vineyard wine, and Barbaresco must be Nebbiolo alone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-gaz9gt0q","q":"From the 1996 vintage Gaja moved its three single vineyard wines out of the Barbaresco DOCG. Under which name were they sold until 2012?","opts":["Langhe Nebbiolo","Barolo","Roero","Nebbiolo d'Alba"],"a":0,"exp":"They were labelled Langhe Nebbiolo so that a little Barbera could join the Nebbiolo; from the 2013 vintage they returned to Barbaresco, Nebbiolo alone.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Consorzio Barolo Barbaresco Alba Langhe e Dogliani: Barbaresco DOCG","url":"https://www.langhevini.it/en/le-denominazioni-tutelate-dal-consorzio/barbaresco-docg/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Gaja (wine)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaja_(wine)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Fine and Rare: Gaja, the estate at the heart of Barbaresco","url":"https://www.frw.co.uk/editorial/gaja-the-legendary-estate-at-the-heart-of-barbaresco","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wilson Daniels: Gaja Barbaresco 2023 fact sheet","url":"https://images.salsify.com/images/xgzq1u082u9vjje2e2vc/Gaja_2023_Barbaresco_Fact_Sheet.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Gaja goes back to Barbaresco with single vineyard wines","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/gaja-barbaresco-single-vineyard-314996/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-sut8q7n9": {"id":"fl-sut8q7n9","key":"giacomo-conterno","group":"piedmont","region":"piedmont","producer":"Giacomo Conterno","producerId":"p-giacomo-conterno","say":"JAH-koh-moh kon-TEHR-noh, mon-for-TEE-noh","place":"Monforte d'Alba, Barolo DOCG, Langhe, Piedmont","founded":"1908","wine":{"name":"Barolo Riserva Monfortino","appellation":"Barolo DOCG","classification":"Riserva: at least sixty two months of ageing before release, eighteen of them in wood","colour":"red","grapes":"Nebbiolo alone.","sites":"The Francia vineyard in Serralunga d'Alba, bought by the family in 1974 and the source of every Monfortino from 1978; with the 2015 vintage a selection from Arione, in the same commune, joined it for the first time.","making":"Only in years the family judges worthy, a selection of the grapes is fermented apart in open topped wooden vats, with four to five weeks on the skins. The wine then ages for some seven years in large Slavonian oak casks, against about four for the house's other Barolo, before bottling.","glass":"Sour cherry, dried rose and orange peel over tar, iron and leather, with a note of liquorice; pale for its power, with towering, fine grained tannin, high acidity and a very long finish.","ageing":"Released only after years in cask, it wants a further decade or more in bottle in a strong vintage and lasts for many decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; give a young bottle two hours or more in a decanter and pour an old one slowly off its sediment; a large glass with a wide bowl.","label":[{"term":"Monfortino","means":"Named for Monforte d'Alba, the family's home village, though the grapes grow in Serralunga d'Alba."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Under the Barolo rules, at least sixty two months of ageing before release, eighteen of them in wood."},{"term":"Barolo","means":"The appellation: Nebbiolo grown in the Barolo zone and aged thirty eight months or more, eighteen of them in wood."}]},"table":"Monfortino is Giacomo Conterno's Barolo Riserva, made only in years the family judges worthy, from the Francia vineyard in Serralunga, fermented long in open wooden vats and aged some seven years in large casks. Decant it well and expect it to need time.","story":"The house traces its beginnings, by most accounts, to 1908, when the family opened a tavern near Monforte d'Alba and sold its own wine there. After the First World War the founder's son, whose name the house bears, set aside a Barolo for long ageing; first bottled, by most accounts, in the 1920s, it took the name Monfortino from his home village, and until 1978 it was made from bought grapes. He passed the firm to his two sons, and in 1969 the younger left to found his own estate, Poderi Aldo Conterno, while the elder kept the family name. In 1974 the elder brother bought Cascina Francia, a farm in Serralunga d'Alba, and from the 1978 vintage Monfortino came only from it. His own son took over the cellar at his death, and the 2015 vintage was the first to add a selection from Arione, in the same commune. The method has changed little: a long fermentation in open wooden vats, years in large Slavonian casks, and a decision each year whether Monfortino will be made at all. Monfortino is traditional Barolo at its slowest: released late, built to be kept, and named for a village its grapes do not come from.","others":[{"name":"Barolo Francia","note":"The house's Barolo from the Francia vineyard in Serralunga, the same hillside that gives Monfortino."},{"name":"Barbera d'Alba Francia","note":"Barbera grown on the same Serralunga hillside."}],"traps":["Monfortino is named for Monforte d'Alba, the family's village, but its grapes grow in Serralunga d'Alba.","Giacomo Conterno and Poderi Aldo Conterno are separate houses: the younger brother left the family firm in 1969 to found his own.","Monfortino is not made every year: it exists only in vintages the family judges worthy of a long Riserva."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-tvihsug9","front":"Giacomo Conterno's Monfortino: where do its grapes grow?","back":"In the Francia vineyard in Serralunga d'Alba, bought in 1974, with Arione added for the first time in the 2015 vintage; the name honours Monforte."},{"id":"fl-6imss0h0","front":"How long does Monfortino spend in large casks, against the house's other Barolo?","back":"Some seven years, against about four, after four to five weeks on the skins in open wooden vats."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-dh7p5xs6","q":"A guest orders Monfortino expecting a wine of Monforte d'Alba. Where do its grapes actually grow?","opts":["Monforte d'Alba","La Morra","Barbaresco","Serralunga d'Alba"],"a":3,"exp":"Since 1978 Monfortino has come from the Francia vineyard in Serralunga d'Alba, with Arione added in 2015; Monforte d'Alba gives it only its name.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-jsp4oh37","q":"In 1969 the Conterno family firm divided between two brothers. What did the younger brother do?","opts":["He joined the Barbaresco cooperative","He bought Cascina Francia in Serralunga","He founded Poderi Aldo Conterno","He took over the family tavern in Monforte"],"a":2,"exp":"The younger brother left in 1969 to found Poderi Aldo Conterno; it was the elder, keeping the Giacomo Conterno name, who bought Cascina Francia in 1974.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Consorzio Barolo Barbaresco Alba Langhe e Dogliani: Barolo DOCG production rules","url":"https://www.langhevini.it/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/DOCG-Barolo.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Giacomo Conterno","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giacomo_Conterno","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Conterno Monfortino Barolo 1978","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/wine-regions/piedmont-wine-region/barolo/wine-legend-conterno-monfortino-barolo-1978-355915/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Giacomo Conterno technical sheet","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/media/184560/italy-conterno-tech-sheet-202604.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Liv-ex: Giacomo Conterno producer profile","url":"https://liv-ex.com/?p=39606","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-c8yzzhnu": {"id":"fl-c8yzzhnu","key":"bartolo-mascarello","group":"piedmont","region":"piedmont","producer":"Bartolo Mascarello","producerId":"p-bartolo-mascarello","say":"BAR-toh-loh mahs-kah-REL-loh","place":"Barolo, Barolo DOCG, Langhe, Piedmont","founded":"1919","wine":{"name":"Barolo","appellation":"Barolo DOCG","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Nebbiolo alone.","sites":"Four sites blended into one wine: Cannubi, San Lorenzo and Rué in the commune of Barolo, and Rocche dell'Annunziata in La Morra.","making":"The grapes are fermented with native yeasts in concrete vats, followed by a long maceration on the skins. The Barolo then rests for some two and a half to three years in large Slavonian oak casks, never in barrique, before bottling.","glass":"Pale garnet, with rose petal, sour cherry and tar over dried orange peel and a hint of liquorice; lifted and fragrant rather than dense, with firm, dusty tannin and bright acidity.","ageing":"Tight for its first decade, it opens at ten to fifteen years and lasts for decades in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; open a young bottle into a decanter an hour or two ahead and ease an old one off its sediment; a large, wide bowled glass.","label":[{"term":"Bartolo Mascarello","means":"The house's name since Bartolo's time; the family firm was earlier known as Cantina Mascarello."},{"term":"Barolo","means":"Nebbiolo alone from the Barolo zone, a DOCG that asks at least thirty eight months of ageing, eighteen in wood."}]},"table":"Bartolo Mascarello makes one Barolo, blending Cannubi, San Lorenzo and Rué in Barolo with Rocche dell'Annunziata in La Morra, fermented in concrete and aged in large Slavonian casks, never barrique. Pale yet firm, fragrant rather than dense, it opens only after its first decade.","story":"Bartolo's father, taught by his own father, founded Cantina Mascarello in the village of Barolo in 1919 by most accounts, though others give 1918 or 1920. Bartolo joined him in 1945 and learned the trade from him. The family blended its vineyards into one Barolo, as the old houses of the village did: Cannubi, San Lorenzo and Rué in Barolo, and Rocche dell'Annunziata across the hill in La Morra. From the 1980s many neighbours turned to single vineyard bottlings, shorter macerations and new French barriques. Bartolo refused all three and became an outspoken voice of tradition in what came to be called the Barolo Wars. Late in life he painted labels by hand, and one that read No Barrique, No Berlusconi, aimed at new oak and at Silvio Berlusconi (1936 to 2023) in the election of 2001, became a collector's piece. He died in March 2005, aged seventy eight, and his daughter took over and kept his methods. The wine is an argument in a bottle: that Barolo is a blend of places and of patience, and that the commune can matter more than the cru.","others":[{"name":"Dolcetto d'Alba","note":"The house's Dolcetto, the early drinking red of the Langhe."},{"name":"Langhe Freisa","note":"The house's Freisa, an old red grape of Piedmont, under the Langhe DOC."}],"traps":["Bartolo Mascarello in Barolo is a separate house from Giuseppe Mascarello e Figlio of Monchiero.","There is no single vineyard Cannubi from this house: all four sites go into the one Barolo.","No Barrique, No Berlusconi was a label Bartolo painted by hand, not the name of a wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-oai4mxai","front":"Bartolo Mascarello's Barolo: which four vineyards go into it?","back":"Cannubi, San Lorenzo and Rué in Barolo, with Rocche dell'Annunziata in La Morra, blended into one wine."},{"id":"fl-0ha0g7kz","front":"Which Barolo maker painted a label reading No Barrique, No Berlusconi?","back":"Bartolo Mascarello, around the Italian election of 2001, against new oak and against Berlusconi."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-2gbldxv8","q":"A guest asks for Bartolo Mascarello's Cannubi. What should the sommelier explain?","opts":["It sold its Cannubi parcel to a neighbour in the 1980s","It blends Cannubi with three other sites into one Barolo","It bottles Cannubi on its own only in Riserva years","It sells Cannubi under the Langhe Nebbiolo label instead"],"a":1,"exp":"Bartolo Mascarello makes a single Barolo from Cannubi, San Lorenzo, Rué and Rocche dell'Annunziata; there is no single vineyard bottling, Riserva or otherwise.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-e2dce48q","q":"During the Barolo Wars, which change did Bartolo Mascarello refuse?","opts":["Ageing in new French barriques","Fermenting in concrete vats","Blending several vineyards together","Ageing in large Slavonian casks"],"a":0,"exp":"He rejected new barriques, with the shorter macerations and single vineyard labels that came with them; concrete vats, blending and large Slavonian casks were his own way.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Bartolo Mascarello","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bartolo_Mascarello","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Barolo's Iconoclast Dies","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/webfeature/show/id/Barolos-Iconoclast-Dies_2441","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Stepping Back in Time","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/stepping-back-in-time-15277","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Anti-Berlusconi label becomes collector's item","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/anti-berlusconi-label-becomes-collectors-item-106339/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Bartolo Mascarello Barolo technical sheet","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/media/183908/italy-mascarello-bartolo-barolo-tech-sheet-0523.pdf","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ncoaz5np": {"id":"fl-ncoaz5np","key":"bruno-giacosa","group":"piedmont","region":"piedmont","producer":"Bruno Giacosa","producerId":"p-bruno-giacosa","say":"jah-KOH-zah, leh ROK-keh del fahl-LET-toh","place":"Neive, with the vineyard in Serralunga d'Alba, Barolo DOCG, Piedmont","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Barolo Le Rocche del Falletto Riserva","appellation":"Barolo DOCG","classification":"Riserva, declared only in years the house judges exceptional and marked by a red label","colour":"red","grapes":"Nebbiolo alone.","sites":"Le Rocche, the upper part of the Falletto amphitheatre in Serralunga d'Alba, facing south to south west on calcareous clay; the family bought Falletto in the early 1980s.","making":"Fermented and macerated on the skins in vat, the wine is aged for some two and a half to three years in large casks of French oak, then rests in bottle before it is released as a Riserva.","glass":"Rose petal, red cherry and tar over liquorice and dried orange peel, with a hint of white truffle; taut and perfumed, with high acidity and fine but very firm tannin.","ageing":"Closed for ten years and more, it opens slowly through its second decade and lasts for decades after.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle two hours or more ahead and an old one gently, leaving its sediment behind; a large, wide bowled glass.","label":[{"term":"Riserva","means":"Printed on the red label, kept for chosen years; in other years the wine wears a white label."},{"term":"Azienda Agricola Falletto","means":"The name for wines from the family's own vineyards; Casa Vinicola Bruno Giacosa marks wines from bought grapes."},{"term":"Falletto Vigna Le Rocche","means":"The name later labels print for the same parcel within the Falletto vineyard."}]},"table":"Released under a red label only in chosen years, Bruno Giacosa's Le Rocche del Falletto Riserva is Nebbiolo from the upper part of the family's own Falletto vineyard in Serralunga, aged two and a half to three years in large casks. Taut and perfumed, it asks for years and decanting.","story":"Bruno Giacosa (1929 to 2018) was born in Neive and learned the trade in the family cellar from his father and grandfather, in a business that made wine from bought grapes. For decades he did the same, choosing fruit from growers he knew vineyard by vineyard, and from the 1964 vintage he bottled a Barbaresco from Santo Stefano in Neive under the name of its site, one of the early wines of a single vineyard in the Langhe. In the early 1980s he bought his first vineyard, Falletto, an amphitheatre of calcareous clay in Serralunga d'Alba, and the wines of his own vines took a second name, Azienda Agricola Falletto, beside the Casa Vinicola that bought grapes. The upper part of the hill, Le Rocche, gave the Barolo he held back in years he judged exceptional and released as a Riserva under a red label; in other years it wore the white. In 1996 he added parcels in Asili and Rabajà in Barbaresco. Of the 2006 vintage the house bottled no Barolo or Barbaresco at all, selling the wine in bulk. After his death in January 2018 his daughter led the house. On a list, the red label is the sign to look for: a Riserva kept for years when the vineyard and the season both deserved it.","others":[{"name":"Barolo Falletto","note":"Barolo from the wider Falletto vineyard, under the white label."},{"name":"Barbaresco Asili Riserva","note":"Red label Barbaresco from the house's parcel in Asili, bought in 1996."},{"name":"Roero Arneis","note":"The house's white from Arneis grown in the Roero, across the Tanaro from the Langhe."}],"traps":["The red label marks the Riserva and the white label the standard bottling; Le Rocche del Falletto has appeared under both.","Le Rocche del Falletto and Falletto Vigna Le Rocche are two names for the same parcel, not two vineyards.","Giacosa's cellar is in Neive, a Barbaresco commune, yet its Falletto vineyard lies in Serralunga d'Alba, in Barolo."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-jobyuxte","front":"On a Bruno Giacosa Barolo, what does a red label tell you?","back":"That it is a Riserva, released only in years the house judged exceptional; the standard bottling wears a white label."},{"id":"fl-7sin8usc","front":"Where does Le Rocche lie within Giacosa's Falletto vineyard, and in which commune?","back":"The upper part of the Falletto amphitheatre, facing south to south west, in Serralunga d'Alba."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ivgmwrk9","q":"A guest holds a Bruno Giacosa Barolo with a red label. What does the colour signal?","opts":["A Barbaresco rather than a Barolo","A wine aged in new French barriques","A Riserva from a year the house judged exceptional","A Barolo made from grapes bought from growers"],"a":2,"exp":"Giacosa kept the red label for Riserva bottlings declared in chosen years; wines from bought grapes are marked by the Casa Vinicola name, not by the colour.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2k652aok","q":"Bruno Giacosa bought his first vineyard in the early 1980s. Which was it?","opts":["Cannubi in Barolo","Asili in Barbaresco","Santo Stefano in Neive","Falletto in Serralunga d'Alba"],"a":3,"exp":"Falletto in Serralunga was his first vineyard; Santo Stefano was bought fruit bottled from 1964, and the Asili parcel came in 1996.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Bruno Giacosa","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruno_Giacosa","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Bruno Giacosa producer page","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/producer/bruno-giacosa-barolo/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Superiore.de: Bruno Giacosa Barolo Falletto Vigna Le Rocche Riserva 2020","url":"https://www.superiore.de/en/wine/piedmont/bruno-giacosa/barolo-falletto-vigna-le-rocche-riserva-docg-2020.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Bruno Giacosa Asili Barbaresco Riserva 2004","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-bruno-giacosa-asili-barbaresco-riserva-2004-444841/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Bruno Giacosa will not bottle 2006 vintage","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/bruno-giacosa-will-not-bottle-2006-vintage-69732/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-cb7of0cx": {"id":"fl-cb7of0cx","key":"produttori","group":"piedmont","region":"piedmont","producer":"Produttori del Barbaresco","producerId":"p-produttori-del-barbaresco","say":"proh-doot-TOH-ree del bar-bah-RESS-koh","place":"Barbaresco, Barbaresco DOCG, Langhe, Piedmont","founded":"1958","wine":{"name":"Barbaresco","appellation":"Barbaresco DOCG","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Nebbiolo alone.","sites":"A blend of the members' vineyards across the commune of Barbaresco, whose named crus include Asili, Rabajà, Montestefano, Ovello and Pora.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel at about 28 degrees Celsius, with three to four weeks on the skins and pumping over two or three times a day. The wine then ages for twenty months to two years, as the vintage decides, in large oak casks.","glass":"Red cherry, rose and raspberry over tar, liquorice and dried orange peel; pale garnet, fresh and savoury, with firm, fine tannin and a clean finish.","ageing":"Good to drink about five years on, it keeps well for fifteen to twenty in a strong year.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter suits a young bottle; a large, wide bowled glass.","label":[{"term":"Produttori del Barbaresco","means":"The producers of Barbaresco: the village's growers' cooperative, founded in 1958."},{"term":"Barbaresco","means":"The appellation of the label: Nebbiolo of the Barbaresco zone, with twenty six months of ageing at the least, nine in wood."}]},"table":"Produttori del Barbaresco is the village cooperative, and its Barbaresco blends the members' Nebbiolo from across the commune, fermented in steel and aged about two years in large casks. Fresh and savoury, with Nebbiolo's perfume, it teaches the appellation in a single glass.","story":"Barbaresco's first cooperative cellar was set up in the village castle in 1894 by the head of Alba's royal school of oenology, who gathered a small group of growers around him. After his death the cellar was shut under fascism, in the 1920s by the cooperative's account. In 1958 the parish priest, who had come to the village after the Second World War, brought nineteen growers together to found the Produttori del Barbaresco. The opening vintage was made in the yard beside his house with second hand equipment, and most of it was sold in bulk to pay for a cellar, finished in 1960 at the foot of the village tower. From the 1967 vintage the cooperative bottled its first single vineyard wines, Asili, Ovello, Pora and Moccagatta among them; they grew into nine Riserve, made only in vintages judged excellent and released after four years. Barbaresco became a DOC in 1966 and a DOCG in 1980. The plain Barbaresco carries the members' fruit from across the commune: one village, one grape and many growers, in a bottle a guest can open young.","others":[{"name":"Barbaresco Riserva Asili","note":"One of the nine single vineyard Riserve, first bottled as a cru in 1967 and made only in vintages judged excellent."},{"name":"Barbaresco Riserva Rabajà","note":"Another of the nine crus, aged some thirty months in large oak casks."}],"traps":["The cooperative's Riserve are made only in vintages judged excellent; in other years there is no cru Riserva at all.","The cooperative of 1958 is not the cellar of 1894: the earlier cellar was shut in the 1920s, and the priest founded a new one.","Muncagota on a Riserva label is the cru first bottled in 1967 as Moccagatta: one vineyard under a later name."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-6wxzlf9c","front":"Where was the Produttori del Barbaresco's opening vintage of 1958 made?","back":"In the yard beside the parish priest's house, with second hand equipment; the cellar at the foot of the tower followed in 1960."},{"id":"fl-i2yy21yi","front":"Produttori del Barbaresco: how many single vineyard Riserve, and in which years?","back":"Nine: Asili, Montefico, Montestefano, Muncagota, Ovello, Pajè, Pora, Rabajà and Rio Sordo, made only in vintages judged excellent."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-yi9gdb1p","q":"Produttori del Barbaresco's plain Barbaresco ferments in steel. How is it then aged?","opts":["Steel only, bottled the spring after the harvest","About two years in large oak casks","A year in new barriques, then a year in steel","Some five years in large casks"],"a":1,"exp":"The Barbaresco ferments in steel with three to four weeks on the skins and ages twenty months to two years in large casks; longer cask ageing is kept for the Riserve.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-t288ppq8","q":"In 1958 nineteen growers founded the Produttori del Barbaresco. Who brought them together?","opts":["The village's parish priest","The head of Alba's school of oenology","A merchant house from Alba","The mayor of Barbaresco"],"a":0,"exp":"The parish priest gathered nineteen growers in 1958; the head of Alba's royal school of oenology had founded the earlier cellar of 1894, shut in the 1920s.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Produttori del Barbaresco: Our story","url":"https://www.produttoridelbarbaresco.com/en/the-winery/the-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Produttori del Barbaresco: Barbaresco DOCG 2022 sheet","url":"https://www.produttoridelbarbaresco.com/wp-content/uploads/barbaresco-docg-2022_EN_produttori-del-barbaresco.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Produttori del Barbaresco: Barbaresco docg","url":"https://www.produttoridelbarbaresco.com/en/wines/barbaresco-docg/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Produttori del Barbaresco: Asili cru","url":"https://www.produttoridelbarbaresco.com/en/crus/cru-asili/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Consorzio Barolo Barbaresco Alba Langhe e Dogliani: Barbaresco DOCG","url":"https://www.langhevini.it/en/le-denominazioni-tutelate-dal-consorzio/barbaresco-docg/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-yiqs2lwb": {"id":"fl-yiqs2lwb","key":"antinori","group":"tuscany-chianti","region":"tuscany","producer":"Marchesi Antinori","producerId":"p-marchesi-antinori","say":"an-tee-NOR-ee, teen-yah-NEL-loh","place":"San Casciano in Val di Pesa, Chianti Classico zone, Tuscany","founded":"1385","wine":{"name":"Tignanello","appellation":"Toscana IGT","classification":"Indicazione Geografica Tipica, the category Italian law created in 1992","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese, about four fifths, with Cabernet Sauvignon and a little Cabernet Franc, the blend the house has kept since the 1982 vintage.","sites":"The Tignanello vineyard alone, on the family's estate of that name between the Greve and Pesa valleys: slopes facing south west at about 350 to 400 metres, on stony, limestone rich soils of Pliocene marine marl with alberese, on the same hillside as Solaia.","making":"The berries are destemmed and sorted by hand, then fermented and macerated on their skins in tanks for one to three weeks, and the malolactic conversion is finished in barrel. The wine spends about twelve to eighteen months in small barrels, French oak with a little Hungarian, the lots blended a few months before bottling, and about a year in bottle before release.","glass":"Sour cherry, red plum and iris drawn tight by cassis and cedar, with tobacco leaf and a touch of vanilla from the barrique; medium to full bodied, with fresh Sangiovese acidity and fine, firm tannin.","ageing":"Approachable five or six years after the vintage, it is at its fullest between about ten and twenty years and keeps longer in a strong year.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or so, an old one gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Tignanello","means":"The vineyard on the family's estate in the Chianti Classico zone that gives every grape in the wine."},{"term":"Toscana IGT","means":"The regional category for Tuscany; the first vintages, made before the category existed, were sold as Vino da Tavola."},{"term":"Marchesi Antinori","means":"The Florentine family house, in the wine trade since 1385 by its own account."}]},"table":"With the 1971 vintage Marchesi Antinori took Tignanello outside the Chianti rules: Sangiovese with Cabernet from one vineyard in the Chianti Classico zone, raised in small French barrels. Cherry and iris meet cassis and cedar, all held in by firm tannin.","story":"By the family's own account, a forebear joined the Florentine guild of wine merchants, the Arte dei Vinattieri, in 1385, and in 1506 a descendant bought the palace in Florence that bears the family name. For centuries the house traded wine and made Chianti. The modern turn came from the Tignanello vineyard, with the house oenologist Giacomo Tachis (1933 to 2016). The 1970 was made there as a Chianti Classico Riserva, with Canaiolo and a little white Trebbiano and Malvasia as the rules then required, and aged in small oak barrels. The 1971 went further: the family left the Chianti rules and sold it as a Vino da Tavola della Toscana, named for its vineyard. The white grapes were gone with the 1975 vintage, and by the 1982 the blend had settled at about four fifths Sangiovese with Cabernet Sauvignon and a little Cabernet Franc. The house claims it as the first Sangiovese raised in small barrels and among the first Chianti Classico reds made without white grapes. Solaia, its Cabernet led sibling from the same hillside, followed in 1978, and when Italian law created the IGT category in 1992, such wines found a legal home. Learn it as a founding Super Tuscan: the 1971 date, and Sangiovese in the lead.","others":[{"name":"Solaia","note":"The Cabernet Sauvignon led sibling from the same hillside, first made in 1978, with Sangiovese added in later vintages."},{"name":"Badia a Passignano","note":"Chianti Classico Gran Selezione from the family's Badia a Passignano estate: Sangiovese inside the appellation."},{"name":"Guado al Tasso","note":"The family's estate in Bolgheri and its Bolgheri Superiore, a blend of Bordeaux grapes."}],"traps":["Tignanello is Sangiovese led with Cabernet; Solaia is Cabernet Sauvignon led with Sangiovese. Reversing them is the usual error.","Tignanello is made only in years the family judges fit: there is none of 1972, 1973, 1974, 1976, 1984, 1992 or 2002.","The IGT category did not exist in 1971, so the early vintages were labelled Vino da Tavola."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-rzzwoc3z","front":"Tignanello: what changed between the 1970 and the 1971 vintage?","back":"The 1970 was a Chianti Classico Riserva with white grapes; the 1971 left the Chianti rules and was sold as Vino da Tavola under the vineyard's name."},{"id":"fl-le14fudq","front":"Which Antinori red is Cabernet Sauvignon led, from the hillside it shares with Tignanello?","back":"Solaia, first made in 1978."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-fqqmxlr4","q":"A guest asks what is in Tignanello. Which blend has the house used since the 1982 vintage?","opts":["Sangiovese alone from the Tignanello vineyard","Cabernet Sauvignon with Sangiovese and Cabernet Franc","Sangiovese with Cabernet Sauvignon and Cabernet Franc","Sangiovese with Canaiolo, Trebbiano and Malvasia"],"a":2,"exp":"Since 1982 Tignanello has been about four fifths Sangiovese with Cabernet Sauvignon and a little Cabernet Franc; the Cabernet led blend is Solaia's, and the white grapes left in 1975.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rg4r11l8","q":"With which vintage did Antinori first sell Tignanello outside the Chianti rules, as a table wine named for its vineyard?","opts":["1970","1975","1978","1971"],"a":3,"exp":"The 1971 was sold as Vino da Tavola della Toscana; the 1970 had been a Chianti Classico Riserva, 1975 dropped the white grapes and 1978 is Solaia's first vintage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Marchesi Antinori: Tignanello, historical data","url":"https://www.antinori.it/en/vino/tignanello-en/historical-data-2/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marchesi Antinori: Tignanello, the soil","url":"https://www.antinori.it/en/vino/tignanello-en/soil/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marchesi Antinori: A story passed down through 26 generations","url":"https://www.antinori.it/en/family/family-in-depth/intro-en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Italian wine father Giacomo Tachis dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/italian-wine-father-giacomo-tachis-dies-290637","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Gambero Rosso: After 50 years, Tignanello still captures the spirit of the times","url":"https://www.gamberorossointernational.com/?p=536790","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-lqbjpz7d": {"id":"fl-lqbjpz7d","key":"ricasoli","group":"tuscany-chianti","region":"tuscany","producer":"Barone Ricasoli","producerId":"p-barone-ricasoli","say":"ree-KAH-zo-lee, kah-STEL-loh dee BROH-lee-oh","place":"Brolio, Gaiole in Chianti, Chianti Classico, Tuscany","founded":"1141","wine":{"name":"Castello di Brolio","appellation":"Chianti Classico DOCG","classification":"Gran Selezione, the tier of Chianti Classico above Riserva, approved in 2013","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese, the great majority, at times with a little of another red grape; the rules for Gran Selezione have since asked for nine tenths Sangiovese or more.","sites":"Vineyards around the castle at Brolio, up to about 490 metres in the house's sheets, on the three soils of Chianti Classico: macigno sandstone, galestro of the Scaglia Toscana and alberese limestone of the Monte Morello formation.","making":"Fermented in temperature controlled stainless steel at about 24 to 27 degrees Celsius, with some two weeks on the skins. It is then raised for eighteen months to almost two years in 500 litre tonneaux, about a third of them new, with a few months in bottle before release. The first vintage, the 1997, was raised in barriques.","glass":"Red cherry, violet and iris with dried herbs, liquorice and a touch of cedar from the tonneaux; firm, fine grained tannin and bright acidity carry it to a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Firm when young, it opens from about six to eight years after the vintage, and the wine of a strong year keeps fifteen to twenty.","serve":"Serve at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius in a large, wide bowled glass, giving a young bottle an hour in a decanter and easing an old one off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Chianti Classico","means":"The DOCG of the historic Chianti zone between Florence and Siena, its bounds first drawn by a Grand Ducal edict of 1716."},{"term":"Gran Selezione","means":"The category above Riserva: grapes from the bottler's own vineyards and at least thirty months of ageing, three of them in bottle."},{"term":"Gaiole","means":"One of the eleven village units the rules allow on Gran Selezione alone, printed on this wine from the 2020 vintage."},{"term":"Gallo Nero","means":"The black rooster of the consorzio, on every bottle of Chianti Classico since 2005."}]},"table":"From the vineyards around Brolio, the castle at Gaiole the family has held since 1141 by its own account, Barone Ricasoli makes Castello di Brolio, its Chianti Classico Gran Selezione of Sangiovese. Expect red cherry and iris, with a firm, fine grained grip.","story":"A document in the family archive shows the Ricasoli holding the castle of Brolio in 1141, and wine has long been made there. Bettino Ricasoli (1809 to 1880), the Iron Baron, twice prime minister of the new Kingdom of Italy, set down his view of Chianti in 1872, in a letter to a professor at the University of Pisa: a wine led by Sangiovese, with Canaiolo to soften it and the white Malvasia. Blends of that kind shaped Chianti's rules for a century. Around 1970 the family sold the Brolio name to the drinks group Seagram while keeping the land, and the brand later passed to the Australian house of Hardy's. In 1993 the family bought back the brand and the winery and set about replanting the vineyards; Castello di Brolio, the estate's new flagship, followed with the 1997 vintage, which by the house's account made its name abroad. When the consorzio's members approved Gran Selezione in 2013, the wine took its place in the new tier, and from the 2020 vintage its label adds the village unit Gaiole. Know Bettino and his three grapes for the examination, and pour this wine as the castle's own answer, from the soils around it.","others":[{"name":"Colledilà","note":"Chianti Classico Gran Selezione from a single vineyard of the Brolio estate."},{"name":"Brolio Chianti Classico","note":"Sangiovese led and released younger than the Gran Selezione."},{"name":"Casalferro","note":"The estate's Merlot, sold outside the Chianti Classico DOCG."}],"traps":["Bettino Ricasoli's Chianti of 1872 included the white Malvasia; white grapes have been forbidden in Chianti Classico since 2006.","Brolio and Castello di Brolio are two wines: the first is the estate's Chianti Classico, the second its Gran Selezione from the vineyards around the castle.","Gran Selezione asks for estate grapes and long ageing; it does not ask for a single vineyard."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-9wp81uqt","front":"Barone Ricasoli: to whom did Bettino Ricasoli write his view of Chianti in 1872, and which grapes did it name?","back":"A professor at the University of Pisa; Sangiovese led, with Canaiolo and the white Malvasia."},{"id":"fl-c01bsfyy","front":"Castello di Brolio: on which three soils do its vineyards around the castle lie?","back":"Macigno sandstone, galestro of the Scaglia Toscana and alberese limestone."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-u1vbzbmv","q":"Gran Selezione is printed on a bottle of Castello di Brolio. What does the term promise a guest?","opts":["Estate grapes and at least thirty months of ageing","A blend that must hold Canaiolo and Malvasia","A single vineyard named on the label","Grapes from the zone and one year in barrel"],"a":0,"exp":"Gran Selezione requires grapes from the bottler's own vineyards and at least thirty months of ageing, three in bottle; it does not require a single vineyard, and white grapes are forbidden.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-x2yu0nm3","q":"In which year did the Ricasoli family buy back the Brolio brand and winery from the drinks trade?","opts":["2013","1993","1872","1984"],"a":1,"exp":"The family regained the brand and the winery in 1993, after the Seagram and Hardy's years; 1872 is Bettino Ricasoli's letter, 1984 Chianti's DOCG and 2013 the vote for Gran Selezione.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Barone Ricasoli: The Ricasolis and the Chianti Classico wine area","url":"https://www.ricasoli.com/en/the-ricasolis-and-the-chianti-classico-wine-area/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Barone Ricasoli: Castello di Brolio 2021, Chianti Classico DOCG Gran Selezione Gaiole","url":"https://www.ricasoli.com/en/product/castello-di-brolio-2021/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Consorzio Vino Chianti Classico: Types of Chianti Classico","url":"https://www.chianticlassico.com/en/wine/types/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wein-Plus Glossary: Ricasoli","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/ricasoli","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Eat Magazine: Wines of Worth, Barone Ricasoli and the Chianti recipe","url":"https://eatmagazine.ca/wine/wines-of-worth-barone-ricasoli-the-chianti-recipe","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-sainm6dg": {"id":"fl-sainm6dg","key":"fontodi","group":"tuscany-chianti","region":"tuscany","producer":"Fontodi","producerId":"p-fontodi","say":"fon-TOH-dee, flah-chah-NEL-loh","place":"Panzano, Greve in Chianti, Chianti Classico zone, Tuscany","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Flaccianello della Pieve","appellation":"Colli della Toscana Centrale IGT","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese alone, from the estate's own vines.","sites":"At first the Flaccianello vineyard alone; from early in the 2000s a selection of the estate's own parcels in the Conca d'Oro, the amphitheatre of slopes below Panzano, on calcareous clay and schist at height.","making":"Wild yeasts ferment the grapes in stainless steel, with at least three weeks on the skins. It then rests eighteen months to two years in French oak, mostly barriques of Allier and Tronçais; later vintages finish in larger casks before bottling.","glass":"Black cherry, plum and iris with espresso, liquorice and warm earth; full and ripe for Sangiovese, held in line by firm, polished tannin and fresh acidity to a long finish.","ageing":"Dense and closed in youth, it opens from about eight years after the vintage; the stronger vintages last twenty years or more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, in a large Bordeaux glass; one to two hours in a decanter for a young bottle, and a gentle pour off the sediment for an old one.","label":[{"term":"Flaccianello della Pieve","means":"The wine's name, first given to one vineyard's Sangiovese and kept for the estate's selection."},{"term":"Colli della Toscana Centrale IGT","means":"The regional category of the hills of central Tuscany, chosen in place of Chianti Classico though the estate lies inside the zone."},{"term":"Fontodi","means":"The estate in the Conca d'Oro below Panzano, bought in 1968 by a family of terracotta makers."}]},"table":"Labelled an IGT though it grows inside the Chianti Classico zone, Flaccianello della Pieve is Fontodi's Sangiovese alone from the Conca d'Oro below Panzano, with 1981 as its first vintage. Dark cherry, iris and espresso over polished tannin; give it a large glass.","story":"The family that bought Fontodi in 1968 was long known in Chianti for its terracotta tiles; the farm lies in the valley below Panzano called the Conca d'Oro, the golden shell, for its amphitheatre shape as the estate tells it. With the 1981 vintage the family made a wine of Sangiovese alone, from a vineyard called Flaccianello. Chianti then had to include white grapes, so the wine stood outside the appellation; it has stayed outside, labelled Colli della Toscana Centrale IGT, although the rules have since changed and Sangiovese alone may be Chianti Classico. Through the 1980s and 1990s the wine was the vineyard's own; early in the 2000s it became a selection of the estate's parcels in the Conca d'Oro, while Vigna del Sorbo stayed inside the appellation as the estate's single vineyard Chianti Classico. The estate is farmed organically and certified, and it fires terracotta of its own. With Le Pergole Torte of 1977 and Cepparello of 1980, Flaccianello opens a second line of the Super Tuscan story, one with no Cabernet in it at all. Of the three, it carries the riper, darker voice of Panzano Sangiovese.","others":[{"name":"Vigna del Sorbo","note":"Chianti Classico Gran Selezione from one vineyard of the estate, the house's single vineyard wine inside the appellation."},{"name":"Fontodi Chianti Classico","note":"Sangiovese from the Conca d'Oro: the house style at its plainest."}],"traps":["Flaccianello is Sangiovese alone: not every wine called a Super Tuscan holds Cabernet or Merlot.","Flaccianello is labelled Colli della Toscana Centrale IGT, not Toscana IGT and not Chianti Classico.","Panzano is a village of Greve in Chianti and one of the village units of Chianti Classico, not a commune of its own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-4l2uyx12","front":"Fontodi: in which vintage was Flaccianello della Pieve first made, and from what?","back":"1981, from Sangiovese alone."},{"id":"fl-4x86r0fz","front":"Why is the valley below Panzano where Fontodi's vines grow called the Conca d'Oro?","back":"By Fontodi's account for its amphitheatre shape, the golden shell; others trace the gold to the wheat once grown there."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-e7g6ssea","q":"Flaccianello della Pieve grows inside the Chianti Classico zone. Under which name is it labelled?","opts":["Chianti Classico Gran Selezione","Chianti Colli Fiorentini DOCG","Colli della Toscana Centrale IGT","Toscana IGT"],"a":2,"exp":"Fontodi labels it Colli della Toscana Centrale IGT; the near miss is Toscana IGT, the label of Tignanello and Cepparello, and the estate keeps Gran Selezione for Vigna del Sorbo.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1c51a30x","q":"In which year did the family that made Fontodi a wine estate buy the farm below Panzano?","opts":["1977","1956","1981","1968"],"a":3,"exp":"The family bought Fontodi in 1968; 1981 is Flaccianello's first vintage, 1977 that of Le Pergole Torte and 1956 the year Isole e Olena was bought.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Fontodi: Chi siamo","url":"https://www.fontodi.com/en/Chi-siamo.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Fontodi: Panzano in Chianti","url":"https://www.fontodi.com/en/Home.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Fontodi's Flaccianello della Pieve, ten vintages compared","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/fontodi-flaccianello-wines-403921-403921/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Best of Wines: Flaccianello della Pieve","url":"https://bestofwines.com/wine/flaccianello-della-pieve/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vino.com: Flaccianello della Pieve 2021, Azienda Agricola Fontodi","url":"https://www.vino.com/en/dettaglio/colli-toscana-centrale-igt-2021-flaccianello-della-pieve-azienda-agricola-fontodi.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9b4sii7p": {"id":"fl-9b4sii7p","key":"montevertine","group":"tuscany-chianti","region":"tuscany","producer":"Montevertine","producerId":"p-montevertine","say":"mon-teh-ver-TEE-neh, leh PEHR-go-leh TOR-teh","place":"Radda in Chianti, Chianti Classico zone, Tuscany","founded":"1967","wine":{"name":"Le Pergole Torte","appellation":"Toscana IGT","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese alone.","sites":"The estate's historic vineyards above Radda, planted between 1968 and 1999, among them the Le Pergole Torte vineyard of 1968, which faces north north east; the house stands at about 425 metres.","making":"Picked by hand and fermented with two pump overs a day in glass lined cement vats, with no temperature control, for about three weeks; the malolactic conversion also takes place in cement. Ageing takes about twelve months in Allier barriques and twelve in casks of Slavonian oak, then some three months in bottle.","glass":"Pale ruby; sour cherry, rosehip and violet with dried herbs and a cold, stony edge; light on its feet, with high acidity, fine, taut tannin and a long, perfumed finish.","ageing":"Tight for its first years, it comes round from about eight to ten years after the vintage, and a strong year lasts for decades.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; a young bottle wants an hour in a decanter and an old one a careful pour off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass shows the perfume.","label":[{"term":"Le Pergole Torte","means":"The name of a vineyard of the estate, planted in 1968, given to the wine since its first vintage of 1977."},{"term":"Toscana IGT","means":"Tuscany's regional category; from the 1982 vintage on, no wine of the estate has carried a Chianti name."},{"term":"Montevertine","means":"The estate above Radda, bought in 1967, and also the name of its Sangiovese blend with Canaiolo and Colorino."}]},"table":"Pale and perfumed, rosehip and sour cherry over taut tannin, Le Pergole Torte is nothing but Sangiovese, grown by Montevertine in high vineyards above Radda. First made in 1977, it is the wine of an estate that refused to add white grapes and left the Chianti Classico consorzio in 1981.","story":"A steel industrialist bought Montevertine, a farmhouse above Radda, in 1967 as a place for holidays. He restored the house, planted vines, set up a small cellar to make wine for friends and clients, and sold his first vintage, the 1971, as Chianti Classico Montevertine. His first wine of Sangiovese alone came with the 1977 vintage, named for his Le Pergole Torte vineyard, by the estate's account the first of its kind in the Radda zone. Chianti at the time had to contain white Trebbiano and Malvasia; he refused to add them, and in 1981 he left the Chianti Classico consorzio. From 1982 his wines carried no Chianti name at all, and Le Pergole Torte was sold as table wine and later as Toscana IGT. In the 1980s its label took a woman's face drawn by an artist friend of the founder, in designs that vary from vintage to vintage. The vineyards have been farmed organically since 2009. The rules moved towards him, white grapes leaving Chianti Classico in 2006 and Sangiovese alone becoming lawful, but the estate has stayed outside. Here is an estate that left its appellation to be purer, not more international.","others":[{"name":"Montevertine","note":"The estate's red of Sangiovese with a little Canaiolo and Colorino, sold as Chianti Classico Montevertine from 1971 to 1981."},{"name":"Pian del Ciampolo","note":"The young vine red: the same Sangiovese, Canaiolo and Colorino, drawn lighter."}],"traps":["Le Pergole Torte is Sangiovese alone; its fame has nothing to do with Cabernet.","Montevertine bottles no wine as Chianti Classico, by choice, though it lies inside the zone at Radda.","Le Pergole Torte, Cepparello and Flaccianello are all Sangiovese alone; Le Pergole Torte came first, with the 1977 vintage, before 1980 and 1981."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-mhzd7oi9","front":"Montevertine: why did the estate leave the Chianti Classico consorzio in 1981?","back":"Its founder refused to add the white grapes the Chianti rules then required."},{"id":"fl-thgfzbt4","front":"In which vessels does Le Pergole Torte ferment, and with what temperature control?","back":"Glass lined cement vats, with none."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-qe3he4ve","q":"How does Montevertine raise Le Pergole Torte between fermentation and bottling?","opts":["A year in barriques, then a year in Slavonian casks","Eighteen months in large chestnut casks","Two years in new French barriques alone","A year in cement, then early bottling"],"a":0,"exp":"Montevertine gives about twelve months in Allier barriques and twelve in casks of Slavonian oak; it ferments in cement but is not raised there, and new barrique alone is the near miss.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1i4q8jbn","q":"Which was the first vintage of Montevertine's Le Pergole Torte, made from Sangiovese alone?","opts":["1981","1977","1971","1967"],"a":1,"exp":"Le Pergole Torte began with 1977; 1971 is the estate's first vintage, 1967 the year it was bought and 1981 the year it left the consorzio.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Montevertine: Azienda","url":"https://www.montevertine.it/azienda/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Montevertine: Le Pergole Torte","url":"https://www.montevertine.it/prodotti/le-pergole-torte/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Montevertine: Vigneti","url":"https://www.montevertine.it/vigneti/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Montevertine","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/montevertine/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"We Wealth: Le Pergole Torte, un grande Sangiovese in purezza","url":"https://www.we-wealth.com/en/news/le-pergole-torte-un-grande-sangiovese-in-purezza","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ph1q0yrn": {"id":"fl-ph1q0yrn","key":"isole-e-olena","group":"tuscany-chianti","region":"tuscany","producer":"Isole e Olena","producerId":"p-isole-e-olena","say":"EE-zo-leh eh oh-LEH-nah, chep-pah-REL-loh","place":"San Donato in Poggio, Barberino Tavarnelle, Chianti Classico zone, Tuscany","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Cepparello","appellation":"Toscana IGT","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese alone.","sites":"A selection of grapes from the estate's older vineyards in the western hills of the Chianti Classico zone, at about 350 to 450 metres, mostly on galestro, a rocky schist and clay.","making":"The grapes macerate on their skins for longer than those of the estate's Chianti Classico. The wine is then raised for eighteen months or more in small French oak barrels, about a third of them new, and rests in bottle before release.","glass":"Red cherry, violet and dried herbs with orange peel and a touch of cedar; medium bodied and fresh, with fine, chalky tannin, drawn long rather than broad.","ageing":"Fragrant within a few years of the vintage, it is at its fullest from about eight to fifteen years, and longer after a strong year.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter helps a young bottle; a large, wide bowled glass.","label":[{"term":"Cepparello","means":"A name the estate chose for the wine, not a village and not a vineyard designation."},{"term":"Toscana IGT","means":"Tuscany's regional category, carried because the wine was born outside the Chianti rules."},{"term":"Isole e Olena","means":"The estate named for two neighbouring hamlets, Isole and Olena, bought and joined as one in 1956."}]},"table":"One grape alone, Sangiovese, goes into Isole e Olena's Cepparello, from old vines in the western hills of Chianti Classico; first made in 1980, it is raised for a year and a half or more in French barriques. Red cherry, violet and dried herbs, drawn fine and long.","story":"Two neighbouring estates at San Donato in Poggio, around the hamlets of Isole and Olena, were bought in 1956 by a family from Piedmont and joined as one. The founder's son took charge in the middle of the 1970s and set out to learn what Sangiovese could do on its own. The view in Chianti then held that a fine wine could not be made from Sangiovese alone, and the law asked for white grapes in the blend. In 1980 he made the first Cepparello, pure Sangiovese from the estate's old vines, sold outside the appellation as a table wine; it later took the Toscana IGT label. Its name is said to come from a small stream, the Borro Cepparello, that runs among the estate's vineyards. With Le Pergole Torte and Flaccianello it stood among the Sangiovese wines that left Chianti Classico when its rules would not have them pure. The estate also grew known for its Vin Santo, and for small plantings of Syrah, Chardonnay and Cabernet Sauvignon. In 2022, after more than six decades in one family, Isole e Olena was sold to the EPI group. Against Panzano and Radda, the western hills bring perfume and clarity rather than weight, and Cepparello is the place to taste them.","others":[{"name":"Isole e Olena Chianti Classico","note":"Sangiovese with a little Canaiolo, long a fixture on wine lists."},{"name":"Vin Santo del Chianti Classico","note":"The estate's sweet wine of dried Trebbiano and Malvasia grapes."}],"traps":["Cepparello is a wine name, not a vineyard designation within the DOCG.","Vin Santo of Tuscany is not Vinsanto of Santorini.","Cepparello and Flaccianello are both Sangiovese alone, but Cepparello is labelled Toscana IGT and Flaccianello Colli della Toscana Centrale IGT."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-b4uo0t7r","front":"Isole e Olena's Cepparello: which vintage came first, and from which vines?","back":"1980, Sangiovese alone from the estate's old vines."},{"id":"fl-jfmbb1yc","front":"Where does the estate name Isole e Olena come from?","back":"Two neighbouring hamlets, Isole and Olena, bought and joined as one in 1956."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-nuu8w8l8","q":"Which grapes go into Isole e Olena's Cepparello?","opts":["Sangiovese with Syrah","Sangiovese with Canaiolo","Sangiovese alone","Sangiovese with Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":2,"exp":"Cepparello is Sangiovese alone, from old vines; Canaiolo goes into the estate's Chianti Classico, and the estate's Syrah is bottled on its own.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-empt50k1","q":"When Cepparello was first made in 1980, what kept it out of Chianti Classico?","opts":["It was blended with Cabernet Sauvignon","Its grapes came from outside the Classico zone","It was made from bought grapes","The rules then required white grapes in the blend"],"a":3,"exp":"Chianti's rules then required white Trebbiano and Malvasia, and Cepparello was Sangiovese alone; its grapes grow on the estate inside the zone, and the first vintage held no Cabernet.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Isole e Olena: Cepparello 2020","url":"https://shop.isoleeolena.it/de/eur/en/products/cepparello-2020-0-75-litri","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Isole e Olena, Cepparello wines to drink and to keep","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/isole-e-olena-producer-profile-382217/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Italy's Finest Wines: Isole e Olena","url":"https://www.italysfinestwines.it/isole-e-olena/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Liberty Wines: Isole e Olena, Cepparello","url":"https://www.libertywines.co.uk/our-portfolio/IO103-cepparello","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The World of Fine Wine: Cepparello 2006 to 2020","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/tasting-notes/cepparello-2006-2020-pure-super-tuscan","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-j7cx81we": {"id":"fl-j7cx81we","key":"biondi-santi","group":"tuscany-montalcino","region":"tuscany","producer":"Biondi-Santi","producerId":"p-biondi-santi","say":"bee-OHN-dee SAHN-tee, broo-NEL-loh","place":"Tenuta Greppo, Montalcino, Brunello di Montalcino DOCG, Tuscany","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino Riserva","appellation":"Brunello di Montalcino DOCG","classification":"Riserva of the Brunello di Montalcino DOCG, released from the sixth year after the harvest","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese alone, the strain the estate calls Sangiovese Grosso, from its own vines at Greppo.","sites":"The Greppo hillside south east of Montalcino town, between about 300 and 500 metres by an estate sheet, on stony soils of mixed origin, the galestro shale prized among them; the Riserva comes from the oldest vines, over twenty five years old.","making":"By the estate's sheet for the 2016 vintage, the grapes ferment with native yeasts in upright Slavonian oak vats. The wine then ages for three years in large Slavonian oak casks, with no new wood, and rests in bottle until its release, no sooner than 1 January of the sixth year after the harvest. The estate makes a Riserva only when it thinks the year deserves one.","glass":"Sour cherry, dried rose and tea leaf over iron and a touch of leather; pale garnet, taut and high toned, with high acidity, fine but firm tannin and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Closed and severe for its first decade or more, it unfolds over many decades; the estate keeps bottles of its 1888 and 1891 Riservas in its own cellar.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle two hours ahead, and stand an old one upright for a day before pouring it off its sediment; a large, tulip shaped glass.","label":[{"term":"Brunello di Montalcino","means":"Sangiovese alone from the Montalcino zone, aged at least two years in oak before release."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Released no sooner than 1 January of the sixth year after the harvest, a year after the ordinary Brunello."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita","means":"Italy's guaranteed tier, granted to Brunello di Montalcino in 1980."}]},"table":"Ferruccio Biondi Santi made the 1888 at Greppo, counted by the consorzio as the first wine labelled Brunello di Montalcino, and Biondi-Santi's Riserva comes from that hillside: Sangiovese alone, three years in large Slavonian casks. Decant it and give it time, for it is taut and slow to open.","story":"Greppo belonged to the Santi family in the nineteenth century, and by the consorzio's account the first wine to win recognition under the name Brunello, of the 1865 vintage, was made by a Montalcino chemist of that family and honoured in 1869. His grandson Ferruccio Biondi Santi, who died in 1917, held to Sangiovese alone and to long ageing in large casks while oidium and phylloxera threatened the region's vines. His 1888 is counted by the consorzio as the first wine officially labelled Brunello di Montalcino, and bottles of it are kept at Greppo, topped up and recorked in 1927, 1970 and 1985. Ferruccio's son and then his grandson carried the estate through the twentieth century. The estate's own Sangiovese selection was registered as the clone BBS11 in 1978, and Brunello, a DOC since 1966, became by the consorzio's account Italy's first DOCG in 1980. The grandson died in 2013, and at the end of 2016 the family sold a majority of the estate to the French group EPI. The Riserva is where the denomination's long ageing began: when a guest asks what Brunello was before it was a rule, this is the bottle to explain it.","others":[{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino","note":"The estate's annata Brunello, released a year before the Riserva of the same vintage."},{"name":"Rosso di Montalcino","note":"Greppo Sangiovese aged far more briefly, a lighter frame for the same voice."}],"traps":["Brunello di Montalcino is Sangiovese alone; Chianti Classico requires only a Sangiovese minimum, so no blending rule of Chianti applies here.","The 1888 is counted as the first wine labelled Brunello di Montalcino, but the name Brunello was first honoured, in 1869, for a Santi wine of the 1865 vintage.","The Castello di Montepò wines are a separate business of the family, not wines of Tenuta Greppo."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-36ead57p","front":"Biondi-Santi: what is BBS11, and when was it registered?","back":"The estate's own Sangiovese selection from Greppo, named for the family and formally registered as a clone in 1978."},{"id":"fl-aixpwzsz","front":"Which vintage of Biondi-Santi does the consorzio count as the first wine officially labelled Brunello di Montalcino?","back":"The 1888, made by Ferruccio Biondi Santi; bottles are kept at Greppo, recorked in 1927, 1970 and 1985."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-c0hn1y9t","q":"At the table, a guest wants to know what sets Biondi-Santi's Riserva apart from the estate's ordinary Brunello. What do you tell them?","opts":["A share of Cabernet Sauvignon in the blend","Grapes from the estate's oldest vines","Ageing in new French barriques","Grapes bought from the south of the zone"],"a":1,"exp":"The Riserva is drawn from Greppo's oldest parcels, long named as vines over twenty five years old; like all Brunello it is Sangiovese alone, and the estate ages it in large old Slavonian casks, never new barriques.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rjs7l79y","q":"The consorzio counts a Greppo wine of 1888 as the first officially labelled Brunello di Montalcino. Who made it?","opts":["Ferruccio Biondi Santi","A Florentine landowner at Sant'Angelo in Colle","Two brothers of a New York importing family","An insurance broker from Milan"],"a":0,"exp":"Ferruccio Biondi Santi, grandson of the Santi chemist, made the 1888; the New York brothers founded Castello Banfi only in 1978.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Consorzio del vino Brunello di Montalcino: 1861 to 2011, 150 years of wine history","url":"https://www.consorziobrunellodimontalcino.it/en/1746/1861-2011-150-years-of-italian-history-and-150-years-of-wine-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Biondi-Santi: Brunello di Montalcino Riserva 1888","url":"https://www.biondisanti.it/en/prodotti/vini/7/vini/79/index.php?p=prodotti&lg=en&id_cat=7&id_prod=79&a=1888","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Biondi-Santi: Brunello di Montalcino Riserva 2016","url":"https://www.biondisanti.it/en/prodotti/vini/7/vini/79/index.php?p=prodotti&lg=en&id_cat=7&id_prod=79&a=2016","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: French firm EPI purchases majority stake in Biondi-Santi","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/epi-purchases-majority-stake-in-biondi-santi-wine-estate","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Franco Biondi Santi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franco_Biondi_Santi","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-5t87o3rb": {"id":"fl-5t87o3rb","key":"il-poggione","group":"tuscany-montalcino","region":"tuscany","producer":"Tenuta Il Poggione","producerId":"p-il-poggione","say":"eel pohj-JOH-neh","place":"Sant'Angelo in Colle, Brunello di Montalcino DOCG, Tuscany","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino","appellation":"Brunello di Montalcino DOCG","classification":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita, granted to Brunello di Montalcino in 1980","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese only, picked by hand from the oldest vines on the estate.","sites":"The family's vineyards around Sant'Angelo in Colle, some ten kilometres south of Montalcino, between 150 and 450 metres, in a warm, breezy corner between Monte Amiata and the sea; its Sangiovese is selected from the estate's own old vines.","making":"For fifteen to twenty days the grapes ferment in stainless steel with native yeasts, the cap of skins kept submerged in the wine throughout; malolactic follows in steel. About three years in large French oak casks of 30 and 50 hectolitres come next, before the wine rests in bottle.","glass":"Ripe cherry and plum with tobacco leaf, dried rose and a touch of liquorice; generous and warm for Brunello, with fresh acidity and firm, ripe tannin.","ageing":"Drinks from about eight to ten years after the vintage, and a strong year carries it past twenty.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter suits a young bottle, while an older one wants a slow pour off its deposit; a generous red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Brunello di Montalcino","means":"Sangiovese alone, released from 1 January of the fifth year after the harvest, after two years at least in oak."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita","means":"Guaranteed and controlled origin: Brunello's rank since 1980, after a DOC granted in 1966."}]},"table":"Sant'Angelo in Colle, on the warm southern side of Montalcino, gives Il Poggione's Brunello di Montalcino: Sangiovese from the estate's oldest vines, fermented with the cap held under the wine and aged about three years in large French casks. Its ripe generosity is Brunello's southern face.","story":"Late in the nineteenth century, as the family tells it, a Florentine landowner heard a shepherd who wintered his flock around Montalcino describe the country around Sant'Angelo in Colle; he went to see it, bought land there and founded the farm his descendants have kept ever since. The estate counts itself among the first to sell its wine as Brunello di Montalcino, early in the twentieth century. In 1964 the family planted Vigna Paganelli on an alluvial terrace, the vineyard that gives its Riserva. Brunello was made a DOC in 1966, and in 1967, when the growers formed the consorzio, the house was, by its own account, among its founding members; the DOCG followed in 1980. Through those decades the Brunello kept a method of its own. The grapes ferment in stainless steel with the cap of skins held under the wine, and the wine spends about three years in large casks of French oak before it is bottled. Its warmth is the warmth of the south of the hill, between Monte Amiata and the sea air. Taught beside Biondi-Santi's Greppo, higher on the hill and nearer the town, Il Poggione shows how the south of Montalcino gives a riper, rounder Sangiovese.","others":[{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino Riserva Vigna Paganelli","note":"From the vineyard planted in 1964 on an alluvial terrace, made as a Riserva."},{"name":"Rosso di Montalcino","note":"The estate's Sangiovese released far sooner, a fresher view of the house."}],"traps":["Sant'Angelo in Colle is a village within the Brunello di Montalcino zone, not a separate denomination; the label says Brunello di Montalcino.","Il Poggione and Poggio Antico are different estates, and neither is Castello Banfi's Poggio alle Mura or Poggio all'Oro."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-361n3wy0","front":"Il Poggione: how is the cap of skins handled while its Brunello ferments?","back":"It is kept submerged in the wine, in stainless steel with native yeasts, for fifteen to twenty days."},{"id":"fl-ydpjagh8","front":"Which Il Poggione vineyard, planted in 1964 on an alluvial terrace, gives its Riserva?","back":"Vigna Paganelli."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-mcnbkmpy","q":"For how long, and in what, does Il Poggione age its Brunello before bottling?","opts":["Eighteen months in new French barriques","Five years in concrete tanks","One year in chestnut barrels","About three years in large French oak casks"],"a":3,"exp":"Il Poggione ages its Brunello about three years in large French oak casks of 30 and 50 hectolitres; small new barriques belong to the modern camp, not to this house.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1hz4q30t","q":"Il Poggione counts itself among the founders of the Brunello consorzio. In which year was the consorzio formed?","opts":["1888","1932","1967","1980"],"a":2,"exp":"The consorzio was formed in 1967, a year after Brunello became a DOC; 1980 is the year of the DOCG.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Tenuta Il Poggione: Estate","url":"https://www.tenutailpoggione.it/en/274/estate","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tenuta Il Poggione: Terroir","url":"https://www.tenutailpoggione.it/en/223/terroir","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tenuta Il Poggione: Brunello di Montalcino 2018 technical sheet","url":"https://tenutailpoggione.it/pdfgen/sheet/8/109/en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Il Poggione, tasting four decades of Brunello","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/il-poggione-brunello-411461/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Consorzio del vino Brunello di Montalcino: The wines","url":"https://www.consorziobrunellodimontalcino.it/en/583/wines","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-u9nlbgz5": {"id":"fl-u9nlbgz5","key":"banfi","group":"tuscany-montalcino","region":"tuscany","producer":"Castello Banfi","producerId":"p-castello-banfi","say":"BAHN-fee, POHJ-joh ahl-LOH-roh","place":"Poggio alle Mura, Montalcino, Brunello di Montalcino DOCG, Tuscany","founded":"1978","wine":{"name":"Poggio all'Oro Brunello di Montalcino Riserva","appellation":"Brunello di Montalcino DOCG","classification":"Riserva of the Brunello di Montalcino DOCG, released no sooner than a year after the annata","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese alone, as every Brunello must be.","sites":"A single vineyard on a broad plateau at about 230 to 250 metres, on brown clay and silt holding calcareous stones and pebbles that drain well.","making":"Made only in vintages the estate thinks worthy of it. Since the estate built its Horizon tanks, which join French oak to stainless steel, it has fermented in them at controlled temperature; earlier vintages fermented in steel. From 2015 it has spent thirty months or more in French oak, most of it in casks of 60 to 90 hectolitres and the rest in 350 litre barrels.","glass":"Red cherry and black plum with iris, cigar box and a touch of vanilla; fuller and rounder than the Greppo style, with lively acidity and polished, supple tannin.","ageing":"Released no sooner than the sixth year after the harvest, it opens over the next few years and holds for twenty years and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, as the estate suggests; decant for an hour to lift the perfume and pour a mature bottle off its sediment; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Poggio all'Oro","means":"The single plateau vineyard of the estate, bottled on its own only in chosen years."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Brunello held back longer, leaving the cellar no sooner than 1 January of the sixth year after the harvest."},{"term":"Castello Banfi","means":"The Montalcino estate founded in 1978 by the New York importing family."}]},"table":"A New York importing family built Castello Banfi, and Poggio all'Oro is its single vineyard Brunello di Montalcino Riserva, made from one broad plateau since the 1985 vintage and only in chosen years. It is riper and rounder than the old school, with supple tannin.","story":"The name is older than the estate. The import firm the Mariani family founded in New York in 1919 was named, as the family tells it, for a woman who was the first lay person to run a pope's household. From 1969 its fortune grew on Riunite Lambrusco, which it sold across the United States. In 1978 two brothers of the second generation came to Montalcino and founded Castello Banfi on land in the south west of the zone. Studies of the estate's soils began in 1980, mapping where Sangiovese would do well; in 1983, by the estate's own timeline, it acquired the medieval castle of Poggio alle Mura, and the winery opened on 12 September 1984. The first Poggio all'Oro followed with the 1985 vintage, a Riserva from one plateau, made only in years the estate judges worthy. Banfi brought scale, a technical cellar and an American sales network to a small hill town, and the estate credits one of the brothers with launching Brunello in the United States. Poggio all'Oro answers a question worth asking at the table: whether a large estate can bottle a single site with the patience Brunello asks.","others":[{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino Poggio alle Mura","note":"Brunello from the vineyards around the castle, also made as a Riserva."},{"name":"Castello Banfi Brunello di Montalcino","note":"The annata, and the face of the house in export markets."},{"name":"Florus","note":"Moscadello di Montalcino, the zone's sweet white from Moscato Bianco."}],"traps":["Poggio all'Oro and Poggio alle Mura are two different Banfi wines: the first from one plateau, the second from the vineyards around the castle.","Banfi's size does not loosen the rule: its Brunello, Poggio all'Oro included, is Sangiovese alone.","Banfi is the name of the import firm founded in New York in 1919, not of a Montalcino family or a village."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-4etadnu9","front":"Castello Banfi's Poggio all'Oro: when did it begin, and how often is it made?","back":"1985, a Riserva from a single plateau vineyard, made since in chosen years only."},{"id":"fl-zg1h3pkf","front":"Which brand of Lambrusco built the fortune of the New York firm behind Castello Banfi, from 1969?","back":"Riunite, imported and sold across the United States."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-rt9e479y","q":"Where does Castello Banfi grow the grapes for Poggio all'Oro?","opts":["A broad plateau of clay and stones","The vineyards around the castle of Poggio alle Mura","Bought grapes from across the zone","A steep slope below the town walls"],"a":0,"exp":"Poggio all'Oro is one broad plateau at about 230 to 250 metres, on brown clay with stones and pebbles; the vineyards around the castle give Poggio alle Mura instead.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-5gwo9tfp","q":"In 1978 two brothers founded Castello Banfi at Montalcino. What business had built their family's fortune?","opts":["Making Champagne in Reims","Importing wine into America","Shipping out of Genoa","Banking in Florence"],"a":1,"exp":"The Mariani family's New York import firm, founded in 1919, grew on Riunite Lambrusco from 1969; that business built the Montalcino estate.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Banfi: Mondo Banfi","url":"https://www.banfi.it/eng/banfi-world/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Banfi: 12 September 1984, the Banfi winery is founded in Montalcino","url":"https://www.banfi.it/eng/september-12-1984-the-banfi-winery-is-founded-in-montalcino/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Banfi: Poggio all'Oro 2016 technical sheet","url":"https://www.banfi.it/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/PAO2016_ENG.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Castello Banfi's Poggio all'Oro, vertical tasting","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/castello-banfis-poggio-alloro-vertical-tasting-478655/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Banfi Vintners","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banfi_Vintners","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-cw1z1174": {"id":"fl-cw1z1174","key":"casanova-di-neri","group":"tuscany-montalcino","region":"tuscany","producer":"Casanova di Neri","producerId":"p-casanova-di-neri","say":"kah-zah-NOH-vah dee NEH-ree, teh-NOO-tah NWOH-vah","place":"Montalcino, Brunello di Montalcino DOCG, Tuscany","founded":"1971","wine":{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino Tenuta Nuova","appellation":"Brunello di Montalcino DOCG","classification":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita; Tenuta Nuova is a vineyard name, not an official cru","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese, and nothing else, from the estate's own vines on the Tenuta Nuova site.","sites":"Tenuta Nuova, the new property, is land where, by the estate's account, nobody had planted Sangiovese before, chosen after a study of the local microclimates and their exposures, with its clones, rootstocks and planting density chosen by study as well.","making":"Bunches are picked by hand and passed through an optical sorter, then ferment with native yeasts for about twenty four days in truncated cone stainless steel tanks at controlled temperature. It then spends some thirty months or more in oak, by the 2019 vintage in French tonneaux in place of barriques, and a year or more in bottle before release.","glass":"Black cherry and plum with espresso, cocoa and a touch of clove; darker, rounder and more polished than the traditional camp, with ripe, firm tannin and fresh acidity under the fruit.","ageing":"Approachable sooner than traditional Brunello, it opens from about eight years after the vintage and a strong year holds for fifteen to twenty.","serve":"Pour at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius after an hour or two in a decanter; a large glass with a wide bowl.","label":[{"term":"Tenuta Nuova","means":"New property: the name the house gave a site it planted to Sangiovese for the first time."},{"term":"Brunello di Montalcino","means":"The denomination: Sangiovese only, from the Montalcino zone, kept two years or more in oak before release."}]},"table":"Casanova di Neri planted Tenuta Nuova, by its own account, on land that had never carried Sangiovese, and the Brunello di Montalcino from it began with the 1993 vintage. Dark, polished and generous, it stands in the modern camp of Montalcino.","story":"In 1971 the founder began to farm on the eastern side of Montalcino, and the estate dates its first Brunello harvest to 1978. Its first vineyard, as the estate tells it, was Cerretalto, a natural amphitheatre above the Asso river, bottled on its own from the 1981 vintage and only in selected years; Le Cetine, Pietradonice and Podernovo followed in time. In 1991 the founder handed the estate to his son, and two years later came the wine that changed its standing: Tenuta Nuova, the new property, first made in 1993 from land where, the estate says, nobody had grown Sangiovese, chosen after a study of the local microclimates. Riper, darker and more polished than the Brunello of the old houses, it carried the modern style of Montalcino to a wide audience. By the 2019 vintage the house had moved towards shorter maceration and gentler oak, ageing Tenuta Nuova in French tonneaux rather than barriques. Montalcino has no official cru system, so the name on the label is the estate's own. Tenuta Nuova takes the other side from Biondi-Santi and Soldera in Montalcino's old argument between large old casks and smaller, newer wood.","others":[{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino Cerretalto","note":"From a natural amphitheatre above the Asso river, first bottled on its own with the 1981 vintage, and only in selected years."},{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino","note":"The house's more traditional bottling, its annata."}],"traps":["Montalcino has no legal cru classification: Tenuta Nuova and Cerretalto are names the estate gives its own sites.","Tenuta Nuova is a Casanova di Neri wine, not a separate producer; the words mean new property."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ayma3nqz","front":"Casanova di Neri's Tenuta Nuova: in what vessels does it ferment, and for how long?","back":"Truncated cone stainless steel tanks at controlled temperature, for about twenty four days, after the bunches pass an optical sorter."},{"id":"fl-eaap2l2j","front":"Which Casanova di Neri vineyard lies in a natural amphitheatre above the Asso river?","back":"Cerretalto, bottled on its own as a Brunello from the 1981 vintage, and only in selected years."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-qp9gf8kn","q":"Tenuta Nuova means new property. What, by Casanova di Neri's account, was new about the site?","opts":["It lies inside the walls of the town","It was the estate's first vineyard","It was bought from Biondi-Santi","Nobody had planted Sangiovese there before"],"a":3,"exp":"Tenuta Nuova, new property, is land where, by the estate's account, nobody had planted Sangiovese; the estate's first vineyard was Cerretalto.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-lbu0vwj8","q":"Casanova di Neri was founded in 1971. With which vintage did Tenuta Nuova first appear?","opts":["1971","1978","1993","2001"],"a":2,"exp":"Tenuta Nuova came with the 1993 vintage, two years after the founder handed on the estate; 1978 was the estate's first Brunello harvest.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Casanova di Neri: The estate","url":"https://www.casanovadineri.it/en/casanova-di-neri.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Casanova di Neri: Tenuta Nuova","url":"https://www.casanovadineri.it/en/tenuta-nuova-brunello-di-montalcino.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Casanova di Neri: Vineyards","url":"https://www.casanovadineri.it/en/vigneti.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Berry Bros. and Rudd: 2019 Brunello di Montalcino Tenuta Nuova","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-20198117975-2019-brunello-di-montalcino-tenuta-nuova-casanova-di-neri-tuscany-italy","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Cellar Tours: Casanova di Neri winery","url":"https://www.cellartours.com/italy/italian-wineries/casanova-di-neri","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-vj469dtd": {"id":"fl-vj469dtd","key":"soldera","group":"tuscany-montalcino","region":"tuscany","producer":"Case Basse di Gianfranco Soldera","producerId":"p-soldera","say":"KAH-zeh BAHS-seh, sol-DEH-rah","place":"Tavernelle, Montalcino, Toscana IGT, Tuscany","founded":"1972","wine":{"name":"Soldera Case Basse Sangiovese","appellation":"Toscana IGT, written Toscana IGP on later labels","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Sangiovese alone, from the estate's own vines, picked by hand.","sites":"The Case Basse and Intistieti vineyards near Tavernelle in the south west of Montalcino, at about 320 metres, farmed beside a botanical garden of roses, with nesting boxes and beehives among the rows for the birds and insects that keep the vines healthy.","making":"The berries are checked one by one, then ferment spontaneously with native yeasts in large Slavonian oak vats. The wine then ages for several years in large Slavonian oak casks before it is bottled.","glass":"Pale garnet; dried rose, sour cherry and orange peel over forest floor and a note of tea, light in colour and weight yet long and searching, with silky tannin and bright acidity.","ageing":"Released only after long cask ageing, it drinks from about ten years after the vintage and develops for decades in bottle.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant gently an hour ahead, pouring an older bottle slowly off its sediment; a large Burgundy glass for the perfume.","label":[{"term":"Toscana IGT","means":"The regional category the estate has used since the 2006 vintage in place of Brunello, written IGP on later labels."},{"term":"Case Basse","means":"The farm near Tavernelle bought in 1972, its name printed beside Soldera's."},{"term":"Sangiovese","means":"The one grape, which a Toscana wine may name on its label."}]},"table":"Toscana IGT, not Brunello, has been on the label of Soldera Case Basse since the 2006 vintage, though the wine is pure Sangiovese from a Montalcino estate founded in 1972. It is fermented with native yeasts in large oak vats and aged for years in big Slavonian casks.","story":"Born in Treviso and an insurance broker in Milan, Gianfranco Soldera (1937 to 2019) went looking for a wine estate with his wife in 1972 and bought Case Basse, a farm near Tavernelle in the south west of Montalcino. Together they planted it afresh and set a botanical garden of roses beside the vines, with nests and hives for the birds and insects that keep the vines healthy. In the cellar he fermented with native yeasts in large oak vats and aged his wine for years in big Slavonian casks; in good years it was bottled as Brunello di Montalcino Riserva. On the night of 2 December 2012 a former employee opened the taps of his casks, and most of the wine of six vintages, 2007 to 2012, ran to waste. Fellow producers and the consorzio offered wine of their own to make good the loss, and he turned it down; in March 2013 he left the consorzio. From the 2006 vintage, which the attack did not reach, his wine has been labelled Toscana IGT. He died in February 2019. No Brunello appears on its label, and Case Basse is famous all the same.","others":[{"name":"Brunello di Montalcino Riserva Case Basse","note":"The label of good vintages before 2006; other years were bottled as Brunello di Montalcino."}],"traps":["From the 2006 vintage Soldera is Toscana IGT, not Brunello di Montalcino, though it is pure Sangiovese grown inside the Brunello zone.","The vandalism of December 2012 destroyed most of the 2007 to 2012 wine in cask, which is why certain years are missing or scarce.","Intistieti is the estate's other vineyard, beside Case Basse, and not a house of its own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ftfs6cr9","front":"Soldera Case Basse: why does the label say Toscana IGT rather than Brunello di Montalcino?","back":"The estate left the Brunello consorzio in 2013, after the vandalism of 2012, and chose Toscana IGT from the 2006 vintage, trusting its own name over Brunello's."},{"id":"fl-65xy73he","front":"What happened in the Case Basse cellar of Soldera on the night of 2 December 2012?","back":"A former employee opened the taps of the casks, and most of the wine of the 2007 to 2012 vintages was lost."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-czr8nifc","q":"How, and with which yeasts, is Case Basse fermented at Soldera?","opts":["Carbonic maceration in sealed concrete tanks","Native yeasts in large Slavonian oak vats","Cultured yeasts in temperature controlled steel","Whole clusters in clay amphorae"],"a":1,"exp":"The estate ferments spontaneously with native yeasts in large Slavonian oak vats; it uses no cultured yeasts, and its vats are oak, not steel.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-q9h7gps6","q":"Before he bought Case Basse in 1972, what was Gianfranco Soldera's trade?","opts":["An insurance broker in Milan","A wine importer in New York","A chemist in Montalcino","A cooper in Slavonia"],"a":0,"exp":"He was an insurance broker in Milan; the chemist is in the Santi story at Greppo, and the New York importers founded Banfi.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Soldera Case Basse: Soldera wines by vintage","url":"https://www.soldera.it/en/soldera-wines-by-vintage/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Soldera Case Basse: Toscana IGP Sangiovese 2021 technical sheet","url":"https://www.soldera.it/wp-content/uploads/SCHEDA_2021_UK.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Gianfranco Soldera, Brunello winemaker, dies at 82","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/gianfranco-soldera-dies-at-82","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Brunello's Soldera accused of deeply offensive libel","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/brunello-s-soldera-accused-of-deeply-offensive-libel-19514/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Case Basse di Gianfranco Soldera","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Case_Basse_di_Gianfranco_Soldera","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-1r9mc2wg": {"id":"fl-1r9mc2wg","key":"san-guido","group":"tuscany-bolgheri","region":"tuscany","producer":"Tenuta San Guido","producerId":"p-tenuta-san-guido","say":"sahs-see-KAH-yah","place":"Bolgheri, Bolgheri Sassicaia DOC, on the Tuscan coast","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Sassicaia","appellation":"Bolgheri Sassicaia","classification":"A DOC of its own since 2013, a sub-zone of Bolgheri DOC from 1994","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority and at least four fifths by rule, with Cabernet Franc for the rest.","sites":"Parcels of the estate between about 100 and 400 metres, facing west and south west, on the slopes below Castiglioncello di Bolgheri, where the Cabernet was planted in the 1940s, and lower down; soils of limestone, marl and pebbles with some clay, the stones that give the wine its name.","making":"Each variety is fermented apart in temperature controlled stainless steel and macerated for ten days to about two weeks; the malolactic conversion follows in steel. The wine then spends about two years in French barriques, a share of them new, where the rules ask at least eighteen months in barrels of no more than 225 litres.","glass":"Cassis and redcurrant with cedar, tobacco leaf and the Mediterranean scrub of the coast, more fragrance than weight; medium to full bodied, with fresh acidity and fine, close grained tannin.","ageing":"Reserved for its first eight to ten years, it opens slowly and lasts for decades in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Bolgheri Sassicaia","means":"The denomination of the estate's own vineyards, a sub-zone of Bolgheri until it became a DOC of its own in 2013."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata","means":"The Italian protected appellation, with rules for the grapes, the yields and the time in barrel."},{"term":"Tenuta San Guido","means":"The Incisa della Rocchetta family's estate at Bolgheri, which makes the wine."}]},"table":"Sassicaia began the fame of the Tuscan coast: Tenuta San Guido's Cabernet Sauvignon with a little Cabernet Franc from stony slopes at Bolgheri, with a DOC of its own since 2013. Cedar and cassis ride on fine tannin; decant it young.","story":"Marchese Mario Incisa della Rocchetta (1899 to 1983) took the wines of Bordeaux as his model, and in 1944 he planted a small vineyard near Castiglioncello di Bolgheri, with Cabernet among its vines. By the estate's account the cuttings came from the Salviati family's estate near Pisa; the story that they came from Lafite is often told, and the family has denied it. Until 1967 the wine was made for family and friends alone, named Sassicaia, the stony place, for its ground. Persuaded by his son and his nephew of the Antinori family, he sold it from the 1968 vintage, released in the early 1970s as a Vino da Tavola, since no DOC then covered it. Giacomo Tachis (1933 to 2016), the Antinori oenologist, brought temperature controlled fermentation to the cellar, and in 1978 Sassicaia won a tasting of Cabernets from around the world held by Decanter in London. When the Bolgheri DOC first admitted red wine in 1994, Sassicaia became its named sub-zone, and in 2013 Bolgheri Sassicaia became a DOC of its own. Its 1985 is a vintage of history. Sassicaia tells the Super Tuscan story in one bottle: a private Cabernet, a table wine, then a denomination of its own.","others":[{"name":"Guidalberto","note":"The second wine, Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot, first released in the 2000s."},{"name":"Le Difese","note":"The third wine, Cabernet Sauvignon with Sangiovese, a lighter and earlier red of the house."}],"traps":["Sassicaia is Cabernet Sauvignon with Cabernet Franc; Ornellaia is the Bolgheri red with Merlot and Petit Verdot in its blend.","The first vintage sold was 1968; the wines made before it were for the family and never sold.","Bolgheri Sassicaia became a DOC of its own in 2013; from 1994 it was a sub-zone of Bolgheri DOC, and before that a Vino da Tavola."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-9iy0i3ep","front":"Sassicaia: what does the name mean, and what in the vineyard gave it?","back":"The stony place: the pebbles and stones in the estate's soils of limestone and marl."},{"id":"fl-pkaivnzl","front":"With which vintage did Tenuta San Guido begin to sell Sassicaia?","back":"1968, released in 1971; the wines made before it were kept for family and friends."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ludwv2cg","q":"Under the rules of Bolgheri Sassicaia and the house's own blend, which grapes make Sassicaia?","opts":["Merlot with Cabernet Franc","Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot and Petit Verdot","Cabernet Sauvignon with Cabernet Franc","Sangiovese with Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":2,"exp":"The rules ask at least four fifths Cabernet Sauvignon, and the estate completes it with Cabernet Franc; Merlot and Petit Verdot belong to Ornellaia's blend, not to Sassicaia's.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-9inbwz4g","q":"Bolgheri Sassicaia stopped being a sub-zone of Bolgheri and became a DOC of its own. In which year?","opts":["1983","1994","1968","2013"],"a":3,"exp":"It became a denomination in its own right in 2013; 1994 is the year it became a named sub-zone, when the Bolgheri DOC first admitted red wine.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Tenuta San Guido: History","url":"https://www.tenutasanguido.com/en/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tenuta San Guido: Bolgheri Sassicaia DOC 2020","url":"https://www.tenutasanguido.com/en/sassicaia-2020-en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Vini Bolgheri DOC: Bolgheri Sassicaia production code","url":"https://www.bolgheridoc.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/10/disciplinare-sassicaia-en.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Tenuta San Guido Sassicaia 1985","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-tenuta-san-guido-sassicaia-1985-374473/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein.plus: Sassicaia","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/sassicaia","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-s4i2vt8o": {"id":"fl-s4i2vt8o","key":"ornellaia","group":"tuscany-bolgheri","region":"tuscany","producer":"Tenuta dell'Ornellaia","producerId":"p-ornellaia","say":"or-nel-LAH-yah","place":"Bolgheri, Bolgheri DOC, on the Tuscan coast","founded":"1981","wine":{"name":"Ornellaia","appellation":"Bolgheri Superiore","classification":"Bolgheri DOC Superiore, the DOC's red with at least two years of ageing, one in oak","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon in the lead, with Merlot, Cabernet Franc and Petit Verdot in shares that change with the year.","sites":"Parcels across the estate on the plain and lower slopes below Bolgheri, on soils the house calls marine, alluvial and volcanic in origin: sand and marl lower down, clay dotted with limestone pebbles a little higher.","making":"Each parcel and variety is fermented apart in stainless steel and, in later vintages, concrete tanks, and the malolactic conversion takes place mostly in barriques, most of them new. After about a year the lots are blended and returned to barrel for six to eight months more, eighteen to twenty months in all, and the wine then rests a year in bottle before release.","glass":"Ripe cassis, black plum and black cherry with graphite, sweet tobacco and sun warmed herbs; full bodied and polished, with ripe, dense tannin and a long, generous finish.","ageing":"Generous enough to drink at eight to ten years, it is fuller at fifteen, and a strong vintage keeps two decades or more.","serve":"A Bordeaux glass at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a young bottle takes an hour or two in a decanter, and an old one should be poured gently off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Bolgheri DOC Superiore","means":"The Bolgheri red aged at least two years from the January after the harvest, one of them in oak."},{"term":"Tenuta dell'Ornellaia","means":"The estate at Bolgheri founded in 1981, where Masseto was first made."}]},"table":"Ornellaia leads with Cabernet Sauvignon, adding Merlot, Cabernet Franc and Petit Verdot: the Bolgheri Superiore of Tenuta dell'Ornellaia, founded in 1981 by a younger son of the Antinori family. Poured beside Sassicaia, it reads riper and fuller.","story":"It was a younger son of the Antinori family, the Florentine wine house, who founded Tenuta dell'Ornellaia on land at Bolgheri in 1981; the founder of Sassicaia was his uncle by marriage. André Tchelistcheff (1901 to 1994), a Russian born veteran of the Napa Valley, advised on the planting, and on his advice a hill of blue clay on the estate went to Merlot. The first Ornellaia was the 1985. The clay hill gave its first wine in 1986, bottled as Merlot dell'Ornellaia, and from the 1987 vintage it was called Masseto. Red wine entered the Bolgheri DOC, until then a denomination of white and rosé, in 1994, and Ornellaia carries its Superiore rank. From 1999 the Californian house of Robert Mondavi bought into the estate, with the Frescobaldi family of Florence soon beside it as partners, and in 2005, after Mondavi had itself been sold, the Frescobaldi bought the rest. Masseto was later run as an estate of its own. Ornellaia is the wine that showed Bolgheri was a region and not one estate, and the riper, Merlot inflected foil to Sassicaia's Cabernet with Cabernet Franc.","others":[{"name":"Le Serre Nuove dell'Ornellaia","note":"The second wine, labelled Bolgheri DOC Rosso, from the same estate."},{"name":"Masseto","note":"Merlot from the blue clay hill of the estate, first made in 1986 and named Masseto from the 1987 vintage."}],"traps":["Ornellaia is Bolgheri DOC Superiore, not Toscana IGT and not Bolgheri Sassicaia.","Masseto is a Merlot from the estate's clay hill, not the grand vin of Ornellaia, and it was later run as a separate estate.","Ornellaia was founded by a younger son of the Antinori family, not by Marchesi Antinori, the family's Florentine house."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-2d6fsj7c","front":"Ornellaia: how long after the estate's founding did the first vintage come, and which was it?","back":"1985, four years after the estate was founded in 1981."},{"id":"fl-80sxb8yx","front":"Which Merlot began on a blue clay hill of Ornellaia, first bottled as Merlot dell'Ornellaia?","back":"Masseto, first made with the 1986 vintage and named so from the 1987."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-lz069qeb","q":"A guest compares Ornellaia with Sassicaia. Which grape does Ornellaia's blend use that Sassicaia's does not?","opts":["Cabernet Sauvignon","Merlot","Sangiovese","Cabernet Franc"],"a":1,"exp":"Ornellaia blends Merlot, with Petit Verdot, into Cabernet Sauvignon and Cabernet Franc; Sassicaia is Cabernet Sauvignon with Cabernet Franc, so Cabernet Franc is in both.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-4u8pvnx3","q":"Which family bought the whole of Tenuta dell'Ornellaia in 2005, after sharing it with a Californian house?","opts":["The Frescobaldi family of Florence","The Incisa della Rocchetta family","The della Gherardesca family","The Antinori family of Florence"],"a":0,"exp":"The Frescobaldi, partners beside Robert Mondavi from the early 2000s, bought the rest in 2005; a younger Antinori son had founded the estate in 1981 and sold it on.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Ornellaia: History and Ownership","url":"https://www.ornellaia.com/en/history-and-ownership/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Ornellaia: Philosophy and Terroir","url":"https://www.ornellaia.com/en/philosophy-and-terroir/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Ornellaia: Ornellaia 2022, Bolgheri DOC Superiore Rosso, technical sheet","url":"https://www.ornellaia.com/filemanager/view/15ac5dec-ad5f-4daa-b380-1e1558c640fa","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Ornellaia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ornellaia","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: The making of a megastar, Masseto","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/the-making-of-a-megastar-masseto-549410/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-iwb86d6x": {"id":"fl-iwb86d6x","key":"grattamacco","group":"tuscany-bolgheri","region":"tuscany","producer":"Grattamacco","producerId":"p-grattamacco","say":"grah-tah-MAHK-koh","place":"A hill between Castagneto Carducci and Bolgheri, Bolgheri DOC, Tuscany","founded":"1977","wine":{"name":"Bolgheri Superiore Grattamacco","appellation":"Bolgheri Superiore","classification":"Superiore under the Bolgheri DOC, a red aged two years or more, one in oak","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, about two thirds, with Merlot and a smaller share of Sangiovese.","sites":"The estate's own hill between Castagneto Carducci and Bolgheri, facing the Tyrrhenian Sea at about 100 to 200 metres, above the estates of the plain, with the sea breeze across it.","making":"Hand picked, the grapes ferment with their own yeasts in open conical oak vats, the cap punched down by hand, with long macerations. Eighteen months in French barriques, from new to second use, and a year in bottle follow before release.","glass":"Cassis and blackberry with myrtle, sour cherry and a salty, savoury edge, the Sangiovese showing beneath the Cabernet; medium to full bodied, firm and fresh rather than plush.","ageing":"Taut for its first six to eight years, it opens in its second decade; from a strong vintage it keeps twenty years or more.","serve":"Decant a young bottle an hour or two before serving at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, in a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Bolgheri Superiore","means":"The Superiore rank of Bolgheri red: two years of ageing or more, counted from 1 January after the harvest, one of them in oak."},{"term":"Grattamacco","means":"The estate on its hill above the coast, founded in 1977, and the name of its flagship red."}]},"table":"The second estate of Bolgheri, founded in 1977, Grattamacco grows its Bolgheri Superiore on a hill above the sea between Castagneto Carducci and Bolgheri: Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot and Sangiovese. It tastes savoury and firm rather than plush.","story":"Grattamacco began in 1977, when its founder bought a farm on a hill between Castagneto Carducci and Bolgheri, facing the sea. After San Guido it was the second estate of Bolgheri to plant for fine wine, and the estate calls its red the second Super Tuscan of the zone. Its first commercial vintage, 1982, sold as a table wine, blended Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Sangiovese by the estate's account, three years before the first Ornellaia. In 1986 the house planted Vermentino in place of Trebbiano, by its own account the first in Bolgheri. Created in 1983 for white and rosé, the Bolgheri DOC took in red wine in 1994; Grattamacco's red is a Bolgheri Superiore under those rules, which allow Sangiovese up to half the blend. Most of its neighbours left Sangiovese out, as the consorzio notes, but Grattamacco kept a share of it, fermented with wild yeasts in open oak vats. In 2002 the estate passed into ColleMassari, a family group of Tuscan estates. Open Grattamacco to show that Bolgheri Superiore may hold Sangiovese, and to taste Bolgheri from the hills rather than the plain: tighter, saltier and more savoury than the reds below it.","others":[{"name":"Grattamacco Bolgheri Rosso","note":"The estate's younger red under the Bolgheri DOC, released before the Superiore."},{"name":"Grattamacco Bolgheri Vermentino","note":"The white from Vermentino, planted by the house in 1986 in place of Trebbiano."}],"traps":["Bolgheri DOC allows Sangiovese and Syrah up to half the blend, so Grattamacco's Sangiovese is within the rules and no sign of an IGT.","Grattamacco stands on a hill between Castagneto Carducci and Bolgheri, higher than the estates of the plain, which changes the style."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-frc40t70","front":"Grattamacco: which grape sets its Bolgheri Superiore apart from most of its neighbours?","back":"Sangiovese, a smaller share beside Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot."},{"id":"fl-vr6844d8","front":"In which year was Grattamacco founded, the second estate of Bolgheri after San Guido?","back":"1977; its first commercial vintage was 1982."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-7u66l275","q":"Which method of fermentation does Grattamacco use for its Bolgheri Superiore?","opts":["In clay amphorae buried in the ground","By carbonic maceration in sealed vats","With wild yeasts in open conical oak vats","With cultured yeasts in closed steel tanks"],"a":2,"exp":"The house ferments spontaneously in open oak vats, punched down by hand, with long macerations; closed steel tanks and cultured yeasts are the opposite of its method.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-yz9l3q6s","q":"Grattamacco was the second estate of Bolgheri to plant for fine wine. Which was its first commercial vintage?","opts":["1968","1985","1977","1982"],"a":3,"exp":"The estate began in 1977 and sold its first wine from the 1982 vintage; 1985 is the first Ornellaia and 1968 the first Sassicaia sold.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"ColleMassari: The Grattamacco estate","url":"https://www.collemassariwines.it/en/estates/grattamacco/the-estate/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"ColleMassari: Grattamacco Bolgheri Superiore DOC","url":"https://www.collemassariwines.it/en/wines/grattamacco/grattamacco-bolgheri-superiore-doc/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"ColleMassari: Grattamacco","url":"https://www.collemassariwines.it/en/estates/grattamacco/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vino Joy: Grattamacco, 35 years in the making","url":"https://vino-joy.com/2021/04/09/grattamacco-35-years-in-the-making/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Vini Bolgheri DOC: Wines","url":"https://www.bolgheridoc.com/en/wines/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-s2rt1pm8": {"id":"fl-s2rt1pm8","key":"le-macchiole","group":"tuscany-bolgheri","region":"tuscany","producer":"Le Macchiole","producerId":"p-le-macchiole","say":"leh mahk-KYOH-leh, PAH-leh-oh","place":"Bolgheri, Bolgheri DOC zone, on the Tuscan coast","founded":"1983","wine":{"name":"Paleo Rosso","appellation":"Toscana IGT","classification":"Indicazione Geografica Tipica, the regional category the house chooses for its single variety wines","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Franc alone since the 2001 vintage; the earlier vintages were blends led by Cabernet Sauvignon.","sites":"The estate's own vineyards at Bolgheri: for the later vintages Casa Nuova, on clay with stones of sandstone, Vignone, over limestone rock, and Ulivino, planted between 1998 and 2016; the 2001 came from Contessine, planted in 1983, and Puntone.","making":"The skins stay with the wine for three to six weeks of fermentation and maceration, in concrete tanks in later vintages. Then come fourteen to twenty months, mostly in barriques, a large share or all of them new; conical wooden vats and a stoneware amphora have since joined them.","glass":"Blackcurrant leaf, bramble and red plum with graphite, tobacco and the wild herbs of the coast, the green lift of Cabernet Franc kept ripe; full bodied, with fine, firm tannin and fresh acidity.","ageing":"Ready from about six years, it is fuller at ten to fifteen, and the strong years run to twenty or more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, with an hour or so in a decanter for a young bottle; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Paleo","means":"A wild herb of the Tuscan coast, by the house's account, which gave the wine its name."},{"term":"Toscana IGT","means":"Indicazione Geografica Tipica for the whole of Tuscany, the house's choice for its single variety reds from Bolgheri."}]},"table":"Le Macchiole's Paleo Rosso was a blend until the 2001 vintage made it Cabernet Franc alone from Bolgheri, labelled Toscana IGT by the house's choice. Look for bramble, graphite and coastal herbs, the tannin fine.","story":"When the first rules of the Bolgheri DOC were written in 1983, for white and rosé, a young couple founded Le Macchiole there: Eugenio Campolmi, who died in 2002 aged forty, and his wife; the house dates its vineyards to that year. Paleo, named for a wild herb of the coast, was first made in 1989, a blend of Cabernet Sauvignon with a little Sangiovese, and the blend later took in Cabernet Franc. In 1994 came Messorio, a Merlot alone, and Scrio, a Syrah alone. With the 2001 vintage Paleo itself became Cabernet Franc alone. After his death the next year his widow took the estate on, later with their sons, keeping each wine to a single variety and the Toscana IGT on the label. Paleo Rosso, Cabernet Franc from Bolgheri, teaches two lessons: that a wine may change its grapes and keep its name, so a vintage must be dated before it is described, and that a single variety from inside the Bolgheri zone may carry the IGT rather than the DOC.","others":[{"name":"Messorio","note":"Merlot alone, first made in 1994, the house's other single variety red of Bolgheri."},{"name":"Scrio","note":"Syrah alone, a reminder that Bolgheri is not only Bordeaux grapes."},{"name":"Bolgheri Rosso","note":"The house's blended red under the Bolgheri DOC."}],"traps":["Paleo Rosso began as a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon and became Cabernet Franc alone only with the 2001 vintage.","Paleo Rosso, Messorio and Scrio are labelled Toscana IGT, not Bolgheri DOC, though all three grow inside Bolgheri.","Messorio is Le Macchiole's Merlot; Masseto is the Merlot of the clay hill that was first part of Ornellaia."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-n4m8vgt0","front":"Paleo Rosso: with which vintage did it become Cabernet Franc alone?","back":"2001; before that it was a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon, first made in 1989."},{"id":"fl-y5vz7q54","front":"Le Macchiole makes a single variety red of Merlot and one of Syrah. Name them.","back":"Messorio, the Merlot, and Scrio, the Syrah."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-hbiyq6uu","q":"A guest orders Paleo Rosso of the 2016 vintage. Which grape is in the glass?","opts":["Cabernet Franc alone","Merlot alone","Cabernet Sauvignon with Sangiovese","Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot"],"a":0,"exp":"Since the 2001 vintage Paleo Rosso has been Cabernet Franc alone; Cabernet Sauvignon with Sangiovese was the first blend of 1989, and Merlot alone is Messorio.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-4quraym2","q":"Le Macchiole first made Paleo in 1989. What was the wine before the 2001 vintage?","opts":["A Syrah from young vines","A blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon","A Sangiovese from the hills","A Merlot from a clay hill"],"a":1,"exp":"The 1989 was Cabernet Sauvignon with a little Sangiovese, and Cabernet Franc joined the blend later; Merlot went to Messorio and Syrah to Scrio.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Le Macchiole: The winery","url":"https://www.lemacchiole.it/en/azienda.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Le Macchiole: Paleo Rosso","url":"https://www.lemacchiole.it/en/vini/paleorosso.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Le Macchiole: Paleo Rosso 1989","url":"https://www.lemacchiole.it/en/vini/paleorosso.html?anno=1989","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Le Macchiole: Messorio","url":"https://www.lemacchiole.it/en/vini/messorio.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Rising star Campolmi dies at 40","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/rising-star-campolmi-dies-at-40-106448/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-oriop3uy": {"id":"fl-oriop3uy","key":"tua-rita","group":"tuscany-bolgheri","region":"tuscany","producer":"Tua Rita","producerId":"","say":"TOO-ah REE-tah, reh-dee-GAHF-fee","place":"Notri, Suvereto, in the Alta Maremma on the Tuscan coast","founded":"1984","wine":{"name":"Redigaffi","appellation":"Toscana IGT","classification":"Indicazione Geografica Tipica, the regional category for Tuscany","colour":"red","grapes":"Merlot alone, from the estate's own vines.","sites":"Merlot on the estate's Notri hillside at Suvereto, in the Colline Metallifere south of Bolgheri: markedly clay soils with a good share of pebbles, densely planted and cordon trained.","making":"About ten days of fermentation in temperature controlled stainless steel are followed by some twenty five days more on the skins. The wine is aged for eighteen to twenty months in new French barriques, bottled in summer and rested several months in bottle before release.","glass":"Black cherry, plum and blackberry with cocoa, liquorice and sweet tobacco, a touch of vanilla from the new barrels; full bodied and velvety, with ripe, dense tannin and a long, warm finish.","ageing":"Rich and open within five or six years, it fills out by ten; a strong vintage keeps fifteen to twenty years.","serve":"A large Bordeaux glass, at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a young bottle is the better for an hour or so in a decanter.","label":[{"term":"Redigaffi","means":"Named, by the house's account, for a small stream that flows near the estate."},{"term":"Toscana IGT","means":"A category open to wine from anywhere in Tuscany, under which the house sells its Merlot from Suvereto."},{"term":"Tua Rita","means":"The estate's name: one founder's given name and surname, reversed."}]},"table":"South of Bolgheri, on the Notri hillside at Suvereto, Tua Rita grows the Merlot for Redigaffi, first set apart in two barrels in 1994 and aged in new French oak. Plum, black cherry and cocoa, velvety in the mouth; give it an hour in a decanter.","story":"Tua Rita takes its name from one of its two founders, her first name and surname reversed. In 1984 she and her partner bought land in the Notri hills at Suvereto, south of Bolgheri, in the Alta Maremma; by the house's account they came to live close to nature, and wine came later. Giusto di Notri, a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon, was first made with the 1992 vintage. In 1994 the couple set two barriques of Merlot apart from the rest, and they became the first Redigaffi, named for a small stream near the property. The 2000 vintage of Redigaffi drew wide notice abroad and is told as the estate's breakthrough. After the founder's partner died in 2010, the next generation of the family took on its running. Remember Redigaffi as Merlot alone from clay at Suvereto, aged in new French barriques, and as a sign that the coast's fine wine runs south of Bolgheri into the Colline Metallifere.","others":[{"name":"Giusto di Notri","note":"The estate's first wine, from the 1992 vintage: a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon, with Merlot."}],"traps":["Redigaffi is Merlot alone from Suvereto, south of Bolgheri, and is Toscana IGT, not Bolgheri DOC.","Tua Rita is the estate's name, a founder's name reversed, and never the name of the wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xxykhd61","front":"Redigaffi: what is in the bottle, and where does it grow?","back":"Merlot alone, from the Tua Rita estate on the Notri hillside at Suvereto, south of Bolgheri."},{"id":"fl-gmor6rjg","front":"Which Tua Rita wine came first, with the 1992 vintage, two years before the first Redigaffi?","back":"Giusto di Notri, a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-1lu07s0s","q":"A guest tasting Redigaffi notices vanilla. Which ageing explains it?","opts":["Two years in clay amphorae","Three years in large Slavonian casks","Eighteen to twenty months in new French barriques","Nine months in stainless steel tanks"],"a":2,"exp":"The house ages Redigaffi eighteen to twenty months in new French barriques, the source of its touch of vanilla; large Slavonian casks belong to traditional Sangiovese, not to this Merlot.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ze00vykm","q":"Tua Rita set two barriques of Merlot apart from the rest of its wine and made the first Redigaffi. In which vintage?","opts":["1984","2000","1992","1994"],"a":3,"exp":"The first Redigaffi is the 1994; the estate was founded in 1984, Giusto di Notri began with the 1992, and 2000 is the vintage that drew notice abroad.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Tua Rita: Company, our story","url":"https://www.tuarita.it/la-nostra-storia/?lang=en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tua Rita: Redigaffi","url":"https://www.tuarita.it/vini/redigaffi/?lang=en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tua Rita: Redigaffi, Toscana Merlot IGT, technical sheet","url":"https://www.tuarita.it/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/redigaffi.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Suvereto Wine: Tua Rita","url":"https://www.suveretowine.com/en/suvereto-winerie/tua-rita/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Tua Rita producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/tua-rita-producer-profile-395943/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-95kabiv3": {"id":"fl-95kabiv3","key":"quintarelli","group":"veneto","region":"veneto","producer":"Giuseppe Quintarelli","producerId":"p-quintarelli","say":"joo-ZEP-peh kwin-tah-RELL-ee","place":"Cerè, Negrar, Valpolicella Classico, Veneto","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Amarone della Valpolicella Classico","appellation":"Amarone della Valpolicella","classification":"DOCG since 2010, from the historic Classico zone","colour":"red","grapes":"Corvina and Corvinone lead, with Rondinella and a small share of Cabernet, Nebbiolo, Croatina and Sangiovese.","sites":"The family's hillside vineyards around Negrar in the Classico zone, among them the hill of Monte Ca' Paletta above the hamlet of Cerè, where the cellar stands.","making":"The grapes are dried for months in the estate's lofts, pressed in winter, in a normal year no earlier than December under the appellation's rules, and fermented slowly to dryness. The wine then rests about seven years in large casks of Slavonian oak before bottling. The family makes it only in years it thinks worthy of the name; in other years the same dried grape wine becomes Rosso del Bepi.","glass":"Crushed and dried cherry, fig and dried flowers over exotic spice, cinnamon, cedar and woodland herbs, with citrus peel and a balsamic note for lift; full and warm, yet fresh, with fine tannin and a very long finish.","ageing":"Released many years after the vintage and ready to drink then, it keeps for many years more in a cool cellar.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; open or decant it an hour or two ahead, an old bottle gently off its sediment; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Amarone della Valpolicella","means":"The dry red of dried grapes, raised to DOCG with the decree of 2010."},{"term":"Classico","means":"From the historic hill communes of Valpolicella north west of Verona, Negrar among them."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita","means":"DOCG: Italy's guaranteed appellation, its rules set by decree."}]},"table":"Corvina led grapes from the family's hills above Negrar, dried for months, fermented dry and kept about seven years in large Slavonian oak, make Quintarelli's Amarone della Valpolicella Classico. Full and spiced yet fresh, it needs opening ahead and suits game or aged cheese.","story":"The family began as sharecroppers: before the First World War the founder worked vines with his brothers at Figari, in the commune of Marano. In 1924 he moved to the Negrar valley and set up his own estate at Cerè, on the hill of Monte Ca' Paletta. His youngest son, Giuseppe Quintarelli (1927 to 2012), known as Bepi, took charge around 1950. He worked slowly: grapes dried in the lofts, years in large casks of Slavonian oak, about seven for the Amarone, and labels written and glued on by hand. In years the estate would not declare an Amarone, as in 2008 and 2010, the same dried grape wine went out as Rosso del Bepi, under his own nickname. Amarone della Valpolicella became a DOCG with the decree of 2010, and Bepi died in January 2012 at eighty four, after which the estate passed to his daughter's family. Amarone has two poles, and this is the traditional one: unhurried, long in cask and late to release, the style against which modern, denser bottlings are set.","others":[{"name":"Rosso del Bepi","note":"The Amarone grapes and method in years the family will not declare an Amarone, bottled as a Veneto IGT."},{"name":"Alzero","note":"Cabernet Franc and Cabernet Sauvignon with some Merlot, from dried grapes, labelled Cabernet Veneto IGT and never called Amarone."},{"name":"Recioto della Valpolicella Classico","note":"The sweet wine of the same dried grapes, its fermentation stopped before dryness."}],"traps":["Rosso del Bepi is declassified Amarone fruit and method, not a second wine from other vineyards.","Alzero is a dried grape blend of the two Cabernets and Merlot, a Veneto IGT, and may not be called Amarone, however it is made.","Amarone is dry; the sweet wine of the same dried grapes is Recioto della Valpolicella."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-m5tfzy0y","front":"Giuseppe Quintarelli: what is Rosso del Bepi?","back":"The estate's dried grape wine in years it will not declare an Amarone, such as 2008 and 2010, sold under Giuseppe Quintarelli's nickname."},{"id":"fl-1qko7lpo","front":"How long does Quintarelli's Amarone rest in large Slavonian oak casks before bottling?","back":"About seven years, far beyond the two years the appellation asks of an Amarone."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-2hbz3he5","q":"Beside Corvina, Corvinone and Rondinella, which grapes play a small part in Quintarelli's Amarone?","opts":["Cabernet, Nebbiolo, Croatina and Sangiovese","Garganega and Trebbiano di Soave","Glera, Verdiso and Perera","Merlot, Raboso and Teroldego"],"a":0,"exp":"The estate's Amarone carries a small share of Cabernet, Nebbiolo, Croatina and Sangiovese beside its Corvina led core; Garganega and Trebbiano di Soave are the white grapes of Soave.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-se0gj8wh","q":"In some years Giuseppe Quintarelli would not declare an Amarone. Under which name did the same dried grape wine go out then?","opts":["Alzero","Rosso del Bepi","Recioto della Valpolicella","Primofiore"],"a":1,"exp":"Rosso del Bepi, named for Giuseppe's nickname, carries the Amarone grapes and method in years such as 2008 and 2010; Alzero is the house's dried grape Cabernet blend.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Decanter: Giuseppe Quintarelli dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/guiseppe-quintarelli-dies-33019/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"iDealwine: Giuseppe Quintarelli, the Maestro of Amarone","url":"https://www.idealwine.info/?p=4628","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Primal Wine: Giuseppe Quintarelli, the master of Valpolicella","url":"https://primalwine.com/blogs/natural-wine-producers-primal-wine/giuseppe-quintarelli-the-master-of-valpolicella","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Library: Giuseppe Quintarelli Alzero 2016","url":"https://winelibrary.com/wines/cabernet-franc/2016-giuseppe-quintarelli-alzero-142871","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Consorzio Tutela Vini Valpolicella: Amarone della Valpolicella DOCG regulations","url":"https://www.consorziovalpolicella.it/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/DOCG_Amarone_della_Valpolicella-ENG.pdf","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-ffxf4xbe": {"id":"fl-ffxf4xbe","key":"bertani","group":"veneto","region":"veneto","producer":"Bertani","producerId":"p-bertani","say":"ber-TAH-nee","place":"Grezzana, Valpantena, and Arbizzano di Negrar, Valpolicella, Veneto","founded":"1857","wine":{"name":"Amarone della Valpolicella Classico","appellation":"Amarone della Valpolicella","classification":"DOCG since 2010, from the historic Classico zone","colour":"red","grapes":"Corvina Veronese, the great majority, with a little Rondinella.","sites":"The Corvina vineyards of Tenuta Novare at Arbizzano di Negrar, in the Classico zone, which gave the first official vintage of the house's Amarone in 1958.","making":"The grapes dry through the autumn on racks in the estate's lofts and are pressed in winter, never before December in a normal year by the appellation's rules, then fermented slowly in concrete vats. The wine ages at least seven years in large casks of Slavonian oak, of fifty to one hundred hectolitres, in the historic cellar at Grezzana before release.","glass":"Plum, morello and dried cherry over tea leaf, liquorice and tobacco; dry and firm, with fresh acidity carrying the weight. With age it turns to dried fruit, medlar and truffle.","ageing":"Ready to drink once its long years in cask are over, it keeps for decades: the house's own library of the wine runs back to the first vintage of 1958.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant an older bottle slowly, so the sediment stays behind; a large glass for red wine.","label":[{"term":"Amarone della Valpolicella Classico","means":"The dry red of dried grapes from the historic Classico zone, a DOCG since 2010."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita","means":"DOCG: the guaranteed tier of Italian appellations, above DOC."},{"term":"Bertani","means":"The house founded by two brothers in the Valpantena in 1857."}]},"table":"Old school and dry, Bertani's Amarone della Valpolicella Classico is Corvina and Rondinella from Tenuta Novare in Negrar, dried, fermented in concrete and aged at least seven years in large Slavonian oak. Morello, tea and liquorice make it a partner for braised meat or hard cheese.","story":"Two brothers of the Bertani family founded the house in 1857 in the Valpantena, the valley that runs north from Verona east of the Classico zone. As the house tells it, one of them travelled to Burgundy and learned the new ways of the vineyard and the cellar from his friend Jules Guyot (1807 to 1872), the agronomist whose name the Guyot training system carries. By its own account its Secco Bertani created a style of dry Veronese red, the house received a royal warrant from the Kingdom of Italy in 1923, and its Soave was poured at the coronation in London in 1937. In 1958 the Corvina vineyards of Tenuta Novare, at Arbizzano di Negrar in the Classico zone, gave the first official vintage of Bertani's Amarone. Its making is long and slow: grapes dried in lofts, fermentation in concrete and at least seven years in large Slavonian oak at the Grezzana cellar, so that the house's library of the wine runs from 1958 onwards. At the end of 2011 control of the brand, the Grezzana cellars and Tenuta Novare passed to the wine company of the Angelini group, while the family kept part of its land for a new estate. The library is the point: the classic dry Amarone of long cask ageing, with half a century of vintages to show how it keeps.","others":[{"name":"Secco Bertani","note":"The house's historic dry Veronese red, a style the house says it created."},{"name":"Amarone della Valpolicella Valpantena","note":"The house's Amarone of its home valley, a named subzone of Valpolicella."},{"name":"Recioto della Valpolicella Valpantena","note":"The sweet wine of dried grapes from the same valley."}],"traps":["Dried grapes do not make Bertani's Amarone sweet: the house's sweet wine of dried grapes is its Recioto.","Valpantena is a named subzone of Valpolicella, not a separate appellation; the Classico Amarone comes from Negrar, not from the Valpantena.","The Bertani house and the Bertani family's later estate have been separate businesses since the sale of 2011."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-x4a51tmm","front":"Bertani: which vineyards gave the first official vintage of its Amarone, and in which year?","back":"The Corvina vineyards of Tenuta Novare at Arbizzano di Negrar, in 1958."},{"id":"fl-v8zstr5p","front":"Bertani's Amarone Classico: in what casks, and for how long, before release?","back":"At least seven years, in casks of fifty to one hundred hectolitres at the Grezzana cellar."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-bvktw6tb","q":"What does Bertani's Amarone della Valpolicella Classico go through before release?","opts":["Five years in clay amphorae underground","Eighteen months in new French barriques","Seven years or more in large Slavonian oak","A year in stainless steel, then bottle"],"a":2,"exp":"Bertani ferments in concrete and ages the wine at least seven years in Slavonian oak casks of fifty to one hundred hectolitres; new barriques belong to a modern style, not this one.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2ynaiz49","q":"Bertani was founded by two brothers in 1857. Where, by the house's account, did one of them learn the new viticulture of his day?","opts":["In Champagne, at a house in Reims","In Bordeaux, from the Médoc brokers","In Piedmont, at the school of Alba","In Burgundy, from Jules Guyot"],"a":3,"exp":"The house tells that the brother travelled to Burgundy and learned from his friend Jules Guyot, the agronomist of the Guyot training system; the Médoc brokers are part of Bordeaux's history, not Bertani's.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bertani: About us","url":"https://www.bertani.net/about-us/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bertani: Amarone della Valpolicella Classico","url":"https://www.bertani.net/wines/tradition/amarone-della-valpolicella-classico/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bertani: The Library, the vertical of Amarone della Valpolicella Classico","url":"https://library.bertani.net/en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"WineNews: the Angelini group and Bertani","url":"https://winenews.it/en/?p=311885","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Jules Guyot","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jules_Guyot","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-lb6ypzan": {"id":"fl-lb6ypzan","key":"masi","group":"veneto","region":"veneto","producer":"Masi","producerId":"p-masi","say":"MAH-zee, kahm-poh-fee-OR-een","place":"Gargagnago, Sant'Ambrogio di Valpolicella, Veneto","founded":"1772","wine":{"name":"Campofiorin","appellation":"Rosso del Veronese IGT","classification":"Indicazione Geografica Tipica, outside the Valpolicella DOC rules","colour":"red","grapes":"Corvina for the larger part, with Rondinella and a little Molinara.","sites":"Corvina, Rondinella and Molinara of the Verona hills, the country around the house's cellars in Valpolicella; as a Verona IGT the wine is not bound to one appellation's vineyards.","making":"The three grapes are first made into a fresh red. That wine is then refermented with whole, semi dried grapes of the same varieties, some 30 per cent by the house's figure, kept apart from the grapes for Amarone. It ages at least eighteen months in wood, mostly large casks of Slavonian oak with the rest in barrels of 600 litres, then rests some months in bottle.","glass":"Ripe and dried cherry with berry fruit, cinnamon and a hint of raisin from the dried grapes; round and fairly full, with soft tannin and good length.","ageing":"Drinkable from release; by the house's own reckoning it keeps for fifteen to twenty years in a good cellar.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; open it a little ahead or decant it briefly; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Campofiorin","means":"The wine's own name, first made with the 1964 vintage."},{"term":"Rosso del Veronese IGT","means":"A red of the Verona IGT, which the house also writes Rosso Verona; not a Valpolicella DOC."},{"term":"Masi","means":"The house, named for the Vaio dei Masi, the small valley where the family's vineyards began."}]},"table":"In 1964 Masi refermented a young red on the semi dried skins left from Amarone, and that wine, Campofiorin, gave the Ripasso method its name. Made since the 1980s with whole semi dried grapes, it is a Verona IGT of ripe cherry and cinnamon with soft tannin.","story":"The Boscaini family dates its history in wine to 1772, with vineyards in the Vaio dei Masi, a small valley of Valpolicella that gave the house its name, and counts seven generations since. In 1964 the house refermented a freshly made red of Corvina, Rondinella and Molinara on the semi dried skins left from making Amarone, and called the method Ripasso, a word it found in the old cellar talk of Valpolicella. The wine was Campofiorin, first sold in 1967 and labelled Ripasso. In 1973 Masi began to work on the wines of the Serego Alighieri counts, descendants of the poet of the Divine Comedy, whose son bought the family's estate at Gargagnago in 1353. In the 1980s the house's technical group refined the method into a double fermentation with whole semi dried grapes in place of Amarone skins. Valpolicella Ripasso, made on the skins of Amarone or Recioto, became a DOC of its own in 2010, while Campofiorin stayed a Verona IGT, which the house calls a Supervenetian. With the 2020 vintage Masi restyled the label in a shape that recalls the 1964 one. When a guest asks where Ripasso came from, this is the wine to name.","others":[{"name":"Costasera Amarone della Valpolicella Classico","note":"The house's widely seen Amarone, from the Classico zone."},{"name":"Mazzano Amarone della Valpolicella Classico","note":"The single vineyard Amarone from a high, cool site."},{"name":"Brolo Campofiorin Oro","note":"A Campofiorin from a brolo, a walled vineyard, aged in smaller oak casks."}],"traps":["Campofiorin is a Verona IGT, not a Valpolicella Ripasso DOC, though Masi gave the Ripasso method its name.","Campofiorin is refermented with whole semi dried grapes, not on Amarone skins as in the 1964 original and in Ripasso DOC.","Serego Alighieri is an estate name, not an appellation."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-f2fdqhbz","front":"Masi Campofiorin: what is the young wine refermented with?","back":"Whole, semi dried grapes of Corvina, Rondinella and Molinara, some 30 per cent by the house's figure, kept apart from the Amarone grapes."},{"id":"fl-65nifdmi","front":"In which vintage was Masi's Campofiorin first made, the wine that gave Ripasso its name?","back":"1964; it reached the market in 1967."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-o81ihy9l","q":"Expecting a Valpolicella Ripasso DOC, a guest orders Masi's Campofiorin. Under which appellation is it labelled?","opts":["A Verona IGT","Valpolicella Ripasso DOC","Valpolicella Classico Superiore DOC","Amarone della Valpolicella DOCG"],"a":0,"exp":"Campofiorin is labelled a Verona IGT, Rosso del Veronese or Rosso Verona; although Masi named the Ripasso method, its double fermentation with whole semi dried grapes is not the Ripasso DOC's refermentation on Amarone or Recioto skins.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-g87xmimv","q":"In the 1980s Masi changed how Campofiorin is made. What did the change bring in?","opts":["Carbonic maceration of whole bunches","Refermentation with whole semi dried grapes","Fermentation in new French barriques","Fortification with grape spirit"],"a":1,"exp":"Masi's technical group refined the 1964 method, which used the skins left from Amarone, into a double fermentation with whole semi dried grapes; barriques, spirit and carbonic maceration play no part.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Masi: Campofiorin, Rosso Verona IGT","url":"https://www.masi.it/en/wines/campofiorin-rosso-verona-igt-red-wine/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Masi: The history of the Boscaini family","url":"https://masi.it/en/campofiorin-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Masi: Campofiorin has an update","url":"https://www.masi.it/en/news/campofiorin-has-an-update/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Charton Hobbs: Masi Campofiorin","url":"https://www.chartonhobbs.com/en/catalogue/nat/masi-251788/campofiorin-1582601/0","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Business International: Pressure on Ripasso","url":"https://www.wine-business-international.com/wine/styles-regions/pressure-ripasso","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-rau6lxfd": {"id":"fl-rau6lxfd","key":"pieropan","group":"veneto","region":"veneto","producer":"Pieropan","producerId":"p-pieropan","say":"pee-EH-roh-pahn, kahl-vah-REE-noh","place":"Soave, Soave Classico, Veneto","founded":"1880","wine":{"name":"Soave Classico Calvarino","appellation":"Soave Classico","classification":"DOC, from the historic hill zone of Soave","colour":"white","grapes":"Garganega, about seven tenths, with Trebbiano di Soave for the rest.","sites":"The Calvarino vineyard in the Soave Classico hills, bought by the founder in 1901: volcanic soil rich in Eocene basalt, the vines trained on the pergola veronese.","making":"Trebbiano di Soave is picked from mid September and Garganega in October, and the free run juice is fermented at cool, controlled temperatures in glass lined concrete tanks. The wine then rests on its fine lees in those tanks for a year or a little more, stirred by hand from time to time, and a few months in bottle before release. No oak is used.","glass":"Wet stone and a saline, iodine note over elderflower, acacia and jasmine, with lemon and fresh almond on the finish; dry, direct and fresh, light in body but long.","ageing":"Taut when young, it opens over a few years and keeps for ten years and more in a good vintage.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, as the house advises; no need to decant; a white wine glass of medium size.","label":[{"term":"Soave Classico","means":"The historic hill zone of the Soave appellation, around the town of Soave."},{"term":"Calvarino","means":"The vineyard's name, little Calvary, for a slope steep and hard to work."},{"term":"Pieropan","means":"The family house of Soave, founded in 1880."}]},"table":"One volcanic vineyard gives Pieropan's Calvarino, a Soave Classico bottled on its own since 1971: Garganega with Trebbiano di Soave, raised in concrete on its lees and never in oak. Expect wet stone, elderflower and almond; it sits well beside lake fish or risotto.","story":"A town doctor of Soave founded the house in 1880. In 1901 he bought the family's first vineyard, Calvarino, on volcanic hillside in the Classico zone; its name, little Calvary, is said to come from how steep and hard the slope was to work. By the family's account the house was among the first to print the name Soave on its labels, in the 1930s. The founder's grandson and namesake took charge in the 1960s, after studying oenology and viticulture at Conegliano. In 1971 he bottled Calvarino on its own, by the family's account the first Italian white named for a single vineyard, and in 1978 he added La Rocca, from the hill below Soave castle and raised in wood. Calvarino is raised instead in glass lined concrete, resting on its lees for a year and more. The grandson died in April 2018 at seventy one, and the house went on under his two sons. Side by side, the two make a lesson in Soave: Calvarino on basalt, taut and saline, and La Rocca on limestone, the richer of the two and raised in wood, both from Garganega in the Classico hills.","others":[{"name":"Soave Classico La Rocca","note":"Garganega from the hill below Soave castle, raised in wood, first made under the name in 1978."},{"name":"Recioto di Soave Classico Le Colombare","note":"The sweet wine of dried Garganega, a DOCG."},{"name":"Calvarino 5","note":"A Calvarino held in concrete for more than ten years before bottling and released long after its harvest."}],"traps":["Soave is the place and Garganega the grape; Trebbiano di Soave is a different variety from Trebbiano Toscano.","Calvarino is raised in concrete without oak; it is La Rocca that is raised in wood.","Calvarino is a Soave Classico DOC; Recioto di Soave is the sweet DOCG of dried grapes."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-kw4750mj","front":"Pieropan Calvarino: on what soil does the vineyard lie, and how is the wine raised?","back":"Volcanic soil rich in basalt; it rests a year and more on its lees in glass lined concrete, with no oak."},{"id":"fl-89niuzbf","front":"Which Pieropan Soave comes from the hill below Soave castle and is raised in wood?","back":"La Rocca, first made under that name in 1978."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-1mdgyr9j","q":"A guest compares Pieropan's two single vineyard Soaves. Which describes Calvarino?","opts":["Limestone, raised in oak barrels","Dried grapes, fermented to sweetness","Volcanic basalt, raised in glass lined concrete","Alluvial plain, picked by machine"],"a":2,"exp":"Calvarino grows on Eocene basalt and rests on its lees in glass lined concrete, never oak; limestone and wood belong to the house's La Rocca.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1davw64q","q":"In 1971 Pieropan bottled Calvarino on its own. What did that make it, by the family's account?","opts":["The first Soave raised in oak","The first Soave sold as a DOCG","The first sparkling wine of Soave","The first Italian white named for one vineyard"],"a":3,"exp":"By the family's account Calvarino was the first Italian white named for a single vineyard; the house's first Soave raised in wood was La Rocca, from 1978.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Pieropan: The Pieropan Family","url":"https://www.pieropan.it/en/family/la-famiglia","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Pieropan: Calvarino","url":"https://www.pieropan.it/en/wines/calvarino","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Pieropan: Calvarino technical sheet","url":"https://www.pieropan.it/scheda-tecnica-vini/Calvarino_en.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Soave wine pioneer Leonildo Nino Pieropan dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/obituaries/soave-wine-pioneer-leonildo-nino-pieropan-dies-388612","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Liberty Wines: Pieropan Calvarino Soave Classico","url":"https://www.libertywines.co.uk/our-portfolio/PI102-calvarino-soave-classico","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-nzczuie0": {"id":"fl-nzczuie0","key":"bisol","group":"veneto","region":"veneto","producer":"Bisol","producerId":"p-bisol","say":"bee-ZOHL, kar-TEET-seh","place":"Santo Stefano, Valdobbiadene, Conegliano Valdobbiadene, Veneto","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Valdobbiadene Superiore di Cartizze Dry","appellation":"Valdobbiadene Superiore di Cartizze","classification":"DOCG since 2009; a subzone with its own rules since 1969","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Glera, the Prosecco grape: alone, by the house's sheets, where the appellation asks for at least 85 per cent.","sites":"The family's parcels on the Cartizze hill at Santo Stefano di Valdobbiadene: a steep crest rising to about 300 metres, sunny and sheltered from cold winds, on friable soil over a bed of rock.","making":"The grapes are pressed gently and cold, and the must settles cold for about a week before fermenting in stainless steel. The base wine rests on its lees, then makes its second fermentation in sealed pressure tanks, the Martinotti method, for at least thirty days, with no malolactic conversion, and is finished Dry.","glass":"Apricot, white peach and green apple with acacia blossom and pear; a soft mousse and a gentle sweetness held in balance by fresh acidity.","ageing":"Made to drink young, while its fruit and blossom are fresh; it is not a wine to lay down.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; not decanted; a tulip shaped white wine glass rather than a narrow flute.","label":[{"term":"Superiore di Cartizze","means":"From the one hill of Cartizze above Valdobbiadene, the subzone at the head of the appellation."},{"term":"Dry","means":"Despite the word, softly sweet: sweeter than Brut and Extra Dry."},{"term":"DOCG","means":"The guaranteed appellation of the Conegliano Valdobbiadene hills, since 2009."}]},"table":"On the one hill of Cartizze above Valdobbiadene, where the family's records go back to 1542, Bisol makes its Cartizze: Prosecco Superiore of Glera, made in pressure tanks and finished Dry. Softly sweet with apricot, white peach and acacia, it is served cold, as an aperitif or with fruit tarts.","story":"A record of 1542, as the family tells it, first places the Bisols on the Cartizze hill above Santo Stefano di Valdobbiadene, then written Chartice. At the start of the twentieth century a forebear built a thriving wine trade, broken off by the First World War; his son revived it in the mid 1920s and, after the Second World War, travelled the hills of Valdobbiadene and Conegliano to buy plots. The land gained its law on 2 April 1969, when the Ministry of Agriculture recognised Conegliano Valdobbiadene as a DOC, with Superiore di Cartizze as a subzone of its own. In 2009 the hills were raised to DOCG, and the grape was renamed Glera so that Prosecco could be protected as the name of a place. Cartizze is one sheltered crest between the hamlets of San Pietro di Barbozza, Santo Stefano and Saccol, divided among many owners, and its wine is usually finished Dry, softer and sweeter than the Brut of the wider hills. In 2014 Bisol became part of the Lunelli group. Two things a guest often gets wrong meet here: that a Prosecco can name a single hill, and that Dry on its label means softly sweet.","others":[{"name":"Relio Rive di Guia","note":"A Valdobbiadene Prosecco Superiore from the Rive of Guia, one of the appellation's named hillsides."},{"name":"Crede","note":"A Valdobbiadene Prosecco Superiore DOCG of the house's hill vineyards."}],"traps":["Cartizze labelled Dry is sweeter than Brut and Extra Dry, not drier.","Cartizze is a hill shared by many owners, not one producer's vineyard.","Prosecco Superiore is made sparkling in pressure tanks, not by the traditional method of Champagne."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-21amo0hj","front":"Bisol's Cartizze is labelled Dry. How sweet is it?","back":"Softly sweet, sweeter than Brut and Extra Dry: Dry is one of the sweeter styles of Prosecco."},{"id":"fl-yxmo5p08","front":"When did the Cartizze subzone of Bisol's flagship first gain legal recognition?","back":"On 2 April 1969, within the Conegliano Valdobbiadene DOC; the hills became DOCG in 2009."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-5tm0ocpt","q":"A guest asks for a bone dry Prosecco and points to Bisol's Cartizze Dry. What do you tell them?","opts":["It is a still wine, not sparkling","It is softly sweet, sweeter than Brut","It is the driest style, below Brut Nature","It is made by the traditional method"],"a":1,"exp":"Dry is a sweeter style than Extra Dry and Brut, and Cartizze is usually finished Dry; for a bone dry wine offer a Brut instead.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-vyu92y27","q":"Conegliano Valdobbiadene, with Superiore di Cartizze as its subzone, was first recognised as a DOC in which year?","opts":["1969","2009","1987","1924"],"a":0,"exp":"The Ministry of Agriculture recognised the DOC on 2 April 1969; 2009 is the year the hills became DOCG and the grape took the name Glera.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bisol: The Story","url":"https://bisol.it/bisol/en/the-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bisol: Cartizze","url":"https://bisol.it/bisol/en/wines/cartizze/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bisol: Cartizze fact sheet","url":"https://images.salsify.com/images/z0btnzhzpxxi6k3gtej7/Bisol_2025_Cartizze_Superiore_FS.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Consorzio Conegliano Valdobbiadene Prosecco: the Consortium","url":"https://www.prosecco.it/en/consortium/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Italian Wine Central: Conegliano Valdobbiadene Prosecco DOCG","url":"https://italianwinecentral.com/denomination/conegliano-valdobbiadene-prosecco-docg/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-6fat88pe": {"id":"fl-6fat88pe","key":"jermann","group":"friuli","region":"friuli","producer":"Jermann","producerId":"p-jermann","say":"YER-mahn, too-NEE-nah","place":"Villanova di Farra, Farra d'Isonzo, Venezia Giulia IGT, Friuli Venezia Giulia","founded":"1881","wine":{"name":"Vintage Tunina","appellation":"Venezia Giulia IGT","classification":"Venezia Giulia IGT, the regional tier below DOC, by the house's choice","colour":"white","grapes":"Sauvignon and Chardonnay with Ribolla Gialla and Malvasia, and for many years a little Picolit; the mix follows the vintage.","sites":"The Tunina vineyard at Ronco del Fortino, on hill soils of marl and sandstone of Eocene origin, where the varieties grow together as one field blend.","making":"The varieties grow together and are picked together late, about two weeks after the usual harvest; accounts differ on whether they then ferment as one or apart. The wine rests on its fine lees for longer than is usual before bottling. It saw no wood in the 1990s; some later vintages have had a part raised in large oak.","glass":"Ripe pear, acacia honey and wildflowers over salted almond and a little peach, with a touch of vanilla in the years that see oak; broad and textured, yet fresh, with a long almond finish.","ageing":"Open and generous on release; by the house's account it keeps seven or eight years, and longer than ten in a strong vintage.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; it needs no decanter; a white wine glass with a generous bowl.","label":[{"term":"Vintage Tunina","means":"The wine's name: Vintage is part of the name, and the year beside it is the harvest."},{"term":"Venezia Giulia","means":"The regional IGT of Friuli Venezia Giulia, freer than a DOC in the grapes it allows."}]},"table":"Vintage Tunina, the white Jermann first sold with the 1975, comes from one field at Villanova di Farra where Sauvignon, Chardonnay and native grapes grow together and are picked late together. Broad yet fresh, with pear, acacia honey and salted almond, it should be cool rather than icy.","story":"Villanova di Farra became the family's home in 1881, when its founder settled there from Burgenland in Austria, as the house tells it, and the Jermanns farmed there for generations. In the early 1970s a descendant who had trained at the wine schools of Conegliano and San Michele, and studied abroad for a time, set out to make a white of several varieties grown together in one vineyard and picked late together. The trials began with the 1973 harvest, and the first wine sold as Vintage Tunina was the 1975. The name is that of the vineyard at Ronco del Fortino; by the house's account, the name recalls a former owner called Antonia, Tunina in the local diminutive, and the story links it to a Venetian housekeeper of that name loved by Giacomo Casanova (1725 to 1798). The wine is bottled under the regional IGT, outside the DOC, by the house's choice, and for many years it carried a little Picolit. In 2021 Marchesi Antinori took a majority of the estate. Start the Friulian white blend here: several grapes, one field, and texture rather than the voice of a single variety.","others":[{"name":"Vinnae","note":"The house's white led by Ribolla Gialla, the native grape of the Gorizia hills, with a little Friulano and Riesling."},{"name":"Capo Martino","note":"A white blend led by Friulano and raised in wood."}],"traps":["Vintage Tunina is a field blend of several grapes, never a varietal wine, and Vintage is part of its name rather than a vintage designation.","It is labelled Venezia Giulia IGT, not Collio DOC or Friuli Isonzo DOC, by the house's choice.","Jermann's Ribolla led white is Vinnae; Vintage Tunina carries Ribolla only as one part of its blend."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-sua0ylex","front":"Jermann's Vintage Tunina: which harvest gave the first trials, and which wine was the first sold under the name?","back":"The 1973 harvest gave the trials; the 1975 was the first sold as Vintage Tunina."},{"id":"fl-qe4xq60k","front":"Whose name, the story goes, does the Tunina vineyard of Jermann recall?","back":"Antonia, called Tunina, a former owner, and a Venetian housekeeper of that name loved by Casanova."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-22sd9rh5","q":"When are the grapes for Jermann's Vintage Tunina picked?","opts":["Early, then dried for months on mats","Early, before the usual harvest, for freshness","Late, about two weeks after the usual harvest","In midwinter, frozen on the vine"],"a":2,"exp":"The house picks the Tunina vineyard late, about two weeks after the usual harvest, for ripeness and texture; the grapes are neither picked early nor dried or frozen.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-e6yw79do","q":"After trials of the 1973 harvest, which vintage was the first Jermann sold under the name Vintage Tunina?","opts":["1965","1981","1971","1975"],"a":3,"exp":"The house dates the trials to the 1973 harvest and the first wine sold under the name to the 1975; 1981 is the first vintage of Livio Felluga's Terre Alte, another Friulian blend.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Jermann: The family's history","url":"https://www.jermann.it/_uk/azienda","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Jermann: Factsheet of Vintage Tunina","url":"https://www.jermann.it/download/prodotti/1_scheda_en.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Jermann Vintage Tunina 1997","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-jermann-vintage-tunina-1997-386376/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Antinori expands into Friuli white wines with Jermann deal","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/antinori-jermann-friuli-white-wine-455958/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Gambero Rosso International: Antinori acquires the majority of the Jermann winery","url":"https://www.gamberorossointernational.com/?p=364363","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-0x7bnssz": {"id":"fl-0x7bnssz","key":"livio-felluga","group":"friuli","region":"friuli","producer":"Livio Felluga","producerId":"p-livio-felluga","say":"LEE-vee-oh fel-LOO-gah, TEHR-reh AHL-teh","place":"Brazzano di Cormòns, vineyards at Rosazzo, Rosazzo DOCG, Friuli Venezia Giulia","founded":"1956","wine":{"name":"Terre Alte","appellation":"Rosazzo","classification":"DOCG since 2011, a white blend led by Friulano from the Rosazzo hills","colour":"white","grapes":"Friulano, at least half as the Rosazzo DOCG requires, with Pinot Bianco and Sauvignon for the rest.","sites":"The house's hill vineyards at Rosazzo, round the old abbey south of Cividale, on the marl and sandstone of Eocene origin called ponca.","making":"Picked by hand into small crates, the grapes are lightly destemmed, left briefly on their skins and gently pressed. The settled must begins its fermentation in stainless steel and moves to oak casks, where it finishes fermenting and goes through malolactic. It then stays on its lees for savour and length, and is bottled in the summer.","glass":"Pear and hay over acacia and hawthorn blossom, with almond, wild herbs and crushed stone; full and creamy from the lees, with fresh acidity and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Ready on release, it gains depth and the tertiary notes of age over the years that follow in bottle, as the house means it to.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; no need to decant, though it opens in a large glass; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Rosazzo","means":"The DOCG of 2011 for white blends of at least half Friulano, from the hills round the abbey of Rosazzo."},{"term":"Terre Alte","means":"Italian for high lands: the name of the blend first made with the 1981 vintage."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita","means":"Spelled out in full: the guaranteed appellation, the tier Rosazzo reached in 2011."}]},"table":"Terre Alte means high lands, and Livio Felluga's white of 1981 comes from the Rosazzo hills: Friulano with Pinot Bianco and Sauvignon, begun in steel, finished in oak casks and kept on its lees. Pear, acacia and almond run to a saline finish, and a large glass helps it open.","story":"Born at Isola d'Istria, then part of Austria-Hungary, Livio Felluga (1914 to 2016) settled at the foot of the Rosazzo hills at the end of the 1930s, before the war took him to the front. After the war he bought vineyards on the hill of Rosazzo, round its old abbey, and he founded the house at Brazzano in 1956. That year, by the house's account, he conceived a label printed with an old map of the Friulian hills, to say where the wine came from in the years before Italy had DOCs, and the map has marked the house's wines ever since. Terre Alte followed with the 1981 vintage: Friulano, Pinot Bianco and Sauvignon from the Rosazzo hills, made as one wine rather than three. In 2011 Rosazzo, until then a subzone of the Colli Orientali, became a DOCG for white blends of at least half Friulano, and Terre Alte is labelled under it. Livio Felluga died at his home in Brazzano in December 2016, at the age of 102, and the house stayed with his family. Terre Alte and the map say the same thing: the hill comes first and the grapes serve it.","others":[{"name":"Friulano","note":"The house's single variety Friulano of the Colli Orientali, the regional grape in its plain form."},{"name":"Picolit","note":"The sweet white of the shy native Picolit grape, from the Colli Orientali."}],"traps":["Terre Alte is a blend led by Friulano, not a varietal Friulano; the house bottles its Friulano on its own.","Rosazzo is a DOCG within the Friuli Colli Orientali hills of the province of Udine, not part of the Collio, though the Felluga cellar stands at Brazzano by Cormòns."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-2i903rkh","front":"Livio Felluga: what has the house printed on its labels since 1956?","back":"An old map of the Friulian hills, to show where the wine comes from."},{"id":"fl-0l6cm0x9","front":"Terre Alte: which DOCG does it carry, and which grape must lead the blend?","back":"Rosazzo DOCG, created in 2011; Friulano must make at least half the blend."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-m80uk795","q":"Under the Rosazzo DOCG that Livio Felluga's Terre Alte carries, which grape must make at least half the blend?","opts":["Sauvignon","Friulano","Ribolla Gialla","Pinot Bianco"],"a":1,"exp":"The Rosazzo rules ask for at least half Friulano, with Sauvignon and Pinot Bianco or Chardonnay each a fifth to three tenths; Ribolla Gialla may add no more than a tenth.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-80a5dxuo","q":"With which vintage was Livio Felluga's Terre Alte first made?","opts":["1981","1956","2011","1965"],"a":0,"exp":"Terre Alte was born with the 1981 vintage; 1956 is the year of the map label and 2011 the year Rosazzo became a DOCG.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Livio Felluga: Terre Alte","url":"https://liviofelluga.it/portfolio/terre-alte","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Livio Felluga: Estate","url":"https://www.liviofelluga.it/?lang=en&section=pages&subsection=azienda","kind":"primary"},{"title":"AIS: Rosazzo DOCG production rules","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Friuli%20Venezia%20Giulia/DOCG/Rosazzo.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"MMD USA: Livio Felluga, the story","url":"https://mmdusa.net/portfolio/livio-felluga/story","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Dissapore: Livio Felluga has died","url":"https://www.dissapore.com/notizie/e-morto-livio-felluga/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-vvjd0aur": {"id":"fl-vvjd0aur","key":"schiopetto","group":"friuli","region":"friuli","producer":"Schiopetto","producerId":"p-schiopetto","say":"skyoh-PET-toh","place":"Capriva del Friuli, Collio DOC, Friuli Venezia Giulia","founded":"1965","wine":{"name":"Collio Friulano","appellation":"Collio","classification":"DOC Collio Goriziano or Collio, the hill zone of Gorizia province, since 1968","colour":"white","grapes":"Friulano alone, the grape once labelled Tocai Friulano.","sites":"The house's hill vineyards at Capriva del Friuli, round the Archbishop's palace whose vines the Church leased to the founder, on marly soils of Eocene origin.","making":"Fermented for ten to twelve days in stainless steel at a controlled 16 degrees Celsius, the wine rests eight months on its lees in steel, with no wood, and then at least five months in bottle before release.","glass":"Pear and pear skin with white blossom and a little almond, ending on the grape's bitter almond note; medium bodied, clean and precise, with fresh acidity and no oak.","ageing":"Fresh and open when it is released, it keeps and deepens for some years in a strong vintage.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; pour it straight from the bottle with no decanting, into a medium sized white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Collio","means":"The DOC of the Gorizia hills along the Slovenian border, on the marl and sandstone called ponca."},{"term":"Friulano","means":"The grape sold as Tocai Friulano until the name Tocai was withdrawn in the EU from 2007."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata","means":"DOC: Italy's controlled appellation, below DOCG, its rules set by decree."}]},"table":"No wood touches Schiopetto's Collio Friulano: Friulano alone from the Capriva hills, first made in 1965, fermented cool in steel and kept eight months on its lees. White blossom and pear end on the grape's bitter almond; offer it with prosciutto di San Daniele.","story":"Before he turned to wine, Mario Schiopetto (1930 to 2003) worked in his family's inn. He travelled to cellars across Europe, in Germany above all for the making of white wine and in France for finesse. He began making wine in 1963 and released his first Tocai, a single variety white, in 1965, by the house's account. He was among the first in Friuli to ferment white wine in stainless steel under temperature control, for freshness and clean varietal aroma, and his cold, clean whites became the pattern of the region's modern style. The Church leased him the vineyards round the Archbishop's palace at Capriva, from 1970 in the house's telling, though another account dates the lease to 1965; he restored them, and in 1989 they became his own. When he died in 2003, aged seventy two, his children carried on. In 2007 the name Tocai was withdrawn in the EU, to protect Hungary's Tokaj, and the wine became Friulano on the label. In April 2014 new owners took over the estate and undertook to keep its style. This Friulano is that style at its plainest: one variety, no wood, nothing between the grape and the glass.","others":[{"name":"Collio Pinot Bianco","note":"Pinot Bianco alone from the Collio hills, first produced in 1967 by the house's sheets."},{"name":"Blanc des Rosis","note":"The house's white blend led by Friulano, raised like the Friulano for eight months on its lees in steel."}],"traps":["Friulano and Tocai Friulano are one grape: the name Tocai was withdrawn from 2007, and the grape has nothing to do with Hungary's Tokaji.","Schiopetto's Friulano is the clean, steel made pole of Friulian white; the skin macerated orange wines of Oslavia are a separate and later movement."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8vzucopw","front":"Schiopetto: in which year did the house release its first Tocai, the wine later labelled Friulano?","back":"1965, two years after its founder began making wine, by the house's account."},{"id":"fl-19p3n1bf","front":"How is Schiopetto's Collio Friulano raised before release?","back":"Eight months on its lees in stainless steel, with no wood, then at least five months in bottle."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-kayn3ru8","q":"Does Schiopetto's Collio Friulano see wood or clay before it is bottled?","opts":["Yes: a year in new French barriques","Partly: a third in large Slavonian casks","Yes: months in buried clay amphorae","No: steel throughout, eight months on its lees"],"a":3,"exp":"The house ferments and raises its Friulano in stainless steel, eight months on the lees, then bottles it; barriques and amphorae are the ways of other Friulian cellars.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-l0mv80rv","q":"From whom did Mario Schiopetto first lease the Capriva vineyards that became his own in 1989?","opts":["The commune of Capriva del Friuli","A cooperative of Cormòns growers","The Church, round the Archbishop's palace","A merchant family of Gorizia"],"a":2,"exp":"The Church leased him the vineyards round the Archbishop's palace at Capriva, and he restored them and made them his own in 1989; no commune, cooperative or merchant held them.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Schiopetto: History","url":"https://www.schiopetto.it/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Schiopetto: Friulano technical sheet","url":"https://www.schiopetto.it/wp-content/uploads/2021/04/Friulano-2019_eng.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Father of Friuli Schiopetto dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/father-of-friuli-schiopetto-dies-103959/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Callmewine: Schiopetto producer page","url":"https://www.callmewine.com/en/schiopetto-B698.htm","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Grandi Bottiglie: Mario Schiopetto producer page","url":"https://grandibottiglie.com/en/produttore/mario-schiopetto.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-c0fub1gq": {"id":"fl-c0fub1gq","key":"gravner","group":"friuli","region":"friuli","producer":"Gravner","producerId":"p-gravner","say":"GRAHV-ner","place":"Oslavia, Gorizia, Venezia Giulia IGT, Friuli Venezia Giulia","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Ribolla Gialla","appellation":"Venezia Giulia IGT","classification":"Venezia Giulia IGT, the regional tier, chosen in place of the Collio DOC","colour":"orange","grapes":"Ribolla Gialla alone, the native white grape of the Collio, grown as Rebula across the Slovenian border.","sites":"The family's hill vineyards at Oslavia on the Slovenian border, on the marl and sandstone of the Collio; the house's own pages record a planting of Ribolla at Hum in 1996.","making":"Fermented with wild yeasts in Georgian clay amphorae buried in the ground, the wine stays on its skins for about six months. Pressed, it returns to amphora for some months more, then rests for years in large casks of Slavonian oak, about six as a rule, and leaves the cellar some seven years after the harvest.","glass":"Amber in the glass: dried apricot, orange peel and acacia honey over beeswax, resin, tea leaf and roasted almond; dry and deep, with a fine grip of tannin, fresh acidity and long, savoury length.","ageing":"Ready to drink when it leaves the cellar, about seven years after the vintage, it can keep for many years more somewhere cool.","serve":"14 to 16 degrees Celsius, cellar cool and never chilled, warmer if it seems closed; open it an hour ahead or decant it, as the aromas open with air; a large white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Ribolla","means":"The grape as the house prints it: Ribolla Gialla in full, Rebula across the Slovenian border."},{"term":"Venezia Giulia","means":"The regional IGT under which the house bottles its wines rather than as Collio DOC."}]},"table":"A white grape made like a red, Gravner's Ribolla Gialla is orange wine: fermented on its skins for months in Georgian amphorae buried in the earth, then aged for years in large oak. Amber, with apricot, honey, tea and a grip of tannin, it comes to the table cellar cool.","story":"By its own account the family bought its house at Oslavia, on the hill above Gorizia, with a little land, in 1901. In the 1980s the son of the house modernised the cellar with stainless steel tanks and French barriques. He came to distrust that style. When hail in 1996 destroyed most of the Ribolla crop, he macerated what survived on its skins, and the long maceration became the house's way. A journey to Georgia in 2000 showed him the qvevri, clay jars buried in the earth, and the first of them arrived at Oslavia in 2001; clay took over the cellar in the years that followed. Early in the 2010s the vines of grapes from elsewhere were pulled up, leaving Ribolla and the native red Pignolo. The wines are bottled as Venezia Giulia IGT rather than as Collio DOC, and each leaves the cellar some seven years after its harvest. The story of Italian orange wine is told from this Ribolla: a white grape treated as a red, then given years of patience before anyone pours it.","others":[{"name":"Bianco Breg","note":"A white blend of Chardonnay, Sauvignon, Pinot Grigio and Riesling Italico made the same way, until those vines were pulled up early in the 2010s."},{"name":"Rosso Gravner","note":"A red, Merlot with a small part of another grape by the house's sheet for the 2010, fermented in amphora and raised for years in Slavonian oak."}],"traps":["Ribolla Gialla is a white grape: the amber colour comes from months on the skins, not from red grapes or from age alone.","Gravner bottles as Venezia Giulia IGT, not Collio DOC, though its vines stand in the Collio hills.","Radikon, its neighbour at Oslavia, macerates in large oak vats; the buried Georgian amphorae are Gravner's."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-34fydpbb","front":"Gravner: what happened in 1996 that turned its Ribolla towards long skin contact?","back":"Hail destroyed most of the Ribolla crop, and what survived was macerated on its skins."},{"id":"fl-kvvasufl","front":"Where did the clay vessels of Gravner's Ribolla come from, and in which year did the first arrive?","back":"From Georgia, after a journey there in 2000; the first arrived at Oslavia in 2001."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-jjeloypr","q":"About how long does Gravner's Ribolla Gialla stay on its skins in amphora?","opts":["About six months","About three weeks","About eight days","About two years"],"a":0,"exp":"The Ribolla macerates about six months on its skins in buried amphorae before pressing; eight days was the skin contact Radikon, its neighbour, first gave its Oslavje, in 1997.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-5j1zqkl5","q":"From which country did Gravner import the buried clay jars it first brought to Oslavia in 2001?","opts":["Portugal","Georgia","Greece","Spain"],"a":1,"exp":"After a journey to Georgia in 2000 the first qvevri arrived in 2001; the tinajas of Spain and the talhas of Portugal are other clay traditions.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Gravner: Vino","url":"https://www.gravner.it/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Producer profile, Gravner","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/producer-profile-gravner-381970/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Primal Wine: Gravner","url":"https://primalwine.com/blogs/natural-wine-producers-primal-wine/gravner","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Callmewine: Ribolla Gialla Anfora Gravner 2018","url":"https://us.callmewine.com/ribolla-gialla-anfora-gravner-2018-P65241.htm","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Falstaff: Gravner 2011 Ribolla IGT","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/wines/gravner-2011-ribolla-igt","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2utl5lzv": {"id":"fl-2utl5lzv","key":"radikon","group":"friuli","region":"friuli","producer":"Radikon","producerId":"p-radikon","say":"","place":"Oslavia, Gorizia, Venezia Giulia IGT, Friuli Venezia Giulia","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Oslavje","appellation":"Venezia Giulia IGT","classification":"Venezia Giulia IGT, the regional tier, used in place of the Collio DOC","colour":"orange","grapes":"Chardonnay, Sauvignon and Pinot Grigio, as the house first made it in 1992; merchants list later vintages as Chardonnay and Sauvignon alone.","sites":"The family's hill vineyards at Oslavia, next to Gravner's by the Slovenian border, on Collio marl and sandstone.","making":"Fermented with wild yeasts in large oak vats, the wine stays about three months on its skins, as every wine of the house has since 2002. It then rests three years or more in large casks of Slavonian oak and a year or two in bottle, and goes out with no sulphur added.","glass":"Deep amber: dried apricot, orange peel and cider apple over walnut, wild herbs and tea leaf, with a little honey; dry, with chewy tannin, bright acidity and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Some four or five years pass before release, when it is ready; it then keeps for many years and gains depth in bottle.","serve":"Cellar cool, 14 to 16 degrees Celsius and never chilled, a little warmer if it seems closed; decant it or open it an hour ahead so the aromas can open; a large white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Oslavje","means":"Oslavia written in Slovene, for the hamlet above Gorizia where the vines grow."},{"term":"Venezia Giulia","means":"An IGT covering the whole region, chosen here over the Collio DOC."}]},"table":"Named in Slovene for its hamlet, Oslavje is Radikon's orange wine: Chardonnay and Sauvignon, with Pinot Grigio in its early years, about three months on the skins in oak vats, then years in large Slavonian casks with no sulphur added. It pours amber, with apricot and orange peel.","story":"The vines at Oslavia were first planted by the grandfather of the man who made the estate's name, who grew Ribolla for the family's own wine. His grandson took on the vineyards in the late 1970s and bottled the estate's first wines from the 1979 vintage, as the house records. Oslavje followed in 1992, a blend of Chardonnay, Sauvignon and Pinot Grigio named with the hamlet's Slovene name. In 1995, the turning point in the house's own words, he macerated his Ribolla on its skins, and from 1997 Oslavje had the same treatment, at first for about eight days. By 2002 every wine spent about three months on its skins and went into bottle with no sulphur added; the house also moved to a new cork and to bottles of half a litre and a litre. He died on 11 September 2016, and his son took sole charge. Oslavje carries the Oslavia way to grapes from elsewhere: Chardonnay and Sauvignon given months on their skins, in wood rather than clay, and kept long before release.","others":[{"name":"Ribolla","note":"The native Ribolla Gialla alone, the first wine the house macerated on its skins, in 1995."},{"name":"Jakot","note":"Friulano on its skins; the name is Tocai written backwards, a protest at the loss of the name."}],"traps":["Oslavje is a blend of grapes from elsewhere made on the skins; Radikon's Ribolla is the native grape bottled alone.","Oslavje and the house's other long macerated wines come in half litre and litre bottles rather than 750 millilitres, so check the size on a list."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-988c9otb","front":"Radikon: what does the name of its wine Oslavje mean?","back":"It is the Slovene name of Oslavia, the hamlet above Gorizia where the vines grow."},{"id":"fl-7u3anri2","front":"In which year did Radikon first macerate its Ribolla on the skins, the house's turning point?","back":"1995; Oslavje followed in 1997, and by 2002 every wine had about three months on its skins with no sulphur added."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ni5qo15u","q":"Which grapes went into Radikon's Oslavje when the house created it in 1992?","opts":["Ribolla Gialla alone","Friulano and Malvasia Istriana","Pinot Bianco and Picolit","Chardonnay, Sauvignon and Pinot Grigio"],"a":3,"exp":"The house's history gives Oslavje of 1992 as Pinot Grigio, Sauvignon and Chardonnay; Ribolla Gialla alone is the house's separate Ribolla.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-f0gnt26j","q":"By which year had Radikon given every wine about three months on its skins and stopped adding sulphur?","opts":["2016","1979","2002","1992"],"a":2,"exp":"The house's timeline puts the change at 2002; 1979 was the first vintage bottled and 1992 the year Oslavje was born.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Radikon: History","url":"https://www.radikon.it/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Radikon: Wines","url":"https://www.radikon.it/en/vini/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Natural wine pioneer Stanko Radikon dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/obituaries/natural-wine-pioneer-stanko-radikon-dies-331308","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bowler Wine: Oslavje IGT Venezia Giulia","url":"https://www.bowlerwine.com/wine-or-spirit/oslavje-igt-venezia-giulia/2020","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Dynamic Vines: Radikon","url":"https://dynamicvines.com/producers/radikon","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ee5xlcco": {"id":"fl-ee5xlcco","key":"ferrari","group":"trentino-alto-adige","region":"trentino-alto-adige","producer":"Ferrari","producerId":"p-ferrari","say":"feh-RAH-ree, ree-ZEHR-vah del fon-dah-TOH-reh","place":"Trento, Trento DOC, Trentino","founded":"1902","wine":{"name":"Giulio Ferrari Riserva del Fondatore","appellation":"Trento DOC","classification":"","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, a Blanc de Blancs.","sites":"Maso Pianizza, a vineyard of the house on the slopes above Trento, some 500 to 600 metres up by most accounts, the source of the first vintage in 1972.","making":"The base wine is made in stainless steel. The second fermentation follows in bottle, and the wine then rests at least seven and a half years on its lees in the house's cellars, more than ten in some vintages, before it is disgorged, with very little sugar added at the dosage.","glass":"Golden apple, candied citrus peel and chamomile over toast, almond paste and a chalky edge; a fine, persistent mousse, a creamy, layered texture and a long, dry finish.","ageing":"Ready to drink when it is released after its long rest on the lees, it keeps developing in bottle for a decade and more.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius, as the house advises, with no decanting; a tulip glass or a white wine glass rather than a narrow flute.","label":[{"term":"Trentodoc","means":"The collective name of the Trento DOC, the appellation for classic method sparkling wine from the province of Trento."},{"term":"Riserva del Fondatore","means":"The founder's reserve: the house's name for its long aged wine, in honour of Giulio Ferrari."}]},"table":"From Maso Pianizza on the slopes above Trento comes Ferrari's Giulio Ferrari Riserva del Fondatore, a Chardonnay fermented a second time in bottle and left more than seven years on its lees. It shows how mountain Chardonnay takes long ageing; pour it cool, into a tulip rather than a flute.","story":"Giulio Ferrari, born in 1879 in the Trentino, then part of the Austrian Tyrol, trained at the agrarian institute of San Michele all'Adige and studied abroad, Champagne among the places he learned. Struck, as the Trentodoc institute tells it, by the likeness between the hills of Trentino and Champagne, he returned to Trento in 1902 and began to make sparkling wine by the classic method, with its second fermentation in bottle. He is credited with bringing Chardonnay to the Trento hills. In 1952, childless and in his seventies, he sold his small house to a Trento wine shop owner of the Lunelli family, who, in the house's telling, went deep into debt to buy it. In 1972 the new owners set aside some five thousand bottles from the grapes of Maso Pianizza for long ageing, and in 1980, the house's history says, they emerged as the first Giulio Ferrari Riserva del Fondatore. In 1993 Trento DOC was recognised as an appellation for classic method sparkling wine alone, by the Trentodoc institute's account the first in Italy. Ask how Italian sparkling wine ages, and the Riserva del Fondatore replies: Chardonnay alone, from one hillside, after seven years and more on its lees.","others":[{"name":"Ferrari Perlé","note":"A vintage Blanc de Blancs of Chardonnay, the house's middle tier between the Brut and the Riserva del Fondatore."},{"name":"Ferrari Brut","note":"The non vintage Chardonnay that carries the Trento DOC name abroad."},{"name":"Ferrari Rosé","note":"A non vintage rosé of Pinot Noir and Chardonnay."}],"traps":["Ferrari of Trento takes its name from its founder, Giulio Ferrari, not from the car maker of Maranello.","Trento DOC is in Trentino; Franciacorta, the other classic method appellation guests name, is in Lombardy.","The Riserva del Fondatore is Chardonnay alone, a Blanc de Blancs, while the house's rosé carries Pinot Noir."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-np83t17a","front":"Giulio Ferrari Riserva del Fondatore: which grape, and from which vineyard?","back":"Chardonnay alone, from Maso Pianizza, a high vineyard of the house on the slopes above Trento."},{"id":"fl-y46p0r1c","front":"Which vintage was the first Riserva del Fondatore, and in which year was it released?","back":"The 1972, set aside from Maso Pianizza grapes and released in 1980, the house says."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-cmdyu4z4","q":"How long does Ferrari's Giulio Ferrari Riserva del Fondatore spend on its lees before it is disgorged?","opts":["About three years","Over seven years","About fifteen months","Over thirty years"],"a":1,"exp":"By the house's sheets it spends from about ninety months to over a hundred and twenty on the lees, more than seven years, far longer than the three years of many vintage sparkling wines.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-aqvpcxdl","q":"In 1952 the childless Giulio Ferrari sold his Trento house. To whom?","opts":["A Trento wine shop owner of the Lunelli family","The agrarian institute of San Michele all'Adige","The Champagne house of Moët et Chandon","A cooperative of Trentino growers"],"a":0,"exp":"He sold it to a Trento wine merchant of the Lunelli family, who went into debt to buy it; San Michele all'Adige is the agrarian institute where Ferrari trained.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Ferrari Trento: About us, the history of the house","url":"https://www.ferraritrento.com/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Ferrari Trento: Giulio Ferrari Riserva del Fondatore","url":"https://www.ferraritrento.com/en/collection/riserve/giulio-ferrari-riserva-del-fondatore/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Ferrari Trento: technical sheet of Giulio Ferrari Riserva del Fondatore 2004","url":"https://www.ferraritrento.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Giulio-Ferrari-Riserva-del-Fondatore-2004.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Trentodoc: History of the Trento DOC","url":"https://www.trentodoc.com/en/institute-and-brand/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Glass of Bubbly: Giulio Ferrari, father of Trentodoc","url":"https://glassofbubbly.com/guilio-ferrari-father-of-trentodoc/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-8xt9uze3": {"id":"fl-8xt9uze3","key":"foradori","group":"trentino-alto-adige","region":"trentino-alto-adige","producer":"Foradori","producerId":"p-foradori","say":"foh-rah-DOH-ree, grah-NAH-toh, teh-ROHL-deh-goh","place":"Mezzolombardo, Campo Rotaliano, Trentino","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Granato","appellation":"Vigneti delle Dolomiti IGT","classification":"Indicazione Geografica Tipica, the regional tier below DOC","colour":"red","grapes":"Teroldego alone.","sites":"Vineyards of the house on the Campo Rotaliano, the plain of gravel and sand laid down by the Noce and the Adige: at first old vines of the estate, later several parcels.","making":"Fermented in open wooden vats with some weeks on the skins. For years part of the wine was raised in small oak barrels; later vintages are aged about a year to fifteen months in large wooden casks, with a short time in cement before bottling.","glass":"Black cherry and blackberry over iron, dried herbs and a touch of violet; fresh acidity and firm, slightly rustic tannin, more savoury than lush, finishing long and dark.","ageing":"Firm and closed at first, it opens after about five years, and a strong vintage keeps for well over a decade.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter helps a young bottle; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Vigneti delle Dolomiti","means":"A regional IGT, wider in area and freer in its rules than the Teroldego Rotaliano DOC."},{"term":"Teroldego","means":"The red grape of Trentino, at home on the Campo Rotaliano, the plain north of Trento."}]},"table":"Foradori raises Granato, its Teroldego, in wood from old vines on the gravel of the Campo Rotaliano. Decant it young: black cherry, iron and dried herbs over firm tannin show that the grape can make a red to keep.","story":"The Campo Rotaliano, the plain of gravel where the Noce meets the Adige north of Trento, is the home of Teroldego, and in 1939 the Foradori family bought a winery at Mezzolombardo on its edge. In 1984, after her father's death, the family's daughter, about twenty years old, took over the cellar. She began to propagate Teroldego by massal selection from the estate's old vines, choosing quality over quantity, and in the late 1980s she made the first Granato from those old vines, raised in wood and meant to age. It showed that the grape of the plain could make a red of depth and long life. From 2002 the whole estate was farmed biodynamically, and Demeter certified it in 2009. Later in the 2000s she began to make wine in clay amphorae, which came to shape single vineyard Teroldego such as Sgarzon, while Granato stayed in wood: small barrels in its early years, large casks later. Granato is labelled under the regional IGT rather than the Teroldego Rotaliano DOC. For a guest new to Teroldego, start here: Granato shows what the grape of the Campo Rotaliano gives from old vines and time in wood.","others":[{"name":"Sgarzon","note":"Teroldego from a single vineyard, aged in clay amphorae and lighter in frame than Granato."},{"name":"Fontanasanta Manzoni Bianco","note":"A white from Manzoni Bianco, a crossing of Riesling and Pinot Bianco, made with skin contact."}],"traps":["Granato grows on the Campo Rotaliano, yet it is labelled Vigneti delle Dolomiti IGT, not Teroldego Rotaliano DOC.","Teroldego is a parent of Lagrein; both are native to Trentino and Alto Adige, and they are two grapes, not one.","The clay amphorae of Foradori shape single vineyard wines such as Sgarzon; Granato is raised in wood."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-rzqb2fys","front":"Foradori's Granato: name its grape and the vines the first one came from.","back":"Teroldego alone, first from old vines of the estate on the gravel of the Campo Rotaliano."},{"id":"fl-q416bbfb","front":"What did the young heir of Foradori do with Teroldego after taking over the cellar in 1984?","back":"She propagated it by massal selection from old vines, choosing quality over quantity, which led to the first Granato in the late 1980s."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-0lnt54mg","q":"Vigneti delle Dolomiti is printed on a bottle of Foradori Granato, and a guest wants to know what it means. What is it?","opts":["The name of the estate's biodynamic certifier","A single walled vineyard on the Campo Rotaliano","A regional IGT, wider than the Teroldego Rotaliano DOC","A DOCG for the long aged reds of Trentino"],"a":2,"exp":"It is the regional IGT on Granato's label, a tier below DOC and wider than the Teroldego Rotaliano DOC; it names no single vineyard.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-i32y21f7","q":"In which year did the Foradori family buy its winery at Mezzolombardo, on the Campo Rotaliano?","opts":["1984","1902","1893","1939"],"a":3,"exp":"The family bought the winery in 1939; 1984 is the year the family's daughter took over the cellar and began the work that led to Granato.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Decanter: Producer profile, Foradori","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/producer-profile-foradori-377861/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Quill and Pad: Foradori, exciting local grape varieties from Trentino","url":"https://quillandpad.com/2022/07/26/foradori-exciting-local-grape-varieties-from-trentino-italy/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Foradori press archive: Wine Spectator, Scaling new heights in Italy's Dolomites","url":"https://www.elisabettaforadori.com/novita/Rassegna-Stampa/Scaling-New-Heights-in-Italy-s-Dolomites--Wine-Spectator.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bottle of Italy: Cantina Foradori","url":"https://bottleofitaly.com/en/pages/cantina-foradori","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Grandi Bottiglie: Granato 2019, Foradori","url":"https://www.grandibottiglie.com/en/granato-2019-foradori.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-xunnx78e": {"id":"fl-xunnx78e","key":"terlano","group":"trentino-alto-adige","region":"trentino-alto-adige","producer":"Cantina Terlano","producerId":"p-cantina-terlano","say":"tehr-LAH-noh, TEHR-lah-ner, FOR-behrk","place":"Terlano, Alto Adige Terlano DOC, Alto Adige","founded":"1893","wine":{"name":"Terlaner Pinot Bianco Riserva Vorberg","appellation":"Alto Adige Terlano DOC","classification":"Riserva, a longer aged wine under the Alto Adige DOC rules","colour":"white","grapes":"Pinot Bianco alone, Weißburgunder in German.","sites":"The Vorberg, steep slopes facing south and south west above Terlano at about 450 to 650 metres, on volcanic quartz porphyry with sandy, quartz rich, slightly acid soils.","making":"Slow, cool fermentation at a controlled temperature takes place in large oak casks, with malolactic fermentation by the house's notes; about a year on its fine lees in large traditional casks follows before bottling.","glass":"Ripe pear, white peach and quince over chamomile, almond and a flinty, salty edge; full and creamy from the lees, yet held taut by fresh acidity through a long finish.","ageing":"It opens after about five tight years and keeps for twenty and more, gaining honey and dried herbs.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; a young bottle gains from a decanter or from time in the glass; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Terlano, Terlaner","means":"The subzone of the Alto Adige DOC around the village, named in Italian and in German."},{"term":"Weißburgunder","means":"The German name of Pinot Bianco, which the label may print beside the Italian."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"A longer aged wine under the rules of the Alto Adige DOC."},{"term":"Vorberg","means":"The steep porphyry hillside above Terlano that gives the grapes."}]},"table":"Steep porphyry slopes above the village give Cantina Terlano its Vorberg, a Pinot Bianco Riserva fermented and aged on its lees in large oak casks. Pear, quince and a flinty edge over firm acidity show how long the whites of Alto Adige can last.","story":"In 1893 twenty four growers of Terlano, then in the Austrian Tyrol, joined forces as a cooperative to free themselves from the large landowners who ruled the wine trade, as the house's history has it, and the oldest bottle in its cellar dates from that founding year. The village's vineyards climb steep slopes of volcanic quartz porphyry, and, by its own reckoning, the house has made a blend of Pinot Bianco, Chardonnay and Sauvignon since its founding. From 1955 to 1993 its cellar master, convinced that the wines were not given their due, quietly set aside about five hundred bottles of every vintage. Tasted years later, they had aged with grace, and the house has kept his method of long ageing on the fine lees, which it names after him. Its Rarity wines rest at least ten years on their lees in steel tanks before bottling, and the archive of old vintages he began lets the house show what time does to its whites. Vorberg, the steep porphyry hillside above the village, gives the Pinot Bianco Riserva that carries the idea into every vintage: large oak casks, a year on the lees and a wine built to keep. Let it settle any doubt that Pinot Bianco can age.","others":[{"name":"Terlaner I Primo Grande Cuvée","note":"A blend, mainly Pinot Bianco with up to a third Chardonnay and some Sauvignon."},{"name":"Rarity","note":"A wine, often Pinot Bianco, aged at least ten years on its lees in steel tanks before bottling."},{"name":"Terlaner Cuvée","note":"The classic blend of Terlano's three traditional white grapes: Pinot Bianco, Chardonnay and Sauvignon."}],"traps":["Terlaner is simply the German form of Terlano, not a grape or a style.","Rarity is no vineyard: it is a wine held at least ten years on its lees in steel tanks before bottling.","In Alto Adige a cooperative is no lesser tier: Cantina Terlano is a cooperative of growers, and Vorberg is its Riserva."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-6neh05vj","front":"Cantina Terlano's Vorberg: what rock lies beneath the slope, and what grape grows on it?","back":"Pinot Bianco, on steep south and south west slopes of volcanic quartz porphyry above Terlano."},{"id":"fl-48frs0s4","front":"What did Cantina Terlano's cellar master of 1955 to 1993 quietly do with every vintage?","back":"He set aside about five hundred bottles, convinced the wines could age; tasted years later, they had."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ftfyza4e","q":"In which vessels is Cantina Terlano's Vorberg Riserva fermented and then aged on its lees?","opts":["Large oak casks","Clay amphorae","New small barriques","Glass lined concrete tanks"],"a":0,"exp":"The house ferments Vorberg slowly in large oak casks and ages it about a year on its lees in large traditional casks; small new barriques would mark it with wood.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-86x54lim","q":"How did Cantina Terlano begin as a winery in 1893?","opts":["A Bolzano merchant bought the village vineyards","Twenty four growers joined as a cooperative","The parish priest founded a cellar for the poor","A monastery opened its cellars to the village"],"a":1,"exp":"Twenty four growers founded it to free themselves from the large landowners; it was a parish priest who founded Cantina Tramin, in 1898.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cantina Terlano: History","url":"https://www.cantina-terlano.com/en/terlano/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cantina Terlano: Wine Archive","url":"https://www.cantina-terlano.com/en/longevity/wine-archive/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cantina Terlano: Vorberg","url":"https://www.cantina-terlano.com/en/wines/vorberg-13/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Cantina Terlano Pinot Blanc, wines reviewed","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/cantina-terlano-pinot-blanc-top-wines-reviewed-424923/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"World of Fine Wine: Cantina Terlano, Italian white wines","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/homepage-featured-articles/cantina-terlano-fine-italian-white-wines","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-bhx12epi": {"id":"fl-bhx12epi","key":"tramin","group":"trentino-alto-adige","region":"trentino-alto-adige","producer":"Cantina Tramin","producerId":"p-cantina-tramin","say":"trah-MEEN, NOOSS-bow-mer, geh-VURTS-trah-mee-ner","place":"Tramin, Termeno in Italian, Alto Adige DOC, Alto Adige","founded":"1898","wine":{"name":"Gewürztraminer Nussbaumer","appellation":"Alto Adige DOC","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Gewürztraminer alone.","sites":"Vineyards near the Nussbaumer farm at Söll, Sella in Italian, above Tramin, about 350 to 550 metres up facing mainly south east, on calcareous gravel with loam over porphyry.","making":"The must is clarified by natural settling and fermented slowly at a controlled temperature of about 18 degrees Celsius in small stainless steel tanks. The wine then matures in small steel tanks on its lees until the end of the following August, at least fourteen months in all before release.","glass":"Lychee, rose petal and ginger over candied orange peel and clove; broad, almost oily in texture, yet dry and fresh, ending long on bitter almond.","ageing":"Perfumed and ready on release, it gains depth for five to ten years when the vintage is strong.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; it needs no decanter; a white wine glass with a generous bowl.","label":[{"term":"Gewürztraminer","means":"The spiced Traminer, the grape whose name, by tradition, recalls the village of Tramin."},{"term":"Alto Adige, Südtirol","means":"The DOC of the province of Bolzano, printed in Italian, in German or in both."},{"term":"Nussbaumer","means":"The farm near Söll whose vineyards give the wine."}]},"table":"In Tramin, the village said to have given the Traminer its name, Cantina Tramin makes Nussbaumer, a dry Gewürztraminer from vineyards at Söll above it. Lychee, rose and ginger over a broad, oily texture stay fresh in the glass, a cool match for spiced dishes.","story":"Tramin, Termeno in Italian, is the village whose name the Traminer grape is said to carry, and the house holds that Gewürztraminer has grown on its slopes for over a thousand years. In 1898 the village's parish priest, Christian Schrott (1853 to 1936), who also sat in the Austrian parliament, founded a growers' cooperative to protect the livelihood of its small growers, as the house tells it. In 1971 it merged with the cooperative of Egna, Neumarkt in German, founded in 1893. Nussbaumer takes its name from a farm near Söll, the hamlet called Sella in Italian, at the foot of Mount Roen above the village, where the vineyards lie on calcareous gravel and loam over porphyry. Fermented in small steel tanks and kept on its lees until the summer after the harvest, it is perfumed and broad, yet fresh, with only a little sugar left. With the 2009 vintage the cellar added Epokale, a Gewürztraminer laid down for some seven years in the disused Monteneve mine at Ridanna before release. Where did the grape's name begin? With Nussbaumer the answer is in the glass and on the map.","others":[{"name":"Epokale Gewürztraminer","note":"A selection laid down for some seven years in the disused Monteneve mine at Ridanna before release; the first was the 2009."},{"name":"Stoan","note":"The house's white blend, led by Chardonnay."}],"traps":["The village is Termeno in Italian and Tramin in German: one place, two names.","The link between Gewürztraminer and Tramin is a naming tradition, not a settled origin of the grape.","Nussbaumer is made dry or close to it, with only a little sugar left, though its perfume of lychee and rose can suggest sweetness."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-hfl1pul6","front":"Cantina Tramin's Nussbaumer: what does the name refer to, and where?","back":"A farm near Söll, Sella in Italian, at the foot of Mount Roen above Tramin, whose vineyards give the wine."},{"id":"fl-n3l8dnxk","front":"Who founded Cantina Tramin in 1898, and why?","back":"Christian Schrott, the parish priest of Tramin and a member of the Austrian parliament, to protect the livelihood of the small growers."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-12fvd8j2","q":"A diner smelling lychee and rose asks whether Cantina Tramin's Nussbaumer Gewürztraminer is sweet. Which describes the wine?","opts":["Sweet, from grapes left late on the vine","Sweet, from grapes dried on mats for months","Sweet, fortified with grape spirit","Dry or nearly so, fermented in steel"],"a":3,"exp":"The house ferments Nussbaumer in small stainless steel tanks to dryness or close to it, with only a little sugar left, and keeps it on its lees until the following summer; its lychee and rose perfume only suggests sweetness.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-uluxzmj1","q":"Cantina Tramin merged in 1971 with the cooperative of which neighbouring village?","opts":["Caldaro, Kaltern in German","Terlano, Terlan in German","Egna, Neumarkt in German","Magrè, Margreid in German"],"a":2,"exp":"Tramin merged in 1971 with the cooperative of Egna, founded in 1893; Terlano's cooperative, also of 1893, is a separate cellar to the north.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cantina Tramin: Our story","url":"https://cantinatramin.it/en/our-dream/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cantina Tramin: The Gewürztraminer","url":"https://cantinatramin.it/en/our-dream/the-gewurztraminer/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cantina Tramin: Nussbaumer Gewürztraminer","url":"https://cantinatramin.it/en/selezioni/nussbaumer-gewurztraminer-en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Winebow: Tramin","url":"https://www.winebow.com/our-brands/tramin","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Deutsche Biographie: Schrott, Christian","url":"https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/pnd1032940174.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3128rhar": {"id":"fl-3128rhar","key":"lageder","group":"trentino-alto-adige","region":"trentino-alto-adige","producer":"Alois Lageder","producerId":"p-alois-lageder","say":"AH-loys LAH-geh-der, LUR-ven-gahng","place":"Magrè sulla Strada del Vino, Margreid in German, Alto Adige","founded":"1823","wine":{"name":"Löwengang Chardonnay","appellation":"Alto Adige DOC","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone.","sites":"Vineyards around the Löwengang estate at Magrè, on sandy, gravelly soils rich in limestone: an alluvial fan of dolomite rock laid down by the Fenner stream.","making":"Fermented with its own yeasts in barriques and larger casks, as the house describes it; the wine then stays on its lees for several months and rests about a year in bottle before release.","glass":"White peach, ripe pear and lemon curd over hazelnut and a little toast; creamy in texture, held by fresh acidity, with a long, saline finish of wet stone.","ageing":"Reserved on release, it opens over three to five years and holds for ten years and longer.","serve":"10 to 13 degrees Celsius, in a white Burgundy glass; air opens a young bottle, in a carafe or in the glass.","label":[{"term":"Löwengang","means":"The manor at Magrè, bought by the family in 1934, that names the wine."},{"term":"Alto Adige, Südtirol","means":"One DOC under two names, Italian and German: the appellation of the province of Bolzano."}]},"table":"Löwengang, the Lageder family's manor at Magrè, names the barrel fermented Chardonnay of Alois Lageder, made with its own yeasts in barriques and larger casks and given a year in bottle before release. Peach, pear and hazelnut over fresh acidity; serve it cool, not cold.","story":"The house traces its beginning to 1823, when the family's founder, a wagon maker in Bolzano, began to trade in wine; his successors bought vineyards and began to make wine themselves. In 1934 the founder's great grandson bought the Löwengang estate at Magrè, a manor of medieval origin whose name, the house relates, goes back to the Gaun family, raised to knights in 1674 with the title von und zu Löwengang. In the early 1980s the family resolved to make a Chardonnay fermented and aged in barrel, as Burgundy does, and by the house's account the first Löwengang Chardonnay, of the 1983 vintage, reached the market in 1984. In 1995 the house moved its seat from Bolzano to Magrè, and from 2004 its own vineyards turned to biodynamic farming. Löwengang carries the estate's name and shows what the limestone rich gravel of Magrè gives Chardonnay when it is fermented in wood with its own yeasts and given time. Learn it by name as a Chardonnay of Alto Adige: barrel fermented, from one estate, and made to keep.","others":[{"name":"Gaun Chardonnay","note":"A separate Chardonnay of the house, with part of the grapes fermented whole."},{"name":"Cor Römigberg","note":"A single vineyard Cabernet Sauvignon from the warm Römigberg slope."}],"traps":["Löwengang names the manor and more than one wine of the house; the barrel fermented white is Löwengang Chardonnay.","Gaun is a different Chardonnay of the house, not a second wine of Löwengang.","Magrè and Margreid are the Italian and German names of the same village, a double naming found across Alto Adige."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-a6ik5nne","front":"Alois Lageder's Löwengang Chardonnay: how is it fermented and aged?","back":"With its own yeasts in barriques and larger casks, on its lees for several months, then about a year in bottle before release."},{"id":"fl-8nbq566y","front":"When did the Lageder family buy the Löwengang estate at Magrè?","back":"In 1934; the house itself traces its trade in wine to 1823 in Bolzano."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-g1scuw64","q":"Where do the grapes of Alois Lageder's Löwengang Chardonnay grow?","opts":["Around Magrè, on gravel rich in limestone","On the gravel plain of the Campo Rotaliano","High in the Val Venosta near Merano","On the porphyry slopes above Terlano"],"a":0,"exp":"The vineyards lie around the Löwengang estate at Magrè, on limestone rich sand and gravel; the porphyry above Terlano gives Cantina Terlano's Vorberg.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-l6qismxa","q":"Alois Lageder traces its beginning to 1823 in Bolzano. What did the founder do that year?","opts":["Bought the Löwengang manor","Began to trade in wine","Planted the first vines at Magrè","Founded a growers' cooperative"],"a":1,"exp":"A wagon maker by trade, he began to trade in wine in 1823; the family bought the Löwengang manor at Magrè only in 1934.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Alois Lageder: Family","url":"https://aloislageder.eu/about-us/family","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Alois Lageder: Inedito, history","url":"https://aloislageder.eu/wines/inedito-en/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Alois Lageder: Löwengang Chardonnay and Löwengang Uvaggio Storico","url":"https://aloislageder.eu/wines/masterpieces/loewengang-wines","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Alois Lageder: Biodynamics","url":"https://aloislageder.eu/biodynamics-diversity/biodynamics","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Blick: Lageder's Chardonnay, thirty years of Löwengang","url":"https://www.blick.ch/life/essen/genuss/lageders-chardonnay-30-jahre-loewengang-lageder-suedtirol/522r63x","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-kj6q6jdh": {"id":"fl-kj6q6jdh","key":"mastroberardino","group":"campania","region":"campania","producer":"Mastroberardino","producerId":"p-mastroberardino","say":"mah-stroh-behr-ar-DEE-noh, tow-RAH-zee RAH-dee-chee","place":"Atripalda in Irpinia, with Taurasi vineyards at Montemarano, Campania","founded":"1878","wine":{"name":"Taurasi Radici Riserva","appellation":"Taurasi DOCG","classification":"Riserva of the Taurasi DOCG: four years before release, eighteen months of them in wood","colour":"red","grapes":"Aglianico alone, where the Taurasi rules ask for at least eighty five per cent of it.","sites":"By the house's recent sheets, a single vineyard at about 550 metres at Montemarano, inside the Taurasi zone, facing south east on calcareous clay; the Riserva is drawn only from the house's Montemarano fruit.","making":"Picked by hand in early November. After fermentation the wine spends about thirty months in French barriques and Slavonian oak casks, then at least three years in bottle at Atripalda before release, well beyond the four years the Riserva rules ask. A Riserva is made only in the vintages the house judges fine enough.","glass":"Sour cherry, dried plum and violet over tar, leather and ash, with tobacco and liquorice as it ages; high acidity and dense, firm tannin carry a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Tight and tannic for its first decade, it opens through its second and can last for decades more; the house's newest sheet speaks of sixty years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; give a young bottle two hours or more in a decanter, and pour an old one gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Taurasi","means":"Aglianico from the hills around the village of Taurasi in Irpinia, a DOC from 1970 and a DOCG from 1993."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Aged at least four years before release, eighteen months of them in wood, by the DOCG's rules."},{"term":"Radici","means":"Italian for roots: the name the house has given its Taurasi since the 1986 vintage."}]},"table":"About thirty months in oak and three years in bottle pass before Mastroberardino's Taurasi Radici Riserva leaves Atripalda: Aglianico alone, from a high vineyard at Montemarano. Sour cherry, tar and firm tannin call for a decanter and a decade.","story":"The family has made wine at Atripalda, by its own account, since the middle of the eighteenth century, and in 1878 a forebear registered the firm with the Avellino Chamber of Commerce and began to send its wines abroad. After the Second World War that forebear's grandson took charge young, on his father's death, and with his brothers he replanted Aglianico, Fiano and Greco from the few surviving vines, while many growers turned to grapes that cropped more heavily. Taurasi, the Aglianico of the hills around the village of Taurasi, became a DOC in 1970 and a DOCG in 1993. From the 1986 vintage, by most accounts, the house gave its Taurasi the name Radici, roots, keeping a Riserva for the years it judged fine enough. In 1994 the family divided: one brother kept the name and the Atripalda cellar, another took much of the vineyard land and founded Terredora. Two years later the house began, with the archaeological authorities, to replant vines inside the excavations of Pompeii, and the first Villa dei Misteri followed with the 2001 vintage. Radici Riserva is Taurasi as the house has long made it: slow, firm and held for years before release, and a lesson in what Aglianico becomes with time.","others":[{"name":"Taurasi Radici","note":"The house's Taurasi without the Riserva's longer keeping, from its vineyards at Montemarano and Mirabella Eclano."},{"name":"Fiano di Avellino Radici","note":"The house's Fiano under the same name, the white it held to when the grape was close to lost."},{"name":"Villa dei Misteri","note":"Piedirosso with Sciascinoso from the vines the house replanted inside Pompeii, first made with the 2001 vintage; the vines later passed to another house."}],"traps":["Taurasi is Aglianico from Campania; Aglianico del Vulture is the same grape across the border in Basilicata.","Terredora is not a second label of Mastroberardino: it was founded by the brother who left the family firm in 1994.","The house gives the name Radici to a Fiano as well, so say Taurasi Radici Riserva in full."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-7upfwax4","front":"Mastroberardino's Taurasi Radici Riserva: how long in wood and in bottle before release?","back":"About thirty months in French barriques and Slavonian casks, then at least three years in bottle."},{"id":"fl-bf3zo1gx","front":"What did Mastroberardino begin inside the excavations of Pompeii in 1996?","back":"The replanting of vines with the archaeological authorities; the first Villa dei Misteri followed with the 2001 vintage."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-kfyja3er","q":"At the table, a guest wants to know which vineyard gives Mastroberardino's Taurasi Radici Riserva. Where is it?","opts":["Terraces above the sea at Furore","The lower slopes of Vesuvius above Naples","A high vineyard at Montemarano in Irpinia","The Vulture volcano in Basilicata"],"a":2,"exp":"A single vineyard at about 550 metres at Montemarano, inside the Taurasi zone, is the source in the house's own sheets; Vulture is Aglianico's other home, in Basilicata, and its wine can never be Taurasi.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-p36eadd8","q":"When the family firm of Mastroberardino split in two in 1994, what did the brother who left go on to do?","opts":["Sell his share to an Avellino cooperative","Replant the vineyards inside Pompeii","Take over the estate of Feudi di San Gregorio","Found Terredora with much of the vineyard land"],"a":3,"exp":"One brother kept the name and the Atripalda cellar while another took much of the vineyard land and founded Terredora; the Pompeii vineyards were Mastroberardino's own project, begun in 1996.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Mastroberardino: La famiglia","url":"https://mastroberardino.com/la-famiglia/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Mastroberardino: Radici Taurasi Riserva DOCG technical sheet","url":"https://mastroberardino.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/RADICI-TAURASI-RISERVA-DOCG-ENG.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Regione Campania: Taurasi DOCG production rules","url":"https://agricoltura.regione.campania.it/pubblicazioni/pdf/DOCGTAU1.PDF","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Mastroberardino producer page","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/producer/mastroberardino/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Mastroberardino producer profile, a taste of elegance","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/mastroberardino-producer-profile-a-taste-of-elegance-475800","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-qrhs8jcy": {"id":"fl-qrhs8jcy","key":"feudi","group":"campania","region":"campania","producer":"Feudi di San Gregorio","producerId":"p-feudi-di-san-gregorio","say":"FEH-oo-dee dee sahn greh-GOR-ee-oh, pee-eh-trah-KAHL-dah","place":"Sorbo Serpico, Irpinia, Fiano di Avellino DOCG, Campania","founded":"1986","wine":{"name":"Fiano di Avellino Pietracalda","appellation":"Fiano di Avellino DOCG","classification":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita, granted to Fiano di Avellino in 2003","colour":"white","grapes":"Fiano, from the one Pietracalda vineyard.","sites":"The Pietracalda vineyard in the house's home commune of Sorbo Serpico, between about 400 and 600 metres on clay and limestone soils, by the house's own sheet; Pietracalda is Italian for hot stone.","making":"The juice is fermented in stainless steel at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius and the wine never sees wood. The house's earlier sheets give four to five months in steel on the fine lees with repeated stirring; its sheet for the 2022 vintage, labelled Riserva, gives twelve months on the lees in steel and three in bottle before it is released.","glass":"Pear, quince and hazelnut over acacia blossom and a smoky, struck flint edge, waxy in texture and fresh, with a saline finish that lasts; honey and toasted nuts come with age.","ageing":"Fresh and ready within a year or two of the harvest; a good vintage then gains honey, beeswax and toasted hazelnut over five to ten years.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, as the house's own sheet suggests, poured without decanting into a white wine glass with room for its perfume.","label":[{"term":"Fiano di Avellino","means":"Fiano from the hills around Avellino in Irpinia, made a DOC in 1978 and raised to DOCG in 2003."},{"term":"Riserva","means":"Printed on the 2022 vintage by the house's sheet; the DOCG asks for at least a year of ageing before release."},{"term":"Pietracalda","means":"The single vineyard at Sorbo Serpico whose name the wine carries."}]},"table":"No oak touches Pietracalda, the Fiano di Avellino of one high vineyard at Sorbo Serpico, which Feudi di San Gregorio ferments and keeps on its lees in steel. Pear, hazelnut and a smoky edge over fresh acidity, served cool, take on honey with age.","story":"Fiano di Avellino, the white of the hills around the city, became a DOC in 1978. Eight years later, in 1986, two families of the province founded Feudi di San Gregorio at Sorbo Serpico, according to the house, and made the province's grapes in a modern cellar: clean fruit, cool fermentation in steel and, for the whites, a long rest on the lees. Beside its Fiano and Greco the house made an Aglianico from old vines, Serpico, and a Merlot, Pàtrimo. Pietracalda is the Fiano of a single vineyard in the house's home commune, high on clay and limestone and never touched by oak: fermented cool in steel and left on its fine lees for texture. In 2003 Fiano di Avellino became a DOCG. In 2004 the house opened a new winery at Sorbo Serpico, the work of a Japanese architect. The house's sheet for the 2022 vintage labels Pietracalda a Riserva, kept a full year on its lees. In one glass, Pietracalda says what Fiano is: grown high in Irpinia, made without wood, and able to age.","others":[{"name":"Serpico","note":"Aglianico from old vines in Irpinia, sold outside the Taurasi DOCG; the house's red statement."},{"name":"Greco di Tufo Cutizzi","note":"The house's Greco from the Tufo hills, firmer and more citrus peel than its Fiano."},{"name":"Taurasi Piano di Montevergine Riserva","note":"The house's Taurasi, from its estate inside the DOCG."}],"traps":["Fiano is waxy and nutty, Greco firmer and more citrus peel: do not swap Pietracalda for Cutizzi.","Serpico is Aglianico grown in Irpinia but is not Taurasi; Pietracalda is the house's Fiano, never its red.","Fiano di Avellino and Greco di Tufo are separate DOCGs, not two styles of one wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-is95ytmv","front":"Feudi di San Gregorio's Pietracalda: what vessel, and how long on the lees?","back":"Stainless steel and no oak; four to five months on the lees in earlier sheets, a year for the Riserva of 2022."},{"id":"fl-rkgpwjzn","front":"In what year, and in which Irpinian commune, did two families found Feudi di San Gregorio?","back":"In 1986, at Sorbo Serpico in the province of Avellino."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-gz1t9nhv","q":"A guest who dislikes oaky whites is considering Feudi di San Gregorio's Pietracalda. How is it made?","opts":["It spends a year in large chestnut casks","It is fermented in steel and kept on its lees","It is fermented and aged in new barriques","It is aged under flor like a Fino"],"a":1,"exp":"The house's sheets give fermentation and ageing in stainless steel on the fine lees, with no wood; its texture comes from the lees, not from new barriques.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-npydmph5","q":"Feudi di San Gregorio was founded in 1986, eight years after Fiano di Avellino became a DOC. When did Fiano di Avellino become a DOCG?","opts":["2003","1970","1993","1989"],"a":0,"exp":"The DOCG was approved by decree in July 2003; 1993 is the year Taurasi became a DOCG, and 1970 the year of its DOC.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Feudi di San Gregorio: home page and history","url":"https://www.feudi.it/en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Feudi di San Gregorio: Discover our winery","url":"https://www.feudi.it/en/book-a-visit","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Feudi di San Gregorio: Pietracalda technical sheet","url":"https://www.feudi.it/public/files/items/43/scheda/pietracalda_en.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Feudi di San Gregorio: Pietracalda Fiano di Avellino DOCG Riserva 2022 sheet","url":"https://store.feudi.it/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/Pietracalda-Riserva-Scheda-tecnica-ITA.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Regione Campania: Fiano di Avellino DOCG production rules","url":"https://agricoltura.regione.campania.it/pubblicazioni/pdf/DOCGFIA1.PDF","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-y709tqbf": {"id":"fl-y709tqbf","key":"montevetrano","group":"campania","region":"campania","producer":"Montevetrano","producerId":"p-montevetrano","say":"mon-teh-veh-TRAH-noh","place":"San Cipriano Picentino, in the hills above Salerno, Campania","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Montevetrano","appellation":"Colli di Salerno IGT","classification":"Indicazione Geografica Tipica, the category below DOC and DOCG","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the larger part, with Merlot and about a tenth of Aglianico; the first vintages were Cabernet with a little Aglianico alone.","sites":"The estate's own vineyards on the hills of San Cipriano Picentino above Salerno, facing south west; no bought grapes go into the wine.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel after about a seventh of the juice is drawn off, with frequent punching down and some twenty days on the skins; the malolactic follows. The wine then ages for eight to fourteen months by vintage in 225 litre French oak barriques, most of them new, and six months in bottle before it leaves the estate.","glass":"Cassis, black cherry and graphite over tobacco leaf and dark chocolate, with the ashy, sour cherry edge of Aglianico; full and polished, its ripe, firm tannin balanced by fresh acidity.","ageing":"Approachable after five years or so, it goes on developing for fifteen to twenty years in bottle, as the estate itself suggests.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; open a young bottle into a decanter an hour or two before the meal; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Colli di Salerno","means":"The Indicazione Geografica Tipica named for the hills of Salerno, under which the estate sells its Cabernet led blend."},{"term":"Montevetrano","means":"The name of the estate and of its flagship red alike."}]},"table":"Montevetrano is a small estate's blend of Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Aglianico from the hills above Salerno, first made in 1991 and first sold with the 1993 vintage. Dark and polished, with an ashy Aglianico edge, it wants decanting and a Bordeaux glass.","story":"Montevetrano grew from a friendship. In the middle of the 1980s, as the estate tells it, a group of wine lovers set out to experiment with vines at San Cipriano Picentino, in the hills above Salerno. One of them was an oenologist who would later make his name as a consultant, and they grew Cabernet Sauvignon beside Aglianico, in a region whose classic red was Aglianico. After six years of work in the vineyard, the first bottles, of the 1991 vintage, were Cabernet with a little Aglianico and were made only for friends; a few more followed from 1992, and 1993 is the vintage the estate gives as its first for sale. By the middle of the 1990s Merlot had joined the blend, which settled at about six parts Cabernet to three of Merlot and one of Aglianico, all from the estate's own vines and aged in French barriques, most of them new. The wine is sold as Colli di Salerno IGT, outside the rules of any DOC: a Bordeaux blend with a Campanian accent. Call it the southern answer to the Super Tuscans: international grapes, a shadow of Aglianico, and the estate's own name on the label.","others":[{"name":"Core","note":"The estate's red from Aglianico alone, the grape that seasons the flagship."}],"traps":["Montevetrano is an IGT, Colli di Salerno, not a Campanian DOCG such as Taurasi.","Its Aglianico is a seasoning, about a tenth of the blend, not the base.","The 1991 was made only for friends, and 1993 is the first vintage given for sale, so both years appear in accounts of the first Montevetrano."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-vg8w556n","front":"Montevetrano: which three grapes, and which leads the blend?","back":"Cabernet Sauvignon leads, with Merlot and about a tenth of Aglianico."},{"id":"fl-69tt3ssn","front":"What became of Montevetrano's 1991, its opening vintage?","back":"It was bottled only for friends; 1993 is the vintage the estate gives as its first for sale."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-irk9qnqm","q":"A guest who knows Taurasi asks how the blend of Montevetrano is built. Which grape leads, and with what?","opts":["Merlot, with Cabernet Sauvignon and Aglianico","Piedirosso, with Aglianico and Merlot","Cabernet Sauvignon, with Merlot and Aglianico","Aglianico, with Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot"],"a":2,"exp":"Cabernet Sauvignon is the larger part, with Merlot and about a tenth of Aglianico; Merlot joined only in the 1990s, and Aglianico is the seasoning, not the base.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-mwkktnhu","q":"Montevetrano's first vintage was bottled in tiny quantity, only for friends. Which year was it?","opts":["1980","1997","1985","1991"],"a":3,"exp":"The 1991 came after six years of work in the vineyard and went only to friends; 1993 is the vintage the estate gives as its first for sale.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Montevetrano: Azienda","url":"https://www.montevetrano.it/en/azienda/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Montevetrano: Montevetrano 1996","url":"https://www.montevetrano.it/en/1996-2/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Montevetrano: Montevetrano 2018 technical sheet","url":"https://www.montevetrano.it/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Montevetrano-2018-eng-new..pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Superiore.de: Montevetrano producer page","url":"https://www.superiore.de/en/wine/campania/montevetrano","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Scatti di Gusto: Montevetrano, trent'anni in quattro annate","url":"https://www.scattidigusto.it/2023/12/17/montevetrano-30-anni-stupendi-in-4-annate","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-tt5sn3ae": {"id":"fl-tt5sn3ae","key":"marisa-cuomo","group":"campania","region":"campania","producer":"Marisa Cuomo","producerId":"p-marisa-cuomo","say":"mah-REE-zah KWOH-moh, fee-or-DOO-vah","place":"Furore, Costa d'Amalfi DOC, Campania","founded":"1980","wine":{"name":"Furore Bianco Fiorduva","appellation":"Costa d'Amalfi Furore","classification":"Costa d'Amalfi DOC since 1995, with Furore one of its three named subzones","colour":"white","grapes":"Ripoli, the larger part, with Fenile and Ginestra in about equal shares: three old grapes of the Amalfi coast.","sites":"Terraces cut into the cliffs of the Furore coast, from about 200 to 550 metres, facing south over the sea on dolomitic limestone rock, worked by hand.","making":"The grapes are picked by hand late, in the last ten days of October, and pressed gently, and the juice is settled cold. It then ferments slowly at about 12 degrees Celsius in oak barriques, for about three months by one of the house's sheets; another gives six months in small oak casks before bottling.","glass":"Candied apricot, citrus peel and dried wild herbs over honey and a salty, sea spray edge; full and rounded, fresh, and savoury to the last.","ageing":"Generous from the day it is released, it deepens for several years after a good harvest.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, straight from the bottle with no decanter, into a wide bowled white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Costa d'Amalfi","means":"The DOC of the Amalfi coast, approved in 1995, with Furore, Ravello and Tramonti as named subzones."},{"term":"Furore","means":"The subzone named for the cliff village where the house stands and the grapes grow."},{"term":"Bianco","means":"White; Fiorduva is the house's late picked selection, apart from its plain Furore Bianco."}]},"table":"Ripoli, Fenile and Ginestra from the cliff terraces of Furore on the Amalfi coast, picked late and fermented in oak barriques, make Marisa Cuomo's white, Fiorduva. Poured cool, never cold, its apricot, wild herbs and sea salt carry the weight for richer fish.","story":"Vines grow on narrow terraces above the sea at Furore, a village strung down the cliffs of the Amalfi coast. As the house's own history runs, a family founded the business in 1942 to sell the wines of the Furore coast as Gran Furor Divina Costiera, and in 1980 a man of the family gave it to his bride as a wedding gift and put her name on it; one account dates the gift to 1983, after he bought out his relatives' shares in 1980. The cellar was dug into the dolomitic limestone of the cliff. The house worked the coast's own grapes: Fenile, Ginestra and Ripoli for its whites, Piedirosso and Aglianico for its reds, grown on terraces worked entirely by hand. In 1995 the Costa d'Amalfi became a DOC, with Furore, Ravello and Tramonti as its named subzones, and the house's wines from Furore carry the subzone's name. Fiorduva is its white of the Furore subzone: the three grapes picked late in October and fermented slowly in oak. It matters because of where it comes from: a few terraces between the mountains and the sea, and grapes found almost nowhere else, which survive because the ground was too steep to replant.","others":[{"name":"Furore Rosso","note":"Piedirosso with Aglianico from the same terraces, the red side of the house."},{"name":"Furore Bianco","note":"The house's plain white of Furore, made apart from Fiorduva."}],"traps":["Fenile, Ginestra and Ripoli are grape names, not vineyard names.","Costa d'Amalfi is one DOC with three named subzones, Furore, Ravello and Tramonti; Furore is not a DOC of its own.","Marisa Cuomo is the house's name on the label; the wine itself is Fiorduva, and the plain Furore Bianco is another bottle."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-3s417a1m","front":"Marisa Cuomo's Fiorduva: what goes into the blend, and in what shares?","back":"Ripoli, the larger part, with Fenile and Ginestra: three old grapes of the Amalfi coast."},{"id":"fl-m54o9b9j","front":"Into what rock was the cellar of the Furore house that makes Fiorduva dug?","back":"The dolomitic limestone of the Furore cliffs."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-jaf1yci8","q":"Fiorduva has weight and a rounded texture. Which steps in the making give them?","opts":["Ageing under flor in a solera","Late picking and a slow fermentation in barriques","A second fermentation in the bottle","Long skin contact in clay amphorae"],"a":1,"exp":"The grapes are picked late in October and fermented slowly at low temperature in oak barriques, by the house's sheets; there is no skin contact in amphora and no flor.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-xq6x7xpq","q":"By the account of the house that makes Fiorduva, what happened to the Furore estate in 1980?","opts":["It was given to a bride as a wedding gift","A landslide destroyed its old cellar","It was bought by a Naples cooperative","The Costa d'Amalfi DOC was approved"],"a":0,"exp":"The house says the estate was given in 1980 as a wedding gift and named for the bride; the Costa d'Amalfi DOC came only in 1995.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Marisa Cuomo: Azienda","url":"https://www.marisacuomo.com/azienda/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marisa Cuomo: Furore Bianco Fiorduva","url":"https://www.marisacuomo.com/vini/furore-bianco-fiorduva/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marisa Cuomo: The Company","url":"https://www.marisacuomo.com/company-marisa-cuomo-winery.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"AIS: Costa d'Amalfi DOC production rules","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Campania/DOC/Costa%20d'Amalfi.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Air-oir on the Amalfi Coast","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/cantine-marisa-cuomo-italy-amalfi-coast-wine","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-xf3z11ag": {"id":"fl-xf3z11ag","key":"villa-matilde","group":"campania","region":"campania","producer":"Villa Matilde","producerId":"","say":"VEEL-lah mah-TEEL-deh, fah-LEHR-noh, kah-mah-RAH-toh","place":"Cellole, Falerno del Massico DOC, on the slopes of Roccamonfina, Campania","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Falerno del Massico Vigna Camarato","appellation":"Falerno del Massico","classification":"DOC since 1989, for five communes of Caserta province","colour":"red","grapes":"Aglianico, about four fifths, with Piedirosso for the rest, by the house's sheet.","sites":"The single Camarato vineyard at San Castrese, in the commune of Sessa Aurunca, on the slopes of the extinct Roccamonfina volcano.","making":"Made only in the years the house judges strong enough, the wine ages after fermentation for twelve to eighteen months in Allier oak barriques, about a third of them new and the rest in their second or third year, then as long again in bottle before it is sold.","glass":"Black cherry, plum and violet over tobacco, liquorice and a smoky, savoury edge; full bodied, with Aglianico's high acidity, firm tannin and a finish that stays dry and long.","ageing":"Young, it is firm and shut; it needs time in bottle, and a strong vintage is made for long keeping.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; give a young bottle an hour or two in a decanter; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Falerno del Massico","means":"The DOC of 1989 named for the Roman Falernian, from five communes of Caserta province."},{"term":"Vigna Camarato","means":"Vigna means vineyard: the wine of the one Camarato vineyard at San Castrese."},{"term":"Rosso","means":"Red; the DOC also allows a white and a Primitivo."}]},"table":"Made only in strong years, Villa Matilde's Vigna Camarato is a single vineyard Falerno del Massico of Aglianico with some Piedirosso from the slopes of the Roccamonfina volcano, aged in barriques. Savoury, dark and firm, it needs years in the cellar and air before it is poured.","story":"The Romans prized Falernian, grown in the Ager Falernus beneath Monte Massico; Pliny, Horace and Martial wrote of it, and its Opimian vintage of 121 BC was poured decades after the harvest. Which grapes made it is disputed, and by the early twentieth century, on the house's word, the wine was no longer made. In the 1960s, as the house tells it, a lawyer with a passion for old wines and for the Latin authors set out to bring Falernian back. With friends, professors of the University of Naples among them, he searched the district for vines that had survived phylloxera at the end of the nineteenth century, and the stock he found was replanted on the Massico. The estate he built was named Villa Matilde, for his wife. Its Falerno del Massico, first made with the 1975 vintage by the house's sheet, came before the law: the DOC followed in 1989. The Camarato vineyard at San Castrese gave a wine of its own from the 1981 vintage, made only in strong years. In 1985 the founder's children took charge. Whatever its name owes to Falernian, Vigna Camarato is a modern wine of Aglianico and Piedirosso from the same hills, not a copy of a Roman one.","others":[{"name":"Falerno del Massico Rosso","note":"The estate's younger red of Aglianico and Piedirosso, made since the 1975 vintage, partly aged in large Slavonian casks."},{"name":"Falerno del Massico Bianco","note":"Falanghina from the same hills, the white of the revival."}],"traps":["Falerno del Massico is a DOC of 1989; the Roman Falernian is only its namesake, and accounts differ on which grapes made it.","Vigna Camarato is Aglianico led; Villa Matilde's Falerno del Massico Bianco is Falanghina, so the name Falerno does not tell a colour.","Roccamonfina, the extinct volcano of the Falerno vineyards, is not Vesuvius, the volcano above Naples."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-odzje6n2","front":"Villa Matilde's Vigna Camarato: which grapes, and on what volcano's slopes?","back":"Aglianico, about four fifths, with Piedirosso, from the Camarato vineyard on the slopes of Roccamonfina."},{"id":"fl-5qsilx6f","front":"Which wine of ancient Rome did the founder of Villa Matilde set out in the 1960s to bring back?","back":"Falernian, the wine of Monte Massico that Pliny and Horace wrote of."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-vws0kwn2","q":"What sets Villa Matilde's Vigna Camarato apart from the estate's plain Falerno Rosso?","opts":["A long ageing under flor in the cellar","Falanghina in place of Piedirosso in the blend","One vineyard, strong years only, and barriques","Grapes bought from growers across Caserta"],"a":2,"exp":"Vigna Camarato comes from the single Camarato vineyard, made only in strong years and aged twelve to eighteen months in barriques; the plain Rosso is partly aged in large casks, and neither holds Falanghina.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-hs60h2e0","q":"Villa Matilde's founder searched the Massico in the 1960s with friends from the University of Naples. What were they looking for?","opts":["Vines brought from Tuscany after the war","Cabernet planted by Napoleon's officers","Grapes for a sparkling wine of Naples","Vines that had survived phylloxera"],"a":3,"exp":"In the estate's account, they sought stock that had outlived phylloxera at the end of the nineteenth century, to replant the vines of the Roman Falernian on the Massico.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Villa Matilde: Storia","url":"https://villamatilde.it/storia","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Villa Matilde: Vigna Camarato Falerno del Massico Rosso","url":"https://villamatilde.it/vigna-camarato","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Villa Matilde: Falerno del Massico Rosso sheet","url":"https://www.villamatilde.it/download/Falerno-del-Massico-Rosso.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"AIS: Falerno del Massico DOC production rules","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Campania/DOC/Falerno%20del%20Massico.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Falernian wine","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falernian_wine","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-1p6519gp": {"id":"fl-1p6519gp","key":"tasca","group":"sicily","region":"sicily","producer":"Tasca d'Almerita","producerId":"p-tasca-d-almerita","say":"TAH-skah dahl-meh-REE-tah, ROHS-soh del KOHN-teh","place":"The Regaleali estate in the inland hills, Contea di Sclafani DOC, Sicily","founded":"1830","wine":{"name":"Rosso del Conte","appellation":"Contea di Sclafani DOC","classification":"DOC, Denominazione di Origine Controllata, one rank below DOCG","colour":"red","grapes":"Nero d'Avola, more than half in the vintages of the late 2010s, with Perricone for the rest; some older vintages took in other red grapes of the estate.","sites":"The San Lucio vineyard at Regaleali, about 480 metres up and facing south on sandy loam with some limestone: Perricone and Nero d'Avola planted mixed in 1959, with a block of Nero d'Avola alone added in 1965.","making":"Fermentation in stainless steel starts cool and finishes warmer, with about twenty days on the skins and a full malolactic. The wine then spends about eighteen months in new 225 litre barriques of French oak from Allier and Tronçais before bottling, by the house's sheet for the 2019 vintage.","glass":"Black cherry, red plum and carob over liquorice, tobacco leaf, dried Mediterranean herbs and the vanilla and clove of new oak; full and warm, with ripe, firm tannin, fresh acidity and a long, dusty finish.","ageing":"Dense and oaky when young, it opens after five years or so, and a strong year can keep for two decades and more, turning towards leather and dried fig.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant it an hour or two before pouring when young, and an old bottle slowly, leaving its sediment behind; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Contea di Sclafani","means":"The high inland DOC in which the Regaleali estate lies."},{"term":"Tenuta Regaleali","means":"The family's estate in the interior, bought in 1830, where the wine is grown."},{"term":"Rosso del Conte","means":"The count's red: the name the wine took after it was first sold as Riserva del Conte."}]},"table":"High in the island's interior, on the Regaleali estate, the San Lucio vineyard planted in 1959 gives Tasca d'Almerita's Rosso del Conte, Nero d'Avola with Perricone. Black cherry, carob and firm tannin follow eighteen months in new oak; decant it, then pour it with lamb.","story":"Regaleali, a great estate in the hills of the island's interior between Palermo and Caltanissetta, was bought in 1830 by two brothers of the Tasca family. The land reform of the 1950s, the family recalls, took more than half of the estate. In 1959 the count who then led the family planted Perricone and Nero d'Avola together on the hill of San Lucio. The vineyard gave a red of its own with the 1970 vintage, sold as Riserva del Conte and later named Rosso del Conte, the count's red, and the house calls it the first single vineyard wine of Sicily. At a time when much of the island's red left it in bulk, Regaleali bottled a Sicilian red from one vineyard, made to keep. The count's son later planted international varieties at Regaleali, and the family went on to vineyards on Etna, on Salina and on the island of Mozia. Through Rosso del Conte a guest meets inland Sicily: high, far from the sea, and a red that rewards years in the cellar.","others":[{"name":"Riserva del Conte","note":"The old name, made again from the same vineyard from the 2010 vintage after forty years, and aged in 500 litre chestnut barrels."},{"name":"Nozze d'Oro","note":"The estate's white, Inzolia with a local selection of Sauvignon."},{"name":"Tascante","note":"The family's Nerello Mascalese from Etna."}],"traps":["Regaleali is the estate and Tasca d'Almerita the family: a bottle may carry both names, and the flagship is Rosso del Conte.","Rosso del Conte was at first sold as Riserva del Conte; the house brought that name back with the 2010 vintage for a separate, chestnut aged wine of the same vineyard.","Rosso del Conte is Nero d'Avola with Perricone from the inland hills, not with Frappato, which is the partner of Cerasuolo di Vittoria."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-nw5fdxf9","front":"Tasca d'Almerita's Rosso del Conte: which vineyard gives it, and when was it planted?","back":"San Lucio at Regaleali, planted with Perricone and Nero d'Avola together in 1959."},{"id":"fl-9as63yly","front":"Under what name was the first vintage of Rosso del Conte, the 1970, sold?","back":"Riserva del Conte; the wine was later named Rosso del Conte, the count's red."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-lzhakked","q":"Which two grapes make Tasca d'Almerita's Rosso del Conte, the count's red of Regaleali?","opts":["Nero d'Avola with Cabernet Sauvignon","Nero d'Avola with Perricone","Nerello Mascalese with Nerello Cappuccio","Nero d'Avola with Frappato"],"a":1,"exp":"The San Lucio vineyard was planted with Perricone and Nero d'Avola together in 1959; Nero d'Avola with Frappato is the blend of Cerasuolo di Vittoria, from the south east.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-36bbmcn8","q":"Which estate in the hills of the interior did two brothers of the Tasca family buy in 1830?","opts":["Regaleali","Samperi","Mozia","Contessa Entellina"],"a":0,"exp":"The brothers bought Regaleali in 1830, and Rosso del Conte grows on its San Lucio hill; Contessa Entellina is Donnafugata's estate, and Mozia an island where the family came later.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Tasca d'Almerita: Our story","url":"https://tascadalmerita.it/en/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tasca d'Almerita: Rosso del Conte, Nero d'Avola and Perricone","url":"https://tascadalmerita.it/en/wines/tenuta-regaleali-rosso-del-conte/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tasca d'Almerita: Rosso del Conte 2019 technical sheet","url":"https://cdn.tascadalmerita.it/tasca/Tenuta-Regaleali-Rosso-Del-Conte-2019-ENG.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Terra Madre: Tasca d'Almerita, history, Regaleali and Mozia","url":"https://www.terramadre.it/en/tasca-d-almerita-regaleali-mozia-history.htm","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Italian Wines: Tasca d'Almerita winery page","url":"https://www.italianwines.co.uk/wineries/sicily/tasca-d-almerita","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-sgp59omf": {"id":"fl-sgp59omf","key":"donnafugata","group":"sicily","region":"sicily","producer":"Donnafugata","producerId":"p-donnafugata","say":"don-nah-foo-GAH-tah, ben ree-EH","place":"Pantelleria, the volcanic island off Tunisia, Passito di Pantelleria DOC, Sicily","founded":"1983","wine":{"name":"Ben Ryé Passito di Pantelleria","appellation":"Passito di Pantelleria DOC","classification":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata, for the island's sweet wine of dried Zibibbo","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Zibibbo alone, the Muscat of Alexandria of Pantelleria.","sites":"Low bush vines of Zibibbo, each set in a hollow dug against the wind, on terraces walled with dry stone across several contrade of the house's vineyards on Pantelleria, a volcanic island closer to Tunisia than to Sicily.","making":"Picked in two passes. From August, grapes of an early picking are laid out to dry in the sun and wind; later grapes are pressed for a fresh must, and the dried ones, stripped from their stalks by hand, are added to it in batches as it ferments in stainless steel, a month and more in all. The wine then rests some months in tank and ten months or more in bottle, by the house's sheets.","glass":"Dried apricot, fig and date over candied orange peel, honey and saffron, with a lift of thyme and a salty edge; sweet, dense and long, kept fresh by bright acidity.","ageing":"Open and fragrant from release, it deepens towards date, toffee and walnut over a decade and more in bottle.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, or a little warmer to open its perfume; leave the decanter aside and pour it into a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Passito di Pantelleria","means":"The sweet wine of Zibibbo grown on Pantelleria, made in part from grapes dried in the sun."},{"term":"Ben Ryé","means":"From the Arabic for son of the wind, for the wind that sweeps the island's vines."},{"term":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata","means":"The DOC rank: grown and made under the rules of the island's denomination."}]},"table":"On windswept, volcanic Pantelleria, Donnafugata grows the Zibibbo of Ben Ryé on low bush vines and dries part of it in the sun to add to the fermenting must. Apricot, fig and honey with a salty freshness: pour this Passito di Pantelleria cold, beside blue cheese or almond pastry.","story":"Marsala had long known the Rallo family's wine, and the family's old cellars in the town date, by the house's account, from 1851. In 1983 Giacomo Rallo (1937 to 2016) and his wife founded Donnafugata, with the family's vineyards at Contessa Entellina, which she had taken in hand a few years before. She chose the name from the novel The Leopard: Donnafugata, the woman in flight, recalls, in the house's version, a Bourbon queen who fled Naples and found refuge on the lands where its vineyards lie. In 1989 the house came to Pantelleria, the volcanic island off Tunisia where Zibibbo grows as low bush vines in hollows dug against the wind, and that year it made the first Ben Ryé, son of the wind in Arabic. In 2008 it restored a walled Pantellerian garden at Khamma and gave it to FAI, the Italian national trust, and in 2014 UNESCO inscribed the island's way of growing its bush vines as intangible cultural heritage of humanity. Giacomo Rallo died in 2016, and his children took the house on. Ben Ryé carries the sweet wine of the Sicilian islands to the table: grapes dried by sun and wind on a small island, kept fresh by bright acidity.","others":[{"name":"Mille e una Notte","note":"The house's red from Contessa Entellina, led by Nero d'Avola."},{"name":"Tancredi","note":"Nero d'Avola with Cabernet Sauvignon, a long running blend of the house."}],"traps":["Zibibbo on Pantelleria is Muscat of Alexandria, not Muscat Blanc à Petits Grains.","Passito di Pantelleria and Moscato di Pantelleria are separate styles of the same grape under the island's rules, so name the passito in full.","Donnafugata is a name taken from a novel, not a place on Pantelleria: the house began at Marsala and Contessa Entellina in western Sicily."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-tyaay3pn","front":"Donnafugata's Ben Ryé: how are the sun dried grapes used?","back":"Zibibbo dried in the sun and wind is added in batches to fresh must as it ferments in steel."},{"id":"fl-4e88n3an","front":"What does the name Ben Ryé mean, and what was its first vintage?","back":"Son of the wind, from the Arabic; the first vintage was 1989, the year Donnafugata came to Pantelleria."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-gphvwbmb","q":"By which method does Donnafugata give Ben Ryé its richness?","opts":["Grapes frozen on the vine before pressing","Grapes left on the vine until noble rot sets in","Grape spirit added to stop the fermentation","Sun dried Zibibbo added to fermenting fresh must"],"a":3,"exp":"Grapes dried in the sun and wind are added in batches to fresh must as it ferments; neither noble rot nor added spirit appears on the house's sheets.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-iiiyqcut","q":"One of Donnafugata's founders chose the house's name in 1983. Where does it come from?","opts":["The Arabic for son of the wind","A contrada on Pantelleria","The novel The Leopard","A Marsala merchant of 1851"],"a":2,"exp":"The founder's wife took it from The Leopard, and the house ties its meaning, the woman in flight, to a fleeing queen; son of the wind is the meaning of Ben Ryé.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Donnafugata: Quality wine for generations","url":"https://www.donnafugata.it/en/roots/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Donnafugata: Ben Ryé Passito di Pantelleria DOC","url":"https://www.donnafugata.it/en/product/ben-rye/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage: the vite ad alberello of Pantelleria","url":"https://ich.unesco.org/fr/RL/la-pratique-agricole-traditionnelle-de-la-culture-de-la-vite-ad-alberello-taille-de-la-vigne-en-gobelet-de-la-communaute-de-pantelleria-00720","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Italian wine veteran Giacomo Rallo dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/italian-wine-veteran-giacomo-rallo-dies-300786/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Terra Madre: Ben Ryé Passito di Pantelleria, Donnafugata","url":"https://www.terramadre.it/en/sweet-wines/ben-rye-doc-donnafugata.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3hlg9kas": {"id":"fl-3hlg9kas","key":"benanti","group":"sicily","region":"sicily","producer":"Benanti","producerId":"p-benanti","say":"beh-NAHN-tee, PYEH-trah mah-REE-nah","place":"Milo, on the eastern slope of Etna, Etna DOC, Sicily","founded":"1988","wine":{"name":"Etna Bianco Superiore Pietra Marina","appellation":"Etna DOC","classification":"Superiore: Etna Bianco from the commune of Milo alone, at least eighty per cent Carricante","colour":"white","grapes":"Carricante alone, where the Superiore rules ask for at least eighty per cent.","sites":"Old bush vines on small terraces walled with dry stone at Milo on Etna's eastern slope, in Contrada Rinazzo by the house's recent sheets, at about 800 metres, open to light, to the sea and to its winds.","making":"From the middle of October the grapes are picked by hand and pressed gently, and the juice is fermented for about twelve days in temperature controlled stainless steel with a selected native yeast. The wine never sees oak: it rests two years and more on its fine lees in steel, stirred often, about thirty months by the recent sheets, then a year in bottle before it is sold.","glass":"Lemon rind, green apple and white peach over chamomile, aniseed and the ash and wet stone of the volcano; lean and taut, its high, salty acidity carrying the finish a long way.","ageing":"Citric and taut for its first three to five years, it gains beeswax, honey and a smoky depth across ten years and more in a good year.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; a decanter is not needed, though air helps a young bottle; a white wine glass with a wide bowl.","label":[{"term":"Etna Bianco Superiore","means":"Etna Bianco from the commune of Milo alone, with at least eighty per cent Carricante."},{"term":"Etna DOC","means":"The volcano's denomination of 1968, the island's first DOC."},{"term":"Pietra Marina","means":"The house's name for its Carricante from Milo, bottled from the 1990 vintage on."}]},"table":"Milo, on the volcano's eastern face, is where Benanti grows the Carricante of Pietra Marina on old terraced bush vines; the Etna Bianco Superiore then rests for years on its lees in steel, never in oak. Lemon rind, ash and salty acidity reward years in bottle and a cool pour.","story":"At the end of the nineteenth century, the house records, a forebear made wine for his own pleasure at Viagrande, on the south eastern slope of Etna. In 1988 Giuseppe Benanti (1945 to 2023), born in Catania, founded a winery at Castiglione di Sicilia on the volcano's northern side, first called Tenuta di Castiglione. Few houses then bottled wine on Etna, whose DOC of 1968 had been the island's first, and the two harvests before 1990 went on trials of the mountain's grapes and slopes. The 1990 vintage gave the first wines to be bottled, among them Pietra Marina, Carricante from the terraces of Milo on the eastern face, the one commune whose white may be called Etna Bianco Superiore; it was shown at Vinitaly in 1993. In 1998, as the house tells it, the family added the old vineyards of Monte Serra at Viagrande, where its forebear had lived. In 2012 the founder handed the house to his twin sons, and he died in 2023. Pietra Marina is what Etna gives beyond its reds: Carricante from Milo, slow to open and long to live.","others":[{"name":"Rovittello","note":"Etna Rosso of Nerello Mascalese from Castiglione di Sicilia on the northern slope, one of the house's first wines."},{"name":"Serra della Contessa","note":"Nerello Mascalese with Nerello Cappuccio from old vines at Monte Serra, on the south eastern slope."}],"traps":["Etna Bianco Superiore may come only from the commune of Milo and needs at least eighty per cent Carricante; plain Etna Bianco needs sixty.","Many lists spell the wine Pietramarina; the house prints Pietra Marina, and it is not the house's Contrada Rinazzo, a separate Etna Bianco Superiore from the same contrada of Milo.","Benanti began in 1988 at Castiglione di Sicilia on the northern slope, not at Viagrande, where the family's forebear had made wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-jikp8nyr","front":"Benanti's Pietra Marina: name the grape and the commune it comes from.","back":"Carricante, from terraced bush vines at Milo on Etna's eastern slope."},{"id":"fl-mfgtksrb","front":"In what year, and on which side of Etna, was Benanti founded?","back":"In 1988, at Castiglione di Sicilia on the northern side, first as Tenuta di Castiglione."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-wrjjufh4","q":"What lets Benanti's Pietra Marina call itself Etna Bianco Superiore?","opts":["It spends at least two years in oak","It is Carricante grown in the commune of Milo","It blends Carricante from every slope of Etna","It comes from vines above a thousand metres"],"a":1,"exp":"The Superiore is kept for Etna Bianco from Milo with at least eighty per cent Carricante; height and oak are not the rule, and Pietra Marina sees steel alone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-v7zy3g4w","q":"Benanti's first wines to be bottled, Pietra Marina among them, came from which vintage?","opts":["1990","1998","2012","1968"],"a":0,"exp":"The first harvest was 1988 and the first bottled vintage 1990; 1968 is the year of the Etna DOC and 1998 the year the house added Monte Serra at Viagrande.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Benanti: Profile and fact sheet 2020","url":"https://www.benanti.it/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/BENANTI-Profile-and-Fact-Sheet-2020-1-1.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Benanti: Pietra Marina Etna Bianco Superiore technical sheet","url":"https://benanti.it/wines/BENANTI-Scheda-tecnica-Etna-Bianco-Superiore-Pietra-Marina.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"AIS: Etna DOC production rules","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Sicilia/DOC/Etna.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Giuseppe Benanti, who helped spark Mount Etna's wine renaissance, dies at 78","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/giuseppe-benanti-who-helped-spark-mount-etna-s-wine-renaissance-dies-at-78","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wilson Daniels: Benanti Pietra Marina Etna Bianco DOC Superiore","url":"https://wilsondaniels.com/wine/benanti/pietra-marina-etna-bianco-doc-superiore/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-e4z7nhxo": {"id":"fl-e4z7nhxo","key":"de-bartoli","group":"sicily","region":"sicily","producer":"Marco De Bartoli","producerId":"p-marco-de-bartoli","say":"MAR-koh deh BAR-toh-lee, VEK-kyoh sahm-PEH-ree","place":"Contrada Samperi near Marsala, western Sicily","founded":"1978","wine":{"name":"Vecchio Samperi","appellation":"None: sold as wine outside the Marsala DOC","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Grillo alone, from the family's vineyards at Samperi.","sites":"The family's vineyards around the baglio in Contrada Samperi, about twelve kilometres from Marsala, on soils rich in limestone, with Grillo planted between 1970 and 1996.","making":"Grillo picked very ripe in the last week of September is pressed softly and fermented with wild yeasts in oak and chestnut barrels. The wine then joins a perpetuo begun at Samperi in 1978: the casks are never emptied, a little is drawn off each year and they are topped up with young wine. No spirit is added, and the blend averages fifteen years and more.","glass":"Walnut, toasted almond and dried apricot over bitter orange peel, iodine and sea salt; bone dry and warm, with a long, tangy finish that tastes of the casks.","ageing":"Ready on release, shaped already by its years in the perpetuo, and it keeps for many years in bottle.","serve":"12 to 14 degrees Celsius, cool rather than cold, as for a dry Oloroso; no decanter; a small glass for white wine.","label":[{"term":"Vecchio Samperi","means":"Old Samperi: named for the contrada near Marsala where the family's baglio stands."},{"term":"Marco De Bartoli","means":"The house, named for its founder, who began the perpetuo at Samperi in 1978."}]},"table":"Because it is never fortified, Marco De Bartoli's Vecchio Samperi may not be called Marsala: it is Grillo aged for years in a perpetuo of oak and chestnut casks at Samperi. Walnut, dried apricot and sea salt, bone dry: an aperitif served cool, or a partner for aged cheese.","story":"Marsala's trade with England began in 1773, and the wine shipped from the port was strengthened with spirit for the voyage. By most accounts the growers of the countryside had kept their own wine unfortified, drawing from casks they never emptied. At Samperi, the family's baglio about twelve kilometres from the town, where, the family says, vines and cellars go back over two centuries, Marco De Bartoli (1945 to 2011), a former racing driver, set out to make that older wine again. In 1978 he began a perpetuo of old Grillo, topping its casks each year with wine of very ripe grapes, and in 1980 he named its first bottling Vecchio Samperi. Since no spirit was added, the Marsala rules kept it from the name, and it was sold simply as wine. In 1984 he first bottled a passito on Pantelleria, at Bukkuram. In 1995 the Marsala prosecutors charged him with adulterating his wine; he was cleared five years later, the court finding that no offence had taken place. After his death in 2011 the estate passed to his children. Taste in Vecchio Samperi what Marsala was before the English: dry, oxidative and strong by nature alone.","others":[{"name":"Marsala Superiore Riserva","note":"The house's fortified Marsala of Grillo, its case that the denomination can be serious."},{"name":"Bukkuram","note":"Passito di Pantelleria of sun dried Zibibbo, first bottled in 1984."},{"name":"Vecchio Samperi Ventennale","note":"A bottling of the same perpetuo with an average age of about twenty years."}],"traps":["Vecchio Samperi is unfortified, so it cannot legally be called Marsala, whatever a list may print beside it.","Marsala tiers run Fine, Superiore, Superiore Riserva, Vergine and Vergine Stravecchio; Vecchio Samperi stands outside them all.","Bukkuram is De Bartoli's Passito di Pantelleria; do not confuse it with Donnafugata's Ben Ryé from the same island."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-01gej2w8","front":"Marco De Bartoli's Vecchio Samperi: why may it not be called Marsala?","back":"No spirit is added, and the Marsala rules ask for a fortified wine."},{"id":"fl-wm7zzfsh","front":"What did the founder of Marco De Bartoli begin at Samperi in 1978?","back":"A perpetuo of old Grillo, its casks never emptied and topped up each year; its first bottling was named Vecchio Samperi in 1980."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-j0nyxacc","q":"A guest sees Vecchio Samperi listed among the Marsalas. How do you explain where it belongs?","opts":["A Marsala Vergine aged five years in wood","A Marsala Superiore sweetened with cooked must","A fortified Zibibbo from Pantelleria","An unfortified Grillo outside the Marsala DOC"],"a":3,"exp":"Vecchio Samperi is Grillo aged in a perpetuo with no spirit added, so the Marsala rules exclude it; a Marsala Vergine, its nearest cousin, is a fortified wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-byy4lys8","q":"In 1995 the Marsala prosecutors brought a charge against Marco De Bartoli. How did it end?","opts":["He agreed to fortify his wine","He moved his cellar to Pantelleria","He was cleared five years later","He gave up the Samperi estate"],"a":2,"exp":"Charged with adulteration in 1995, he was cleared five years later, the court finding no offence; the family kept Samperi and its perpetuo.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Marco De Bartoli: Vecchio Samperi, the traditional wine of Marsala","url":"https://www.marcodebartoli.com/en/wines/vecchio-samperi/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marco De Bartoli: Vecchio Samperi Quarantennale","url":"https://marcodebartoli.it/en/vino/vecchio-samperi-quarantennale/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marco De Bartoli: Samperi estate in Marsala","url":"https://marcodebartoli.it/en/samperi-estate-marsala/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Balarm: the visionary grower Marco De Bartoli","url":"https://www.balarm.it/news/il-vignaiolo-visionario-che-ha-fatto-grande-la-sicilia-nel-mondo-marco-de-bartoli-127368","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Barberry: Marco De Bartoli producer page","url":"https://www.barberry.io/producers/8d6cdbba-67bf-4a6c-a39e-48c4b5be3a45","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-lpw7523a": {"id":"fl-lpw7523a","key":"cos","group":"sicily","region":"sicily","producer":"COS","producerId":"p-cos","say":"KOHS, cheh-rah-SWOH-loh dee veet-TOH-ree-ah","place":"Vittoria, in the province of Ragusa, Cerasuolo di Vittoria Classico DOCG, Sicily","founded":"1980","wine":{"name":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria Classico","appellation":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria Classico DOCG","classification":"Classico, the historic heart of Sicily's one DOCG, granted in 2005","colour":"red","grapes":"Nero d'Avola, about three fifths, with Frappato for the rest; the DOCG allows half to seven tenths Nero d'Avola.","sites":"The house's vineyards around its cellar outside Vittoria, about 230 metres up, on red sand and clay over limestone that lies thirty to sixty centimetres below the surface.","making":"Native yeasts ferment the grapes on their skins, in concrete tanks by most merchants' sheets, and the house makes it without chemical additions. The wine then ages in large casks of Slavonian oak, a year to eighteen months by most sheets, some vintages with a further spell in concrete; the DOCG holds a Classico back until 31 March of the second year after the harvest.","glass":"Sour cherry, red plum and pomegranate over rose petal, white pepper and dried orange peel; medium bodied, with fine, light tannin, fresh acidity and a dusty, savoury close.","ageing":"Fragrant and ready within three or four years of the harvest, it gains dried cherry and leather over a decade when the year is good.","serve":"14 to 16 degrees Celsius, a little cool; it needs no decanting, though a young bottle opens with air; a Burgundy bowl.","label":[{"term":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria","means":"Nero d'Avola with Frappato from around Vittoria, a DOC since 1973 and, since 2005, Sicily's one DOCG."},{"term":"Classico","means":"The historic inner zone, held back until 31 March of the second year after the harvest."},{"term":"DOCG","means":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita, the rank above DOC in Italian wine law."}]},"table":"With Frappato beside its Nero d'Avola, COS's Cerasuolo di Vittoria Classico grows on red sand over limestone outside Vittoria, is fermented with native yeasts and aged in large oak casks. Sour cherry, pomegranate and white pepper on light tannin: chill it slightly and pour it even with tuna.","story":"Three young friends of Vittoria first made wine together in 1980, in the old cellar of one of their families and from vines his father gave them. The house's name joins the initials of their three surnames. Their first vintage, picked on 5 October 1980, filled fewer than fifteen hundred bottles. In 1985 one of the three left for medicine and his sister took his share. Cerasuolo di Vittoria, the red of Nero d'Avola and Frappato from the red sands of the south east, had been a DOC since 1973, and the friends aimed to make it as cleanly as they could, with native yeasts and no chemical additions. In September 2000 their first clay amphorae arrived from Spain, and Pithos, a wine fermented and aged in jars set into the ground, was born. In 2005 Cerasuolo di Vittoria became Sicily's first DOCG, and the house says its wine was the first to carry the new name. Sicily's one DOCG, as COS makes it, is paler and more perfumed than the island's reputation, and made with a light hand.","others":[{"name":"Pithos Rosso","note":"Nero d'Avola with Frappato fermented and aged in clay amphorae set into the ground, first made in 2000."},{"name":"Frappato","note":"Frappato alone, pale and perfumed."}],"traps":["Cerasuolo di Vittoria is a red DOCG of Sicily; Cerasuolo d'Abruzzo is a rosato of Montepulciano.","COS is the initials of its three founders, not a place, and has nothing to do with Château Cos d'Estournel of Saint-Estèphe.","Classico is the historic inner zone of the DOCG and waits longer before release than plain Cerasuolo di Vittoria, which may leave on 1 June of the year after the harvest."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-mfi4a9w9","front":"COS: where does the name of the house come from?","back":"The initials of the surnames of the three friends who founded it in 1980."},{"id":"fl-56ggxe27","front":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria: what share of Nero d'Avola do the DOCG rules allow, and what is the rest?","back":"Half to seven tenths Nero d'Avola, the rest Frappato; COS's Classico runs about three fifths Nero d'Avola."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-1694i6py","q":"Why does COS's Cerasuolo di Vittoria Classico drink lighter than most Sicilian reds?","opts":["Frappato blended with Nero d'Avola","Nerello Mascalese from high on Etna","Grapes dried in the sun before pressing","A long rest under flor yeast"],"a":0,"exp":"The DOCG blends Nero d'Avola with thirty to fifty per cent Frappato, a pale, perfumed grape; Nerello Mascalese is Etna's grape and has no place in Cerasuolo.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-c4bq7s0h","q":"Cerasuolo di Vittoria, the wine COS's founders set out to make cleanly in 1980, became a DOCG in which year?","opts":["2000","2005","1980","1973"],"a":1,"exp":"It was a DOC from 1973 and became a DOCG by decree in 2005; 2000 is the year the house's first amphorae arrived from Spain.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"COS: The company","url":"https://www.cosvittoria.it/en/the-company/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"COS: Cerasuolo di Vittoria and Pithos","url":"https://www.cosvittoria.it/en/cerasuolo-vittoria-and-pithos/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"AIS: Cerasuolo di Vittoria DOCG production rules","url":"https://aisitalia.it/Disciplinari/Sicilia/DOCG/Cerasuolo%20di%20Vittoria.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinography: The wines of COS and the soul of Vittoria, Sicily","url":"https://www.vinography.com/2023/12/the-wines-of-cos-and-the-soul-of-vittoria-sicily","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Primal Wine: COS, a Sicilian winery","url":"https://primalwine.com/blogs/natural-wine-producers-primal-wine/cos-a-sicilian-winery-blog-natural-wine","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-x0mildo4": {"id":"fl-x0mildo4","key":"lopez-de-heredia","group":"rioja","region":"rioja","producer":"R. López de Heredia Viña Tondonia","producerId":"p-lopez-de-heredia","say":"LOH-peth deh eh-REH-dyah, VEE-nyah ton-DOH-nyah","place":"Haro, Rioja Alta, Rioja","founded":"1877","wine":{"name":"Viña Tondonia Reserva","appellation":"Rioja","classification":"Reserva, an ageing category of the DOCa Rioja","colour":"red","grapes":"Tempranillo, about three quarters, with Garnacho and a little Graciano and Mazuelo, all from the house's own vineyards.","sites":"Viña Tondonia, a group of vineyards joined by the founder in a great meander of the Ebro on its right bank beside Haro, on alluvial clay with a high share of limestone, planted from 1913.","making":"Fermented with the cellar's own yeasts in large old oak vats, then aged six years in American oak barrels built and mended by the house's own coopers, racked twice a year and fined with fresh egg whites. It is bottled unfiltered and rests in the cellar until the house judges it ready.","glass":"Pale ruby turning to brick at the rim; dried cherry, red plum, orange peel and dill over old cedar, toast and a hint of vanilla. Very dry and savoury, light to medium in body, with fresh acidity and tannin that is fine yet firm.","ageing":"Ready to drink when it reaches the market, years after the vintage, it goes on gaining savoury depth for many years in a cool, steady cellar.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; stand an older bottle upright the day before and decant it gently off its sediment; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Viña Tondonia","means":"The house's vineyard in the Ebro meander by Haro, not the name of the company."},{"term":"Reserva","means":"A red aged at least three years in all, at least one of them in oak barrels."},{"term":"Rioja Denominación de Origen Calificada","means":"Rioja's appellation, of the higher Calificada rank since 1991."}]},"table":"In a bend of the Ebro by Haro lies the vineyard of López de Heredia's Viña Tondonia Reserva: mostly Tempranillo, six years in old American oak, fined with egg whites. Pale, dry and savoury, with dried cherry and orange peel, it wants a gentle decanting and a place at table.","story":"R. López de Heredia carries the name of its founder, who in 1877 began building his bodega beside the railway station at Haro; the house holds it to be the oldest bodega in Haro, and the station quarter later filled with others, CVNE in 1879 and La Rioja Alta in 1890 among them. In 1913 and 1914 he bought and joined a group of vineyards in a great meander of the Ebro and planted them, naming the whole Viña Tondonia after Tondon, the old name of that bend in the river. The vineyard gave the house its flagship and, in time, the company its full name. The family has held to the same ways for well over a century: fermentation in large oak vats, years in American oak barrels built and mended by its own coopers, racking twice a year and fining with egg whites. Between 2001 and 2006 the architect Zaha Hadid (1950 to 2016) designed a pavilion for the bodega to house an older stand, built for a world fair of 1910 and found again in its outbuildings. Tondonia Reserva makes the case for traditional Rioja: a red released ready to drink, and proof that long age in barrel is a style rather than a fault.","others":[{"name":"Viña Tondonia Blanco Reserva","note":"Viura with Malvasía, aged for years in old oak barrels and deliberately oxidative: the white that tests style against fault."},{"name":"Viña Bosconia Reserva","note":"From the Bosconia vineyard on clay and limestone beside the Ebro; the rounder red, often taught against Tondonia."},{"name":"Viña Tondonia Gran Reserva","note":"The red held at least nine years in barrel, by the house's account, before bottling."}],"traps":["Viña Tondonia is the vineyard, not the company: the house is R. López de Heredia, and Bosconia is a different vineyard, not a higher or lower tier.","The pale, brick rim and savoury notes of an old Tondonia are the house style of long barrel ageing, not a fault of oxidation.","Its Reserva spends six years in barrel, where the law asks a Rioja Reserva for one."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-5rsz5iir","front":"López de Heredia: where is Viña Tondonia, and what gave it its name?","back":"A vineyard in a meander of the Ebro beside Haro, planted from 1913; Tondon was the old name of that bend in the river."},{"id":"fl-c3cl76ye","front":"How long does Viña Tondonia Reserva spend in barrel, and how is it fined?","back":"Six years in American oak, racked twice a year, then fined with fresh egg whites and bottled unfiltered."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6nerljd3","q":"A guest is surprised that a Rioja Reserva can taste so pale and savoury. What in López de Heredia's cellar explains Viña Tondonia Reserva's style?","opts":["A year in steel tanks, then in bottle","Two years in new French oak barrels","Six years in old American oak barrels","A long rest on its lees in concrete"],"a":2,"exp":"The Reserva spends six years in barrel, racked twice a year, which gives its pale colour and savoury, dried fruit notes; new French oak would give a darker, more toasted wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-lua3dalt","q":"In 1913 and 1914 López de Heredia's founder bought and planted the vineyard that gave Viña Tondonia its name. Where does it lie?","opts":["In Rioja Oriental near Alfaro","On the slopes of the Sierra de Cantabria","On the high ground above Logroño","In a meander of the Ebro beside Haro"],"a":3,"exp":"Viña Tondonia lies in a great bend of the Ebro just outside Haro, named from Tondon, the old word for that meander; the Sierra de Cantabria rises further north, above Rioja Alavesa.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"R. López de Heredia: Viña Tondonia, the vineyard","url":"https://www.lopezdeheredia.com/english/vinedos/tondonia.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"R. López de Heredia: Viña Tondonia Reserva Tinto","url":"https://www.lopezdeheredia.com/spanish/vinos/tondoniaRT.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Foods and Wines from Spain: Haro's station district","url":"https://www.foodswinesfromspain.com/en/wine/articles/2019/july/haro-rioja-station-district","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Zaha Hadid Architects: López de Heredia wine pavilion","url":"https://www.zaha-hadid.com/?p=11862","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VinePair: López de Heredia and its ways in the cellar","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/lopez-de-heredia-spain-winery-sustainability","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-l5solz67": {"id":"fl-l5solz67","key":"la-rioja-alta","group":"rioja","region":"rioja","producer":"La Rioja Alta S.A.","producerId":"p-la-rioja-alta","say":"lah RYOH-hah AHL-tah","place":"Haro, Rioja Alta, Rioja","founded":"1890","wine":{"name":"Gran Reserva 890","appellation":"Rioja","classification":"Gran Reserva, an ageing category of the DOCa Rioja","colour":"red","grapes":"Tempranillo, the great majority, with small shares of Graciano and Mazuelo.","sites":"Vineyards of the house: in its sheet for the 2011 vintage, Tempranillo from old vines at Villalba, Briñas and Labastida near Haro, with Graciano from its Montecillo estate at Fuenmayor, west of Logroño.","making":"The fermentation is in tanks, with the Graciano kept apart; the wine then spends about six years in American oak barrels made by the house, racked by hand from barrel to barrel by candlelight, ten times and more over those years, and rests in bottle in the Haro cellars before release.","glass":"Garnet with a brick rim; dried strawberry, candied plum and raisin over cedar, tea, cinnamon, coconut and vanilla. Elegant, with fine tannin and fresh acidity, finishing long on sweet spice and balsamic herbs.","ageing":"The house releases it drinking well, long after the vintage, and by its account it keeps gaining finesse and complexity for many years more.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; an older bottle wants a slow pour into a decanter, its sediment left behind; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Gran Reserva","means":"In Rioja, a red aged at least five years, two of them in oak barrel and two in bottle."},{"term":"890","means":"Recalls 1890, the founding year of the house; not a vintage, an age or a cask number."},{"term":"La Rioja Alta S.A.","means":"The company of Haro; Rioja Alta is also the name of the subregion."}]},"table":"The number on La Rioja Alta's Gran Reserva 890 recalls the Haro house's founding in 1890, and the wine is made only in exceptional years: mostly Tempranillo, about six years in American oak, racked by candlelight. Pale garnet, with dried strawberry, cedar and cinnamon, it suits roast lamb.","story":"La Rioja Alta was founded in 1890 in Haro's station quarter as the Sociedad Vinícola de La Rioja Alta, by five families of Rioja and the Basque Country; by the house's own account a woman was its first president. Its first winemaker, a Frenchman, made the Reserva 1890, from which the Gran Reserva 890 descends. In 1904 the company merged with the Ardanza bodega, owned by one of its founders, and marked the union with a Reserva 1904, which became the Gran Reserva 904. Through the twentieth century the house stayed with long ageing in American oak and with racking its barrels by hand by candlelight, and its own coopers, it says, build its barrels at Haro. The 890 appears only in years the house judges exceptional and spends about six years in barrel, then more in bottle, so it reaches the market long after the vintage, far beyond the five years the law asks of a Gran Reserva. Its number trips candidates, who read it as a vintage or an age. Pale and fragrant, the 890 is the classic picture of Haro's long aged Gran Reserva, and the number on its label always wants explaining at the table.","others":[{"name":"Gran Reserva 904","note":"The other numbered Gran Reserva, recalling the merger of 1904; Tempranillo with Graciano, and made more often."},{"name":"Viña Ardanza Reserva","note":"The house Reserva, Tempranillo with Garnacha, a tier below the numbered wines."}],"traps":["890 and 904 are not vintages, ages or cask numbers: they recall 1890, the founding, and 1904, the merger.","La Rioja Alta S.A. is a house of Haro; Rioja Alta is also the subregion, so read the company's name on the label with care.","Both 890 and 904 are Gran Reservas in law; the 890's longer time in barrel is the house's custom, not a higher legal rank."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-f5l0y3g7","front":"La Rioja Alta: what does the number on its Gran Reserva 890 recall?","back":"1890, the year the house was founded in Haro's station quarter; it is not a vintage or an age."},{"id":"fl-6sea1d6g","front":"How is La Rioja Alta's Gran Reserva 890 aged in the cellar?","back":"About six years in American oak barrels made by the house, racked by hand by candlelight, then further time in bottle."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-s4mp40kv","q":"A guest reads 890 on a bottle from La Rioja Alta and takes it for the vintage. What do you tell them?","opts":["It recalls 1890, the year the house was founded","It is the vintage, 1890, held back in the cellar","It is the number of the cask it came from","It counts the months the wine spent in oak"],"a":0,"exp":"890 recalls the founding of the house in 1890, as 904 recalls the merger of 1904; neither number is a vintage, an age or a cask.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-omi4s4qh","q":"Where in Haro did five families found La Rioja Alta in 1890?","opts":["In the Tondonia meander of the Ebro","In the quarter by the railway station","On the banks of the river Tirón","Inside the old walls by the church"],"a":1,"exp":"La Rioja Alta stands in the Barrio de la Estación, the station quarter where López de Heredia had begun building in 1877 and CVNE followed in 1879; Tondonia is López de Heredia's vineyard by the Ebro.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"La Rioja Alta: The group's history","url":"https://www.riojalta.com/en/la-rioja-alta-group/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"La Rioja Alta: Gran Reserva 890","url":"https://www.riojalta.com/en/wines_rioja-alta/gran-reserva-890/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"La Rioja Alta: How the wines are made","url":"https://riojalta.com/en/la-rioja-alta-group/making","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador DOCa Rioja: Classification","url":"https://riojawine.com/en-gb/the-designation/classification/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: La Rioja Alta, the art of ageing","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/la-rioja-alta-the-art-of-ageing-435442/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-09eu4v86": {"id":"fl-09eu4v86","key":"murrieta","group":"rioja","region":"rioja","producer":"Marqués de Murrieta","producerId":"p-marques-de-murrieta","say":"mar-KESS deh moo-RYEH-tah, kahs-TEE-yoh ee-GUY","place":"Finca Ygay, Logroño, Rioja Alta, Rioja","founded":"1852","wine":{"name":"Castillo Ygay Gran Reserva Especial","appellation":"Rioja","classification":"Gran Reserva under the DOCa Rioja's ageing rules; Especial is the house's word","colour":"red","grapes":"Tempranillo, the great majority, with Mazuelo for the rest; the share of Mazuelo changes with the year.","sites":"La Plana, a single vineyard at the highest point of the Finca Ygay estate, at about 485 metres, south east of Logroño in Rioja Alta.","making":"Tempranillo and Mazuelo are aged apart for two to three years in 225 litre barrels, American oak for the Tempranillo and, in some years, French oak for the Mazuelo. Once blended the wine rests in concrete vats, then for years in bottle before the house releases it.","glass":"Ripe plum, black cherry and dried fig over tea leaf, cedar, wild Mediterranean herbs and dried flowers, with an iron, savoury edge; polished tannin, fresh acidity and a harmonious finish that lasts.","ageing":"Built to last for decades, and young even at thirty in a strong year, though it can be opened as soon as the house lets it go.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; with age it throws a sediment, so decant an older bottle with care; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Castillo Ygay","means":"The castle of the Ygay estate south east of Logroño: a bottling of the estate, not a separate property."},{"term":"Gran Reserva Especial","means":"A Gran Reserva in law, aged five years at least; Especial is the house's own addition."},{"term":"Marqués de Murrieta","means":"The title granted to the founder in 1872, and the name of the house."}]},"table":"Named for the castle of Marqués de Murrieta's Ygay estate by Logroño, Castillo Ygay is the house's Gran Reserva Especial, from La Plana and only in years it judges exceptional. Tempranillo with a little Mazuelo, savoury, with black cherry, fig and cedar, it rests long in barrel and bottle.","story":"Luciano de Murrieta (1822 to 1911) was born in Peru and learnt the ways of Bordeaux before he turned to Rioja. In 1852, as the house tells it, he made his first Rioja with Bordeaux methods, in the Logroño cellars of the Duke of la Victoria, aiming for a wine that would endure over time. In 1872 the crown made him Marqués de Murrieta, and he set his house on the Ygay estate south east of the city, where the castle that names the flagship stands. Castillo Ygay became the estate's Gran Reserva Especial, reserved for vintages the house judged exceptional and held for years in barrel, then in bottle, before release. In 1983 the estate was bought by the Cebrián-Sagarriga family, who restored the castle, declared a museum in 2019, and later built a new winery beside it. The red is drawn from La Plana alone, and its white sister makes the house's point even more plainly: the Castillo Ygay Blanco of 1986 spent twenty one years in American oak. Castillo Ygay reads Rioja's five years for a Gran Reserva as a floor, not a target.","others":[{"name":"Castillo Ygay Blanco Gran Reserva Especial","note":"Viura with Malvasía, held for decades in barrel and made in very rare years; the 1986 spent twenty one years in American oak."},{"name":"Dalmau","note":"The modern red of the house, Tempranillo with Cabernet Sauvignon and Graciano, aged in French oak."},{"name":"Capellanía","note":"A barrel aged white of Viura from a named parcel inside the Ygay estate."}],"traps":["Castillo Ygay is a bottling of the Marqués de Murrieta estate, not a separate property.","Gran Reserva Especial is no legal rank: in law the wine is a Gran Reserva, and Especial is the house's own word.","Murrieta's Ygay estate lies south east of Logroño in Rioja Alta, not in Rioja Alavesa."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-h9e5ffj5","front":"Marqués de Murrieta: which vineyard gives Castillo Ygay?","back":"La Plana, the highest part of the Ygay estate, at about 485 metres, south east of Logroño."},{"id":"fl-bvv5igye","front":"Which two grapes make Castillo Ygay, and how are they aged?","back":"Tempranillo, the great majority, and Mazuelo, aged apart in barrel for two to three years, then blended, rested in concrete and held in bottle."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-o6fn2fhv","q":"Castillo Ygay's label reads Gran Reserva Especial. How should a guest read the word Especial?","opts":["Especial is a legal tier above Gran Reserva in Rioja","Especial means it was aged in French oak alone","In law it is a Gran Reserva; Especial is the house's word","Especial marks a wine grown in Rioja Alavesa"],"a":2,"exp":"Rioja's ageing terms stop at Gran Reserva, at least five years with two in barrel and two in bottle; Especial is Murrieta's own word, not a higher legal tier.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-osy67jq4","q":"In 1852 Luciano de Murrieta made his first Rioja with methods learnt abroad. Where had he learnt them?","opts":["In Porto","In Champagne","In Jerez","In Bordeaux"],"a":3,"exp":"Murrieta brought the methods of Bordeaux to Rioja in 1852, seeking a red that would last; Jerez, Porto and Champagne make fortified and sparkling wines.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Marqués de Murrieta: About us","url":"https://marquesdemurrieta.com/en/pages/sobre-nosotros-marques-de-murrieta","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marqués de Murrieta: Castillo Ygay Gran Reserva Especial","url":"https://marquesdemurrieta.com/en/pages/castillo-ygay-tinto-gran-reserva-especial","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Marqués de Murrieta: Castillo Ygay Blanco Gran Reserva Especial 1986","url":"https://marquesdemurrieta.com/en/pages/castillo-ygay-blanco-gran-reserva-especial-1986","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bodegas Marqués de Murrieta","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodegas_Marqu%C3%A9s_de_Murrieta","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Marquess of Murrieta","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marquess_of_Murrieta","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-vga8utj5": {"id":"fl-vga8utj5","key":"cvne","group":"rioja","region":"rioja","producer":"CVNE","producerId":"p-cvne","say":"KOO-neh, eem-peh-RYAHL","place":"Haro, Rioja Alta, Rioja","founded":"1879","wine":{"name":"Imperial Gran Reserva","appellation":"Rioja","classification":"Gran Reserva, an ageing category of the DOCa Rioja","colour":"red","grapes":"Tempranillo, the great majority, with Graciano and a little Mazuelo.","sites":"Small vineyards of Rioja Alta around Villalba and Torremontalbo, at about 550 to 650 metres.","making":"After the malolactic fermentation in concrete vats, the wine spends about two years in French and American oak barrels, racked every eight months, then at least three years in bottle in the house's century old cellars at Haro. Only exceptional years give it.","glass":"Deep cherry red; black cherry, bramble and liquorice over clove, roasted coffee, cigar leaf, chocolate and toffee, with a balsamic lift, velvety tannin and a long, fragrant finish.","ageing":"Ready on release after its years in barrel and bottle, it holds and deepens for many years in a cool cellar, turning towards leather, tobacco and dried fig.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, the house advising the upper end; an old bottle is decanted gently, its sediment left in the bottle; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"CVNE","means":"Compañía Vinícola del Norte de España, the wine company of northern Spain; said Cune."},{"term":"Imperial","means":"The brand, named from a special bottling for the English market in the imperial pint."},{"term":"Gran Reserva","means":"Rioja's legal ageing term above Reserva: five years at least, two in barrel and two in bottle."}]},"table":"Imperial Gran Reserva is CVNE's Rioja Alta red, from small vineyards near Haro and made in exceptional years alone: mostly Tempranillo with Graciano, two years in oak and three more in bottle. Expect dark cherry, clove and cigar leaf, and call the house Cune, as Spain does.","story":"CVNE began in Haro in 1879, when two brothers of the Real de Asúa family set up a wine company near the town's railway station; the house traces the move to one brother's asthma, for which a doctor had prescribed a drier climate than that of Bilbao. Until 1882 the firm traded as Corcuera, Real de Asúa y Compañía, before it took the name Compañía Vinícola del Norte de España; its initials, read with the V as a U, gave the spoken name Cune. In 1909 the house raised a barrel hall whose steel roof spans it with no pillar inside, a design that a plaque in the hall credits to the engineer of the Eiffel Tower. Imperial followed in the 1920s, its name taken, in the house's telling, from a special bottling for the English market in the measure called the imperial pint. Viña Real, from the Rioja Alavesa side, gave the house a second voice, and descendants of the founding family kept control through the century. Imperial Gran Reserva is not made every year, only in exceptional ones, and it is aged in the old Haro cellars. Set beside Viña Real from Alavesa, it shows the Rioja Alta side of one house, and its label settles why CVNE is said Cune.","others":[{"name":"Viña Real Gran Reserva","note":"The Rioja Alavesa counterpart, from the house's separate cellar above Laguardia: another subzone, not another tier."},{"name":"Contino","note":"A single estate at Laserna in Rioja Alavesa, part of the house and bottled under its own name."},{"name":"Monopole","note":"A white Rioja of Viura and one of the long standing white wine brands of Spain."}],"traps":["Imperial is Rioja Alta and Viña Real is Rioja Alavesa: two subzones of one house, not two quality tiers.","Cune is how CVNE is spoken, from reading the V of its initials as a U; it is the same house.","Monopole is a brand of white Rioja, not a Burgundian monopole."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-mwc1sbpr","front":"CVNE: where does the name of its Imperial come from?","back":"A special bottling for the English market in the measure called the imperial pint, in the 1920s."},{"id":"fl-n6o5qdy7","front":"Which subzone gives Imperial Gran Reserva, and which gives its sister Viña Real?","back":"Imperial is Rioja Alta, from vineyards near Haro; Viña Real is Rioja Alavesa, from a cellar above Laguardia."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-xvbb8xp7","q":"A guest compares CVNE's Imperial Gran Reserva with the Viña Real Gran Reserva of the same house. What separates the two?","opts":["The subzone: Rioja Alta against Rioja Alavesa","The vessel: chestnut casks against steel","The grape: Garnacha against Tempranillo","The legal tier: Gran Reserva against Crianza"],"a":0,"exp":"Imperial comes from Rioja Alta vineyards near Haro and Viña Real from Rioja Alavesa, each with its own cellar; both are led by Tempranillo and both are made as Gran Reservas.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-wv820kum","q":"By CVNE's own account, its founders came to Haro in 1879 partly for one brother's health. From which city's damper climate had they come?","opts":["Logroño","Bilbao","Bordeaux","Madrid"],"a":1,"exp":"A doctor prescribed a drier climate than Bilbao's for one of the Real de Asúa brothers, as the house tells it; Logroño is Rioja's own capital, not the city they left.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"CVNE: From 1879 to today, the history","url":"https://cvne.com/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"CVNE: Imperial Gran Reserva","url":"https://cvne.com/en/wines/imperial-gran-reserva/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"CVNE: Imperial Gran Reserva 2016 data sheet","url":"https://cvne.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/IMPERIAL-GRAN-RVA-16-ING.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Spanish Icons, CVNE Imperial Gran Reserva","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/spanish-icons-cvne-imperial-gran-reserva-550194/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Gourmet Groceries: CVNE, Rioja and Ribera del Duero","url":"https://gourmetgroceries.com/c-v-n-e-rioja-ribera-del-duero-wine/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-wklvi5iv": {"id":"fl-wklvi5iv","key":"muga","group":"rioja","region":"rioja","producer":"Bodegas Muga","producerId":"p-muga","say":"MOO-gah, PRAH-doh EH-neh-ah","place":"Haro, Rioja Alta, Rioja","founded":"1932","wine":{"name":"Prado Enea Gran Reserva","appellation":"Rioja","classification":"Gran Reserva, an ageing category of the DOCa Rioja","colour":"red","grapes":"Tempranillo, the great majority, with Garnacha, Mazuelo and Graciano for the rest.","sites":"High vineyards of the house in the north west of Rioja Alta, around Sajazarra, Cellorigo and Fonzaleche, at 550 to 700 metres on calcareous clay and alluvial soils; always the last grapes Muga picks.","making":"Wood from vat to barrel: the wine ferments in the house's oak vats, then spends at least three years in barrels of American and French oak, racked by gravity and fined with egg white, and at least three more in bottle, leaving Haro six years and more after the vintage.","glass":"Bright black cherry in colour; ripe cherry and black plum over cedar, cocoa and warm sweet spice. A silky, juicy attack, medium body, lively acidity and very fine tannin; balsamic herbs linger at the end.","ageing":"Approachable when released, after six years and more, it keeps for many years beyond, its fruit drying as tobacco and leather come through.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant an older bottle with a steady hand and stop at the sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Bodegas Muga","means":"The family house of Haro, founded in 1932 and in the station quarter since 1970."},{"term":"Gran Reserva","means":"A Rioja red held at least five years before sale, two of them in barrel and two in bottle."},{"term":"Rioja Denominación de Origen Calificada","means":"The appellation, Spain's first DO in 1925 and its first Calificada in 1991."}]},"table":"Muga picks Prado Enea's grapes last, from its coolest vineyards, and makes this Gran Reserva only when the year is exceptional: Tempranillo led, fermented in oak vats and kept six years and more in barrel and bottle before release. Beside roast lamb it pours silky, with cherry, cedar and cocoa.","story":"A married couple founded Bodegas Muga in Haro in 1932, and the husband gave the house his surname. The house chose wood at every step: fermentation in oak vats, ageing in barrels its own coopers build and mend, racking by gravity and fining with egg white. At the end of the 1960s the family made the first Prado Enea, a Gran Reserva drawn from its coolest vineyards in the years it judged exceptional and in no others. In 1970 the family moved into a building of the Barrio de la Estación, Haro's station quarter, beside the houses founded there since 1877. The family later added Torre Muga, a modern red aged in French oak, so that the two styles of Rioja sit under one roof. Prado Enea's grapes are always the last the house picks, and the wine spends at least three years in barrel and three in bottle before it leaves Haro, a year past the law's five. Prado Enea is the traditional half of Muga's two styles: a Gran Reserva made in wood from vat to barrel, ready to drink when it leaves the cellar.","others":[{"name":"Torre Muga","note":"Muga's modern red, Tempranillo with Mazuelo and Graciano in French oak; made to stand against Prado Enea."},{"name":"Muga Blanco","note":"A barrel fermented white of Viura with Malvasía, in a fresher modern manner."}],"traps":["Prado Enea is the traditional Gran Reserva and Torre Muga the modern French oak red: one house, two styles.","Haro, Muga's home, lies in Rioja Alta; do not place the house in Rioja Alavesa.","Prado Enea's six years in barrel and bottle pass the Gran Reserva rule of five; the extra year is the house's choice, not the law's."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-at2515fq","front":"Bodegas Muga: which vineyards give Prado Enea, and when are they picked?","back":"High vineyards in the north west of Rioja Alta, at 550 to 700 metres; always the last grapes of the harvest."},{"id":"fl-s7k39e20","front":"Prado Enea: what time in barrel and in bottle comes before its release?","back":"At least three years in American and French oak barrels and three more in bottle: six years and more in all."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-r8u3er62","q":"Which description matches the making of Muga's Prado Enea Gran Reserva?","opts":["Fermented in amphora, then aged in concrete","Fermented in concrete, then aged in chestnut casks","Fermented in large oak vats, then years in barrel","Fermented in steel, then aged only in bottle"],"a":2,"exp":"Muga ferments Prado Enea in its oak vats and ages it at least three years in barrel; the steel, amphora and concrete answers describe other cellars.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2ge2t4u1","q":"Bodegas Muga was founded in Haro in 1932. In which year did it move into the Barrio de la Estación?","opts":["1879","1913","1952","1970"],"a":3,"exp":"The house was founded in 1932 and moved to the station quarter in 1970; 1879 is the founding of CVNE nearby, and 1913 the planting of López de Heredia's Tondonia.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bodegas Muga: The Muga family","url":"https://www.bodegasmuga.com/en/muga-family/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bodegas Muga: Prado Enea Gran Reserva","url":"https://www.bodegasmuga.com/en/prado-enea-gran-reserva/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Spanish Wine Lover: Bodega Muga","url":"https://spanishwinelover.com/winerie/muga","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Spanish Icons, Muga Prado Enea Gran Reserva","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/spanish-icons-muga-prado-enea-gran-reserva-550671","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Cellar Tours: Muga winery guide","url":"https://www.cellartours.com/spain/spanish-wineries/muga","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-unk52goz": {"id":"fl-unk52goz","key":"vega-sicilia","group":"ribera-del-duero","region":"ribera-del-duero","producer":"Vega Sicilia","producerId":"p-vega-sicilia","say":"BEH-gah see-THEE-lyah, OO-nee-koh","place":"Valbuena de Duero, Valladolid, Ribera del Duero, Castilla y León","founded":"1864","wine":{"name":"Único","appellation":"Ribera del Duero","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Tinto Fino, the local Tempranillo, by far the larger part, with a small share of Cabernet Sauvignon.","sites":"Plots of the estate at Valbuena that lie between the slopes below the limestone plateau and the river's alluvial plain, at about 700 to 900 metres: fine colluvium with small fragments of limestone over sand and quartz gravel, with angular limestone stones on the surface.","making":"Native yeasts start the fermentation in wooden vats, where the malolactic follows. The wine then moves between barrels of 225 litres, French and American, new and used, and large wooden vats, five to six years in wood by the house's sheets and pages, then the rest of its time in bottle, so that it leaves the estate at least ten years after its harvest.","glass":"Blackcurrant and dried cherry over cedar, cigar box and a cool, minty lift, then tobacco, leather and polished wood; fine, silky tannin, fresh acidity and a fragrant finish that lingers.","ageing":"Already resolved when it is sold, a decade after the harvest, it keeps for decades more; the house speaks of forty to sixty years in a good cellar.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a gentle decant takes an older bottle off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Único","means":"The estate's first wine, sold about ten years after its harvest."},{"term":"Ribera del Duero","means":"The Denominación de Origen of the upper Duero valley, created in 1982."},{"term":"Reserva Especial","means":"A different bottling: several vintages of Único blended and sold without a year."}]},"table":"From Valbuena de Duero, Vega Sicilia's Único is mostly Tinto Fino with a little Cabernet Sauvignon, held at least ten years between wood and bottle before it is sold. It needs only a gentle decant off its sediment, for it arrives already resolved, with cedar, tobacco and mint.","story":"The name Vega de Sicilia was first written for this stretch of the Duero in 1577, by the house's account. In 1848 the Lecanda family bought the estate from the Marquis of Valbuena, and in 1864 the buyer's son began to make wine here, planting cuttings from Bordeaux of Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Malbec beside the local Tinto Fino. He was no trader, the house admits, and in 1888 the estate was sold to a man named Herrero; in 1897 another Herrero, no relation, bought it at auction. In 1907, on the house's word, a Rioja merchant who leased the estate, seeking wine to make up for the phylloxera that had struck Rioja, hired a Basque winemaker to run its cellar, and the first Único is usually dated to the 1915 vintage. A seed company, Prodes, bought Vega Sicilia in 1950, and in 1965 it passed to a Venezuelan businessman of Czech origin who had tasted its wines in New York. In April 1982, the year Ribera del Duero became a Denominación de Origen, the Álvarez family bought the estate. Through every change of hands the wine kept its slow making, years in wood and years in bottle. Único teaches patience: the red that made the valley's name long before the Denominación, sold only when it is ready.","others":[{"name":"Valbuena 5","note":"Tinto Fino with a little Merlot from the same estate, named for its five years between wood and bottle before release."},{"name":"Único Reserva Especial","note":"A blend of three vintages of Único sold without a year, a new set of vintages with each release."}],"traps":["Único Reserva Especial blends several vintages and carries no year; it is not a Reserva in the ageing ladder of the Denominación.","Valbuena 5, read quinto, is named for its five years between wood and bottle before release, not for a vineyard or a fifth growth.","Alión, a separate winery the Álvarez family created in Ribera del Duero, is not a cuvée of Vega Sicilia."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-b8u5i8tv","front":"Vega Sicilia Único: how long is it held at the estate before it is sold, and in what?","back":"At least ten years: five to six in barrels and large wooden vats, the rest in bottle."},{"id":"fl-gj0ci0ue","front":"Which vines did the founder's son plant at Valbuena from 1864 beside the local Tinto Fino?","back":"Cuttings from Bordeaux: Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot and Malbec. Vega Sicilia's Único is made with a little Cabernet Sauvignon."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ulhk741x","q":"Único reaches the market already resolved. What in its making explains that?","opts":["A short, cool fermentation in steel tanks","About ten years between wood and bottle before release","Grapes dried on mats for weeks before pressing","A blend of three vintages in every bottle"],"a":1,"exp":"The house holds Único five to six years in barrels and large vats, then in bottle, for ten years in all; blending three vintages is the Reserva Especial, not Único.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-5bchdta9","q":"Which family bought Vega Sicilia in April 1982, the year Ribera del Duero became a Denominación de Origen?","opts":["The Álvarez family","The Herrero family","The Palacio family of Rioja","The Lecanda family"],"a":0,"exp":"The Álvarez family bought it in 1982; two unrelated Herreros held it from 1888, the second's family until the sale to Prodes in 1950, and the Lecandas founded it.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Vega Sicilia: History","url":"https://www.temposvegasicilia.com/en/presentation/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vega Sicilia: Único","url":"https://www.temposvegasicilia.com/en/wine/234/unico","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vega Sicilia: Único 2015 technical sheet","url":"https://www.temposvegasicilia.com/f/en/wine/unico-en-1727.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Vega Sicilia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vega_Sicilia","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Spanish Wine Lover: Vega Sicilia","url":"https://spanishwinelover.com/108-winerie-bodegas","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-g94j91bt": {"id":"fl-g94j91bt","key":"pingus","group":"ribera-del-duero","region":"ribera-del-duero","producer":"Dominio de Pingus","producerId":"p-dominio-de-pingus","say":"doh-MEE-nee-oh deh PING-gus","place":"La Horra and Quintanilla de Onésimo, Ribera del Duero, Castilla y León","founded":"1995","wine":{"name":"Pingus","appellation":"Ribera del Duero","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Tinto Fino alone, the local name for Tempranillo.","sites":"Old bush vines at La Horra, farmed biodynamically, above all two neighbouring parcels, both planted in 1929: Barroso, on an old terrace of the Duero with gravel and sand over clay and limestone, and San Cristóbal, a clay slope facing south west.","making":"The old bush vines give low yields, and the wine is aged in new French oak barrels. From the late 2010s part of it has also rested in large oak vats beside the barrels.","glass":"Blueberry, blackberry and black cherry over violet, liquorice and graphite, with a flinty edge; opaque and dense yet polished, its tannin fine and ripe, its finish long.","ageing":"Dense and closed in its first years, it opens slowly over a decade and keeps for twenty years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle an hour or two ahead to let it open; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Pingus","means":"The estate's grand vin, from its old bush vines at La Horra."},{"term":"Dominio de Pingus","means":"The name of the estate: the domain of Pingus, after its founder's nickname."},{"term":"Ribera del Duero","means":"The appellation: a red here must be at least three quarters Tempranillo."}]},"table":"Old bush vines at La Horra, above all two parcels planted in 1929, give Dominio de Pingus its Pingus, Tinto Fino alone, raised in new French oak. Dense, dark and polished, with violet and graphite, it is the modern face of Ribera beside the long aged Único.","story":"A young Danish winemaker came to Ribera del Duero in the early 1990s to build up the cellar of a new estate, Hacienda Monasterio. He turned to the old bush vines of La Horra, and in 1995 he made the first Pingus from two parcels there, Barroso and San Cristóbal, both planted in 1929. He named the wine after his own childhood nickname. The 1995 won an American critic's praise, and the story goes that part of that small first vintage was lost when the ship carrying it to America sank off the Azores, though the accounts differ on the year. Flor de Pingus, from a wider set of vineyards around La Horra, became the second wine, and the vines came to be farmed biodynamically. Later the founder began PSI, a project with the valley's growers that buys their old vine fruit so that their vineyards are worked rather than pulled up. From the late 2010s part of Pingus has rested in large oak vats beside the new barrels. Pingus stands at the other pole of Ribera: where Único is made by years in the cellar, Pingus is made by old vines and low yields, and its 1995 showed that the valley had more than one house to know.","others":[{"name":"Flor de Pingus","note":"The second wine, Tinto Fino from a wider set of vineyards around La Horra."},{"name":"PSI","note":"A separate project with the valley's growers, buying and reviving their old vine fruit; never a declassified Pingus."}],"traps":["Tinto Fino on a Pingus sheet is Tempranillo under its Ribera name, not a separate grape.","Flor de Pingus is the second wine and PSI a separate growers' project; neither is Pingus declassified.","Pingus has no Cabernet Sauvignon; the famous Ribera red with a share of that Bordeaux grape is Vega Sicilia's Único."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-zgytf6h5","front":"Dominio de Pingus: which two parcels at La Horra lie at the heart of Pingus, and when were they planted?","back":"Barroso and San Cristóbal, old bush vines of Tinto Fino planted in 1929."},{"id":"fl-3xttey78","front":"Pingus: in which year was its first vintage made?","back":"1995, made at La Horra by a Danish winemaker who had come to Ribera in the early 1990s."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-3r3nj4b4","q":"Pingus comes from old bush vines, above all two parcels planted in 1929. In which village of Ribera del Duero do they grow?","opts":["Peñafiel","Valbuena de Duero","La Horra","Pesquera de Duero"],"a":2,"exp":"Barroso and San Cristóbal lie at La Horra; Valbuena de Duero is the home of Vega Sicilia and Pesquera de Duero that of Tinto Pesquera.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-6ikuwo3h","q":"Years before he made Pingus in 1995, its Danish founder came to Ribera del Duero to build up the cellar of which estate?","opts":["Protos at Peñafiel","Tinto Pesquera","Vega Sicilia at Valbuena","Hacienda Monasterio"],"a":3,"exp":"He came in the early 1990s to work at Hacienda Monasterio, then made his own wine at La Horra; Vega Sicilia, the nearest guess, is the old Valbuena estate Pingus is set against.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Dominio de Pingus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominio_de_Pingus","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Dominio de Pingus: legal notice","url":"https://www.pingus.es/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Corney and Barrow: Dominio de Pingus","url":"https://www.corneyandbarrow.com/dominio-de-pingus","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vila Viniteca: Pingus","url":"https://www.vilaviniteca.es/en/pingus","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Berry Bros and Rudd: 2018 Pingus","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-20188106120-2018-pingus-ribera-del-duero-spain","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-7mclfufx": {"id":"fl-7mclfufx","key":"pesquera","group":"ribera-del-duero","region":"ribera-del-duero","producer":"Tinto Pesquera","producerId":"p-tinto-pesquera","say":"TEEN-toh pes-KEH-rah","place":"Pesquera de Duero, Valladolid, Ribera del Duero, Castilla y León","founded":"1972","wine":{"name":"Tinto Pesquera Reserva","appellation":"Ribera del Duero","classification":"Reserva: at least three years of ageing, one of them in oak barrels","colour":"red","grapes":"Tinto Fino alone, the local Tempranillo, with no Bordeaux grape in the blend.","sites":"The family's own vineyards around Pesquera de Duero, in the Valladolid stretch of the valley, planted to Tinto Fino alone.","making":"The wine is aged at least fourteen months in American oak barrels of 225 litres, by the house's own sheets, and rests in bottle before it leaves the cellar. The Denominación asks a Reserva for three years of ageing with at least twelve months in oak.","glass":"Blackberry, plum and black cherry over vanilla, cocoa and tobacco, with the dill and coconut American oak can give; firm, dusty tannin and a warm, toasty finish.","ageing":"Firm and oak marked on release, it softens over five to ten years and holds well beyond.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; give a young bottle an hour in a decanter, and pour an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Reserva","means":"Aged at least three years, a year or more of it in oak barrels."},{"term":"Tinto Pesquera","means":"The name of the wine and the house, after the village of Pesquera de Duero."},{"term":"Ribera del Duero","means":"The Denominación de Origen the founder helped bring about in 1982."}]},"table":"Founded in 1972 in the village whose name it carries, Tinto Pesquera makes its Reserva from Tinto Fino alone and ages it in American oak: blackberry, vanilla and tobacco over firm, dusty tannin. In the 1980s it joined Vega Sicilia on wine lists abroad.","story":"Pesquera de Duero, a village on the river in the Valladolid stretch of the valley, was the birthplace of both Alejandro Fernández (1932 to 2021) and his wife. With money from the sale of a patent, by the family's account, they bought a small sixteenth century stone press house in the village, and there in 1972 they began to make wine of Tinto Fino alone from the family's vines; the first Tinto Pesquera, named for their village, came from the 1975 vintage. Fernández was among the growers who pressed for a Denominación de Origen, and when the ministry's order of 21 July 1982 created Ribera del Duero it counted about ten wineries. In the middle of the 1980s an American critic praised the house's 1982 wines, likening them to Pétrus, and Pesquera found its way onto wine lists abroad beside Vega Sicilia. Its first idea, Tinto Fino alone and American oak, did not change while French oak became the modern habit of the valley. Other wineries followed under the family, Condado de Haza at Roa among them, and Fernández died in Santander in May 2021, aged 88. In the Reserva a guest tastes Ribera as its first modern generation made it: one grape, American oak and the name of a village on the label.","others":[{"name":"Tinto Pesquera Crianza","note":"Tinto Pesquera's younger red, Tinto Fino alone with a shorter time in oak."},{"name":"Janus Gran Reserva","note":"The house's Gran Reserva, made only in years the family judges worthy."},{"name":"Condado de Haza","note":"A second estate of the family, at Roa de Duero."}],"traps":["Tinto Pesquera is Tinto Fino alone, with no Cabernet Sauvignon or Merlot, unlike Vega Sicilia's Único.","Janus is the house's Gran Reserva, not a separate vineyard or estate.","Reserva on a Ribera label means three years of ageing with at least twelve months in oak; Gran Reserva asks five years with two in oak."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-5t069nay","front":"Tinto Pesquera Reserva: which grape, and which oak?","back":"Tinto Fino alone, aged in American oak barrels of 225 litres."},{"id":"fl-6k36fvax","front":"Tinto Pesquera: when did the family begin to make wine in its stone press house, and which vintage came first?","back":"1972 in the press house; the first Tinto Pesquera came from the 1975 vintage."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-jxh5kvxv","q":"Which ageing does Ribera del Duero ask of a red labelled Reserva, such as Tinto Pesquera Reserva?","opts":["Eighteen months, with no rule on oak","Three years, at least one of them in oak","Five years, at least two of them in oak","Two years, at least six months in oak"],"a":1,"exp":"A Reserva needs thirty six months of ageing with at least twelve in oak barrels; five years with two in oak is the Gran Reserva.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-w1tep48k","q":"Tinto Pesquera's founder was among those who pressed for Ribera del Duero's Denominación de Origen. When was it created?","opts":["1982","1864","1975","1972"],"a":0,"exp":"A ministerial order of 21 July 1982 created the Denominación; 1972 is the year the family began to make wine in its press house and 1975 the first Tinto Pesquera.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wine Industry Advisor: Alejandro Fernández, initiator of Ribera del Duero, passed away","url":"https://wineindustryadvisor.com/2021/05/25/passing-of-alejandro-fernandez/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Familia Fernández Rivera: History","url":"https://familiafernandezrivera.com/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Spanish Wine Lover: Obituary, Alejandro Fernández, founder of Pesquera","url":"https://spanishwinelover.com/obituary-alejandro-fernandez-1932-2021-founder-of-pesquera","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Ribera del Duero: Pliego de condiciones","url":"https://www.riberadelduero.es/sites/default/files/2023-08/PLIEGO_DOUE%2031-07-23_0.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Monsieur Touton: Tinto Pesquera Reserva sheet","url":"https://www.mtouton.com/products/spain/castilla-y-leon/ribera-del-duero/97242-2020/tinto-pesquera-reserva","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-nijdf86a": {"id":"fl-nijdf86a","key":"protos","group":"ribera-del-duero","region":"ribera-del-duero","producer":"Protos","producerId":"","say":"PROH-tohs","place":"Peñafiel, Valladolid, Ribera del Duero, Castilla y León","founded":"1927","wine":{"name":"Protos Gran Reserva","appellation":"Ribera del Duero","classification":"Gran Reserva: at least five years of ageing, two of them in oak barrels","colour":"red","grapes":"Tinto Fino alone, the local Tempranillo.","sites":"Small parcels of very old Tinto Fino in the valley, the vines more than sixty years old by the house's sheet.","making":"The fermentation runs in stainless steel at 26 to 28 degrees Celsius, with three to four weeks on the skins. It then spends at least twenty four months in oak barrels, about four fifths French and the rest American, most of them new, and at least thirty six months in bottle before it is sold.","glass":"Deep cherry red with a garnet rim; fruit compote, cocoa, dark chocolate and roasted coffee over tobacco, toast and a light smoke, with round tannin and a long, silky finish.","ageing":"Drinkable from its release, after five years and more in the cellars, it has by the house's own note a long life ahead in bottle.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, as the house advises; decant gently off any sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Gran Reserva","means":"At least five years of ageing, two of them or more in oak barrels."},{"term":"Ribera del Duero","means":"The Denominación de Origen, whose name the house gave up to it in 1982."},{"term":"Protos","means":"The Greek word for first, the name the house took in 1982."}]},"table":"The Peñafiel house that gave Ribera del Duero its name in 1982, Protos ages its Gran Reserva two years in mostly French oak and three in bottle, so the Tinto Fino arrives mellow for a roast of lamb, all cocoa, toast and tobacco.","story":"Eleven growers of Peñafiel, friends since childhood, joined in March 1927 to found a winery in the town, the house recounts, and its first wines, of 1927 and 1928, won gold medals at the Barcelona exposition of 1929. It traded under the name Ribera Duero. About 1970 the house dug an ageing cellar into the hill beneath Peñafiel's castle, with more than two kilometres of galleries. When the Denominación de Origen was created in 1982, the winery, then a co-operative by the regulating council's own account, let the new council use its registered name, Ribera Duero, for the whole region, and took for itself the name Protos, the Greek word for first, in keeping with its motto, Ser Primero, to be first. In 2008 the house opened a new winery near the old, designed by the British architects Rogers Stirk Harbour and Partners with Alonso Balaguer and Arquitectos Asociados. The Gran Reserva keeps the long view of the founders' cellar: it is sold only after two years and more in oak and three in bottle. Remember Protos as the house that gave the region its name; its Gran Reserva is Ribera in its classical form, released only when long rested.","others":[{"name":"Protos Reserva","note":"Tinto Fino with a shorter time in oak and bottle, the step below the Gran Reserva."},{"name":"Protos '27","note":"A red named for the founding year, first made with the 2014 vintage for the house's ninetieth anniversary, its label modelled on the house's label for the 1927 vintage."}],"traps":["Protos is the house's own name, Greek for first; Ribera Duero was its old name, which it gave to the Denominación in 1982.","Protos Gran Reserva needs five years of ageing with at least two in oak; the house gives it two in barrel and three in bottle."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-1pritfpo","front":"Protos: which name did the house give up to the new Denominación in 1982?","back":"Ribera Duero, the name it had traded under; it then took the name Protos."},{"id":"fl-lzyj3fln","front":"Protos Gran Reserva: what does it spend in oak, and then in bottle, before it is sold?","back":"At least twenty four months in oak, mostly French and mostly new, then at least thirty six in bottle."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-4ei4rpvq","q":"How is Protos Gran Reserva aged before it is released?","opts":["One year in oak, then two in bottle","Six years in large vats, then four in bottle","Eighteen months in steel, then a year in bottle","Two years in oak, then three in bottle"],"a":3,"exp":"The house gives it at least twenty four months in barrel and thirty six in bottle, beyond the five years with two in oak the rules ask; years in large vats and a decade before release are Vega Sicilia's way with Único.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-y067fgf2","q":"The Peñafiel winery of 1927 traded as Ribera Duero until 1982. What became of that name?","opts":["It was sold to a co-operative in Roa","A sister winery in Rueda took it","The new Denominación took it for the whole region","It was kept for the house's white wine"],"a":2,"exp":"In 1982 the house let the new regulating council use Ribera Duero for the region and took the name Protos; the name went to the region, not to another winery or wine.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bodegas Protos: History","url":"https://www.bodegasprotos.com/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bodegas Protos: Protos Gran Reserva","url":"https://www.bodegasprotos.com/es/vino/gran-reserva/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Ribera del Duero: dossier, forty years of Ribera del Duero","url":"https://riberadelduero.es/sites/default/files/2022-05/DOSSIER%20-%2040%20AN%CC%83OS%20DE%20RIBERA%20DEL%20DUERO.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter China: Protos, a true Spanish pioneer","url":"https://www.decanterchina.com/en/promotion/protos-a-true-spanish-pioneer","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinissimus: Bodegas Protos","url":"https://www.vinissimus.com/es/bodega/bodegas-protos","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-yknhd846": {"id":"fl-yknhd846","key":"aalto","group":"ribera-del-duero","region":"ribera-del-duero","producer":"Aalto","producerId":"","say":"AHL-toh, peh EH-seh","place":"Quintanilla de Arriba, Valladolid, Ribera del Duero, Castilla y León","founded":"1999","wine":{"name":"Aalto PS","appellation":"Ribera del Duero","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Tinto Fino alone, the local Tempranillo.","sites":"Very old vines, sixty to a hundred years old by the house's sheets, from selected sites in La Horra and La Aguilera, with Fresnillo de las Dueñas in some years.","making":"The malolactic fermentation takes place in new barrels, and the wine is then aged in new French oak barrels for about twenty months, nineteen to twenty four by the house's sheets, before it is bottled.","glass":"Liquorice, Indian ink and flint over very ripe blackberry, with toast, coffee and tobacco from the new oak; dense and powerful, its tannin round despite the intensity.","ageing":"Young, it is dense and marked by its oak; it needs several years in bottle and keeps for two decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; pour a young bottle into a decanter an hour or two ahead; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"PS","means":"Pagos Seleccionados: the selected sites, the house's wine of very old vines."},{"term":"Ribera del Duero","means":"The upper Duero's Denominación de Origen, for reds mostly of Tempranillo."}]},"table":"Aalto PS, for Pagos Seleccionados, gathers Tinto Fino from very old vines at La Horra and La Aguilera, with its malolactic in new barrels and its ageing in new French oak: liquorice, Indian ink and ripe blackberry. Decant it an hour ahead.","story":"Two men who knew Ribera del Duero from the inside founded Aalto in February 1999: a winemaker who had run the cellar of Vega Sicilia for some thirty years, until 1998, and a former head of the Denominación's regulating council. They set out to gather old vines from many villages of the valley rather than to farm a single estate, and until the 2004 harvest the house made its wines in rented premises at Roa. Its own winery at Quintanilla de Arriba, in Valladolid, built in stages, was ready in part for the 2005 harvest, and in 2006 the Masaveu and Nozaleda families joined the venture. A second stage, built for visitors, followed in 2016. Beside Aalto, the house's broader red, stands the selection called PS, for Pagos Seleccionados, the chosen sites: Tinto Fino from very old vines in villages such as La Horra and La Aguilera, with its malolactic in new barrels and its ageing in new French oak. Aalto PS turns the classical training of Vega Sicilia's cellar to the old vines of many villages: a modern Ribera with long experience behind it.","others":[{"name":"Aalto","note":"The house's broader red, Tinto Fino from many villages of the valley, a wider blend of sites than PS."}],"traps":["PS stands for Pagos Seleccionados, the selected sites; it is not an ageing term such as Reserva or Gran Reserva.","Aalto's founding winemaker came from Vega Sicilia, but Aalto is a separate house; Vega Sicilia's sister estate in Ribera is Alión.","Aalto's winery stands at Quintanilla de Arriba, but PS draws on old vines in other villages such as La Horra and La Aguilera."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-v02n7nf5","front":"Aalto PS: what do the letters stand for?","back":"Pagos Seleccionados: selected sites of very old Tinto Fino vines, in villages such as La Horra and La Aguilera."},{"id":"fl-b2ps06tk","front":"Where had Aalto's founding winemaker run the cellar for some thirty years before 1999?","back":"At Vega Sicilia, until 1998."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-c5itnyjb","q":"In the cellar, what handling does Aalto PS receive?","opts":["Malolactic in new barrels, then new French oak","Two years in American oak, then a year in bottle","A decade between large vats and bottle","Steel alone, bottled within the year"],"a":0,"exp":"The house's sheets give the malolactic in new barrels and about twenty months in new French oak; a decade between vats and bottle is Único's way at Vega Sicilia, where the founding winemaker worked.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1ivinpq2","q":"Until the 2004 harvest, where did Aalto make its wines?","opts":["At a co-operative in Aranda de Duero","In rented premises at Roa","In Vega Sicilia's cellar at Valbuena","In the Protos galleries at Peñafiel"],"a":1,"exp":"From 1999 until the 2004 harvest the house worked in rented premises at Roa; its own winery at Quintanilla de Arriba took its first harvest in 2005.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Aalto: The Winery","url":"https://aalto.es/en/the-winery/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Aalto: Press dossier","url":"https://aalto.es/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/aalto-dossierpdf.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Aalto: PS 2015 technical sheet","url":"https://aalto.es/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/ps-2015.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Producer profile, Aalto","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/producer-profiles/producer-profile-aalto-245625/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Trialto: Aalto PS, Pagos Seleccionados 2021","url":"https://trialto.kork.ca/en/our-wineries/aalto/ps-pagos-seleccionados/2021","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-sn1fvb29": {"id":"fl-sn1fvb29","key":"alvaro-palacios","group":"priorat","region":"priorat","producer":"Álvaro Palacios","producerId":"p-alvaro-palacios","say":"AHL-vah-roh pah-LAH-thyohs, lehr-MEE-tah","place":"Gratallops, Priorat, Catalonia","founded":"1989","wine":{"name":"L'Ermita","appellation":"Priorat","classification":"Gran Vinya Classificada of DOQ Priorat, the first so named, from the 2017 vintage","colour":"red","grapes":"Garnacha, the great majority, from old bush vines, with a little Cariñena and a few white vines planted among them.","sites":"L'Ermita, a steep slope of llicorella facing north east above Gratallops, below the hermitage of Our Lady of Consolation, with bush vines planted before the middle of the twentieth century.","making":"Fermented in oak vats, the cap punched down, with a long maceration of about six weeks. Malolactic fermentation and ageing follow in French oak for about a year to a year and a half; the cellar has moved from new barrels towards large oval foudres, used for the 2023 and the 2024.","glass":"Black cherry, raspberry and violet over dried thyme, liquorice and slate dust; deep, yet more floral and fresh than its weight suggests, with fine, firm tannin and slate again on the long finish.","ageing":"Tight in its first years, it opens after five to ten and lasts two decades and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant it an hour or two before serving if young, gently off its sediment if old; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"L'Ermita","means":"Catalan for the hermitage: the vineyard takes its name from the chapel on the hill above it."},{"term":"Gran Vinya Classificada","means":"A great classified vineyard, the rung above Vinya Classificada on Priorat's own ladder; L'Ermita holds it from the 2017 vintage."},{"term":"Denominació d'Origen Qualificada","means":"Priorat's appellation in Catalan, of the qualified rank that only Rioja shares in Spain."}]},"table":"Álvaro Palacios takes L'Ermita from one steep slope of old Garnacha on llicorella slate below a hermitage above Gratallops, the first Gran Vinya Classificada of Priorat. Deep yet floral and stony, it wants a decanter and a large glass.","story":"Born into the family of Palacios Remondo, a wine house of Rioja, the founder studied oenology in Bordeaux, working at Pétrus as a student. In 1989, at the invitation of the founder of Clos Mogador, he joined the newcomers gathering at Gratallops, who made their first wines together from that vintage, each sold as a clos; his label was Clos Dofí, the name that passed to the wine of Finca Dofí, his first vineyard. In 1993 he acquired the slope that became L'Ermita: old bush Garnacha on llicorella facing north east, below the hermitage of Our Lady of Consolation, which gave the wine its name, and the first vintage followed that year. Around it he built a ladder of his own, from the regional Camins del Priorat and Les Terrasses through the Gratallops village wine to Finca Dofí and L'Ermita, and the family went on to make wine in Bierzo, at Descendientes de J. Palacios, beside Palacios Remondo in Rioja. Over the years the cellar moved from new barrels towards large oval foudres. In 2019 the denomination presented its ladder of village, site and classified vineyard wines, and L'Ermita was named its first Gran Vinya Classificada, from the 2017 vintage. L'Ermita puts the last rung of Priorat's ladder in one glass: one slope, old Garnacha and slate.","others":[{"name":"Finca Dofí","note":"From the founder's first vineyard at Gratallops; the wine began as Clos Dofí, one of the five clos labels of the revival."},{"name":"Gratallops Vi de Vila","note":"The village tier of Priorat's own ladder, Garnacha with Cariñena from the slopes around Gratallops."},{"name":"Les Terrasses","note":"The regional wine, Garnacha with Cariñena, and the usual introduction to llicorella."}],"traps":["Llicorella is a dark slate shot with quartz, not limestone, and it is the soil of L'Ermita.","Les Terrasses and Camins del Priorat are regional wines of the house, not second wines of L'Ermita.","Priorat is written DOQ in Catalan and DOCa in Castilian, a qualified rank it shares only with Rioja."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-jn11nhgy","front":"L'Ermita: on what soil, and facing which way, does the vineyard lie?","back":"A steep slope of llicorella slate facing north east above Gratallops, below the hermitage of Our Lady of Consolation."},{"id":"fl-z8fk9fpq","front":"Which rung of Priorat's ladder did Álvaro Palacios's L'Ermita reach first, and from which vintage?","back":"Gran Vinya Classificada, the great classified vineyard, the rung above Vinya Classificada, from the 2017 vintage."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-serjj40k","q":"Which vines grow on L'Ermita, Álvaro Palacios's slope above Gratallops?","opts":["Syrah and Merlot on terraced clay","Tempranillo grafted on to old rootstock","Old bush Garnacha with a little Cariñena","Young Cabernet Sauvignon trained on wires"],"a":2,"exp":"L'Ermita is old bush Garnacha with a little Cariñena and a few white vines, on llicorella; its vines are old and bush trained, not young vines on wires.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ddxv7hiz","q":"Álvaro Palacios acquired the slope of L'Ermita in 1993. What gave the wine its name?","opts":["A former owner of the old terraces","The Palacios family house in Rioja","The Carthusian priory of Scala Dei","A hermitage chapel on the hill above"],"a":3,"exp":"The vineyard lies below the hermitage of Our Lady of Consolation, and ermita is Catalan for hermitage; Scala Dei is the priory that named the whole region, not this slope.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"DOQ Priorat: wineries and wines in the levels of the classification","url":"https://doqpriorat.org/en/?p=4349","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Alvaro Palacios L'Ermita 1993","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/alvaro-palacios-lermita-1993-299678/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Alvaro Palacios, Spanish Wanderer","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/alvaro-palacios-spanish-wanderer-246470/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Álvaro Palacios Priorat technical sheet","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/media/183310/palacios-tech-sheet.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Escapement Magazine: L'Ermita, Álvaro Palacios, Priorat DOQ","url":"https://escapementmagazine.com/articles/lermita-alvaro-palacios-priorat-doq/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-pcr7auw0": {"id":"fl-pcr7auw0","key":"clos-mogador","group":"priorat","region":"priorat","producer":"Clos Mogador","producerId":"p-clos-mogador","say":"klohs moh-gah-DOR","place":"Gratallops, Priorat, Catalonia","founded":"1979","wine":{"name":"Clos Mogador","appellation":"Priorat","classification":"Vi de Finca Qualificada, by the house's account the first wine in Spain so ranked","colour":"red","grapes":"Garnacha leads, with Cariñena, then Syrah and Cabernet Sauvignon, in proportions that move with the year.","sites":"The estate's own vineyard around the cellar at Gratallops, which the house calls an immense amphitheatre of llicorella slate; the separate Manyetes parcel makes a wine of its own.","making":"The fermentation, on native yeasts alone, takes place in large oak vats or open 300 litre barrels, with a long maceration of four weeks and more. It is then aged in large old oak foudres, with smaller casks beside them in some years, for a year to a year and a half before bottling.","glass":"Dark cherry, blackberry and wild thyme over hot stone and graphite; full and warm, but savoury rather than sweet, with firm, fine tannin and a dry, stony close.","ageing":"Firm and closed for five years or so, it opens slowly, and a strong vintage keeps for twenty years.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour or two in a decanter for a young bottle, a gentle decant off the sediment for an old one; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Clos","means":"The French word for an enclosed vineyard, which the newcomers of 1989 took for their estate wines."},{"term":"Vi de Finca Qualificada","means":"A single estate rank, as the house describes it: grapes from the estate alone, bottled there."},{"term":"Priorat","means":"The denomination, written DOQ in Catalan; Priorat and Rioja alone hold Spain's qualified rank."}]},"table":"In 1989 the Gratallops estate of Clos Mogador led Priorat's revival: its red is Garnacha and Cariñena, with some Syrah and Cabernet, from its own amphitheatre of llicorella, fermented in oak and aged in large foudres. Warm, dark and savoury, it wants an hour in a decanter.","story":"In 1979 a winemaker of a family of French origin, long in the wine trade of Catalonia, bought land at Gratallops with his family: the beginning of Clos Mogador. In 1989 he drew a small group of newcomers to the village, and for the first three vintages, 1989 to 1991, the five pooled their grapes in one rented cellar and sold the one wine under clos labels that settled into five: Clos Mogador, Clos Dofí, Clos Erasmus, Clos Martinet and Clos de l'Obac. The French word clos, an enclosed vineyard, gave each label its first word. From 1992 each house made its own wine, and Clos Mogador went on drawing on the family's vineyard on the llicorella around the cellar, and the house later added Manyetes, from a parcel of Cariñena bottled apart, and a barrel aged white, Nelin. In time the estate gained the rank of Vi de Finca Qualificada, by its own account the first wine in Spain to hold it, a rank that asks for grapes from the estate alone. In later years the house turned from small casks to large old foudres, fermenting with native yeasts alone. Clos Mogador is the founding estate of the five clos, the house where modern Priorat began, and its red is savoury rather than sweet.","others":[{"name":"Manyetes","note":"A wine of its own from the separate Manyetes parcel at Gratallops, led by Cariñena with Garnacha."},{"name":"Nelin","note":"The house's white, led by Garnacha Blanca and aged in barrel; a white, not a second wine of the red."}],"traps":["The five clos of the revival were Mogador, Dofí, Erasmus, Martinet and l'Obac; a later Priorat wine with clos on its label was not one of them.","Nelin is the white of Clos Mogador, not a second wine of the red.","Vi de Finca Qualificada is a single estate rank, not one of the village, site and vineyard rungs that the DOQ presented in 2019."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-g5pf60f3","front":"Under which five clos labels did the newcomers' pooled wine of 1989 to 1991 settle?","back":"Clos Mogador, Clos Dofí, Clos Erasmus, Clos Martinet and Clos de l'Obac."},{"id":"fl-5eir5xgn","front":"Which rank does Clos Mogador claim to have been the first wine in Spain to hold?","back":"Vi de Finca Qualificada, a single estate rank for wine from the estate's own grapes alone."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-d1jmtbgb","q":"Clos Mogador, the Gratallops estate's red, is made from which grapes?","opts":["Tempranillo and Garnacha with a little Graciano","Garnacha and Cariñena with Syrah and Cabernet Sauvignon","Garnacha alone from one slope facing north","Cariñena alone from the Manyetes parcel"],"a":1,"exp":"Clos Mogador is Garnacha led, with Cariñena, Syrah and Cabernet Sauvignon; the Cariñena of the Manyetes parcel is bottled apart, under its own name.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-35sw2lig","q":"The family behind Clos Mogador bought land at Gratallops in 1979. With which vintage came the first Clos Mogador?","opts":["1989","2009","1999","1974"],"a":0,"exp":"The first Clos Mogador was the 1989, made with four other clos in one shared cellar; 1974 is the first Cartoixa of Scala Dei, and 1999 and 2009 come a decade and two too late.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Clos Mogador: the wine","url":"https://www.closmogador.com/asp/vi.asp?prod=vi&idioma=EN","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Clos Mogador: the making of the wines","url":"https://www.closmogador.com/asp/vinificacio.asp?prod=vinificacio&idioma=EN","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Clos Mogador","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clos_Mogador","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"World of Fine Wine: Priorat's gang of five","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/homepage-featured-articles/priorat-gang-of-five","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein.plus glossary: Clos Mogador","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/clos-mogador","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-0y5fen67": {"id":"fl-0y5fen67","key":"clos-erasmus","group":"priorat","region":"priorat","producer":"Clos Erasmus","producerId":"p-clos-erasmus","say":"klohs eh-RAHS-moos","place":"Gratallops, Priorat, Catalonia","founded":"1989","wine":{"name":"Clos Erasmus","appellation":"Priorat","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Garnacha, the larger part, with Syrah for the rest; the share of Syrah moves with the year.","sites":"Seventeen terraces, old Garnacha among them, on a hillside just outside Gratallops, on llicorella slate; younger vines and other parcels go to the second wine, Laurel.","making":"Picked by hand, then fermented with native yeasts, mostly in oak, with concrete and clay amphorae beside it since the 2010s; the maceration lasts about five weeks. It then spends about a year and a half in French oak barrels, a share of them new, with a little of the wine kept in amphorae.","glass":"Kirsch, black cherry and incense over crushed slate and dried thyme; dense and dark, cut by fresh acidity, with fine, firm tannin and a long, stony finish.","ageing":"It needs eight to ten years to open from a dense, closed youth, and lasts well past twenty.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a young bottle wants two hours in a decanter, an old one only a gentle decant off its sediment; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Clos Erasmus","means":"The estate's wine, named for the humanist Erasmus of Rotterdam and his In Praise of Folly."},{"term":"Priorat","means":"The denomination, DOQ in Catalan: one of Spain's two denominations of qualified rank, with Rioja."}]},"table":"On seventeen terraces of llicorella outside Gratallops, Clos Erasmus grows Garnacha with Syrah; the tiny estate was one of the five clos of 1989. Dark and dense, with fresh acidity, incense and crushed slate, it needs a decanter and, before that, years in the cellar.","story":"Seventeen terraces of vines on a hillside outside Gratallops were the start of Clos Erasmus: its founder, a Swiss newcomer to Priorat, bought them in 1988 and made her first wine with the 1989 vintage, among the five newcomers of the village. For three vintages, 1989 to 1991, the newcomers shared a rented cellar and one pooled wine, and of the clos labels it bore, which came down to five, hers was Clos Erasmus. She named it for the humanist Erasmus of Rotterdam (about 1466 to 1536), an allusion to his In Praise of Folly and an answer to those who called the venture madness. Once the five went their own ways in 1992, hers stayed small: one hillside of terraces, Garnacha led, with Syrah beside it. The company took the name Clos i Terrasses, and the flagship kept the name of Erasmus. Laurel, the second wine, comes from younger vines and other parcels. In the 2010s concrete and clay amphorae joined the oak in the cellar, for a share of the fermentation and a little of the ageing. Examiners usually ask for Clos Erasmus beside L'Ermita when they want the region's classic names: a wine of one terraced hillside, made in small amounts.","others":[{"name":"Laurel","note":"The second wine, Garnacha led, from younger vines and other parcels of the estate."}],"traps":["Laurel is the second wine of the same estate, not a separate house.","The company is Clos i Terrasses; Clos Erasmus is the name of its flagship wine.","The name honours the Dutch humanist Erasmus of Rotterdam, not a saint or a place in Priorat, and the founder is Swiss, not Catalan."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-0pjjk1wi","front":"Clos Erasmus: for whom is the wine named, and why?","back":"For the humanist Erasmus of Rotterdam: an allusion to his In Praise of Folly and an answer to those who called the venture madness."},{"id":"fl-lt6aefpf","front":"Clos Erasmus: what is the company behind the wine called, and what does its name recall?","back":"Clos i Terrasses, after the seventeen terraces of vines the founder bought outside Gratallops in 1988."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-n4febr67","q":"A guest wants to know the grapes of Clos Erasmus as it is made in the 2010s and 2020s. What do you say?","opts":["Tempranillo with Cabernet Sauvignon","Cariñena with a little Garnacha","Syrah with a little Viognier","Garnacha with Syrah"],"a":3,"exp":"Clos Erasmus is Garnacha led, with Syrah for the rest; Cariñena leads other Priorat wines, such as the Manyetes of Clos Mogador, not this hillside.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-thzcp5yr","q":"The founder of Clos Erasmus named the wine with one book of its namesake in mind, an answer to those who doubted her. Which book?","opts":["Utopia","The Book of the Courtier","In Praise of Folly","The Prince"],"a":2,"exp":"The name alludes to In Praise of Folly by Erasmus of Rotterdam, an answer to those who called the venture madness; Utopia is Thomas More's, not Erasmus's.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"World of Fine Wine: Priorat's gang of five","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/homepage-featured-articles/priorat-gang-of-five","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"European Cellars: Clos i Terrasses, folly takes many forms","url":"https://www.europeancellars.com/?p=2963","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"OWC: Clos i Terrasses, Clos Erasmus Priorat","url":"https://www.owc.lu/en/clos-erasmus-priorat/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Clos Erasmus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clos_Erasmus","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine.com: Clos i Terrasses Clos Erasmus 2022","url":"https://www.wine.com/product/clos-i-terrasses-clos-erasmus-2022/2748885","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9ztgdqrt": {"id":"fl-9ztgdqrt","key":"mas-martinet","group":"priorat","region":"priorat","producer":"Mas Martinet","producerId":"","say":"mahs mar-tee-NET","place":"Falset, in the Gratallops wine area, Priorat, Catalonia","founded":"1981","wine":{"name":"Clos Martinet","appellation":"Priorat","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Garnacha in most years the larger part, with Cariñena, Syrah and small shares of Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon, in proportions that move with the year.","sites":"Plots of the Mas d'en Martinet estate, which lies in the municipality of Falset but which the house places in the Gratallops wine area, on llicorella slate with many different aspects.","making":"Long macerations of about five weeks, part of the fruit with its stems; for the 2022 part of the wine, the Garnacha especially, was made by carbonic maceration and blended back. The wine then ages for about a year in a range of vessels, wood, concrete and clay among them.","glass":"Plum, black cherry and liquorice over rosemary and warm slate; full and ripe but fresh, the tannin fine and firm, the finish savoury and stony.","ageing":"It opens within a few years of release, then keeps and gains for fifteen years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter when young; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Mas Martinet","means":"The house, named for its estate, the Mas d'en Martinet; a mas is a Catalan farmhouse with its land."},{"term":"Clos Martinet","means":"The flagship red, one of the five clos labels of Priorat's revival, first made in 1989."}]},"table":"Mas Martinet's family estate in the Gratallops wine area gives Clos Martinet, first made in 1989 as one of the revival's five clos: a Garnacha led blend with Syrah and Cariñena on llicorella. Ripe yet fresh, and stony, it should breathe in a decanter for an hour first.","story":"The family behind Mas Martinet came to Priorat in 1981, according to the house, and spent its first years studying the estate's slopes, rootstocks, grapes, climate and soil before it planted the vineyards that give its wines. The founder was among the handful of newcomers who made the first clos wines together at Gratallops: for the vintages of 1989 to 1991 the group worked from pooled grapes in a single rented cellar, and Clos Martinet was one of the labels it bottled, beside Clos de l'Obac, Clos Dofí, Clos Erasmus and Clos Mogador. From 1992 the five houses worked apart. In 2000, by its own history, the founder's daughter took charge of the estate, and with her came an agroecological approach that treats the vineyards and the land around them as one living whole. Under her the house moved away from the methods of the revival's first years, towards long macerations with stems and a wider range of vessels than the small barrel, and for the 2022 part of the wine went through carbonic maceration. In Clos Martinet the second generation of the revival works on a wine of the first: the same estate and the same clos name, made with a different hand.","others":[{"name":"Martinet Bru","note":"Another red from the estate's own vineyards, planted on the strength of the family's studies of the 1980s."},{"name":"Camí Pesseroles","note":"A red from vineyards shaped by the same studies of the land; camí is Catalan for a path."}],"traps":["Mas Martinet is the house and Clos Martinet its flagship; Martinet Bru is another red of the estate, not the same wine under a second name.","The cellar lies in the municipality of Falset, yet its vineyards belong to the Gratallops wine area of the DOQ."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-akiz0nw0","front":"Clos Martinet: how was it made in its first three vintages, 1989 to 1991?","back":"From grapes pooled with four other newcomers in one rented cellar at Gratallops, the one wine sold under each newcomer's clos label."},{"id":"fl-41r75i22","front":"Which grape leads Clos Martinet in most years, and which others join it?","back":"Garnacha, with Cariñena, Syrah and small shares of Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-vflhqul3","q":"Where do the grapes of Mas Martinet's Clos Martinet grow?","opts":["On the Manyetes parcel beside Clos Mogador","On the Mas d'en Martinet estate, in the Gratallops wine area","On bought fruit from vineyards across the DOQ","On the high terraces below the Montsant at Escaladei"],"a":1,"exp":"The house places the plots of Mas d'en Martinet in the Gratallops wine area, though the estate lies in the municipality of Falset; the terraces below the Montsant belong to Scala Dei.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-inpys78c","q":"Mas Martinet's family came to Priorat in 1981. What did it do first, as the house tells it?","opts":["Studied the soils, rootstocks and climate before planting","Bought the old cellar of the priory of Scala Dei","Joined the cooperative of Falset as growers","Planted Tempranillo brought from Rioja"],"a":0,"exp":"By its own account the family spent its first years studying the estate's slopes, rootstocks, grapes, climate and soil before planting; the priory's lands passed to the families behind Scala Dei.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Mas Martinet: History","url":"https://www.masmartinet.com/history/?lang=en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Turisme Priorat: Mas Martinet Viticultors","url":"https://turismepriorat.org/en/get-organised/local-producers/mas-martinet-viticultors","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"World of Fine Wine: Priorat's gang of five","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/homepage-featured-articles/priorat-gang-of-five","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Flatiron Wines: Mas Martinet Clos Martinet 2021","url":"https://sf.flatiron-wines.com/products/mas-martinet-priorat-clos-martinet-2021-750ml","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Grand Cru Wijnen: 2022 Mas Martinet Clos Martinet","url":"https://www.grandcruwijnen.nl/en/2022-mas-martinet-clos-martinet","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ls3p7pjw": {"id":"fl-ls3p7pjw","key":"scala-dei","group":"priorat","region":"priorat","producer":"Cellers de Scala Dei","producerId":"","say":"SKAH-lah DAY, kar-TOH-shah","place":"Escaladei, La Morera de Montsant, Priorat, Catalonia","founded":"1844","wine":{"name":"Scala Dei Cartoixa","appellation":"Priorat","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Garnacha, the great majority, with Cariñena for the rest, from old vines.","sites":"Old vines on terraces of clay and on steep slopes of slate, at about 400 to 800 metres, in the house's vineyards around Escaladei below the cliffs of the Montsant.","making":"Each lot is fermented apart in small cement vats at a controlled temperature, with about three weeks on the skins, as the house describes it. The wine is then aged for about eighteen months before the final blend is bottled.","glass":"Red cherry, raspberry and dried thyme over fennel and wet slate; fresher and lighter on its feet than the Priorat of the lower villages, with bright acidity and fine tannin, ending on a note of stone.","ageing":"Open within a few years of the vintage, it lasts ten to fifteen years when well kept.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; half an hour or so in a decanter will do for a young bottle; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Cartoixa","means":"Catalan for a charterhouse: the Carthusian monastery of Escaladei beside the cellar."},{"term":"Scala Dei","means":"Latin for the ladder of God, the name of the monastery whose priory gave Priorat its name."}]},"table":"Beside the charterhouse of Escaladei, whose priors named Priorat, Cellers de Scala Dei makes Cartoixa, its old vine red: Garnacha with a little Cariñena from high slopes under the Montsant. First bottled in 1974, it is fresh, red fruited and stony.","story":"Carthusian monks founded the charterhouse of Santa Maria d'Escaladei at the foot of the Montsant cliffs in the twelfth century, at the wish of the Aragonese crown, the first house of their order in the Iberian Peninsula. The story goes that a shepherd had dreamt of angels climbing a stair to heaven on the spot, and the monks called it Scala Dei, the ladder of God. Its priors ruled the villages around, the Priorat, and planted vines. In 1835 the state's confiscation of church lands drove the monks out, and the buildings fell to ruin. The house dates its origin to 1844, when five families who had bought the monastery's lands founded a company to make wine, and by its account their bottles of Priorat won a gold medal at the Universal Exhibition in Paris in 1878. The cellar was refounded in 1974, and its first Cartoixa, of that vintage, is by several accounts the first wine bottled under the Priorat denomination. By 1990 the owners of the ruined charterhouse had given it to the Generalitat de Catalunya for its recovery, and in 2000 the Codorníu group of Penedès bought into the company. For a sommelier, Cartoixa ties the modern denomination to the monks who named it: Priorat bottled before the revival of 1989.","others":[{"name":"Scala Dei Prior","note":"A younger red of the house, which by its account owes its mineral notes to the llicorella."},{"name":"Masdeu","note":"From the Masdeu vineyard, set above 650 metres on what the house calls a natural balcony over Priorat."}],"traps":["Scala Dei is a place, a cellar and a monastery, not a grape or a style; the priory gave the whole region its name.","Cartoixa is the house's red, named for the charterhouse; the Cartoixa d'Escaladei itself is the monastery, given to the Generalitat by 1990."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-sp5584p6","front":"Cellers de Scala Dei: what does the name mean, and what legend lies behind it?","back":"The ladder of God: a shepherd, the story goes, dreamt of angels climbing a stair to heaven on the spot."},{"id":"fl-2amfar6x","front":"Which vintage gave the first bottled Cartoixa, and what claim does it carry?","back":"The 1974, by several accounts the first wine bottled under the Priorat denomination."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-eddwwyxa","q":"How does Cellers de Scala Dei make its Cartoixa?","opts":["Three years of ageing in old chestnut casks","Whole bunches trodden in the old monastery cellar","A blend of bought wine from across the DOQ","Each lot fermented apart in small cement vats"],"a":3,"exp":"By the house's account each lot is fermented apart in small cement vats, with about three weeks on the skins, then aged about eighteen months; nothing is trodden in the old monastery.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-js31pwb4","q":"What drove the monks out of the charterhouse of Scala Dei in 1835?","opts":["Phylloxera in the vineyards of the priory","A fire set by Napoleon's troops","The state's confiscation of church lands","The sale of the estate to Codorníu"],"a":2,"exp":"The confiscation of church property in 1835 forced the monks out, and the lands later passed to five families; Codorníu bought into the cellar only in 2000.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cellers de Scala Dei: Nine centuries of history","url":"https://cellersdescaladei.com/en/pages/historia","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cellers de Scala Dei: Scala Dei Cartoixa","url":"https://cellersdescaladei.com/en/products/scala-dei-cartoixa","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Patrimoni Generalitat de Catalunya: Carthusian Monastery of Escaladei","url":"https://patrimoni.gencat.cat/en/monuments/monuments/carthusian-monastery-of-escaladei","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Cartoixa d'Escaladei","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartoixa_d%27Escaladei","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Spanish Wine Lover: Priorat comes of age as Scala Dei celebrates its fiftieth anniversary","url":"https://spanishwinelover.com/priorat-comes-of-age-as-scala-dei-celebrates-its-50th-anniversary","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-y220zyii": {"id":"fl-y220zyii","key":"gonzalez-byass","group":"jerez","region":"jerez","producer":"González Byass","producerId":"p-gonzalez-byass","say":"gon-THAH-leth, TEE-oh PEH-peh","place":"Jerez de la Frontera, Jerez-Xérès-Sherry, Andalusia","founded":"1835","wine":{"name":"Tío Pepe Fino","appellation":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Palomino Fino alone, as the house's pages give it.","sites":"Albariza, the white chalk soil of Jerez, in the house's vineyards of the Macharnudo and Carrascal pagos outside the town.","making":"The Palomino must is fermented and the new wine fortified to 15 per cent, the strength at which flor thrives. It then ages under a veil of flor for at least four years in American oak butts, passing down the criaderas of the house's Tío Pepe solera in Jerez, and is clarified before bottling, unlike the En Rama.","glass":"Pale straw; green almond, chalk and sea spray over the yeasty, bready scent of flor. Bone dry, light and taut, closing clean and saline.","ageing":"Bottled ready to drink; a Fino is drunk for its freshness rather than kept, so open a young bottle.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, chilled in a bucket of ice and water and poured with no decanting into a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Tío Pepe","means":"Uncle Pepe: the founder's uncle José, whose name the solera and the wine carry."},{"term":"Fino","means":"Dry sherry of Palomino aged under a veil of flor, which keeps it pale and fresh."},{"term":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry","means":"The appellation, named in Spanish, French and English."}]},"table":"Tío Pepe, the Fino González Byass named for its founder's uncle, is Palomino from the chalk of Jerez aged at least four years under flor in American oak. Bone dry, saline and almond scented, it wants a cold glass and salted almonds or fried fish.","story":"In 1835 a young man of twenty three founded a bodega, small at first, in Jerez de la Frontera, and the house carries his surname, González. As the house tells it, his uncle José, Tío Pepe to the family, taught him the craft of the bodega and favoured a pale, very dry wine aged under flor, and the founder named a solera of it for his uncle. In 1844, by the house's own account, it shipped its first casks of this Fino abroad, to England, and its agent there, an English merchant, later became a partner and gave the firm the second half of its name, Byass. In 1935, the centenary year, the house's head of advertising dressed the bottle as a figure in a jacket and a hat, holding a guitar, and that figure later crowned a lit sign over Madrid's Puerta del Sol that became a landmark of the city. Tío Pepe is a Fino many guests know by name, so it is a natural place to begin teaching what flor does to a wine: it keeps the Palomino pale, dry and saline for years in cask.","others":[{"name":"Tío Pepe En Rama","note":"The same Fino bottled with little clarification, in limited spring releases."},{"name":"Tío Pepe Una Palma","note":"Fino from a few selected butts of the Tío Pepe solera, aged more than six years under flor."},{"name":"Noé Pedro Ximénez VORS","note":"A Pedro Ximénez from a solera with a certified average age of thirty years or more."}],"traps":["Tío Pepe is a Fino aged in Jerez, not a Manzanilla: a Manzanilla can only be aged in Sanlúcar de Barrameda.","A solera blends wines of many years, so Tío Pepe carries no vintage on its label.","Tío Pepe is the wine and the founder's uncle; the house that makes it is González Byass."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-sxekzztp","front":"Tío Pepe: whose name does the wine carry?","back":"The founder's uncle José, Uncle Pepe to the family, who, the house says, taught him the craft."},{"id":"fl-lmcnyuxn","front":"How long does Tío Pepe age under flor, and in what?","back":"At least four years, in American oak butts, passing down the criaderas and solera."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-c6bcp93e","q":"Why does González Byass's Tío Pepe stay pale and dry after years in cask?","opts":["Blending with Pedro Ximénez before bottling","A veil of flor keeps the air from it","Fortification to seventeen per cent","Ageing in steel tanks rather than oak"],"a":1,"exp":"Fortified to 15 per cent, the wine grows flor, a veil of yeast that shields it from the air and keeps it pale and dry; at seventeen per cent the flor dies, as in an Oloroso.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-29nhzg8a","q":"González Byass, by its own account, first shipped casks of Fino abroad in 1844. Where did they go?","opts":["England","France","Cuba","The Netherlands"],"a":0,"exp":"The casks went to England in 1844; the house's English agent later became a partner, which is why the firm carries the name Byass.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Tío Pepe: Our story","url":"https://www.tiopepe.com/int-en/our-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tío Pepe: Tío Pepe Fino","url":"https://www.tiopepe.com/en/product/tio-pepe-fino","kind":"primary"},{"title":"González Byass: Tío Pepe, from Jerez to the world","url":"https://www.gonzalezbyass.com/en/news/wineries/tio-pepe-jerez-world-story-icon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"González Byass: the Tío Pepe sign","url":"https://www.gonzalezbyass.com/node/1041","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tío Pepe: Una Palma","url":"https://www.tiopepe.com/en/product/una-palma","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-67p8y2xx": {"id":"fl-67p8y2xx","key":"la-gitana","group":"jerez","region":"jerez","producer":"Bodegas Hidalgo La Gitana","producerId":"p-hidalgo-la-gitana","say":"lah hee-TAH-nah, ee-DAHL-goh","place":"Sanlúcar de Barrameda, Manzanilla-Sanlúcar de Barrameda, Andalusia","founded":"1792","wine":{"name":"La Gitana Manzanilla","appellation":"Manzanilla-Sanlúcar de Barrameda","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Palomino Fino alone, from the family's own vineyards.","sites":"The family's vineyards on white albariza near Sanlúcar, above all the El Cuadrado estate in the Balbaina Alta pago, with Miraflores, a pago open to the Atlantic.","making":"Once fermented, the Palomino is set to age under flor in soleras the house dates to 1792. It spends about three years in the house's bodegas in the lower town of Sanlúcar, beside the river, where the damp sea air keeps the veil of flor thick the year round.","glass":"Very pale; chamomile, green apple and sea salt over fresh yeast and a nutty note. Light and bone dry, sharp and saline to the last.","ageing":"A Manzanilla is at its brightest young: drink La Gitana soon after release and keep an opened bottle cold.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, well chilled; leave the decanter aside; a slender tasting glass or a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Manzanilla","means":"Sherry aged under flor in Sanlúcar de Barrameda; the same wine aged in Jerez would be a Fino."},{"term":"Sanlúcar de Barrameda","means":"The seaside town at the mouth of the Guadalquivir, where every Manzanilla must be aged."},{"term":"La Gitana","means":"The gypsy woman: the brand's name, with a painted portrait of a gypsy woman on the label."}]},"table":"Aged by the river in Sanlúcar, where sea air keeps the flor thick, La Gitana is Hidalgo's Manzanilla, Palomino from the family's vines in Balbaina and Miraflores. Very cold, with prawns or fried fish, it shows pale, light and salty, scented with chamomile.","story":"The house dates itself to 1792, when its founder bought a small bodega for storing wine in Sanlúcar de Barrameda, the port at the mouth of the Guadalquivir. The business grew through the nineteenth century, and late in that century, the story goes, a gypsy woman sold the house's Manzanilla, not yet branded, at her bar in Málaga, and her customers asked for it as the gypsy's wine. The family kept the name La Gitana, and a portrait of a gypsy woman, painted for the firm, became its label. In 1964 Manzanilla gained an appellation of its own, Manzanilla-Sanlúcar de Barrameda, under the same Consejo Regulador as Jerez. The Hidalgo family grows its own Palomino in the Balbaina and Miraflores pagos and ages it in soleras that, by its account, go back to the founding year. Its Pastrana, a Manzanilla Pasada from one vineyard in Miraflores, shows what longer ageing does as the flor begins to thin. La Gitana teaches the difference the sea makes: the same Palomino aged inland in Jerez would be sold as a Fino, while in the damp, mild air of Sanlúcar the flor grows thicker and the wine comes out lighter and more saline.","others":[{"name":"Pastrana Manzanilla Pasada","note":"From the family's Pastrana vineyard in the Miraflores pago, a Manzanilla aged until the flor begins to thin."},{"name":"Napoleón Amontillado","note":"The house's flor wine aged on in contact with air after the flor has gone."}],"traps":["Call La Gitana a Manzanilla, never a Fino: its ageing in Sanlúcar de Barrameda gives it that name.","A Manzanilla Pasada such as Pastrana is a flor wine whose veil has thinned; it is not an Amontillado.","Manzanilla is made from Palomino like Fino; neither the grape nor the method differs, only the town where it ages."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-if8vnoco","front":"La Gitana Manzanilla: in which pagos are its grapes grown?","back":"In the family's own vineyards in Balbaina and Miraflores, on albariza near Sanlúcar."},{"id":"fl-c08115i7","front":"In which year does the house behind La Gitana date its founding, and how did it begin?","back":"1792, when its founder bought a small bodega for storing wine in Sanlúcar de Barrameda."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ousple56","q":"On a list, Hidalgo's La Gitana sits under Manzanilla, not Fino. What puts it there?","opts":["It is made from Moscatel rather than Palomino","It ages without flor, in contact with air","It is fortified to seventeen per cent","It ages under flor in Sanlúcar de Barrameda"],"a":3,"exp":"Manzanilla is flor aged Palomino matured in Sanlúcar, where sea air keeps the flor thick; the grape is the same as a Fino's, and seventeen per cent would kill the flor.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ogwk1oz3","q":"As the story goes, where did the gypsy woman who gave La Gitana its name sell the wine?","opts":["On the quays of Cádiz","At the fish market of Sanlúcar","At her bar in Málaga","At the April Fair of Seville"],"a":2,"exp":"The tale is told of a gypsy woman who sold the house's wine at her bar in Málaga; the family kept the name, and a portrait of a gypsy woman painted for the firm became the label.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bodegas Hidalgo La Gitana: home and history","url":"https://lagitana.es/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bodegas Hidalgo La Gitana: Manzanillas","url":"https://lagitana.es/manzanillas/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Selectus Wines: Hidalgo La Gitana, Manzanilla and salt","url":"https://selectuswines.com/en/hidalgo-la-gitana-manzanilla-and-salt/2693","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Spanish Wine Lover: Hidalgo-La Gitana","url":"https://spanishwinelover.com/winerie/hidalgo-la-gitana","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Sherry Wines, the Consejo Regulador: Manzanilla","url":"https://www.sherry.wine/sherry-wine/dry-sherry-wines/manzanilla","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-eemc0akw": {"id":"fl-eemc0akw","key":"valdespino","group":"jerez","region":"jerez","producer":"Valdespino","producerId":"p-valdespino","say":"bal-deh-SPEE-noh, ee-noh-THEN-teh","place":"Jerez de la Frontera, Jerez-Xérès-Sherry, Andalusia","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Inocente Fino","appellation":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Palomino Fino alone, from a single vineyard.","sites":"Macharnudo Alto, the house's vineyard high on the Macharnudo pago outside Jerez, planted on pure albariza chalk; every grape of Inocente comes from it.","making":"Unusually for a Fino, the must is fermented in American oak butts with native yeasts, as the house has long done. The new wine rests nine to ten months as sobretabla, then passes down a solera of ten criaderas, more than most Finos use, for about a decade under flor before bottling.","glass":"Salted almond, hazelnut skin and fresh yeast over old wood; fuller in body than most Finos yet bone dry, its salt lingering long.","ageing":"Its decade under flor has given it depth by the time it is bottled, so drink it while it is fresh.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; skip the decanter; a white wine glass gives its depth room.","label":[{"term":"Fino","means":"Dry sherry aged under flor; Inocente spends about a decade under the veil."},{"term":"Macharnudo","means":"The pago, a named district of albariza vineyards outside Jerez, not a village or an appellation."},{"term":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry","means":"The sherry appellation, its name given in three languages."}]},"table":"From one vineyard, Macharnudo Alto, comes Valdespino's Inocente: a Fino fermented in oak butts and aged about a decade under flor through ten criaderas. Fuller and nuttier than most Finos yet bone dry and salty, it can stand beside jamón or richer fish.","story":"Valdespino traces its roots, by its own telling, to 1264, when the Castilian king took Jerez and, as legend has it, rewarded one of the knights who had fought beside him, a Valdespino, with a vineyard. The house's records of a wine trade under the name reach back to 1430, and in 1883 it became a supplier of sherry to the Spanish royal household. While most of Jerez moved its fermentation into tanks, Valdespino kept fermenting the must of its Macharnudo vineyard in oak butts, and it kept long soleras of Fino, Amontillado and Palo Cortado. In 1999 the house passed to the Estévez group of Jerez, and the Inocente solera went on under its new owners. Inocente is the single vineyard Fino an examiner asks for: proof that a pago can speak through flor, and a lesson in how much depth a decade under the veil and ten criaderas can give a dry wine.","others":[{"name":"Tío Diego Amontillado","note":"The same Macharnudo base wine taken past the flor stage and aged on in contact with air."},{"name":"Cardenal Palo Cortado VORS","note":"A Palo Cortado of the VORS category, its solera certified at an average of thirty years and more."}],"traps":["Macharnudo is a pago, a named vineyard district on albariza, not a village appellation.","Tío Diego is the Amontillado from the same base wine, not a different vineyard.","Inocente is fermented in oak butts, not steel, and passes through ten criaderas where most Finos use fewer."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-dcley9uh","front":"Valdespino's Inocente: which vineyard gives its grapes?","back":"Macharnudo Alto, the house's vineyard on pure albariza in the Macharnudo pago outside Jerez."},{"id":"fl-jzvjl3m3","front":"What is unusual about how Inocente is fermented?","back":"Its must is fermented in American oak butts with native yeasts, where most Fino ferments in tanks."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-cvr9enp4","q":"An examiner asks for a Fino from one named vineyard, fermented in cask. Which bottle do you name?","opts":["Valdespino's Inocente","Lustau's San Emilio","González Byass's Tío Pepe","Hidalgo's La Gitana"],"a":0,"exp":"Inocente comes only from Macharnudo Alto and is fermented in American oak butts; Tío Pepe draws on more than one pago, La Gitana is a Manzanilla and San Emilio a sweet Pedro Ximénez.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qygjzzmw","q":"Valdespino tells of a knight rewarded with a vineyard at Jerez by the Castilian king. What year does the house give for that reward?","opts":["1430","1264","1999","1883"],"a":1,"exp":"The house places the reward in 1264, when Jerez was taken; 1430 is the year of its first recorded wine trade, 1883 its royal appointment and 1999 the year it passed to the Estévez group.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Grupo Estévez: Valdespino bottles Fino Inocente en rama","url":"https://www.grupoestevez.com/valdespino-refuerza-la-autenticidad-de-su-fino-inocente-embotellandolo-exclusivamente-en-rama/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Grupo Estévez: Inocente en Rama","url":"https://www.grupoestevez.com/tienda/inocente/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Valdespino","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valdespino","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Valdespino","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/valdespino/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Liberty Wines: Fino Inocente, Pago de Macharnudo","url":"https://www.libertywines.co.uk/our-portfolio/VA801-fino-inocente-pago-de-macharnudo","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-rcda4uxb": {"id":"fl-rcda4uxb","key":"lustau","group":"jerez","region":"jerez","producer":"Lustau","producerId":"p-lustau","say":"loos-TAOW, PEH-droh hee-MEH-neth","place":"Jerez de la Frontera, Jerez-Xérès-Sherry, Andalusia","founded":"1896","wine":{"name":"Pedro Ximénez San Emilio","appellation":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Pedro Ximénez alone.","sites":"Pedro Ximénez grapes, laid out in the sun after picking until nearly raisins; the house's pages name no single vineyard.","making":"After picking, the bunches are spread in the sun, the asoleo, until they are nearly raisins. Fermentation of the thick, sugary must starts slowly and is halted with wine spirit to keep the grapes' own sweetness. The wine then ages in contact with air in the house's soleras and is bottled at an average age of about twelve years.","glass":"Very dark; raisin, baked fig, prune and treacle over coffee bean, black liquorice and brown sugar. Dense and syrupy, sweet and long.","ageing":"About twelve years of oxidative ageing in the solera have done their work by bottling, so there is no need to cellar it.","serve":"12 to 14 degrees Celsius, slightly chilled; the decanter can stay on the shelf; a white wine glass, in small measures.","label":[{"term":"Pedro Ximénez","means":"The grape, and a style: sweet sherry from sun dried Pedro Ximénez."},{"term":"Solera Familiar","means":"The house's range of family soleras, earlier sold as Solera Reserva."},{"term":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry","means":"Sherry's Denominación de Origen, written in Spanish, French and English."}]},"table":"Sun dried until nearly raisins, the grapes of Lustau's San Emilio give a Pedro Ximénez whose fermentation is stopped early with wine spirit, then aged about twelve years in solera. Poured lightly chilled beside dark chocolate, it is thick and very sweet, with fig, raisin and coffee.","story":"Lustau began in 1896 with a court clerk in Jerez who, in the house's telling, grew vines on his family's estate, the Finca Nuestra Señora de la Esperanza, and aged wine in his spare time to sell to the large exporting houses: an almacenista, a stockholder who ages sherry but does not ship it. In 1931 his daughter bought a bodega near the centre of Jerez and moved the soleras there, and her husband later took the business into the old Santiago quarter of the city; the firm came to carry his name, Lustau. In 1945 it began to step out of the almacenista's role with brands of its own. In 1981 it turned its past into a range, bottling the soleras of small almacenistas under their own names, and in 1990 it joined the Caballero group, a Spanish family company of wines and spirits. San Emilio, its Pedro Ximénez, sits at the far end of the sherry scale from the flor wines. Use it to teach that the sweetness of a Pedro Ximénez comes from the sun on the grapes, not from sugar added to the wine.","others":[{"name":"Almacenista range","note":"Single soleras of small stockholders, bottled under the almacenista's own name since 1981."},{"name":"East India Solera","note":"A cream of Oloroso and Pedro Ximénez, recalling the casks that once crossed to the East Indies in the warm holds of ships."},{"name":"Puerto Fino","note":"Fino aged in El Puerto de Santa María, the third town of the sherry triangle."}],"traps":["An almacenista ages and stores sherry but does not bottle or ship it; Lustau began as one before it shipped under its own name.","The sweetness of Pedro Ximénez comes from sun dried grapes and a fermentation halted early, not from sugar added afterwards.","San Emilio carries an average age of about twelve years; it is not a certified VOS or VORS, which require twenty and thirty."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-92s2xb6u","front":"Lustau's San Emilio: how are its grapes treated before pressing?","back":"Laid out in the sun until nearly raisins, the asoleo, which concentrates their sugar."},{"id":"fl-yhf0lk3t","front":"What was Lustau's founder before the house shipped wine under its own name?","back":"An almacenista: a court clerk who aged sherry on his estate and sold it to the large exporting houses."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-xbph9cnu","q":"Where does the sweetness of Lustau's San Emilio come from?","opts":["Cane sugar added to a dry Oloroso","A Fino blended with concentrated must","Sun dried grapes and a fermentation halted with spirit","Grapes left to freeze on the vine"],"a":2,"exp":"The Pedro Ximénez is dried in the sun until nearly raisins and wine spirit stops the fermentation, keeping the grapes' own sugar; nothing is added to a dry wine afterwards.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-jmk63u09","q":"Lustau made the almacenista visible by bottling small stockholders' soleras under their own names. When did it begin?","opts":["1896","1931","1945","1981"],"a":3,"exp":"The Almacenista range began in 1981; 1896 is the founding, 1931 the move to a bodega near the centre of Jerez, and 1945 the start of brands of its own.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Lustau: About Lustau","url":"https://lustau.es/en/about-lustau/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Lustau: Pedro Ximénez San Emilio","url":"https://lustau.es/en/coleccion/sherry-wines,solera-familiar-en/pedro-ximenez-san-emilio/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Sherry Wines, the Consejo Regulador: Pedro Ximénez","url":"https://www.sherry.wine/sherry-wine/naturally-sweet-sherry-wine/pedro-ximenez","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Lustau history","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/g/1284/lustau/lustau-history","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decántalo: Lustau Pedro Ximénez San Emilio","url":"https://www.decantalo.com/us/en/lustau-pedro-ximenez-san-emilio.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-jipm1par": {"id":"fl-jipm1par","key":"harveys","group":"jerez","region":"jerez","producer":"Harveys","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Jerez, Jerez-Xérès-Sherry, Andalusia, for a Bristol merchant","founded":"1796","wine":{"name":"Harveys Bristol Cream","appellation":"Jerez-Xérès-Sherry","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Palomino, the great majority, with Pedro Ximénez for sweetness.","sites":"Palomino and Pedro Ximénez of the sherry country around Jerez, aged there in solera; the house names no single vineyard.","making":"A blend of more than thirty wines, Fino, Amontillado, Oloroso and Pedro Ximénez among them, each aged by the solera system in American oak butts, for at least three years on the house's word. The dry wines give it nuttiness and the Pedro Ximénez its sweetness.","glass":"Amber to mahogany; prune, toffee and toasted hazelnut over caramel, praline and a hint of chestnut. Sweet and silky, with a nutty finish drier than the nose suggests.","ageing":"A solera blend, meant to be opened and poured rather than laid down.","serve":"12 degrees Celsius in a white wine glass, as the house advises, or over ice with a slice of orange; a decanter is not needed.","label":[{"term":"Harveys","means":"The Bristol wine merchant whose name the blend has carried since it was first sold."},{"term":"Bristol Cream","means":"A richer, sweeter blend than the Bristol Milk that came before it."}]},"table":"First sold in 1882 by a Bristol merchant, Harveys Bristol Cream blends more than thirty wines aged in solera in Jerez: Fino, Amontillado and Oloroso, sweetened with Pedro Ximénez. Pour it at 12 degrees Celsius in a wine glass, or over ice with orange.","story":"A Bristol merchant began trading wine in Denmark Street in 1796, and in 1822 the firm took on an apprentice whose family name it would come to carry: Harvey. Sweet sherry had long gone by the old Bristol name of Bristol Milk, and the firm sold one. Two brothers of the Harvey family later blended a richer, smoother sherry, first sold in 1882, and it is said that a lady visitor, offered the Milk and then the new wine, said that if that was the milk, this must be the cream. The house dates its Royal Warrant to 1895 and, as it tells it, it dressed its wines in the blue glass of Bristol. Since the early 1970s, according to the house, Bristol Cream has been blended in Jerez, from more than thirty wines aged in solera, yet it kept the name of the English port that first sold it. Behind the name lies the answer to what a cream sherry is: dry sherries of the solera, Oloroso among them, sweetened with Pedro Ximénez, a style the Consejo Regulador names Cream and serves at 10 to 12 degrees Celsius.","others":[{"name":"Harveys Very Old Amontillado VORS","note":"A medium Amontillado certified at thirty years of average age or more; the house's English pages call it Fine Old Amontillado."},{"name":"Harveys Palo Cortado VORS","note":"Palo Cortado of the same VORS category, thirty years of average age and more."}],"traps":["Bristol Cream is a sherry blended from wines of the Jerez soleras; Bristol is the merchant's city, which gave the blend its name, not a place where its grapes grow.","A cream sherry is dry sherry sweetened, chiefly with Pedro Ximénez; it is not a Pedro Ximénez, nor a wine made with cream.","Bristol Milk came first; Bristol Cream was the richer blend that followed, first sold in 1882."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ip8trvbs","front":"Harveys Bristol Cream: what goes into the blend?","back":"More than thirty wines, Fino, Amontillado, Oloroso and Pedro Ximénez among them, each aged in solera in American oak."},{"id":"fl-ura2vf1a","front":"In the house's tale, how did Bristol Cream get its name?","back":"A lady visitor, offered Bristol Milk and then the new blend, said that if that was the milk, this must be the cream."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-sf2myrzj","q":"Which description of Harveys Bristol Cream would you give a guest who asks what kind of sherry it is?","opts":["A blend of dry sherries with Pedro Ximénez","A Pedro Ximénez of one solera alone","An Oloroso with nothing added","A Manzanilla sweetened with grape must"],"a":0,"exp":"Bristol Cream blends more than thirty wines, Fino, Amontillado and Oloroso among them, with Pedro Ximénez for sweetness; an Oloroso alone would be dry.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-iovqrq4r","q":"Bristol Cream was first sold by a Bristol merchant in 1882. What was the name of the sweet sherry it followed?","opts":["Bristol Brown","Bristol Milk","Bristol Sack","Bristol Pale"],"a":1,"exp":"Sweet sherry in Bristol went by the old name of Bristol Milk; the story goes that a visitor called the richer new blend the cream.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Harveys: Harveys Bristol Cream Sherry","url":"https://harveyssherry.com/harveys-bristol-cream.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Harveys: Frequently asked questions","url":"https://harveyssherry.com/faqs.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Harveys: Historia, Vinos de Jerez Harveys","url":"https://harveys.es/harveys-historia-descubre/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: John Harvey and Sons","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Harvey_%26_Sons","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Atlas Obscura: Bristol Milk","url":"https://www.atlasobscura.com/foods/bristol-milk","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-n0vfuew7": {"id":"fl-n0vfuew7","key":"pazo-de-senorans","group":"rias-baixas","region":"rias-baixas","producer":"Pazo de Señorans","producerId":"p-pazo-de-senorans","say":"PAH-thoh deh sen-yoh-RAHNS","place":"Vilanoviña, Meis, Val do Salnés, Rías Baixas, Galicia","founded":"1989","wine":{"name":"Pazo Señorans Selección de Añada","appellation":"Rías Baixas","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Albariño alone, as a wine labelled Rías Baixas Albariño must be.","sites":"Os Bancales, a single plot of old Albariño on the slope behind the sixteenth century pazo at Vilanoviña, in Meis, on granite; the vines average more than forty five years by the house's sheet.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel with no malolactic fermentation, the wine then rests on its lees in steel tanks for more than thirty months, as the house's sheet gives, and sees no oak at any stage. It spends at least a year more in bottle before it is released.","glass":"Pale straw with green glints; white peach, ripe lemon and bay leaf over sea salt, with a broad, dense body built by the long lees, bright acidity and a saline length.","ageing":"It comes to market ready, several years after the harvest; the house's own sheet gives it five years more at its peak, and it can hold for a decade.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, a little warmer than a young Albariño; it needs no decanter, though it opens in a wide white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Selección de Añada","means":"A selection of one vintage, held for years on its lees before release; a dry wine, never a late harvest."},{"term":"Rías Baixas","means":"The Denominación de Origen of the Galician coast, ratified in 1988; Meis lies in its Val do Salnés subzone."},{"term":"Albariño","means":"Under the Rías Baixas rules a wine named for the grape is Albariño alone."}]},"table":"Pazo de Señorans holds its Selección de Añada on the lees in steel for thirty months and more, then a year in bottle, never in oak, so this Albariño from Meis comes to the table broad and saline, and it shows how well the grape can age.","story":"Pazo de Señorans takes its name from a pazo, a Galician manor house, that it dates to the sixteenth century, at Vilanoviña in the parish of Meis, in the Val do Salnés. On 11 October 1980 the Spanish government recognised the Denominación Específica Albariño. Because the law of the day would not let a denomination carry the name of a grape, the regulating council recalls, it was ratified as the Denominación de Origen Rías Baixas in July 1988, with Val do Salnés among its first three subzones. The family that held the pazo began making wine there in 1989, and the estate also has its own distillery for orujo, the spirit of the pressed skins. Where Albariño is mostly bottled young, the family set aside the fruit of a single plot of old vines behind the house and kept its wine in steel on its lees for more than thirty months, then a year in bottle, to show that the grape could age: that wine is Selección de Añada. Pour it for a guest who thinks of Albariño as a summer white. It is the grape given years, broader and saltier, and it belongs beside turbot or scallops rather than with an aperitif.","others":[{"name":"Pazo Señorans Albariño","note":"The house's young wine, bottled each year from the estate's Albariño of the Val do Salnés."},{"name":"Pazo Señorans Aguardiente de Orujo","note":"A spirit distilled from the pressed skins in the estate's own distillery."}],"traps":["Selección de Añada is a dry Albariño held for years on its lees, never a late harvest or a sweet wine.","Its long ageing is in steel tanks, not in oak barrels: the breadth comes from the lees, not from wood.","Pazo is Galician for a country manor house; on a label it names the house, not a style or a rank."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-njn1ovxi","front":"Pazo de Señorans Selección de Añada: what long rest comes before its release?","back":"More than thirty months in stainless steel tanks, with no oak, and then at least a year in bottle."},{"id":"fl-292kccfa","front":"Pazo de Señorans: what is the house named for?","back":"A pazo, a Galician manor house of the sixteenth century, at Vilanoviña in the parish of Meis."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-wguyqzsl","q":"Pazo de Señorans Selección de Añada never sees oak, yet it is broad and dense. What gives it that breadth?","opts":["Grapes left late on the vine to shrivel","A share of Treixadura from Condado do Tea","More than thirty months on its lees in steel","A year of ageing in new French oak barrels"],"a":2,"exp":"The house keeps it on its lees in stainless steel for over thirty months, then a year in bottle; it never sees a barrel, and it is Albariño alone, picked for a dry wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1dn8fthh","q":"In which year did the family begin making wine at Pazo de Señorans, in Meis?","opts":["1928","1980","1973","1989"],"a":3,"exp":"Wine was first made at the pazo in 1989, the year after Rías Baixas became a Denominación de Origen; 1980 is the year of the Denominación Específica Albariño, 1928 of Fefiñanes's brand and 1973 of Do Ferreiro's first wine.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Pazo de Señorans online shop: Selección de Añada","url":"https://shop.pazodesenorans.com/productos/ver/24/pazo-senorans-seleccion-de-anada-2013-075-litros-caixa-de-6-botellas..html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Pazo de Señorans: guided visit information","url":"https://www.pazodesenorans.com/PDF/Wine-Tourism.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Rías Baixas: Our history","url":"https://doriasbaixas.com/en/our-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"European Cellars: Pazo de Señorans Albariño Selección de Añada","url":"https://www.europeancellars.com/wine/albarino-seleccion-anada/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Scholar Guild: Pazo de Señorans producer guide","url":"https://www.winescholarguild.com/producer-guide/spain/galicia/1259-pazo-de-senorans","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-tzbbjwr4": {"id":"fl-tzbbjwr4","key":"fefinanes","group":"rias-baixas","region":"rias-baixas","producer":"Palacio de Fefiñanes","producerId":"p-fefinanes","say":"feh-fee-NYAH-nehs","place":"Plaza de Fefiñanes, Cambados, Val do Salnés, Rías Baixas, Galicia","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Albariño de Fefiñanes","appellation":"Rías Baixas","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Albariño alone, from Cambados in the Val do Salnés.","sites":"Albariño from vineyards around Cambados, on the Arousa inlet in the Val do Salnés, brought to the cellar inside the palace on the town square.","making":"It ferments at a low temperature in stainless steel vats, with no oak, as the house's own sheet gives, and is bottled before the middle of the year after the harvest, to be sold and drunk young.","glass":"Lemon pith, green apple and acacia blossom over crushed shell and a touch of bay; light in body, its bright, crisp acidity leaving the mouth clean and salty.","ageing":"Made to be drunk young, in the year or two after bottling; for an Albariño of Fefiñanes with more time behind it, the house makes its III Año.","serve":"About 10 degrees Celsius, as the house's sheet advises; straight from the bottle with no decanter, into a medium white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Albariño de Fefiñanes","means":"The brand the house created and registered in 1928, with a label drawn that year."},{"term":"Bodegas del Palacio de Fefiñanes","means":"The cellar inside the palace of the viscounts of Fefiñanes, on the square of Cambados."},{"term":"Rías Baixas","means":"Galicia's coastal Denominación de Origen since 1988, whose Val do Salnés subzone takes in Cambados."}]},"table":"Inside the old palace of the viscounts of Fefiñanes, on the square of Cambados, the house makes Albariño de Fefiñanes, a brand registered in 1928. Cool fermented in steel and bottled young, it tastes of lemon pith, apple and salt, a wine for oysters.","story":"The palace of Fefiñanes stands on the square of Cambados, by the Arousa inlet. Its building, begun by one family in the sixteenth century, was carried on, as the house tells it, by a son of the estate born there in 1583 who served the king and became the first Viscount of Fefiñanes, and it was finished in the seventeenth century. Wine has been made in the palace since that century, by the house's account, and in the first years of the twentieth its cellar became a business. In 1928 the house created and registered the brand Albariño de Fefiñanes and released its first vintage under it, with a label drawn that year by a jeweller and jet carver of Santiago de Compostela; it was one of the first white wines of the region to carry a commercial label. In 1929, the house records, the wine won an award at the international exhibition in Barcelona. In 1988 the new Denominación de Origen Rías Baixas placed Cambados in its Val do Salnés subzone, and in 1995 the house first made a barrel fermented Albariño named for the viscount's birth year, 1583. Albariño de Fefiñanes stays the plain one: steel, no oak, bottled young, the Salnés at its simplest, under a name and a label of 1928.","others":[{"name":"1583 Albariño de Fefiñanes","note":"Fermented in used Bordeaux barrels of French oak and briefly on its lees, first made in 1995."},{"name":"Albariño de Fefiñanes III Año","note":"Fermented in steel, then held long in tank, part of that time on its lees, and sold in its third year."}],"traps":["The figure 1583 is the birth year of the first Viscount of Fefiñanes, not a vintage and not the date of the palace.","III Año means the wine is sold in its third year after long ageing in tank, not that it spent three years in bottle.","The plain Albariño de Fefiñanes sees no oak; the barrels belong to 1583 Albariño de Fefiñanes."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-kyna1kxo","front":"Albariño de Fefiñanes: what happened to the brand and its label in 1928?","back":"The house created and registered the brand, a Santiago jeweller and jet carver drew the label, and the first vintage was released under it."},{"id":"fl-i5042anh","front":"What does the figure 1583 on a Fefiñanes label stand for?","back":"The birth year of the first Viscount of Fefiñanes; it names the house's barrel fermented Albariño."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-qsgzk8fx","q":"Which of these is how the plain Albariño de Fefiñanes is made?","opts":["Fermented cool in steel and bottled young","Blended with Treixadura from Condado do Tea","Fermented in French oak with stirred lees","Held thirty months in tank before release"],"a":0,"exp":"The house ferments it at a low temperature in stainless steel and bottles it before the middle of the next year; the barrels are for 1583 and the long tank ageing for III Año.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-9o6a8lx6","q":"The Fefiñanes cellar created and registered its brand, Albariño de Fefiñanes, with a label drawn the same year. Which year was it?","opts":["1988","1928","1583","1929"],"a":1,"exp":"The brand, its label and its first vintage all date from 1928; 1929 is the year of its Barcelona award, 1988 of the Denominación de Origen and 1583 of the first viscount's birth.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bodegas del Palacio de Fefiñanes: Albariño de Fefiñanes","url":"https://www.fefinanes.com/en/wine/albarino-de-fefinanes/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bodegas del Palacio de Fefiñanes: Albariño de Fefiñanes 2023 technical data","url":"https://www.fefinanes.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Albari%C3%B1o-de_Fefi%C3%B1anes-Ficha-de-Cata-ENG.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bodegas del Palacio de Fefiñanes: 1583 Albariño de Fefiñanes","url":"https://www.fefinanes.com/en/wine/1583-albarino-de-fefinanes/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Spanish Wine Lover: Bodega Palacio de Fefiñanes","url":"https://spanishwinelover.com/winerie/palacio-de-fefinanes","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Kysela Père et Fils: Bodegas del Palacio de Fefiñanes","url":"https://www.kysela.com/bodegas-del-palacio-de-fefinanes.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-g751c2zk": {"id":"fl-g751c2zk","key":"martin-codax","group":"rias-baixas","region":"rias-baixas","producer":"Martín Códax","producerId":"","say":"mar-TEEN KOH-dahks","place":"Cambados, Val do Salnés, Rías Baixas, Galicia","founded":"1985","wine":{"name":"Martín Códax Albariño","appellation":"Rías Baixas","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Albariño alone.","sites":"Small plots worked by the member growers in the heart of the Val do Salnés, the cool, coastal subzone around Cambados.","making":"The growers pick by hand, and the grapes come in from their plots in the heart of the Val do Salnés. After fermentation the wine is kept for a time on its lees, which the house says balances the aromas of the lees with the grape's own, and it is bottled young.","glass":"Lemon, pink grapefruit and white peach with a touch of rose petal; light and bright, brisk in acidity and faintly salty at the close.","ageing":"Drink it within a year or two of the harvest, while the fruit and the sea air in it are at their brightest.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no decanter; a standard white wine glass, with shellfish, grilled fish or tapas.","label":[{"term":"Martín Códax","means":"A Galician jongleur of the thirteenth century whose seven love songs of the Vigo sea survive, six with their music."},{"term":"Albariño","means":"The grape, and the whole of the wine: a Rías Baixas label that names Albariño holds no other."},{"term":"Rías Baixas","means":"The Denominación de Origen of Galicia's southern coast, ratified in July 1988."}]},"table":"Martín Códax is the Cambados winery founded in 1985 by fifty growers of the Val do Salnés and named for a medieval Galician poet; its Albariño is bright, lemony and lightly salty, made to pour young with shellfish.","story":"Martín Códax was a jongleur of the thirteenth century, thought to have come from Vigo, whose seven cantigas de amigo give voice to a girl waiting by the sea of Vigo for a lover who has gone. The songs survive with their music, six of the seven notated, on a single parchment that a Madrid antiquarian bookseller found in the early twentieth century, and it is held in the Morgan Library in New York. In 1985, by the house's history, fifty growers of the Val do Salnés joined their grapes and their capital to build a winery at Cambados. Its first Albariño, which carried the poet's name, followed in 1986, from plots in the heart of the Salnés. Two years later, in July 1988, Rías Baixas was ratified as a Denominación de Origen, grown out of the Denominación Específica Albariño of 1980, and the growers' winery grew with it. Its later labels keep the poet close: Vindel is named for the parchment that holds his songs. Martín Códax Albariño is the grape's young, bright face, and the name on its label is that of a medieval singer of the sea.","others":[{"name":"Martín Códax Lías","note":"Albariño stirred on its fine lees for six to seven months, then held up to a year in tank before bottling."},{"name":"Martín Códax Vindel","note":"An Albariño named for the parchment that holds the poet's seven songs."}],"traps":["Martín Códax is a growers' winery at Cambados, not one family's estate, and the name on the label is a medieval poet's, not a founder's.","Martín Códax Albariño is Albariño alone; a wine labelled with the Val do Salnés subzone may hold other approved grapes for up to three tenths."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-5nrfowir","front":"Who was Martín Códax, whose name the Cambados winery took?","back":"A Galician jongleur of the thirteenth century, whose seven love songs of the Vigo sea survive with music for six of them."},{"id":"fl-fo6hsmw9","front":"Martín Códax: when was the winery founded, and by whom?","back":"In 1985, by fifty growers of the Val do Salnés who pooled their grapes and their capital."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-houomcvo","q":"A guest asks which grapes are in Martín Códax Albariño. What do you answer?","opts":["Godello with a little Albariño","Albariño with Treixadura","Albariño alone","Albariño with Loureira and Caíño Blanco"],"a":2,"exp":"A Rías Baixas wine named for Albariño must be that grape alone; Loureira and Caíño Blanco belong to O Rosal blends, and Treixadura to those of Condado do Tea.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-71gmci8z","q":"The seven songs of Martín Códax survive with their music on one parchment. Where is it held?","opts":["The monastery of Armenteira","The town hall of Cambados","The cathedral archive of Santiago","The Morgan Library in New York"],"a":3,"exp":"The parchment, found by a Madrid bookseller in the early twentieth century and notated for six of the songs, is held in the Morgan Library in New York, far from the coast it sings of.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bodegas Martín Códax: Winery","url":"https://www.martincodax.com/en/winery/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bodegas Martín Códax: Our wines","url":"https://www.martincodax.com/en/our-wines/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter China: Martín Códax, Atlantic Albariño","url":"https://www.decanterchina.com/en/promotion/martin-codax-atlantic-albari-o","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Martin Codax","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_Codax","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"OMI facsimiles: the Vindel parchment of Martin Codax","url":"https://omifacsimiles.com/brochures/vindel.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-z1gpjqo5": {"id":"fl-z1gpjqo5","key":"do-ferreiro","group":"rias-baixas","region":"rias-baixas","producer":"Do Ferreiro","producerId":"p-do-ferreiro","say":"doh feh-RAY-roh, THEH-pahs VEH-lyahs","place":"Lores, Meaño, Val do Salnés, Rías Baixas, Galicia","founded":"1973","wine":{"name":"Do Ferreiro Cepas Vellas","appellation":"Rías Baixas","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Albariño alone, from very old vines.","sites":"One plot of Albariño in front of the family house at Lores, in Meaño, trained high on pergolas, its vines more than two hundred years old by the family's account.","making":"Native yeasts ferment it in stainless steel tanks, and it is then raised for about a year on its fine lees before it is bottled, the span moving a little with the vintage.","glass":"Quince, ripe pear and lemon peel over sea salt and beeswax, with a dense, textured body, firm acidity and a long, saline finish.","ageing":"Firm and reserved in its first years, it rewards time in bottle; its importer gives it ten to twenty years in a good cellar.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, not colder; let a young bottle open in a carafe or in the glass; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Cepas Vellas","means":"Galician for old vines: here one plot of very old Albariño, not a legal category."},{"term":"Do Ferreiro","means":"Galician for the blacksmith's, the name the family gave its wine."},{"term":"Rías Baixas","means":"The appellation, ratified in 1988; Meaño is part of its Val do Salnés subzone."}]},"table":"At Lores, in Meaño, Do Ferreiro makes Cepas Vellas from one plot of Albariño on pergolas the family dates back more than two centuries. Native yeasts and a long rest on its lees give a dense, salty white built to last.","story":"Ferreiro is Galician for blacksmith, and Do Ferreiro, the blacksmith's, honours the family's old trade at the forge, as its importer tells it. At Lores, in Meaño in the Val do Salnés, the family grew Albariño, and in 1973 the founder began making wine at the family house. The founder, by the same account, was among those who founded the Denominación de Origen Rías Baixas in 1988, fourteen wineries in all by the regulating council's count. In front of the house grows the plot behind Cepas Vellas: Albariño that the family dates to the eighteenth century, its vines more than two hundred years old, trained high on pergolas so that the Atlantic wind can dry the bunches. Its grapes are kept apart from the family's younger vines, fermented with the native yeasts of the vineyard and given about a year on their fine lees, so that the wine gains texture and salt rather than perfume. The house holds to the pergola, the old way of the Salnés. Cepas Vellas shows what age gives Albariño twice over: a single plot of very old vines, dense and saline, whose wine repays years in the cellar.","others":[{"name":"Do Ferreiro Albariño","note":"The estate wine, from the family's younger granite plots around Meaño and its neighbouring villages."}],"traps":["Cepas Vellas is the house's name for this plot's wine, not a legal category of Rías Baixas.","Albariño is trained high on pergolas here to fight the Atlantic damp, so that air moves under the canopy, not to raise the yield.","A list may print the company's name, which is the founder's own, rather than Do Ferreiro: it is the same Meaño house."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-sv3ndh3m","front":"Do Ferreiro Cepas Vellas: what does the name mean, and what does it describe?","back":"Galician for old vines: one plot in front of the family house at Lores, its vines more than two centuries old by the family's account."},{"id":"fl-pfs4u0nw","front":"Do Ferreiro: how is the old vine Albariño fermented and raised?","back":"With native yeasts in stainless steel, then about a year on its fine lees before bottling."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-7stydbeo","q":"How are the old vines behind Do Ferreiro Cepas Vellas trained?","opts":["Along terraces walled in schist","High on pergolas, against the Atlantic damp","Low as free standing bush vines","On wires close to warm slate"],"a":1,"exp":"The old Albariño of Lores is trained high on pergolas so that the sea wind can dry the bunches; bush vines and slate or schist terraces belong to drier, warmer places.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-acbs9mky","q":"A family of growers at Lores began making wine at its house in 1973 and called it Do Ferreiro. What does the name mean?","opts":["The blacksmith's","The ferryman's","The iron gate","The old mill"],"a":0,"exp":"Ferreiro is Galician for blacksmith, the family's old trade by its importer's account; the name has nothing to do with a ferry, a gate or a mill.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"De Maison Selections: Do Ferreiro","url":"https://demaisonselections.com/producer/do-ferreiro/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"De Maison Selections: Do Ferreiro Albariño Cepas Vellas","url":"https://demaisonselections.com/portfolio-items/df-cepasvellas/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bodeboca: Albariño Do Ferreiro Cepas Vellas 2022","url":"https://www.bodeboca.com/vino/albarino-do-ferreiro-cepas-vellas-2022","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinissimus: Do Ferreiro Cepas Vellas","url":"https://www.vinissimus.com/en/wine/do-ferreiro-cepas-vellas","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Rías Baixas: The evolution of the D.O. since 1980","url":"https://doriasbaixas.com/la-evolucion-de-la-d-o-rias-baixas-desde-1980-hasta-la-actualidad/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-jpf19k0r": {"id":"fl-jpf19k0r","key":"terras-gauda","group":"rias-baixas","region":"rias-baixas","producer":"Terras Gauda","producerId":"","say":"TEH-rrahs GOW-dah","place":"O Rosal, by the Miño, Rías Baixas, Galicia","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Terras Gauda O Rosal","appellation":"Rías Baixas","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Albariño, about seven tenths, with Caíño Blanco and Loureira for the rest, their shares changing with the year.","sites":"Vineyards at O Rosal, near the mouth of the Miño by the Portuguese border; the subzone's rules ask for at least seven tenths Albariño and Loureira, with every grape grown in O Rosal.","making":"Picked by hand, each variety is kept apart, given a cold soak before fermentation whose length suits the grape, and fermented cool in stainless steel with native yeasts from the house's own vineyards. The three wines are then blended, cold stabilised, filtered and bottled young.","glass":"Pear, apple and ripe peach with grapefruit and a balsamic, minty lift over a stony, salty frame; rounder than a Salnés Albariño, its lively acidity keeping it fresh to the end.","ageing":"Open and bright on release and at its freshest in the first two or three years, though its acidity carries it some years more.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius, poured as it comes, without a carafe, into a white wine glass, beside octopus, clams or grilled sardines.","label":[{"term":"O Rosal","means":"A Rías Baixas subzone by the Miño; a wine labelled with it holds at least seventy per cent Albariño and Loureira, all grown there."},{"term":"Rías Baixas","means":"The Denominación de Origen ratified in July 1988, with O Rosal among its three first subzones."}]},"table":"Where the Miño meets the Atlantic, Terras Gauda blends its O Rosal: mostly Albariño, with Loureira and Caíño Blanco, a rare grape the house set out to restore. Pear, apple and a minty lift, served cool, suit it to octopus.","story":"O Rosal lies in the south of Rías Baixas, where the Miño meets the Atlantic at the Portuguese border. It was one of the three first subzones of the Denominación de Origen of July 1988, and its rules allow a blend: at least seventy per cent Albariño and Loureira, with other authorised grapes beside them, every one grown in the subzone. Terras Gauda was founded there by a Galician family at the turn of the 1990s, in 1989 by one account of its importer and 1990 by another. Its founders set out to restore Caíño Blanco, a white grape of the valley that had become rare, and made it a partner of Albariño and Loureira in their O Rosal. The house picks and ferments each variety apart, with yeasts from its own vineyards, and blends the three before bottling, so the wine shows the subzone rather than one grape. A label that names Albariño holds that grape alone; Terras Gauda O Rosal teaches the other legal path, a subzone blend, and a guest who asks why the bottle does not say Albariño should hear about Caíño Blanco.","others":[{"name":"Terras Gauda Etiqueta Negra","note":"The same three grapes of O Rosal, fermented in French oak and then aged on their lees."}],"traps":["Terras Gauda O Rosal is a blend, not a varietal Albariño: a label naming the O Rosal subzone may hold Loureira, Caíño Blanco and other local grapes.","O Rosal blends Albariño with Loureira by the rules, while Condado do Tea pairs it with Treixadura; do not swap the two subzones' partners."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ciatmddx","front":"Terras Gauda O Rosal: which two grapes join Albariño in the blend?","back":"Caíño Blanco and Loureira, in shares that change with the year."},{"id":"fl-bxzmygok","front":"Terras Gauda's O Rosal label: what must the wine hold, by the subzone's rules?","back":"At least seventy per cent Albariño and Loureira, other authorised grapes for the rest, and every grape grown in O Rosal."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-5x8fw8ch","q":"How does Terras Gauda make its O Rosal blend?","opts":["Albariño alone, aged a year on stirred lees","Grapes dried on racks before pressing","Each grape fermented apart in steel, then blended","All three fermented together in new oak"],"a":2,"exp":"Each variety is picked and fermented on its own, cool in stainless steel with native yeasts, and the three are blended before bottling; oak is kept for Etiqueta Negra.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-yusmalxl","q":"Which rare white grape did the founders of Terras Gauda set out to restore at O Rosal?","opts":["Treixadura","Torrontés","Godello","Caíño Blanco"],"a":3,"exp":"The house restored Caíño Blanco and blends it with Albariño and Loureira; Treixadura is the partner grape that the rules name for Condado do Tea instead.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Trinchero Family Wine and Spirits: Terras Gauda","url":"https://www.tfewines.com/brands/terras-gauda/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Interempresas: recovering varieties, the challenge of Terras Gauda","url":"https://www.interempresas.net/Quimica/101382-Recuperar-variedades-para-elaborar-vinos-'unicos'-el-reto-de-Terras-Gauda.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bodegas Terras Gauda: Terras Gauda 2023 tasting notes","url":"https://www.terrasgauda.com/en/terras_gauda/fichas_de_cata/Tasting%20Notes%20Terras%20Gauda%202023.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Enterwine: Terras Gauda 2024","url":"https://www.enterwine.com/en/terras-gauda-2024","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Consejo Regulador Rías Baixas: The wines of D.O. Rías Baixas","url":"https://doriasbaixas.com/en/the-wines-of-d-o-rias-baixas/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-iyctgtd5": {"id":"fl-iyctgtd5","key":"taylors","group":"douro","region":"douro","producer":"Taylor's","producerId":"p-taylor-fladgate","say":"","place":"Vila Nova de Gaia and Quinta de Vargellas, Porto, Douro","founded":"1692","wine":{"name":"Taylor's Vintage Port","appellation":"Porto","classification":"Vintage, a special category of Port approved by the IVDP tasting chamber","colour":"fortified","grapes":"A field blend of Douro red grapes: Touriga Nacional and Touriga Franca at its core, with Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca, Tinto Cão and others.","sites":"Quinta de Vargellas in the Douro Superior, the backbone of the blend, with Quinta de Terra Feita in the Pinhão valley and, since 2000, a little of Quinta do Junco beside it, all on steep terraced schist.","making":"Grapes from the house's own quintas are trodden by foot in lagares, the granite ones of Vargellas among them, and grape spirit is added partway through fermentation to stop it and keep the wine sweet. The wine then matures in wood for a little under two years, about twenty months for the 2017, before bottling; the rest of its life is spent in bottle.","glass":"Violet, blackberry and cassis over graphite and liquorice; sweet and dense, held by firm, grippy tannin and a long, dry edge. Old bottles turn to dried fig, cedar and leather.","ageing":"Firm and closed when young; a strong declared year wants twenty years or more in bottle and keeps for decades beyond.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; stand the bottle upright for a day, then decant it off its crust; a small wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Vintage","means":"Port of one declared year, approved by the IVDP and bottled young to age in bottle."},{"term":"Porto","means":"The protected name of Port, the fortified wine of the Douro, in Portuguese."},{"term":"Taylor Fladgate","means":"The name the house uses in some markets, the United States among them, for the same Port."}]},"table":"Founded in 1692, Taylor's treads by foot the grapes of its own quintas for its declared Vintage Port, with Vargellas in the Douro Superior as the backbone. Violet and blackberry sit on firm, grippy tannin; lay it down for decades, then decant it off its crust.","story":"Taylor's dates its start to 1692, when an English merchant of the Bearsley family came to Portugal, trading at first in the lean red wine of the Minho rather than Port. In 1744 the house bought its first property in the Douro, near Régua, remembered in its First Estate Port. Partners joined through the nineteenth century, and the house says it took the name Taylor, Fladgate & Yeatman in 1844. Between 1893 and 1896 it bought the three properties of Vargellas in the Douro Superior, two of them from the contractor who had built the railway along the river, and joined them into one quinta. Phylloxera had ruined the vines; a partner of the Yeatman family rebuilt the estate, and by 1908, in the house's telling, its wine was good enough for the Vintage blend. In 1974, in a time of political turmoil in Portugal, Taylor's bought Quinta de Terra Feita in the Pinhão valley, whose wine it had bought since the previous century. The house declares a Classic Vintage only in years it judges strong, announcing it by custom on 23 April of the second year; in other good years Vargellas is bottled alone. Taylor's means firm, slow Vintage Port, a wine for the long haul.","others":[{"name":"Quinta de Vargellas Vintage Port","note":"The single quinta Vintage, from Vargellas grapes alone, bottled in years when no Classic Vintage is declared."},{"name":"Quinta de Vargellas Vinha Velha","note":"A small selection from the old vines of Vargellas, made only in rare years."},{"name":"Taylor's First Estate","note":"A Reserve Port named for the house's first Douro property, bought near Régua in 1744."}],"traps":["A Classic Vintage is declared by the house in strong years; in good undeclared years Quinta de Vargellas is bottled alone, and it is Vintage Port all the same.","Taylor's and Taylor Fladgate are one house: the second name is the one the house uses in some markets, the United States among them.","Vintage Port spends under three years in wood and the rest of its life in bottle, so it throws a crust and must be decanted; a tawny does not."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-oi21eo1k","front":"Before it shipped Port, in what wine did the merchant who began Taylor's first trade?","back":"The lean red wine of the Minho."},{"id":"fl-x9kzvbmb","front":"On what day does Taylor's announce a Classic Vintage declaration, by house custom?","back":"23 April, St George's Day, in the second year after the harvest."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-13oeq067","q":"Which method does Taylor's use at its quintas to make its Vintage Port?","opts":["Treading by foot in lagares","Ageing under flor in a solera","Heating the wine in a tank","Fermenting in new oak barrels"],"a":0,"exp":"Taylor's treads the grapes of its own quintas by foot in lagares and stops the fermentation with grape spirit; flor and soleras belong to sherry, and heated tanks to Madeira.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-s3cc8btu","q":"In which years did Taylor's buy the properties of Vargellas, the quinta that gives the backbone of its Vintage Port?","opts":["1974 to 1976","1893 to 1896","1844 to 1848","1744 to 1748"],"a":1,"exp":"The house bought the three Vargellas properties between 1893 and 1896 and joined them; 1744 brought its first Douro property near Régua, and 1974 Terra Feita.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Taylor's: Our History","url":"https://www.taylor.pt/en/about/our-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Taylor's: Quinta de Vargellas, the winery","url":"https://www.taylor.pt/en/port-wine-vineyards/quinta-de-vargellas/the-vargellas-winery","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Taylor's: Quinta de Terra Feita, the Taylor purchase","url":"https://www.taylor.pt/en/port-wine-vineyards/quinta-de-terra-feita/taylor-purchase","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Taylor's: 2017 Vintage Port","url":"https://www.taylor.pt/en/port-wine/classic-vintage/2017-vintage-port","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Taylor's: Quinta de Vargellas Vintage Port","url":"https://www.taylor.pt/en/port-wine/quinta-de-vargellas-vintage","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-ktlligu2": {"id":"fl-ktlligu2","key":"grahams","group":"douro","region":"douro","producer":"Graham's","producerId":"p-grahams","say":"","place":"Vila Nova de Gaia and Quinta dos Malvedos, Porto, Douro","founded":"1820","wine":{"name":"Graham's Vintage Port","appellation":"Porto","classification":"Vintage, a special category of Port approved by the IVDP tasting chamber","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Touriga Franca and Touriga Nacional at its heart, with Sousão and grapes from old mixed vines, in shares that change with each declared year.","sites":"Quinta dos Malvedos, the house's first vineyard, bought in 1890, high on the north bank of the Douro near Tua, where the Cima Corgo gives way to the Douro Superior, with Quinta do Tua, Quinta das Lages, Vila Velha and Vale de Malhadas, on the Douro's terraced schist.","making":"At the house's winery at Malvedos, robotic lagares built to copy the human foot, installed in 2000, tread the grapes, and grape spirit halts the fermentation. About two years in wood follow at the house's lodge of 1890 in Vila Nova de Gaia, where all its wines are aged, and the wine is bottled by the third year after the harvest, as the rules require.","glass":"Ripe plum, blackberry and violet over dark chocolate and liquorice, with a touch of eucalyptus; rich and sweet through the middle, gripped by firm, ripe tannin and long in the mouth.","ageing":"Rich enough to tempt young, but a strong year needs fifteen to twenty years to open and goes on for decades after.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; after a spell standing upright, pour it off its crust into a decanter an hour or so before serving; small wine glasses.","label":[{"term":"Vintage Port","means":"The IVDP approved Port of a single declared year, in bottle by the third year after the harvest."},{"term":"Porto or Port","means":"The Douro's fortified wine under its protected name, in Portuguese or in English."}]},"table":"At the heart of Graham's Vintage Port is Quinta dos Malvedos, bought in 1890 near Tua on the Douro's north bank by a house founded in 1820. Rich and plummy, it needs years in bottle and a decanter, and it is poured after dinner.","story":"Two brothers of the Graham family were in the textile trade, and in 1820, as the house tells it, they accepted twenty seven barrels of Port in payment of a debt and so found themselves in the wine business. In 1882 a young Scot came out from Glasgow to work for the Grahams; his family, the Symingtons, became Port shippers in their own right. In 1890 the house bought its first vineyard, Quinta dos Malvedos, on a promontory above the Douro near Tua, and its vines became the cornerstone of Graham's Vintage Port; the lodge in Vila Nova de Gaia where the house ages its wines carries the same year in its name. In 1970 the young Scot's grandsons bought Graham's from the Graham family. The Vintage Port is made in the house's own winery at Malvedos, where robotic lagares built to copy the human foot have done the treading since 2000. Only a year the house judges strong becomes a declared Vintage; a good year that falls short is bottled as Malvedos alone. Among the Symington family's Vintage Ports, Graham's is the rich, plummy voice.","others":[{"name":"Graham's Quinta dos Malvedos Vintage Port","note":"Malvedos alone, bottled as a Vintage in good years the house leaves undeclared."},{"name":"Graham's 20 Year Old Tawny","note":"A blend of wines of many years aged in cask at the lodge of 1890, with the character of twenty years in wood."},{"name":"Graham's Six Grapes","note":"A Reserve Ruby of the house, sold ready to drink."}],"traps":["Quinta dos Malvedos on a label is a single quinta Vintage of an undeclared year, not Graham's declared Vintage Port.","Graham's was founded by the Graham brothers in 1820 and bought by the Symington family only in 1970, though a Symington first worked for the house in 1882.","Six Grapes is a Reserve Ruby ready to drink, not a Vintage Port."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-bji2r0iu","front":"Graham's: how did two brothers in the textile trade enter the Port business in 1820?","back":"They accepted twenty seven barrels of Port in payment of a debt, as the house tells it."},{"id":"fl-jh5rabpj","front":"Graham's bought its first vineyard in 1890, near Tua on the Douro's north bank. Which quinta is it?","back":"Quinta dos Malvedos, the heart of the Vintage Port's blend."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-h6u2a7vw","q":"Before bottling, Graham's Vintage Port spends a short time in wood. Where?","opts":["A solera of old butts in Jerez","Heated steel tanks at the quinta","New French barrels at Quinta dos Malvedos","Wood at the house's lodge in Gaia"],"a":3,"exp":"Graham's ages all its wines at its lodge of 1890 in Vila Nova de Gaia; a Vintage spends about two years in wood there before bottling, not in new barrels at the quinta.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rjj9rsrv","q":"The Symington family bought Graham's from the family that founded it. When?","opts":["1890","1882","1970","1820"],"a":2,"exp":"The Symingtons bought Graham's in 1970; 1882 is when their forebear first came to Porto to work for the Grahams, and 1890 when the house bought Malvedos.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Graham's: Heritage","url":"https://www.grahams-port.com/heritage","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Graham's: Winemaking","url":"https://www.grahams-port.com/winemaking","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Graham's: Quinta dos Malvedos vineyard sheet","url":"https://www.grahams-port.com/files/vineyard/015/15_quinta-dos-malvedos-_file.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Graham's: 2017 Vintage Port wine description","url":"https://www.grahams-port.com/files/year/8_2017_file.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"IVDP: Port wines, special categories","url":"https://www.ivdp.pt/en/wines/port-wines/special-categories/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-9temat93": {"id":"fl-9temat93","key":"noval","group":"douro","region":"douro","producer":"Quinta do Noval","producerId":"p-quinta-do-noval","say":"KEEN-tah doo noo-VAHL, nah-syoo-NAHL","place":"Above Pinhão in the Cima Corgo, Porto, Douro","founded":"1715","wine":{"name":"Quinta do Noval Nacional Vintage Port","appellation":"Porto","classification":"Vintage, a special category of Port approved by the IVDP tasting chamber","colour":"fortified","grapes":"A field blend of many Douro varieties planted together, among them Touriga Nacional, Touriga Franca, Tinto Cão, Tinta Roriz and Sousão, every vine on its own ungrafted roots.","sites":"The Nacional parcel, a small plot at the heart of the Noval vineyard on the terraced schist above Pinhão. Its vines stand on their own roots rather than American rootstock and are replanted the same way, so they are not pre-phylloxera survivors.","making":"Foot treading in the quinta's granite lagares, exactly as for the rest of the Noval vineyard, comes first, and a dose of grape spirit cuts the fermentation short. The wine is then held for about eighteen months in large casks of oak and chestnut in the estate's temperature controlled cellars in the Douro before bottling.","glass":"Black cherry, violet and blackberry, with liquorice and dark chocolate beneath, deeper and denser than the estate's other Vintage Port; sweet yet gripped by tight, firm tannin. With age it moves towards dried fig, cedar and tobacco.","ageing":"Slower to open than Noval's own Vintage Port; the house gives it a drinking window of five to a hundred years after bottling, and strong years are kept for decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a day upright, then a gentle decanting off the crust shortly before serving; a small glass for wine.","label":[{"term":"Nacional","means":"The ungrafted parcel inside Quinta do Noval; the house reads the name as Portuguese vines in Portuguese soil."},{"term":"Quinta","means":"A wine estate in Portugal; here the Noval estate above Pinhão."},{"term":"Vintage Port","means":"A declared year's Port, bottled young so that it ages in the bottle; Nacional is declared apart."}]},"table":"Dense and slow to open, Quinta do Noval Nacional is a Vintage Port trodden by foot and declared only when the house judges a year worthy of it, from a small parcel of ungrafted vines at the heart of the Noval estate above Pinhão.","story":"The name Noval first appears in the land registers in 1715. Phylloxera left the vineyard in ruins; in 1894 a Port shipper bought the estate and replanted it, and his son in law went on to widen its narrow old terraces so that the vines could make better use of the land and the sun. Inside the vineyard a small plot was planted on its own roots, ungrafted; the house says its name, Nacional, means Portuguese vines in Portuguese soil, with no foreign rootstock. Noval made its name in 1931, on the house's own word, by declaring both a Vintage Port and a Nacional in a year when most shippers did not, with the world in depression and the large 1927 vintage unsold. In 1981 a fire destroyed the house's lodge, offices and records in Vila Nova de Gaia, and the stocks were later moved to a temperature controlled cellar on the quinta. The family that had held Noval since 1894 sold it in 1993, and it joined AXA Millésimes. Nacional is declared on its own judgement: in 1996 it was declared when Noval was not, and in 2007 Noval was declared without it. It is the Douro's lesson in what ungrafted vines can give.","others":[{"name":"Quinta do Noval Vintage Port","note":"The estate's Vintage from the rest of the vineyard, declared on its own judgement apart from the Nacional."},{"name":"Quinta do Noval Colheita","note":"A tawny from a single harvest, aged long in cask before bottling."}],"traps":["Nacional is a parcel inside Quinta do Noval, not a separate quinta, and it is declared apart from Noval's own Vintage Port.","Ungrafted is not pre-phylloxera: the Nacional vines grow on their own roots and are replanted that way.","Nacional names the ungrafted parcel, not the grape Touriga Nacional; the wine is a field blend."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-tscm522o","front":"What did the fire of 1981 destroy for Quinta do Noval, and where did the stocks go after?","back":"The lodge, offices and records in Vila Nova de Gaia; the stocks later moved to a temperature controlled cellar on the quinta."},{"id":"fl-fskxgph4","front":"Name a year when Quinta do Noval declared its Nacional but not its own Vintage Port.","back":"1996; in 2007 the reverse, Noval declared without the Nacional."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-eg5mtgul","q":"What sets Quinta do Noval Nacional apart from Noval's own Vintage Port?","opts":["Its vines grow ungrafted on their own roots","It is made from Touriga Nacional alone","It is aged for decades in small casks","It comes from bought grapes upriver"],"a":0,"exp":"Nacional comes from a small parcel of ungrafted vines inside the estate; it is a field blend of many varieties, and its name does not refer to Touriga Nacional.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-5qpglgqc","q":"Quinta do Noval made its name by declaring a Vintage and a Nacional in a year when most shippers held back. Which year?","opts":["1963","1931","1945","1927"],"a":1,"exp":"Noval declared both in 1931, in the depression and with the large 1927 vintage unsold, when most houses did not declare; 1927 was the year the trade had declared widely.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Quinta do Noval: Our history","url":"https://www.quintadonoval.com/en/history/our-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quinta do Noval: Historical timeline","url":"https://www.quintadonoval.com/f/fichier_attache/cn/3/historical-timeline.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quinta do Noval: The particularity of our Nacional Vintage Port","url":"https://www.quintadonoval.com/en/terroir/the-particularity-of-our-nacional-vintage-port","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quinta do Noval: Nacional Vintage Port","url":"https://www.quintadonoval.com/en/vin/12/nacional-vintage-port","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vintus: Quinta do Noval","url":"https://vintus.com/?p=909","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-pyeobp3l": {"id":"fl-pyeobp3l","key":"barca-velha","group":"douro","region":"douro","producer":"Casa Ferreirinha","producerId":"p-casa-ferreirinha","say":"BAR-kah VEL-yah, KAH-zah feh-ray-REE-nyah","place":"Quinta da Leda, Douro Superior, Douro DOC, Portugal","founded":"1751","wine":{"name":"Barca Velha","appellation":"Douro","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Touriga Franca and Touriga Nacional at its heart, with Tinta Roriz and other Douro grapes; the shares change with each declared year.","sites":"Quinta da Leda in the Douro Superior gives the greater part, with grapes from other vineyards at several altitudes in the same subregion; the first vintages were made at Quinta do Vale Meão, a Ferreira family estate there.","making":"The wine is aged for about a year and a half in French oak barrels, most of them new, then rests for years in bottle while the house watches it. Only then does the winemaking team decide whether the year is Barca Velha or Reserva Especial, so a vintage reaches the market eight or nine years after the harvest.","glass":"Cedar and cigar box over blackcurrant, sour cherry and plum; savoury and restrained rather than ripe, with fine, firm tannin, fresh acidity and a long, dry finish.","ageing":"Released when the house judges it ready to begin drinking, eight or nine years after the harvest, it goes on gaining for decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant an hour ahead, an old bottle gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Barca Velha","means":"Old boat in Portuguese; the name is printed only in declared years."},{"term":"Douro, Denominação de Origem Controlada","means":"The appellation of the valley's unfortified wines; Port carries the separate name Porto."},{"term":"Casa Ferreirinha","means":"The house name of Ferreira's unfortified Douro wines, after Dona Antónia Ferreira's nickname."}]},"table":"Barca Velha is Casa Ferreirinha's unfortified Douro red, first made in 1952 and released after long rest in bottle, only in years its makers think fit to bear the name. Savoury, cedar framed and fresh, it showed that Port's grapes could make a dry red to keep.","story":"Dona Antónia Adelaide Ferreira (1811 to 1896), widowed at thirty three, took charge of the Ferreira house, founded in 1751, and added estate after estate in the Douro; her countrymen called her Ferreirinha, little Ferreira. The dry wine that carries her nickname began more than half a century after her death. Fernando Nicolau de Almeida (1913 to 1998), whose father was the house's technical director, wanted a Douro red made with the care given to Vintage Port. At Quinta do Vale Meão, in the heat of the Douro Superior, he cooled the fermenting wine with ice brought by lorry from Matosinhos, near Porto, and in 1952 declared his first Barca Velha, selling it only in 1960, when he judged it ready to begin drinking. The harvest of 1960 gave the first Reserva Especial, the name for strong years judged short of Barca Velha. The Ferreira house joined Sogrape in 1987, and in later decades Quinta da Leda in the Douro Superior became the main source of the blend. The 2015, released after nine years, was the twenty first Barca Velha since 1952. For a sommelier it marks where the Douro's fine dry reds begin.","others":[{"name":"Casa Ferreirinha Reserva Especial","note":"From strong years the team judges closer to Reserva Especial than Barca Velha; first made in 1960."},{"name":"Quinta da Leda","note":"The single estate red of the Douro Superior quinta that gives most of Barca Velha."}],"traps":["Barca Velha is not made every year: strong years that fall short may be released as Reserva Especial, and many years give neither.","Ferreirinha is the nickname of Dona Antónia Ferreira, a person, not a place.","Barca Velha is an unfortified red of the Douro DOC, not a Port, though it comes from the same grapes."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-gv9g9icx","front":"Barca Velha: who made the first vintage, in 1952, and at which quinta?","back":"Fernando Nicolau de Almeida, at Quinta do Vale Meão in the Douro Superior."},{"id":"fl-t1x0o58w","front":"What becomes of a Casa Ferreirinha vintage that is strong but not judged Barca Velha?","back":"It may be released as Reserva Especial, a name first used for the 1960."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-bbsflg5i","q":"A guest orders Barca Velha expecting a sweet Port to finish the meal. What do you tell them?","opts":["It is a Vintage Port from one quinta","It is a tawny Port aged in cask","It is a dry, unfortified Douro red","It is a sweet red of dried grapes"],"a":2,"exp":"Barca Velha is an unfortified red of the Douro DOC, dry and savoury; it shares Port's grapes but is never fortified, so it is not a Vintage Port.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-8wkuxe6h","q":"Barca Velha was born with the 1952 vintage. When did that wine go on sale?","opts":["1987","1956","1952","1960"],"a":3,"exp":"Fernando Nicolau de Almeida held his 1952 back for eight years and sold it in 1960, when he judged it ready; 1987 is the year the Ferreira house joined Sogrape.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Sogrape: Porto Ferreira, a secular tradition","url":"https://sogrape.com/brand/porto-ferreira","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Sogrape: Barca-Velha 2015, the twenty first vintage","url":"https://sogrape.com/press/barca-velha-2015-21st-vintage-of-douros-most-iconic-wine","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Sogrape: Casa Ferreirinha presents Reserva Especial 2017","url":"https://sogrape.com/press/casa-ferreirinha-reserva-especial-2017-released-after-9-years-of-ageing","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Fernando Nicolau de Almeida","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_Nicolau_de_Almeida","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Casa Ferreirinha Barca Velha 2015 released","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2024/07/outstanding-casa-ferreirinha-barca-velha-2015-released/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-1juiop02": {"id":"fl-1juiop02","key":"sandeman","group":"douro","region":"douro","producer":"Sandeman","producerId":"p-sandeman","say":"","place":"Vila Nova de Gaia, Porto, Douro","founded":"1790","wine":{"name":"Sandeman 20 Year Old Tawny Port","appellation":"Porto","classification":"Tawny with an indication of age, a special category of Port approved by the IVDP","colour":"fortified","grapes":"A blend of Douro red grapes: Touriga Franca, Tinta Roriz, Tinta Barroca, Tinta Amarela and Tinto Cão.","sites":"Grapes from vineyards along the Douro, blended and aged downriver in the house's lodges at Vila Nova de Gaia, across the river from Porto.","making":"The grapes are fermented on their skins until grape spirit is added to stop the fermentation and keep the sugar. The wines then age for many years in oak casks in the house's lodges at Vila Nova de Gaia, and wines of several ages are blended to show the character of twenty years in wood.","glass":"Amber with a red edge; walnut, toasted almond, dried fig and orange peel over caramel and a hint of cinnamon, sweet and silky, lifted by fresh acidity; the nutty finish lingers.","ageing":"Ready to drink when bottled and made to be drunk rather than laid down: its ageing is done in cask, and it throws no crust.","serve":"12 to 14 degrees Celsius, lightly chilled; it needs no decanter; a white wine glass of modest size.","label":[{"term":"20 Years Old","means":"An indication of age: the blend shows the character of twenty years in cask, not the age of every wine."},{"term":"Tawny","means":"Port aged in wood until its colour fades towards amber and its fruit turns to nuts and caramel."},{"term":"Porto","means":"Port's protected name in Portuguese; an aged tawny's label also gives its bottling year."}]},"table":"Under the caped Don of a house founded in London in 1790, Sandeman 20 Year Old Tawny is Port aged for many years in oak casks in its lodges at Vila Nova de Gaia. Walnut, orange peel and caramel; pour it lightly chilled, straight from the bottle.","story":"In 1790 a Scot of the Sandeman family, twenty five years old and the son of a cabinetmaker, borrowed from his father to set up as a wine merchant in London. From the start the house dealt in Port and sherry, the two fortified wines of Iberia, and at first it traded not from offices of its own but from Tom's Coffee House in Birchin Lane, as merchants then did. The house recalls that the founder bought lodges at Vila Nova de Gaia to hold its Port before shipment, and by the middle of the nineteenth century it was shipping fine old tawny aged in its cellars. In 1928 an artist at a printing firm drew the Don, a silhouette in the black cape of a Portuguese student and a Spanish hat, one for Port and one for sherry. In 1952 the firm became a public company; Seagram took it over in 1979 or 1980, as accounts differ, and in 2002 it joined Sogrape, the Portuguese wine group. The 20 Year Old shows what decades in cask do to Port: fruit turned to walnut, orange peel and caramel, poured cool and without a decanter.","others":[{"name":"Sandeman Vintage Port","note":"The house's declared Vintage, bottled young and decanted off its crust."},{"name":"Sandeman Quinta do Seixo Vintage Port","note":"A Vintage from the house's own quinta in the Cima Corgo, made in some years."},{"name":"Sandeman Late Bottled Vintage","note":"Port of a single year kept four to six years in wood before bottling."}],"traps":["Twenty years on a tawny label is an indication of age, the character of the blend, not the age of every wine in it.","Sandeman was founded in London by a Scot, not in Porto; its Port ages in the lodges of Vila Nova de Gaia across the river.","Decanting is for Vintage Port, which leaves a crust in the bottle; a 20 Year Old Tawny did its ageing in cask and leaves none."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-577vqufu","front":"Sandeman: what does the Don wear, and what does each piece stand for?","back":"A Portuguese student's black cape for Port and a Spanish hat for sherry."},{"id":"fl-h7b4nk69","front":"Where did the young Sandeman house trade in London before it had offices of its own?","back":"From Tom's Coffee House in Birchin Lane, as merchants then did."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-u3gu51ye","q":"Does the age on Sandeman 20 Year Old Tawny mean every drop in it is twenty years old?","opts":["No: it spent twenty years under flor","No: the blend shows twenty years' character","Yes: it spent twenty years in bottle","Yes: every wine in it is from one harvest"],"a":1,"exp":"An indication of age describes the character of a blend of wines of several ages aged in wood; a tawny from a single harvest would be a Colheita, not a 20 Year Old.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-pqhj85z6","q":"The Sandeman Don, the caped silhouette on the label, was first drawn in which year?","opts":["1928","1790","1880","1952"],"a":0,"exp":"The Don was drawn in 1928 at a printer's studio; 1790 is the founding in London, and 1952 the year the family firm became a public company.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Sandeman: History","url":"https://www.sandeman.com/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Sandeman: Arts collection, the Don","url":"https://www.sandeman.com/sandeman-arts/collection/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Sandeman: Porto Tawny 20 Year Old","url":"https://www.sandeman.com/port-wine/tawny/porto-tawny-20-year-old-2020/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"IVDP: Vinhos do Porto, categorias especiais","url":"https://www.ivdp.pt/pt/vinhos/vinhos-do-porto/categorias-especiais/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Grape to Glass: Sandeman 20 Years Tawny Port sheet","url":"https://grape-to-glass.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/Sandeman-20-Years-Tawny-Port.pdf","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-80lepyim": {"id":"fl-80lepyim","key":"blandys","group":"madeira","region":"madeira","producer":"Blandy's","producerId":"p-blandys","say":"BLAN-deez, MAHM-zee","place":"Funchal, Madeira DOP, island of Madeira","founded":"1811","wine":{"name":"Blandy's 10 Year Old Malmsey","appellation":"Madeira","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Malvasia alone, the grape sold in English as Malmsey.","sites":"Malvasia bought from growers on the island's terraced vineyards, then aged in Blandy's Wine Lodge in the centre of Funchal, the São Francisco lodge, named for the Franciscan convent on whose site it stands.","making":"The Malvasia is fermented with its own yeasts and fortified with grape spirit early, which keeps much of its sugar. Canteiro ageing follows, in seasoned oak casks under the warm roof of the lodge, and the wines blended for bottling average ten years of age, by the house's account.","glass":"Deep amber; honey, caramel, sweet confectionery and tea leaf over walnut and a breath of smoked wood. Sweet, soft and full, kept fresh by the high acidity of Madeira, with a long finish that recalls old brandy.","ageing":"Ready to drink when it is bottled, and it holds for years in bottle; once opened it keeps for months, which suits a bottle poured by the glass.","serve":"About 16 degrees Celsius, slightly cool, as the house advises; no need to decant; a small white wine glass, after dinner or with dessert.","label":[{"term":"Malmsey","means":"The English name for Malvasia, the sweetest of the four noble grapes of Madeira."},{"term":"10 Years Old","means":"An age statement for a blend of wines of several ages, averaging about ten years, as the house describes it."},{"term":"Madeira","means":"The protected name of the island's fortified wine; its unfortified wines carry another, Madeirense."}]},"table":"Blandy's 10 Year Old Malmsey is the sweet Malvasia of the family house founded in Funchal in 1811, aged for years in oak casks under the warm roof of its lodge. Honey, caramel and walnut are kept fresh by acidity; pour it slightly cool after dinner.","story":"John Blandy (1783 to 1855) came to Madeira from England in 1808 with a letter of introduction to the island's wine merchants, and in 1811 he set up on his own, with two of his brothers, as a wine shipper and general trader. The house tells of a trade that reached Russia, northern Europe, the West Indies and North America. On his death in 1855 his son took charge. In the nineteenth century the family gathered buildings on the site of the Franciscan convent of São Francisco into the lodge where its fine wines age. In 1913 a group of shippers formed the Madeira Wine Association to pool their resources; Blandy's joined in 1925, and in 1981 the association took the name Madeira Wine Company. In 1989 the family took the Symington family, Port shippers of Oporto, into partnership, and in 2011, its bicentenary year, an agreement gave the Blandys control of the company once more. The 10 Year Old Malmsey stands at the sweet end of the house's age ladder and is a ready way to show a guest what canteiro does: years of gentle warmth under a lodge roof, rather than months in a heated tank.","others":[{"name":"Blandy's Frasqueira","note":"Single harvest, single noble grape wines aged at least twenty years in cask before bottling."},{"name":"Blandy's Sercial 10 Year Old","note":"Sercial at the same age: dry where the Malmsey is sweet."},{"name":"Blandy's Colheita Malmsey","note":"Malmsey of a single harvest, bottled after at least five years in cask."}],"traps":["Malmsey and Malvasia are one grape and one style: Malmsey is the English name the label prints, and it is the sweetest of the four noble grapes.","Canteiro is years of natural warmth in cask under the lodge roof; estufagem is at least three months in a tank heated to between 45 and 50 degrees Celsius. The 10 Year Old is canteiro."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xh5313l5","front":"Blandy's 10 Year Old Malmsey: which grape, and where does it sit on the sweetness ladder?","back":"Malvasia, the sweetest of the four noble grapes: Sercial, Verdelho, Boal, then Malvasia."},{"id":"fl-ka1d22h5","front":"In what year did Blandy's join the Madeira Wine Association, formed by shippers in 1913?","back":"1925; the association took the name Madeira Wine Company in 1981."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-s77ibhqe","q":"Which ageing gave Blandy's 10 Year Old Malmsey its caramel and walnut?","opts":["Three months in a tank heated near 50 degrees","A long rest in bottle in a cool cellar","Years under a veil of flor in a solera","Years in oak casks under the warm lodge roof"],"a":3,"exp":"The 10 Year Old is aged by canteiro, in seasoned oak casks under the warm roof of the lodge in Funchal; heated tanks, estufagem, serve the house's younger blends.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-3zvqkf3b","q":"Which family of Port shippers did the Blandys take into partnership in the Madeira Wine Company in 1989?","opts":["The Offley family","The Croft family","The Symington family","The Sandeman family"],"a":2,"exp":"The Blandys invited the Symington family of Oporto in 1989 to strengthen sales and marketing; in 2011, the bicentenary year, the Blandys took back control.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Blandy's: About us","url":"https://blandys.com/en/about-us/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Blandy's: 10 Years Old Malmsey wine description","url":"https://blandys.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/1_Blandys_Madeira-Wine_Fortified-10-yo-Malmsey.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Madeira Wine Company: Our history and the Blandy family","url":"https://www.madeirawinecompany.com/en/about-us/our-history.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Blandy: Blandy group history","url":"https://blandy.com/en/about/blandy-group-history.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cultura Madeira: Madeira Wine Company, São Francisco wineries","url":"https://cultura.madeira.gov.pt/en/madeira-wine-company-s%C3%A3o-francisco-wineries.html","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-vi9x9tup": {"id":"fl-vi9x9tup","key":"henriques","group":"madeira","region":"madeira","producer":"Henriques & Henriques","producerId":"p-henriques-e-henriques","say":"en-REE-kesh, ver-DEL-yoo","place":"Câmara de Lobos, Madeira DOP, island of Madeira","founded":"1850","wine":{"name":"Henriques & Henriques 15 Year Old Verdelho","appellation":"Madeira","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Verdelho alone, the medium dry step of the four noble grapes.","sites":"Verdelho partly from the house's own vineyard at Quinta Grande above Câmara de Lobos, terraced between 350 and 700 metres and planted mostly with Verdelho, as the house and its importer tell it.","making":"The Verdelho is fermented and then fortified with neutral grape spirit while some sugar remains, which leaves it medium dry. It ages by canteiro, in oak casks warmed only by the climate in the house's lodges at Câmara de Lobos, and is bottled as a blend of wines of several ages carrying the 15 Years Old statement.","glass":"Dark gold with amber glints; raisin, honey, Seville orange and dried apricot over nuts and old wood. Medium dry, with caramel and nut on the palate and a keen acidity that lingers.","ageing":"Drinkable as soon as it is bottled and long lived after; an opened bottle keeps its character for months, so one bottle can serve many evenings.","serve":"13 to 16 degrees Celsius, the island's guidance for Madeira, the older the wine the warmer; decanting is unnecessary; a small white wine glass, as an aperitif.","label":[{"term":"Verdelho","means":"The noble grape of the medium dry style, between Sercial and Boal on the ladder."},{"term":"15 Years Old","means":"The wine's age statement: it names a blend of several ages, never a single harvest."},{"term":"Henriques & Henriques","means":"The house name, from two sons of the founder who joined in partnership after his death in 1912."}]},"table":"A Câmara de Lobos house of 1850 that grows Verdelho on its own terraces, Henriques & Henriques ages its 15 Year Old Verdelho slowly in cask. A little cool and medium dry, it makes an aperitif, with orange peel, raisin and nuts cut by sharp acidity.","story":"A man of an old landowning family of Câmara de Lobos, settled on the south coast west of Funchal for generations, set up the firm in 1850, the year the house gives for its founding. It was a hard time to begin: oidium struck the island's vines in the early 1850s and phylloxera in 1872. When the founder died in 1912 two of his sons joined in partnership, and the firm took the name it carries, Henriques & Henriques; in 1913 the family's holdings were gathered into one company, and from 1925, the house says, it shipped its own wines abroad. Unlike most Madeira shippers, which buy their grapes from the island's many smallholders, the house has long farmed substantial vineyards of its own around Câmara de Lobos. In 1992 it began a programme of growth, building a new ageing cellar at Câmara de Lobos and a winemaking centre on its land at Quinta Grande, where its largest vineyard is planted mostly with Verdelho. Its 15 Year Old range, one bottling for each noble grape, lets a student taste the ladder side by side, and the Verdelho is the middle step: drier than Boal, softer than Sercial, a medium dry Madeira that shows how much acidity the island's wines keep after years in cask.","others":[{"name":"Henriques & Henriques 15 Year Old Sercial","note":"The same fifteen years from Sercial, dry where the Verdelho is medium dry."},{"name":"Henriques & Henriques Terrantez","note":"A bottling of the rare noble grape, medium dry in style, for which the house is a dependable source."}],"traps":["Terrantez is its own noble grape, not another name for Boal or Verdelho.","Verdelho is the medium dry step, between Sercial and Boal; a guest who asks for the driest Madeira wants Sercial."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-lczywpxx","front":"Henriques & Henriques: which noble grape fills its largest vineyard, at Quinta Grande?","back":"Verdelho, on terraces between 350 and 700 metres above Câmara de Lobos."},{"id":"fl-m20bk2oz","front":"How did Henriques & Henriques come by its doubled name?","back":"Two sons of the founder joined in partnership after his death in 1912."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-2gb4nhce","q":"A guest wants a Madeira neither dry nor sweet as an aperitif. Where does Henriques & Henriques 15 Year Old Verdelho sit?","opts":["Medium dry, between Sercial and Boal","Medium sweet, between Verdelho and Malvasia","Sweet, the richest of the noble grapes","Dry, the driest of the noble grapes"],"a":0,"exp":"Verdelho is the medium dry noble grape, second on the ladder of Sercial, Verdelho, Boal and Malvasia; Sercial is the dry one.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-sysmla1k","q":"Henriques & Henriques began a programme of growth with a new ageing cellar at Câmara de Lobos. When was that?","opts":["1913","1992","1850","1925"],"a":1,"exp":"The house dates the new cellar, and a winemaking centre at Quinta Grande, to 1992; 1913 was the year the family's holdings became one company.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Henriques & Henriques: Our history","url":"https://henriquesehenriques.pt/en/our-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Henriques & Henriques: Our vineyards","url":"https://henriquesehenriques.pt/en/our-vineyards/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Henriques & Henriques: Verdelho 15 years","url":"https://henriquesehenriques.pt/en/wines/verdelho-15-years/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Haus Alpenz: H&H Verdelho 15 Year Old Madeira","url":"https://alpenz.com/product-verdelho_15.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"DICA, Região Autónoma da Madeira: Consumir o Vinho Madeira","url":"https://dica.madeira.gov.pt/index.php/outros-temas/diversos/733-consumir-o-vinho-madeira","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-bufjtul3": {"id":"fl-bufjtul3","key":"barbeito","group":"madeira","region":"madeira","producer":"Barbeito","producerId":"p-barbeito","say":"bar-BAY-too, mal-vah-ZEE-ah","place":"Câmara de Lobos, Madeira DOP, island of Madeira","founded":"1946","wine":{"name":"Barbeito Malvasia 20 Years Old","appellation":"Madeira","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Malvasia alone, the sweetest of the four noble grapes.","sites":"Malvasia from the north coast at São Jorge and Arco de São Jorge, with fruit from Fajã dos Padres on the south coast in some lots, by the house's lot sheets.","making":"Fermentation is stopped with grape spirit at 96 per cent, which leaves the wine sweet. Years by canteiro follow in oak casks, French oak in the lots the house details, under the warmth of the lodge, before it is bottled in numbered lots without caramel for colour.","glass":"Amber; toffee, nuts, smoke and a resinous hint of pine, with lime peel at the edge. Sweet but never heavy: a high, cutting acidity carries it long and leaves it fresh.","ageing":"Sound in bottle for many years, though ready on release; an opened bottle holds up for months. Lots differ a little, so the lot number tells one bottling from the next.","serve":"15 to 16 degrees Celsius, the island's guidance for older Madeira; a blend needs no decanting; a small white wine glass and a small measure.","label":[{"term":"Malvasia","means":"The sweetest noble grape, Malmsey in English; Barbeito prints the Portuguese name."},{"term":"20 Years Old","means":"One age statement over a blend of many harvests, not the wine of a single year."},{"term":"Lot or Lote","means":"The number of the bottling: the 20 Years Old is released in separate lots that differ a little."}]},"table":"No caramel goes into Barbeito Malvasia 20 Years Old: the Câmara de Lobos firm founded in 1946 ages it many years in cask and bottles it in numbered lots. Toffee and smoke meet lime peel and a cutting acidity in a sweet wine; pour a small measure.","story":"Vinhos Barbeito was founded in 1946 by Mário Barbeito de Vasconcelos (1905 to 1985), born in Funchal and, as the house recalls him, a collector of books on the island's history and on Columbus. Its importer describes those years as a difficult moment for a new Madeira house. After his death in 1985 his daughter, who had worked in the firm since 1976, ran the house, and her son joined her in 1989. In 1991 the Kinoshita family, who had sold Barbeito's wines in Japan since 1967, joined the house in a joint venture, and in the early 1990s the founder's grandson took charge of the house and its winemaking. He stopped colouring the wines with caramel, began in 1995 to age Tinta Negra by canteiro, and, on the house's own word, was the first to name that red grape on a Madeira label. Barbeito became known for wines bottled as the casks made them, for single casks and for numbered lots. Its Malvasia 20 Years Old, released lot by lot from north coast fruit, shows that clarity in a sweet wine: rich, yet sharp enough to end cleanly, proof that a sweet Madeira need not be heavy.","others":[{"name":"Barbeito single cask bottlings","note":"Single casks of one harvest bottled unblended, of a noble grape or of Tinta Negra."},{"name":"Barbeito Frasqueira","note":"Its vintage wines: one noble grape of one year, kept twenty years or more in cask."},{"name":"Barbeito Malvasia 50 Years Old O Japonês","note":"A limited bottling of Malvasia aged fifty years, named in honour of the house's Japanese partner."}],"traps":["Tinta Negra is a red grape and the island's most planted, not a filler; Barbeito bottles it under its own name.","Bottled without caramel, a Barbeito can look paler than another house's Madeira of the same age; the colour is no sign of youth."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-9p50ymtj","front":"Barbeito Malvasia 20 Years Old: where on the island does its Malvasia grow?","back":"Mostly on the north coast at São Jorge, with Fajã dos Padres on the south coast in some lots."},{"id":"fl-n4heo4vv","front":"What did Barbeito stop adding to its Madeiras under the founder's grandson?","back":"Caramel for colour."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-0qs0vk52","q":"A guest notices a lot number on Barbeito Malvasia 20 Years Old. What does it tell?","opts":["The year the grapes were picked","The number of the single cask it came from","The vineyard parcel at São Jorge","Which blended release of the wine it is"],"a":3,"exp":"The 20 Years Old is a blend released lot by lot, so the number names the bottling; a single harvest carries its year, and Barbeito's single casks a cask number.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-8frnulck","q":"In 1991 Barbeito began a joint venture that steadied the house as it changed course. With whom?","opts":["The Symington family of Oporto","The Blandy family of Funchal","The Kinoshita family, its importers in Japan","A cooperative of Câmara de Lobos growers"],"a":2,"exp":"The Kinoshita family had sold Barbeito in Japan since 1967 and joined it in 1991; the Symingtons partnered Blandy's, not Barbeito, from 1989.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Vinhos Barbeito: Who we are","url":"https://www.vinhosbarbeito.com/en/2-barbeito-wine-company/who-we-are.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinhos Barbeito: Malvasia 20 Y.O. Lot 14050","url":"https://www.vinhosbarbeito.com/en/our-wines/signature-wines/175-barbeito-malvasia-20-years-old-lot-14050.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: Barbeito Madeira producer page","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/producer/barbeito-madeira/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Barbeito Malvasia Lote 14050 20 Year Old review","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-reviews/portugal/madeira/barbeito-malvasia-lote-14050-20-year-old-madeira-portugal-2311/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"DICA, Região Autónoma da Madeira: Consumir o Vinho Madeira","url":"https://dica.madeira.gov.pt/index.php/outros-temas/diversos/733-consumir-o-vinho-madeira","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-9boe5276": {"id":"fl-9boe5276","key":"doliveira","group":"madeira","region":"madeira","producer":"Pereira d'Oliveira","producerId":"","say":"peh-RAY-rah doh-lee-VAY-rah, boo-AHL","place":"Funchal, Madeira DOP, island of Madeira","founded":"","wine":{"name":"D'Oliveira Frasqueira Boal","appellation":"Madeira","classification":"Frasqueira: one harvest of one noble grape, at least twenty years in wood","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Boal alone, the noble grape of the medium sweet style, spelt Bual in English.","sites":"Each bottling is one year's Boal; many of the family's old wines came from its own vineyards in São Martinho, a parish of Funchal, and they age in cask in its lodge in the Rua dos Ferreiros.","making":"Boal is fortified with grape spirit partway through fermentation, which leaves it medium sweet. The wine of one harvest then ages for at least twenty years in old oak casks in the lodge, often far longer, and the family bottles only what its orders need, printing the bottling year on the back label.","glass":"Dark amber; butter caramel, honey and fig with blood orange and clementine peel. Rich and medium sweet, with the weight of Boal, cut by a high acidity, and clean as it fades slowly.","ageing":"Ready when it leaves the cask after its decades in wood; a sound bottle lasts for decades more, and an opened one lives on for months.","serve":"15 to 16 degrees Celsius, as the island advises for older Madeira; decant it, and draw an old cork with care, since it can crumble; a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Boal","means":"The Portuguese spelling of Bual, the noble grape of the medium sweet style."},{"term":"Frasqueira","means":"One harvest of one noble grape, at least twenty years in wood; not every label prints the word."},{"term":"Meio Doce","means":"Medium sweet: the sweetness the rules tie to Boal, in Portuguese and often in English beside it."}]},"table":"One harvest of Boal, kept in cask by a Funchal family that bottles its old vintages only to order: that is D'Oliveira Frasqueira Boal. Medium sweet, with caramel, fig and orange peel and a high acidity, it wants a decanter and a cellar cool pour.","story":"Pereira d'Oliveira is a family house of Funchal whose beginnings are told in more than one way. Most accounts date it to 1850, while its American importer traces the company's origin to the 1880s and to a landowner and grower of São Martinho, a parish on the city's western side, where the family's vineyards gave many of its old wines. The family kept its single harvests apart in cask, holding that Madeira ages better in wood than in glass, and bottled only what its orders needed. So its lodge in the Rua dos Ferreiros of old Funchal came to hold wines reaching far back: Verdelho of 1850, which its importer traces to the São Martinho vineyards, Boal from the first years of the twentieth century, each sold as a Frasqueira with its bottling year printed on the back label. Under the island's rules a Frasqueira is one harvest of one noble grape aged at least twenty years in wood, and the house's often wait far longer. Its Boal of 1908 has been bottled a century and more after its harvest. Taste one to learn what time in cask does to Madeira: rich, yet fresh after a hundred years.","others":[{"name":"D'Oliveira Frasqueira Verdelho","note":"Medium dry single harvests; the house's old Verdelhos reach back to 1850, from its São Martinho vineyards by its importer's account."},{"name":"D'Oliveira Frasqueira Malvasia","note":"The sweet end of the house's single harvests, with vintages reaching back into the nineteenth century."}],"traps":["Boal and Bual are the same grape spelt two ways; Boal is the Portuguese spelling the house prints.","The year on the front of a Frasqueira is the harvest, not the bottling: a Boal of 1908 may have been bottled a century later.","A Frasqueira needs at least twenty years in wood; a Colheita, also from one harvest, needs only five."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-p5vc597c","front":"D'Oliveira Frasqueira Boal: which two years can a bottle show?","back":"The harvest, and the bottling year on the back label, which tells how long the wine spent in cask."},{"id":"fl-gk0n34rn","front":"Why does Pereira d'Oliveira keep its harvests in cask and bottle them only to order?","back":"The family holds that Madeira ages better in wood than in glass."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ulkk1eoi","q":"How does D'Oliveira Frasqueira Boal differ from a Colheita?","opts":["At least twenty years in wood, against five","Heated in tanks, against cask ageing","Dry in style, against medium sweet","A blend of several harvests, against one"],"a":0,"exp":"Both come from a single harvest; a Frasqueira needs at least twenty years in wood and a Colheita at least five, so a blend of harvests fits neither.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-6wmds91m","q":"By its importer's account, the old Verdelhos of Pereira d'Oliveira, back to 1850, came from the family's own vineyards in one parish of Funchal. Which?","opts":["São Pedro","São Martinho","Monte","Santa Maria Maior"],"a":1,"exp":"The family's old Verdelhos, from 1850 on, came from its vineyards in São Martinho, on the city's western side; the lodge stands in the Rua dos Ferreiros of the old town.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Madeira Wine Guide: D'Oliveiras producer guide","url":"https://madeirawineguide.com/producers/doliveiras/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Rare Wine Co: D'Oliveira Madeira producer page","url":"https://www.rarewineco.com/growers/doliveira-416/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Big Fortified Tasting: Pereira d'Oliveira directory entry","url":"https://www.thebft.co.uk/index.php/business-directory/item/pereira-d-oliveira","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"World of Fine Wine: D'Oliveiras Boal tasting note","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/wines/3524/doliveiras-boal","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"DICA, Região Autónoma da Madeira: Consumir o Vinho Madeira","url":"https://dica.madeira.gov.pt/index.php/outros-temas/diversos/733-consumir-o-vinho-madeira","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-mi1j2jyr": {"id":"fl-mi1j2jyr","key":"hm-borges","group":"madeira","region":"madeira","producer":"H.M. Borges","producerId":"","say":"BOR-zhesh, mal-vah-ZEE-ah","place":"Funchal, Madeira DOP, island of Madeira","founded":"1877","wine":{"name":"H.M. Borges 15 Year Old Malvasia","appellation":"Madeira","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Malvasia only, bought from the island's growers.","sites":"Growers across the island sell the house its Malvasia, since it farms no vineyards of its own; the wine ages in its lodge in the Rua 31 de Janeiro in Funchal, once a cereal mill.","making":"Fermentation at a controlled temperature gives way to fortification with grape spirit, which keeps its sweetness. The wine then rests by canteiro in French and American oak casks under the lodge roof; the blend bottled under the 15 Years Old statement draws on wines of several ages.","glass":"Amber in colour; vanilla, caramel and nuts on the nose, then dried fig, raisin and chocolate. Sweet and full, with molasses on the finish and Madeira's acidity to lift it.","ageing":"It can be drunk from release or kept for years in bottle; once opened it lasts for months.","serve":"13 to 14 degrees Celsius, as the house advises for its sweet Malvasia; there is nothing to decant; a small white wine glass, with dark chocolate or petits fours, as the house suggests.","label":[{"term":"Malvasia","means":"The Portuguese name of Malmsey, the sweetest noble grape; some export labels print the English one."},{"term":"15 Years Old","means":"The age the blend declares, drawn from wines of several ages rather than one harvest."},{"term":"H.M. Borges","means":"The founder's initials and surname; his family has run the house since 1877."}]},"table":"Since 1924 H.M. Borges, a Funchal family house of 1877 that buys its grapes, has aged its wines slowly in a former cereal mill, its 15 Year Old Malvasia among them. Sweet, with caramel, fig and chocolate held up by acidity, it belongs beside dessert or dark chocolate.","story":"Initials and a surname make the house's name, those of a Funchal merchant who in 1877 set out, in the house's version, to seek wines from across the island and age them. Its first premises, nearer the centre of the city, lacked what shipping abroad required, and in 1924 it moved into a building that had been a mill for grinding cereal, fitting it out as a lodge while keeping its old walls; from there it became a producer and exporter in its own right. The house has stayed in the founder's family ever since. Like most Madeira shippers it owns no vineyards and buys from growers across the island, choosing by grape: Sercial, Verdelho, Boal, Malvasia and the red Tinta Negra. Its fine wines age by canteiro, the name taken from the wooden beams, canteiros, on which the casks rest, in French and American oak under the roof of the old mill. The house takes each of the four noble grapes to fifteen years, and the Malvasia is the one for dessert. For a sommelier it stands for the small family house outside the large companies that ages the island's sweet wine the slow way, and it gives a guest a sweet Madeira with acidity enough to leave the palate fresh.","others":[{"name":"H.M. Borges 10 Year Old Malvasia","note":"The same grape at ten years, lighter and younger."},{"name":"H.M. Borges 15 Year Old Sercial","note":"Sercial at fifteen years, the dry counterpart of the house's Malvasia."},{"name":"H.M. Borges Single Harvest Tinta Negra","note":"A single harvest of the red Tinta Negra, bottled sweet; the house has released one of 2005."}],"traps":["Sweet Madeira is served a little cool, not cold like Fino: the house advises 13 to 14 degrees Celsius for its Malvasia.","H.M. Borges of Funchal is a different house from Henriques & Henriques of Câmara de Lobos, though both are old family firms."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-gcxjwguf","front":"H.M. Borges: how do its fine wines age, and in what wood?","back":"By canteiro, on wooden beams under the lodge roof, in French and American oak casks."},{"id":"fl-ygk53ryc","front":"Where does H.M. Borges get the Malvasia for its 15 Year Old?","back":"From growers across the island: the house farms no vineyards of its own."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-t0c0kmxm","q":"A guest orders H.M. Borges 15 Year Old Malvasia with dessert. At what temperature does the house advise serving it?","opts":["9 to 10 degrees Celsius","6 to 8 degrees Celsius","13 to 14 degrees Celsius","18 to 20 degrees Celsius"],"a":2,"exp":"The house advises 13 to 14 degrees Celsius for its sweet Malvasia and Boal, slightly cool; 6 to 8 is the range for Fino and Manzanilla, served cold.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-unjj4tpx","q":"The Funchal lodge that H.M. Borges moved into in 1924, becoming an exporter in its own right, had once served another purpose. What was it?","opts":["A Franciscan convent","A sugar cane mill","A Jesuit college","A mill for grinding cereal"],"a":3,"exp":"By the house's account the building was a cereal mill; the Franciscan convent of São Francisco gave its name to the Blandy lodge nearby.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"H.M. Borges: Madeira Wine, the house's history","url":"https://www.hmborges.com/en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"H.M. Borges: 15 Years Old Malvasia","url":"https://www.hmborges.com/en/vinho/16-15-anos-doce/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"H.M. Borges: FAQs","url":"https://www.hmborges.com/en/page/faqs/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Madeira Wine Guide: H.M. Borges","url":"https://madeirawineguide.com/producers/h-m-borges/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: H.M. Borges Malmsey 15 Years Old review","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-reviews/portugal/madeira/h-m-borges-malmsey-15-years-old-madeira-madeira-111702/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-fu77zfms": {"id":"fl-fu77zfms","key":"egon-muller","group":"mosel","region":"mosel","producer":"Egon Müller","producerId":"p-egon-muller","say":"AY-gon MUL-er, SHARTS-hof-bair-ger","place":"Wiltingen, the Saar, Mosel","founded":"1797","wine":{"name":"Scharzhofberger Riesling Spätlese","appellation":"Mosel","classification":"Prädikatswein, Spätlese: late picked, at least 76 degrees Oechsle on the Mosel","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone, part of it from ungrafted vines planted more than a century ago.","sites":"The Scharzhofberg, the steep south facing hill of grey slate above the Scharzhof at Wiltingen, in the cold Saar valley; the estate farms its share from the house at its foot.","making":"The hill is picked in several selective passes, so that each Prädikat is a true selection. The juice ferments slowly with wild yeasts in traditional thousand litre oak Fuder casks in the deep cellar of the Scharzhof, and the fermentation is stopped with natural sugar left behind, the wine low in alcohol and high in acid.","glass":"White peach, apricot and lime blossom over wet slate and green apple; the sugar reads as texture rather than sweetness against a piercing Saar acidity, and age adds honey, quince and smoke.","ageing":"Delicious young for its fruit, it then closes for some years, and a strong vintage keeps for thirty years and more.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass, with crab, pork cooked with fruit, or on its own.","label":[{"term":"Scharzhofberger","means":"The vineyard, an Ortsteil, printed without the village name Wiltingen."},{"term":"Spätlese","means":"Late picked, at least 76 degrees Oechsle on the Mosel; here naturally sweet."},{"term":"Mosel","means":"The wine region, named Mosel-Saar-Ruwer until 2007; the Saar lies within it."}]},"table":"Low in alcohol and naturally sweet, Egon Müller's Scharzhofberger Riesling Spätlese grows on the steep grey slate hill above the Scharzhof in the cold Saar valley. Peach and apricot sit over slate, and the acid is so high that the sugar reads as texture.","story":"Through the Middle Ages the Scharzhof and its hill belonged to a monastery of Trier, and the slope was planted with vines long before any Müller lived there. When the French, who then held the land, took church property and sold it, the estate went to auction in 1797, and a forebear of the family bought it; his descendants, the Müllers among them, have kept it since. Several heads of the house carried the name Egon, so the label names a line rather than one man. Germany's wine law of 1971 fixed the Prädikat ladder of Kabinett, Spätlese and Auslese that the labels follow, and in 2007 the region's name, Mosel-Saar-Ruwer, was shortened to Mosel. Through those changes the estate held to sweet wine of very low alcohol and very high acid, fermented with wild yeasts in Fuder casks and picked in several passes so that each Prädikat is a true selection. Lots of its Auslese and Trockenbeerenauslese are sold at the Trier auction of the Grosser Ring, the association of the Mosel's auction estates founded there in 1908. A candidate who knows the Spätlese knows the Saar's measure, the hill and the hand of the auction wines in a frame a table can share.","others":[{"name":"Le Gallais Wiltinger Braune Kupp","note":"The family's second estate in Wiltingen, a separate vineyard under its own label."},{"name":"Scharzhofberger Riesling Kabinett","note":"The lightest wine of the hill, and the test of whether a candidate hears the Saar."},{"name":"Scharzhofberger Riesling Auslese","note":"Selected bunches, often touched by noble rot; a gold capsule marks the richer lots."}],"traps":["Scharzhofberger is the single vineyard; Wiltinger Scharzberg is the Grosslage that borrows its sound across much of the Saar.","Several growers own parcels of the Scharzhofberg, so the vineyard name alone does not mean Egon Müller.","The Saar is a part of the Mosel region, not a region of its own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xn850nuj","front":"Egon Müller's Scharzhofberger: what is Wiltinger Scharzberg, a name that sounds almost the same?","back":"The Grosslage over much of the Saar, a collective name and not the single vineyard."},{"id":"fl-0r6qmkun","front":"In which side valley of the Mosel does Egon Müller's Scharzhof lie, and what does it give the wine?","back":"The Saar, the coldest of the three valleys: later ripening, very high acid and low alcohol."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-awphnuow","q":"A guest asks what the word Spätlese on Egon Müller's Scharzhofberger promises. What do you say?","opts":["Grapes picked late, at a legal minimum ripeness","A dry wine from a classified Grosse Lage","Grapes frozen on the vine before pressing","A wine aged late in new oak barrels"],"a":0,"exp":"Spätlese means late picked, at least 76 degrees Oechsle on the Mosel; a dry wine from a Grosse Lage is a Grosses Gewächs, and frozen grapes make Eiswein.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-d1w0b57m","q":"In which year did a forebear of the family of Egon Müller buy the Scharzhof at auction, after the French sold church property?","opts":["1911","1797","1971","1882"],"a":1,"exp":"The estate was auctioned in 1797 and bought by a forebear of the family; 1882 is a sale of Maximin Grünhaus, 1911 the founding of Joh. Jos. Prüm and 1971 the German wine law.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"VDP: the VDP.Mosel-Saar-Ruwer and the Grosser Ring","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/the-wines/the-vdpregions/mosel","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Primum Familiae Vini: Egon Müller Scharzhof","url":"https://pfv.org/en/member/egon-muller-scharzhof","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"wein.plus glossary: Egon Müller-Scharzhof","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/egon-mueller-scharzhof","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Westgarth Wines: Weingut Egon Müller, master of Saar Riesling","url":"https://westgarthwines.com/blogs/news/weingut-egon-m-ller-scharzhof-master-of-saar-riesling","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Falstaff: Ungrafted vines, in search of the holy grail","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/news/ungrafted-vines-in-search-of-the-holy-grail","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-l6q6ngdy": {"id":"fl-l6q6ngdy","key":"jj-prum","group":"mosel","region":"mosel","producer":"Joh. Jos. Prüm","producerId":"p-jj-prum","say":"PREWM, VAY-len-er ZON-en-oor","place":"Wehlen, the Mittelmosel, Mosel","founded":"1911","wine":{"name":"Wehlener Sonnenuhr Riesling Auslese","appellation":"Mosel","classification":"Prädikatswein, Auslese: selected bunches, at least 83 degrees Oechsle on the Mosel","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Riesling alone, from the blue slate of the Sonnenuhr.","sites":"The Wehlener Sonnenuhr, a steep slope of blue Devonian slate across the river from Wehlen, named for the sundial set in its rock in 1842.","making":"Selected bunches, ripe and often touched by noble rot, are picked by hand and kept apart for the Auslese. The juice ferments cool with wild yeasts, mostly in stainless steel with a few old wooden casks as the year decides, and rests long on its lees; the fermentation is stopped with natural sugar left, and the young wine keeps a reductive, struck match smell and a faint prickle.","glass":"In youth struck match and smoke over white peach and lime, with a faint prickle; with age honey, candied apricot, quince and slate, sweet yet weightless and lifted by bright acidity.","ageing":"Often unapproachable for years, it opens after ten and keeps for several decades, turning to honey and smoke.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle briefly so the struck match can clear; a white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Wehlener Sonnenuhr","means":"The sundial vineyard of Wehlen; the village comes first, with er added."},{"term":"Auslese","means":"Selected bunches at a legal minimum ripeness, often with noble rot; sweet."},{"term":"Goldkapsel","means":"A gold capsule, named on a list, for a richer selection of the same Prädikat; a long gold capsule richer again."}]},"table":"Expect struck match when a young JJ Prüm Wehlener Sonnenuhr Riesling Auslese is opened: the house's sweet wines from the blue slate slope named for its sundial need years. With age they turn to honey and smoke while staying light on the tongue.","story":"The sundial that names the Sonnenuhr was set in the slate across the river from Wehlen in 1842 by a member of the Prüm family. In 1911 the family estate was divided among heirs, and one of them founded the estate whose label carries his name in short. His son worked there from 1920 and led it until his death in 1969, and he is credited with the style it is known by: light, sweet wines, fermented with wild yeasts and stopped with natural sugar, that smell of struck match when young and seem closed for years. Germany's wine law of 1971 set the Prädikat names on the labels; within them the house marks a richer Auslese with a gold capsule and a richer one again with a long gold capsule. Other Prüm estates share the name and the village, which keeps guests and candidates alert. The estate's fame rests on sweet wines that a patient cellar turns into honey and smoke over decades. Two habits come with it: read the village before the vineyard, and look at the capsule as well as the print.","others":[{"name":"Wehlener Sonnenuhr Riesling Spätlese","note":"The same vineyard one step lower, the Prüm wine most often quoted on a list."},{"name":"Wehlener Sonnenuhr Riesling Kabinett","note":"The lightest Prädikat of the slope, at the Mosel's floor of 70 degrees Oechsle."},{"name":"Graacher Himmelreich Riesling Kabinett","note":"From the Graach slope beside Wehlen, broader in build, useful for contrast."}],"traps":["Several Prüm estates share the name, S.A. Prüm and Studert-Prüm among them; Joh. Jos. Prüm is a separate house.","The struck match and the prickle of a young bottle are the house's signature, not a fault.","Sonnenuhr means sundial and appears at Wehlen, Zeltingen and Brauneberg, so the village name carries the answer."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-3lytt1dn","front":"Joh. Jos. Prüm: what does a gold capsule on its Auslese tell a guest?","back":"A richer selection within the same Prädikat; a long gold capsule marks a richer one again."},{"id":"fl-m013o5mw","front":"Who set the sundial in the Wehlener Sonnenuhr, and when?","back":"A member of the Prüm family, in 1842, in the slate across the river from Wehlen."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-b9mp44b7","q":"A guest opens a young Joh. Jos. Prüm Wehlener Sonnenuhr Auslese and finds it smells of struck match. What do you say?","opts":["A sign the grapes were frozen on the vine","A cork taint, so the bottle should go back","The house's reductive youth, which air and age will clear","The smell of new oak barrels in the cellar"],"a":2,"exp":"Prüm's young wines are known for a reductive, struck match smell and a faint prickle that clear with air and time; a corked wine smells of damp cardboard, and the house uses no new oak.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-lneewwkz","q":"In which year did a division of the family's holdings at Wehlen create the estate of Joh. Jos. Prüm?","opts":["1797","1842","1971","1911"],"a":3,"exp":"The estate began with the division of 1911; 1842 is the sundial, 1797 the Scharzhof's sale at auction and 1971 the German wine law.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Weingut Joh. Jos. Prüm","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weingut_Joh._Jos._Pr%C3%BCm","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Joh. Jos. Prüm producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/joh-jos-prum-producer-profile-10-wines-tasted-516272","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"CellarTracker: Joh. Jos. Prüm producer article","url":"https://www.cellartracker.com/m/articles/producer/110","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"wein.plus glossary: S. A. Prüm and the Prüm family","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/s-a-pruem","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Falstaff: Weingut Joh. Jos. Prüm","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/wineries/weingut-joh-jos-pruem","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-umt0dhmk": {"id":"fl-umt0dhmk","key":"dr-loosen","group":"mosel","region":"mosel","producer":"Dr. Loosen","producerId":"p-dr-loosen","say":"LOH-zen, AIR-den-er pray-LAHT","place":"Bernkastel, with the Prälat at Erden, Mosel","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Erdener Prälat Riesling Auslese","appellation":"Mosel","classification":"Prädikatswein: Auslese, the selected harvest, 83 degrees Oechsle at least on the Mosel","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Riesling alone, from old vines on their own roots, some of them a century old.","sites":"The Prälat, a small, steep, rocky slope of red slate across the river from the village of Erden, facing south and sheltered between the river and a massif of rock; it is counted the warmest site of the Mittelmosel.","making":"Selected bunches, many touched by noble rot, which the sheltered site gives in most years by the estate's account, ferment spontaneously with wild yeasts in large old oak casks. The fermentation stops with natural sugar left behind, and the wine stays on its lees, unstirred, for about a year before bottling.","glass":"Ripe apricot, candied orange peel and blood orange over smoke, ginger and red slate, with honey and saffron; rich and concentrated, yet carried by bright acidity to a long, warm finish.","ageing":"Rich enough to enjoy within five years, it gains depth for twenty and keeps for decades.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass and a modest pour, with blue cheese or a fruit tart.","label":[{"term":"Erdener Prälat","means":"The Prälat vineyard of Erden; the village name comes first."},{"term":"Auslese","means":"Selected late bunches at a legal minimum ripeness; on the Mosel, sweet."},{"term":"Mosel","means":"The region; Erden lies in the Mittelmosel, downriver from Bernkastel."}]},"table":"Old ungrafted vines on a steep red slate slope between rock and river, counted the warmest site of the Mittelmosel, give Dr. Loosen's Erdener Prälat Riesling Auslese. It is rich and sweet, apricot and blood orange over smoke and ginger, yet bright.","story":"Around Bernkastel the Loosens have owned vines for more than two centuries, by the family's own account, and over that time they gathered parcels in the steep slopes downriver: the Würzgarten at Ürzig and the Treppchen and the Prälat at Erden. The Prälat was part of the Treppchen until it was marked out as a site of its own at the end of the nineteenth century, and it was named in honour of a prelate and professor from Ürzig, a grower's son. Its red slate between rock and water makes it the warm heart of that stretch of the river, and the family's vines there grow on their own roots. In 1988 a son of the family took over the estate and set out to carry Mosel Riesling back onto the wine lists of the world; the estate joined the VDP in 1993. That work took it beyond the valley. In 1999 it made the first Eroica Riesling with Chateau Ste. Michelle of Washington State, and it also took on an estate in the Pfalz. The Prälat Auslese stayed at the centre of the range as its most concentrated wine. Set beside a Wehlen wine, it shows the other face of the slate, red rock and warmth, orange and ginger where blue slate gives peach and lime.","others":[{"name":"Ürziger Würzgarten Riesling Spätlese","note":"Red, iron rich Rotliegend rock of volcanic origin mixed with slate, below the cliffs at Ürzig: the spice garden that names the vineyard."},{"name":"Wehlener Sonnenuhr Riesling Kabinett","note":"Blue slate at Wehlen, the classic counterpoint to Ürzig and Erden."},{"name":"Erdener Treppchen Riesling Grosses Gewächs","note":"The dry wine of the steep slope named for an old stone stair, beside the Prälat."}],"traps":["The red rock of Ürzig's Würzgarten is iron rich Rotliegend of volcanic origin mixed with slate, not the blue slate of Wehlen and Graach.","The estate also makes wine in the Pfalz and in Washington State, so a Loosen label is not always a Mosel wine.","Erdener Prälat and Erdener Treppchen are two different vineyards of the same village; the Prälat was marked out of the Treppchen late in the nineteenth century."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-53mzd7l5","front":"Dr. Loosen's Erdener Prälat: on what rock, and why is it so warm?","back":"Red slate on a steep, south facing slope, sheltered between a massif of rock and the river."},{"id":"fl-2xjjzgle","front":"With which Washington State house did Dr. Loosen make Eroica Riesling from 1999?","back":"Chateau Ste. Michelle, of Woodinville."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-rau72xb3","q":"A guest asks why Dr. Loosen's Erdener Prälat Auslese tastes riper and warmer than a Wehlen wine. Which reason fits?","opts":["Red slate on a sheltered slope between rock and river","Blue slate on a cool, high plateau","A year of ageing in new oak barrels","Limestone and marl like the upper Mosel"],"a":0,"exp":"The Prälat is a sheltered red slate slope counted the warmest site of the Mittelmosel; blue slate is Wehlen's, and limestone the Obermosel's above Trier.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-bexlpdrp","q":"A guest reading Dr. Loosen's Erdener Prälat asks whom the vineyard's name honours. What do you say?","opts":["A Prince-Abbot of Fulda","A prelate and professor from neighbouring Ürzig","An abbot of St Maximin at Trier","An Archbishop of Trier cured by its wine"],"a":1,"exp":"By the VDP's account the site was marked out of the Treppchen late in the nineteenth century and named for a prelate from Ürzig; the cured archbishop is the legend of Bernkastel's Doctor.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weingut Dr. Loosen: About us","url":"https://drloosen.de/en/pages/uber-uns","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Dr. Loosen: Erdener Prälat","url":"https://drloosen.de/en/collections/erdener-pralat","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: Weinberg.online, Prälat","url":"https://www.vdp.de/de/die-weine/weinbergonline/lage/4096-praelat-vdpgrosse-lage","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Ste. Michelle Wine Estates: Eroica","url":"https://www.smwe.com/estates/details/eroica","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Best of Wines: Weingut Dr. Loosen","url":"https://bestofwines.com/wine/wine-producer/weingut-dr-loosen/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-o6f5ok89": {"id":"fl-o6f5ok89","key":"thanisch","group":"mosel","region":"mosel","producer":"Wwe. Dr. H. Thanisch, Erben Thanisch","producerId":"p-thanisch","say":"TAH-nish, BAIRN-kas-tel-er DOCK-tor","place":"Bernkastel, below the Doctor, Mosel","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Bernkasteler Doctor Riesling Spätlese","appellation":"Mosel","classification":"Prädikatswein, the Spätlese rung: grapes picked late, 76 degrees Oechsle at least here","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Bernkasteler Doctor, a small, steep slope directly above the town of Bernkastel, facing south south west at gradients up to 70 per cent: dark, rocky, weathered Devonian slate rich in fine earth, planted mostly with ungrafted Riesling.","making":"Picked by hand in several selective passes, the grapes are pressed and the juice ferments with its own wild yeasts, in stainless steel or in oak casks as the year and the lot decide; the fermentation is stopped to keep natural sweetness.","glass":"Smoke and apple skin over white peach, lemon and grey slate; dense and upright, with the Doctor's weight behind a firm acidity and a sweetness that reads lighter than it is.","ageing":"Firm and closed for five years or more, it opens slowly and keeps for twenty and longer in a strong vintage.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass, with smoked trout or roast pork.","label":[{"term":"Berncasteler Doctor","means":"The old spelling of Bernkasteler Doctor that the house keeps on its labels: the Doctor vineyard of Bernkastel."},{"term":"Wwe.","means":"Witwe, widow: the estate is named for the widow of Dr. H. Thanisch, who ran it from 1895."},{"term":"Erben Thanisch","means":"The heirs of Thanisch, one of two estates that share the name after the family division of 1988."}]},"table":"Thanisch has held a share of the Bernkasteler Doctor, the steep slate slope right above the town, since the nineteenth century, and its Spätlese is the house's classic reading of the site. Smoky and dense, apple and peach, it is sweet but stands upright.","story":"The legend of the Doctor is set in 1360: an Archbishop of Trier, lying ill with a fever at Landshut castle above Bernkastel, was brought wine from this slope and recovered, and he named it the true doctor. The vineyard has carried the name since. The Thanisch family has grown vines on the Middle Mosel since the first half of the seventeenth century, by its own account, and it came to hold a share of the Doctor in the nineteenth. The head of the family, a doctor by degree and a member of the Prussian parliament, died in 1895 at forty one; his widow took charge with three small children, and her title stayed on the label: Wwe., short for Witwe, before Dr. H. Thanisch. By the estate's account she was among the founders of the Grosser Ring, the Mosel's auction estates, in 1908. In 1988 the family divided its holdings between heirs, and two estates came to bear the name, Erben Thanisch and Erben Müller-Burggraef, each with its own part of the Doctor. The Spätlese is the house's classic reading of the site: smoky, dense and firm. Read the long name to the end, then, since the heirs and not the widow tell you which house made the wine.","others":[{"name":"Bernkasteler Doctor Riesling Auslese","note":"The site's fullest form, in a ripe year."},{"name":"Bernkasteler Lay Riesling","note":"A neighbouring Bernkastel slope the estate also holds, lighter than the Doctor."},{"name":"Graacher Himmelreich Riesling","note":"From the broad Graach slope next door, a gentler wine than the Doctor."}],"traps":["Two estates share the Thanisch name after an inheritance split: Erben Thanisch and Erben Müller-Burggraef.","The Doctor is divided among only a few owners, both Thanisch estates and Wegeler among them.","Bernkasteler Kurfürstlay is a Grosslage and Bereich Bernkastel a district blend; neither is the Doctor."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-6sl5x5l9","front":"What does Wwe. mean on a Thanisch label?","back":"Witwe, widow: the estate is named for the widow of Dr. H. Thanisch, who took charge on his death in 1895."},{"id":"fl-z1vvxg8k","front":"Thanisch's Bernkasteler Doctor: what is its soil, and where does it lie?","back":"Dark, rocky, weathered Devonian slate rich in fine earth, on a slope as steep as 70 per cent directly above Bernkastel."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-4fk7nsfd","q":"A guest sees Bernkasteler Doctor on two bottles, one from Erben Thanisch and one from Erben Müller-Burggraef. What do you explain?","opts":["One comes from a Doctor vineyard at Graach","One is the first wine and the other the second","One is a Grosslage blend from around the town","Two estates of one family share the vineyard and the name"],"a":3,"exp":"An inheritance split left two Thanisch estates, each with a share of the Doctor; the Grosslage is Bernkasteler Kurfürstlay, and the Doctor lies only at Bernkastel.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-swqsmrjf","q":"The legend of the Bernkasteler Doctor tells of a cure in 1360. Who was cured?","opts":["A Duke of Burgundy","A Holy Roman Emperor","An Archbishop of Trier","A Prince-Abbot of Fulda"],"a":2,"exp":"The legend has an Archbishop of Trier recovering from fever at Landshut castle with wine from the slope; the Prince-Abbot of Fulda is part of the Spätlese story of Johannisberg.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"VDP: VDP.Estate Wwe. Dr. H. Thanisch, Erben Thanisch","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/die-winzer/mosel/vdpweingut-wwe-dr-h-thanisch-erben-thanisch","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: Vineyard.Online, Doctor","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/the-wines/vineyardonline/lage/4032-doctor","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bernkastel-Kues: the legend of the Doctor","url":"https://en.bernkastel.de/regions/citiy-of-bernkastel-kues/legend-of-the-doctor-of-the-bernkastel","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"wein.plus glossary: Wwe. Dr. H. Thanisch, Erben Thanisch","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/wwe-dr-h-thanisch-erben-thanisch-2","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Valckenberg: Berncasteler Doctor Riesling Auslese 2019, Wwe. Dr. H. Thanisch","url":"https://www.valckenberg.com/en/portfolio/wein/706-berncasteler-doctor-riesling-auslese-2019-ht8-19","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-bk97bmlm": {"id":"fl-bk97bmlm","key":"maximin-grunhaus","group":"mosel","region":"mosel","producer":"Maximin Grünhaus","producerId":"","say":"MAX-ih-min GREWN-hoy-zer, AHPTS-bairg","place":"Mertesdorf, the Ruwer, Mosel","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Maximin Grünhäuser Abtsberg Riesling Kabinett","appellation":"Mosel","classification":"Prädikatswein, Kabinett: the lightest rung, at least 70 degrees Oechsle on the Mosel","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Abtsberg, the abbot's hill, first in rank of the estate's three hills: a rounded hill of blue Devonian slate on the steep south facing side of the Ruwer valley at Mertesdorf, with slopes as steep as 75 per cent.","making":"After pressing and settling, the juice ferments only with wild yeasts, in thousand litre oak Fuder casks or small steel tanks, in the estate's historic cellar; the fermentation ends with a gentle natural sweetness left in the wine.","glass":"Green apple, lime and blackcurrant leaf over wet slate, with a hint of white peach; feather light, racy and long, its gentle sweetness reading almost dry against the Ruwer's acidity.","ageing":"Bright and charming young, a good Abtsberg Kabinett keeps and gains for ten to twenty years.","serve":"7 to 9 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass, as an aperitif or with river trout.","label":[{"term":"Maximin Grünhäuser","means":"The estate's name standing in place of a village, as an Ortsteil does, before each vineyard."},{"term":"Abtsberg","means":"The abbot's hill, first in rank of the three, above Herrenberg and Bruderberg."},{"term":"Kabinett","means":"The lightest Prädikat, at least 70 degrees Oechsle on the Mosel; here gently sweet."}]},"table":"Abtsberg means the abbot's hill, first in rank of the three hills of Maximin Grünhaus, an estate on the Ruwer once held by the Benedictine abbey of St Maximin at Trier. Feather light, green apple and blackcurrant leaf over blue slate, its Riesling Kabinett tastes nearly dry.","story":"The Grünhaus first appears in writing in 966, when an imperial charter confirmed a gift that a Frankish king had made in the seventh century to the Benedictine abbey of St Maximin at Trier, and the abbey's monks worked its slopes for centuries. Its three hills take their names from the abbey's order of rank: the Abtsberg the abbot's, the Herrenberg the lords', the Bruderberg the brothers'. In 1811, under Napoleon's secularisation, the estate was sold at auction to its first lay owner, a baron, and it passed through another family before 1882, when a baron of the Stumm family bought it; his daughter brought it to the von Schubert family as her dowry on her marriage. The three vineyards, each a monopole of the estate, are printed under the estate's own name, Maximin Grünhäuser, which German law treats as an Ortsteil standing in place of a village. The Ruwer is a small, cool side valley, and its wines are delicate, scented with herbs and blackcurrant leaf. The Abtsberg is the estate's first wine in rank, and its Kabinett the lightest way to meet it: in three names the estate keeps the order in which an abbey ranked its own hills.","others":[{"name":"Maximin Grünhäuser Herrenberg Riesling","note":"The lords' hill, second in rank of the three, its upper part on red slate."},{"name":"Maximin Grünhäuser Bruderberg Riesling","note":"The brothers' hill, third in the abbey's order of the three."},{"name":"Maximin Grünhäuser Abtsberg Riesling Auslese","note":"The same hill in a ripe year: selected bunches, some touched by noble rot."}],"traps":["Abtsberg, Herrenberg and Bruderberg are three vineyards of one estate, each a monopole, named for the abbey's ranks: abbot, lords, brothers.","Maximin Grünhaus lies on the Ruwer, a side valley within the Mosel region, not on the Saar.","Maximin Grünhäuser stands in place of a village name, as an Ortsteil does, so no village appears on the label."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-b9yfsmob","front":"Maximin Grünhaus: what do Abtsberg, Herrenberg and Bruderberg mean, in order of rank?","back":"The abbot's hill, the lords' hill and the brothers' hill."},{"id":"fl-30b85dbh","front":"In which side valley of the Mosel does Maximin Grünhaus lie?","back":"The Ruwer, which joins the Mosel just below Trier from the east."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-93h3zwhz","q":"A guest asks why a Maximin Grünhäuser Abtsberg label names no village. What do you say?","opts":["The estate is an Ortsteil, standing in place of a village","The vineyard is a Grosslage across the Ruwer","German law forbids village names on a Kabinett","The wine is a blend of the estate's three hills"],"a":0,"exp":"Maximin Grünhäuser is an Ortsteil, an estate with village standing, so it stands in place of a village; the Abtsberg is a single vineyard, not a blend or a Grosslage.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-lvhpg61v","q":"In which year does Maximin Grünhaus first appear in writing, in a charter confirming it to the abbey of St Maximin at Trier?","opts":["1882","966","1811","1136"],"a":1,"exp":"A charter of 966 is the first record; 1811 is its sale at auction under Napoleon, 1882 its purchase by the Stumm family and 1136 the founding of Kloster Eberbach in the Rheingau.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Maximin Grünhaus: History","url":"https://www.maximingruenhaus.de/en/winery/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maximin Grünhaus: Historie","url":"https://maximingruenhaus.de/erleben/historie/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maximin Grünhaus: Lagen","url":"https://maximingruenhaus.de/lagen","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maximin Grünhaus: Vineyards","url":"https://maximingruenhaus.de/en/vineyards/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maximin Grünhaus: Cellar","url":"https://www.maximingruenhaus.de/en/winery/cellar","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-kd1out6f": {"id":"fl-kd1out6f","key":"schloss-johannisberg","group":"rheingau","region":"rheingau","producer":"Schloss Johannisberg","producerId":"p-schloss-johannisberg","say":"shloss yo-HAHN-is-bairg, GREWN-lahk","place":"Johannisberg, Geisenheim, Rheingau","founded":"1100","wine":{"name":"Schloss Johannisberger Riesling Grünlack Spätlese","appellation":"Rheingau","classification":"Prädikatswein, Spätlese: late picked, at least 85 degrees Oechsle in the Rheingau","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone, as the hill has been planted since 1720.","sites":"The estate's single vineyard in one piece on the hill below the schloss, above the Rhine: Taunus quartzite under fertile loam and loess, with a little iron oxide that tints the soil red.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel, by the estate's own sheets, and kept on its fine lees until bottling in the spring after the harvest. The fermentation ends with a clear natural sweetness left in the wine and a low alcohol, well under ten per cent.","glass":"Ripe apple, white peach and quince over orange zest and wet stone; a clear sweetness held by firm Rheingau acidity, with age bringing honey, toast and smoke.","ageing":"Pleasing young, it gains depth for ten to twenty years, and a strong vintage lasts for decades.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Grünlack","means":"The green capsule of the estate's colour code, marking its Spätlese."},{"term":"Schloss Johannisberger","means":"The estate's own name, a district of Geisenheim allowed on the label with no village before it."},{"term":"Spätlese","means":"Late picked, at least 85 degrees Oechsle in the Rheingau; the word the estate's legend of 1775 gave the language."}]},"table":"A green capsule marks Schloss Johannisberg's Grünlack Spätlese, Riesling from the hill where, as the estate tells it, the late harvest of 1775 gave Germany the word. Apple and peach over stone, it is plainly sweet with a firm backbone.","story":"The estate counts twelve centuries of wine on its hill, and around 1100 Benedictine monks founded a monastery there. By 1716, when the Prince-Abbot of Fulda bought it, the monastery lay in ruins; Fulda built a summer palace and from 1720 planted the hill to Riesling alone. In 1775, so the story goes, the courier sent to Fulda for the Prince-Abbot's leave to pick came back late; the grapes had shrivelled and caught noble rot, the wine made from them was a revelation, and Spätlese, the late harvest, entered the language. The estate's chronology adds its first Eiswein in 1858. On 1 July 1816, after the Congress of Vienna, the Emperor Francis I of Austria (1768 to 1835) gave the schloss to his foreign minister, Prince Klemens von Metternich (1773 to 1859), with a tithe of the yield owed each year to the Habsburgs. By its own account its cellar library holds bottles back to 1748. The estate came to code its wines by the colour of the capsule, the Rotlack for Kabinett, the Grünlack for Spätlese and the Silberlack for its dry Grosses Gewächs, so a guest can read the style from across the room. The Grünlack carries the legend into the glass; tell it as the estate's claim rather than as proven history.","others":[{"name":"Schloss Johannisberger Riesling Rotlack Kabinett","note":"The red capsule: a Kabinett, off dry by the estate's own sheets."},{"name":"Schloss Johannisberger Riesling Silberlack Grosses Gewächs","note":"The silver capsule, the estate's dry flagship from the same hill."}],"traps":["Johannisberg here is a district of Geisenheim in the Rheingau; Johannisberg in the Swiss Valais is a name for Silvaner.","The Spätlese story of 1775 is the estate's claim, to be told as such rather than as proven history.","Johannisberger Erntebringer is a Grosslage over neighbouring villages, not Schloss Johannisberg."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8yoqfjot","front":"Schloss Johannisberg's capsules: which colour marks the Spätlese, and which the dry Grosses Gewächs?","back":"Grünlack, green, for the Spätlese; Silberlack, silver, for the dry Grosses Gewächs."},{"id":"fl-ms1h3wq5","front":"What happened at Schloss Johannisberg in 1775, as the estate tells it?","back":"The courier bringing leave to pick came back late; the overripe grapes, touched by noble rot, made a celebrated wine, and Spätlese got its name."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ai3wbnzj","q":"A guest sees a green capsule on a Schloss Johannisberger Riesling. What does it tell you before you read the label?","opts":["The wine is an Eiswein from a frozen harvest","The wine is the estate's dry Grosses Gewächs","The wine comes from a bought vineyard","The wine is the estate's Spätlese"],"a":3,"exp":"The estate codes its Prädikate by capsule colour: Grünlack, green, is the Spätlese, while silver marks the dry Grosses Gewächs and red the Kabinett.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-r0267e7u","q":"As the estate tells it, whose courier arrived late with leave to harvest at Johannisberg in 1775?","opts":["The Archbishop of Trier","The Abbot of Eberbach","The Prince-Abbot of Fulda","The Emperor of Austria"],"a":2,"exp":"The estate then belonged to the Prince-Abbots of Fulda; the Archbishop of Trier is the Doctor's legend, and the Emperor of Austria gave the schloss to Metternich in 1816.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Schloss Johannisberg: History","url":"https://schloss-johannisberg.de/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Schloss Johannisberg: Grünlack Spätlese 2023 sheet","url":"https://schloss-johannisberg.de/app/uploads/Gruenlack-Spaetlese-2023-en-1.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wines of Germany: Schloss Johannisberg","url":"https://www.winesofgermany.com/our-regions/highlight-of-wine-culture/308/schloss-johannisberg","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Schloss Johannisberg","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schloss_Johannisberg","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Regierungspräsidium Darmstadt: Mindestmostgewichte für Rheingau und Bergstraße","url":"https://rp-darmstadt.hessen.de/sites/rp-darmstadt.hessen.de/files/2022-04/mindestmostgewichte_0.pdf","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-iqci62y7": {"id":"fl-iqci62y7","key":"robert-weil","group":"rheingau","region":"rheingau","producer":"Robert Weil","producerId":"p-robert-weil","say":"VILE, KEE-drikh GRAY-fen-bairg","place":"Kiedrich, Rheingau","founded":"1875","wine":{"name":"Kiedrich Gräfenberg Riesling Trockenbeerenauslese","appellation":"Rheingau","classification":"Prädikatswein, Trockenbeerenauslese: shrivelled noble rot berries, at least 150 degrees Oechsle","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Riesling alone, from berries shrivelled by noble rot.","sites":"The Gräfenberg, a steep slope above Kiedrich facing south south west, with gradients up to 60 per cent on deep, stony soils of phyllite mixed with loess.","making":"Pickers go through the vineyard again and again, taking shrivelled, noble rot berries one by one. The thick, sticky juice ferments slowly to a low alcohol, well under ten per cent, with a great weight of natural sugar left behind.","glass":"Dried apricot, candied orange peel and saffron over acacia honey and caramelised pineapple; syrupy yet electric, its great sweetness carried by high acidity to a finish that seems to last for minutes.","ageing":"Already rich on release, it lives for half a century and more, slowly turning towards toffee, black tea and dried fig.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a small white wine glass and a small pour, alone or with a fruit tart.","label":[{"term":"Kiedrich Gräfenberg","means":"The village and the vineyard; older labels read Kiedricher Gräfenberg."},{"term":"Trockenbeerenauslese","means":"Dried berry selection: shrivelled noble rot berries picked one by one, at least 150 degrees Oechsle."},{"term":"Rheingau","means":"The region on the Rhine's north bank between Wiesbaden and Rüdesheim."}]},"table":"Single shrivelled berries, picked one by one on the steep slate of the Gräfenberg above Kiedrich, make Robert Weil's Riesling Trockenbeerenauslese. Honeyed and syrupy, apricot and orange peel, yet bright with acidity, it is a dessert served in a small glass.","story":"The estate began with a scholar. Its founder, a German professor at the Sorbonne in Paris, bought his first vines on the Kiedricher Berg in 1867, by the estate's account, and the events before the war of 1870 drove him out of France. In 1875, the year the house counts as its founding, he added more of the Kiedrich slope, and he bought the manor house of a late English baronet in the village, which became the seat of the estate. As the house tells it, its Spätlese and Auslese wines were poured at imperial and royal courts across Europe. In 1988 the Japanese house of Suntory took a majority of the estate, with the founder's great grandson, the fourth generation, in charge of the wine. By the estate's own account the Gräfenberg has given every Prädikat up to Trockenbeerenauslese in every vintage since 1989, while the same slope gives a dry Grosses Gewächs. That run made Weil the usual counterweight to the Mosel when a question asks for German sweet Riesling. Against a Saar Trockenbeerenauslese the Rheingau's way with noble rot is plain to taste: the same grape in a fuller, firmer frame.","others":[{"name":"Kiedrich Gräfenberg Riesling Grosses Gewächs","note":"The dry wine of the same slope, the estate's dry flagship."},{"name":"Kiedrich Gräfenberg Riesling Auslese","note":"Selected bunches with noble rot; gold capsule lots sit above the standard bottling."},{"name":"Kiedrich Turmberg Riesling","note":"A second Kiedrich slope the estate holds, bottled on its own."}],"traps":["The Gräfenberg is at Kiedrich; do not place it at Erbach or Rüdesheim.","A Trockenbeerenauslese is sweet: trocken here means the dried berry, not a dry wine.","A Rheingau Trockenbeerenauslese needs the same 150 degrees Oechsle as the Mosel's, though the lower Prädikate ask more here."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-31u5gmac","front":"Robert Weil's Trockenbeerenauslese: what does trocken mean in the name?","back":"The dried, shrivelled berry, picked one by one; the wine itself is intensely sweet."},{"id":"fl-t8p9pp2x","front":"On what rock does Robert Weil's Gräfenberg grow, and where?","back":"Phyllite mixed with loess, on a steep slope above Kiedrich facing south south west."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-pikkkvi4","q":"A guest sees Trockenbeerenauslese on Robert Weil's Kiedrich Gräfenberg and asks if it is a dry wine. What do you say?","opts":["No: it is intensely sweet, from shrivelled noble rot berries","No: it is a sparkling Sekt from late picked grapes","Yes: it is the estate's Grosses Gewächs by another name","Yes: trocken means dry, so it is a dry Riesling"],"a":0,"exp":"Trockenbeerenauslese means a selection of dried berries, shrivelled by noble rot, and the wine is intensely sweet; the dry wine of the Gräfenberg is the Grosses Gewächs.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-6m82szsh","q":"Before the estate of Robert Weil was founded at Kiedrich in 1875, where had its founder made his career?","opts":["As a cellar master at Eberbach","As a professor in Paris","As a merchant in London","As a banker in Frankfurt"],"a":1,"exp":"The founder was a professor at the Sorbonne in Paris until the events before the war of 1870 drove him out; Eberbach was the Cistercians' abbey, never his.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weingut Robert Weil: Tradition","url":"https://www.weingut-robert-weil.com/en/winery/tradition","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Robert Weil: Kiedrich Gräfenberg Riesling Trockenbeerenauslese sheet","url":"https://www.weingut-robert-weil.com/Assets/RobertWeil/Media/Expertisen/EN/Kiedrich_Graefenberg_Riesling_Trockenbeerenauslese.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Robert Weil: Estate brochure","url":"https://www.weingut-robert-weil.com/Assets/RobertWeil/Media/Gutsbroschuere/Weingut_Robert_Weil_Estate_Brochure.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Suntory: Robert Weil","url":"https://suntory.com/brands/robertweil","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wein.plus Wine Guide: Weingut Robert Weil","url":"https://wineguide.wein.plus/weingut-robert-weil","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-fostr4ra": {"id":"fl-fostr4ra","key":"schloss-vollrads","group":"rheingau","region":"rheingau","producer":"Schloss Vollrads","producerId":"p-schloss-vollrads","say":"shloss FOLL-rahts","place":"Winkel, Oestrich-Winkel, Rheingau","founded":"1211","wine":{"name":"Schloss Vollrads Riesling Kabinett","appellation":"Rheingau","classification":"Prädikatswein, Kabinett: the ripeness level below Spätlese; a VDP village wine on the estate's labels","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone, the grape of almost the whole estate.","sites":"The estate's vineyards at Winkel, its own monopole around the moated tower above the village among them, on loess over Taunus quartzite, between the Rhine and the Taunus.","making":"The Kabinett is made in two styles: off dry, labelled feinherb, and fruity, plainly sweet at a low alcohol. By the estate's own sheet the feinherb is raised wholly in stainless steel; the estate's dry village wine is bottled apart, as 1716 Cabinet.","glass":"Green apple, lemon zest and white peach over wet stone, with a hint of fresh mint; light, crisp and restrained, the sweetness of each style balanced by firm acidity.","ageing":"Fresh and easy young, it keeps and gains for ten years and more in a good vintage.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass, with river fish, asparagus or a light salad.","label":[{"term":"Schloss Vollrads","means":"The estate, an Ortsteil of Winkel; on its dry 1716 Cabinet the name stands alone, with no village."},{"term":"Winkel","means":"The village, printed on the Kabinett as a VDP village wine in the estate's labels since at least 2020."},{"term":"Kabinett","means":"The Prädikat below Spätlese; the estate makes it off dry or fruity and sweet."},{"term":"feinherb","means":"Off dry; a Vollrads Kabinett without the word is fruity and plainly sweet."}]},"table":"Schloss Vollrads, above Winkel, dates its sales of wine to 1211 by its own account. Its Riesling Kabinett, from loess over quartzite, comes off dry, labelled feinherb, or fruity and sweet: light and crisp, green apple and lemon over stone.","story":"In 1211 the Greiffenclau family appear at Winkel in the service of the Archbishop of Mainz, and a sale of wine recorded that year is where Schloss Vollrads begins its own history. From about 1330 the family lived in the moated tower at the heart of the estate, for more than three hundred and fifty years, until they built the manor house beside it in 1684. The name passed in time to the Matuschka-Greiffenclau line. Erwein Graf Matuschka-Greiffenclau (1938 to 1997), who took over the indebted estate in 1975, was among the founders in 1984 of Charta, the growers' association that set out to bring dry Rheingau Riesling back to the table; its vineyards joined the VDP in 1999, the year the Rheingau's Erstes Gewächs arrived. His death in 1997 ended nearly eight centuries of the family at Vollrads, and the estate passed to a regional savings bank, the Nassauische Sparkasse, which kept its name and its Riesling. The Kabinett is the estate's everyday voice and the plainest sign of its long habit of making one wine in more than one style. Its label is the lesson: read feinherb, or its absence, before you pour.","others":[{"name":"Schloss Vollrads 1716 Cabinet Riesling trocken","note":"The estate's dry village wine, labelled Schloss Vollrads alone, fermented partly in a large wooden cask and partly in steel."},{"name":"Schloss Vollrads Schlossberg Grosses Gewächs","note":"The dry Riesling of the estate's VDP Grosse Lage, the Schlossberg."},{"name":"Schloss Vollrads Riesling Spätlese","note":"The classical Rheingau reading of the Prädikat, riper and fuller than the Kabinett."}],"traps":["The Greiffenclau family's centuries at Vollrads ended in 1997, so the family and the estate's later owners are two chapters.","Schloss Vollrads and Schloss Johannisberg are separate estates, both Ortsteile that may be labelled without a village name.","A Vollrads Kabinett may be off dry or fruity: the Prädikat sets ripeness at harvest, not sweetness in the glass."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-023trcod","front":"From which year does Schloss Vollrads date its sales of wine?","back":"1211, by the estate's own account."},{"id":"fl-l06irdyb","front":"Schloss Vollrads: which wine carries the estate's name alone, and what village does its Kabinett name?","back":"The dry 1716 Cabinet, labelled Schloss Vollrads as an Ortsteil; the Kabinett names Winkel as a VDP village wine."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-nzdp17gp","q":"A guest orders Schloss Vollrads Riesling Kabinett expecting a bone dry wine. What on the label tells you how sweet it is?","opts":["Whether it says Grosses Gewächs","Which vineyard of Winkel it names","Whether the capsule is green or red","Whether it says feinherb, the off dry style"],"a":3,"exp":"Vollrads makes its Kabinett off dry, labelled feinherb, or fruity and sweet; its dry village wine is the 1716 Cabinet, and the capsule code is Schloss Johannisberg's.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-njnjxbpo","q":"Which growers' association, founded in 1984 with the head of the Vollrads family among its founders, revived dry Rheingau Riesling?","opts":["The Grosser Ring","The VDP","Charta","The Bernkasteler Ring"],"a":2,"exp":"Charta, founded in 1984, set a standard for dry Rheingau Riesling, and its vineyards joined the VDP in 1999; the Grosser Ring and the Bernkasteler Ring are Mosel auction groups.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Schloss Vollrads: The history of Schloss Vollrads, timeline","url":"https://www.schlossvollrads.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/Schloss_Vollrads-Timeline.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Schloss Vollrads: 2024 Winkel Rheingau Riesling Kabinett feinherb","url":"https://shop.schlossvollrads.com/2024-Winkel-Rheingau-Riesling-Kabinett-feinherb/1240-2024","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Deutsche Biographie: Matuschka-Greiffenclau, Erwein Graf von","url":"https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/sfz100_00403_1.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Schloss Vollrads","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schloss_Vollrads","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Selection: 40 years of Charta Riesling","url":"https://selection-magazin.de/en/40-years-of-charta-riesling","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-wm18z171": {"id":"fl-wm18z171","key":"georg-breuer","group":"rheingau","region":"rheingau","producer":"Georg Breuer","producerId":"p-georg-breuer","say":"BROY-er, RUE-des-hime BAIRG SHLOSS-bairg","place":"Rüdesheim am Rhein, Rheingau","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Rüdesheim Berg Schlossberg Riesling","appellation":"Rheingau","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Berg Schlossberg, the steep castle hill of the Rüdesheimer Berg at the Rhine's bend, facing south south west on Taunus quartzite and phyllite slate, a soil that drains freely and stores the heat.","making":"Fermented and raised for most of a year in old oak casks of the Rheingau's traditional sizes, then bottled dry. The wine is labelled by village and vineyard alone, with no Prädikat.","glass":"Smoke, struck flint and wet slate over lime, yellow apple and blood orange; taut and bone dry, firm in grip and long, with a stony, faintly bitter edge at the finish.","ageing":"Tight for its first five to eight years, it then opens and keeps for twenty years and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Berg Schlossberg","means":"The castle hill of the Rüdesheimer Berg, the steep slope at the Rhine's bend."},{"term":"Rüdesheim","means":"The village, written before the vineyard; older labels read Rüdesheimer."},{"term":"Riesling","means":"The grape; no Prädikat follows, since the estate labels by site alone and the wine is dry."}]},"table":"Georg Breuer labels its Rüdesheim Berg Schlossberg Riesling by place alone, with no Prädikat, the estate's way with its dry wines. Grown on steep quartzite and slate at the Rhine's bend and raised in old casks, it is bone dry, smoky and taut.","story":"Dry Riesling named for its vineyard is the idea that made the Breuer name. The estate grew from a Rüdesheim wine business founded in 1880, which a partner from the Breuer family took over early in the twentieth century, and it carries the name of a later head of the family. In the 1980s his son led it towards one idea: that Rheingau Riesling should be dry, and should be named for the vineyard it came from rather than for its ripeness at harvest. In 1984 he was among the founders of Charta, the growers' association that set a standard for dry Rheingau Riesling from good sites. The estate went further and dropped the Prädikat words from its labels altogether, naming its wines by village and vineyard alone. Charta's vineyards joined the VDP in 1999, the year the Rheingau's Erstes Gewächs arrived, and the VDP's Rheingau members moved to Grosses Gewächs with the 2012 vintage. He died suddenly in May 2004, aged 57, and his daughter took over the estate young and kept its course. The Berg Schlossberg, the steep quartzite and slate of the castle hill above the river, is the summit of the range. A place on the label and nothing else was the argument, and no wine of the estate makes it louder than the Berg Schlossberg.","others":[{"name":"Rauenthal Nonnenberg","note":"A monopole at Rauenthal, broader and spicier than the Rüdesheim wines."},{"name":"Rüdesheim Berg Rottland","note":"The warmer of the Berg sites, rounder in fruit."},{"name":"Rüdesheim Berg Roseneck","note":"The Berg slope beside the Schlossberg, on slate and quartzite."}],"traps":["The estate labels by site without a Prädikat, so the absence of Kabinett or Spätlese is a choice, not an omission.","Rüdesheim's Berg sites are slate and quartzite, unlike the loess and loam of the central Rheingau.","Rüdesheim Berg Schlossberg lies at the Rhine's bend in the Rheingau; Schlossberg is also an Alsace Grand Cru and a common vineyard name."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-3m620a2b","front":"Why does Georg Breuer's Berg Schlossberg carry no Kabinett or Spätlese on its label?","back":"The estate labels its dry wines by village and vineyard alone, with no Prädikat."},{"id":"fl-cad2erjn","front":"Georg Breuer's Berg Schlossberg: on what rock, and where?","back":"Taunus quartzite and phyllite slate, on a steep slope facing south south west at Rüdesheim, where the Rhine turns north."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-5q3m4crb","q":"A guest asks whether Georg Breuer's Rüdesheim Berg Schlossberg is sweet, since no Prädikat appears. What do you say?","opts":["It is sweet; the Prädikat is printed on the capsule","It is dry; the estate names only the place","It is off dry, as every Rheingau Riesling is","It is sweet; a missing Prädikat means late harvest"],"a":1,"exp":"Breuer makes its Rieslings dry and labels them by village and vineyard alone; the Rheingau makes every style, and a capsule code is Schloss Johannisberg's habit.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-5mllbttp","q":"In which year did the Rheingau's Erstes Gewächs arrive, after the dry campaign Georg Breuer helped lead?","opts":["1999","1971","1984","2012"],"a":0,"exp":"Erstes Gewächs came to the Rheingau in 1999, when Charta's vineyards joined the VDP; 1984 is Charta's founding, 2012 the VDP's move to Grosses Gewächs and 1971 the German wine law.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"VDP: Berg Schlossberg, VDP.Grosse Lage","url":"https://www.vdp.de/de/die-weine/weinbergonline/lage/7005-berg-schlossberg","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: The VDP.Rheingau","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/the-wines/the-vdpregions/rheingau","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Riesling champion Bernard Breuer dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/riesling-champion-bernard-breuer-dies-101794","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Georg Breuer producer page","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/georg-breuer/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein.plus Wine Guide: Weingut Georg Breuer","url":"https://wineguide.wein.plus/weingut-georg-breuer","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2oslxytc": {"id":"fl-2oslxytc","key":"kloster-eberbach","group":"rheingau","region":"rheingau","producer":"Kloster Eberbach","producerId":"","say":"KLOH-ster AY-ber-bahkh, SHTINE-bair-ger","place":"Eberbach, above Hattenheim, Rheingau","founded":"1136","wine":{"name":"Steinberger Riesling","appellation":"Rheingau","classification":"An Ortsteil: the walled vineyard registered with the standing of a village","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Steinberg, the walled vineyard some 900 metres from the abbey above Hattenheim, a monopole of the state domaine on a slope facing south to south west: sandy loess and loam below, stony slate and quartzite higher up.","making":"Made since 2008 in the Steinbergkeller, the cellar the state domaine opened beside the vineyard. The Steinberger is bottled dry and in the Prädikat styles, and a Wild Ferment cuvée ferments on the vineyard's own wild yeasts; the dry Grosses Gewächs is labelled Steinberg alone.","glass":"Yellow apple, quince and lemon peel over smoke and wet stone; dry, firm and broad in the Rheingau way, with a long, faintly salty finish.","ageing":"Firm young, it opens after three to five years and keeps for fifteen years and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for half an hour; a white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Steinberger","means":"Wine of the Steinberg, registered in 1971 as a part of Hattenheim and labelled without a village name."},{"term":"Kloster Eberbach","means":"The Cistercian abbey of 1136, whose name the state wine domaine carries."},{"term":"trocken","means":"Dry, the style of the domaine's everyday Steinberger."}]},"table":"Planted by the Cistercians of Kloster Eberbach and walled by them in the eighteenth century, the Steinberg above Hattenheim gives Steinberger Riesling. Made dry, it is broad and firm, yellow apple and quince over smoke and stone.","story":"An earlier religious house in the wooded valley above Hattenheim had failed when, in 1136, twelve monks came from Clairvaux, the abbey of Bernard of Clairvaux (1090 to 1153), at the request of the Archbishop of Mainz and founded the Cistercian abbey of Eberbach. Their Steinberg, on the slope below the forest, appears by name in the abbey's register of goods in 1211, and in the eighteenth century the monks closed it with a stone wall, finished in 1767. By most accounts the word Cabinet was first written at Eberbach in 1712, for reserve wines the monks set apart in a cellar of their own, and as Kabinett it later came to name a Prädikat. When the abbey was dissolved in 1803 it became the princely wine cellar of the Dukes of Nassau; after Nassau lost the war of 1866 it was a royal Prussian domaine, and after the Second World War it passed to the new state of Hesse, whose state wine domaine made the abbey and the Steinberg its home. The wine law of 1971 registered the Steinberg as a part of Hattenheim, so its wine is labelled Steinberger, with no village name. One wall, one vineyard, one name: eight centuries of monks, dukes, kings and a state stand behind the label.","others":[{"name":"Steinberg Riesling Grosses Gewächs","note":"The dry wine of the walled vineyard as a VDP Grosse Lage, labelled Steinberg alone."},{"name":"Steinberger Crescentia Riesling trocken","note":"A dry Steinberger ranked VDP Erste Lage on the domaine's own VDP page."},{"name":"Steinberger Wild Ferment","note":"A cuvée of the domaine's auction, fermented on yeasts native to the Steinberg."}],"traps":["Steinberg is a vineyard, not a village; Steinberger on a label is an Ortsteil of Hattenheim.","Kloster Eberbach is the Cistercians' abbey and the state wine domaine; Schloss Johannisberg was the Benedictines' hill.","The Steinberg's wall is a work of the eighteenth century, finished in 1767, not of the Middle Ages, though the vineyard is named in 1211."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-3xoncz3n","front":"Kloster Eberbach: what was the Cabinet of 1712, and what word did it give?","back":"Reserve wines the monks set apart in a cellar of their own; it gave the Prädikat Kabinett its name."},{"id":"fl-e3d0c5hu","front":"Steinberger Riesling: who planted the Steinberg, and when was its wall finished?","back":"The Cistercian monks of Kloster Eberbach; they closed it with a stone wall in the eighteenth century, finished in 1767."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-4zoa4j9c","q":"A guest reads Steinberger on a Kloster Eberbach Riesling and asks which village that is. What do you say?","opts":["A Grosslage covering the slopes of Hattenheim","A district name for the whole Rheingau","A village beside Rüdesheim at the Rhine's bend","None: it is the abbey's walled vineyard, an Ortsteil"],"a":3,"exp":"The Steinberg is the Cistercians' walled vineyard above Hattenheim, registered in 1971 with village standing as an Ortsteil; the whole Rheingau as one district is Bereich Johannisberg.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rht72z8z","q":"Which order of monks came to Eberbach in 1136, founded its abbey and planted the vineyard that became the walled Steinberg?","opts":["The Benedictines","The Augustinians","The Cistercians","The Carthusians"],"a":2,"exp":"Twelve Cistercian monks from Clairvaux founded Eberbach in 1136, after an earlier house there had failed; the Benedictines held the hill at Johannisberg.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Kloster Eberbach: History of the Wine Estate","url":"https://kloster-eberbach.de/en/wein/weingut/geschichte-weingut","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Kloster Eberbach: Steinberg Domain","url":"https://kloster-eberbach.de/en/wein/weingut/domaene-steinberg","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Kloster Eberbach: The Reform Movement around 1100","url":"https://kloster-eberbach.de/en/das-kloster/historie/zisterzienser","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Steinberg, Kloster Eberbach","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steinberg,_Kloster_Eberbach","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Kabinett","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kabinett","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-55f7xq2p": {"id":"fl-55f7xq2p","key":"burklin-wolf","group":"pfalz","region":"pfalz","producer":"Dr. Bürklin-Wolf","producerId":"p-burklin-wolf","say":"BURK-lin VOLF, FOR-ster KEERKH-en-shtook","place":"Wachenheim, with the vineyard at Forst, Mittelhaardt, Pfalz","founded":"1597","wine":{"name":"Forster Kirchenstück G.C.","appellation":"Pfalz","classification":"G.C., the estate's own Grand Cru rank; the site is a VDP.Grosse Lage","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Kirchenstück, a small vineyard directly behind the church of Forst, enclosed by walls and the sandstone houses of the village, which hold the day's warmth: a gentle slope falling towards the village, on basalt, sandstone, limestone rubble and clay over a bank of limestone.","making":"From vines farmed biodynamically across the whole estate since 2005. Like every G.C. wine of the house, it ferments with wild yeasts and is raised only in large oak casks before it is bottled dry.","glass":"White peach, lime zest and pink grapefruit over wet stone and a faint smoky note; dry, taut and finely textured, with bright acidity, a salty grip and a long, tightly wound finish.","ageing":"Reserved for its first five years or so, it opens slowly and keeps for twenty years and more in a good vintage.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"G.C.","means":"Grand Cru: the highest rank in the estate's own classification of its vineyards, not a term of German wine law."},{"term":"Kirchenstück","means":"The church piece: the walled vineyard beside the church of Forst."},{"term":"Forst","means":"The Mittelhaardt village between Wachenheim and Deidesheim where the Kirchenstück lies."}]},"table":"Beside the church at Forst lies the small walled Kirchenstück, which a Bavarian survey of 1828, by the estate's account, placed above every other site; Dr. Bürklin-Wolf bottles its dry Riesling as Forster Kirchenstück G.C. Fermented with wild yeasts in large casks, it is taut, smoky and long.","story":"The house traces its roots in Wachenheim to 1597, and its history names a Bürklin of that age, a town clerk who became mayor of Wachenheim. The modern estate began with a marriage. In 1875 Albert Bürklin (1844 to 1924), a lawyer who later sat in the Reichstag, married a daughter of the Wolf family of Wachenheim, and her family's vineyards in Wachenheim, Forst, Deidesheim and Ruppertsberg came together under both names. He moved to Wachenheim and built the estate up. In 1924 it passed to a great nephew of the same name, Albert Bürklin (1907 to 1979), who by the estate's account led the rebuilding of Pfalz wine after the Second World War and added vineyards and cellars; his eldest daughter took over in 1990. Long before, in 1828, a royal Bavarian survey had ranked the vineyards of the Pfalz for land tax, and the estate records that the Kirchenstück alone received the highest mark. In the 1990s the house made that survey the base of its own ladder, in the manner of Burgundy, printing G.C. and P.C. on its labels, and by 2005 the whole estate was farmed biodynamically. The Kirchenstück stands at the summit of that ladder: a small walled vineyard read as a Grand Cru, though German law knows no such word.","others":[{"name":"Forster Pechstein G.C.","note":"Riesling from the black basalt of the Pechstein, smoky and savoury beside the Kirchenstück."},{"name":"Gaisböhl G.C.","note":"A monopole, a vineyard the estate holds alone, bottled under its own G.C. mark."},{"name":"Wachenheimer Gerümpel P.C.","note":"The home village's leading site, a step below the G.C. wines on the estate's ladder."}],"traps":["G.C. and P.C. are the estate's own marks, built on a survey of 1828; they are not a classification under German wine law.","Bürklin-Wolf, von Buhl and Bassermann-Jordan are the three historic Mittelhaardt estates candidates confuse; Bürklin-Wolf is the one at Wachenheim.","The Kirchenstück of Forst in the Pfalz is not the Kirchenstück of Hohen-Sülzen in Rheinhessen, a separate site."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-u07tn6fp","front":"By which year was the whole Dr. Bürklin-Wolf estate farmed biodynamically?","back":"By 2005, across every vineyard of the estate."},{"id":"fl-176zjj4a","front":"What encloses the Kirchenstück of Forst, and what stands beside it?","back":"Walls and the sandstone houses of the village, which hold the day's warmth, with the church directly beside it; Bürklin-Wolf bottles it as a G.C."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-v86bgy94","q":"A guest reads G.C. on Dr. Bürklin-Wolf's Forster Kirchenstück and asks what it means. What do you say?","opts":["The estate's own Grand Cru rank, built on an 1828 survey","A Prädikat for grapes picked late and very ripe","A mark for wine aged in new French oak barrels","A Grand Cru rank set by German wine law"],"a":0,"exp":"G.C. is the estate's own mark, a ladder built on the royal Bavarian survey of 1828 in the manner of Burgundy; German wine law sets no Grand Cru rank, and G.C. says nothing of ripeness or of barrels.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2dqmdv64","q":"Dr. Bürklin-Wolf owes its double name to a marriage of 1875 with the Wolf family of Wachenheim. Who was the groom?","opts":["Franz Peter Buhl, founder of a Deidesheim estate","Albert Bürklin, a lawyer who later sat in the Reichstag","Andreas Jordan, the Deidesheim grower of 1802","A Huguenot grower of the Catoir family of Haardt"],"a":1,"exp":"Albert Bürklin (1844 to 1924) married into the Wolf family in 1875 and moved to Wachenheim; Jordan and Buhl belong to the Deidesheim estates that grew from the Jordan inheritance.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weingut Dr. Bürklin-Wolf: History","url":"https://www.buerklin-wolf.de/en/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Dr. Bürklin-Wolf: Terroir","url":"https://www.buerklin-wolf.de/en/terroir","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: Kirchenstück, Forst, VDP.Grosse Lage","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/the-wines/vineyardonline/lage/6112-kirchenstueck","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wein.plus Wine Glossary: Dr. Bürklin-Wolf","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/dr-buerklin-wolf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Dr. Bürklin-Wolf producer page","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/weingut-dr-burklin-wolf-e-k/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-j4xnz7xy": {"id":"fl-j4xnz7xy","key":"bassermann-jordan","group":"pfalz","region":"pfalz","producer":"Geheimer Rat Dr. von Bassermann-Jordan","producerId":"","say":"BAH-ser-mahn YOR-dahn, FOR-ster yay-zoo-EE-ten-gar-ten","place":"Deidesheim, Mittelhaardt, Pfalz; the vineyard lies at Forst","founded":"1718","wine":{"name":"Forster Jesuitengarten Riesling Grosses Gewächs","appellation":"Pfalz","classification":"VDP.Grosses Gewächs: the dry wine of a VDP.Grosse Lage, the association's highest site rank","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Jesuitengarten at Forst, named for the old Jesuit house of Neustadt that owned it, on sandy loam, sandstone rubble, marly clay and basalt.","making":"The grapes are pressed gently and the must is split: part ferments with the vineyard's own yeasts in traditional oak Stückfass casks, part in stainless steel. Both stay on their lees until bottling, and the wine is bottled dry.","glass":"Ripe yellow peach, orange zest and pear over honeysuckle and a smoky, stony thread; dry yet broad and juicy, more perfume than grip, with ripe acidity, ending long and savoury.","ageing":"Open and generous within a few years of the vintage, it keeps and deepens for fifteen years and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; half an hour in a decanter opens a young bottle; a large glass for white wine.","label":[{"term":"Geheimer Rat","means":"Privy councillor, an honorary title of the old German states, kept in the estate's name."},{"term":"Grosses Gewächs","means":"Great growth: the dry wine the VDP allows only from a site it ranks Grosse Lage."},{"term":"Jesuitengarten","means":"The Jesuits' garden, a Forst vineyard once owned by the Jesuits of Neustadt."}]},"table":"Named for the Jesuits who once owned the vineyard, Bassermann-Jordan's Forster Jesuitengarten Riesling is a dry Grosses Gewächs, broad, ripe and gently smoky, for roast pork or river fish. Part of it ferments in traditional oak casks with its own yeasts.","story":"A Jordan forebear founded the estate in 1718, as its own history has it. Its maker was Andreas Jordan (1775 to 1848), who took it over at twenty after his parents' deaths and built a house of quality from shrinking assets. He is credited with selling, from 1802, Pfalz wine named for its village and its vineyard. He died in 1848 without a will, and his estate was divided equally among his three children; from the shares came three houses, Jordan, Buhl and Deinhard, the last of them the estate that became von Winning. From 1899 two brothers ran the house together under the Bassermann-Jordan name, Ludwig Bassermann-Jordan (1869 to 1914) and Friedrich von Bassermann-Jordan (1872 to 1959). Friedrich published his history of viticulture, the work that made his name, in 1907, and in 1910 the brothers were among the founders of the association of natural wine auctioneers from which the VDP grew. The vaulted cellar beneath the estate keeps old vintages back to 1811. In 2002 the estate was sold to a Neustadt entrepreneur. The Jesuitengarten gathers the Jordan inheritance: a Forst site, a house of 1718, and the habit of naming a wine for its vineyard.","others":[{"name":"Forster Kirchenstück Riesling Grosses Gewächs","note":"The house's wine from the small walled vineyard beside the church of Forst."},{"name":"Forster Ungeheuer Riesling Grosses Gewächs","note":"Its Grosses Gewächs from the Ungeheuer, another Forst site."}],"traps":["Bassermann-Jordan, von Buhl and von Winning all grew from one estate, Andreas Jordan's, divided among his three children after his death in 1848.","The Jesuitengarten of Forst in the Pfalz is not the Jesuitengarten of Winkel in the Rheingau, a different site in another region.","Geheimer Rat in the estate's name is a title, privy councillor, not a vineyard or a style of wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-arxf64o8","front":"Bassermann-Jordan: how far back do the vintages in its cellar run?","back":"To 1811: the vaulted cellar at Deidesheim keeps bottles from the early nineteenth century on."},{"id":"fl-ktcyjso5","front":"What is Andreas Jordan, maker of the Bassermann-Jordan estate, credited with selling from 1802?","back":"Pfalz wine named for its village and its vineyard."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-xo9usmlv","q":"Why is the Forst vineyard behind Bassermann-Jordan's Grosses Gewächs called the Jesuitengarten?","opts":["Its wine was served at the Jesuit college of Mainz","Jesuit monks first planted Riesling in the Pfalz there","It once was land of the Jesuit house of Neustadt","Jesuits walled it after the Thirty Years War"],"a":2,"exp":"The name recalls the Jesuit college of Neustadt, which held land there from 1764; no source ties the site to Jesuits planting Riesling, walling it or serving its wine at Mainz.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-3p6wygeu","q":"In 1910 the two Bassermann-Jordan brothers helped to found an association of estates. Which body later grew from it?","opts":["The Comité Champagne","The Vinea Wachau","The Traditionsweingüter","The VDP"],"a":3,"exp":"The brothers were among the founders of the association of natural wine auctioneers from which the VDP grew; the Vinea Wachau and the Traditionsweingüter are Austrian, and the Comité Champagne is the trade body of Champagne.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weingut Bassermann-Jordan: Pioniergeist","url":"https://www.bassermann-jordan.de/pioniergeist","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Bassermann-Jordan: Forster Jesuitengarten Grosses Gewächs 2024","url":"https://shop.bassermann-jordan.de/10-2024-forster-jesuitengarten-grosses-gewachs-riesling-trocken.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: Jesuitengarten, Forst, VDP.Grosse Lage","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/the-wines/vineyardonline/lage/6101-jesuitengarten","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wein.plus Wine Glossary: Bassermann-Jordan","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/bassermann-jordan","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Meininger: Weingut Geheimer Rat Dr. von Bassermann-Jordan","url":"https://www.meininger.de/wein/weingueter/weingut-geheimer-rat-dr-von-bassermann-jordan","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-qpxo10cu": {"id":"fl-qpxo10cu","key":"von-buhl","group":"pfalz","region":"pfalz","producer":"Reichsrat von Buhl","producerId":"","say":"RIKES-raht fon BOOL, FOR-ster PEKH-shtine","place":"Deidesheim, Mittelhaardt, Pfalz, with the vineyard at Forst","founded":"1849","wine":{"name":"Forster Pechstein Riesling Grosses Gewächs","appellation":"Pfalz","classification":"VDP.Grosses Gewächs from the Pechstein, a VDP.Grosse Lage of Forst","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Pechstein at Forst, named for its black basalt: rubble from the Pechsteinkopf, an old volcano above the slope, mixed with weathered red sandstone and clay, and basalt brought from an old quarry nearby.","making":"Fermented mostly in the Doppelstück, the 2400 litre oak cask of the Rhine lands, with a small part in stainless steel; the wine is then bottled dry, as every Grosses Gewächs must be.","glass":"Smoke, struck flint and crushed stone over lime, white peach and lemon peel; dry and taut, darker and more savoury than its Forst neighbours, with firm acidity and a salty finish.","ageing":"Closed and smoky in youth, it opens after five years or so and keeps for fifteen to twenty.","serve":"11 to 12 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter helps a young bottle; a tall white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Reichsrat","means":"A title borne by the Buhl family and kept in the estate's name."},{"term":"Pechstein","means":"Pitch stone: the vineyard named for the black basalt in its soil."},{"term":"GG","means":"Short for Grosses Gewächs, the VDP's dry wine from a Grosse Lage."}]},"table":"The black basalt of an old volcano above Forst gives its smoke to Reichsrat von Buhl's Forster Pechstein Riesling, which ferments mostly in large oak casks. Dry and darker voiced than its Forst neighbours, it wants grilled fish.","story":"The Deidesheim estate of Andreas Jordan, split among his three children after his death in 1848, gave this house its start: in 1849 Franz Peter Buhl (1809 to 1862), who had married Jordan's daughter, founded it with her share. His son, Franz Armand Buhl (1837 to 1896), inherited it, and the estate holds that its wine was poured for the toast at the opening of the Suez Canal in 1869. He took the name to the Reichstag, where he sat from 1871 to 1893 and helped to shape the first German wine law of 1892, above all its ban on adulterated wine. The title of Reichsrat, borne by the family, stayed in the estate's name. In 1989 the estate was leased to a Japanese businessman, a lease that ran until 2013; during it, in 2005, an entrepreneur from Neustadt bought the estate, and when the lease ended a new team took over the cellar. The Pechstein, its basalt shed by the Pechsteinkopf and brought from a quarry, gives a darker, smokier wine than its neighbours. Poured beside a Kirchenstück or a Jesuitengarten, it makes plain what basalt adds to Forst.","others":[{"name":"von Buhl Riesling Brut","note":"Traditional method Sekt of Riesling, the sparkling wine the house is also known for."},{"name":"Forster Kirchenstück Riesling Grosses Gewächs","note":"The estate's Kirchenstück, from the walled site at the Forst church."}],"traps":["Reichsrat von Buhl is a Deidesheim estate, though its Pechstein lies in the next village, Forst.","Von Buhl's year is 1849: it began as one of the three shares of Andreas Jordan's estate, divided after 1848, beside Bassermann-Jordan and von Winning.","The Pechstein's basalt is partly native, shed by the Pechsteinkopf, and partly brought from an old quarry nearby."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-shypq3dk","front":"Reichsrat von Buhl's Pechstein: what gives the vineyard its name?","back":"Its black basalt, from the Pechsteinkopf, an old volcano above Forst; Pech means pitch."},{"id":"fl-5vcwp1g1","front":"Which Buhl sat in the Reichstag from 1871 and helped to shape the wine law of 1892?","back":"Franz Armand Buhl (1837 to 1896), son of the founder of Reichsrat von Buhl."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-h91djimc","q":"A guest asks why Reichsrat von Buhl's Forster Pechstein tastes smokier than other Forst Rieslings. Which reason fits?","opts":["Black basalt from an old volcano in its soil","Charred new barriques used for its fermentation","Late picking with a large share of noble rot","Grapes dried on straw mats before pressing"],"a":0,"exp":"The Pechstein is named for its basalt, shed by the Pechsteinkopf volcano and brought from an old quarry nearby; the wine ferments mostly in large Doppelstück casks, not charred new barriques.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-3lpu3l2e","q":"Reichsrat von Buhl was founded at Deidesheim in 1849. From what did it come?","opts":["A Jesuit house of Neustadt closed by the state","One of three shares of the divided Jordan estate","A growers' cooperative founded after a war","The Bavarian crown's own cellar in the Pfalz"],"a":1,"exp":"Andreas Jordan's estate was divided among his three children after his death in 1848, and Franz Peter Buhl, his son in law, founded the house with his wife's share; Jesuit land gave the Jesuitengarten its name, not this estate.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weingut Reichsrat von Buhl: the estate","url":"https://von-buhl.de/en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: Pechstein, Forst, VDP.Grosse Lage","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/the-wines/vineyardonline/lage/6030-pechstein","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Deutsche Biographie: Buhl, Armand","url":"https://www.deutsche-biographie.de/sfz35399.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Weingut Reichsrat von Buhl","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weingut_Reichsrat_von_Buhl","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"SommSelect: Reichsrat von Buhl Pechstein Grosses Gewächs","url":"https://sommselect.com/products/somm2302-rvb19fpgg-750","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-hrmjkbjv": {"id":"fl-hrmjkbjv","key":"muller-catoir","group":"pfalz","region":"pfalz","producer":"Müller-Catoir","producerId":"p-muller-catoir","say":"MUE-ler kah-TWAHR, REES-lah-ner","place":"Haardt, Neustadt an der Weinstrasse, Pfalz","founded":"1744","wine":{"name":"Rieslaner Auslese Herzog","appellation":"Pfalz","classification":"Prädikatswein, Auslese: selected ripe bunches, from the Herzog, a VDP.Erste Lage","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Rieslaner alone, a crossing of Silvaner and Riesling bred in Franconia in 1921.","sites":"The Herzog at Haardt, on the edge of Neustadt, a VDP.Erste Lage on Haardt sandstone, where the estate grows the Rieslaner it bottles as Auslese, Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese.","making":"Picked by hand, with a share of noble rot in many years, then fermented cool in stainless steel so that the fruit and the grape's high acidity stay bright; the wine is bottled sweet, at a moderate alcohol.","glass":"Ripe peach, mango and lime over honey, eucalyptus and dried hay, with a mushroomy touch of noble rot; sweet and full, cut through by vivid acidity, finishing clean, tangy and long.","ageing":"Lovely young for its fruit, it gains depth for many years, its high acidity holding the sweetness in line.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a small white wine glass and a small pour.","label":[{"term":"Rieslaner","means":"The grape: a crossing of Silvaner and Riesling, not a kind of Riesling."},{"term":"Auslese","means":"Selected harvest: the Prädikat for very ripe bunches, here made as a sweet wine."},{"term":"Herzog","means":"The vineyard at Haardt, an Erste Lage in the VDP's ranking of sites."}]},"table":"Not Riesling but Rieslaner, a crossing of Silvaner and Riesling, goes into Müller-Catoir's Rieslaner Auslese Herzog, a sweet wine grown on sandstone at Haardt. Mango, peach and honey ride on piercing acidity, so it can close a meal with a fruit tart.","story":"According to the estate, the Catoirs, a Huguenot family, have grown wine at Haardt, on the edge of Neustadt, since 1744. In 1968, the estate relates, an heir took over a property split by inheritance and, over some forty years, brought it back into one hand. With a cellar master who served the house from the early 1960s until the early 2000s, he raised its standing: they gave as much care to Scheurebe, Muskateller and Rieslaner as to Riesling, and the wines they made were vivid and aromatic. Rieslaner was bred in Franconia in 1921 from Silvaner and Riesling and approved for planting in the Pfalz in 1968; it keeps high acidity, catches noble rot readily, and is grown mainly in Franconia and the Pfalz. Older bottlings of the estate named other sites, the Bürgergarten among them, but its own list leads its Rieslaner from the Herzog, a site on Haardt sandstone, as Auslese, Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese, a ladder of sweetness from one vineyard. It answers the guest who asks to move past Riesling: a German sweet wine from a crossing rather than from Riesling itself.","others":[{"name":"Bürgergarten Im Breumel Riesling GG","note":"The estate's dry Grosses Gewächs, pressed only from the Breumel parcel of the Bürgergarten at Haardt."},{"name":"Scheurebe Kabinett","note":"Grapefruit and blackcurrant leaf, the Pfalz aromatic the house takes as seriously as Riesling."},{"name":"Rieslaner Trockenbeerenauslese Herzog","note":"Shrivelled noble rot berries from the same site, at a tiny yield."}],"traps":["Rieslaner is a crossing of Silvaner and Riesling, not a clone or a style of Riesling.","Müller-Catoir has no link to Müller-Thurgau, whatever the shared name.","The Rieslaner Auslese on the estate's own list is from the Herzog; older Rieslaners named the Bürgergarten, which gives the dry Riesling of the house."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-81wznw1v","front":"Müller-Catoir: what did the heir who took over in 1968 set out to do with the split property?","back":"Bring it back into one hand, over some forty years, as the estate tells it."},{"id":"fl-zmx4p4jg","front":"Which Haardt vineyard does Müller-Catoir name on its Rieslaner Auslese?","back":"The Herzog, a VDP.Erste Lage on Haardt sandstone."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-gnltdtur","q":"Rieslaner on Müller-Catoir's Auslese: is it a kind of Riesling?","opts":["Yes: a clone of Riesling chosen for sweet wine","Yes: Riesling picked late in the Herzog","No: a crossing of Silvaner and Riesling","No: a crossing of Riesling and Bukettraube"],"a":2,"exp":"Rieslaner was bred in 1921 from Silvaner and Riesling; Riesling and Bukettraube are the parents of Scheurebe, another grape the estate grows.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-us5c6pmo","q":"The family behind Müller-Catoir has grown wine at Haardt since 1744, by the estate's account. From what background did it come?","opts":["A Jesuit house of Neustadt","A Bavarian noble line from Munich","A Swiss family of grape breeders","A Huguenot family with roots in France"],"a":3,"exp":"The Catoirs are a Huguenot family, growing wine at Haardt since 1744 by the estate's account; the Müller in the name has nothing to do with Müller-Thurgau or Swiss breeding.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weingut Müller-Catoir: About","url":"https://www.mueller-catoir.de/en/about","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Müller-Catoir: Rieslaner Auslese Herzog","url":"https://www.mueller-catoir.de/en/product/rieslaner-edelsuess-herzog-auslese","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Müller-Catoir: Rieslaner Beerenauslese Herzog sheet","url":"https://www.mueller-catoir.de/en/product/pdf/rieslaner-edelsuess-herzog-beerenauslese-2018-375","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Rieslaner","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rieslaner","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein.plus Wine Glossary: Rieslaner","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/rieslaner","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-wuw8xygp": {"id":"fl-wuw8xygp","key":"becker","group":"pfalz","region":"pfalz","producer":"Friedrich Becker","producerId":"","say":"FREED-rikh BECK-er, KAHM-er-bairg","place":"Schweigen, Pfalz, from vines across the border at Wissembourg in Alsace","founded":"1973","wine":{"name":"Spätburgunder Kammerberg Grosses Gewächs","appellation":"Pfalz","classification":"VDP.Grosses Gewächs from the Kammerberg, a VDP.Grosse Lage","colour":"red","grapes":"Spätburgunder, the German name for Pinot Noir, alone.","sites":"The Kammerberg, part of the Schweigener Sonnenberg on the French side of the border: a slope falling south towards Wissembourg at up to 30 per cent, on weathered limestone, loam and clay over pure limestone.","making":"The berries are destemmed and fermented in open wooden vats, the cap punched down each day; the mash is then carried to the press by hand and pressed gently. The wine matures slowly in French oak barriques, for up to twenty months, before bottling.","glass":"Sour cherry, wild strawberry and red currant over clove, smoke and a cool, chalky stoniness; fine grained and taut, with fresh acidity, toast from the barrique in youth and a long, savoury close.","ageing":"Tight and marked by its oak for its first five years, it opens with time in bottle and lasts fifteen years and more.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; an hour's decanting suits a young bottle; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Spätburgunder","means":"The German name for Pinot Noir; later labels may print Pinot Noir instead."},{"term":"KB","means":"The initials under which the estate's later labels name the Kammerberg, its parcel in France."},{"term":"Grosses Gewächs","means":"Often shortened to GG: a dry wine of the VDP from a vineyard it ranks Grosse Lage."},{"term":"Pfalz","means":"The German region on the label, though the Kammerberg's vines stand across the border in France."}]},"table":"Just across the border in Alsace, on limestone, grow the vines of Friedrich Becker's Spätburgunder Kammerberg, yet the wine is sold as Pfalz from Schweigen. Destemmed, fermented in open wooden vats and raised in French barriques, it is cherry fruited, chalky and fine grained.","story":"Until the 1970s the Beckers of Schweigen ran a mixed farm on the southern edge of the Pfalz and sold their wine to the village cooperative. In 1973 the son who took over from his parents left the cooperative and filled the first bottles under the family's own name, with a fox from one of Aesop's fables on the label, in the estate's own story. He turned to Spätburgunder, which the estate says has grown on these limestone slopes for centuries. Those slopes are the heart of the story. The Schweigener Sonnenberg runs over the border into France, and the Kammerberg lies on its French side, facing Wissembourg, yet its wine is made at Schweigen and sold as Pfalz. Benedictine monks of the abbey at Wissembourg once kept Kammerweine from it in their cellar; the site then lay forgotten until 1965, when, by the VDP's account and the estate's, a Becker took it up again. So the Kammerberg makes a favourite trap: a German Grosses Gewächs grown in France.","others":[{"name":"Spätburgunder Sankt Paul Grosses Gewächs","note":"From the Sankt Paul, a second parcel of the Sonnenberg on the French side, also on limestone."},{"name":"Spätburgunder Heydenreich Grosses Gewächs","note":"From the Heydenreich, another of the estate's Grosse Lage sites for Pinot Noir."},{"name":"Pinot Noir Schweigen","note":"The estate's village Pinot Noir, beneath the Grosse Lage wines on its ladder."}],"traps":["The Kammerberg lies in France, at Wissembourg, yet the wine is labelled Pfalz: it is a parcel of the Schweigener Sonnenberg on the French side, farmed and pressed from Schweigen.","Spätburgunder is Pinot Noir under its German name, not a late harvest wine.","Kammerberg and Sankt Paul are parcels within the Schweigener Sonnenberg, not villages of their own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-18eqvlpe","front":"How is Friedrich Becker's Kammerberg fermented and raised?","back":"Destemmed, fermented in open wooden vats with the cap punched down each day, then up to twenty months in French barriques."},{"id":"fl-2mzp8wwb","front":"What changed at the Becker estate of Schweigen in 1973?","back":"The son who took over left the village cooperative and filled the estate's first bottles, with the fox on the label."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ajcc6bs5","q":"A guest is surprised that a Pfalz Spätburgunder from Friedrich Becker grows in France. What explains the Kammerberg's label?","opts":["Alsace law allows a German name on any Pinot Noir","It is a parcel of the Sonnenberg, which runs into France","The wine is made in France and only bottled in Germany","The vines were moved from Alsace to Schweigen in 1973"],"a":1,"exp":"The Kammerberg is a parcel of the Schweigener Sonnenberg on its French side, farmed from Schweigen and sold as Pfalz; the vines were never moved, and the wine is made at Schweigen.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ylqnp1kn","q":"Monks once kept Kammerweine from the Kammerberg in their cellar. Which house did they serve?","opts":["The Benedictine abbey of Wissembourg","The Cistercian abbey of Eberbach","The cathedral chapter of Speyer","The Jesuit house of Neustadt"],"a":0,"exp":"The VDP records that Benedictine monks of the abbey at Wissembourg kept Kammerweine in their cellar; Eberbach was the Cistercians' abbey in the Rheingau, far to the north.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weingut Friedrich Becker: Unser Weingut","url":"https://weingut-friedrich-becker.de/weingut/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weingut Friedrich Becker: Red wines","url":"https://www.friedrichbecker.de/en/winemaking/red-wines/14/red-wines.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: Kammerberg, Schweigen, VDP.Grosse Lage","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/the-wines/vineyardonline/lage/6306-kammerberg","kind":"primary"},{"title":"VDP: Estate Friedrich Becker","url":"https://www.vdp.de/en/die-winzer/pfalz/vdpestate-friedrich-becker","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Irish Times: Run for the border wine","url":"https://www.irishtimes.com/life-and-style/food-and-drink/drink/run-for-the-border-wine-1.543214","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-dty7b1n9": {"id":"fl-dty7b1n9","key":"fx-pichler","group":"wachau","region":"wachau-kamptal-kremstal","producer":"F.X. Pichler","producerId":"p-fx-pichler","say":"ef IKS PIKH-ler, KEL-er-bairk","place":"Oberloiben by Dürnstein, Wachau, Niederösterreich","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Riesling Smaragd Ried Kellerberg","appellation":"Wachau DAC","classification":"Smaragd of the Vinea Wachau; Riedenwein, the single vineyard tier of the Wachau DAC","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Kellerberg above Dürnstein: steep dry stone terraces of Gföhl gneiss facing south to south east, sheltered from the westerly winds of the Ice Ages, so patches of loess survive. Its name, from the baroque Kellerschlössl of Dürnstein, dates only from 1787 and gathered several smaller vineyards.","making":"Picked by hand late in the autumn, as the Wachau DAC requires of every wine and the ripeness of a Smaragd asks. The codex of the Vinea Wachau forbids any enrichment of the must and allows a Smaragd no hint of oak, and the wine is fermented dry; the house speaks of as little intervention as it can manage in the cellar.","glass":"Mango, passion fruit and pineapple over ripe yellow peach and a stony core; dense and broad for a dry Riesling, with high extract, ripe acidity and an alcohol that reads as weight rather than heat, and it lingers.","ageing":"Broad from the start, it is at its fullest after eight to ten years; strong vintages last twenty years and beyond.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, cool rather than cold; decant a young bottle for an hour if it seems closed; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Smaragd","means":"The Vinea Wachau's ripest dry style, at least twelve and a half per cent alcohol, named for the emerald lizard."},{"term":"Ried Kellerberg","means":"The single vineyard; Austrian law asks for the word Ried before a vineyard's name."},{"term":"Dürnsteiner Kellerberg","means":"The older form on the label, the Kellerberg of Dürnstein, before Ried was required."},{"term":"Wachau DAC","means":"The appellation since the 2020 vintage; its single vineyard wines are Grüner Veltliner or Riesling alone."}]},"table":"On steep gneiss terraces above Dürnstein in the Wachau, F.X. Pichler grows its Riesling Smaragd Ried Kellerberg. Dry yet broad, with mango and ripe peach over stone, it carries the weight of the valley's ripest style, so pour it cool rather than cold.","story":"A grower from Rohrendorf near Krems, a forebear of the family, moved into the Wachau in the middle of the nineteenth century, the house recounts, and a grandson bought a property at Oberloiben in 1898; the house dates its winery there to 1905. The F.X. of the name, a later grower of the family who studied at the wine school of Krems and worked vintages in Germany and Alsace, set out to show each site at the full ripeness of its grapes. In 1983 the growers of the valley founded the Vinea Wachau, with its codex against enrichment and its three styles of Steinfeder, Federspiel and Smaragd, and F.X. Pichler stood with it from the beginning. Two years later a scandal over doctored sweet wines emptied Austria's export markets and brought a strict new wine law. The Riesling Kellerberg of 1990 was, by the house's own reckoning, the wine that carried its name abroad, as the dry Smaragd wines of the Wachau led Austria's recovery. In 2009 the family moved into a new winery at Oberloiben, and the Kellerberg has been a Ried of the Wachau DAC since the 2020 vintage. In the glass it is the measure of how much ripeness a dry Riesling can carry.","others":[{"name":"Riesling Smaragd Ried Loibenberg","note":"Warmer and broader, from the hill above Loiben, the direct comparison with the Kellerberg."},{"name":"Grüner Veltliner Smaragd Ried Kellerberg","note":"Grüner Veltliner at full Smaragd weight from the same terraces."},{"name":"Unendlich","note":"A selection made only in years the house judges fit, a Riesling from 1998 and a Grüner Veltliner from 2003; the name means endless."}],"traps":["F.X. Pichler is not Rudi Pichler or Pichler-Krutzler: several unrelated Pichler estates work the Wachau, so read the initials.","Smaragd is a trademark of the Vinea Wachau, a growers' association: it measures ripeness, says nothing of the vineyard and has no standing in Austrian law."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-wy6wkkw6","front":"F.X. Pichler's Kellerberg: what rock lies under its terraces?","back":"Gföhl gneiss, a metamorphic rock, with patches of loess that the Ice Ages left on the sheltered south east slope."},{"id":"fl-vhcy5vrb","front":"Where does the name of F.X. Pichler's Kellerberg come from, and how old is it?","back":"From the baroque Kellerschlössl of Dürnstein; the name dates only from 1787 and gathered several smaller vineyards."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-eizx3onw","q":"Smaragd on the label of F.X. Pichler's Riesling Ried Kellerberg leads a guest to expect sweetness. What is true of the wine?","opts":["It is a sweet wine of grapes touched by noble rot","It is late picked and keeps some natural sugar","It is a dry wine aged a year in new oak","It is dry: the word marks the Wachau's ripest dry style"],"a":3,"exp":"Smaragd is the Vinea Wachau's ripest dry style, fermented dry to at least twelve and a half per cent alcohol; the codex allows it no hint of oak, so the new oak answer is wrong as well.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-nq1a45xm","q":"By the house's own account, the Riesling Kellerberg of which vintage first carried the name of F.X. Pichler abroad?","opts":["1985","2009","1990","1983"],"a":2,"exp":"The house dates its breakthrough abroad to the Kellerberg of 1990; 1983 is the founding of the Vinea Wachau, 1985 Austria's wine scandal and 2009 the house's new winery.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"F.X. Pichler: History","url":"https://fx-pichler.at/en/winery/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"F.X. Pichler: Philosophy","url":"https://fx-pichler.at/en/winery/philosophy/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Kellerberg, vineyard details","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/en/mywachau/vineyards/vineyard-details/myw_vineyard/kellerberg","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinea Wachau: the three wine styles Steinfeder, Federspiel and Smaragd","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/en/translate-to-englisch-vinea-wachau/vinea-wine-styles","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Wachau now a DAC region","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/press-media/news/news-1/article/wachau-now-a-dac-region","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-myqg2mi5": {"id":"fl-myqg2mi5","key":"hirtzberger","group":"wachau","region":"wachau-kamptal-kremstal","producer":"Franz Hirtzberger","producerId":"p-franz-hirtzberger","say":"HIRTS-bair-ger, ZING-er-ree-del","place":"Spitz, in the western Wachau, Niederösterreich","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Riesling Smaragd Ried Singerriedel","appellation":"Wachau DAC","classification":"Smaragd of the Vinea Wachau; Riedenwein, the single vineyard tier of the Wachau DAC","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone.","sites":"The Singerriedel at Spitz, first recorded in 1486, narrow terraces held by dry stone walls: shallow, sandy, meagre soil over paragneiss, with mica schist and bands of quartzite lower down; Riesling grows on the steep terraces and Grüner Veltliner at the foot.","making":"The terraces are harvested by hand late in the autumn, as the Wachau DAC requires and a Smaragd's ripeness asks, and the Vinea Wachau's codex allows no enrichment. Fermented dry in stainless steel, the wine is often then raised in large wooden casks of three to five thousand litres, which give it no taste of oak; some vintages stay in steel.","glass":"Apricot, ripe yellow peach and lime zest, with orange blossom, over the smoky note of wet rock; full and creamy yet dry, with firm acidity and a long trail of salt.","ageing":"Generous young, it settles after about five years and, in a strong vintage, goes on for two decades and more, gaining honey and dried apricot.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; if a young bottle seems tight, give it an hour in a decanter; a generous white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Smaragd","means":"The ripest of the Vinea Wachau's three dry styles, from twelve and a half per cent alcohol, named after the emerald green lizard."},{"term":"Ried Singerriedel","means":"The single vineyard, a steep wall of narrow terraces at Spitz, first recorded in 1486."},{"term":"Wachau DAC","means":"The valley's appellation from the 2020 vintage, which demands that every wine be picked by hand."}]},"table":"Narrow dry stone terraces at Spitz, in the western Wachau, are home to Franz Hirtzberger's Riesling Smaragd Ried Singerriedel. Full and creamy yet dry, with ripe peach and apricot on a stony core, it rewards a decade in the cellar.","story":"The modern Hirtzberger estate, at Spitz in the western Wachau, begins with the grower of the family who took over his parents' holding in 1948: by the account of the Vinea Wachau, he bottled its wine for the first time and picked out the terraces of single vineyards the family farms. In 1983 he was one of the growers who founded the Vinea Wachau, the association that bars any enrichment of the must and sorts a dry wine by its ripeness as Steinfeder, Federspiel or Smaragd. His son took over the estate that same year, saw it through the scandal year of 1985 and the stricter law it brought, and led the association from 1988 to 2012, as the dry wines of the Wachau found their way abroad. The Singerriedel, the steep wall of narrow terraces on paragneiss at Spitz, gave the house its signature Riesling, picked late for Smaragd and fermented dry. From the 2020 vintage the Wachau DAC has carried it as a Ried, a single vineyard of its own. In 2014 a son of the family founded a separate estate, the Weinhofmeisterei at Wösendorf, so read the whole name on the label: the Singerriedel of Spitz is Franz Hirtzberger's.","others":[{"name":"Grüner Veltliner Smaragd Ried Honivogl","note":"Grüner Veltliner of full Smaragd weight, ripe, powerful and dry, often named with the house beside the Singerriedel."},{"name":"Riesling Smaragd Ried Hochrain","note":"A second single vineyard Riesling Smaragd of the house, from the Hochrain."}],"traps":["The Weinhofmeisterei at Wösendorf, founded in 2014 by a son of the family, is a separate estate; Franz Hirtzberger is the house at Spitz.","Honivogl and Singerriedel are vineyard names, not producers.","However ripe it is, a Smaragd is a dry wine: the word measures ripeness, never sweetness."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-qs8dh49f","front":"On what rock and soil does Franz Hirtzberger's Singerriedel stand?","back":"Paragneiss, with mica schist and bands of quartzite, under shallow, sandy soil held by dry stone walls."},{"id":"fl-mlmq364u","front":"What did the Hirtzberger grower who took over in 1948 do, by the Vinea Wachau's account?","back":"He bottled the estate's wine for the first time and picked out the single vineyard terraces the family farms."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-taojmsrk","q":"Where do the vines of Franz Hirtzberger's Riesling Smaragd Ried Singerriedel grow?","opts":["The deep loess plain east of Krems","Narrow dry stone terraces at Spitz","A fan of stream debris at Unterloiben","The sandstone hill above Zöbing"],"a":1,"exp":"The Singerriedel is a steep wall of terraces on paragneiss at Spitz, in the western Wachau; the sandstone hill above Zöbing is the Heiligenstein of the Kamptal, and the fan at Unterloiben is the Schütt.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-i1shljhi","q":"The grower who took over the Hirtzberger holding at Spitz in 1948 later helped to found which body?","opts":["The Vinea Wachau","The Grosser Ring of Trier","The Österreichische Traditionsweingüter","The Domäne Wachau cooperative"],"a":0,"exp":"He was among the founders of the Vinea Wachau in 1983, whose codex forbids enrichment; the Traditionsweingüter is a separate, private association, the one behind the 1ÖTW mark.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Weingut Franz Hirtzberger","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/en/mywachau/companies/company-details/myw_company/weingut-franz-hirtzberger","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Singerriedel, vineyard details","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/en/mywachau/vineyards/vineyard-details/myw_vineyard/singerriedel","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Hundert Sessel und zwei Hirtzberger Brüder","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/news/events/archiv/detail/news/hundert-sessel-und-zwei-hirtzberger-brueder","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Vinea Wachau feiert 40 Jahre","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/fileadmin/user_upload/Presseaussendung_40_Jahre_Vinea.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Falstaff: Franz Hirtzberger 2018 Riesling Smaragd Ried Singerriedel","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/wines/weingut-franz-hirtzberger-2018-riesling-smaragd-ried-singerriedel","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9yz0xq1z": {"id":"fl-9yz0xq1z","key":"knoll","group":"wachau","region":"wachau-kamptal-kremstal","producer":"Emmerich Knoll","producerId":"p-knoll","say":"EM-er-ikh KNOLL, SHUTT","place":"Unterloiben by Dürnstein, Wachau, Niederösterreich","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Grüner Veltliner Smaragd Ried Schütt","appellation":"Wachau DAC","classification":"Smaragd of the Vinea Wachau; Riedenwein, the single vineyard tier of the Wachau DAC","colour":"white","grapes":"Grüner Veltliner alone.","sites":"The Schütt, astride the boundary of Dürnstein and Unterloiben, on a fan of stream debris at the mouth of the Menthalgraben, between the Höhereck and the Loibenberg: deep, sandy and stony soil of weathered gneiss, poor in fine earth, so it drains fast.","making":"The grapes come in by hand, as the Wachau DAC requires, late in the autumn, and the Vinea Wachau's codex rules out any enrichment. Once fermented dry, the wine is raised mostly in large, old oak casks that give it air and texture rather than a taste of wood, with some stainless steel beside them.","glass":"White pepper, green pear and quince over lentils and wet stone, with tobacco leaf as it ages; dense and savoury rather than fruity, dry and long, with a fine, stony grip.","ageing":"Dense and savoury young, it deepens over five to ten years, and a strong year will hold for twenty years or longer.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; give a young bottle an hour of air in a decanter; a large bowled white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Smaragd","means":"Emerald, for the green lizard: the Vinea Wachau's ripest dry style, at twelve and a half per cent alcohol or more."},{"term":"Ried Schütt","means":"The single vineyard, a fan of gneiss debris between Dürnstein and Unterloiben."},{"term":"Saint Urban","means":"The baroque image of the saint that has marked Knoll bottles since 1962."}]},"table":"The baroque Saint Urban stands on the label of Emmerich Knoll's Grüner Veltliner Smaragd Ried Schütt, grown on a stony fan of gneiss at Unterloiben. Peppery, savoury and dense, it is long lived: cellar it for years and give it some air in the glass.","story":"Since the early nineteenth century the Knolls have grown vines at Unterloiben, at the eastern end of the Wachau. In the house's memory, Knoll wine went into glass bottles from the 1950s, and in 1962 the baroque image of Saint Urban first appeared on the label, where it has stayed. The Knoll wines carry the styles of the Vinea Wachau, the valley's growers' association of 1983, in which Smaragd marks the ripest dry wine. When the scandal of 1985 brought a tighter wine law, the house kept its own pace, with late picking and large old oak casks. From 1988 it added a Vinothekfüllung, a dry selection of late picked Grüner Veltliner made in nearly every year, joined by a Riesling in 2000. Its vineyards lie around the village, the Loibenberg, the Kellerberg and the Kreutles among them, with the Pfaffenberg across the border in the Kremstal, yet the house names the Schütt before them all. With the 2020 vintage the Schütt became a Ried of the Wachau DAC. Here is the proof that Grüner Veltliner from the Wachau can last for decades.","others":[{"name":"Riesling Smaragd Ried Schütt","note":"Riesling from the same stony fan, slow to open and long lived."},{"name":"Grüner Veltliner Smaragd Vinothekfüllung","note":"A dry selection of late picked grapes, made in nearly every year since 1988."},{"name":"Riesling Ried Pfaffenberg","note":"From a site across the border in the Kremstal, so it carries no Smaragd."}],"traps":["Knoll's Pfaffenberg lies in the Kremstal, outside the Wachau, so the Vinea Wachau's three styles are closed to it.","Vinothekfüllung is Knoll's own word for a selection; neither the law nor the Vinea Wachau defines it.","The Saint Urban figure on the label is the house's own mark, not a regional seal."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-kyk2p00e","front":"Emmerich Knoll's Ried Schütt: how was the ground of the vineyard formed?","back":"As a fan of stream debris at the mouth of the Menthalgraben: deep, sandy, stony weathered gneiss that drains fast."},{"id":"fl-hn9hhxk2","front":"What did Emmerich Knoll add to its range in 1988?","back":"A Vinothekfüllung, a dry selection of late picked Grüner Veltliner made in nearly every year since, joined by a Riesling in 2000."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-kqmd1mxh","q":"Emmerich Knoll raises its Grüner Veltliner Smaragd Ried Schütt in oak, yet it shows no taste of wood. Why?","opts":["It is aged in chestnut casks, never in oak","The barrels are charred heavily to hide their flavour","The casks are large and old, so they give no oak flavour","It spends only a few days in new barrels"],"a":2,"exp":"Knoll raises its Smaragd mostly in large old oak casks, with some steel, which give air and texture without wood flavour; a few days in new barrels would leave toast and vanilla, and the codex allows a Smaragd no hint of oak.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-rcf3445e","q":"Which image has stood on the labels of Emmerich Knoll since 1962?","opts":["The ruined castle of Dürnstein","A falconer's lure","An emerald green lizard","A baroque Saint Urban"],"a":3,"exp":"The baroque Saint Urban first appeared on Knoll bottles in 1962; the emerald lizard gives the Smaragd style its name and the falconer's lure the Federspiel.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Weingut Knoll","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/en/mywachau/companies/company-details/myw_company/weingut-knoll","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinea Wachau: Schütt, vineyard details","url":"https://www.vinea-wachau.at/en/mywachau/vineyards/vineyard-details/myw_vineyard/schuett/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"SommSelect: Behind the Wine, Weingut Emmerich Knoll","url":"https://sommselect.com/blogs/tasting-notes/behind-the-wine-weingut-emmerich-knoll","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Source Imports: Weingut Emmerich Knoll, Riesling Ried Schütt Smaragd","url":"https://thesourceimports.com/?p=11494","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Sommelier Academy: Emmerich Knoll","url":"https://sommelier-academy.de/topic/emmerich-knoll/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-j3qww8wm": {"id":"fl-j3qww8wm","key":"brundlmayer","group":"wachau","region":"wachau-kamptal-kremstal","producer":"Bründlmayer","producerId":"p-brundlmayer","say":"BREWNDL-my-er, TSUR-bing-er HY-lig-en-shtine","place":"Langenlois, Kamptal, Niederösterreich","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Riesling Zöbinger Heiligenstein Alte Reben","appellation":"Kamptal DAC","classification":"Riedenwein of the Kamptal DAC; Erste Lage, marked 1ÖTW, of the Österreichische Traditionsweingüter","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling alone, from old vines, a few of them planted before the Second World War.","sites":"The Zöbinger Heiligenstein above Zöbing, a bare hill of Permian sandstone and conglomerate with volcanic grains, its face held by dry stone terraces built by hand from the 1920s; part of the house's share is trained on the Lyra, a divided, lyre shaped trellis.","making":"The juice ferments in stainless steel at fifteen to twenty degrees Celsius, and the wine then rests on its lees, in steel and in large wooden casks, until it is bottled some months later.","glass":"Orange peel, yellow peach and apricot over smoke, warm stone and dried thyme; dry and firm, with fine, salty acidity, a silky weight and real length.","ageing":"Taut on release, it opens after five to seven years, and a strong vintage outlives two decades.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter while it is young; a white wine glass with room in the bowl.","label":[{"term":"Ried Zöbinger Heiligenstein","means":"The single vineyard on the sandstone hill above Zöbing; newer labels print Ried Heiligenstein."},{"term":"Alte Reben","means":"Old vines: the house's old plantings on the Heiligenstein."},{"term":"1ÖTW","means":"The Traditionsweingüter's own mark for a site it ranks Erste Lage; Austrian law framed classified sites only in 2023."},{"term":"Kamptal DAC","means":"The appellation since the 2008 vintage, Grüner Veltliner and Riesling above all."}]},"table":"Old vines on the Zöbinger Heiligenstein, the Kamptal's hill of desert sandstone above Zöbing, are the source of Bründlmayer's Riesling Alte Reben. Dry and firm, apricot and orange peel over smoke and warm stone, it keeps for decades.","story":"Bründlmayer is a family estate of Langenlois, in the Kamptal, whose Heuriger stands in a Renaissance vintner's house in the town. Its signature hill, the Heiligenstein above Zöbing, is usually said to appear in a monastic register of 1280 as Hellenstein, for the heat of its bare slope, before the name became Heiligenstein, the holy stone. From the 1920s dry stone terraces were built by hand on its face, and some of the vines on it lived through the Second World War. In 1980 the son of the house succeeded his parents; in time part of the hill was trained on the Lyra trellis, and a sparkling wine made in the bottle joined the range. In 1996 the Bründlmayers lent a hand when a new family took on Schloss Gobelsburg nearby. The Kamptal DAC followed with the 2008 vintage, and from 2010 the Traditionsweingüter, a private association of estates, ranked its members' sites, the Heiligenstein among its Erste Lagen. In 2015 the vineyards were certified organic. Alte Reben is the house's selection from its old vines on the hill, Austrian Riesling grown on desert sandstone rather than on the Wachau's gneiss.","others":[{"name":"Grüner Veltliner Ried Lamm","note":"Grüner Veltliner from deep loess and loam on the south east side of the Heiligenstein, rich and long."},{"name":"Riesling Ried Heiligenstein","note":"The hill's Riesling without the old vine selection, the step below the Alte Reben."},{"name":"Extra Brut Reserve","note":"Sparkling wine made by the traditional method, its second fermentation in the bottle."}],"traps":["The Heiligenstein is Permian sandstone and conglomerate with volcanic grains, not limestone, whatever its holy stone name suggests.","The Kamptal is not the Wachau: Smaragd, Federspiel and Steinfeder never appear on Bründlmayer's labels.","1ÖTW is the private mark of the Traditionsweingüter, not a rank in Austrian wine law."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-nkigpjx0","front":"Bründlmayer's Heiligenstein: what rock is the hill, despite its holy name?","back":"Permian sandstone and conglomerate with volcanic grains, laid down in a desert; no limestone."},{"id":"fl-heaomjr6","front":"What does Alte Reben on Bründlmayer's Heiligenstein Riesling mean?","back":"Old vines: the house's selection from its old plantings on the hill, a few planted before the Second World War."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-rxlcunx9","q":"By the house's own sheets, how is Bründlmayer's Riesling Alte Reben from the Heiligenstein made?","opts":["Steel fermentation, then rest on its lees in large casks","Left sweet by stopping the fermentation early","Picked frozen on the vine in a hard winter","Fermented and aged a year in new barriques"],"a":0,"exp":"By the house's own sheets the juice ferments in stainless steel and the wine then rests on its lees in steel and large wooden casks; a year in new barriques would give it toast and vanilla.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-bp8h65gr","q":"In 1996 the Bründlmayers helped a new family take over the running of which neighbouring estate?","opts":["Nikolaihof","Schloss Gobelsburg","Domäne Wachau","Weingut Prager"],"a":1,"exp":"Gobelsburg's own history says its new managers took over from the monks of Zwettl in 1996 with the Bründlmayers' help; Nikolaihof, Domäne Wachau and Prager are Wachau houses.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bründlmayer: Ried Zöbinger Heiligenstein Alte Reben, factsheet","url":"https://www.bruendlmayer.at/en/wines/zoebinger-heiligenstein-1oetw-alte-reben-riesling-2018/factsheet/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bründlmayer: Vineyard","url":"https://www.bruendlmayer.at/en/winery/vineyard/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: the legal basis for an official vineyard classification","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/press-media/news/news-1/article/austria-defines-the-legal-basis-for-an-official-vineyard-classification-system","kind":"primary"},{"title":"wein.plus lexicon: Heiligenstein","url":"https://glossary.wein.plus/heiligenstein","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Schloss Gobelsburg: History","url":"https://www.gobelsburg.at/en/castle/history/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-mqhpv5lh": {"id":"fl-mqhpv5lh","key":"gobelsburg","group":"wachau","region":"wachau-kamptal-kremstal","producer":"Schloss Gobelsburg","producerId":"p-schloss-gobelsburg","say":"shloss GOH-bels-boorg, LAHM","place":"Gobelsburg by Langenlois, Kamptal, Niederösterreich","founded":"1171","wine":{"name":"Grüner Veltliner Ried Lamm","appellation":"Kamptal DAC","classification":"Riedenwein of the Kamptal DAC; Erste Lage, marked 1ÖTW, of the Österreichische Traditionsweingüter","colour":"white","grapes":"Grüner Veltliner alone.","sites":"The Lamm, on the south east side of the Heiligenstein, where the hill eases into gentler slopes: deep loess and loam, mixed with material washed down from the hill's Permian rock and volcanic stone.","making":"Fermented and matured in large traditional casks of five to six thousand litres, made of Austrian oak from the nearby Manhartsberg forest, with an occasional new cask among them. Under the Kamptal DAC a single vineyard wine may not be submitted for its state test number before April of the year after the harvest.","glass":"Ripe pear, quince and yellow apple over white pepper, lentils and toasted hazelnut, with a hint of honey; broad, creamy and dry, fresh in its acidity and savoury to the last.","ageing":"Its richness makes it ready on release; it builds depth over five to ten years, and a strong vintage keeps fifteen years or longer.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; a young bottle wants half an hour in a decanter; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Ried Lamm","means":"The single vineyard of loess and loam on the south east side of the Heiligenstein."},{"term":"1ÖTW","means":"An Erste Lage in the private ranking of the Traditionsweingüter; classified sites entered Austrian law only in 2023."},{"term":"Kamptal DAC","means":"Grüner Veltliner and Riesling above all: the appellation of the Kamptal since the 2008 vintage."}]},"table":"Deep loess and loam on the south east side of the Heiligenstein, a hill where the monks of Zwettl held vines from 1171, as the house tells it, carry Schloss Gobelsburg's Grüner Veltliner Ried Lamm. Broad and dry, pear, quince and white pepper, it matures in large Austrian oak casks.","story":"The Cistercian monks of Zwettl Abbey received their first vineyards in the Kamptal, on the Heiligenstein and the Gaisberg, in 1171, by the house's history. The castle of Gobelsburg passed through many noble families until 1740, when its last aristocratic owner sold it to the abbey, which already held an estate at Kammern nearby; late in the eighteenth century, after a fire at Kammern, the making of the wine moved to Gobelsburg. The monks ran the cellar themselves until 1995. In 1996 a family took over the running of the estate on a long lease, with early help from the Bründlmayers of Langenlois, while the abbey kept the ownership. The new managers added a Tradition line, an ode to the cellar ways of Gobelsburg between 1800 and 1850. With the 2008 vintage the Kamptal became a DAC, and the private ranking of the Traditionsweingüter put the Lamm and the Heiligenstein among its Erste Lagen. In 2021 the house marked eight hundred and fifty years of winegrowing. Fullest of its Grüner Veltliners, the Lamm is a Kamptal wine of full weight from loess and loam that carries a monastic estate's history.","others":[{"name":"Grüner Veltliner Tradition","note":"The house's homage to the cellar ways of Gobelsburg between 1800 and 1850, a range of its own."},{"name":"Riesling Ried Heiligenstein","note":"Riesling from the hill where the monks of Zwettl received their first vineyards in 1171."}],"traps":["Look for no Wachau terms on a Gobelsburg label: the estate is in the Kamptal, where Smaragd, Federspiel and Steinfeder have no place.","Tradition names a range of the house's own, built on a method; Austrian law has no such category.","The monks of Zwettl ran the cellar until 1995; from 1996 a family ran the estate, so the wine is not made by monks."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-pnesjwin","front":"Schloss Gobelsburg's Lamm: what soil lies on the south east side of the Heiligenstein?","back":"Deep loess and loam, mixed with material washed down from the hill's Permian rock and volcanic stone."},{"id":"fl-fp2jbgg9","front":"What does Schloss Gobelsburg's Tradition range set out to recall?","back":"The cellar ways of Gobelsburg between 1800 and 1850; the managers of 1996 added the line."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-f70tpc8d","q":"Schloss Gobelsburg's Grüner Veltliner Ried Lamm carries 1ÖTW on its label. What does the mark mean?","opts":["The ripest dry style of the Vinea Wachau","A wine aged a full year before release","A Grosse Lage in Austria's national wine law","An Erste Lage of a private growers' ranking"],"a":3,"exp":"1ÖTW is the Traditionsweingüter's own mark for a site it ranks Erste Lage; Austrian law framed its own classified sites only in 2023, and Smaragd, the Vinea Wachau's ripest dry style, never appears on a Kamptal wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-p8hqvmgt","q":"The Cistercian abbey of Zwettl bought Schloss Gobelsburg from its last noble owner. In what year?","opts":["1171","1996","1740","2008"],"a":2,"exp":"The abbey bought the castle in 1740; its monks had held vineyards nearby since 1171, a family took over the running on a lease in 1996, and 2008 brought the Kamptal DAC.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Schloss Gobelsburg: History","url":"https://www.gobelsburg.at/en/castle/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Schloss Gobelsburg: Grüner Veltliner Lamm","url":"https://www.gobelsburg.at/en/wines/schloss-gobelsburg/gruener-veltliner-lamm/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Skurnik: Grüner Veltliner Ried Lamm Erste Lage, Schloss Gobelsburg","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/sku/gruner-veltliner-ried-lamm-erste-lage-schloss-gobelsburg/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Schloss Gobelsburg","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schloss_Gobelsburg","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Kamptal","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/winegrowing-regions/niederoesterreich-lower-austria/kamptal","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-snqo66p1": {"id":"fl-snqo66p1","key":"kracher","group":"burgenland","region":"burgenland","producer":"Weinlaubenhof Kracher","producerId":"p-kracher","say":"KRAHKH-er, TROCK-en-BAIR-en-OWS-lay-zeh","place":"Illmitz, in the Seewinkel on the eastern shore of the Neusiedlersee, Burgenland","founded":"1959","wine":{"name":"Kracher Trockenbeerenauslese","appellation":"Burgenland","classification":"Prädikatswein: Trockenbeerenauslese, from shrivelled, botrytised berries of at least 30 degrees KMW","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Welschriesling, Chardonnay, Scheurebe, Traminer and Muskat Ottonel, and Zweigelt in some years; most numbered wines are of one grape, the Grande Cuvée a blend.","sites":"Flat vineyards between Illmitz and the shore of the shallow Neusiedlersee, where autumn mists off the water bring noble rot almost every year.","making":"The year's botrytised wines are kept apart, each mostly of one grape. No sugar may be added: the sweetness is the grape's own, left when the fermentation stops. Zwischen den Seen wines are raised in steel tanks or large wooden casks for clear fruit, Nouvelle Vague wines in new barriques. Since 1995 every Trockenbeerenauslese of the year has been numbered, the denser the wine, the higher its number.","glass":"Glazed pineapple, candied lemon and mango over honey in the Zwischen den Seen wines, with the toast and vanilla of new oak in the Nouvelle Vague; luscious and dense, yet lifted by fresh acidity, and long.","ageing":"Open and fruity on release, it keeps for decades, the fruit slowly giving way to honey, dried apricot and toffee.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a small white wine glass, and small pours.","label":[{"term":"Trockenbeerenauslese","means":"Prädikatswein from shrivelled, botrytised berries of at least 30 degrees KMW, never sweetened or chaptalised."},{"term":"No. 1, No. 2 and upward","means":"The wine's place in that year's collection: the denser the wine, the higher the number, by the house's own account."},{"term":"Zwischen den Seen","means":"Between the lakes: the line raised in steel tanks or large wooden casks, for pure fruit."},{"term":"Nouvelle Vague","means":"New wave: the line raised in new barriques, the Grande Cuvée among them."}]},"table":"Weinlaubenhof Kracher, at Illmitz by the Neusiedlersee, numbers each year's Trockenbeerenauslesen, the denser the wine the higher the number. The Welschriesling from steel or large casks gives glazed pineapple and honey, while the Nouvelle Vague wines take the toast of new oak.","story":"The estate began as a small mixed farm at Illmitz, in the Seewinkel; the house says its farmer had long wanted to make wine, and in 1959 the winery was founded. That same year his son, Alois Kracher (1959 to 2007), was born. Trained as a chemist, the son began working beside his father in 1981, at twenty two, and turned the estate towards the botrytised sweet wines that the lake's autumn mists make possible. In the years after the diethylene glycol scandal of 1985, which had wrecked the name of Austria's sweet wines abroad, the Kracher wines went out into the world, and in the house's telling the 1991 vintage made their name. From 1995, by the house's reckoning, every Trockenbeerenauslese of a year was numbered, the denser the wine the higher its number, each in one of two lines, Zwischen den Seen or Nouvelle Vague. Alois Kracher died of cancer in December 2007, at forty eight, and his son took the estate on. A run of Kracher numbers on a list is a lesson in botrytis at one sitting: grape against grape, steel against new oak, and the number telling how dense each wine will be.","others":[{"name":"Grande Cuvée Trockenbeerenauslese","note":"A blend of Chardonnay and Welschriesling, raised in new barriques and numbered among the Nouvelle Vague wines."},{"name":"Beerenauslese Cuvée","note":"Welschriesling and Chardonnay at Beerenauslese ripeness, the lighter way into the house's sweet wines."},{"name":"Auslese Cuvée","note":"Welschriesling and Chardonnay at Auslese ripeness, and lighter again."}],"traps":["The numbers rank density within one vintage, the higher the denser; they are no measure of quality, and the No. 7 of one year is not the No. 7 of another.","Zwischen den Seen and Nouvelle Vague name the vessel and the style, steel or large casks against new barriques, not the sweetness or the site.","Welschriesling is no relation of Riesling, and no Illmitz wine is ever an Ausbruch, a name kept for Rust across the lake."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-c1v4eq4h","front":"Kracher: from which vintage has every Trockenbeerenauslese of the year carried a number?","back":"1995, as the house dates it: the denser the wine, the higher the number."},{"id":"fl-44bi5jrv","front":"Which Kracher line is raised in new barriques, and which in steel tanks or large casks?","back":"Nouvelle Vague in new barriques; Zwischen den Seen in steel tanks or large wooden casks."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-1f4atnd7","q":"A guest compares two Kracher Trockenbeerenauslesen of one vintage, No. 3 and No. 9. What does the higher number tell the guest?","opts":["The wine is denser and sweeter","The grapes grew on a higher slope","The wine is judged the better of the two","The wine has spent longer in bottle"],"a":0,"exp":"The house numbers each year's Trockenbeerenauslesen by density, the higher the denser and sweeter; the number ranks no quality, and the No. 9 is no older than the No. 3.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-z0ws9y49","q":"Alois Kracher joined his father's estate at Illmitz in 1981, at twenty two. What had he trained as?","opts":["A banker","A chemist","A chef","A cooper"],"a":1,"exp":"He trained as a chemist and then turned the family estate to botrytised sweet wine; the estate itself had begun in 1959 as a small mixed farm, not a cooperage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Weinlaubenhof Kracher: History","url":"https://www.kracher.at/en/about_us/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Weinlaubenhof Kracher: Definitions","url":"https://www.kracher.at/en/news_press/definitions","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Prädikat levels","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/wine-styles/10-nobly-sweet-wines/praedikat-levels","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Alois Kracher dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/alois-kracher-dies-85990/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Austria-Forum: Alois Kracher","url":"https://austria-forum.org/af/AustriaWiki/Alois_Kracher","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-d3wvrobz": {"id":"fl-d3wvrobz","key":"feiler-artinger","group":"burgenland","region":"burgenland","producer":"Feiler-Artinger","producerId":"p-feiler-artinger","say":"FY-ler AR-ting-er, ROOST-er OWS-brookh","place":"Rust, on the western shore of the Neusiedlersee, Burgenland","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Ruster Ausbruch","appellation":"Ruster Ausbruch DAC","classification":"Prädikatswein at Trockenbeerenauslese ripeness, at least 30 degrees KMW, from Rust alone","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"White grapes of Rust, the grape or the blend changing with the bottling: Weissburgunder, Chardonnay, Welschriesling, Pinot Gris, Neuburger and Gelber Muskateller among them.","sites":"Vineyards within the town limits of Rust, on limestone and schist sloping gently down to the western shore of the lake, whose autumn mists bring noble rot; the Ried Oberer Wald gives one bottling of its own.","making":"The shrivelled, botrytised berries are picked by hand, broken out of the bunch, which, the house explains, gives Ausbruch its name, and every grape must grow within the town limits of Rust. Nothing may be added to sweeten it. Recent bottlings are raised in barriques, new in part for the Pinot Cuvée and used for the Essenz; some older vintages were raised partly in steel.","glass":"Candied apricot, orange peel and honey, with the faint toast of a used barrique; rich and sweet but never syrupy, its fresh acidity carrying it a long way.","ageing":"Charming young for its apricot fruit, which over decades in bottle turns slowly to dried fruit, toffee and honey.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, poured from the bottle without decanting, in small measures and a small glass, beside blue cheese or an apricot tart.","label":[{"term":"Ruster Ausbruch DAC","means":"From the 2020 vintage, Rust's own appellation, reserved for sweet wine from botrytised, naturally shrivelled berries grown in the town."},{"term":"Prädikatswein","means":"Wine of a Prädikat, picked ripe and never chaptalised or sweetened: its sweetness is the grape's own."},{"term":"Pinot Cuvée","means":"A blend of Weissburgunder and Chardonnay, with Pinot Gris and Neuburger in some years."}]},"table":"Feiler-Artinger keeps Rust's old sweet wine alive in a baroque house on the town's main street: Ruster Ausbruch, honeyed and full of candied apricot yet fresh, made from botrytised berries broken out of the bunch by hand.","story":"Rust, on the western shore of the Neusiedlersee, has lived by sweet wine for five centuries. In June 1524 Mary of Hungary (1505 to 1558) granted its growers the right to burn an R into their casks, and in 1681, as Austrian Wine tells it, the town bought its charter as a royal free town from Emperor Leopold I (1640 to 1705) for sixty thousand gulden and five hundred pails of Ausbruch. In 1936 a couple of the Feiler family bought a baroque burgher's house at Hauptstrasse 3, in the old centre, and made wine there; the husband first bottled it in 1947, and in 1953, on the house's own word, he made Ruster Ausbruch again after the war. The next generation ran the estate with a sister of the family, and in the middle 1980s, as the glycol scandal of 1985 shook Austria's sweet wine, her married name, Artinger, joined Feiler's on the label. In 2016 the law reserved the word Ausbruch to Rust, and from the 2020 vintage Ruster Ausbruch DAC became, in Austrian Wine's words, the country's first origin protected for sweet wine alone, picked at Trockenbeerenauslese ripeness. Feiler-Artinger's Ausbruch is the answer to a question every candidate meets: a place, not a technique, and Rust's own.","others":[{"name":"Ruster Ausbruch DAC Essenz","note":"The densest bottling, very sweet and low in alcohol, raised for twenty months and more in used barriques."},{"name":"Solitaire","note":"The house's red blend: Blaufränkisch with Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon."}],"traps":["Ausbruch is Rust's alone: an Illmitz or Seewinkel sweet wine of the same ripeness is a Trockenbeerenauslese, never an Ausbruch.","Rust lies on the lake's western shore beside the Leithaberg; Illmitz and the Seewinkel lie across the water to the east.","Ausbruch once sat between Beerenauslese and Trockenbeerenauslese; from the 2020 vintage Ruster Ausbruch DAC is picked at Trockenbeerenauslese ripeness."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-i613ecl8","front":"Feiler-Artinger: when did the family buy its baroque house in Rust, and when did it first bottle wine?","back":"It bought the house at Hauptstrasse 3 in 1936 and first bottled its wine in 1947."},{"id":"fl-9fwux73j","front":"Where does the word Ausbruch come from, as Feiler-Artinger explains it?","back":"From the shrivelled berries broken out of the bunch by hand at harvest."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-q1tfefgc","q":"Feiler-Artinger may label its sweet wine Ruster Ausbruch, while an Illmitz estate of the same ripeness may not. Why?","opts":["Only a growers' club may use the word","Only Furmint may be used for an Ausbruch","An Ausbruch must age ten years in cask","The name is reserved to grapes grown in Rust"],"a":3,"exp":"Since the law of 2016 and the DAC of 2020, Ausbruch is Rust's alone, from botrytised grapes grown in the town and picked by hand. No single grape is required, so Furmint is not the reason.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-tam07j8g","q":"In 1681 Rust bought its charter as a royal free town from Emperor Leopold I. What did it pay, as Austrian Wine tells it?","opts":["Its vineyards on the Leithaberg","A fleet of boats for the lake","Gulden and pails of Ausbruch","A tithe of its grain harvest"],"a":2,"exp":"The town paid sixty thousand gulden and five hundred pails of Ausbruch. The privilege of branding its casks with an R came earlier, in 1524, from Mary of Hungary.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Feiler-Artinger: Die Geschichte","url":"https://www.feiler-artinger.at/die-geschichte/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Feiler-Artinger: Ruster Ausbruch","url":"https://www.feiler-artinger.at/ruster-ausbruch/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Leithaberg, including Rust","url":"https://www.austrianwine.com/our-wine/winegrowing-regions/burgenland/leithaberg-incl-rust","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Falstaff: Weingut Feiler-Artinger in Rust","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/wineries/weingut-feiler-artinger","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Burgenland Vinothek: Feiler-Artinger, Rust","url":"https://burgenland-vinothek.at/produzent/feiler-artinger-rust/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2entfuwb": {"id":"fl-2entfuwb","key":"moric","group":"burgenland","region":"burgenland","producer":"Moric","producerId":"p-moric","say":"MO-ritz, LUTS-mahns-boorg","place":"Lutzmannsburg in Mittelburgenland, with the cellar at Grosshöflein, Burgenland","founded":"2001","wine":{"name":"Blaufränkisch Lutzmannsburg Alte Reben","appellation":"Burgenland","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Blaufränkisch alone.","sites":"A selection of the house's oldest vines at Lutzmannsburg, in the south of Mittelburgenland, the eldest about a century old: sand, clay and loam, partly over limestone, on a plateau the house describes as an ancient volcano.","making":"Fermented on native yeasts in open wooden vats, about three weeks on the skins, then aged for about twenty months on its fine lees in large, used oak casks and bottled without fining or filtration, so that no new oak shows.","glass":"Dark cherry and blackberry with cracked black pepper and violet; rounder and darker than the Neckenmarkt, yet fine boned and savoury, with bright acidity and fine, firm tannin.","ageing":"Tight in its first years, it opens after five or so and keeps for two decades in a strong vintage.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter suits a young bottle; a Burgundy glass, for its perfume.","label":[{"term":"Alte Reben","means":"Old vines: here a selection of the house's oldest at Lutzmannsburg, picked parcel by parcel."},{"term":"Lutzmannsburg","means":"The village in the south of Mittelburgenland where the vines grow."},{"term":"Burgenland","means":"The broad origin of the federal state, rather than the Mittelburgenland DAC in which the village lies."}]},"table":"Moric ferments its Blaufränkisch Lutzmannsburg Alte Reben, from the house's oldest vines in the south of Mittelburgenland, with native yeasts in open wooden vats, then rests it in large used casks rather than new oak. It is dark cherry and black pepper, savoury and fine.","story":"Moric, said MO-ritz, dates from the 2001 vintage. Its maker came from the Velich family of Apetlon, in the Seewinkel, whose estate, Weingut Velich, is a separate house. Through the 1990s much Austrian red wine had chased weight, with long extraction and new barriques, and Moric went the other way. Its founder took on old Blaufränkisch vines, many on long leases and some about a century old, at Neckenmarkt in the north of Mittelburgenland and at Lutzmannsburg in the south, to compare what each village's soil and air gave the one grape, and took the fruit to a cellar at Grosshöflein on the Leithaberg. There the wines ferment with their own yeasts in open wooden vats and rest in large used casks for as long as they need, so that the vineyard speaks rather than the oak. With the 2005 vintage Mittelburgenland became a DAC for Blaufränkisch alone, yet the Lutzmannsburg is labelled simply Burgenland. Lutzmannsburg, on a plateau of sand, clay and loam partly over limestone, gives the rounder, darker wine; Neckenmarkt, on steep slopes of slate and limestone, the tauter one. Poured side by side, the two Alte Reben are the argument Moric makes: one grape, two villages, two wines.","others":[{"name":"Blaufränkisch Neckenmarkt Alte Reben","note":"Old vines on steep slopes of slate and limestone, the tauter, more structured of the pair."},{"name":"Blaufränkisch Burgenland","note":"The entry Blaufränkisch, labelled simply Burgenland, that carries the house's reading of the grape."}],"traps":["Moric is a separate house from Weingut Velich of Apetlon, though the two spring from one family.","Lutzmannsburg lies inside Mittelburgenland, yet Moric's wine is labelled Burgenland, not Mittelburgenland DAC.","Zweigelt is a crossing of Blaufränkisch and St. Laurent, never another name for Blaufränkisch."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-hn3v8rmt","front":"Moric Lutzmannsburg Alte Reben: what ground do its vines grow on, in the house's description?","back":"Sand, clay and loam, partly over limestone, on a plateau over an ancient volcano."},{"id":"fl-v2tiu0p7","front":"Which two villages give Moric its two old vine Blaufränkisch?","back":"Neckenmarkt in the north of Mittelburgenland and Lutzmannsburg in the south."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-3sn7dth0","q":"What lets Moric's Lutzmannsburg Alte Reben taste of its vineyard more than of oak?","opts":["A long cold soak in stainless steel","Native yeasts and old casks, no new barriques","A share of Zweigelt to soften it","A short rest in small new barrels"],"a":1,"exp":"Moric ferments with its own yeasts and rests the wine about twenty months in large used casks, so no new oak shows; the wine is Blaufränkisch alone, with no Zweigelt.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-s99jsd5x","q":"Moric's founder came from a family estate in the Seewinkel before he began Moric with the 2001 vintage. In which village is that estate?","opts":["Apetlon","Deutschkreutz","Rust","Gols"],"a":0,"exp":"Weingut Velich is at Apetlon in the Seewinkel; Rust lies on the far side of the lake, to the west, and Deutschkreutz is a Blaufränkisch village of Mittelburgenland.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Moric: Blaufränkisch tasting list, 2009","url":"https://www.moric.at/2009_verkostungsliste_eng.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinography: MORIC, the apogee of Blaufränkisch","url":"https://www.vinography.com/2014/08/moric_the_apogee_of_blaufrnkis","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Lebendige Weine: Moric, producer page","url":"https://www.lebendigeweine.de/en/moric/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Berry Bros and Rudd: 2020 Blaufränkisch, Lutzmannsburg, Alte Reben, Moric","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-20208066491-2020-blaufrankisch-lutzmannsburg-alte-reben-moric-burgenland-austria","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Grapeguru: Weingut Moric","url":"https://www.grapeguru.de/en/knowledge/wineries/moric","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-frx5kpm5": {"id":"fl-frx5kpm5","key":"triebaumer","group":"burgenland","region":"burgenland","producer":"Ernst Triebaumer","producerId":"p-ernst-triebaumer","say":"TREE-bow-mer, MAH-ree-en-tahl","place":"Rust, in the hills above the western shore of the Neusiedlersee, Burgenland","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Blaufränkisch Ried Mariental","appellation":"Burgenland","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Blaufränkisch alone.","sites":"The Ried Mariental at Rust: lean limestone soil, a microclimate of its own and very old vines, as the house describes it; the family's Oberer Wald, an inherited site whose rows run north to south, gives a cooler, slimmer wine.","making":"After fermentation on its skins, it spends close to two years in small oak barrels ahead of bottling; it is a dense, oak framed Blaufränkisch, built to be cellared rather than drunk young.","glass":"Sour cherry, blackberry and plum over cedar and cocoa, with a stony edge; firm, fine grained tannin and fresh acidity carry the oak to a long, taut finish.","ageing":"Firm and marked by oak when young, it needs about ten years to settle and lasts for twenty and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Ried Mariental","means":"The named single vineyard at Rust, of lean limestone soil, that gives the wine its name."},{"term":"Rust","means":"The free town on the lake's western shore, the village named on the label."},{"term":"Blaufränkisch","means":"The grape, called Kékfrankos in Hungary and Lemberger in Germany."}]},"table":"Ernst Triebaumer's Blaufränkisch Ried Mariental grows on lean limestone soil at Rust, and its 1986 vintage opened a new chapter for Austrian red wine. Close to two years in small oak barrels make it firm and dark, so give it ten years.","story":"Like Rust's other growers, who have made sweet Ausbruch for centuries, the Triebaumers made it too, but their name travels on a dry red. In 1976, as the house tells it, a Rust couple who felt they lacked the equipment to do justice to their plot in the Mariental sold it to the family. Its lean limestone soil, its microclimate and its very old Blaufränkisch vines suited a red to keep. The 1986 Blaufränkisch Mariental came only a year after the diethylene glycol affair of 1985 had left Austrian wine's name abroad in ruins, and the house recalls that it had won almost every tasting it entered by 1988; Falstaff dates a new era of Austrian red wine from it. In later vintages the wine has spent close to two years in small oak barrels, a firmer frame than the large casks of much Austrian Blaufränkisch. Beside it the family bottles the Oberer Wald, a site it inherited, whose rows run north to south, as a slimmer, cooler counterpart. When a guest asks where Austria's modern Blaufränkisch began, the Mariental of 1986 is the vintage the story is usually told from.","others":[{"name":"Blaufränkisch Ried Oberer Wald","note":"An inherited site whose rows run north to south: a cooler, slimmer Blaufränkisch."},{"name":"Ruster Ausbruch","note":"The town's sweet wine from botrytised, shrivelled berries, made by the house beside its reds."}],"traps":["Several Triebaumer estates make wine in Rust; the Mariental of 1986 is the Ernst Triebaumer estate's, so read the full name on the label.","Kékfrankos in Hungary and Lemberger in Germany are Blaufränkisch by other names, whereas Zweigelt, its offspring, is a grape of its own.","The Mariental spends close to two years in small oak barrels, not in the large casks of much modern Blaufränkisch."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-zccoesoh","front":"Which vintage of Ernst Triebaumer's Blaufränkisch Ried Mariental marked a turning point for Austrian red wine?","back":"1986, the year after the glycol scandal."},{"id":"fl-r6m4rzkn","front":"Ernst Triebaumer's Ried Mariental at Rust: what kind of soil does the house say it has?","back":"Lean limestone soil, with a microclimate of its own and very old vines."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6elutst2","q":"How is Ernst Triebaumer's Blaufränkisch Ried Mariental aged before it is bottled?","opts":["A year in concrete eggs","Four years in large old casks","Six months in stainless steel only","Nearly two years in small oak barrels"],"a":3,"exp":"The Mariental rests close to two years in small oak barrels; large old casks are the way of other Blaufränkisch, not of this one.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-8pa7rgmf","q":"The Triebaumer family came by its Mariental plot at Rust in 1976. How, by the house's own account?","opts":["It was a gift of the Esterházy princes","It came with a house on the main street","A Rust couple sold it to the family","The family planted it on cleared forest"],"a":2,"exp":"A local couple who felt they lacked the equipment to do justice to the plot sold it to the family in 1976; the first famous vintage followed ten years later.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Ernst Triebaumer: Vineyard, the sites","url":"https://www.triebaumer.com/seite.mv?20-10-00-00+=&lg=en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Falstaff: Weingut Ernst Triebaumer","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/wineries/weingut-ernst-triebaumer","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"wein.plus: Triebaumer, Ernst","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/triebaumer-ernst","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Flaschenpost: Blaufränkisch Mariental, Weingut Ernst Triebaumer","url":"https://www.flaschenpost.ch/en/blaufraenkisch-mariental_weingut-ernst-triebaumer","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Falstaff: 2022 Blaufränkisch Ried Mariental, wine entry","url":"https://www.falstaff.com/en/wines/weingut-ernst-triebaumer-2022-blaufraenkisch-ried-mariental-1","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ewiz73ke": {"id":"fl-ewiz73ke","key":"prieler","group":"burgenland","region":"burgenland","producer":"Prieler","producerId":"","say":"PREE-ler, GOLT-bairg","place":"Schützen am Gebirge, on the Leithaberg west of the Neusiedlersee, Burgenland","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Blaufränkisch Ried Goldberg","appellation":"Leithaberg DAC","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Blaufränkisch alone.","sites":"The Goldberg above Schützen am Gebirge, on the slope of the Leitha hills: mica schist under clayey loam, its glittering flecks the gold of the name, as the house tells it. Schist warms quickly, so the Goldberg ripens warmer than the cooler limestone Marienthal.","making":"Aged for about two years in wood before bottling. For the 2022 the house's own sheet gives the detail: fermentation in open vats with the cap punched down by hand, some three weeks of skin contact, then twenty six months in large casks.","glass":"Black cherry, blackberry and sloe with cracked pepper and a savoury note of dried herbs, over something cool and stony; the tannin firm and finely grained, the acidity fresh, the finish long and savoury.","ageing":"Its youthful firmness eases after five to eight years, and a warm vintage will go twenty years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; open a young bottle into a decanter an hour or two ahead; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Leithaberg DAC","means":"The appellation of the Leitha hills west of the lake; its red, Blaufränkisch led, counts from the 2008 vintage."},{"term":"Ried Goldberg","means":"Ried marks a single vineyard: this one lies above Schützen am Gebirge, on mica schist, and is recorded since 1555."}]},"table":"Mica schist lies under Prieler's Blaufränkisch Ried Goldberg, above Schützen am Gebirge on the slope of the Leithaberg, and the glitter of the mica is the gold in its name. After about two years in wood it shows black cherry and pepper over firm tannin.","story":"The Prieler family's roots in Schützen am Gebirge, on the western side of the Neusiedlersee, run deep: the house says the family appears in the village's records as early as 1347, and the Goldberg, the slope of mica schist above the village, is named in documents of 1555. A grandfather of the family, the house recounts, first put its wine into bottles, and in 1972 the next generation gave the mixed farm over wholly to wine, with Blaufränkisch and the Pinot grapes at its heart, as two importers' histories tell it. Schist warms easily in the sun, the house explains, so the Goldberg ripens warmer than the family's Marienthal on limestone, and the pair show how one grape answers two rocks. Leithaberg DAC came into force in 2010, its reds Blaufränkisch led and counted from the 2008 vintage, and the Goldberg has since come to carry Leithaberg DAC and Ried Goldberg on its label. Beside them the family bottles the Haidsatz, limestone over schist, as a Pinot Blanc of one vineyard. Set the Goldberg against a Blaufränkisch from limestone and the schist shows in a wine that is darker, more peppery and firm.","others":[{"name":"Blaufränkisch Leithaberg DAC Ried Marienthal","note":"From the cooler Marienthal at Oggau, on limestone, the counterpart to the Goldberg's schist."},{"name":"Pinot Blanc Leithaberg DAC Ried Haidsatz","note":"The house's white of one vineyard, Weissburgunder from limestone over schist."}],"traps":["Schützen am Gebirge lies on the Leithaberg, west of the lake; Deutsch Schützen is a village of the Eisenberg in the far south.","The gold of the Goldberg is, in the house's words, the glitter of mica in its schist, not a golden wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-0pfhiu0d","front":"Prieler: when did the family turn its mixed farm wholly over to wine, and to which grapes above all?","back":"In 1972, with Blaufränkisch and the Pinot grapes at its heart, as two importers' histories tell it."},{"id":"fl-5xgyfwcx","front":"In what year is the Goldberg above Schützen am Gebirge first named in documents?","back":"1555, by the house's account."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-r4udfnmr","q":"Prieler's Blaufränkisch Ried Goldberg ripens warmer than the family's Marienthal. What reason does the house give?","opts":["Mica schist that warms quickly in the sun","Longer ageing in new barriques","Grapes dried on reed mats first","A share of Zweigelt in the blend"],"a":0,"exp":"The house says schist warms easily, so the Goldberg ripens warmer than the cooler Marienthal on limestone; the wine is Blaufränkisch, with no Zweigelt, and its grapes are never dried.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-497w1dvk","q":"The house says the Prieler family appears in the records of Schützen am Gebirge from which century?","opts":["The nineteenth century","The fourteenth century","The seventeenth century","The twentieth century"],"a":1,"exp":"The family is named in the village's records in 1347, by the house's account; the Goldberg itself is named in documents of 1555, in the sixteenth century.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Prieler: the family and the estate","url":"https://www.prieler.at/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Prieler: Sites","url":"https://www.prieler.at/en/sites/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Prieler: Ried Goldberg Blaufränkisch 2022","url":"https://shop.prieler.at/products/ried-goldberg-blaufrankisch-2022-bio","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Skurnik: Prieler, producer page","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/weingut-familie-prieler/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Austrian Wine: Leithaberg DAC","url":"https://austrianwine.com/our-wine/dac-districtus-austriae-controllatus/dac-regions/leithaberg-dac","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-szmaha2z": {"id":"fl-szmaha2z","key":"royal-tokaji","group":"tokaj","region":"tokaj","producer":"The Royal Tokaji Wine Company","producerId":"p-royal-tokaji","say":"TOH-koy-ee, MAY-zesh MAHY","place":"Mád, Tokaj, north east Hungary; the vineyard lies at Tarcal","founded":"1990","wine":{"name":"Royal Tokaji Mézes Mály 6 Puttonyos","appellation":"Tokaji Aszú","classification":"Great First Growth, ranked above the first class in Tokaj's eighteenth century classification","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Furmint for the greater part, with Hárslevelű; some older vintages also held a little Sárga Muskotály, the yellow Muscat.","sites":"Mézes Mály, a warm slope above Tarcal whose loess topsoil, the house says, gives the wine its honeyed, blossom scented cast; the hill's name is recorded from the Middle Ages.","making":"Aszú berries, shrivelled by noble rot and picked one by one, soak for some two days in must that is already fermenting before they go to the press. The fermentation finishes in used casks of Zemplén oak, of three hundred and five hundred litres, where the wine matures for about two years in cellars the house dates to the thirteenth century.","glass":"Apricot, orange marmalade and honey, with a scent of blossom that the house puts down to the loess; dense and very sweet, with Furmint's high, saline acidity holding it upright to the end.","ageing":"Sweet and open from the day it is released, it lives for decades, its acidity holding the sugar in balance as the colour deepens towards amber.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; leave the decanter aside; a small pour in a little white wine glass, with the dessert or as the dessert.","label":[{"term":"Tokaji Aszú","means":"The sweet wine of noble rotted aszú berries steeped in must or wine; at least 120 grams of sugar a litre."},{"term":"6 Puttonyos","means":"An optional mark since the 2013 vintage, kept for an Aszú of at least 150 grams of sugar a litre."},{"term":"Great 1st Growth","means":"The house's words on later vintages, where older labels said 1st Growth, for the vineyard's historic rank."}]},"table":"Royal Tokaji draws its Mézes Mály 6 Puttonyos, an Aszú, from one historic vineyard at Tarcal, ranked above the first class in the eighteenth century. Furmint's bright acidity holds up its honey and apricot, and the house credits the loess with its scent of blossom.","story":"Mézes Mály, whose name speaks of honey, is a hill at Tarcal recorded in the Middle Ages, far older than the house that bottles it, and Tokaj's eighteenth century surveys set it with its neighbour Szarvas above the first class. Through the communist decades the district's vines were worked by the state and its cooperatives, and the old vineyard names left the labels. In 1990, as Hungary left communism, a British wine writer and a winemaker from Denmark joined about sixty growers of Mád to found the company, among the first foreign ventures in Tokaj after the change. Its first wine, by its own account, was an Aszú of 1990 from the Betsek vineyard, and through its first decade it made Aszú alone, reviving the custom of naming the classified vineyards on the label: Betsek, Nyulászó, Szent Tamás and Mézes Mály. In 2010, its twentieth year, it opened a new winery at Mád, and later vintages of Mézes Mály, once labelled First Growth, carry the words Great First Growth. It reads Tokaj's old map of vineyards back into the glass, with the same Aszú method, one named site and the loess beneath it.","others":[{"name":"Royal Tokaji Aszú 5 Puttonyos, Red Label","note":"The house's widely poured Aszú, the bottle most guests meet before the single vineyards."},{"name":"Szent Tamás Aszú 6 Puttonyos","note":"A first class site of Mád, firmer and stonier than Mézes Mály."},{"name":"Royal Tokaji Essencia","note":"The free run nectar of the aszú berries, made only in years that allow it."}],"traps":["Mézes Mály lies at Tarcal, not at Mád: the company is based at Mád but bottles vineyards of more than one village.","Puttonyos 3 and 4 were abolished from the 2013 vintage; every Aszú holds at least 120 grams of sugar a litre, and 6 Puttonyos marks at least 150.","Tokaj is the place and Tokaji the wine; Mézes Mály and Szarvas, both at Tarcal, were the two vineyards set above the first class."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-1syvn60z","front":"Royal Tokaji Mézes Mály: in which village does the vineyard lie, and on what topsoil?","back":"Tarcal, on loess, which the house credits with the wine's honeyed, blossom scented character."},{"id":"fl-w1fc0mjq","front":"Which vineyard gave The Royal Tokaji Wine Company its first wine, an Aszú of 1990?","back":"Betsek, a first class site of Mád: an Aszú 5 Puttonyos, by the house's own account."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-n5rkhrgb","q":"Royal Tokaji's Mézes Mály carries the words Great First Growth. What do they tell a guest?","opts":["It comes from vines planted before the war at Mád","It is the first wine the house releases each year","It is made of aszú berries alone, with no base wine","Its vineyard was ranked above the first class long ago"],"a":3,"exp":"Tokaj's eighteenth century surveys set Mézes Mály and Szarvas, both at Tarcal, above the first class; the words name the vineyard's rank, not a release order or a level of sugar.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-t2uje3g0","q":"Foreign partners and the growers of Mád founded The Royal Tokaji Wine Company together. When?","opts":["1992","1737","1990","2010"],"a":2,"exp":"The company was founded in 1990 as Hungary left communism; 1737 is the royal decree that closed the Tokaj district, and 2010 the year of its new winery at Mád.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Royal Tokaji: Our Estate","url":"https://royal-tokaji.com/our-estate/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Royal Tokaji: Mézes Mály Great 1st Growth Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos 2018","url":"https://royal-tokaji.com/portfolio/mezes-maly-great-1st-growth-tokaji-aszu-6-puttonyos-2018/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wilson Daniels: Royal Tokaji and the classification of the vineyards","url":"https://wilsondaniels.com/?p=33672","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Vinous: A Mád idea, Royal Tokaji 1990 to 2013","url":"https://v1.vinous.com/articles/mad-idea-royal-tokaji-1990-2013-jul-2019","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Royal Tokaji, the first decade tasting","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/royal-tokaji-the-first-decade-378530/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-mcpbwkmq": {"id":"fl-mcpbwkmq","key":"oremus","group":"tokaj","region":"tokaj","producer":"Tokaj Oremus","producerId":"p-oremus","say":"OR-eh-moosh, TOLCH-vah","place":"Tolcsva, in the north of the Tokaj district, Hungary","founded":"1993","wine":{"name":"Oremus Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos","appellation":"Tokaji Aszú","classification":"","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Furmint and Hárslevelű, with Zéta and Sárga Muskotály, Tokaj's yellow Muscat, in most vintages.","sites":"Parcels around Tolcsva in the north of the district, on volcanic rock and clay, and in the historic Oremus vineyard on a hill at Sátoraljaújhely that lent the estate its name.","making":"By the old measure, as the house describes it, six puttonyos, hods of about 25 kilograms of aszú berries, go to each 136 litre gönci barrel of must. The berries soak for a day or two until they swell, are gently pressed and ferment slowly for weeks in new Hungarian oak; the wine then ages two to three years in small barrels of 136 and 220 litres in cellars cut into volcanic rock, and rests in bottle before release.","glass":"Honey, candied orange peel and saffron over a hint of wild berries; silky and deeply sweet, with a sharp acidity that leaves the palate clean.","ageing":"Held back through its years in barrel and bottle, it drinks well once released, and a cool cellar will keep it for decades.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; it needs no decanter; a small wine glass, alone at the end of the meal.","label":[{"term":"Tokaj Oremus","means":"The estate's name, from the historic Oremus vineyard; oremus is Latin for let us pray."},{"term":"Tokaji Aszú","means":"Shrivelled, noble rotted berries soaked in must; never below 120 grams of sugar a litre."},{"term":"6 Puttonyos","means":"Six hods of aszú berries to a gönci barrel in the old measure; by law at least 150 grams of sugar a litre."}]},"table":"Tokaj Oremus, the Tolcsva estate bought in 1993 by the family of Vega Sicilia, gives its Aszú 6 Puttonyos two to three years in small barrels in volcanic cellars. Honey, candied orange and saffron meet sharp acidity, and the wine rests in bottle as well before release.","story":"Long before it named an estate, Oremus, Latin for let us pray, named a vineyard on a hill at Sátoraljaújhely in the north of Tokaj, and legend holds that there, about 1630, a Calvinist preacher at the court of the Rákóczi princes made the first Aszú. Historians treat the tale as legend: aszú is named in a document of 1571, and the preacher's own long chronicle says nothing of Aszú. The house holds that the Oremus vineyard was ranked of the first class, Primae Classis, in 1772. After communism, in 1993, the Álvarez family, who had bought Vega Sicilia in Ribera del Duero in 1982, bought an estate at Tolcsva and set up Tokaj Oremus, one of several ventures from abroad that put money into the district in those years. In 1999 a new winery rose at Tolcsva, joined to a maze of cellars the house traces to the thirteenth century, and around 2000 it began its dry Furmint, Mandolás. The Aszú is made the old way, by the puttony and the gönci barrel, and held long before release, in the manner of Vega Sicilia. Two answers meet in it: one family in Spain and in Tokaj, and a vineyard whose name carries the founding legend of Aszú.","others":[{"name":"Mandolás","note":"Dry Furmint named for the estate's Mandulás hill at Tolcsva, one of the early dry wines of modern Tokaj."},{"name":"Oremus Tokaji Aszú 5 Puttonyos","note":"The same method with less sugar, also aged long in the cellar before release."},{"name":"Oremus Eszencia","note":"The syrup that runs freely from the aszú berries, fermenting for years to only a few degrees of alcohol."}],"traps":["Oremus is also the old name of the grape Zéta, a cross of Furmint and Bouvier renamed in 1999: grape, vineyard and estate are three different things.","Oremus was bought in 1993 by the family that had bought Vega Sicilia, in Ribera del Duero, in 1982: one family, two countries, two unrelated answers.","The first Aszú of about 1630 is a legend, not a record: aszú is named in a document of 1571."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-1pd1ylk9","front":"Tokaj Oremus: what is Mandolás, and what is it named for?","back":"The estate's dry Furmint, begun around 2000 and named for its Mandulás hill at Tolcsva."},{"id":"fl-hk3u4kdz","front":"Which grape of Tokaj went by the name Oremus until 1999?","back":"Zéta, a cross of Furmint and Bouvier, quick to take noble rot."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-914zfhyv","q":"A guest asks how Tokaj Oremus makes its Aszú 6 Puttonyos. Which description matches what the house itself says?","opts":["Grapes raisined on the vine, then aged in bottle alone","Aszú berries soaked a day or two in must, then aged in small barrels","Frozen grapes pressed, then aged in steel tanks","Grapes dried on mats, then aged in large chestnut casks"],"a":1,"exp":"The house soaks the aszú berries a day or two in must and ages the wine two to three years in small barrels; grapes dried on mats make a passito, not an Aszú.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-irhpy5xp","q":"Legend says a preacher made the first Aszú from the Oremus vineyard about 1630. In which earlier year is aszú named in a document?","opts":["1571","1737","1772","1993"],"a":0,"exp":"Historians found aszú named in a document of 1571, so the tale of about 1630 is a legend; in 1737 a royal decree closed the district, and 1772 is the house's date for the vineyard's first class rank.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Vega Sicilia: Oremus, the winery","url":"https://www.temposvegasicilia.com/en/presentation/wineries/5/oremus","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vega Sicilia: Oremus Aszú 6","url":"https://www.temposvegasicilia.com/en/wine/242/oremus-aszu-6","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Michelin Guide: the Tokaj cellars that have made Aszú wines famous","url":"https://guide.michelin.com/us/en/article/wine/tokaj-the-cellars-that-have-made-aszu-wines-world-famous","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"CEEOL: Történelmi Szemle, the Oremus vineyard and its legend","url":"https://www.ceeol.com/search/article-detail?id=259694","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wines of Hungary: ten famous figures in Hungarian wine history","url":"https://winesofhungary.hu/magazine/ten-famous-figures-in-hungarian-wine-history","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-iu88f2ni": {"id":"fl-iu88f2ni","key":"disznoko","group":"tokaj","region":"tokaj","producer":"Disznókő","producerId":"p-disznoko","say":"DIS-noh-kur","place":"Mezőzombor, at the southern edge of the Tokaj hills, Hungary","founded":"1992","wine":{"name":"Disznókő Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos","appellation":"Tokaji Aszú","classification":"From vineyards ranked first class in the eighteenth century, as the estate presents them","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Furmint alone for the aszú berries in the sheets of 2016 and 2019, steeped in a base wine of Furmint and Hárslevelű.","sites":"One unbroken tract of vines at Mezőzombor, named for the wild boar stone, a large volcanic rock at its heart; its first class sites include Disznókő, Hangács and Lajos, and the vineyard is recorded from 1413.","making":"Most of the aszú berries are steeped whole for about two days in fermenting must, the rest crushed and soaked for half a day, then pressed. The lots are kept apart and aged for about two years in barrels of 225 litres, mostly used, with a little new Hungarian oak, before they are blended.","glass":"Ripe apricot, peach and quince over acacia honey and a touch of sandalwood; unctuous and lavishly sweet, yet fresh, with clean, lifted acidity and a long finish touched with green spice.","ageing":"Ready to drink on release, as the estate says, yet able to keep for thirty years and more on the strength of its acidity.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, or 11 to 13 as the estate itself suggests; there is nothing to decant; a small white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Disznókő","means":"Wild boar stone: the vineyard's name, from a large volcanic rock at its heart."},{"term":"Tokaji","means":"Of Tokaj: the suffix i turns the place into the name of its wine."},{"term":"Aszú 6 Puttonyos","means":"An Aszú of at least 150 grams of sugar a litre; the puttonyos figure is optional."}]},"table":"Named for the wild boar stone in its vineyard, the Mezőzombor estate of Disznókő steeps Furmint berries two days in fermenting must for its Aszú 6 Puttonyos. Apricot, peach and acacia honey come with clean, lifted acidity.","story":"Records of 1413 already name the vineyard Disznókő, the wild boar stone, a name taken from a large volcanic rock at its heart or, some geographers say, from the shape of its hill. In a survey of Tokaj's vineyards drawn up in 1732, by the estate's account, its slopes were ranked first class. Under communism the vines were farmed intensively, and by 1992 they were in poor shape. That year AXA Millésimes, the wine arm of the French insurer, bought the property, a plain sign of the foreign money that reopened Tokaj after 1989. Replanting began in 1994, at a much higher density, with better oriented rows and modern trellising, and in 1995 a new winery designed by a Hungarian architect was built, its cellar cut about ten metres into the rhyolite. The estate keeps its lots apart in barrel and blends them only after about two years, and it later bottled the Kapi parcel as an Aszú of its own. Asked how foreign capital rebuilt Tokaj, a sommelier can answer with Disznókő, one old vineyard replanted and made anew.","others":[{"name":"Disznókő Kapi Vineyard Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos","note":"Aszú of the Kapi parcel alone, the estate's single vineyard bottling."},{"name":"Disznókő Tokaji Late Harvest","note":"A lighter sweet wine below Aszú, made without the steeping of aszú berries."},{"name":"Disznókő Tokaji Száraz Szamorodni","note":"A dry wine of whole bunches with their botrytised berries, as they come."}],"traps":["Late Harvest and Szamorodni are not Aszú: only Aszú steeps selected aszú berries in must or wine.","Disznókő is a single vineyard estate at Mezőzombor, not a village of Tokaj.","Sárga Muskotály is a permitted grape of Tokaj, so a Muscat note does not rule out a Tokaji, though Disznókő's recent Aszú berries are Furmint alone."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-r2jmawol","front":"Disznókő: what does the name mean, and where does it come from?","back":"Wild boar stone, from a large volcanic rock at the heart of the vineyard, or from the shape of the hill, as some say."},{"id":"fl-3ipplvep","front":"Disznókő: what began in 1994, two years after the estate changed hands?","back":"Replanting, at a much higher density, with better oriented rows and modern trellising."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-tpikciyd","q":"How does Disznókő steep the aszú berries for its Aszú 6 Puttonyos, by the estate's own sheet?","opts":["Whole, for about a month in grape spirit","Crushed, for about two weeks in finished dry wine","Not at all: the berries are pressed straight away","Mostly whole, for about two days in fermenting must"],"a":3,"exp":"Most of the berries steep whole for about two days in fermenting must and the rest, crushed, for about half a day; berries soaked in grape spirit would make no Aszú.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-bq9uleju","q":"Disznókő's vineyard is recorded from 1413. In which year did AXA Millésimes buy the estate?","opts":["1989","1732","1992","1995"],"a":2,"exp":"AXA Millésimes bought the estate in 1992; 1732 is the survey that ranked its slopes first class and 1995 the year its new winery was built.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Disznókő: History","url":"https://www.disznoko.hu/en/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Disznókő: Winemaking facilities","url":"https://www.disznoko.hu/en/news/2/winemaking-facilities","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Disznókő: The Aszú winemaking","url":"https://www.disznoko.hu/en/elaboration/the-aszu-winemaking","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Disznókő: Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos 2019","url":"https://www.disznoko.hu/en/vin/41/disznok-tokaji-aszu-6-puttonyos","kind":"primary"},{"title":"González Byass: Disznókő Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos 2016, technical sheet","url":"https://www.gonzalezbyass.com/sites/www.gonzalezbyass.com/files/2025-10/Disznoko%20Aszu%206%20Puttonyos%202016.pdf","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-328vvv21": {"id":"fl-328vvv21","key":"szepsy","group":"tokaj","region":"tokaj","producer":"Szepsy","producerId":"p-szepsy","say":"SEP-shee, SENT TAH-mahsh","place":"Mád, Tokaj, north east Hungary","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Szepsy Szent Tamás Furmint","appellation":"Tokaji","classification":"A first class vineyard of Mád in Tokaj's eighteenth century classification","colour":"white","grapes":"Furmint, the grape the label names.","sites":"Szent Tamás, Saint Thomas, a large vineyard hill at the edge of Mád, on red clay over rhyolite tuff and zeolite.","making":"The vines are kept to very low yields and the grapes picked for a dry wine of this one vineyard alone. It ferments to dryness in Hungarian oak barrels of about three hundred litres, some of them new, and rests on its lees for some months before bottling, with no sweetness and no puttonyos mark.","glass":"Smoke, lime peel and chalk over green apple and a salty, stony core; dense and taut, with Furmint's high, firm acidity and a saline length.","ageing":"Reserved when young, it broadens over several years in bottle, and its acidity lets it keep for many years.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour if it seems closed; a large, Burgundy shaped white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Tokaji Furmint","means":"A varietal wine of the Tokaj appellation; a dry Furmint carries no puttonyos and no Aszú."},{"term":"Szent Tamás","means":"Saint Thomas: the vineyard, a first class slope at the edge of Mád."}]},"table":"Szepsy's Szent Tamás Furmint is a dry Tokaji from one first class slope at Mád, on red clay and volcanic rock. Smoke, lime and salt sit on searing acidity, so pour it a little cool, in a large glass, as you would a white Burgundy.","story":"The family has made wine in Tokaj since the sixteenth century, by its own account, and links its line to a Calvinist preacher of the Rákóczi court whose name it shares, whom legend credits with the first Aszú about 1630, though the word aszú is already written down in 1571. In the communist years the head of the family tended vines of his own at Mád and delivered the grapes to the state, and in 1990 he took a leading part in founding the Royal Tokaji company with the growers of Mád. In 2000 he fermented the Furmint of Mád's Úrágya vineyard dry, a wine many accounts credit with setting off the movement towards dry single vineyard Furmint. The family's own label followed with dry Furmints of single vineyards, Szent Tamás among them, a slope ranked first class in the eighteenth century, beside Aszú made only when the year allows. Where Tokaj had been known for sweet wine alone, these bottles showed that its volcanic slopes could give dry whites of weight and long life, and other growers of Mád followed. For a sommelier, Szent Tamás is the bottle to open when a guest believes Tokaji must be sweet.","others":[{"name":"Szepsy Urbán Furmint","note":"Dry Furmint from the Urbán vineyard, another classified site of Mád, stonier and tighter."},{"name":"Szepsy Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos","note":"Made in small amounts and only in years that allow it."}],"traps":["Tokaji on a label does not mean sweet: Szepsy's Szent Tamás is a dry white, as much a Tokaji as any Aszú.","Szent Tamás names a vineyard of Mád and also a separate winery of the village; Royal Tokaji, Szepsy and others bottle the vineyard, so read the name of the house.","Furmint's high acidity and smoky weight make it a blind tasting decoy for Chenin Blanc and dry Riesling."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ra4dlkg6","front":"Szepsy Szent Tamás: what does the vineyard's name mean, and where does it lie?","back":"Saint Thomas; a large first class vineyard hill at the edge of Mád, on red clay over rhyolite tuff and zeolite."},{"id":"fl-2blznscg","front":"Szepsy: since when has the family made wine in Tokaj, by its own account?","back":"Since the sixteenth century, around the time aszú first appears in the records."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-szn28rsr","q":"A guest orders Szepsy's Szent Tamás Furmint expecting a sweet Tokaji. What do you tell the guest?","opts":["It is a Szamorodni aged under a veil of yeast","It is a dry Furmint from one first class vineyard","It is an Aszú of six puttonyos from Mád","It is a late harvest wine with light sweetness"],"a":1,"exp":"Szent Tamás is a dry single vineyard Furmint, so it carries no puttonyos; an Aszú or a late harvest wine would be sweet, and a Szamorodni would say so on the label.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qu3r2qso","q":"Which vintage of the Úrágya vineyard's Furmint, fermented dry by the head of the Szepsy family, do many accounts credit with setting off Tokaj's dry Furmint movement?","opts":["2000","2013","1990","1993"],"a":0,"exp":"The Úrágya Furmint of 2000; 1990 is the year Royal Tokaji was founded with the growers of Mád, 1993 the year Oremus was set up at Tolcsva, and 2013 the year the Aszú rules changed.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Szepsy: Galéria, Urbán","url":"https://szepsy.hu/index.php/galeria-urban","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Royal Tokaji: Szent Tamás 1st Growth Tokaji Furmint Dry 2019","url":"https://royal-tokaji.com/portfolio/szent-tamas-1st-growth-tokaji-furmint-dry-2019/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Investment: Wine of Kings reinvented, the dry Furmint revival of Tokaj","url":"https://www.wineinvestment.com/us/learn/magazine/2026/06/wine-of-kings-reinvented-the-dry-furmint-revival-of-tokaj/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Accademia del Vino: Tokaj beyond sweet, dry Furmint's other identity","url":"https://accademiavino.com/winerover/2026-02/tokaj-furmint-azu-secco-dry","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Bottle of Italy: Szepsy Tokaji Furmint Szent Tamás","url":"https://bottleofitaly.com/en/products/tokaji-furmint-szent-tamas-2017-szepsy","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-nlj20b65": {"id":"fl-nlj20b65","key":"dobogo","group":"tokaj","region":"tokaj","producer":"Dobogó","producerId":"","say":"DOH-boh-goh","place":"Tokaj town, where the Bodrog meets the Tisza, Tokaj, Hungary","founded":"1995","wine":{"name":"Dobogó Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos","appellation":"Tokaji Aszú","classification":"","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Mostly Furmint, with Hárslevelű and a little Sárga Muskotály, or yellow Muscat, as the vineyards are planted.","sites":"Volcanic vineyards of Mád, Betsek, Szent Tamás and Úrágya among them; the cellar lies in the centre of Tokaj town.","making":"The base wine is the house's own late harvest wine, Mylitta, from shrivelled, partly botrytised grapes. A kilo of aszú berries goes to each litre of it, and the berries soak for about a day before pressing, which sets off a second fermentation. Two to three years in oak casks follow, then about three more in bottle in the cellar.","glass":"Dates, mango and dried apricot over orange blossom and orange peel, with a hint of jasmine tea; rich and sweet, bright acidity balancing it to a clean end.","ageing":"Five or six years in cask and bottle pass before it leaves the cellar; it is ready to pour then, and in a cool cellar it lasts for decades.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius, straight from the bottle with no decanting; a small glass, for a small measure.","label":[{"term":"Dobogó","means":"The house's name, said to recall the beat of horses' hooves on cobbles as carts came home."},{"term":"Tokaji Aszú","means":"Of Tokaj, the district rather than the town: aszú berries steeped in a base wine, at least 120 grams of sugar a litre."},{"term":"6 Puttonyos","means":"The richer of the two optional marks on an Aszú, at least 150 grams of sugar a litre."}]},"table":"Founded in 1995 by the family of the Unicum liqueur, Dobogó is a small house in Tokaj town. For its Aszú 6 Puttonyos, rich in dates, apricot and orange peel, the house steeps aszú berries in its own late harvest wine, then rests it for years in cask and bottle.","story":"The Zwack family made Unicum, the herbal liqueur, in Pest from 1840. In 1948 the communist state nationalised the business, and the family left Hungary, taking the recipe with them. The head of the family came home in 1988, a year before communism fell, and soon won the firm back. In 1995 his daughter founded Dobogó, a small winery in a house said to date from 1869, in the centre of Tokaj town; its name, the house says, recalls the beat of horses' hooves on the cobbles as the carts came home from the vineyards. The grapes come from vineyards of Mád: Betsek, Szent Tamás and Úrágya. In 2003 a winemaker joined the founder as partner, and since then, by the house's account, at least a barrel of 6 Puttonyos Aszú has been made in every vintage. The house builds its Aszú on its own late harvest wine, Mylitta, rather than on a dry base, and gives it years in cask and bottle before release. Beside the sweet wines it makes dry Furmint of single vineyards such as Úrágya. Dobogó is the reminder that Tokaj's revival was not only the work of large foreign companies, for a Hungarian family, home from exile, built a small house around the classic Aszú.","others":[{"name":"Dobogó Mylitta","note":"The late harvest wine of partly botrytised grapes, aged a year for freshness, which is also the base of the Aszú."},{"name":"Dobogó Úrágya Tokaji Furmint","note":"A dry single vineyard Furmint from vines planted in the early 2000s at the western edge of Mád."}],"traps":["Mylitta is a late harvest wine, not an Aszú: it carries no puttonyos figure, though it is the base wine of the house's Aszú.","Tokaj names both the town where Dobogó stands and the whole district; Tokaji on the label means of the district, not of the town alone.","Dobogó was founded in 1995 by a Hungarian family, the Zwacks of Unicum, not by one of the foreign companies of the early 1990s."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ccfnblv8","front":"Dobogó Aszú 6 Puttonyos: in what base wine are the aszú berries steeped?","back":"The house's own late harvest wine, Mylitta, a kilo of berries to each litre, soaked for about a day."},{"id":"fl-fxr519q4","front":"Dobogó: what sound does the house's name recall, by its own account?","back":"The beat of horses' hooves on the cobbles as the carts came home from the vineyards."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-p4oaiz2u","q":"How long does Dobogó's Aszú 6 Puttonyos spend in cask and in bottle before release, by the house's account?","opts":["Six months in steel, then a year in bottle","Ten years in cask under a veil of yeast","Eighteen months in cask, then straight to market","Two to three years in cask, then about three in bottle"],"a":3,"exp":"The house gives two to three years in oak casks and about three more in bottle; the law asks at least eighteen months in oak, which Dobogó goes beyond.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-d5e0n8wa","q":"Dobogó was founded in Tokaj town in 1995 by a family better known for another drink. Which drink?","opts":["Dreher, the Budapest beer","Törley, the sparkling wine","Unicum, the herbal liqueur","Bikavér, the red of Eger"],"a":2,"exp":"The Zwack family of Unicum founded Dobogó in 1995; Törley names a sparkling wine and Bikavér a red wine of Eger, neither the liqueur the Zwacks made.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Dobogó: Our winery","url":"https://www.dobogo.hu/en","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Dobogó: Our wines","url":"https://www.dobogo.hu/en/our-wines","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Liberty Wines: Dobogó Tokaji Aszú 6 Puttonyos","url":"https://www.libertywines.co.uk/our-portfolio/DO101-tokaji-aszu-6-puttonyos","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Tokaji estate to boldly go into Pinot","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/tokaji-estate-to-boldly-go-into-pinot-108841/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Zwack","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zwack","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-533bd2p1": {"id":"fl-533bd2p1","key":"sigalas","group":"santorini","region":"santorini","producer":"Domaine Sigalas","producerId":"p-sigalas","say":"see-GAH-lahs, kah-vah-lee-EH-ros","place":"Oia, Santorini PDO, Cyclades, Greece; the vineyard lies at Imerovigli","founded":"1991","wine":{"name":"Sigalas Kavalieros","appellation":"Santorini","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Assyrtiko alone, from vines of sixty years and more growing on their own roots.","sites":"Kavalieros, a single high block of old vines in the village of Imerovigli, rooted directly in the island's volcanic ash and pumice.","making":"The juice is fermented in stainless steel tanks at a controlled temperature. The wine then rests on its fine lees in tank for about eighteen months, stirred often in the first months and less as time passes, and it never sees oak before bottling.","glass":"Ripe lemon, lemon verbena and orange blossom over chalk and a strong vein of salt; creamy and full in the mouth, yet held taut by high acidity, and salty to the last.","ageing":"Enjoyable from the start, it is built to keep, and the house expects it to last ten years and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; decanting is unnecessary, though a young bottle opens with a few minutes in the glass; a generous white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Santorini","means":"The island's Protected Designation of Origin for dry white wine of at least 85 per cent Assyrtiko."},{"term":"Kavalieros","means":"The single vineyard block at Imerovigli from which the wine is drawn."}]},"table":"From one high block at Imerovigli, where own rooted vines of sixty years and more grow in volcanic ash, comes Kavalieros, Domaine Sigalas's single vineyard Assyrtiko. Eighteen months on its lees in steel give it a creamy weight around the salt and lemon; pour it with grilled fish.","story":"The founder came from a Santorini family that, like most on the island, grew vines, and he spent the summers of his boyhood there before he trained as a mathematician, as the house's own story has it. In 1991 he bottled his first wine, a dry Santorini, at Oia in the north of the island, and the estate grew up among the vineyards of the plain there, working Assyrtiko on its own roots, since phylloxera never took hold in the ash. Beside the estate's Santorini came wines of single places, and Kavalieros was drawn from one high block of vines more than sixty years old in the village of Imerovigli. Bottled apart and given a long rest on its lees in steel rather than time in oak, it lets the site speak without wood. After a Greek investor bought a majority of the estate, its running passed in January 2020 to Kir-Yianni, the wine company of Naoussa in the north of mainland Greece, and in 2025 Kir-Yianni bought that majority itself. Kavalieros answers the guest who wants to taste one named Santorini vineyard rather than a blend from many villages.","others":[{"name":"Sigalas Santorini","note":"The estate's dry Santorini of Assyrtiko, the wine it first bottled in 1991."},{"name":"Sigalas Vinsanto","note":"The island's sweet wine, from sun dried Assyrtiko and Aidani and given long ageing in oak."}],"traps":["Domaine Sigalas has its cellar at Oia, but Kavalieros grows in another village, Imerovigli, so the wine is not a wine of Oia.","Kavalieros sees no oak: its creamy weight comes from about eighteen months on its fine lees in steel tanks."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-iqm6esdu","front":"Sigalas Kavalieros: where does the vineyard lie, and how old are its vines?","back":"One high block in the village of Imerovigli; vines of sixty years and more, on their own roots in volcanic ash."},{"id":"fl-ayihkom0","front":"Domaine Sigalas: which wine company of Naoussa ran the estate from 2020 and bought a majority in 2025?","back":"Kir-Yianni, from the north of mainland Greece."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-jx0e04vi","q":"Sigalas Kavalieros tastes rich and creamy, and a guest asks how long it spent in oak. What is the true reply?","opts":["No oak: eighteen months on its lees in steel","Twelve months in new French oak barrels","Three months in oak, the rule for a Nykteri","Two years in old casks, as for Vinsanto"],"a":0,"exp":"Kavalieros is fermented and raised in stainless steel, about eighteen months on its fine lees, which gives the creamy texture; three months in oak is the rule for Nykteri, another style.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-z1n4eu06","q":"Domaine Sigalas bottled its first wine, a dry Santorini, at Oia. In what year?","opts":["1947","1991","1903","1994"],"a":1,"exp":"The estate's first Santorini was bottled in 1991; 1903 is the founding of Estate Argyros, 1947 the island's cooperative union and 1994 the founding of Gaia.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine Sigalas: Kavalieros","url":"https://sigalas-wine.com/product/kavalieros/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Sigalas: About","url":"https://sigalas-wine.com/about-en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Enotria and Coe: Sigalas Kavalieros Single Vineyard 2021","url":"https://www.enotriacoe.com/products/sigalas-kavalieros-single-vineyard-2021","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Kir-Yianni: Kir-Yianni takes over the winery management of Domaine Sigalas","url":"https://kiryianni.gr/kir-yianni-s-a-takes-over-the-winery-management-of-domaine-sigalas-in-santorini/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Industry Advisor: Kir-Yianni acquires Domaine Sigalas","url":"https://wineindustryadvisor.com/2025/11/20/kir-yianni-acquires-domaine-sigalas/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ap21avyo": {"id":"fl-ap21avyo","key":"argyros","group":"santorini","region":"santorini","producer":"Estate Argyros","producerId":"p-argyros","say":"ar-yee-ROSS, mon-see-NYOH-ree","place":"Episkopi Gonia, Santorini PDO, Cyclades, Greece","founded":"1903","wine":{"name":"Estate Argyros Cuvée Monsignori","appellation":"Santorini","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Assyrtiko alone, from ungrafted vines.","sites":"The Monsignori parcel at Episkopi Gonia, where Assyrtiko grows on its own roots, low in the island's pumice and volcanic ash, from vines the estate dates to more than two hundred years.","making":"Steel tanks, temperature controlled, take the fermentation; the wine then stays in them on the fine lees for most of a year, ten or eleven months by the merchants' sheets, and goes to bottle without touching oak.","glass":"White peach, lemon curd and beeswax over brine and wet stone; broad, dense and strong in alcohol, with firm acidity beneath and a finish of salt that lingers.","ageing":"Already broad within a year or two of release, it will age, the estate expects, for more than ten years.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, not colder, so that its weight shows; serve it without a decanter, in a large glass for white wine.","label":[{"term":"Cuvée Monsignori","means":"An old vine parcel at Episkopi Gonia, whose name the wine carries."},{"term":"Santorini","means":"The island's own appellation, its dry whites led by Assyrtiko."}]},"table":"At Episkopi Gonia, where ungrafted vines are counted in centuries, one parcel gives Estate Argyros its old vine Assyrtiko, Cuvée Monsignori. Raised in steel tanks on its lees, with no oak, it is broad, waxy and salty, a white to set beside octopus.","story":"Vines have been the Argyros family's work on Santorini since the eighteenth century, the estate says, and it dates itself to 1903, when its founder began making wine under the family's name at Episkopi Gonia. Its vineyards hold old vines on their own roots, many never replanted, for the island's volcanic soil gave phylloxera no foothold. In 1974 a new head of the family took over, and the estate's standing rose under him, as the estate's own history and other accounts have it. The fourth generation followed, and in the 2010s the estate moved into a new winery that keeps the old cellars and the press of 1903 beside it. Cuvée Monsignori comes from the parcel of that name at Episkopi, from vines the estate dates to more than two centuries, made as a white of one place and raised on its lees without oak. From the harvest of 2021 the estate released no Monsignori at all, by its own announcement, and the cuvée returned with the next vintage. Weight, wax and salt are what vines two centuries old give on Santorini, and a glass of Monsignori holds all three.","others":[{"name":"Estate Argyros Assyrtiko","note":"The estate's dry Santorini, fermented in steel and kept a few months on its fine lees."},{"name":"Estate Argyros Vinsanto","note":"Sun dried Assyrtiko with a little Athiri and Aidani from Episkopi, aged in cement vats and then old oak; the Late Release is held years longer."}],"traps":["Monsignori is the name of a parcel, not a rank: the wine carries no classification beyond the Santorini appellation.","Santorini's vines grow ungrafted because phylloxera cannot live in the volcanic ash, not because of any quarantine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-6ayzh52c","front":"Estate Argyros: what did its new winery of the 2010s keep beside it?","back":"The old cellars and the press of 1903, the year to which the estate dates its founding."},{"id":"fl-h5glgxth","front":"Which harvest of Cuvée Monsignori did the estate hold back entirely, by its own announcement?","back":"2021: no Monsignori was released from that harvest."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-9g5l5ckx","q":"For a white with no oak, Estate Argyros's Cuvée Monsignori tastes broad and waxy. Where does that weight come from?","opts":["Three months in new oak, as the Nykteri rule asks","A large share of Athiri and Aidani in the blend","Grapes dried in the sun before pressing","Very old ungrafted vines and months on the lees"],"a":3,"exp":"The Monsignori parcel's ungrafted vines, dated by the estate to more than two hundred years, give dense fruit, and the wine rests ten or eleven months on its lees in steel; it sees no oak and is Assyrtiko alone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-opvec68l","q":"Estate Argyros dates its founding to 1903. When did the head of the family who raised its standing take over?","opts":["1991","1947","1974","2002"],"a":2,"exp":"He took over in 1974, by the estate's own account; 1947 is the founding of the island's cooperative union, 1991 the first Sigalas wine and 2002 the year Vinsanto joined the island's appellation.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Estate Argyros: About","url":"https://estateargyros.com/about/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Estate Argyros: The fourth generation at the helm","url":"https://estateargyros.com/the-fourth-generation-at-the-helm-of-santorinis-oldest-winery/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Estate Argyros: Important announcement","url":"https://estateargyros.com/important-announcement/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Skurnik: Santorini Assyrtiko Cuvée Monsignori, Estate Argyros","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/sku/santorini-assyrtiko-cuvee-monsignori-estate-argyros-4/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik: Estate Argyros producer page","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/estate-argyros/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-iudwre9f": {"id":"fl-iudwre9f","key":"gaia","group":"santorini","region":"santorini","producer":"Gaia Wines","producerId":"p-gaia-wines","say":"thah-lah-SEE-tees","place":"Exo Gonia, Santorini PDO, Cyclades, Greece; a second winery at Nemea","founded":"1994","wine":{"name":"Gaia Thalassitis","appellation":"Santorini","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Assyrtiko alone, from old vines.","sites":"Old vines of seventy to eighty years in the villages of Episkopi, Akrotiri and Pyrgos, on volcanic soils of ash and pumice, with yields kept low.","making":"The chilled, destemmed grapes rest about twelve hours on their skins at around 10 degrees Celsius. Selected yeasts then ferment the juice at 16 to 17 degrees Celsius in stainless steel tanks, with no malolactic fermentation, and the wine is kept in steel, untouched by oak, until bottling.","glass":"Lime zest, grapefruit and lemon over meadow herbs and a faint note of tobacco; dry and taut, all high, cutting acidity, ending long and salty.","ageing":"Bright and ready on release; the house suggests two to three years in the cellar, which soften its edge as honey and riper fruit come through.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; skip the decanter; a white wine glass of middling size.","label":[{"term":"Thalassitis","means":"Of the sea, in Greek: a name tied in accounts to the ancient habit of mixing wine with sea water."},{"term":"Santorini","means":"The appellation of the island, which asks a dry white for at least 85 per cent Assyrtiko."}]},"table":"Thalassitis was Gaia's first wine, a Santorini Assyrtiko from old vines in three villages, fermented cool and kept in steel with no oak. Taut, lemony and salty, it is the island's plain style, made for oysters or feta and poured cold.","story":"Two agronomists founded Gaia in 1994, and its first wine was Thalassitis, a dry Santorini of Assyrtiko, the house relates. Its name means of the sea, and accounts tie it to an ancient Greek habit of mixing wine with sea water. The Santorini winery took over a stone tomato factory built early in the twentieth century on the beach at Exo Gonia, on the island's east coast, which had made purée from the island's small tomatoes until it closed in the 1970s. In 1997 the partners built a second winery among their own vineyards at Koutsi in Nemea, on the Peloponnese, for Agiorgitiko, so that one house speaks for both the island and the mainland. On Santorini they went on to ferment Assyrtiko wild, on its own yeasts, in steel, oak and acacia, and from 2009 they lowered bottles of Thalassitis into the sea off the island to age, an echo of the name. Thalassitis itself stayed the plain style, fermented cool and kept from wood. It shows Santorini Assyrtiko as the island gives it, before oak or long lees change it.","others":[{"name":"Assyrtiko by Gaia Wild Ferment","note":"Santorini Assyrtiko fermented by wild yeasts in steel tanks and in French oak, American oak and acacia barrels."},{"name":"Thalassitis Submerged","note":"Bottles of Thalassitis aged under the sea off Santorini, a project begun in 2009."},{"name":"Gaia Estate","note":"Agiorgitiko from the house's own vineyard at Koutsi in Nemea, on the mainland."}],"traps":["Thalassitis is the unoaked one; the house's oaked Assyrtiko is the Wild Ferment, and its oak fermented Thalassitis was renamed Nykteri in 2019.","Gaia makes wine in two appellations, Santorini for Assyrtiko and Nemea for Agiorgitiko, so read the label for the origin.","Thalassitis Submerged is the same wine aged under the sea, not another grape and not a sweet wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-833qc58i","front":"Gaia Thalassitis: which three villages give its old vine Assyrtiko?","back":"Episkopi, Akrotiri and Pyrgos, as the house names them."},{"id":"fl-rcg2punm","front":"What building on Santorini did Gaia turn into its winery?","back":"A stone tomato factory of the early twentieth century on the beach at Exo Gonia, which made purée until it closed in the 1970s."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-0m95knjy","q":"A guest wants a Gaia Santorini with no taste of oak at all. Which bottle should the sommelier pour?","opts":["Thalassitis, fermented and kept in steel","Gaia's Nykteri, the oak fermented style","Assyrtiko by Gaia Wild Ferment","Gaia Estate, the Nemea Agiorgitiko"],"a":0,"exp":"Thalassitis is fermented cool and kept in steel with no oak; the Wild Ferment is partly fermented in oak and acacia barrels, and Gaia Estate is a red from Nemea.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-kpqo1gul","q":"From 2009 Gaia began an experiment that echoes the name Thalassitis. What was it?","opts":["Drying grapes in the sun for a sweet wine","Ageing bottles under the sea off Santorini","Building a winery at Nemea on the mainland","Fermenting Assyrtiko in acacia barrels"],"a":1,"exp":"From 2009 the house lowered bottles of Thalassitis into the sea to age; the Nemea winery came earlier, in 1997, and acacia barrels are part of the Wild Ferment.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Gaia Wines: About","url":"https://gaiawines.gr/en/about-en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Gaia Wines: Thalassitis","url":"https://gaiawines.gr/en/thalassitis-en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Gaia Wines: Santorini winery and vineyard","url":"https://gaiawines.gr/en/santorini-en/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Winebow: GAI'A Thalassitis","url":"https://winebow.com/node/611661","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Shout: article on Gaia's submerged Thalassitis","url":"https://theshout.com.au/?p=32642","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2b87j1n1": {"id":"fl-2b87j1n1","key":"hatzidakis","group":"santorini","region":"santorini","producer":"Hatzidakis","producerId":"","say":"hah-tzee-DHAH-kees, neekh-TEH-ree","place":"Pyrgos Kallistis, Santorini PDO, Cyclades, Greece","founded":"1997","wine":{"name":"Hatzidakis Nykteri","appellation":"Santorini","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Assyrtiko alone, picked very ripe.","sites":"Vineyards at Megalochori: old, ungrafted Assyrtiko in the island's volcanic soil.","making":"Hand picked and overripe, the grapes spend a night in the cold, then are destemmed and given twelve hours on their skins. The juice ferments with its own yeasts at 17 to 18 degrees Celsius, and the wine matures about twelve months in French oak barrels of several sizes before it is bottled unfiltered.","glass":"Baked apple, preserved lemon and toasted almond over honey, smoke and a breath of sea air; golden, full and broad, with Assyrtiko's acidity keeping the weight fresh.","ageing":"Closed in its youth, it unfolds slowly in bottle, and the house gives it more than ten years.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; a young bottle that seems closed gains from time in a decanter; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Nykteri, or Nikteri","means":"Night work: a dry Santorini of at least 13.5 per cent alcohol, aged at least three months in oak."},{"term":"Santorini","means":"The Protected Designation of Origin under which Nykteri is made as a named style."}]},"table":"The overripe grapes of old Assyrtiko vines at Megalochori make Hatzidakis Nykteri, wild fermented and aged a year in French oak: the riper, oaked style of Santorini, named for the night pressing. Broad and nutty with a salty spine, it suits lobster or roast pork.","story":"Nykteri means night work: grapes picked for it were pressed during the night after the harvest, and the name came to mean a riper dry wine of Santorini, aged in oak, by the account of the national wine body. The house began with an oenologist born on Crete, who came to the island in 1991 to work at the Boutari winery there and rose to be its chief oenologist, and his wife, who ran that winery's hospitality. Her family's vineyard at Pyrgos, abandoned since her parents left the island after the earthquake of 1956, became their own, and in a cave at its end he made a small cellar. The first bottles, a dry Santorini and a red Mavrotragano of the 1997 vintage, were released in 1998, the house records. The house farmed its vineyard organically and had it certified. In 2000 it bought a vineyard above a larger cave for its Vinsanto, and in 2016 it moved to a cellar on the road from Pyrgos to Emporio. The founder died in 2017, and his family took the house on. Its Nykteri shows a guest that Santorini can be rich and oaked and yet taste of salt.","others":[{"name":"Hatzidakis Santorini Familia","note":"The house's dry Santorini, all Assyrtiko, fermented without added yeast and named Familia in 2018 in the founder's honour."},{"name":"Hatzidakis Assyrtiko de Louros","note":"A single vineyard Assyrtiko from Louros at Pyrgos, fermented with its own yeasts in oak and kept twenty four months on its lees in French oak."},{"name":"Hatzidakis Mavrotragano","note":"A red of the island's Mavrotragano grape, one of the house's two first wines, of the 1997 vintage."}],"traps":["Nykteri is a style within the Santorini appellation, not an appellation of its own.","Hatzidakis Nykteri is dry despite its ripeness: the island's sweet wine is Vinsanto, made from grapes dried in the sun."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-5om6vxq2","front":"Hatzidakis Nykteri: how long does it spend in oak, and in what?","back":"About twelve months in French oak barrels of several sizes, then bottled unfiltered."},{"id":"fl-w849jab9","front":"Where did the Hatzidakis house make its first wines, of the 1997 vintage?","back":"In a small cellar cut into a cave at the end of the family vineyard at Pyrgos."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-uy3xd27e","q":"What does the word Nykteri promise on a bottle of Hatzidakis?","opts":["A wine from grapes picked by moonlight on one night","A sweet wine of grapes dried in the sun","A riper dry Santorini aged at least three months in oak","A sparkling Assyrtiko fermented again in bottle"],"a":2,"exp":"Nykteri is a dry Santorini of at least 13.5 per cent alcohol with three months or more in oak; the sun dried sweet wine is Vinsanto, and the name recalls night pressing, not picking.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-driawdeb","q":"The founder of Hatzidakis came to Santorini in 1991 and worked there before founding his own house. To which post did he rise?","opts":["Cellar master at Domaine Sigalas","Director of the island's cooperative union","Vineyard manager for Gaia at Exo Gonia","Chief oenologist at the Boutari winery"],"a":3,"exp":"He came in 1991 to work at Boutari's Santorini winery and became its chief oenologist; the cooperative union is Santo Wines, and Gaia was founded only in 1994.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Hatzidakis Winery: Identity and History","url":"https://www.hatzidakiswines.gr/en/the-winery/identity","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Hatzidakis Winery: Nykteri, barrel aged PDO Santorini white wine","url":"https://www.hatzidakiswines.gr/en/our-wines/nikteri","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine and Spirits Magazine: Remembering Haridimos Hatzidakis","url":"https://www.wineandspiritsmagazine.com/?p=3982","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"SommSelect: Hatzidakis Nykteri Assyrtiko","url":"https://sommselect.com/products/somm2101-hatz19nyk-750","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wines of Greece: Nychteri","url":"https://winesofgreece.org/articles/nychteri/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-onh92ayq": {"id":"fl-onh92ayq","key":"santo","group":"santorini","region":"santorini","producer":"Santo Wines","producerId":"","say":"SAHN-toh, veen-SAHN-toh","place":"Pyrgos, Santorini PDO, Cyclades, Greece","founded":"1947","wine":{"name":"Santo Vinsanto","appellation":"Santorini","classification":"","colour":"sweet white","grapes":"Assyrtiko, the great majority, with Aidani.","sites":"Grapes from the union's member growers across the island, own rooted vines in Santorini's volcanic soil.","making":"Picked in late August, the grapes are laid in the sun to dry for six to ten days, then fermented slowly over forty to sixty days. The wine is aged for years in oak barrels before bottling, three years in used 225 litre barrels for the 2020 vintage.","glass":"Dried apricot, raisin and fig over cinnamon, clove and caramel; deep amber, thick and sweet, yet cut by a fine thread of acidity that keeps it from cloying.","ageing":"Long lived, though ready the day it is released; the union also bottles selections after twelve and twenty years of ageing, darker and turned towards coffee and dates.","serve":"6 to 8 degrees Celsius; no decanter is needed; small measures in a small dessert wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Vinsanto","means":"Santorini's sweet wine of sun dried grapes, at least 51 per cent Assyrtiko, aged at least two years."},{"term":"Santorini","means":"An appellation that covers the island's sweet Vinsanto as well as its dry whites."}]},"table":"Sun dried for a week or more, then fermented slowly and aged years in oak, Assyrtiko and Aidani become Santo Vinsanto, the sweet wine of Santorini's growers' union. It tastes of apricot, raisin and cinnamon, kept lively by its acidity; serve it cool, in small glasses, after dinner.","story":"In 1911, as the union's own history has it, the Santorini Vine and Wine Protection Fund was set up for the island's growers, the body from which Santo Wines grew. In 1947 it became the Union of Santorini Cooperatives, formed to process, export and promote the island's produce, above all its wine. Its members are the island's smallholders, whose grapes it takes in and makes into dry whites and the sweet Vinsanto. In 1992 the union completed a new winery in the village of Pyrgos. For Vinsanto, the sweet wine of grapes dried in the sun after the August harvest, the turning point came in 2002, when, by the union's timeline, European law brought Vinsanto under the Santorini appellation; other accounts add that the island gained sole use of the one word name, while Italy kept Vin Santo, two words, for its own. The union's Vinsanto carries its vintage and spends years in oak, and older bottlings are held for a decade or two more before release. When a guest asks for Santorini's sweet wine, Santo's Vinsanto is the ready answer, its sweetness owed to the sun rather than to added spirit.","others":[{"name":"Santo Vinsanto 12 Years Ageing","note":"A selection of vintages of the same sun dried wine, held at least twelve years in French oak barrels before bottling."},{"name":"Santo Wines Santorini","note":"The union's dry white of Assyrtiko under the Santorini appellation."}],"traps":["Santorini Vinsanto, one word, is sun dried Assyrtiko from the island; Tuscan Vin Santo, two words, is made from other grapes dried indoors.","Santo's Vinsanto owes its sweetness to grapes dried in the sun before fermenting, not to added grape spirit: the union makes it as a naturally sweet wine.","Santo Wines is the island's union of cooperatives, not a family estate."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-g4tml2u9","front":"Santo Wines: when and where did the union complete its new winery?","back":"In 1992, at Pyrgos."},{"id":"fl-ni6t6ql8","front":"What did Santo Wines become in 1947, and what had it been since 1911?","back":"The Union of Santorini Cooperatives; before that, the Santorini Vine and Wine Protection Fund."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-sx614ee0","q":"Which rule of the Santorini appellation must a Vinsanto such as Santo's meet?","opts":["Grapes shrivelled by noble rot on the vine","Sun dried grapes and at least two years of ageing","Frozen grapes pressed in the depth of winter","Grape spirit added to stop the fermentation"],"a":1,"exp":"Vinsanto comes from sun dried grapes, at least 51 per cent Assyrtiko, and needs at least 24 months of ageing; added spirit is no part of that rule, and neither frost nor noble rot plays a part.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-vrvaex20","q":"In 2002 European law brought Santorini's sweet wine of sun dried grapes under the island's appellation. By which name?","opts":["Vinsanto","Nykteri","Vin Santo","Liastos"],"a":0,"exp":"Vinsanto, one word, came under the Santorini appellation in 2002; Vin Santo, two words, is Italy's own, and Nykteri is the island's oaked dry style.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Santo Wines: Milestones","url":"https://santowines.gr/our-story/milestones","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Santo Wines: Facilities","url":"https://santowines.gr/our-story/facilities","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Santo Wines: Santorini Vinsanto fact sheet","url":"https://santowines.gr/images/wines/fact-sheets/updated/Vinsanto_Fact_Sheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wines of Greece: PDO Santorini","url":"https://winesofgreece.org/pdo/pdo-santorini/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Santorini (wine)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santorini_(wine)","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2i7uww8n": {"id":"fl-2i7uww8n","key":"beaulieu","group":"napa","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Beaulieu Vineyard","producerId":"p-beaulieu-vineyard","say":"bohl-YOO, zhorzh duh lah-TOOR","place":"Rutherford, Napa Valley, California","founded":"1900","wine":{"name":"Georges de Latour Private Reserve Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Napa Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, nearly all of it, with a little Petit Verdot and, in some years, a touch of Malbec or Merlot.","sites":"BV Ranch No. 1 and BV Ranch No. 2 on the Rutherford bench, on the west side of the valley floor, vineyards the founder first planted in the early 1900s.","making":"Part of the crop is fermented in new French oak barrels and the rest in stainless steel and concrete tanks; the malolactic fermentation and the ageing follow in barrel, about twenty months or more in French oak, nearly all of it new. For decades it was aged in American oak, until French barrels came in around 1990.","glass":"Cassis, black cherry and cedar over dried sage and cocoa, with the dry, dusty grip André Tchelistcheff called Rutherford dust; firm, fine grained tannin, fresh acidity and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Firm when young, it opens after ten years or so in a strong vintage and can last for decades, as the old vintages tasted long after release have shown.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Georges de Latour","means":"The founder of Beaulieu, who died in 1940; the wine bears his name, not that of another estate."},{"term":"Private Reserve","means":"First the family's own lot of 1936, released for sale under the founder's name, and the house's leading Cabernet ever since."},{"term":"Napa Valley","means":"The appellation on the label; the grapes grow at Rutherford, within it."}]},"table":"Rutherford dust was André Tchelistcheff's name for the dry, dusty grip of Cabernet from the Rutherford bench, and Beaulieu Vineyard's Georges de Latour Private Reserve, which he shaped for decades, is where to taste it. First made in 1936, it pairs that grip with cassis and cedar.","story":"Georges de Latour, a Frenchman, and his wife bought their first land at Rutherford in 1900; on seeing it, the house says, she called it a beau lieu, a beautiful place, and the name stayed. Through Prohibition, by the house's account, Beaulieu kept its cellar working by making wine for the altars of the Catholic Church. In 1938 de Latour brought André Tchelistcheff (1901 to 1994), a Russian born winemaker he had met in France, to Rutherford. Tasting the family's private lot of 1936 Cabernet Sauvignon, Tchelistcheff urged that it be bottled and sold as the house's flagship, and it was released about 1940, the year de Latour died, under his name. Tchelistcheff stayed some three decades, coined the phrase Rutherford dust for the dry, dusty grip of the bench's Cabernet and trained a generation of Napa winemakers. In 1969 the family sold Beaulieu to the Heublein company, and the house has since passed through several drinks groups. For decades the Private Reserve was aged in American oak; French barrels came in around 1990 and have since taken over. Its long run of vintages links Napa before the Second World War to the valley's later Cabernet.","others":[{"name":"BV Reserve Ranch 1 Rutherford Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Cabernet with Petit Verdot from Ranch No. 1, the vineyard around the original estate, bottled apart."},{"name":"BV Rutherford Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The house's everyday Cabernet from the Rutherford bench."}],"traps":["Georges de Latour Private Reserve is Beaulieu's wine, named for its founder: there is no estate called Georges de Latour, and no link to Château Latour in Pauillac.","Rutherford dust is tasting language for a dry, dusty tannin, not a soil type with a legal definition.","Tchelistcheff advised many wineries after he left, but Beaulieu was the house where he made wine for some three decades."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-qlg8whe0","front":"Beaulieu Vineyard: which vintage became the first Georges de Latour Private Reserve?","back":"The 1936, the family's private lot, released for sale about 1940 at Tchelistcheff's urging."},{"id":"fl-04p9cnoy","front":"Which phrase did André Tchelistcheff coin for the Cabernet of the Rutherford bench?","back":"Rutherford dust, for the dry, dusty grip of Beaulieu's Private Reserve."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-pado27c4","q":"Where do the grapes for Beaulieu's Georges de Latour Private Reserve grow?","opts":["Hillside vineyards high on Howell Mountain","Bought fruit from across Sonoma County","The cool Carneros district by the bay","BV Ranch No. 1 and No. 2 on the Rutherford bench"],"a":3,"exp":"The Private Reserve comes from Beaulieu's own Ranches No. 1 and No. 2 on the Rutherford bench, which the founder first planted; Carneros is far cooler and better known for Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-cahygmxv","q":"Beaulieu's winemaker tasted the family's private lot of 1936 Cabernet and urged its release under the founder's name. Who was he?","opts":["Miljenko Grgich","Robert Mondavi","André Tchelistcheff","Warren Winiarski"],"a":2,"exp":"André Tchelistcheff came to Rutherford in 1938 and stayed some three decades; Joe Heitz worked under him at Beaulieu in the 1950s before founding his own house.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Beaulieu Vineyard: History of Georges de Latour Private Reserve","url":"https://www.bvwines.com/blogs/the-pour-by-bv/history-of-georges-de-latour-private-reserve","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Beaulieu Vineyard: 2022 Georges de Latour Private Reserve Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://www.bvwines.com/products/2022-georges-de-latour-private-reserve-cabernet-sauvignon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinous: Vertical tasting of Georges de Latour Private Reserve, 1965 to 2016","url":"https://v1.vinous.com/articles/vertical-tasting-of-beaulieu-vineyard-s-georges-de-latour-private-reserve-cabernet-sauvignon-apr-2019","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Beaulieu Vineyard Georges de Latour Private Reserve 1947","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-beaulieu-vineyard-georges-de-latour-private-reserve-1947-367726/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: André Tchelistcheff","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Tchelistcheff","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-q7ft5n7p": {"id":"fl-q7ft5n7p","key":"heitz","group":"napa","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Heitz Cellar","producerId":"p-heitz-cellar","say":"HYTS","place":"St Helena, with the vineyard at Oakville, Napa Valley, California","founded":"1961","wine":{"name":"Martha's Vineyard Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Oakville, Napa Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, from the one vineyard.","sites":"Martha's Vineyard, on the well drained western bench of Oakville, with eucalyptus trees along its edge. Heitz agreed in 1965, on a handshake, to buy all its Cabernet from the couple who had bought the land in 1963.","making":"After fermentation the wine spends about a year in large, neutral oak tanks, then moves to new barrels of French Limousin oak. It is bottled after years in wood and held a further year in bottle at the winery before release, a regime that has varied from vintage to vintage.","glass":"Boysenberry and cassis lifted by mint and eucalyptus, with cedar, bay leaf and a twist of citrus peel; firm, dusty tannin, lively acidity and a long, cool finish.","ageing":"Released with years already on it, it keeps opening for a decade or two, and strong vintages last for decades.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or so, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Martha's Vineyard","means":"The Oakville vineyard, named for one of the couple who owned it; nothing to do with the island off Massachusetts."},{"term":"Oakville, Napa Valley","means":"The vineyard's American Viticultural Area, printed with the Napa Valley appellation that contains it."},{"term":"Heitz Cellar","means":"The house that makes and bottles the wine at St Helena, though the vineyard lies at Oakville."}]},"table":"Mint and eucalyptus over cassis mark Martha's Vineyard, the Cabernet Sauvignon Heitz Cellar has bottled under that Oakville vineyard's name since the 1966 vintage. It reaches the list with years of age already on it, so decant it gently.","story":"Before he had his own cellar, Joe Heitz, who died in 2000, learned his trade in the 1950s as assistant to André Tchelistcheff (1901 to 1994) at Beaulieu, then taught winemaking at Fresno State. In 1961, with his wife, he bought a small vineyard and winery south of St Helena, and in 1964 the family moved to Spring Valley in the hills east of the town, to a property with a stone cellar of 1898. The early wines were bought in cask and bottled under the Heitz name. In 1965 Heitz agreed on a handshake to take every Cabernet grape from an Oakville vineyard a couple had bought two years before; that first crop went into his regular bottling. From the 1966 vintage he bottled it apart under the vineyard's name, Martha's Vineyard, and the house calls it the first Napa wine labelled by vineyard. The 1970 stood among the California reds at the Paris tasting of 1976. Phylloxera later killed the old vines and the vineyard was replanted, so no Martha's Vineyard was made from the vintages 1993 to 1995. The family sold the winery in 2018. Martha's Vineyard made a Napa vineyard's name worth reading on a label, and its mint is a cue blind tasters learn early.","others":[{"name":"Bella Oaks Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Cabernet from the Bella Oaks vineyard in Rutherford, the house's other vineyard named Cabernet."},{"name":"Trailside Vineyard Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Cabernet from a Rutherford vineyard, bottled under its own name."},{"name":"Grignolino","note":"A light red from the Piedmontese grape of that name, grown by the house in Napa."}],"traps":["Martha's Vineyard lies at Oakville, while the Heitz winery stands at St Helena.","The wine is named for an Oakville vineyard, not the island of Martha's Vineyard in Massachusetts.","Heitz did not plant Martha's Vineyard: it bought the grapes from 1965 on a handshake with the couple who owned the land."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-plf7f9tk","front":"Heitz Cellar: which note in Martha's Vineyard Cabernet do blind tasters learn to spot?","back":"Mint and eucalyptus over cassis, often put down to the eucalyptus trees at the vineyard's edge."},{"id":"fl-psa89ixl","front":"Why was there no Martha's Vineyard Cabernet from the vintages 1993 to 1995?","back":"Phylloxera had killed the old vines, and the vineyard was being replanted."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-kg89x80l","q":"A guest asks how Heitz Martha's Vineyard Cabernet is aged before it reaches the market. What do you tell them?","opts":["Neutral tanks, then new Limousin oak, then a year in bottle","Stainless steel throughout, bottled within the year","New American oak barrels, then straight to market","Large old casks alone, released in its second year"],"a":0,"exp":"Heitz gives the wine about a year in large neutral oak tanks, then new barrels of French Limousin oak, and a further year in bottle; it is never rushed out, so American oak and early release are wrong.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-l53ogg01","q":"Joe Heitz worked as assistant to André Tchelistcheff before founding Heitz Cellar in 1961. At which Napa house?","opts":["Schramsberg","Beaulieu Vineyard","Inglenook","Charles Krug"],"a":1,"exp":"Heitz assisted Tchelistcheff at Beaulieu in Rutherford through the 1950s; Inglenook was a neighbouring Rutherford house, but Tchelistcheff's own cellar was Beaulieu's.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Heitz Cellar: About","url":"https://heitzcellar.com/about/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Heitz Cellar: Martha's Vineyard Cabernet Sauvignon, Oakville, Napa Valley","url":"https://heitzcellar.com/purchase-2/marthas-vineyard-cabernet-sauvignon-oakville-napa-valley/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Heitz Cellar producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/heitz-cellar-producer-profile-442717/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Wine Cellar Insider: Heitz Cellar","url":"https://www.thewinecellarinsider.com/california-wine/heitz-cellar-napa-valley-california-wine-cabernet-sauvignon-chardonnay","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Heitz Wine Cellars","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heitz_Wine_Cellars","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-fvr3mol4": {"id":"fl-fvr3mol4","key":"stags-leap","group":"napa","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars","producerId":"p-stags-leap-wine-cellars","say":"","place":"Stags Leap District, Napa Valley, California","founded":"1970","wine":{"name":"Cask 23 Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Napa Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, nearly all of it; some years add a touch of another Bordeaux grape, such as Cabernet Franc.","sites":"A selection from the estate's two Stags Leap District vineyards: S.L.V., planted in 1970, and FAY, first planted to Cabernet in 1961. Vines on volcanic soils on the eastern hillsides give structure; those on the alluvial lower ground give perfume and fruit.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel tanks, with a small part in new French oak barrels in some years, and taken through the malolactic fermentation. The wine is then aged about twenty months in French oak barrels, all or nearly all of them new, before bottling.","glass":"Violet, black cherry and cassis over cedar, cocoa and a hint of sage, with silky, fine tannin that grips gently and a long, perfumed finish.","ageing":"Supple enough to enjoy within a few years of release, it gains savoury depth over a decade and keeps for twenty years and more in strong vintages.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Cask 23","means":"Named for the large wooden cask that held the one lot set apart in 1974."},{"term":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars","means":"The winery, written with an apostrophe; the district's name, Stags Leap, carries none."},{"term":"Napa Valley","means":"The wider appellation the wine carries; both vineyards lie in its Stags Leap District."}]},"table":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars draws Cask 23, a perfumed, silky Cabernet Sauvignon of violet and black cherry, from its two Stags Leap District vineyards, S.L.V. and FAY. Remember that the Paris winner of 1976 was the house's 1973, not Cask 23.","story":"A neighbour's wine came first: in the late 1960s Warren Winiarski (1928 to 2024), a Chicago born winemaker, tasted the homemade Cabernet of a grower who in 1961, by the house's account, had planted the first Cabernet Sauvignon in what became the Stags Leap District. In 1970 he bought the ranch next door and planted it as Stag's Leap Vineyard, S.L.V., and the first vintage followed in 1972. On 24 May 1976 the house's 1973 Cabernet Sauvignon, from S.L.V. fruit, took first place among the reds at a blind tasting of French and California wines before French judges in Paris, ahead of Château Mouton Rothschild 1970. Cask 23 began with the harvest of 1974: tasting the young lots with André Tchelistcheff (1901 to 1994), the house found one from S.L.V. so distinct that it was bottled apart and named for the large wooden cask that held it. In 1986 Winiarski bought the grower's vineyard and named it FAY in his honour, and from the 1990 vintage Cask 23 blended the two; by the house's count, that 1990 was only the tenth Cask 23. In 2007 Ste. Michelle Wine Estates and the Antinori family of Florence bought the winery from its founder. With Cask 23 the house gives its own reply to the question Paris asked in 1976.","others":[{"name":"S.L.V. Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The first vineyard, planted in 1970, bottled alone; its fruit made the 1973 that won the red flight in Paris."},{"name":"FAY Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The vineyard first planted to Cabernet in 1961 and bought in 1986, bottled alone; the softer, more perfumed of the two."}],"traps":["The Paris winner of 1976 was the house's 1973 Cabernet Sauvignon from S.L.V. fruit; Cask 23 was first made from the 1974 harvest.","Stag's Leap Wine Cellars takes an apostrophe and the Stags Leap District takes none, while Stags' Leap Winery is a separate estate in the same district.","S.L.V. and FAY are the house's two vineyards, each bottled alone as well as blended into Cask 23."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-s7gct4yp","front":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars: where does the name Cask 23 come from?","back":"The large wooden cask that held one S.L.V. lot of 1974, set apart after tasting with André Tchelistcheff."},{"id":"fl-awe6yte3","front":"Which wine won the red flight at the Paris tasting of 1976, and from which vineyard?","back":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars' 1973 Cabernet Sauvignon, from S.L.V., ahead of Château Mouton Rothschild 1970."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-vaamorr3","q":"Which vineyards make up Stag's Leap Wine Cellars' Cask 23?","opts":["To Kalon and Martha's Vineyard in Oakville","Rutherford bench vineyards bought from growers","S.L.V. and FAY, both in the Stags Leap District","Hillside sites on Howell Mountain and Mount Veeder"],"a":2,"exp":"Cask 23 is drawn from the estate's own S.L.V. and FAY vineyards, blended together since the 1990 vintage; To Kalon and Martha's Vineyard lie in Oakville and belong to other houses' wines.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ztplm2nu","q":"In which year did Stag's Leap Wine Cellars buy the neighbouring vineyard it named FAY, for the grower who had planted it?","opts":["1970","1990","1961","1986"],"a":3,"exp":"The founder bought it in 1986 and named it for the grower who had planted Cabernet there in 1961; FAY fruit first went into Cask 23 with the 1990 vintage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars: Cask 23 Cabernet Sauvignon, past vintages","url":"https://www.stagsleapwinecellars.com/past-vintages/cask-23-cabernet-sauvignon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars: the S.L.V. and FAY vineyards","url":"https://www.stagsleapwinecellars.com/estate/vineyards/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Stag's Leap Wine Cellars: 2012 Cask 23 fact sheet","url":"https://www.stagsleapwinecellars.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/SWC_192.14FS_12CASK23_FINAL-1.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Stag's Leap founder Warren Winiarski dies aged 95","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/stags-leap-founder-warren-winiarski-dies-aged-95-531585/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"NPR: The Judgment of Paris, the blind taste test that decanted the wine world","url":"https://www.npr.org/sections/thesalt/2016/05/24/479163882/the-judgment-of-paris-the-blind-taste-test-that-decanted-the-wine-world","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-qt0ym20q": {"id":"fl-qt0ym20q","key":"montelena","group":"napa","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Chateau Montelena","producerId":"p-chateau-montelena","say":"","place":"Calistoga, Napa Valley, California","founded":"1882","wine":{"name":"Chateau Montelena Chardonnay","appellation":"Napa Valley","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay, from vineyards across the Napa Valley.","sites":"Vineyards across the Napa Valley, as the appellation on the label says; the estate's own vineyards at Calistoga give its Cabernet Sauvignon. The Paris wine of 1973 drew most of its fruit from Sonoma County, with the rest from Napa.","making":"Made without the malolactic fermentation, as Mike Grgich made the 1973, so the wine keeps its crisp malic acidity and takes on no buttery note. It is then aged about ten months in French oak barrels, a small share of them new.","glass":"Green apple, white peach and lemon zest over wet stone and a light touch of toasted hazelnut, with bright acidity, no buttery richness and a long, clean finish.","ageing":"Fresh and taut on release, it rounds out over five to ten years, and strong vintages keep for a decade and more.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Chateau Montelena","means":"The estate, its name a contraction of Mount Saint Helena, the peak above Calistoga."},{"term":"Napa Valley","means":"The appellation: the grapes may come from anywhere in the valley, not only the estate."},{"term":"Chardonnay","means":"The grape, the same that won the white flight in Paris with the 1973."}]},"table":"The white flight in Paris in 1976 went to Chateau Montelena's 1973 Chardonnay. Made without malolactic fermentation, as the 1973 was, the wine is crisp and taut, green apple and white peach rather than butter, a Napa white with Burgundy's restraint.","story":"At the foot of Mount Saint Helena, north of Calistoga, Alfred L. Tubbs (1827 to 1896), a San Francisco rope maker, acquired land in 1882; he planted vines and built the stone château, and by his death the winery stood among the valley's large ones. His grandson revived it after Prohibition, in 1933, and in 1958 the family sold the château to a couple who dug Jade Lake and laid out a Chinese garden around it. In 1972 Jim Barrett (1926 to 2013) and his partners bought the estate and hired Mike Grgich (1923 to 2023) to make the wine. Grgich bought grapes for the 1973 Chardonnay, most of them from Sonoma County, and blocked its malolactic fermentation. When French judges tasted it blind in Paris on 24 May 1976, it came first among the whites, ahead of a Meursault Charmes, while Stag's Leap Wine Cellars won the reds; a bottle of it went to the Smithsonian. Grgich left in 1977 to found his own winery. For a sommelier the Chardonnay is the bottle a guest names, because it carries the Paris story, while the estate's own Cabernet is the wine of its Calistoga vineyards.","others":[{"name":"Montelena Estate Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The estate's own Calistoga Cabernet, built for long ageing: the wine of the vineyard rather than of the Paris story."},{"name":"Chateau Montelena Napa Valley Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"A second Cabernet, from vineyards across the valley rather than the estate alone."}],"traps":["Montelena won Paris with Chardonnay; the red flight went to Stag's Leap Wine Cellars.","The 1973 that won in Paris was made mostly from Sonoma County grapes, not from the Calistoga estate.","The château dates from the 1880s, but the wine of the Paris story came only after the revival of 1972."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-m0cf984q","front":"Chateau Montelena Chardonnay: which step of the making does the house leave out, and to what end?","back":"The malolactic fermentation, so the wine keeps its crisp malic acidity and shows no buttery note."},{"id":"fl-2zt8fvcz","front":"Who made the 1973 Chardonnay that won the white flight in Paris in 1976?","back":"Mike Grgich at Chateau Montelena, who left in 1977 to found his own winery."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-wqu2zgvr","q":"A guest who dislikes buttery Chardonnay asks about Chateau Montelena. Why does it taste crisp rather than buttery?","opts":["It comes from Carneros vineyards alone","It is made without malolactic fermentation","It is aged only in stainless steel","It is blended with Sauvignon Blanc"],"a":1,"exp":"Montelena leaves out the malolactic fermentation, which would turn malic acid into softer lactic acid and bring a buttery note; the wine spends months in French oak all the same, so it is not a steel aged wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-bt32c1vd","q":"Most of the grapes in the 1973 Chateau Montelena Chardonnay that won in Paris came from where?","opts":["Sonoma County vineyards","The Carneros district","Oakville on the valley floor","The estate at Calistoga"],"a":0,"exp":"Grgich bought most of the fruit in Sonoma County, in the Alexander and Russian River valleys, with the rest from Napa; the estate's own vineyards gave only a small share.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Chateau Montelena: Curated Experiences brochure","url":"https://montelena.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/CHM_Curated_Brochure_2024-03_ADA.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Chateau Montelena: Chardonnay Set","url":"https://montelena.com/wine/chardonnay-set/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Chateau Montelena 1973","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-chateau-montelena-1973-305096/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Chateau Montelena","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chateau_Montelena","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Jim Barrett (winemaker)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Barrett_(winemaker)","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-cspzj407": {"id":"fl-cspzj407","key":"opus-one","group":"napa","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Opus One","producerId":"w-opus-one","say":"","place":"Oakville, Napa Valley, California","founded":"1979","wine":{"name":"Opus One","appellation":"Napa Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, much the larger part, with Petit Verdot, Cabernet Franc, Merlot and a touch of Malbec, the shares shifting with the year.","sites":"Four estate parcels in the west of Oakville: two within the old To Kalon vineyard, and the Ballestra and River parcels around the winery. Alluvial soils at the foot of the Mayacamas, the gravel giving way to clay and silt towards the Napa River.","making":"The blocks are judged lot by lot, the soils of each helping decide what goes to Opus One and what to Overture. The chosen wine spends about a year and a half in new French oak barrels, is bottled, and rests more than a year in bottle at the winery before its release each October.","glass":"Blackberry, black cherry and cassis with cocoa, cedar and a touch of violet; polished rather than powerful, with fine, ripe tannin, fresh acidity and a long, even finish.","ageing":"Supple on release, it opens fully after eight to ten years in a strong vintage and keeps for two decades and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Opus One","means":"From the musical opus, a composer's work: the name the two founders gave their joint wine."},{"term":"Napa Valley","means":"The appellation Opus One carries, wider than Oakville, where the estate's vineyards lie."}]},"table":"Robert Mondavi and Baron Philippe de Rothschild of Mouton went into partnership to make Opus One, an Oakville red whose first vintage was the 1979. Cabernet Sauvignon leads the other Bordeaux grapes in a polished wine with fine grained tannin.","story":"Hawaii was where the two founders first met, in 1970: Robert Mondavi (1913 to 2008), of the Robert Mondavi Winery at Oakville, and Baron Philippe de Rothschild (1902 to 1988), of Château Mouton Rothschild in Pauillac. In 1978, at Mouton, they agreed to make one wine together: a Napa red on the pattern of Bordeaux. The first vintage, the 1979, was made at the Robert Mondavi Winery, the partnership was announced in 1980, and the 1979 and 1980 were released together in 1984. The name borrows the musical opus, a composer's work. The Baron died in 1988 and his daughter carried the partnership on. Ground was broken for the estate's own winery in 1989, and it was finished in October 1991, in time for that year's harvest; until then every vintage had been made at the Mondavi winery. In 2004 the Mondavi company was bought by Constellation Brands, and its half of Opus One went with it. Overture, the estate's second wine, takes the lots not chosen for the grand vin. Opus One joined a Bordeaux first growth and Napa in one name; open it for a guest curious how the two meet.","others":[{"name":"Overture","note":"The estate's second wine, first made in 1993 from lots not chosen for Opus One; long sold without a vintage, it first carried one with the 2021."}],"traps":["The Rothschild of Opus One is the Mouton branch of the family, not the Lafite branch.","Opus One is its own winery and wine, not a bottling of the Robert Mondavi Winery, though its first vintages were made there.","The first vintage is 1979, but it reached the market only in 1984, together with the 1980."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-tooo8wev","front":"Opus One: where were its vintages made before the estate's own winery was finished in 1991?","back":"At the Robert Mondavi Winery in Oakville."},{"id":"fl-ame3xa1c","front":"Which old Oakville vineyard holds two of the four parcels of the Opus One estate?","back":"To Kalon; the Ballestra and River parcels lie around the winery."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-i0h6yh47","q":"Which grapes make up Opus One, the Oakville red of the Mondavi and Rothschild partnership?","opts":["Merlot above all, with some Cabernet Franc","Cabernet Sauvignon alone, from To Kalon","Cabernet Sauvignon above all, with the other Bordeaux grapes","Cabernet Sauvignon and Syrah in about equal parts"],"a":2,"exp":"Opus One is led by Cabernet Sauvignon, with Petit Verdot, Cabernet Franc, Merlot and a touch of Malbec as the year allows; it is a blend, never Cabernet alone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-bsb342c0","q":"When did the 1979 and 1980 vintages of Opus One reach the market?","opts":["The 1979 alone, in 1981","Together, in 1991","Each a year after its harvest","Together, in 1984"],"a":3,"exp":"The partners held the first vintages back and released the 1979 and 1980 together in 1984; 1991 is the year the estate's own winery was finished.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Opus One: The Estate","url":"https://www.opusonewinery.com/estate/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Opus One: Vintage Notes","url":"https://www.opusonewinery.com/vintage-notes/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Opus One: Who was Baron Philippe de Rothschild?","url":"https://www.opusonewinery.com/moments/our-story/who-was-baron-philippe-de-rothschild/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Opus One Winery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opus_One_Winery","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Robert Mondavi","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Mondavi","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-4xo3r9r3": {"id":"fl-4xo3r9r3","key":"hanzell","group":"sonoma","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Hanzell Vineyards","producerId":"p-hanzell","say":"","place":"Sonoma, Sonoma Valley, Sonoma County, California","founded":"1953","wine":{"name":"Hanzell Chardonnay","appellation":"Sonoma Valley","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, grown on the estate.","sites":"The estate's hillside vineyards above Sonoma town, at the southern foot of the Mayacamas range, looking over the lower Sonoma Valley towards San Pablo Bay; its first Chardonnay vines went in here in 1953.","making":"Fermented largely in temperature controlled stainless steel, then aged in French oak barrels, a share of them new. Malolactic fermentation is held back for most of the wine, so it keeps much of its malic acid; by its own account the house adds no malolactic bacteria and filters the wine to keep that acid in the bottle.","glass":"Lemon peel, green apple and pear over hazelnut and a little toast, firm rather than broad, with taut acidity and a long, nutty finish; age brings honey and toasted bread.","ageing":"Tight and firm in its first years, it needs time in bottle and can keep for decades in a strong vintage.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for half an hour if it seems closed; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Sonoma Valley","means":"The American Viticultural Area, or AVA, around Sonoma town; Sonoma County and Sonoma Coast are other, wider names."},{"term":"Hanzell Vineyards","means":"The estate's name, joining Hana, the founder's wife, with the family name Zellerbach."}]},"table":"Planted in 1953 by an American industrialist who wanted Burgundy in Sonoma, the hillside estate above Sonoma town gives Hanzell Chardonnay. Fermented largely in steel and aged in French oak, it is firm and nutty and rewards years in bottle.","story":"James D. Zellerbach (1892 to 1963), a paper industrialist who admired the wines of Burgundy, set out to make their like in Sonoma. In 1953 he planted his first vines, Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, on the hillside above Sonoma town, and he named the estate Hanzell for his wife, Hana. The small winery that followed, of redwood and local stone, was modelled on the press house of the Clos de Vougeot, and the first vintage, on the house's timeline, came in 1957, the year he went to Rome as ambassador to Italy. With his founding winemaker he brought in small barrels of French oak, rare in California then, and temperature controlled fermenters of stainless steel. After Zellerbach died in 1963 his widow sold the stock, and much of the wine in barrel and bottle went to Heitz Cellar in Napa. The Day family bought and reopened the estate in the mid 1960s, and in 1975 it passed to the de Brye family. A winemaker hired in 1973 stayed until 2001 and held to the firm, long lived style. Hanzell is the estate credited with bringing the small French barrel and the cooled steel tank to California wine.","others":[{"name":"Hanzell Sebella Chardonnay","note":"Another estate Chardonnay, fermented in stainless steel and aged six months in older French oak barrels."},{"name":"Hanzell Pinot Noir","note":"The estate's red from the same hillsides, where Pinot Noir was planted beside the Chardonnay in 1953."},{"name":"Ambassador's 1953 Vineyard Pinot Noir","note":"A Pinot Noir from the original block Zellerbach planted in 1953."}],"traps":["Hanzell's Chardonnay is labelled Sonoma Valley; Sonoma County and Sonoma Coast are wider, different appellations.","The Ambassador's 1953 Vineyard names the original block, planted with Chardonnay and Pinot Noir in 1953, not a vintage.","Hanzell's Chardonnay sees French oak yet keeps much of its malic acid, so it is not a broad, fully malolactic wine in the manner of Kistler."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-fer152pl","front":"Hanzell Chardonnay: which two tools of the cellar is the estate credited with bringing to California?","back":"Small barrels of French oak and temperature controlled stainless steel fermenters, in the 1950s."},{"id":"fl-ooxoyee1","front":"Which Burgundy building gave the model for the Hanzell winery?","back":"The press house of the Clos de Vougeot; the small winery at Sonoma was built after it in redwood and local stone."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-dvlrw8a8","q":"Hanzell Chardonnay strikes a guest as leaner and more taut than many oaked California Chardonnays. Which part of its making explains it?","opts":["Malolactic fermentation held back for most of the wine","Long contact with the skins before pressing","Grapes drawn from cool Sonoma Coast ridges","Fermentation entirely in new American oak barrels"],"a":0,"exp":"Hanzell holds back malolactic fermentation for most of the wine, so it keeps its malic acid; it is aged in French oak, not American, and its vines grow in Sonoma Valley, not on the Sonoma Coast.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-oinngfro","q":"After the founder of Hanzell died in 1963, his widow sold the wine in the cellar. Which Napa house bought much of it?","opts":["Charles Krug","Heitz Cellar","Inglenook","Beaulieu Vineyard"],"a":1,"exp":"Heitz Cellar bought much of the Hanzell wine in barrel and bottle that the founder left; the accounts of the sale name no other Napa house.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Hanzell: Our story and timeline","url":"https://www.hanzell.com/our-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Hanzell: Winemaking","url":"https://www.hanzell.com/winemaking","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Rewards of tradition with Hanzell Chardonnay","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/rewards-of-tradition-with-hanzell-chardonnay-15486","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Press Democrat: Sonoma's historic Hanzell Vineyards endures","url":"https://www.pressdemocrat.com/2009/03/24/sonomas-historic-hanzell-vineyards-endures/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: James David Zellerbach","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_David_Zellerbach","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-innibigj": {"id":"fl-innibigj","key":"kistler","group":"sonoma","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Kistler Vineyards","producerId":"p-kistler","say":"","place":"Vine Hill, Russian River Valley, Sonoma County, California","founded":"1978","wine":{"name":"Kistler Vineyard Chardonnay","appellation":"Sonoma Valley","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, of the one heritage California selection the house plants in all its vineyards.","sites":"The Kistler Vineyard, the house's original planting, in a small bowl high on the western edge of the Mayacamas above Sonoma Valley, on Moon Mountain; the vines are dry farmed on St George rootstock.","making":"Fermented, the house says, by the native yeasts of its underground barrel cellars, in French barrels coopered at Saint-Romain in Burgundy from staves the house buys and air dries for about three years. The wine goes through full malolactic fermentation and rests long on its lees before bottling.","glass":"Hazelnut, lemon curd and baked pear over buttered toast, broad and creamy from the lees, yet held together by bright acidity through a long finish.","ageing":"Generous young, and a good year deepens over the following decade.","serve":"11 to 13 degrees Celsius; no need to decant, though a young bottle opens with air in the glass; a large white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Kistler Vineyard","means":"The house's original estate vineyard in the Mayacamas, bottled on its own since the 1986 vintage."},{"term":"Sonoma Valley","means":"The valley's appellation; the vineyard lies in its eastern hills."}]},"table":"From the house's original vines, high in the Mayacamas above Sonoma Valley, Kistler Vineyard Chardonnay has been bottled on its own since 1986. Native yeasts ferment it in French barrels, and full malolactic gives it breadth, hazelnut and lemon curd kept fresh by its acidity.","story":"Kistler was founded in 1978 by a winemaker and a partner, on the notion, as the house puts it, that California should make wines of site. Its first vintage was 1979. Its original winery stood high in the Mayacamas, above Sonoma Valley east of Glen Ellen, and the partners planted their own vineyard on that range in the house's first years. From the 1986 vintage that vineyard was bottled on its own, one of a row of Chardonnays each named for a single site, among them Dutton Ranch, Durell and McCrea, that made the single vineyard California Chardonnay a wine to collect. In the early 1990s the house outgrew the mountain and moved west to Vine Hill Road in the Russian River Valley, where it had planted vines of its own. By its own account Kistler plants one heritage selection of Chardonnay in every vineyard and ferments with native yeasts in French barrels, so that the site rather than the recipe marks each wine. The founding winemaker stepped down as chief executive at the end of 2017. To pour the Kistler Vineyard is to taste the house's rich, Burgundian Chardonnay at its source, the mountain where it began.","others":[{"name":"Vine Hill Vineyard Chardonnay","note":"From the vines around the winery in the Russian River Valley, the site at the centre of the range."},{"name":"Les Noisetiers Chardonnay","note":"A Sonoma Coast blend of several vineyards, not a single site."},{"name":"Kistler Pinot Noir","note":"Single vineyard Pinot Noirs, the later red arm of the house."}],"traps":["Kistler bottles many separate vineyards; the Kistler Vineyard, its original estate site, leads the range but is far from its only Chardonnay.","Les Noisetiers is a blend of vineyards, not a vineyard of that name.","Kistler's style is full malolactic and long lees contact, unlike the leaner Chardonnays of Stony Hill or Hanzell."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-du9c4i75","front":"Kistler Vineyard Chardonnay: what yeasts ferment it, and in what?","back":"The native yeasts of the house's underground barrel cellars, in French barrels coopered at Saint-Romain."},{"id":"fl-yqznrvp1","front":"Where did Kistler make its wine before it moved to Vine Hill Road?","back":"At its first winery, high in the Mayacamas above Sonoma Valley east of Glen Ellen, until the early 1990s."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-tg9d0mjc","q":"Poured beside Hanzell's Chardonnay, Kistler Vineyard Chardonnay feels broader and creamier. Which reason fits?","opts":["Grapes dried on mats before pressing","Fermentation in steel with malolactic held back","Full malolactic fermentation and long ageing on its lees","A share of Viognier blended in from the estate"],"a":2,"exp":"Kistler puts its Chardonnay through full malolactic fermentation and leaves it long on its lees, which gives breadth; Hanzell is the one that holds back malolactic, and Kistler's wine is Chardonnay alone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-bdce461s","q":"Kistler was founded in 1978. From which vintage has the Kistler Vineyard been bottled as a wine of its own?","opts":["1976","1992","1979","1986"],"a":3,"exp":"The original vineyard has been bottled on its own since the 1986 vintage; 1979 was the house's first vintage, and the move west to Vine Hill Road came in the early 1990s.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Kistler Vineyards: a single clone Chardonnay house","url":"https://www.kistlervineyards.com/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Kistler Vineyards: Kistler Vineyard","url":"https://www.kistlervineyards.com/wine/kistler-vineyard","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: The quiet master of California Chardonnay","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/the-quiet-master-of-california-chardonnay-8770","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Press Democrat: Kistler Vineyards founder stepping down as chief executive","url":"https://www.pressdemocrat.com/2017/12/13/kistler-vineyards-founder-stepping-down-as-ceo-from-sonoma-county-winery/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Kistler Vineyards producer page","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/kistler-vineyards/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-qxaytkg9": {"id":"fl-qxaytkg9","key":"williams-selyem","group":"sonoma","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Williams Selyem","producerId":"p-williams-selyem","say":"","place":"Westside Road, Healdsburg, Russian River Valley, Sonoma County, California","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Williams Selyem Rochioli Riverblock Vineyard Pinot Noir","appellation":"Russian River Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone, from several clones in the block, among them Pommard, 115 and 777 and a selection from Rochioli's West Block.","sites":"The River Block of the Rochioli family's vineyard on Westside Road, on gentle, mostly flat ground of Yolo sandy loam on the bank of the Russian River, where cool breezes meet well drained ground.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel dairy tanks, the vessel the founders took up early for its ratio of skins to juice and the house has kept, then aged for about sixteen months in barrels, about two thirds to four fifths of them new and the rest a year old, by the house's notes for the vintages of 2018 to 2024.","glass":"Black cherry, raspberry and pomegranate over cola, forest floor and a lift of rose petal, silky in texture, with fine tannin and juicy acidity.","ageing":"Open and sappy when young, and a good vintage gains forest floor and dried cherry over a decade.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant only an old bottle, off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Rochioli Riverblock Vineyard","means":"The River Block of the Rochioli vineyard on Westside Road, a grower's site the house buys from."},{"term":"Russian River Valley","means":"The appellation of the river's cool lower valley in Sonoma County."}]},"table":"Rochioli fruit made the name of Williams Selyem in the 1980s, and its Rochioli Riverblock Pinot Noir grows in the riverside block of that vineyard on Westside Road. It pours silky, with black cherry, cola and forest floor.","story":"Two friends began making wine for fun in a garage in the Russian River Valley in the late 1970s, starting with Zinfandel, and sold their first wines under the name Hacienda del Rio. In the early 1980s they made their first Pinot Noir, from grapes grown by the Rochioli family on Westside Road, who had begun planting Pinot Noir there in 1968. The founders soon put their own surnames on the label, and the 1984 vintage was the first called Williams Selyem. A year later came the wine that made them: the 1985 Rochioli Vineyard Pinot Noir won the sweepstakes prize at the California State Fair of 1987 against more than two thousand wines. Demand outran the little garage, and a waiting list to join the house's mailing list followed. At the end of the 1980s a Westside Road grower built them a plain winery on his ranch, and they moved in among the vineyards they bought from. The founders sold the house in 1998. In 2021 the Burgundy house of Faiveley took a minority stake, and in 2024 a majority. The Rochioli Riverblock leads back to the beginning: the house made its name on this vineyard's fruit, and the riverside block is where that fruit grows.","others":[{"name":"Allen Vineyard Pinot Noir","note":"From the neighbouring Westside Road vineyard of the grower who built the founders a winery at the end of the 1980s."},{"name":"Williams Selyem Zinfandel","note":"An old vine sideline, recalling that the founders began with Zinfandel in their garage."}],"traps":["Williams Selyem buys the Rochioli fruit; J. Rochioli bottles Pinot Noir from the same vineyard under its own name.","The founders sold the house in 1998, so the Williams and Selyem of the name took no part in later vintages."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-m7s9361j","front":"Williams Selyem: which wine won the sweepstakes prize at the California State Fair and made the house's name?","back":"Its 1985 Rochioli Vineyard Pinot Noir, chosen over more than two thousand wines."},{"id":"fl-kktp8uhj","front":"Under what name did the founders of Williams Selyem first sell their wine?","back":"Hacienda del Rio; the 1984 vintage was the first labelled Williams Selyem."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ly7zdf4c","q":"On what ground does Williams Selyem's Rochioli Riverblock Pinot Noir grow?","opts":["Sandy loam on the bank of the Russian River","Volcanic hillsides high in the Mayacamas","Marine shale on the ridges of the Sonoma Coast","Gravel benchland above the Alexander Valley floor"],"a":0,"exp":"The River Block lies on gentle, mostly flat Yolo sandy loam beside the Russian River off Westside Road; the Mayacamas hillsides are the country of Hanzell and Kistler's first vineyard.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-f3hgt7jt","q":"Williams Selyem's founders made their first Pinot Noir in the early 1980s. Whose vineyard grew the grapes?","opts":["The Hanzell estate above Sonoma town","The Rochioli vineyard on Westside Road","The Allen vineyard on Westside Road","The Seghesio Home Ranch in Alexander Valley"],"a":1,"exp":"Their first Pinot Noir was made from Rochioli grapes; the Allen vineyard's owner gave them a winery only at the end of the 1980s, and Hanzell and Seghesio lie far from Westside Road.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Williams Selyem: History","url":"https://www.williamsselyem.com/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Williams Selyem: Rochioli Riverblock Vineyard","url":"https://www.williamsselyem.com/vineyards/rochioli-riverblock-vineyard/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Profiles in Pinot Noir, Williams Selyem","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/profiles-in-pinot-noir-williams-selyem-1803","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"J. Rochioli Vineyard and Winery: Our story","url":"https://www.rochioliwinery.com/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Faiveley family acquire majority stake in Russian River wine estates","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2024/06/faiveley-family-acquire-majority-stake-in-russian-river-wine-estates/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-whmzrwlj": {"id":"fl-whmzrwlj","key":"jordan","group":"sonoma","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Jordan Vineyard & Winery","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Healdsburg, Alexander Valley, Sonoma County, California","founded":"1972","wine":{"name":"Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Alexander Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, about three quarters to four fifths, with Merlot, Petit Verdot and a little Malbec, and in some years a touch of Cabernet Franc.","sites":"Growers' vineyards across Alexander Valley, on the valley floor, the benchland and the hillsides, with hillside vines on the estate itself; the house turned to them after phylloxera struck its own valley floor vineyard in the 1990s. Some vintages take a little fruit from Mendocino County or Dry Creek Valley.","making":"Aged for about thirteen months in barrels, somewhat under half of them new, by the house's recent notes; it has been aged in French oak alone since the 2015 vintage, while the first, of 1976, was aged half in American oak. The wine then rests in bottle at the winery and is released about four years after the harvest, as the 1976 was released in 1980.","glass":"Blackcurrant and red cherry over cedar, tobacco leaf and dried herbs, medium bodied rather than heavy, with polished tannin and acidity enough to suit it to the table.","ageing":"Ready to drink on release, it holds and softens for a decade or more when the year is good.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter helps a young bottle; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Alexander Valley","means":"A warm valley appellation in northern Sonoma County, known for rounder Cabernet."},{"term":"Jordan Vineyard & Winery","means":"The house's full name, on the label of its Cabernet and of its Chardonnay."}]},"table":"Alexander Valley's Jordan Vineyard & Winery, founded in 1972 and built in the manner of a French château, makes one red: Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon. Mostly Cabernet with Merlot and Petit Verdot, held in barrel and bottle before release, it stays medium bodied and leaves room for the meal.","story":"The estates of France inspired the couple who founded Jordan in 1972: they bought land in Alexander Valley near Healdsburg and planted Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot on the valley floor. To advise them they hired André Tchelistcheff (1901 to 1994), who had shaped Beaulieu in Napa, and he set the house on a French course. Building of the château began in the mid 1970s, and its cellar was finished a week before the harvest of 1976, the first Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon, which was released in 1980. A Chardonnay followed, and the house kept to those two wines. The winemaker who made that first vintage stayed until 2019. After phylloxera struck its valley floor vines in the 1990s, Jordan turned to growers across the valley and to hillside fruit, which its winemaker found more intense, and planted hillside vines on its own estate. The founders' son took over the running of the house in 2005, and in 2021 it bought another Alexander Valley vineyard. Jordan Cabernet is a study in balance: medium bodied, aged before release and built to partner food, not to overpower it.","others":[{"name":"Jordan Chardonnay","note":"The house's one white, a Russian River Valley Chardonnay."}],"traps":["Jordan Cabernet is labelled Alexander Valley, not Napa Valley; the house is in Sonoma County, across the Mayacamas from Napa.","Jordan holds its Cabernet back for years, so a newly released bottle carries a vintage older than a guest may expect."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-1d3uhs81","front":"Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon: which consultant, once of Beaulieu, set the house's French minded style?","back":"André Tchelistcheff, who advised Jordan from its first years."},{"id":"fl-br86hf57","front":"What struck Jordan's valley floor vineyard in the 1990s, and where did the house turn for its Cabernet?","back":"Phylloxera; it turned to growers across Alexander Valley and to hillside fruit, planting hillside vines on its own estate."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-44s34q33","q":"Besides Cabernet Sauvignon, which grapes complete recent blends of Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon?","opts":["Sangiovese with a little Canaiolo","Syrah, Grenache and a little Mourvèdre","Zinfandel, Petite Sirah and some Carignane","Merlot, Petit Verdot and a little Malbec"],"a":3,"exp":"Jordan blends Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot, Petit Verdot and a little Malbec in the Bordeaux manner; Zinfandel with Petite Sirah is the field blend of old Sonoma vineyards, not of Jordan.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ppeihdzl","q":"The first Jordan Cabernet Sauvignon was picked in 1976, a week after the château's cellar was finished. In which year was it released?","opts":["1985","1978","1980","1977"],"a":2,"exp":"The 1976 was released in 1980, after years in barrel and bottle at the winery, the habit the house has kept; a release in 1977 or 1978 would have left it too little time.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Jordan Winery: Our history","url":"https://www.jordanwinery.com/about-us/our-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Jordan Winery: 2019 Cabernet Sauvignon tasting note","url":"https://www.jordanwinery.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/2019-Jordan-Alexander-Valley-Cabernet-Tasting-Note.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Jordan Vineyard & Winery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jordan_Vineyard_&_Winery","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Jordan Vineyard & Winery producer profile","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/jordan-vineyard-winery-producer-profile-433218/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: André Tchelistcheff","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Tchelistcheff","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ois8qn2e": {"id":"fl-ois8qn2e","key":"seghesio","group":"sonoma","region":"napa-sonoma","producer":"Seghesio Family Vineyards","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Healdsburg, with the Home Ranch in Alexander Valley, Sonoma County","founded":"1895","wine":{"name":"Seghesio Home Ranch Zinfandel","appellation":"Alexander Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Zinfandel, the great majority, with a small share of Petite Sirah.","sites":"The Home Ranch in Alexander Valley, where the founder planted Zinfandel in 1895; as the house tells it, the oldest vines grow near the family's Victorian house at the foot of Rattlesnake Hill.","making":"Picked by hand, sorted and destemmed into small open fermenters, the grapes steep for nine to twelve days with gentle punch downs and no pumping over. The free run wine finishes its malolactic fermentation in barrel and ages for twelve to fifteen months, mostly in neutral French and American oak with a little new French oak, by the house's notes.","glass":"Black raspberry, bramble and blackberry jam with black pepper and clove, ripe and round, with soft tannin and a warm, peppery finish.","ageing":"Most vivid in its first years, while the fruit is bright; a strong vintage keeps and softens for a decade.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Home Ranch","means":"The family's first vineyard, in Alexander Valley, planted to Zinfandel in 1895."},{"term":"Alexander Valley","means":"The ranch's appellation, in northern Sonoma County; Dry Creek Valley is a separate one."}]},"table":"In 1895 a winemaker from Piedmont and his wife planted the Alexander Valley vineyard that gives Seghesio Home Ranch Zinfandel. With a little Petite Sirah and mostly older barrels, it runs ripe, peppery and round, a red for the grill.","story":"The founder left Piedmont for Sonoma County in 1886 to work as a winemaker at the Italian Swiss Colony. In 1895 he and his wife bought a stretch of Alexander Valley and planted Zinfandel there, the vineyard the family calls the Home Ranch. He kept his post at the Colony until 1902, when he finished a winery of his own, and his vines came through Prohibition. Long a seller of bulk wine, the family released wines under the Seghesio name only in 1983. Early in the 1990s the family cut its output sharply and turned to wines from the vineyards it farmed, Zinfandel above all. The Home Ranch Zinfandel is the wine the house builds around the vines of 1895. Crimson Wine Group bought the business from the family in 2011, and family members stayed on for a time in the cellar and the vineyards. Home Ranch shows what an Italian family's Zinfandel planting can become over more than a century.","others":[{"name":"Seghesio Sonoma Zinfandel","note":"A Zinfandel blended from several vineyards and labelled Sonoma County, the house's broader bottling."}],"traps":["The Home Ranch lies in Alexander Valley, while the winery stands in Healdsburg; the label names Alexander Valley.","Petite Sirah in the blend is not Syrah: it is Durif, a separate grape long grown among California's Zinfandel."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-j83nv2d9","front":"Seghesio: when did the family first bottle wine under its own name?","back":"In 1983; for decades before, the family sold its wine in bulk."},{"id":"fl-7uf9nzvo","front":"Seghesio's Home Ranch: beside which house and hill do its old Zinfandel vines grow?","back":"The family's Victorian house, at the foot of Rattlesnake Hill in Alexander Valley."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-68pj2q6u","q":"How is Seghesio Home Ranch Zinfandel raised before bottling, by the house's own notes?","opts":["Three years in new American oak hogsheads","Twelve to fifteen months, mostly in neutral oak barrels","Many years in a solera of old casks","Six months in stainless steel tanks alone"],"a":1,"exp":"The house's sheets give twelve to fifteen months in barrel, mostly neutral French and American oak with a little new French; it is neither raised in steel alone nor in a solera.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-t05iruuw","q":"The founder of Seghesio came from Piedmont in 1886 to make wine in Sonoma County. Where did he work until 1902?","opts":["The Italian Swiss Colony","The Buena Vista winery","The Inglenook estate","The Fountaingrove colony"],"a":0,"exp":"He was a winemaker at the Italian Swiss Colony until 1902, when he finished his own winery; Buena Vista, Inglenook and Fountaingrove play no part in the family's story.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Seghesio Family Vineyards: Our story","url":"https://www.seghesio.com/story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Seghesio Family Vineyards: 2022 Home Ranch Zinfandel sheet","url":"https://www.seghesio.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/2022-Home-Ranch-Zinfandel.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Sonoma County Winegrowers: The Seghesio family","url":"https://sonomawinegrape.org/profile/the-seghesio-family/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Seghesio family sells historic winery","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/exclusive-seghesio-family-sells-historic-winery-45113","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Liberty Wines: Seghesio Home Ranch Alexander Valley Zinfandel","url":"https://www.libertywines.co.uk/our-portfolio/SE106-home-ranch-alexander-valley-zinfandel","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-8695hurq": {"id":"fl-8695hurq","key":"ridge","group":"central-coast","region":"central-coast","producer":"Ridge Vineyards","producerId":"p-ridge-vineyards","say":"","place":"Monte Bello Ridge above Cupertino, Santa Cruz Mountains, California","founded":"1962","wine":{"name":"Monte Bello","appellation":"Santa Cruz Mountains","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the larger part, with Merlot and smaller shares of Petit Verdot and Cabernet Franc; the blend changes with the vintage.","sites":"The Monte Bello vineyard on the ridge above the San Andreas fault, from about 400 to over 800 metres up, on fractured limestone under a layer of weathered greenstone and clay.","making":"Each parcel is fermented apart with the vineyard's own yeasts and goes through its malolactic fermentation naturally. The lots are then tasted and only a part of them chosen for Monte Bello; the blend is raised in barrels of air dried American oak, with a little French, and bottled after well over a year in wood.","glass":"Plum, cassis and blackberry over cedar, tobacco and violet, with an iron edge and, when young, a touch of vanilla from the American oak; leather and anise come with age, over firm, fine tannin and fresh acidity.","ageing":"Tight for its first ten years or so, it unfolds slowly over decades, as the 1971 showed when it was poured again thirty years after Paris.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Monte Bello","means":"The vineyard on Monte Bello Ridge, first planted in the 1880s, and the name of the wine drawn from it."},{"term":"Santa Cruz Mountains","means":"The appellation: the range between San Francisco Bay and the Pacific, south of the city and well away from Napa."},{"term":"Ingredients","means":"Ridge lists on its back labels every ingredient used in making the wine, a habit of the house since the 2011 vintage."}]},"table":"Fifth in Paris in 1976, the 1971 Monte Bello of Ridge Vineyards placed first when the same wines were poured again in 2006. A Cabernet led blend from a limestone ridge in the Santa Cruz Mountains, raised in American oak, it wants years and a decanter.","story":"Long before Ridge, a San Francisco doctor bought land high on Monte Bello Ridge in 1885, planted vines and built a winery, ready for the vintage of 1892, as Ridge tells it. By the 1940s his winery had closed and its vines were abandoned. In 1949 a theologian who had bought an old winery and vineyard just below replanted Cabernet Sauvignon there, and those vines would make the first Ridge wines. In 1959 a small group of engineers from the Stanford Research Institute bought his land, and in 1962 they bonded its winery again as Ridge Vineyards and made the first commercial Monte Bello; in 1968 they bought the doctor's old winery as well. A winemaker who joined them in 1969 led the cellar until 2016, holding to native yeasts, American oak and what the house calls pre-industrial winemaking. In Paris in May 1976, among ten reds of Bordeaux and California judged blind by a French panel, the 1971 Monte Bello placed fifth. Late in 1986 Ridge became part of the Japanese Otsuka group, which promised, Ridge says, to leave the business as it was. Thirty years after Paris, in 2006, the same wines were poured blind again, and the 1971 placed first. It is the wine to pour when a guest asks whether California Cabernet can age.","others":[{"name":"Geyserville","note":"A Zinfandel led field blend from old vines in Alexander Valley, Sonoma, with Carignane and Petite Sirah."},{"name":"Lytton Springs","note":"From Dry Creek Valley, Zinfandel with Petite Sirah and Carignane, labelled as a blend rather than as one grape."}],"traps":["Monte Bello grows in the Santa Cruz Mountains south of San Francisco, not in Napa, though it stood beside Napa's wines in Paris.","Its American oak points blind tasters towards a warm region or towards Rioja, yet the vineyard is high and cool.","The 1971 Monte Bello placed fifth in Paris in 1976 and first at the tasting of 2006; the red that won in 1976 was Stag's Leap Wine Cellars' 1973."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-48dbkvq6","front":"Ridge Monte Bello: what soil lies under the vineyard, and what fault runs below the ridge?","back":"Fractured limestone under weathered greenstone and clay, high above the San Andreas fault."},{"id":"fl-58nv95od","front":"Which Japanese group did Ridge Vineyards join at the end of 1986?","back":"The Otsuka group, a pharmaceutical company."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-mo20drf2","q":"Tasting Monte Bello blind, a guest guesses a warm region from its vanilla. What explains the vanilla?","opts":["Large old casks of Slavonian oak","New French barriques from Burgundy","Barrels of air dried American oak","Oak chips added during fermentation"],"a":2,"exp":"Ridge raises Monte Bello mostly in air dried American oak, which gives the vanilla; the vineyard itself is high and cool, and French barrels play only a small part.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-lo4zjnos","q":"Thirty years after the Paris tasting of 1976, the same reds were poured blind again in 2006. Which placed first?","opts":["Château Haut-Brion 1970","Stag's Leap Wine Cellars 1973","Château Mouton Rothschild 1970","Ridge Monte Bello 1971"],"a":3,"exp":"The 1971 Monte Bello, fifth in 1976, placed first in 2006, ahead of Stag's Leap Wine Cellars' 1973, the red that had won in Paris.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Ridge Vineyards: Monte Bello vineyard","url":"https://www.ridgewine.com/vineyards/monte-bello/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Ridge Vineyards: 2020 Monte Bello technical sheet","url":"https://www.ridgewine.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/20CMB.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Ridge Vineyards: History","url":"https://www.ridgewine.com/about/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Judgment of Paris (wine)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judgment_of_Paris_(wine)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"SevenFifty Daily: How Ridge Vineyards became an industry icon","url":"https://daily.sevenfifty.com/how-ridge-vineyards-became-an-industry-icon/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-gcn9z1rb": {"id":"fl-gcn9z1rb","key":"calera","group":"central-coast","region":"central-coast","producer":"Calera","producerId":"p-calera","say":"kuh-LAIR-uh","place":"Mount Harlan, in the Gavilan Mountains of San Benito County, California","founded":"1975","wine":{"name":"Calera Jensen Vineyard Pinot Noir","appellation":"Mt. Harlan","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone, from vines planted in 1975.","sites":"The Jensen vineyard, the largest of the three parcels planted in 1975, on limestone rich hillside soil at about 670 metres on Mount Harlan, some twenty five miles east of Monterey Bay.","making":"Fermented as whole clusters with native yeasts in open fermenters, punched down and pressed after a fortnight or more in the house's gravity flow winery. The wine then spends sixteen to eighteen months in French oak barrels, about a third of them new.","glass":"Dark red cherry, wild strawberry and black raspberry, with the rosehip and orange peel of the house and touches of red tea and sassafras; wiry acidity and fine, firm tannin, with autumn leaves as it ages.","ageing":"Tight for its first five years or so, it lasts fifteen to twenty years when the vintage is strong.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle briefly if it seems closed, an old one gently off its sediment; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Mt. Harlan","means":"The appellation approved in 1990, drawn around Calera's limestone vineyards high in the Gavilan Mountains."},{"term":"Jensen Vineyard","means":"The largest of the three parcels planted in 1975, named, by the house's account, for the founder's father."},{"term":"Calera","means":"Spanish for lime kiln, after the old lime kiln on the property."}]},"table":"On Mount Harlan, inland from the coast, Calera planted three limestone parcels in 1975, and its Jensen Vineyard Pinot Noir comes from one of them. Whole clusters and native yeasts give a wiry, long lived Pinot Noir of cherry, rosehip and orange peel.","story":"Calera grew out of a search for limestone. Its founder, Josh Jensen (1944 to 2022), worked harvests in Burgundy and came home convinced that Pinot Noir needs limestone soil, which is rare in California. His search ended high in the Gavilan Mountains of San Benito County, at a property where limestone had once been quarried and burnt, and he bought it in 1974. The old lime kiln there gave the house its name, calera being Spanish for lime kiln. In 1975 he planted three parcels of Pinot Noir, Selleck, Reed and Jensen, the largest, named, as the house has it, for his father; the first crop came in 1978. In 1990 Mount Harlan was approved as an appellation of its own, drawn around Calera's vineyards. The house restored the old kiln in 1997, as it tells the story, and in 2017 the Duckhorn group bought Calera; its founder died in 2022, aged 78. Jensen, the parcel that bears the family name, puts to the test whether California soil can speak as clearly as Burgundy's.","others":[{"name":"Selleck Pinot Noir","note":"From another of the three parcels planted in 1975, smaller than Jensen and bottled on its own."},{"name":"Mt. Harlan Viognier","note":"From vines first planted in 1983, among the early plantings of Viognier in California."}],"traps":["Mount Harlan lies some twenty five miles inland in San Benito County, not on the coast; height keeps it cool, with marine air drawn in from Monterey Bay.","Calera is Spanish for lime kiln: the name points to the limestone, not to a family or a place on the coast.","Jensen, Selleck and Reed are parcels of the house's own estate on Mount Harlan, not vineyards bought from growers."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-v5mn9z7c","front":"Calera: what does the Spanish name mean, and why was it chosen?","back":"Lime kiln, after the old kiln on the Mount Harlan property, where limestone was once quarried and burnt."},{"id":"fl-f6mgxgri","front":"In which year was the Mount Harlan appellation approved, and whose vineyards does it hold?","back":"1990; it was drawn around Calera's limestone vineyards in the Gavilan Mountains."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ic05kjhi","q":"Calera's Jensen Vineyard Pinot Noir grows some way inland, yet it stays fresh. Why?","opts":["Ageing in stainless steel rather than oak","Height, at about 670 metres on Mount Harlan","A vineyard on the shore of Monterey Bay","White grapes blended in for lift"],"a":1,"exp":"Jensen lies at about 670 metres in the Gavilan Mountains, and the height, with marine air from the bay, keeps it cool; the vineyard is some twenty five miles inland, not on the shore.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-krlpayqi","q":"What drew Calera's founder to a remote hilltop in San Benito County in 1974?","opts":["Limestone soil, like that of Burgundy","Old Pinot Noir vines left from the 1880s","An old winery standing empty since Prohibition","A deep spring of water for irrigation"],"a":0,"exp":"He came home from Burgundy convinced that Pinot Noir needs limestone, and found it at an old lime quarry on Mount Harlan; his vines were planted new in 1975, not inherited.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Calera: The Calera story","url":"https://www.calerawine.com/our-story/the-calera-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Calera: Mt. Harlan estate vineyards","url":"https://www.calerawine.com/vineyards/mt-harlan-vineyards","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Calera: 2013 Jensen Vineyard Pinot Noir fact sheet","url":"https://www.calerawine.com/assets/client/File/TradeAsset/FactSheets/MTHJensen/CL13JNPN.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine and Spirits Magazine: Josh Jensen, 1944 to 2022","url":"https://www.wineandspiritsmagazine.com/?p=43678","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Josh Jensen, founder of Calera Wine Company, dies at 78","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/josh-jensen-founder-of-calera-wine-company-dies-at-78","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-q3zk4v1e": {"id":"fl-q3zk4v1e","key":"au-bon-climat","group":"central-coast","region":"central-coast","producer":"Au Bon Climat","producerId":"p-au-bon-climat","say":"oh bawn klee-MAH","place":"Santa Maria Valley, Santa Barbara County, California","founded":"1982","wine":{"name":"Au Bon Climat Isabelle Pinot Noir","appellation":"California","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone, from several vineyards.","sites":"A selection of barrels that changes with the year: Bien Nacido in Santa Maria Valley, Sanford and Benedict in Sta. Rita Hills, Los Alamos and others, in some years reaching north to Anderson Valley and the Sonoma Coast.","making":"Each small lot is fermented apart in open fermenters, punched down by hand once or twice a day and moved only by gravity, never pumped. The lots age in new French oak barrels from François Frères for twenty to twenty four months, and the barrels for Isabelle are chosen by tasting over several months.","glass":"Red cherry, wild strawberry and candied cranberry with white pepper, cinnamon and a lift of jasmine; brisk acidity and fine tannin keep it lean and savoury rather than lush.","ageing":"Tight on release, it opens after four or five years and lasts fifteen years and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour if it seems closed; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Isabelle","means":"The selection of chosen barrels from several vineyards, named for the founder's daughter."},{"term":"Au Bon Climat","means":"French for a well exposed vineyard, borrowing Burgundy's word climat for a named site."},{"term":"California","means":"The appellation of most vintages, the barrels coming from more than one county; a few vintages read Santa Barbara County."}]},"table":"Barrels chosen from several vineyards, Bien Nacido and Sanford and Benedict among them, make up Au Bon Climat's Isabelle, a Pinot Noir raised entirely in new French oak. Red cherry and white pepper run through a spare, savoury wine meant for food.","story":"Zaca Mesa gave Jim Clendenen (1953 to 2021) his start, as an assistant winemaker from the harvest of 1978; he then worked vintages in Australia and France, as the house tells it, and came back sure that Santa Barbara County could make wine like Burgundy's. With a partner he made the first Au Bon Climat from the harvest of 1982, in a converted dairy barn at the Los Alamos vineyard; the name, in the house's reading, means a well exposed vineyard. At the start of the 1990s the partner left to found a separate winery, and Clendenen carried on alone. He bought grapes from the cool valleys of the county that run from east to west, made Bien Nacido a vineyard name, and later moved his cellar into a winery on that vineyard, shared with Qupé. While others chased ripeness he held to lean, savoury wines made for the table. In the mid 1990s he began Isabelle, a selection of his chosen barrels of Pinot Noir from all his sources, named for his daughter. After his death in 2021 the house passed to his son and daughter. Isabelle answers anyone who believes California Pinot Noir must be ripe and heavy.","others":[{"name":"Nuits-Blanches au Bon Climat","note":"The house's Chardonnay in the manner of Burgundy, the white beside Isabelle."},{"name":"Bien Nacido Pinot Noir","note":"Pinot Noir from the Santa Maria Valley vineyard the house made known by name."},{"name":"Sanford and Benedict Chardonnay","note":"Chardonnay from the Sta. Rita Hills vineyard of that name."}],"traps":["Bien Nacido is a grower's vineyard that sells grapes to many houses; it is not an Au Bon Climat property, though the house long made its wine in a winery built on it.","Au Bon Climat and Qupé are separate houses that shared one winery.","Isabelle is a blend of barrels from several vineyards, labelled California in most vintages, not a single vineyard wine of Santa Maria Valley."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ldmhchol","front":"Au Bon Climat: what does the name mean, as the house gives it?","back":"A well exposed vineyard, in the French of Burgundy, where a climat is a named site."},{"id":"fl-n1dhzldn","front":"With which Santa Barbara house did Au Bon Climat share its winery on Bien Nacido?","back":"Qupé, a separate house with its own wines."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-w0biofy9","q":"How does Au Bon Climat's Isabelle differ from the house's single vineyard Pinot Noirs?","opts":["It is aged in large old casks, not barrels","It comes from one walled parcel on Bien Nacido","It is a selection of barrels from several vineyards","It is made from bought wine finished at the winery"],"a":2,"exp":"Isabelle draws its chosen barrels from several vineyards each year, from Bien Nacido to Sanford and Benedict, and ages them in new French oak; it is no single parcel of Bien Nacido.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-31rw1k80","q":"In what kind of building did Au Bon Climat make its first wine, from the harvest of 1982?","opts":["A former mission chapel in Santa Barbara","A stone cellar built in the 1880s","A new winery built on Bien Nacido","A converted dairy barn at Los Alamos"],"a":3,"exp":"Clendenen and a partner began in a converted dairy barn at the Los Alamos vineyard; only later did the house move into a winery on Bien Nacido, shared with Qupé.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Au Bon Climat: Our story","url":"https://aubonclimat.com/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Au Bon Climat: 2022 Pinot Noir Isabelle technical sheet","url":"https://media.aubonclimat.com/content/uploads/2025/02/06144531/AuBonClimat-PNI22D-2022-PinotNoir-Isabelle.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: How Au Bon Climat's second generation filled a giant's shoes","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/california/how-au-bon-climats-second-generation-filled-a-giants-shoes-while-dancing-to-their-own-tune/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Santa Barbara Independent: Jim Clendenen, 1953 to 2021","url":"https://www.independent.com/2021/06/17/jim-clendenen-1953-2021/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Au Bon Climat's Jim Clendenen, colorful Santa Barbara pioneer, dies at 68","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/au-bon-climat-s-jim-clendenen-colorful-santa-barbara-pioneer-dies-at-68","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-5kwzz4ax": {"id":"fl-5kwzz4ax","key":"tablas-creek","group":"central-coast","region":"central-coast","producer":"Tablas Creek Vineyard","producerId":"p-tablas-creek","say":"TAH-blahs, ess-PREE duh TAH-blahs","place":"Adelaida District, Paso Robles, Central Coast, California","founded":"1989","wine":{"name":"Esprit de Tablas","appellation":"Adelaida District, Paso Robles","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Mourvèdre in the lead, often two fifths to a half, with Grenache, Syrah and a little Counoise.","sites":"The estate's own vineyard on the limestone hillsides of the Adelaida District west of Paso Robles, planted from cuttings brought from Château de Beaucastel and grown on in the estate's nursery.","making":"Each variety is fermented apart with native yeasts, in large wooden upright vats, open tanks and closed steel tanks. The lots chosen for Esprit are blended in the spring after the harvest, then aged about a further year in French oak foudres of some 4,500 litres before bottling.","glass":"Blackberry, plum and red currant over new leather, black tea, fennel and star anise, with a chalky grip; savoury rather than sweet, with firm, fine tannin and fresh acidity from the cool nights.","ageing":"Approachable a few years after release, it gains over five to ten, and strong vintages last twenty years and more, by the house's own tastings back to 2000.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Esprit de Tablas","means":"The estate's leading red blend, called Esprit de Beaucastel until the name changed with the 2011 vintage."},{"term":"Adelaida District","means":"One of eleven appellations created within Paso Robles in 2014, in the hills of its western side."},{"term":"Paso Robles","means":"The wider appellation of San Luis Obispo County, of which the Adelaida District is part."}]},"table":"Think of Esprit de Tablas as Châteauneuf grammar in California: Tablas Creek Vineyard grows this Mourvèdre led red on Paso Robles limestone from vines brought over from Château de Beaucastel. Expect leather, plum and fennel, savoury and firm; roast lamb is its match.","story":"Soil and climate like Beaucastel's were what the Perrin family of Château de Beaucastel in Châteauneuf-du-Pape and their American importer, Robert Haas (1927 to 2018), sought across California: limestone, warm, sunny days and cool nights. In 1989 they bought a property in the hills west of Paso Robles and named it for the creek that runs through it. They brought vine cuttings from Beaucastel itself, and from 1990 these spent some three years in the quarantine and testing of the United States Department of Agriculture, and the partners then set up a nursery on the estate to graft and grow them. The first vines went into the ground in 1994, and the first red came from the harvest of 1997. The nursery went on to supply Rhône cuttings to many other American growers. The flagship was named Esprit de Beaucastel from the 2000 vintage and took its own name, Esprit de Tablas, with the 2011, so that the estate would stand apart from Beaucastel. In 2014 its hills became the Adelaida District, one of eleven new appellations within Paso Robles, and four years later, in 2018, Haas died, aged 90. Poured beside a Châteauneuf, Esprit makes plain what Mourvèdre brings.","others":[{"name":"Esprit de Tablas Blanc","note":"The white counterpart, a Roussanne led blend from the same estate vineyard."},{"name":"Côtes de Tablas","note":"The estate's red blend made to drink earlier than the Esprit."}],"traps":["The cuttings came from Château de Beaucastel itself, not from French nurseries in general, and passed through American quarantine before planting.","Esprit de Tablas leads with Mourvèdre, unlike most California Rhône blends, which lead with Grenache or Syrah.","Esprit de Beaucastel was this wine's earlier name in Paso Robles, never a wine of Château de Beaucastel in Châteauneuf."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-4gi33b15","front":"Tablas Creek: under what name was its leading red sold before the 2011 vintage?","back":"Esprit de Beaucastel."},{"id":"fl-k5wu3pdl","front":"Which family of Châteauneuf-du-Pape joined an American importer to found Tablas Creek in 1989?","back":"The Perrin family of Château de Beaucastel."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-1qp2sj0b","q":"A guest notes the leather and firm grip of Esprit de Tablas and asks which grape leads the blend. What do you say?","opts":["Syrah","Mourvèdre","Counoise","Grenache"],"a":1,"exp":"Mourvèdre leads, often two fifths to a half of the blend, and gives the leather and grip; Grenache follows it here, where most California Rhône blends lead with Grenache or Syrah.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-yssnaxz8","q":"The cuttings Tablas Creek brought from Beaucastel from 1990 were held for some three years before planting. Why?","opts":["Quarantine and virus testing by the American government","Waiting for the Adelaida District to be approved","Rooting first in Beaucastel's own nursery","Waiting for the estate's soil to be cleared of phylloxera"],"a":0,"exp":"Imported vines must pass the Department of Agriculture's quarantine and testing, which took about three years; the Adelaida District came only in 2014.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Tablas Creek Vineyard: History","url":"https://tablascreek.com/story/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tablas Creek Vineyard: 2015 Esprit de Tablas","url":"https://tablascreek.com/wines/2015_esprit_de_tablas","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tablas Creek Vineyard blog: Stepping out of Beaucastel's shadow","url":"https://blog.tablascreek.com/2013/06/stepping-out-of-beaucastels-shadow/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine History Project: Robert Haas, 1927 to 2018","url":"https://winehistoryproject.org/robert-haas-1927-2018/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Tablas Creek cofounder and Paso Robles pioneer Robert Haas dies at 90","url":"https://winespectator.com/webfeature/show/id/Robert-Haas-Dies-at-90","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-zrvxgmoo": {"id":"fl-zrvxgmoo","key":"bonny-doon","group":"central-coast","region":"central-coast","producer":"Bonny Doon Vineyard","producerId":"p-bonny-doon","say":"luh see-GAR voh-LAHN","place":"Santa Cruz, on the Central Coast of California","founded":"1983","wine":{"name":"Le Cigare Volant","appellation":"Central Coast","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Grenache in the lead in most years, with Syrah, Cinsault and Mourvèdre; the shares, and in some years the leading grape, change with the vintage.","sites":"Bought grapes from vineyards across the Central Coast, Monterey County among them, since the house's first vineyard at Bonny Doon in the Santa Cruz Mountains was given up.","making":"The method has changed over the years. The 2012 and the 2015 were raised partly in puncheons and large wooden uprights, the 2015 in part also in five gallon glass demijohns, while a later release was aged in stainless steel tanks and bottled ten months after the harvest.","glass":"Kirsch, raspberry and red plum with white pepper and wild thyme; medium bodied and moderate in alcohol rather than heavy, with fresh acidity and supple tannin.","ageing":"Generous within two or three years of the vintage, it keeps for a decade and more, as the house's own tastings of older vintages show.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; no need to decant a young bottle, an old one gently off its sediment; a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Le Cigare Volant","means":"French for the flying cigar, a craft of that shape barred from Châteauneuf's vineyards in 1954."},{"term":"Central Coast","means":"The broad appellation from San Francisco Bay to Santa Barbara, since the grapes come from several counties."},{"term":"Bonny Doon","means":"A hamlet in the Santa Cruz Mountains, where the house began and planted its first vineyard."}]},"table":"Homage to Châteauneuf-du-Pape is the idea behind Le Cigare Volant, Bonny Doon Vineyard's Grenache led blend first made in 1984 and named for the flying cigars a Châteauneuf decree of 1954 forbade to land. Kirsch and wild thyme fill the glass, and roast chicken suits it.","story":"In 1954 the village council of Châteauneuf-du-Pape, uneasy at reports of flying saucers, passed an order that barred them from landing in its vineyards; in French such a craft was a flying cigar. Bonny Doon Vineyard was founded in 1983 at Bonny Doon in the Santa Cruz Mountains and its founder soon turned to the grapes of the Rhône. In 1984 he made the first Le Cigare Volant, a blend of Châteauneuf's principal varieties grown under California conditions, in the house's words, and released it in 1986 with a flying saucer on its label. He became a leading voice of the Rhône Rangers, the California growers of Rhône grapes, and from the start of the 2000s an early convert to the screwcap, for wines meant to age as well as for wines to drink young. The first vineyard at Bonny Doon was given up, and the wine has long been made from bought grapes across the Central Coast. In 2020 the founder sold the Bonny Doon brand, Le Cigare Volant with it, and at first stayed on as its winemaker. On a list, Le Cigare Volant stands for Châteauneuf's grapes grown in California.","others":[{"name":"Vin Gris de Cigare","note":"The pink version of the same idea, a dry rosé of Rhône grapes."},{"name":"Le Cigare Blanc","note":"The white Rhône blend of the family, long built on Grenache Blanc with Roussanne."}],"traps":["The name means a flying cigar, a craft barred by Châteauneuf's order of 1954; it has nothing to do with tobacco.","Bonny Doon is a place in the Santa Cruz Mountains, but the grapes come from across the Central Coast, not from an estate there.","The house took up screwcaps for wines meant to age, not only for wines to drink young."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-6etnp72p","front":"Bonny Doon Vineyard: in which year was the first Le Cigare Volant made, and when was it released?","back":"It was made in 1984 and released in 1986."},{"id":"fl-iofq0q2q","front":"Which closure did Bonny Doon Vineyard take up early in the 2000s, for wines meant to age as well?","back":"The screwcap."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-vu8vsj3m","q":"Which grape leads the blend of Bonny Doon's Le Cigare Volant in most vintages?","opts":["Counoise","Syrah","Grenache","Carignan"],"a":2,"exp":"Grenache leads in most years, as at Châteauneuf, with Syrah, Cinsault and Mourvèdre beside it; Syrah plays a supporting part, and in a few vintages Mourvèdre has led instead.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qh9a4q4e","q":"Le Cigare Volant takes its name from an order passed at Châteauneuf-du-Pape in 1954. What did the order forbid?","opts":["Smoking cigars in its wine cellars","Grapes brought in from outside the commune","Planting vines on the village's roads","Flying saucers landing in its vineyards"],"a":3,"exp":"The village council barred flying saucers, called flying cigars in French, from landing in its vineyards; the name has nothing to do with tobacco.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bonny Doon Vineyard: About","url":"https://www.bonnydoonvineyard.com/about/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bonny Doon Vineyard: An apologia for Le Cigare Volant","url":"https://www.bonnydoonvineyard.com/an-apologia-for-le-cigare-volant-an-introduction-to-the-05-vintage-part-1-of-2/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bonny Doon Vineyard: Wines","url":"https://www.bonnydoonvineyard.com/wines/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Which producer's label bears a flying saucer?","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/advice/which-producer-s-label-bears-a-flying-saucer-51689","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bonny Doon Vineyard","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonny_Doon_Vineyard","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-az5x89n7": {"id":"fl-az5x89n7","key":"eyrie","group":"willamette","region":"willamette-valley","producer":"The Eyrie Vineyards","producerId":"p-eyrie-vineyards","say":"EYE-ree","place":"Dundee Hills, Willamette Valley, Oregon","founded":"1966","wine":{"name":"South Block Reserve Pinot Noir","appellation":"Dundee Hills","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone, from the founder's ungrafted vines.","sites":"The South Block, ten rows in a corner of the original Eyrie Vineyard in the Dundee Hills, first planted in 1968 where six soil types meet; the vines grow on their own roots without irrigation.","making":"Made as the house makes all its Pinot Noir: fermented by native yeasts, mostly destemmed with at most a small share of whole clusters, and aged in old oak barrels, barely one in twenty five of them new. Malolactic fermentation comes naturally and in full; the wine is racked little, never fined and only lightly filtered.","glass":"Pale ruby in the glass: sour cherry, wild strawberry and rose petal, then fennel, leather and damp earth; light in body and alcohol, its acidity bright and its tannin fine and gentle.","ageing":"Delicate and perfumed young, it gains savoury depth over twenty years and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"13 to 15 degrees Celsius, a little cool to keep its perfume; decant only an old bottle, gently off its sediment; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"South Block Reserve","means":"Ten rows of the original Eyrie Vineyard, bottled apart since the 1975 vintage."},{"term":"Dundee Hills","means":"An AVA within the Willamette Valley, in force since 2005, so older vintages could not carry it."},{"term":"Pinot Noir","means":"Oregon asks at least 90 per cent of Pinot Noir to use its name, where federal law asks 75."}]},"table":"Ten rows planted in 1968, in a corner of the original Dundee Hills vineyard, give The Eyrie Vineyards its South Block Reserve; the house is credited with the Willamette Valley's first Pinot Noir. Pale, with sour cherry, rose and damp earth, its 1975 carried Oregon's name to Paris and Beaune.","story":"David Lett (1939 to 2008) studied viticulture at the University of California at Davis and came north to plant Pinot Noir against the advice of his professors. In 1965 he brought some three thousand cuttings to the Willamette Valley, set them out in a nursery and then moved them to a former orchard in the Dundee Hills that he bought in 1966. The farm took the name The Eyrie from the red-tailed hawks nesting in its firs. Lett is credited with the valley's first Pinot Noir and the first Pinot Gris in the United States, and he made his first vintage in 1970. The South Block, ten rows planted in 1968, was first bottled on its own in 1975. In 1979 that wine was poured blind among Pinot Noirs at a tasting in Paris held by the guide Gault-Millau, and it finished ahead of several red Burgundies. A year later the Beaune house of Joseph Drouhin restaged the tasting at home, and the Oregon wine came second, narrowly, to a Drouhin Chambolle-Musigny of 1959. Burgundy took notice, and in 1987 the Drouhin family made its own purchase in the Dundee Hills. After Lett's death in 2008 his son set the South Block bottling aside for some years in tribute, then revived it. To open one is to taste the Willamette Valley's Pinot Noir at its source.","others":[{"name":"Original Vines Pinot Noir","note":"From the vines of the first plantings, the house's statement of its own history."},{"name":"Estate Pinot Gris","note":"From the grape Lett is credited with planting first in the United States, in the 1960s."}],"traps":["The South Block was planted in 1968, after the founder's first vines of 1965; it is a corner of the original vineyard, not its first planting.","The 1975 South Block shone at Gault-Millau's tasting in Paris in 1979 and came second at the Beaune rematch of 1980; neither was the Judgement of Paris of 1976.","Its pale colour and light body are the house's chosen style, not a sign of a weak year or of a tired bottle."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-jnt0w807","front":"The Eyrie's South Block Reserve: when were its vines planted, and when was it first bottled apart?","back":"Planted in 1968 in a corner of the original Dundee Hills vineyard; bottled on its own from the 1975 vintage."},{"id":"fl-dt5xs6im","front":"Which Oregon wine came second to a 1959 Drouhin Chambolle-Musigny at a rematch in Beaune in 1980?","back":"The Eyrie Vineyards' 1975 South Block Reserve Pinot Noir, a result that drew the Drouhin family to Oregon."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-iw7o8ozq","q":"Which description fits the way The Eyrie makes its South Block Reserve Pinot Noir?","opts":["Native yeasts and old barrels, barely any of them new","Whole bunches for most of the fermentation","Cultured yeasts and new French oak throughout","Long ageing in large American oak casks"],"a":0,"exp":"The house ferments its reds with native yeasts and buys barely one new barrel in twenty five; whole clusters are at most a small share, and the barrels are old, not new.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-e6h5d4pi","q":"In 1980 a Beaune house restaged a Paris tasting, and The Eyrie's 1975 South Block Reserve came second. What did the result lead to?","opts":["The South Block was first bottled on its own","The Drouhin family bought land in the Dundee Hills","Lett planted his first vines in the Dundee Hills","The Eyrie made its first vintage of Pinot Noir"],"a":1,"exp":"The result drew the Drouhin family of Beaune to Oregon, where it bought Dundee Hills land in 1987; the first vintage of 1970 and the first South Block bottling of 1975 came before the tasting.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"The Eyrie Vineyards: Grapegrowing, the vineyards","url":"https://www.eyrievineyards.com/vineyards.shtml","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Eyrie Vineyards: Winemaking","url":"https://eyrievineyards.com/winemaking.shtml","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, The Eyrie Vineyards South Block Reserve Pinot Noir 1975","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-the-eyrie-vineyards-south-block-reserve-pinot-noir-1975-406198/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: David Lett, founder of Oregon Pinot Noir, dies","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/david-lett-founder-of-oregon-pinot-noir-dies-77276/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: The Eyrie Vineyards","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Eyrie_Vineyards","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-8q4m9od0": {"id":"fl-8q4m9od0","key":"ddo","group":"willamette","region":"willamette-valley","producer":"Domaine Drouhin Oregon","producerId":"p-domaine-drouhin-oregon","say":"doh-MEN droo-AN, loh-REN","place":"Dundee Hills, Willamette Valley, Oregon","founded":"1987","wine":{"name":"Pinot Noir Laurène","appellation":"Dundee Hills","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone, all of it grown on the family's own estate.","sites":"The family's estate in the Dundee Hills, on the red volcanic Jory soil of those hills; Laurène draws on chosen barrels from across the estate rather than one block.","making":"Picked by hand into small boxes, sorted, destemmed and fermented with indigenous yeasts over seven to twelve days of punching down and pumping over, in a four level winery that moves the wine by gravity. After twelve to fourteen months in French oak, a share of it new, the winemaker chooses the barrels with extra complexity, length and depth for Laurène, and the blend rests before bottling.","glass":"Dark and red cherry, dried rose petal and clove over a touch of toast and candied orange peel; silky in texture, held by a firm frame and velvety tannin.","ageing":"By the house's own guide it is at its fullest after ten to twelve years and keeps for about fifteen; younger, it rewards air.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; half an hour in a decanter for a young bottle, a slow pour off the sediment for an old one; a broad bowled Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Laurène","means":"Named for the eldest daughter of the family's winemaker; first made from the 1992 vintage."},{"term":"Domaine Drouhin Oregon","means":"The Oregon estate of the Drouhin family of Beaune, founded in 1987."},{"term":"Dundee Hills","means":"Hills known for red volcanic Jory soil; a sub-appellation of the Willamette Valley in force since 2005."}]},"table":"Burgundy came to the Dundee Hills with Domaine Drouhin Oregon, the Beaune family's estate, and Laurène, named for the winemaker's eldest daughter, is its pick of the cellar's barrels. Its cherry, rose and clove, silky but firmly framed, bring Burgundian restraint to Oregon's red soil.","story":"The Drouhin family's road to Oregon ran through two tastings. At a large blind tasting of wines from many countries, held in Paris in 1979 by the Gault-Millau guide, The Eyrie Vineyards' 1975 South Block Reserve placed above several red Burgundies among the Pinot Noirs. In 1980 the head of the Drouhin house restaged the contest in Beaune, and this time the Oregon wine took second place, a fraction behind the house's own Chambolle-Musigny of 1959. The result stayed with the family. In 1987 it bought land in the Dundee Hills, and the head of the house named his daughter, of the fourth generation, winemaker from the start. The first vintage followed in 1988, and in 1989 the family built a winery on four levels so that grapes and wine could move by gravity rather than by pump. In 1992 the winemaker gave her eldest daughter's name to a new selection of the cellar's chosen barrels, and Laurène is the wine the house holds up as its flagship. Other cuvées carry the names of members of the family, Louise for a further Pinot Noir and Arthur for Chardonnay, and the family added a second estate, Roserock, in the Eola-Amity Hills. Laurène answers a question guests often put: what Burgundy's own hands make of Dundee Hills fruit.","others":[{"name":"Dundee Hills Pinot Noir","note":"The estate's main Pinot Noir, the first wine the family made in Oregon, in 1988."},{"name":"Cuvée Louise","note":"A further selection of Pinot Noir, made only in some years and carrying a family name."},{"name":"Arthur Chardonnay","note":"The estate Chardonnay, under another family name."}],"traps":["Do not credit the Judgement of Paris of 1976 with drawing the Drouhin family to Oregon: The Eyrie's showings of 1979 and 1980 did.","Laurène is a selection of barrels from across the Dundee Hills estate, not a single vineyard; the cuvées carry family names.","Roserock, the family's second Oregon estate, is in the Eola-Amity Hills, not the Dundee Hills."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-5gpbmwrk","front":"Domaine Drouhin Oregon's Laurène: how are its barrels chosen?","back":"After twelve to fourteen months in French oak, the winemaker picks the barrels of estate Pinot Noir with extra complexity, length and depth."},{"id":"fl-8ey36j31","front":"In which year did Domaine Drouhin Oregon buy its Dundee Hills land, and what drew the family there?","back":"1987, after The Eyrie's 1975 Pinot Noir ran a Drouhin Chambolle-Musigny close at Beaune in 1980."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-tly04mkp","q":"What sets Domaine Drouhin Oregon's Laurène apart from its Dundee Hills Pinot Noir?","opts":["It is fermented as whole bunches alone","It comes from the Roserock estate in Eola-Amity","It gathers the cellar's chosen barrels of estate fruit","It is aged for two years in American oak"],"a":2,"exp":"Laurène is made from barrels the winemaker selects for complexity, length and depth, all from the Dundee Hills estate; Roserock lies in the Eola-Amity Hills and gives wines of its own name.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-1qrho831","q":"Domaine Drouhin Oregon bought its Dundee Hills land in 1987. In which year was Laurène first made?","opts":["1988","1989","1980","1992"],"a":3,"exp":"Laurène was first made in 1992; 1988 was the estate's first vintage, 1989 the year its gravity winery was built, and 1980 the year of the Beaune rematch.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine Drouhin Oregon: Our Estate","url":"https://domainedrouhin.com/our-estate/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Drouhin Oregon: Learn More","url":"https://domainedrouhin.com/learn-more-domaine-drouhin-oregon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Maison Joseph Drouhin: Domaine Drouhin Oregon Pinot Noir Laurène","url":"https://www.drouhin.com/en_US/wine/domaine-drouhin-oregon-pinot-noit-laurene/2021","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: 30 years of Domaine Drouhin Oregon","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/30-years-of-domaine-drouhin-oregon-431546/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, The Eyrie Vineyards South Block Reserve Pinot Noir 1975","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-the-eyrie-vineyards-south-block-reserve-pinot-noir-1975-406198/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-thvnymcj": {"id":"fl-thvnymcj","key":"domaine-serene","group":"willamette","region":"willamette-valley","producer":"Domaine Serene","producerId":"","say":"doh-MEN suh-REEN","place":"Dundee Hills, Willamette Valley, Oregon","founded":"1989","wine":{"name":"Evenstad Reserve Pinot Noir","appellation":"Willamette Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone.","sites":"A selection of barrels made each year and labelled Willamette Valley rather than one hill. The house's first vineyard, the Mark Bradford, planted in 1993 in the Dundee Hills and named for the founders' son, began estates that later reached into the Eola-Amity Hills and Yamhill-Carlton.","making":"Each year the cellar sets aside the barrels it chooses for the reserve, a habit of the house since its first vintage. The wine is aged for well over a year in French oak barrels, a good part of them new, and since 2001 it has been made in a winery built on five levels, so that the wine falls from stage to stage by gravity.","glass":"Black cherry, bramble and cola, lifted by lilac over sassafras and clove; generous for Oregon Pinot Noir, with ripe, velvety tannin and a long finish.","ageing":"Generous from the outset; it goes on gaining in the cellar for a decade and more when the year is strong.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for half an hour; a Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Evenstad Reserve","means":"The founding family's name, on the reserve selection of barrels the house has made since its first vintage, 1990."},{"term":"Willamette Valley","means":"The valley's AVA of 1983, wider than the Dundee Hills where the house stands."}]},"table":"The Evenstad Reserve bears the name of the family who founded Domaine Serene in the Dundee Hills in 1989, and it has drawn on barrels set apart each year since the first vintage, 1990. Here the valley shows its riper, richer side: black cherry, cola and clove, full and velvety.","story":"Domaine Serene began on a hilltop in the Dundee Hills. Ken Evenstad (1943 to 2020), who ran the pharmaceutical company Upsher-Smith Laboratories, founded the estate there with his wife in 1989 and named it for their daughter. Their first Pinot Noir followed in 1990, and the barrels they set apart that year made the house's first Reserve, the start of the Evenstad Reserve, which the house calls its flagship. In 1993 they planted their first vineyard, the Mark Bradford, named for their son, and in 1994 they turned a historic building of 1913 in the town of Carlton into their first winery. In 2001 the house moved into a winery of its own on the hill, a building on five levels in which the wine moves by gravity. The house also makes Chardonnay, with an Evenstad Reserve of its own. In 2015 the family bought Château de la Crée in Burgundy's Côte d'Or, taking the house to Burgundy itself. Ken Evenstad died in 2020. Poured beside the pale Pinot Noir of the valley's first growers, the Evenstad Reserve brings out the region's ripe, full voice.","others":[{"name":"Evenstad Reserve Chardonnay","note":"The Chardonnay of the same name, labelled Dundee Hills."},{"name":"Yamhill Cuvée Pinot Noir","note":"A Willamette Valley blend from several hill appellations of the valley, named for Yamhill County."}],"traps":["Evenstad Reserve is labelled Willamette Valley, not Dundee Hills, though the house and its first vineyard stand in the Dundee Hills.","Domaine Serene is an Oregon house founded by an American family in 1989, not a Burgundy domaine; its Burgundy estate, Château de la Crée, came in 2015.","Mark Bradford names a vineyard; the Evenstad Reserve is chosen barrel by barrel, not drawn from one site."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8whdxr54","front":"Domaine Serene's Evenstad Reserve Pinot Noir: what is it, and since which vintage?","back":"A selection of the cellar's chosen barrels, made since the house's first vintage, 1990, and called its flagship."},{"id":"fl-evi42jii","front":"What did Domaine Serene finish building in the Dundee Hills in 2001?","back":"A winery on five levels, built for gravity flow, after years in a converted building of 1913 in Carlton."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-yaidbnil","q":"A guest reads Willamette Valley, not Dundee Hills, on Domaine Serene's Evenstad Reserve. What does that tell the guest?","opts":["It claims the valley's wider AVA rather than one hill","It is a blend of Pinot Noir and Chardonnay","It is a single vineyard wine from Carlton","It was made in the family's Burgundy cellar"],"a":0,"exp":"Willamette Valley is the wider AVA of 1983 that holds the Dundee Hills; the wine is Pinot Noir alone, made in Oregon, and Carlton was only the site of the first winery.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-6yts4bhw","q":"Which Burgundy property did the family behind Domaine Serene buy in 2015?","opts":["A cellar of barrels in the town of Beaune","Château de la Crée in the Côte d'Or","The négociant house of Joseph Drouhin","A Grand Cru parcel in Chablis"],"a":1,"exp":"In 2015 the founding family bought Château de la Crée in Burgundy's Côte d'Or; Joseph Drouhin is the Beaune family behind Domaine Drouhin Oregon, and the house bought neither a Beaune cellar nor a Chablis parcel.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Domaine Serene: About, the timeline","url":"https://www.domaineserene.com/about","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Domaine Serene: 2022 Evenstad Reserve Pinot Noir","url":"https://www.domaineserene.com/shop/2022-domaine-serene-evenstad-reserve-pinot-noir-pinot-noir","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Oregon Wine Press: Ken Evenstad, 1943 to 2020","url":"https://www.oregonwinepress.com/ken-evenstad-1943-2020","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Domaine Serene's co-founder Ken Evenstad dies","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2020/10/domaine-serenes-co-founder-ken-evenstad-dies/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Drug Store News: Upsher-Smith founder Kenneth Evenstad dies at 77","url":"https://drugstorenews.com/upsher-smith-founder-kenneth-evenstad-dies-77","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9bqf8ye9": {"id":"fl-9bqf8ye9","key":"argyle","group":"willamette","region":"willamette-valley","producer":"Argyle","producerId":"p-argyle","say":"","place":"Dundee, Willamette Valley, Oregon","founded":"1987","wine":{"name":"Argyle Extended Tirage Brut","appellation":"Willamette Valley","classification":"","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, Pinot Noir usually the larger part and the share changing with each vintage, with a little Meunier in some later years.","sites":"Pinot Noir and Chardonnay from vineyards across the Willamette Valley; a sister bottling, Extended Tirage Knudsen Vineyard Brut, comes from the Dundee Hills vineyard planted in the early 1970s that has given Argyle fruit since 1987.","making":"Traditional method throughout: the base wines are fermented mostly in stainless steel, with a small share in neutral barrels. After the second fermentation in bottle the wine rests about ten years on its yeast in the house's cellar, and it is disgorged on demand with a low dosage of a few grams a litre.","glass":"Brioche, roasted hazelnut and honeycomb over baked apple, nectarine and orange peel, with a fine, persistent mousse and a dry, saline finish.","ageing":"Released ready to drink after about ten years on its yeast; disgorged on demand so that it reaches the glass fresh, it needs no further cellaring.","serve":"9 to 11 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a tulip or a white wine glass rather than a narrow flute.","label":[{"term":"Extended Tirage","means":"Aged on its yeast in bottle for about ten years before disgorging."},{"term":"Brut","means":"Dry: the term Champagne uses for less than twelve grams of sugar a litre; here only a few."},{"term":"Willamette Valley","means":"A blend of sites from across the whole valley rather than one vineyard."}]},"table":"Founded in the town of Dundee in 1987 to make traditional method sparkling wine, Argyle rests its Extended Tirage Brut, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, about ten years on its yeast before disgorging. Expect brioche and roasted hazelnut over baked apple, with a fine, dry finish.","story":"The belief that the cool Willamette Valley suited sparkling wine made by the traditional method of Champagne lay behind Argyle's founding in 1987, and sparkling wine has been its signature from its first year. Its first winery was a former hazelnut drying plant in the town of Dundee, which the house calls the Nuthouse, and its first bottles were riddled on those premises. From the start it drew fruit from the Knudsen Vineyard in the Dundee Hills, planted in the early 1970s, one of the older vineyards of the valley. The house also kept bottles back from its first vintage to age on their yeast, and its tirage library, the house says, keeps sparkling wines on their lees from vintages reaching back to 1987. Out of that patience came the Extended Tirage wines, held about ten years before they are disgorged, one a blend of the valley and one from the Knudsen Vineyard alone. The house makes still Pinot Noir and Chardonnay as well. For the harvest of 2014, as production grew, it moved its winemaking to nearby Newberg, and the Dundee site was kept for receiving guests. The Extended Tirage Brut shows how far Oregon has taken the method: ten years on its yeast is more than three times the thirty six months from tirage to release that Champagne asks of a vintage wine.","others":[{"name":"Vintage Brut","note":"The vintage dated house sparkling wine of Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Meunier."},{"name":"Extended Tirage Knudsen Vineyard Brut","note":"The same long ageing, from the Knudsen Vineyard in the Dundee Hills alone."},{"name":"Julia Lee's Block Blanc de Blancs","note":"Chardonnay from a single block of the Knudsen Vineyard."}],"traps":["Argyle is Oregon sparkling wine made by the traditional method, not Champagne, whatever the method shares.","The Extended Tirage Brut is a Willamette Valley blend; the Extended Tirage Knudsen Vineyard Brut is the single vineyard bottling from the Dundee Hills.","Argyle's first winery, a former hazelnut drying plant, stands in the town of Dundee; the Dundee Hills is the AVA of the Knudsen Vineyard."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-5uydew2n","front":"Argyle Extended Tirage Brut: how long does it rest on its yeast, and when is it disgorged?","back":"About ten years in bottle, then disgorged on demand, so that each release reaches the glass fresh."},{"id":"fl-94icnweb","front":"What had Argyle's first winery building in the town of Dundee been before 1987?","back":"A hazelnut drying plant, which the house calls the Nuthouse."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-d5gnronz","q":"Where do the brioche and roasted nut notes of Argyle's Extended Tirage Brut come from?","opts":["A large dose of sugar at disgorging","A blend made mostly of Meunier","Fermentation entirely in new oak barrels","About ten years on its yeast before disgorging"],"a":3,"exp":"Long contact with the spent yeast in bottle gives the brioche and nutty notes; the base wines are fermented mostly in steel with a little neutral oak, and the dosage is low.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-20zh9na3","q":"Argyle moved its winemaking for the harvest of 2014. Where did it go?","opts":["To the Knudsen Vineyard in the hills","To a cellar in the Eola-Amity Hills","To a facility in nearby Newberg","To a former hazelnut plant in Dundee"],"a":2,"exp":"Production moved to Newberg for the harvest of 2014, and the house's Dundee site, where the hazelnut drying plant had been its first winery, was kept for guests; the Knudsen Vineyard gives fruit, not a cellar.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Argyle Winery: Sparkling Story","url":"https://argylewinery.com/about/winemaking/sparkling-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Argyle Winery: 2015 Extended Tirage Brut","url":"https://argylewinery.com/product/2015-extended-tirage-brut/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Argyle Winery: Viticulture","url":"https://argylewinery.com/about/viticulture/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Argyle Winery: A Vision Sparkles, the legacy of Argyle Winery in Dundee","url":"https://argylewinery.com/blog/trade-news/a-vision-sparkles-the-enduring-legacy-of-argyle-winery-in-dundee-in-the-heart-of-willamette-valley/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Oregon Wine Press: New to Newberg, Argyle's new winery","url":"https://www.oregonwinepress.com/argyle-newberg","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-8scnukjm": {"id":"fl-8scnukjm","key":"cristom","group":"willamette","region":"willamette-valley","producer":"Cristom Vineyards","producerId":"p-cristom","say":"KRIS-tom","place":"Spring Valley, Eola-Amity Hills, Willamette Valley, Oregon","founded":"1992","wine":{"name":"Mt. Jefferson Cuvée Pinot Noir","appellation":"Eola-Amity Hills","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone, blended from several vineyards.","sites":"The house's estate vineyards in the Eola-Amity Hills, on volcanic soils, mostly Columbia River basalt, cooled by Pacific wind through the Van Duzer Corridor, long joined by fruit from neighbouring growers; from the 2024 vintage the estate alone.","making":"Native yeasts carry the fermentation, with roughly half of the fruit as whole bunches with their stems and the rest destemmed. The wine then spends ten or eleven months on its lees in French oak barrels, about a fifth of them new, and is bottled without fining or filtration.","glass":"Tart cherry, raspberry and plum over rose, black tea and a peppery, savoury lift from the stems; light to medium in body, lively in acidity, with silky tannin and spice that lingers.","ageing":"Lively and open early on; a good year keeps and softens for about a decade.","serve":"15 to 16 degrees Celsius, on the cool side; it needs no decanter; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Mt. Jefferson Cuvée","means":"A blend of vineyards rather than one site, first made in 1994."},{"term":"Eola-Amity Hills","means":"The Willamette Valley sub-appellation of the estate's hills, in force since 2006."},{"term":"Cristom","means":"A name joined from the first names of the founders' two children."}]},"table":"Roughly half whole bunches and native yeasts give the Mt. Jefferson Cuvée of Cristom Vineyards, in the Eola-Amity Hills, its peppery, savoury lift over tart cherry and raspberry. A blend of vineyards, it is the house's flagship and the door to its style.","story":"In 1992 a family bought the abandoned Pellier winery in the Spring Valley of Polk County, in the hills that would become the Eola-Amity Hills, and founded Cristom there, naming it by joining the first names of its two children. Its first vintage was made the same autumn. Of the vines on the property, the family kept one block, planted in 1982 by the earlier owners, and renamed it Marjorie after the founder's mother; it gave a wine of its own from 1994. In 1994, too, came the first Mt. Jefferson Cuvée, a blend of estate fruit and grapes from neighbouring growers, which the house names as its flagship. The founding winemaker had come from Calera in California, where he had made the wine from the vintage of 1978 to that of 1991, and he brought its habit of fermenting Pinot Noir with whole bunches and native yeasts, which became Cristom's mark. Further estate vineyards followed, each named for a woman of the family, Louise and Jessie among them. The Eola-Amity Hills, where the Van Duzer Corridor draws cold Pacific air across the slopes each afternoon, became an AVA in 2006. With the 2024 vintage the Mt. Jefferson Cuvée was made from the estate's own vines alone for the first time. It stands as the house's lesson in what whole bunches bring to Pinot Noir.","others":[{"name":"Marjorie Vineyard Pinot Noir","note":"From the vineyard planted in 1982, before the family bought the property, and bottled on its own since 1994."},{"name":"Louise Vineyard Pinot Noir","note":"One of the estate's single vineyards, each named for a woman of the family."},{"name":"Jessie Vineyard Pinot Noir","note":"A further single vineyard wine of the estate, its name again from the family."}],"traps":["The Mt. Jefferson Cuvée is a blend of vineyards; Marjorie, Louise and Jessie are the house's single vineyard wines.","The Van Duzer Corridor is both the wind gap that cools the Eola-Amity Hills and, since 2019, an AVA of its own.","Whole bunch spice is a mark of Cristom's cellar, not of the Eola-Amity Hills appellation."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-voq0a5ym","front":"Cristom's Mt. Jefferson Cuvée: which two habits of fermentation shape it?","back":"Native yeasts and roughly half whole bunches, stems included, which give its peppery, savoury lift."},{"id":"fl-r4m6jr77","front":"Which abandoned winery's site did Cristom Vineyards buy in 1992?","back":"The Pellier winery in the Spring Valley of Polk County, in what became the Eola-Amity Hills."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-mce5gw1n","q":"A guest asks where the peppery, savoury note in Cristom's Mt. Jefferson Cuvée comes from. Which answer fits?","opts":["A share of Syrah in the blend","Whole bunches, stems included, in the ferment","Ageing in new American oak barrels","Grapes dried on mats before pressing"],"a":1,"exp":"Roughly half the fruit is fermented as whole bunches, and the stems give the spice; the oak is French and mostly used, and the wine is Pinot Noir alone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ob5o3dn2","q":"Cristom's founding winemaker brought whole bunch fermentation to its cellar in 1992. Where had he made wine before?","opts":["Calera, in California","Domaine Drouhin Oregon, in the Dundee Hills","Ridge, in the Santa Cruz Mountains","The Eyrie Vineyards, in the Dundee Hills"],"a":0,"exp":"He made Calera's wines from the vintage of 1978 to that of 1991 and came to Oregon straight from there, bringing its whole bunches and native yeasts; Ridge is known for Cabernet Sauvignon and Zinfandel, not Pinot Noir.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cristom Vineyards: Winemaking","url":"https://cristomvineyards.com/winemaking/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cristom Vineyards: 2024 Mt. Jefferson Cuvée Pinot Noir technical sheet","url":"https://cristomvineyards.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Cristom-Pinot-Noir-Mt-Jefferson-Cuvee-2024-Tech-Sheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cristom Vineyards: Marjorie Vineyard","url":"https://cristomvineyards.com/vineyards/marjorie-vineyard/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Cristom Vineyards","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cristom_Vineyards","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Cristom Vineyards producer page","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/producer/cristom-vineyards/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-hr15bxnf": {"id":"fl-hr15bxnf","key":"ste-michelle","group":"columbia","region":"columbia-valley","producer":"Chateau Ste. Michelle","producerId":"p-chateau-ste-michelle","say":"shah-TOH saynt mee-SHELL, eh-ROH-ih-kuh","place":"Woodinville, west of the Cascades, with vineyards across the Columbia Valley, Washington","founded":"1967","wine":{"name":"Eroica Riesling","appellation":"Columbia Valley","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Riesling, drawn from several vineyards in the desert east of the Cascades.","sites":"Columbia Valley vineyards east of the Cascades: the early vintages drew mostly on the warm Cold Creek Vineyard, later ones more on cooler sites.","making":"Fermented cool, so that the fruit and the acidity stay bright, and raised for some months, chiefly in stainless steel tanks. The wine is bottled with a little sugar left in it, enough to make it off dry against its high acidity.","glass":"Lime, green apple and white peach over orange blossom and a wet stone note; light in body and off dry, the touch of sweetness held in check by bright, high acidity, with a long, juicy finish.","ageing":"Bright and ready at once and at its freshest within a few years, though a good vintage keeps beyond ten years, gaining honey and the petrol note of aged Riesling.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a white wine glass, with crab, pork or a spiced dish from Asia.","label":[{"term":"Eroica","means":"The wine's name, taken from the Eroica, the Third Symphony of Beethoven."},{"term":"Dr. Loosen","means":"The Mosel estate of Bernkastel that makes this Riesling together with Chateau Ste. Michelle."},{"term":"Columbia Valley","means":"The broad appellation of the desert east of the Cascades, which lets the partners blend several vineyards."}]},"table":"Eroica is the Riesling Chateau Ste. Michelle has made since 1999 with Dr. Loosen of the Mosel: Columbia Valley fruit, fermented cool in steel and left off dry, with lime, white peach and a wet stone note. Pour it well chilled beside crab or a spiced dish.","story":"Chateau Ste. Michelle grew out of the end of Prohibition. By the company's account, two Seattle firms, Pommerelle and the National Wine Company, began making fruit wines in 1934, and by the mid 1950s the two had joined as American Wine Growers, a maker of mostly sweet, fortified wines. In 1967 the company launched Ste. Michelle Vintners for wines named by their grape, a Cabernet Sauvignon, a Pinot Noir, a Semillon and a Grenache rosé, and retained André Tchelistcheff (1901 to 1994), the Russian born winemaker of Napa's Beaulieu, as its consultant. In 1972 it began planting Cold Creek Vineyard in the desert east of the Cascades. In 1974 its 1972 Riesling came first in a blind tasting held by the Los Angeles Times, and Riesling became the grape the house is known by. In 1976 it moved into a French style château at Woodinville, near Seattle, built on a lumber baron's estate of 1912, and took the name Chateau Ste. Michelle. In 1999 Chateau Ste. Michelle and the Mosel estate Dr. Loosen began a Riesling made together from Washington grapes, and the first Eroica followed that year, named after the Third Symphony of Ludwig van Beethoven (1770 to 1827). Eroica ties the Mosel to the Columbia Valley: Riesling in the German manner, grown in the Washington desert.","others":[{"name":"Eroica Ice Wine","note":"A sweet Riesling of the same partnership, from grapes frozen on the vine and pressed while frozen."},{"name":"Cold Creek Vineyard Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Cabernet from the desert vineyard the company began planting in 1972, a planting that by the company's account doubled Washington's plantings of wine grapes."}],"traps":["Chateau Ste. Michelle's winery and château stand at Woodinville, west of the Cascades, but the grapes for Eroica grow in the desert east of the mountains.","Eroica is the partnership with Dr. Loosen of the Mosel; Col Solare is a separate Red Mountain partnership with the Antinori family of Tuscany.","Eroica Riesling is off dry, neither a dry Riesling nor a dessert wine; the Eroica Ice Wine is the sweet one."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-doowspn1","front":"Chateau Ste. Michelle: which Mosel estate makes Eroica with it, and from which vintage?","back":"Dr. Loosen of Bernkastel, from the 1999 vintage."},{"id":"fl-c42z0p4p","front":"What did the Seattle company American Wine Growers launch in 1967, and whom did it retain as consultant?","back":"Ste. Michelle Vintners, for wines named by their grape, with André Tchelistcheff of Napa's Beaulieu; it became Chateau Ste. Michelle in 1976."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ifywutmi","q":"Why does Eroica Riesling taste a little sweet yet fresh?","opts":["It is fortified with grape spirit before bottling","It is aged for a year in new oak barrels","It is made from grapes frozen on the vine","It is bottled off dry, its sugar balanced by high acidity"],"a":3,"exp":"Eroica is fermented cool in stainless steel and bottled off dry, its sugar held in check by high acidity; grapes frozen on the vine make the separate Eroica Ice Wine, which is fully sweet.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-dgwaute1","q":"In 1976 the Washington company Ste. Michelle Vintners moved into a French style château and changed its name. Where was the château built?","opts":["Walla Walla, by the Oregon line","Prosser, in the Yakima Valley","Woodinville, near Seattle","Paterson, in the Horse Heaven Hills"],"a":2,"exp":"The château was built at Woodinville, near Seattle, on a lumber baron's estate of 1912; the vineyards, such as Cold Creek, planted from 1972, lie far to the east of the Cascades.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Ste. Michelle Wine Estates: press release on the winery's history","url":"https://www.smwe.com/pressReleases/details/43","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Washington State Wine Commission: History","url":"https://www.washingtonwine.org/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Eroica: Story","url":"https://www.eroicawine.com/story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Eroica: Vineyards","url":"https://www.eroicawine.com/vineyards","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Eroica: 2024 Riesling","url":"https://www.eroicawine.com/wines/2024-riesling","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-5z2smt3t": {"id":"fl-5z2smt3t","key":"quilceda","group":"columbia","region":"columbia-valley","producer":"Quilceda Creek","producerId":"p-quilceda-creek","say":"kwil-SEE-duh","place":"Snohomish, north of Seattle, with vineyards in the Columbia Valley, Washington","founded":"1978","wine":{"name":"Quilceda Creek Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Columbia Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, all or nearly all of it, with a little Merlot in some years.","sites":"Above all Champoux Vineyard in the Horse Heaven Hills, first planted in 1972, with the family's own Mach One vineyard and, in some years, Red Mountain fruit from Klipsun and Tapteil.","making":"The Cabernet is aged in French oak barrels, all or nearly all of them new, for about twenty to twenty two months, as the house's own notes on its vintages record, and is then bottled.","glass":"Ripe and dense yet smooth, with no green edge: cassis, blackberry and black cherry over cedar, cocoa and graphite, held by fine, polished tannin and fresh acidity through a long finish.","ageing":"Dense but already approachable young; the house reckons that the wine of a strong year takes some twenty to twenty five years to reach its peak.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; give a young bottle an hour or two in a decanter, and pour an old one gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Columbia Valley","means":"Wide enough to hold Champoux and the house's other vineyards under one name."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon","means":"The grape named on the label; United States law asks that at least three quarters of the wine be that grape."}]},"table":"A family house at Snohomish, north of Seattle, Quilceda Creek draws its Cabernet Sauvignon above all from Champoux Vineyard in the Horse Heaven Hills. New French oak frames its cassis and graphite, ripe and smooth, with polished tannin.","story":"Quilceda Creek's roots run back to Napa. Its founder was a nephew, on his mother's side, of André Tchelistcheff (1901 to 1994), who shaped Beaulieu's Cabernet, and by the house's account the uncle guided the family at the start. In 1978 the nephew and his wife established the winery at Snohomish, by the house's count the twelfth bonded in Washington since Prohibition, and in 1979 he made its first vintage, a Cabernet Sauvignon, in the family garage. In 1986 the house began buying grapes from the Mercer vineyard in the Horse Heaven Hills, first planted in 1972 and later known as Champoux. In 1996 the Champoux family and a small group of wineries that had long bought its fruit, Quilceda among them, formed a company to own the vineyard, and Quilceda took its first share in 1997. In the early 2000s the founder's son oversaw the planting of the family's Galitzine vineyard on Red Mountain, its vines set close and dense. From the 2009 vintage the house's second wine, made from younger vines and from barrels set aside from the Cabernet, took the name CVR. The house has kept to Cabernet Sauvignon above all, and its wine carries Tchelistcheff's Napa east of the Cascades.","others":[{"name":"Quilceda Creek CVR Columbia Valley Red Wine","note":"The second wine, from younger vines and barrels set aside from the Cabernet, called CVR from the 2009 vintage; the house expects it to peak far sooner."},{"name":"Galitzine Vineyard Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Cabernet from the family's own vineyard on Red Mountain, planted close in the early 2000s, named for the family and first released with the 2004 vintage."}],"traps":["Snohomish, north of Seattle, is where Quilceda Creek makes its wine, not where its grapes grow: those come from east of the Cascades, above all from Champoux.","CVR is Quilceda Creek's second wine, Columbia Valley Red, not a reserve or a vineyard bottling of the Cabernet.","Champoux Vineyard was first planted in 1972 as the Mercer vineyard; Champoux is the name of the family who later ran it, not of a winery."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ja3y0pyw","front":"Quilceda Creek: which vineyard in the Horse Heaven Hills is the heart of its Cabernet?","back":"Champoux Vineyard, which began as the Mercer vineyard in 1972."},{"id":"fl-y9fy5hcw","front":"Which Napa winemaker was the uncle and early guide of the family behind Quilceda Creek?","back":"André Tchelistcheff of Beaulieu, the founder's uncle on his mother's side."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ha0ceyds","q":"Where does most of the fruit in Quilceda Creek's Cabernet Sauvignon grow?","opts":["Champoux Vineyard in the Horse Heaven Hills","Hillsides of the Walla Walla Valley","Bought fruit from across the Yakima Valley","Around the winery at Snohomish"],"a":0,"exp":"The heart of the blend is Champoux in the Horse Heaven Hills, east of the Cascades; Snohomish, where the winery stands, lies west of the mountains, where these grapes are not grown.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-tr4h8p81","q":"Quilceda Creek's founder was guided at the start by his mother's brother, a Napa winemaker. Who was he?","opts":["Joe Heitz","André Tchelistcheff","Louis M. Martini","Robert Mondavi"],"a":1,"exp":"André Tchelistcheff, who shaped Beaulieu's Cabernet, was the founder's uncle; Joe Heitz had worked under Tchelistcheff at Beaulieu but was no relation of the family.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Quilceda Creek: History","url":"https://www.quilcedacreek.com/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quilceda Creek: Champoux Vineyard","url":"https://www.quilcedacreek.com/vineyard/champoux","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quilceda Creek: 2020 Columbia Valley Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://quilcedacreek.com/wines-archive-detail/2020-quilceda-creek-columbia-valley-cabernet-sauvignon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quilceda Creek: 2006 Cabernet Sauvignon Columbia Valley","url":"https://www.quilcedacreek.com/wines-archive-detail/2006-quilceda-creek-cabernet-sauvignon-columbia-valley","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quilceda Creek: 2010 CVR Columbia Valley Red Wine","url":"https://quilcedacreek.com/wines-archive-detail/2010-quilceda-creek-cvr-red-wine","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-lp9oibxp": {"id":"fl-lp9oibxp","key":"leonetti","group":"columbia","region":"columbia-valley","producer":"Leonetti Cellar","producerId":"p-leonetti-cellar","say":"","place":"Walla Walla, Walla Walla Valley, Washington","founded":"1977","wine":{"name":"Leonetti Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Walla Walla Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with small parts of Malbec, Petit Verdot or Cabernet Franc in some years.","sites":"Vineyards across the Walla Walla Valley: Mill Creek Upland, Loess and Seven Hills in the 2000s, later also Holy Roller, the Leonetti Old Block and Serra Pedace, a steep, east facing hillside of shallow, rocky soil first planted in 2010.","making":"Aged for about twenty two months in oak. Earlier vintages mixed French barrels with American, Hungarian and other oak; later ones use French barrels and puncheons, some new and some filled once before, beside large, neutral oval casks, or botti.","glass":"Black cherry, cassis and plum wrapped in cocoa, cedar and clove; full and plush, its ripe, supple tannin lifted by fresh acidity into a long, polished finish.","ageing":"Generous and supple on release, yet the house says it makes the wine to age for twenty to thirty years in a cool cellar.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two and an old one only to leave the sediment behind; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Leonetti Cellar","means":"The house, named for the founder's grandparents, who came from Calabria and, by the family's account, farmed in Walla Walla from 1906."},{"term":"Walla Walla Valley","means":"The appellation of 1984 inside the Columbia Valley, crossing into Oregon; printed on this Cabernet from the 1999 vintage."}]},"table":"The modern Walla Walla Valley had no commercial winery before Leonetti Cellar, bonded in 1977. Its Cabernet Sauvignon, drawn from vineyards across the valley, is plush and ripe, with supple tannin under black cherry, cocoa and cedar.","story":"The house takes its name from a Calabrian family. The founder's grandparents came to Walla Walla from the village of Serra Pedace and, by the house's account, set up their farm there in 1906. In 1974 their grandson planted Cabernet Sauvignon and Riesling on the family land, on the hillside above the old homestead. He and his wife bonded Leonetti Cellar in 1977 as the first commercial winery of the modern Walla Walla Valley, and its first vintage, 1978, gave a Riesling and the house's first Cabernet Sauvignon. Woodward Canyon followed in 1981, and in December 1982 that 1978 Cabernet took first place in a national tasting held by a wine buying guide, the news that put Washington red wine before the whole country. The Walla Walla Valley became an appellation in 1984, and the Cabernet came to draw on vineyards across it, among them a steep, east facing hillside of shallow, rocky soil that the house named Serra Pedace after the grandparents' village. Leonetti is where the modern wine of the Walla Walla Valley begins, and its Cabernet is the bottle that made the valley's name.","others":[{"name":"Leonetti Merlot","note":"The house's Walla Walla Valley Merlot, made beside the Cabernet."},{"name":"Leonetti Reserve","note":"A red blend in the manner of Bordeaux, drawn each vintage from lots the house sets apart."}],"traps":["Leonetti Cellar was the first winery of the modern Walla Walla Valley, in 1977; Woodward Canyon followed in 1981, and L'Ecole No 41 in 1983.","Bonded in 1977, Leonetti Cellar made its first vintage in 1978, the year of the Cabernet that came first in a national tasting in 1982.","The Walla Walla Valley appellation crosses into Oregon; Leonetti Cellar's winery stands on the Washington side, at Walla Walla."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-w2djftei","front":"Leonetti Cellar: what is Serra Pedace?","back":"A steep, east facing hillside vineyard of the house, named after the Calabrian village of the founder's grandparents."},{"id":"fl-g69biz7w","front":"Which winery, bonded in 1977, began the modern wine story of the Walla Walla Valley?","back":"Leonetti Cellar."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-dcakinku","q":"At the table, a guest wants to know how Leonetti's Cabernet Sauvignon is aged. Which is right?","opts":["Ten years in a solera of old barrels","Six months in stainless steel, no oak","Three years in large American oak casks","About twenty two months in oak barrels"],"a":3,"exp":"The house's sheets give about twenty two months in oak, French and American barrels in earlier vintages, French barrels, puncheons and neutral botti in later ones; three years in American oak recalls old Rioja, not Leonetti.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-fqbhxj3z","q":"A couple from Calabria set up a farm in Walla Walla in 1906, the house says. What did their grandson found there in 1977?","opts":["L'Ecole No 41","Cayuse Vineyards","Leonetti Cellar","Woodward Canyon Winery"],"a":2,"exp":"Leonetti Cellar, named for the grandparents, was bonded in 1977, the first winery of the modern valley; Woodward Canyon followed in 1981 and L'Ecole No 41 in 1983.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Leonetti Cellar: Family","url":"https://leonetticellar.com/family/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Leonetti Cellar: Serra Pedace vineyard","url":"https://leonetticellar.com/vineyards/serra-pedace/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Leonetti Cellar: 2020 Cabernet Sauvignon technical sheet","url":"https://leonetticellar.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Leonetti-Cellar-2020-Cabernet-Sauvignon-Tech-Sheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Leonetti Cellar: Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://leonetticellar.com/cabernet-sauvignon-current.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"HistoryLink: Washington red wines burst onto the national scene, 1982","url":"https://www.historylink.org/file/21020","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-jgdiq56u": {"id":"fl-jgdiq56u","key":"woodward","group":"columbia","region":"columbia-valley","producer":"Woodward Canyon Winery","producerId":"p-woodward-canyon","say":"","place":"Lowden, west of Walla Walla, Walla Walla Valley, Washington","founded":"1981","wine":{"name":"Woodward Canyon Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Washington State","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Mostly Cabernet Sauvignon, with a little Petit Verdot and Malbec in some years.","sites":"Old Cabernet blocks, chiefly Champoux in the Horse Heaven Hills, planted from 1972, with its Block One, and Sagemoor north of Pasco, with Weinbau and the estate vineyard at Lowden in some vintages.","making":"As the house's notes describe it, the grapes are picked by hand and sorted, given a cold soak of about two days, then fermented in small stainless steel fermenters; the malolactic fermentation starts on its own, and the wine is aged for about twenty one months in new French oak barrels.","glass":"Blackcurrant, black plum and blueberry against graphite, cedar and dried herbs, with a savoury edge of tomato leaf; dark and firm rather than plush, with fine grained tannin and brisk acidity.","ageing":"Firm and closed when young, it needs eight to ten years in a strong vintage and then holds for twenty or more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a young bottle wants an hour or two of air in a decanter, and an old one should be poured off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Old Vines","means":"Fruit from old Cabernet blocks, chiefly at Champoux, whose vines go back to 1972, and at Sagemoor."},{"term":"Washington State","means":"The state appellation printed on later labels; the old blocks behind the wine lie outside the Walla Walla Valley."}]},"table":"Woodward Canyon's Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon is grown outside the Walla Walla Valley, on old blocks at Champoux and Sagemoor, though the winery, bonded at Lowden in 1981, was the valley's second of the modern era. Dark, savoury and firm, it rewards patience.","story":"At Lowden, west of Walla Walla, the founder planted his first vines on the family farm in the 1970s, Chardonnay first. Woodward Canyon Winery followed there in 1981, bonded by the founder and his wife as the second winery of the modern Walla Walla Valley, after Leonetti Cellar of 1977, and the couple helped win federal approval for the Walla Walla Valley appellation, granted in 1984. The Cabernet called Old Vines began with the winery in 1981 as the Dedication Series, and for its first twenty five years each label honoured a different pioneer of the Walla Walla Valley. The house built the wine on Cabernet from old blocks outside the valley: Sagemoor, north of Pasco, and Champoux in the Horse Heaven Hills, set out in 1972 under another name, which, the house says, hold some of the oldest Cabernet in Washington. Champoux's original Block One became a mainstay of the wine, and in 1996 Woodward Canyon was among the wineries that became part owners of that vineyard with the Champoux family. In time the house added a second Cabernet line, the Artist Series, and a separate Estate Old Vines from the oldest block of its own vineyard at Lowden. Old Vines remains the house's founding Cabernet, dark and savoury, a counterpoint to the plusher Walla Walla wines that came after it.","others":[{"name":"Artist Series Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Made since 1992 and blended from several Washington vineyards; the house itself calls it its flagship, though Old Vines is the older Cabernet."},{"name":"Estate Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"A separate wine, all Cabernet from the oldest block of the estate vineyard at Lowden, fermented with the estate's native yeast."}],"traps":["Woodward Canyon came second among the wineries of the modern Walla Walla Valley, in 1981, four years after Leonetti Cellar; it was never the first.","Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon draws on old blocks across the Columbia Valley, chiefly Champoux and Sagemoor; the Estate Old Vines is a different wine, from the oldest block at Lowden.","Woodward Canyon is a winery, neither a vineyard nor an appellation."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-fh3hx7b7","front":"Woodward Canyon: under what name did the Old Vines Cabernet begin in 1981?","back":"The Dedication Series, whose label honoured a different Walla Walla Valley pioneer each year for twenty five years."},{"id":"fl-7zotyru0","front":"Which two vineyards supply most of Woodward Canyon's Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon?","back":"Champoux in the Horse Heaven Hills and Sagemoor north of Pasco, both home to old Cabernet blocks."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-10xvfdjv","q":"How is Woodward Canyon's Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon aged, by the house's own sheet?","opts":["About twenty one months in new French oak","Ten months in stainless steel, then bottled","Eighteen months in American oak, half new","Two years in large old Slavonian casks"],"a":0,"exp":"The house's sheet gives about twenty one months in new French barrels after fermentation in small stainless steel fermenters; large Slavonian casks belong to Italian tradition, not to this wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ejwimtr0","q":"Woodward Canyon was bonded in 1981. Where does it stand among the wineries of the modern Walla Walla Valley?","opts":["The third, after L'Ecole No 41","The second, after Leonetti Cellar","The fourth, after Cayuse Vineyards","The first, before any other"],"a":1,"exp":"Leonetti Cellar came first in 1977 and Woodward Canyon second in 1981; L'Ecole No 41 followed in 1983, and Cayuse only in 1997.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Woodward Canyon: About our Old Vines","url":"https://woodwardcanyon.com/about-our-old-vines/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Woodward Canyon: 2021 Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon tasting notes","url":"https://woodwardcanyon.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/2021-Old-Vines-Cab-Sauv-7.24.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Woodward Canyon: 2021 Estate Old Vines Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://www.woodwardcanyon.com/product/2021-Estate-Old-Vines-Cabernet-Sauvignon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Walla Walla Valley Wine: Woodward Canyon","url":"https://www.wallawallawine.com/winery/woodward-canyon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"HistoryLink: Washington wine history interview, Woodward Canyon Winery","url":"https://www.historylink.org/file/22740","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-kb7st3dv": {"id":"fl-kb7st3dv","key":"cayuse","group":"columbia","region":"columbia-valley","producer":"Cayuse Vineyards","producerId":"p-cayuse-vineyards","say":"KY-yoos","place":"Milton-Freewater, The Rocks District, Walla Walla Valley, Oregon","founded":"1997","wine":{"name":"Bionic Frog Syrah","appellation":"Walla Walla Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Syrah alone or almost alone, from the one vineyard.","sites":"Coccinelle, a vineyard planted in 1998 on the basalt cobbles of The Rocks District, on the Oregon side of the Walla Walla Valley; coccinelle is French for ladybird.","making":"The vineyard is farmed biodynamically. The grapes ferment on their native yeasts in concrete tanks, with a large share of whole bunches, a share that has grown over the years, and the wine is then aged for some eighteen to twenty one months in French oak, in barrels and larger casks, with little or no new wood.","glass":"Blackberry and violet under smoked meat, black olive brine, iron and a bloody, savoury note; full yet silky, with fine tannin, fresh acidity and a wild, persistent close.","ageing":"It drinks openly and generously at first, then gains savour over ten years or more in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter for a young bottle; a large, wide bowled red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Bionic Frog","means":"The name of the house's Syrah from the Coccinelle vineyard, first made with the 2000 vintage."},{"term":"Walla Walla Valley","means":"The appellation on the label: it crosses the state line, and this vineyard lies on its Oregon side."}]},"table":"On the basalt cobbles of Milton-Freewater, on the Oregon side of the Walla Walla Valley, Cayuse Vineyards farms Coccinelle biodynamically, and Bionic Frog is its Syrah. Smoked meat, olive brine and iron come before the blackberry and violet.","story":"A field of stones set Cayuse in motion. In 1996 a young Frenchman from a Champagne family, in Walla Walla at the time, was driving with a friend on the Oregon side of the valley when he saw open ground littered with stones the size of softballs. As the house tells the story, he dropped his plan to settle in the Willamette Valley and resolved to buy the land and plant it. The stones are rounded basalt laid down by the Walla Walla River, and they put him in mind of Châteauneuf-du-Pape. In 1997 he planted Cailloux, his first vineyard in the stones. Its first Syrah, of 1998, was sold as Cobblestone Vineyard and renamed Cailloux with the 1999 vintage. Coccinelle followed in 1998, and its Syrah, labelled Bionic Frog, began with the 2000 vintage. In 2002 the house began to farm biodynamically, by its own account the first estate in the Walla Walla Valley to do so in full. In 2015 the cobbled fan became an appellation of its own, The Rocks District of Milton-Freewater, wholly in Oregon and inside the Walla Walla Valley. Bionic Frog puts the taste of that ground on a wine list: Syrah that is savoury before it is fruity.","others":[{"name":"Cailloux Vineyard Syrah","note":"Syrah from the house's first vineyard in the stones, planted in 1997; cailloux is French for pebbles."},{"name":"En Chamberlin Vineyard Syrah","note":"Syrah from another of the house's cobbled vineyards in the Rocks District."}],"traps":["The Rocks District of Milton-Freewater lies wholly in Oregon, inside the Walla Walla Valley appellation, which crosses the state line into Washington.","Bionic Frog is the Syrah of one vineyard, Coccinelle; Cailloux is a different vineyard and a different wine of the house.","The cobbles of the Rocks District are basalt laid down by the Walla Walla River, not the quartzite galets of Châteauneuf-du-Pape."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-1yay7g53","front":"Cayuse Vineyards: which vineyard gives Bionic Frog, and what does its name mean?","back":"Coccinelle, planted in 1998 in the Rocks District; coccinelle is French for ladybird."},{"id":"fl-4iyqis1a","front":"In which state do the cobbled vineyards of The Rocks District of Milton-Freewater lie?","back":"Oregon, inside the Walla Walla Valley appellation, which crosses into Washington; Cayuse planted there from 1997."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-oxy71rj6","q":"A guest holding Cayuse's Bionic Frog asks which vineyard the Syrah comes from. What do you say?","opts":["Champoux, in the Horse Heaven Hills","Seven Hills, in the Walla Walla Valley","Coccinelle, in the Rocks District","Cailloux, planted in 1997"],"a":2,"exp":"Bionic Frog comes from Coccinelle, planted in 1998; Cailloux is the house's first vineyard, of 1997, and gives a Syrah under its own name.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-sh5z7d4n","q":"Driving on the Oregon side of the Walla Walla Valley in 1996, a young Frenchman saw a field of stones. What did he do?","opts":["Cleared the stones to plant in the soil beneath","Bought its fruit for a house in Champagne","Left for the Willamette Valley as he had planned","Bought the land and planted vines in the stones"],"a":3,"exp":"He dropped his plan to settle in the Willamette Valley, bought the land and planted Cailloux in 1997, his first vineyard in the stones.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cayuse Vineyards: About","url":"https://cayusevineyards.com/static/about.aspx","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cayuse Vineyards: Wines","url":"https://cayusevineyards.com/static/wines-list.aspx","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinous: Cayuse Vineyards Syrah Bionic Frog, 2000 to 2014","url":"https://billing.vinous.com/articles/cayuse-vineyards-syrah-bionic-frog-2000-2014-oct-2017","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Syrah Bionic Frog, Cayuse Vineyards","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/sku/syrah-bionic-frog-cayuse-vineyards-2-2-3-2-2-2-2-2-2-2/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Spectator: Cayuse takes it a step further","url":"https://www.winespectator.com/articles/cayuse-takes-it-a-step-further-15433","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-21qypjkw": {"id":"fl-21qypjkw","key":"penfolds","group":"barossa","region":"barossa-eden","producer":"Penfolds","producerId":"p-penfolds","say":"","place":"Magill, Adelaide; Grange is drawn from across South Australia","founded":"1844","wine":{"name":"Grange","appellation":"South Australia","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Shiraz, the great majority, often with a small share of Cabernet Sauvignon.","sites":"No single site: Shiraz from several South Australian regions, the Barossa Valley above all, with McLaren Vale, the Clare Valley and, in some years, the Magill Estate vineyard in Adelaide.","making":"Fermented on its skins in tanks with header boards, wooden boards that keep the cap under the juice, the wine is racked into new American oak hogsheads to finish its fermentation and then matures in them for sixteen to twenty one months, by vintage, before bottling.","glass":"Mocha and vanilla from new American oak over blackberry, black plum and mulberry, with liquorice and dark chocolate; full in body and dense, with ripe, firm tannin, fresh acidity and a lasting finish with a graphite edge.","ageing":"Tight and oaky for its first ten years or so, it opens slowly; the house gives a strong vintage a drinking window that runs forty years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for two hours or more, an old one gently off its sediment; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Bin 95","means":"The bin number Grange carries, a house code from the cellar rather than a legal classification."},{"term":"South Australia","means":"The state appellation, wide enough to cover a blend from several of its regions."},{"term":"Shiraz","means":"The grape the label names; up to the 1989 vintage the wine was sold as Grange Hermitage."}]},"table":"Penfolds blends Grange from Shiraz grown across South Australia rather than on one site, finishes it in barrel and ages it in new American oak. Dense, with blackberry, mocha and liquorice, it needs many years; decant it and pour it into a large glass.","story":"The flagship takes its name from The Grange, the stone cottage at Magill, east of Adelaide, where Dr Christopher Rawson Penfold (1811 to 1870), an English physician, settled with his wife in 1844. After his death in 1870 his wife ran the winery, and the house grew on fortified wine. In 1948 Max Schubert (1915 to 1994) became its first chief winemaker, and a visit to Bordeaux in 1950 set him on a red that could live for twenty years and more. He made the first Grange, experimentally, in 1951 from Shiraz grown at Magill and Morphett Vale; it was never sold commercially, and the 1952 was the first vintage released. When senior management tasted the early vintages in 1957 they ordered him to stop, and he made the 1957, 1958 and 1959 in secret in the Magill cellars. Retasted with some age, the early wines changed the board's mind, and production resumed openly with the 1960 vintage. The wine carried the name Grange Hermitage, Hermitage being an old Australian name for Shiraz, up to the 1989 vintage, and it has been made in every vintage since 1951. In 2001, fifty years on, the National Trust of South Australia listed it as a heritage icon. Its counterpart is Henschke's Hill of Grace: one Shiraz blended across regions, the other grown on one hill.","others":[{"name":"Bin 389 Cabernet Shiraz","note":"Matured in barrels that held the previous vintage of Grange, hence its nickname, Baby Grange."},{"name":"St Henri Shiraz","note":"Aged in large old vats with no new oak, the deliberate opposite of Grange in the cellar."},{"name":"Bin 707 Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The Cabernet counterpart to Grange, also raised in new American oak."}],"traps":["Grange is blended from several regions and many vineyards; Hill of Grace is the single vineyard Shiraz, made by Henschke in Eden Valley.","Grange Hermitage and Grange are one wine: Hermitage, an old Australian name for Shiraz, left the label with the 1990 vintage.","St Henri sees no new oak, so it is not a lesser Grange but its opposite in the cellar."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-be9m4dtk","front":"Penfolds Grange: which three vintages did Max Schubert make in secret, and why?","back":"1957, 1958 and 1959, in the Magill cellars, after senior management had ordered him to stop making the wine."},{"id":"fl-10vu1lrh","front":"In what oak does Grange mature, and where does its fermentation end?","back":"In American oak hogsheads, every one new, for sixteen to twenty one months by vintage; the fermentation finishes in barrel."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-i6qy7lc0","q":"Why does the label of Penfolds Grange name no single vineyard?","opts":["Its vineyard is kept secret by the house","It is blended from vineyards across South Australia","Australian law forbids naming a vineyard","It comes from one walled vineyard at Magill"],"a":1,"exp":"Grange draws Shiraz from several regions, the Barossa Valley, McLaren Vale and the Clare Valley among them; the Magill Estate vineyard supplies some years beside them, never the whole wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-gv5sk10e","q":"In 1957 Penfolds' senior management ordered Max Schubert to stop making Grange. What did he do?","opts":["Made three vintages in secret in the Magill cellars","Turned the Grange grapes into fortified wine","Took the idea to a rival house in the Barossa","Waited until the board relented in 1965"],"a":0,"exp":"Schubert made the 1957, 1958 and 1959 without approval in the Magill cellars; the board relented and production resumed openly with the 1960 vintage, not in 1965.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Penfolds: The story of Grange","url":"https://www.penfolds.com/en/about-us/the-story-of-grange.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Penfolds: Bin 95 Grange Shiraz 2021 tasting note","url":"https://www.penfolds.com/on/demandware.static/-/Sites-twe-master-catalog/default/dw6e2436e5/tasting-notes/Penfolds/Penfolds%202025/Penfolds%20Bin%2095%20Grange%20Shiraz%202021%20Tasting%20Note.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Christopher Rawson Penfold","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/penfold-christopher-rawson-4387","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Penfolds Grange","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penfolds_Grange","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Robb Report: Penfolds, beyond the Grange","url":"https://robbreport.com/food-drink/spirits/penfolds-beyond-grange-224551/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-0ivzekmq": {"id":"fl-0ivzekmq","key":"henschke","group":"barossa","region":"barossa-eden","producer":"Henschke","producerId":"p-henschke","say":"HENSH-kee","place":"Keyneton, Eden Valley, Barossa, South Australia","founded":"1868","wine":{"name":"Hill of Grace","appellation":"Eden Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Shiraz alone, from vines on their own roots.","sites":"The Hill of Grace vineyard near Keyneton, facing the Gnadenberg Lutheran church at about 400 metres; its oldest Shiraz, the Grandfathers, dates from about 1860, dry grown and ungrafted on red-brown earth that grades into deep silty loam.","making":"Fermented in open fermenters, as the hand picked first vintage of 1958 was, the wine matures for eighteen to twenty months in oak hogsheads before blending and bottling; the barrels are French above all, with a little American oak in some years, and the share of new wood changes widely by vintage.","glass":"Five spice, star anise, sage and black pepper over blackberry, black plum and blueberry, with cedar from the barrel; perfumed rather than heavy, with velvety, fine-grained tannin, lively acidity and a long finish.","ageing":"Worth leaving for several years after release, it grows more savoury for decades; by the house's reckoning a well cellared bottle of a strong year lasts thirty years and more.","serve":"In a large red wine glass, at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or so, and an old one gently off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Hill of Grace","means":"The English for Gnadenberg, the name of the Lutheran church facing the vineyard, taken from a region of Silesia."},{"term":"Eden Valley","means":"The higher, cooler region of the Barossa zone, an appellation of its own, separate from the Barossa Valley."}]},"table":"Facing the Gnadenberg church at Keyneton, in Eden Valley, Henschke's Hill of Grace is Shiraz whose oldest vines date from about 1860. Plum, five spice and sage over fine tannin give it perfume more than weight: the single vineyard answer to Grange.","story":"Keyneton, in the hills east of the Barossa Valley, was called North Rhine when the family's founder, a stonemason and wheelwright from Silesia, settled there; he had reached South Australia in 1841 and farmed first in the Adelaide Hills and at Krondorf. He planted a small vineyard and made his first wine there in 1868. Close by, around 1860, a settler had planted Shiraz on land beside the Lutheran church of Gnadenberg, the Hill of Grace, and in 1891 the founder's son bought the land with its vines; he was the church's organist for many years. For decades the family made mostly fortified wine for sale in bulk. Cyril Henschke (1924 to 1979), of the fourth generation, turned the house towards dry table wine and towards wine of one vineyard: Mount Edelstone came first, and Hill of Grace followed with the 1958 vintage, hand picked and fermented in open vats. South Australia never had phylloxera, so the oldest block, the Grandfathers, grows on its own roots, and the vineyard came through the state's vine pull scheme of the 1980s, which uprooted a great deal of old Barossa Shiraz. After Cyril's death in 1979 the fifth generation took the house on. Hill of Grace and Grange are the pair a sommelier learns together, one vineyard set against a blend of many.","others":[{"name":"Mount Edelstone","note":"The house's other single vineyard Eden Valley Shiraz, from vines planted in 1912, bottled on its own before Hill of Grace."},{"name":"Cyril Henschke Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The estate Cabernet, named for the man who made the first Hill of Grace."},{"name":"Julius Eden Valley Riesling","note":"Dry Riesling of lime and slate from the high country of Eden Valley."}],"traps":["Hill of Grace is a vineyard name and carries no legal classification; it is not a Grand Cru.","Hill of Grace comes from Eden Valley, an appellation of its own, higher and cooler than the Barossa Valley floor.","Mount Edelstone, not Hill of Grace, was Henschke's first single vineyard Shiraz; Hill of Grace followed with the 1958 vintage."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-rxky7a6m","front":"Hill of Grace: what is the Gnadenberg, and why does the wine carry its name?","back":"The Lutheran church that faces the vineyard; Gnadenberg means Hill of Grace, a name taken from a region of Silesia."},{"id":"fl-739ufoe2","front":"Which Henschke made the first Hill of Grace, and with which vintage?","back":"Cyril Henschke (1924 to 1979), of the fourth generation, with the 1958 vintage."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-8z9r3mb1","q":"Why do the oldest vines of Henschke's Hill of Grace grow on their own roots?","opts":["The family regrafted them after the vine pull","Eden Valley rules forbid grafted vines","South Australia has never had phylloxera","The vineyard lies too high for any vine pest"],"a":2,"exp":"South Australia has kept phylloxera out, so vines planted around 1860 never needed grafting; height is no defence against the pest, and no rule of Eden Valley bans grafted vines.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-0ch5nty5","q":"What became of the vineyard of about 1860, beside a Lutheran church, that a son of Henschke's founder bought in 1891?","opts":["It was pulled out in the 1980s vine pull","It became the site of the Keyneton winery","It became the Mount Edelstone vineyard","It became the Hill of Grace vineyard"],"a":3,"exp":"The vineyard by the Gnadenberg church is Hill of Grace, first bottled on its own with the 1958 vintage; Mount Edelstone is a separate vineyard, planted in 1912.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Henschke: Family history","url":"https://www.henschke.com.au/pages/henschke-family-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Henschke: Hill of Grace Vineyard","url":"https://www.henschke.com.au/pages/hill-of-grace-vineyard","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Winebow: Henschke Hill of Grace","url":"https://www.winebow.com/our-brands/henschke/henschke-hill-grace/2021","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Cyril Alfred Henschke","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/henschke-cyril-alfred-10486","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Henschke","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henschke","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-5l5rsh6d": {"id":"fl-5l5rsh6d","key":"rockford","group":"barossa","region":"barossa-eden","producer":"Rockford","producerId":"p-rockford","say":"","place":"Krondorf Road, Tanunda, Barossa Valley, South Australia","founded":"1984","wine":{"name":"Basket Press Shiraz","appellation":"Barossa Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Shiraz alone, from old vines.","sites":"Small parcels of old, dry grown Shiraz, most of them bought from grower families across the Barossa Valley, some of the vines more than a century old.","making":"Made on restored old equipment, much of it from the nineteenth century and salvaged as other cellars modernised: the fruit is fermented in open fermenters and pressed slowly and gently in a basket press about a century old, then matured in seasoned French and American oak, hogsheads above all with some larger vats, before bottling.","glass":"Blackberry, black plum and mulberry with dark chocolate, black pepper and a tarry, earthy note of forest floor; full in body, with velvety tannin from the gentle press and a long finish.","ageing":"Its first years are tight and tannic; it then softens and gains depth over a decade or more in the cellar.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, in a large red wine glass; a young bottle wants an hour in a decanter, an older one careful decanting off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Basket Press","means":"Pressed in an old basket press, the method the wine is named for, not a vineyard."},{"term":"Barossa Valley","means":"The appellation of the warm valley floor, as distinct from the higher Eden Valley."}]},"table":"Old vine Shiraz bought from Barossa Valley grower families goes into Rockford's Basket Press Shiraz, fermented in open vats and pressed in a century old basket press. With blackberry, chocolate and black pepper on a full, velvety body, it shows the Barossa as it was made before modern machinery.","story":"Rockford grew from a property on Krondorf Road near Tanunda, bought by its founder in 1971, with a stone cottage and outbuildings of the 1850s from the early settlement of Krondorf. The winery was put together from restored old equipment, open fermenters and basket presses among it, much of it of the nineteenth century and salvaged as other cellars modernised, and in 1984 the founder made the first Basket Press Shiraz, Rockford's first vintage. The timing went against the market. In the mid 1980s, in a glut of grapes, the South Australian government paid growers to pull out their vines, and the Barossa lost much of its old Shiraz and Grenache. Rockford bought instead from the growers who had kept their old, dry grown vines, and built the house on its ties with those families more than on vines of its own. The wine became the traditionalist's Barossa Shiraz: slow basket pressing, open vats and a wine of texture rather than force. Its sparkling red, Black Shiraz, made by the traditional method, gave the house a second following. When a guest asks what Barossa Shiraz tasted like before new oak and heavy extraction became its fashion, Basket Press is the bottle to open.","others":[{"name":"Black Shiraz","note":"A sparkling red Shiraz made by the traditional method, dry to off dry, the house's other famous bottle."},{"name":"Rod and Spur","note":"A blend of Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz, the classic Australian pairing."}],"traps":["Rockford is not an estate: Basket Press comes mostly from growers' old vineyards, not from vines around the winery.","Black Shiraz is Rockford's sparkling red; Basket Press is a still Shiraz, and the two are different wines.","A candidate may take Basket Press for a vineyard or a reserve tier; the name is the press."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-m2a4kzjc","front":"Where does the fruit of Rockford's Basket Press Shiraz come from?","back":"Small parcels of old, dry grown Shiraz, most of them bought from Barossa grower families rather than grown on estate vines."},{"id":"fl-08m64010","front":"In which year did Rockford make its first Basket Press Shiraz, and on what equipment?","back":"1984, on restored old equipment, open fermenters and basket presses among it, much of it salvaged from other cellars as they modernised."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-mtozyb0c","q":"What does the name Basket Press tell you about Rockford's Shiraz?","opts":["It is pressed slowly in an old basket press","It is a reserve level above the house Shiraz","It comes from a vineyard called Basket Press","It is fermented in baskets woven of vine canes"],"a":0,"exp":"The name is the method: the wine is pressed gently and slowly in a basket press about a century old; no vineyard and no reserve level is meant.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-au8h8enm","q":"In the mid 1980s, as Rockford began, what did the South Australian government pay Barossa growers to do?","opts":["Replant with Cabernet Sauvignon","Pull out their vines","Graft their vines onto rootstock","Sell their fruit to cooperatives"],"a":1,"exp":"The vine pull scheme paid growers to remove vines in a glut, and much old Shiraz and Grenache was lost; grafting was never needed, since the state has no phylloxera.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Rockford Wines: Visit us","url":"https://www.rockfordwines.com.au/visit-us","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Barossa Australia: Rockford Wines","url":"https://www.barossa.com/listing/rockford-wines","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Langton's: Rockford Basket Press Shiraz","url":"https://www.langtons.com.au/rockford-basket-press-shiraz.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"J.J. Buckley: Realizing what Rockford is all about","url":"https://www.jjbuckley.com/wine-knowledge/blog/realizing-what-rockford-is-all-about/184","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Adelaide AZ: the Barossa Valley after the 1980s vine pull","url":"https://adelaideaz.com/articles/barossa-valley-rescued-by-small-entrepreneurs-after-tragic-loss-of-old-shiraz-and-grenache-vines-in-1980s-pullout","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-7dzypopd": {"id":"fl-7dzypopd","key":"seppeltsfield","group":"barossa","region":"barossa-eden","producer":"Seppeltsfield","producerId":"p-seppeltsfield","say":"SEP-elts-feeld","place":"Seppeltsfield, Barossa Valley, South Australia","founded":"1851","wine":{"name":"100 Year Old Para Vintage Tawny","appellation":"Barossa Valley","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Shiraz and Grenache, alone or together by vintage, from the estate's own Barossa vineyards, as the house describes its recent releases.","sites":"Barossa fruit; the wine is defined less by a vineyard than by the Centennial Cellar at Seppeltsfield, finished in 1878, where its cask rests for a century.","making":"Fermented and fortified with grape spirit while still sweet, as all the Para Tawnies are; a cask of each vintage is then laid down in the Centennial Cellar and left in wood for a hundred years, slowly concentrating, before it is bottled for release.","glass":"Treacle, walnut and burnt toffee with preserved fruit, exotic spice and the nutty tang of rancio; thick and intensely sweet, the sweetness held in check by concentration and a sharp volatile lift.","ageing":"Released ready to drink at a hundred years old; it needs no further cellaring.","serve":"12 to 14 degrees Celsius, cool, as for an old tawny; no need to decant; a small white wine glass and a small measure, after dinner.","label":[{"term":"100 Year Old","means":"The wine spent a century in its cask in the Centennial Cellar before release."},{"term":"Vintage","means":"All of it comes from the one harvest named on the label, never a blend of years."},{"term":"Tawny","means":"A sweet fortified wine aged in wood; Australian wine no longer carries the name Port."}]},"table":"One Barossa harvest and a hundred years in cask: Seppeltsfield's 100 Year Old Para Vintage Tawny is a fortified wine released only when its century in the Centennial Cellar is done. Treacle, walnut and toffee, thick and sweet, it is poured in small measures after dinner.","story":"Born in Lower Silesia, Joseph Ernst Seppelt (1813 to 1868) sailed for South Australia with his family in 1849, and in 1851, by the house's account, he founded the estate in the Barossa Valley that took his name. He died in 1868, and his eldest son, in his early twenties, took the business on and grew it. In 1878 the son completed the Centennial Cellar. That year he chose a puncheon, a large cask, of the estate's fortified wine and gave orders that it was not to be bottled for a hundred years. A cask from every vintage since has been laid down beside it, an unbroken line from 1878 to the present harvest. In 1978 the 1878 was released, a century old, and each year since the next vintage in the line has followed at a hundred years of age. When Australia agreed with the European Union to stop using the name Port, the house's Para wines went on as Tawny. It is history in a small glass: a wine laid down a century before it is poured, from a cellar where every year since 1878 waits its turn.","others":[{"name":"Para Grand Tawny","note":"A younger, blended Para Tawny, the first step on the same ladder."},{"name":"Para 21 Year Old Vintage Tawny","note":"A single vintage released at twenty one years old, a younger rung of the same idea."}],"traps":["Australian fortified wine no longer carries the name Port, a name of the Douro in Portugal; Tawny is the word on the label.","A 100 Year Old Para comes from one vintage, aged a century in its cask; it is not a blend whose wines average a hundred years.","The 1878, released in 1978, began the series; the Centennial Cellar holds a cask of every vintage since."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-3v8pjpg4","front":"Seppeltsfield's 100 Year Old Para: what happened in 1878, and in 1978?","back":"In 1878 a puncheon of that vintage was laid down in the new Centennial Cellar, not to be bottled for a century; in 1978 it was released."},{"id":"fl-1u0uwpkx","front":"What does Vintage mean on a bottle of 100 Year Old Para Vintage Tawny?","back":"All of it comes from one named harvest, aged a century in its own cask, never a blend of years."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-j3thpc6i","q":"A guest holding Seppeltsfield's 100 Year Old Para asks what the year on the label means. What do you tell them?","opts":["The year the Centennial Cellar was built","The year the bottle left the cellar","The average age of a blend of years","The harvest every drop comes from"],"a":3,"exp":"The wine of one harvest spends its century in cask and is released in its hundredth year, so the label's year is the harvest; the release comes a hundred years later.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qcmd8cqr","q":"What did Seppeltsfield lay down in its new Centennial Cellar in 1878?","opts":["The family's archive of labels and ledgers","The first Shiraz cuttings brought from Silesia","A puncheon not to be bottled for a century","A solera begun from the founder's first harvest"],"a":2,"exp":"The founder's son set aside a puncheon of the 1878 vintage with orders that it stay unbottled for a hundred years; it was released in 1978, and a cask of every vintage since has followed.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Seppeltsfield: Historical timeline","url":"https://seppeltsfield.com.au/seppeltsfield-historical-timeline/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Qantas Wine: Seppeltsfield 100 Year Old 1923 Para","url":"https://wine.qantas.com/p/seppeltsfield-seppeltsfield-100-year-old-1923-para-liqueur-100ml/SEP23100YOPAR","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Joseph Ernest Seppelt","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/seppelt-joseph-ernest-4558","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Seppeltsfield (wine)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seppeltsfield_(wine)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Australia banned from using Champagne, Sherry and Port on labels","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/australia-banned-from-using-champagne-sherry-and-port-on-labels-49840","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-7ms3h7nc": {"id":"fl-7ms3h7nc","key":"yalumba","group":"barossa","region":"barossa-eden","producer":"Yalumba","producerId":"p-yalumba","say":"yah-LUM-bah","place":"Angaston, Barossa, South Australia","founded":"1849","wine":{"name":"The Signature Cabernet Sauvignon Shiraz","appellation":"Barossa","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz, the share of each changing with the vintage.","sites":"Barossa vineyards, with Shiraz from vines planted as early as 1945; the first vintage, 1962, joined Eden Valley Shiraz to Cabernet Sauvignon from the house's Oxford Landing vineyard, by its own record.","making":"Matured for eighteen to twenty two months, by vintage, in hogsheads and barriques of French and Hungarian oak, with American oak in some earlier vintages and a fifth or so of the barrels new in recent ones; the first vintage, by the house's record, was raised in French puncheons. The house runs its own cooperage.","glass":"Smoke and a savoury note of grilled meat over blackcurrant, blackberry and plum; the Cabernet gives a frame of firm, fine tannin and the Shiraz flesh in the middle, and the finish is long and dry.","ageing":"Made to cellar, it stays closed and firm for its first years and opens slowly over a decade and more in a strong vintage.","serve":"A Bordeaux glass at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; give a young bottle an hour in a decanter, and decant an old one gently off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"The Signature","means":"Each vintage is dedicated to one person who has given much to Yalumba, its Signatory."},{"term":"Barossa","means":"The zone appellation, which covers both the Barossa Valley floor and the higher Eden Valley."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon Shiraz","means":"The two grapes of the blend: Cabernet, the grape of the Médoc, with the Barossa's Shiraz."}]},"table":"Each vintage of Yalumba's The Signature is dedicated to one person who has served the house, and the wine, made since 1962, is a Barossa blend of Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz. Firm Cabernet tannin frames its blackcurrant and plum, a red to lay down.","story":"Two years after bringing his family to South Australia in 1847, Samuel Smith (1812 to 1889), a brewer from Wareham in Dorset, was working as a gardener at Angaston. That year he bought land of his own and named it Yalumba, by most accounts an Aboriginal word for all the land around; the story goes that he planted his first vines by moonlight, after his day's work on his employer's estate. The house traces itself to that planting and has stayed with his descendants, which makes it, by its own account, Australia's oldest family owned winery, and it keeps its own cooperage. The Signature began with the 1962 vintage, by the house's record a blend of Eden Valley Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon matured in French puncheons, and was first released in 1966. Each vintage is dedicated to one person who has given much to Yalumba, members of its staff among them, and more than fifty have been honoured so far. The blend has moved with the vineyards: the modern wine is drawn from the Barossa and raised in French and Hungarian oak. On a list it stands for the Cabernet Shiraz blend, a pairing Australia made its own, told through one family house and the people who served it.","others":[{"name":"The Octavius","note":"Old vine Barossa Shiraz aged in octaves, small American oak casks made in the house's own cooperage."},{"name":"The Virgilius","note":"Eden Valley Viognier, from a grape the house has long championed."},{"name":"Pewsey Vale The Contours Riesling","note":"Dry Eden Valley Riesling of lime, released with bottle age."}],"traps":["The Signature blends Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz; it is not a single varietal Shiraz like The Octavius.","The Signature's first vintage was 1962, but that wine was not released until 1966.","An octave is a small cask, not a vineyard or a blend term."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-9129p1q4","front":"What does the name of Yalumba's The Signature refer to?","back":"Each vintage is dedicated to one person who has given much to the house, its Signatory; more than fifty have been honoured since 1962."},{"id":"fl-d1n6iwgc","front":"What went into the first vintage of Yalumba's The Signature, the 1962?","back":"Eden Valley Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon from the Oxford Landing vineyard, matured in French puncheons; it was released in 1966."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-g9n3ffax","q":"What sets Yalumba's The Signature apart from a Barossa Shiraz?","opts":["It is aged only in large old vats","It blends Cabernet Sauvignon with Shiraz","It is Shiraz from a single Eden Valley block","It is a fortified red of the Barossa"],"a":1,"exp":"The Signature is a blend of Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz, the share of each changing with the vintage; it is raised in hogsheads and barriques, not old vats, and is a dry table wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-67jgtynp","q":"Samuel Smith founded Yalumba at Angaston in 1849. What had been his trade in England?","opts":["Brewer","Physician","Stonemason","Cooper"],"a":0,"exp":"Smith was a brewer from Wareham in Dorset; the Penfolds founder was the physician, and Henschke's founder the stonemason and wheelwright.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Yalumba: Back vintage, The Signature 1962","url":"https://www.yalumba.com/yalumba-asset-backvintages/back-vintage-the-signature-1962.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Yalumba: The Signature Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz 2023","url":"https://www.yalumba.com/the-signature-cabernet-sauvignon-and-shiraz-2023/YALSIG236.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Samuel Smith","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/smith-samuel-4615","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Samuel Smith (winemaker)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Smith_(winemaker)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Producer profile, Yalumba","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine/producer-profiles/producer-profile-yalumba-374161/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-olr6xuh0": {"id":"fl-olr6xuh0","key":"vasse-felix","group":"margaret-river","region":"margaret-river","producer":"Vasse Felix","producerId":"p-vasse-felix","say":"","place":"Wilyabrup, Margaret River, Western Australia","founded":"1967","wine":{"name":"Tom Cullity Cabernet Sauvignon Malbec","appellation":"Margaret River","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with Malbec for most of the rest; a little Petit Verdot joined some of the early vintages.","sites":"The Home Vineyard at Wilyabrup: the founder's blocks of 1967, old Houghton clone Cabernet on its original roots, with later plantings around them, dry grown in old gravel loam beside the Wilyabrup Brook.","making":"Whole berries, picked by hand, begin a wild fermentation and are pumped over several times a day. The wine is then aged for about eighteen months in French oak barriques, the larger part of them new, before bottling.","glass":"Blackberry, cassis and ripe plum over liquorice, dark chocolate and a smoky, savoury note of nori; full yet poised, with fine, firm tannin and fresh acidity.","ageing":"Closed and firm for its first five years or so, it opens over the following decade and is made to keep for twenty years and more when the year is strong.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Tom Cullity","means":"The founder's name, given to the house's leading red from the 2013 vintage."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon Malbec","means":"Australian law lists the grapes of a blend in descending order, so Cabernet is the larger share."},{"term":"Margaret River","means":"The Geographical Indication: at least 85 per cent of the fruit must come from it."}]},"table":"Tom Cullity, the leading red of Vasse Felix, carries the name of the doctor who planted the region's first modern commercial vineyard there in 1967. Cabernet Sauvignon with Malbec from his Home Vineyard, it is dark and firm, with a long finish; give it air.","story":"Climate research of the mid 1960s likened the cape to Bordeaux, and Dr Tom Cullity (1925 to 2008), a Perth heart specialist, took it up and went looking for land. In 1967 he planted a small vineyard at Wilyabrup, the first modern commercial vineyard of Margaret River, with Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec among the first vines, and called it Vasse Felix. In 1972 he made a Cabernet Sauvignon Malbec and a Riesling, which the house counts as the region's first serious wines. Cullity sold the estate in the mid 1980s, and in 1987 a Perth family bought it. For years the house's leading red was the Heytesbury, a Cabernet led blend. To mark fifty years since the first planting, the house released in 2017 the 2013 vintage of a new flagship, named for the founder and grown in the Home Vineyard around his old vines, with Malbec in the blend as it had been in 1972. Tom Cullity took the Heytesbury red's place at the head of the range. One bottle holds the region's beginning: the first modern vineyard, the first grapes and the man who planted them, poured as a wine for the cellar.","others":[{"name":"Heytesbury","note":"The Cabernet led blend that headed the house's reds until Tom Cullity took its place; the name then went to the Chardonnay alone."},{"name":"Heytesbury Chardonnay","note":"Taut, barrel worked Margaret River Chardonnay of grapefruit and struck flint."}],"traps":["Tom Cullity is Cabernet Sauvignon with Malbec, not a straight Cabernet; Malbec has been in the house's red since its first of 1972.","The vines date from 1967 but the wine does not: the first Tom Cullity is the 2013 vintage, released in 2017.","Vasse Felix is in Margaret River in Western Australia, a long way from every South Australian region."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xo9a0x98","front":"Vasse Felix: which red bears the founder's name, and what is its first vintage?","back":"Tom Cullity, a Cabernet Sauvignon with Malbec; the 2013, released in 2017 for the estate's fiftieth year."},{"id":"fl-al7litpc","front":"Which estate planted the first modern commercial vineyard of Margaret River, at Wilyabrup in 1967?","back":"Vasse Felix, planted by Dr Tom Cullity, a Perth heart specialist."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-7cua0i0r","q":"Which grape does the Tom Cullity of Vasse Felix blend with its Cabernet Sauvignon?","opts":["Cabernet Franc","Shiraz","Malbec","Merlot"],"a":2,"exp":"Malbec has partnered Cabernet in the house's red since 1972 and gives the second name on the label; Merlot, the usual partner in Bordeaux, plays no part.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-glz1u1p0","q":"In which year did Dr Tom Cullity plant the vineyard of Vasse Felix at Wilyabrup?","opts":["1972","1977","1971","1967"],"a":3,"exp":"Cullity planted in 1967, the region's first modern commercial vineyard; 1971 is when the Cullens planted their vineyard nearby, and 1972 the year of his first wines.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Vasse Felix: Our story","url":"https://www.vassefelix.com.au/our-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vasse Felix: 2022 Tom Cullity","url":"https://www.vassefelix.com.au/store/2022-tom-cullity","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Margaret River Wine Association: History","url":"https://margaretriver.wine/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: Vasse Felix replaces Heytesbury with new icon","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2016/04/vasse-felix-replaces-heytesbury-with-new-icon/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Australia: The Blending Rules","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/news/articles/the-blending-rules","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-bpayqyg9": {"id":"fl-bpayqyg9","key":"moss-wood","group":"margaret-river","region":"margaret-river","producer":"Moss Wood","producerId":"p-moss-wood","say":"","place":"Wilyabrup, Margaret River, Western Australia","founded":"1969","wine":{"name":"Moss Wood Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Margaret River","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with small amounts of Cabernet Franc and Petit Verdot.","sites":"The Moss Wood vineyard at Wilyabrup, first planted in 1969: old, dry grown, low yielding vines.","making":"Fermented in small open fermenters, the cap of skins plunged by hand several times a day. Each batch then rests apart in French oak barriques, a quarter or fewer of them new, for well over a year and a half before the blend is made, and the blend often goes back to barrel, so the wine has more than two years in oak in all.","glass":"Blackcurrant, plum and cherry over cedar, tobacco and bay leaf; medium to full in body, with bright acidity and fine, supple tannin, generous without losing its frame.","ageing":"Approachable young but built to keep: a strong vintage holds its freshness for decades, as the 1975 has shown.","serve":"Pour at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius into a Bordeaux glass, decanting a young bottle for an hour or so and an old one gently off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Moss Wood","means":"The name of the vineyard and of the estate, planted at Wilyabrup in 1969."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon","means":"A grape named alone on an Australian label must be at least 85 per cent of the wine."},{"term":"Margaret River","means":"A Geographical Indication on the cape in the south west of Western Australia."}]},"table":"Planted at Wilyabrup in 1969, Moss Wood was the second commercial vineyard of Margaret River, and its Cabernet Sauvignon is the wine of that vineyard. It spends two years and more in French oak and keeps for decades, its blackcurrant, plum and bay leaf set on fine tannin.","story":"Vasse Felix was two years old when, in 1969, a doctor and his wife bought land at Wilyabrup and planted Cabernet Sauvignon there, the young region's second commercial vineyard. The first wine came with the 1973 vintage, and the 1975 Cabernet became the house's measure: the winemaker later told Decanter it was the first international recognition of the cape's Cabernet. In 1979 the founders took on a winemaker and viticulturist, and in 1984 he and his wife leased the estate; by July 1985, as the house tells it, the sale was complete. The new owners later added the neighbouring Ribbon Vale vineyard and kept its wines under their own label, so Moss Wood Cabernet stays the wine of the vineyard of 1969. The house is patient with it: the Cabernet's batches are blended late and the wine has more than two years in barrel; tasted at twenty seven years old, the 1975 showed fine acidity and freshness. Margaret River Cabernet, it shows, can be generous and yet keep its shape for decades.","others":[{"name":"Moss Wood Ribbon Vale","note":"Wines of a neighbouring Wilyabrup vineyard the house added later, kept under their own label."},{"name":"Moss Wood Sémillon","note":"Margaret River Sémillon, riper and fuller than the Hunter's."}],"traps":["Moss Wood was planted in 1969, two years after Vasse Felix: it is the region's second commercial vineyard, not its first.","Margaret River Sémillon such as Moss Wood's is riper and often fuller than the low alcohol Hunter Valley style.","Ribbon Vale wines come from a separate vineyard and are labelled apart; they are not the Moss Wood Cabernet."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-dgmzt8rb","front":"Moss Wood: which vintage of its Cabernet Sauvignon first drew notice abroad?","back":"The 1975, which the winemaker called the first international recognition of the cape's Cabernet."},{"id":"fl-t5plpo4v","front":"Which Wilyabrup vineyard of 1969 was the second commercial vineyard of Margaret River?","back":"Moss Wood, planted by a doctor and his wife; its Cabernet Sauvignon is the flagship."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-qsmjc7fs","q":"How long does Moss Wood usually age its Cabernet Sauvignon in barrel before bottling?","opts":["Over two years in French barriques","Six months in stainless steel","One year in American casks","Five years in old vats"],"a":0,"exp":"The house gives the wine more than two years in French barriques, only a small share of them new, rather than American casks, old vats or a short spell in steel.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-wey0qdox","q":"The house singles out one vintage of Moss Wood Cabernet Sauvignon as the one that made its name. Which is it?","opts":["1985","1975","1973","1969"],"a":1,"exp":"The 1975 is the vintage the house and Decanter single out; 1973 was the first vintage, 1969 the year of planting and 1985 the year of the sale.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Moss Wood: History, 1969","url":"https://www.mosswood.com.au/about/1969-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Moss Wood: Moss Wood Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://www.mosswood.com.au/wines/moss-wood/cabernet-sauvignon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Moss Wood: Moss Wood 2021 Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://www.mosswood.com.au/wines/moss-wood/cabernet-sauvignon/moss-wood-2021-cabernet-sauvignon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Moss Wood Cabernet Sauvignon 1975","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-moss-wood-cabernet-sauvignon-1975-396359/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Australia: The Blending Rules","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/news/articles/the-blending-rules","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-arwd3z3m": {"id":"fl-arwd3z3m","key":"cullen","group":"margaret-river","region":"margaret-river","producer":"Cullen Wines","producerId":"p-cullen-wines","say":"","place":"Wilyabrup, Margaret River, Western Australia","founded":"1971","wine":{"name":"Diana Madeline","appellation":"Margaret River","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with small amounts of Merlot, Cabernet Franc and Malbec.","sites":"The biodynamic Cullen Vineyard at Wilyabrup: Cabernet Sauvignon planted in 1971, Merlot and Cabernet Franc in 1976 and Malbec in 1978, on old granite and gravelly sandy loam over lateritic subsoil.","making":"Picked by hand on fruit days of the biodynamic calendar and fermented with no yeast, acid or malolactic culture added, some lots staying for weeks on their skins. The wine is then aged for some thirteen to fifteen months in barriques and puncheons, about half of them new.","glass":"Cassis, mulberry and plum over cedar, graphite and violet, with a note of iodine and ironstone; fine grained, chalky tannin and a long, cool finish.","ageing":"Supple enough to enjoy young, it holds for two decades and more, and the house claims as long as half a century for a strong year.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius in a Bordeaux glass; an hour or two in a decanter for a young bottle, careful decanting off the sediment for an old one.","label":[{"term":"Diana Madeline","means":"Named for the founder Diana Cullen; the wine was once labelled simply Cabernet Sauvignon Merlot."},{"term":"Cullen","means":"The family name of the founders, Kevin and Diana Cullen, who planted the vineyard in 1971."},{"term":"Margaret River","means":"Wilyabrup, where the vineyard lies, is a district of this Geographical Indication, not a registered subregion."}]},"table":"Biodynamic and picked by the moon, Diana Madeline is the Cabernet blend of Cullen Wines, named for the founder who planted the Wilyabrup vineyard with her husband in 1971. Mostly Cabernet Sauvignon, it is fine, cool and long.","story":"Before any vines, Dr Kevin Cullen (1922 to 1994), the Busselton doctor who drove the Busselton Health Study, and his wife Diana Cullen (1923 to 2003) farmed land at Wilyabrup. In 1965 they thought of sowing lupins, and the agronomist they asked suggested vines instead. In 1966 they planted a small trial plot; as the family tells it, the young vines were sprayed with weedkiller by mistake while the couple were away, and pulled out. In 1971 they planted again, on their own land, and the Cullen Vineyard grew from there, with Merlot and Cabernet Franc in 1976 and Malbec in 1978. Diana Cullen led the winemaking from 1981, their daughter took it on in 1989, and the vineyard was certified biodynamic in 2004. The Cabernet blend, long labelled Cabernet Sauvignon Merlot, took the name Diana Madeline at the start of the 2000s in honour of its founder, and the Chardonnay became Kevin John, for her husband. This is the cape's Cabernet as it becomes when the farming leads and nothing is added in the cellar.","others":[{"name":"Kevin John","note":"The Chardonnay named for the founder, wild fermented Wilyabrup Chardonnay, taut and long."},{"name":"Mangan Vineyard","note":"From the family's second vineyard, planted in 1995 and 1997."}],"traps":["Diana Madeline is Cabernet led: Merlot is only a small part, whatever the older Cabernet Sauvignon Merlot labels suggest.","Kevin John is the Chardonnay and Diana Madeline the red: each is named for one of the two founders.","The trial vines of 1966 were lost; the Cullen Vineyard dates from the planting of 1971."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-wjo4hynd","front":"Cullen Wines: for whom are Diana Madeline and Kevin John named?","back":"The founders, Diana and Kevin Cullen, who planted their Wilyabrup vineyard in 1971."},{"id":"fl-zg1cb238","front":"Which Margaret River Cabernet blend is picked by hand on fruit days of the biodynamic calendar?","back":"Cullen's Diana Madeline, from a vineyard certified biodynamic since 2004."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-dy7rxa2p","q":"Diana Madeline was long labelled with two grapes. Which was named beside Cabernet Sauvignon, though it is only a small part of the blend?","opts":["Malbec","Petit Verdot","Merlot","Shiraz"],"a":2,"exp":"The wine was long labelled Cabernet Sauvignon Merlot, though Merlot is a small part beside the Cabernet; Malbec is in the blend too but was never on the label, and Shiraz plays no part.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-a66ur4e2","q":"What became of the trial vines the Cullens planted at Wilyabrup in 1966?","opts":["Gave the region's first wine","Sold to a neighbouring estate","Grafted over to Chardonnay","Sprayed with weedkiller and pulled out"],"a":3,"exp":"As the family tells it, the young vines were sprayed with weedkiller by mistake while the couple were away; Vasse Felix, not the Cullens, made the region's first wines, and they planted again in 1971.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cullen Wines: The Cullen Family Legacy","url":"https://cullenwines.com.au/pages/the-cullen-family-legacy","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cullen Wines: 2022 Diana Madeline","url":"https://cullenwines.com.au/products/2022-diana-madeline","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cullen Wines: Viticulture","url":"https://cullenwines.com.au/pages/viticulture","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Cullen, Kevin John","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/cullen-kevin-john-27505","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Royal Agricultural Society of Western Australia: Cullen, Diana Madeleine","url":"https://www.raswa.org.au/?p=607","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-6ki8psbs": {"id":"fl-6ki8psbs","key":"leeuwin","group":"margaret-river","region":"margaret-river","producer":"Leeuwin Estate","producerId":"p-leeuwin-estate","say":"","place":"Margaret River, Western Australia","founded":"1974","wine":{"name":"Art Series Chardonnay","appellation":"Margaret River","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Chardonnay alone, of the Gingin clone, whose small berries carry slightly more sugar and acidity.","sites":"Estate blocks with northern aspects and rows running east to west; Block 20, by the front gate and planted in 1976, is the backbone, with fruit of Block 22 beside it.","making":"Hand picked fruit is destemmed cool, given some skin contact, settled and fermented with cultured yeast in new French oak barriques, then aged on its lees, stirred often, for about eleven months. It is bottled after a light fining and released about three years after the vintage.","glass":"Grapefruit, lime curd and white nectarine over poached pear, then toasted hazelnut, cashew and a flinty edge; rich and layered, with taut acidity that carries a long finish.","ageing":"Tight on release despite its years at the estate; it gains nut and honey over a decade and, in a strong year, keeps well beyond it.","serve":"10 to 13 degrees Celsius, not too cold; decant a young bottle briefly if it seems closed; a wide white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Art Series","means":"The estate's leading range, each label carrying a work by a contemporary Australian artist."},{"term":"Margaret River","means":"The region on the label, which must give at least 85 per cent of the fruit; here all of it is estate grown."}]},"table":"In the early 1970s a Napa vintner judged the Leeuwin Estate property right for fine wine, and its white flagship is the Art Series Chardonnay. Fermented and aged in new French oak and held about three years before release, it pairs grapefruit and cashew with taut acidity.","story":"In 1972, the year the house gives, or 1973 by another account, the Napa Valley vintner Robert Mondavi (1913 to 2008) visited the property and judged it right for fine wine; he went on to mentor its owners. The estate dates itself to 1973: a nursery went in in 1974, and from 1975 the vineyard blocks were planted by hand. Block 20, by the front gate, went in in 1976. The first commercial vintage was 1979, and in 1980 came the first Art Series Chardonnay, the estate's first wine with a painting on its label, by a Western Australian artist: the start of a long line of labels carrying Australian paintings. Decanter in London singled out one of its first vintages, the 1980 or the 1981 by different accounts. The wine is fermented in new French oak, bottled some eighteen months after the vintage and released about three years after it, so a bottle on a list is older than most whites beside it. For a sommelier it is the Margaret River white a guest is likely to know by name.","others":[{"name":"Art Series Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The red of the same range, framed like Bordeaux and often overshadowed by the white."},{"name":"Art Series Riesling","note":"Dry Margaret River Riesling, a lighter regional counterpoint."}],"traps":["Art Series is the name of a range with its own painted labels, not a single vineyard; the Chardonnay rests on Block 20.","The Art Series Chardonnay is released about three years after its vintage, so a current release is not a young wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-rwr8j6n1","front":"Which block forms the backbone of Leeuwin Estate's Art Series Chardonnay?","back":"Block 20, by the front gate, planted in 1976, with fruit of Block 22 beside it."},{"id":"fl-zfgswr8x","front":"Which Napa vintner judged the Margaret River site of Leeuwin Estate right for fine wine in the early 1970s?","back":"Robert Mondavi, who went on to mentor its owners."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6ng0oo2y","q":"In what is the Art Series Chardonnay of Leeuwin Estate fermented?","opts":["Large old oak casks","New French oak barriques","Stainless steel tanks","Concrete eggs"],"a":1,"exp":"The wine is fermented and aged about eleven months in new French barriques with its lees stirred; old casks would give little of its toasted, nutty frame.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-xfgtu406","q":"From which vintage is the first Art Series Chardonnay, Leeuwin's first wine with a painting on its label?","opts":["1980","1990","1972","1974"],"a":0,"exp":"The first Art Series Chardonnay is the 1980; 1972 is the year the house gives for Mondavi's visit, 1974 the year the nursery went in, and the first commercial vintage was 1979.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Leeuwin Estate: Our story","url":"https://leeuwinestate.com.au/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Leeuwin Estate: Media kit","url":"https://leeuwinestate.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/Leeuwin-Estate-Media-Kit.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Leeuwin Estate: 2017 Art Series Chardonnay technical and tasting notes","url":"https://leeuwinestate.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2020/06/2017-Art-Series-Chardonnay-Technical-and-Tasting-Notes.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Australia's Leeuwin Estate weaves together fine wine with art","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/title-246195/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Langton's: Leeuwin Estate Art Series Chardonnay","url":"https://www.langtons.com.au/leeuwin-estate-art-series-chardonnay.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-z2xw925l": {"id":"fl-z2xw925l","key":"cape-mentelle","group":"margaret-river","region":"margaret-river","producer":"Cape Mentelle","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Wallcliffe, Margaret River, Western Australia","founded":"1970","wine":{"name":"Cape Mentelle Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Margaret River","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with Petit Verdot and a little Merlot.","sites":"Estate vineyards that began with Wallcliffe, planted in 1970 on a ridge of free draining lateritic gravel from decomposed ironstone over gravelly clay; Trinders and other estate vineyards join it by vintage.","making":"Each batch is fermented apart, with wild and selected yeasts, and some stay on their skins for weeks after fermentation. The wine is then matured on its fine lees in oak, chiefly French barriques and much of it new, for fourteen to twenty months by vintage before the blend is bottled.","glass":"Mulberry, blackcurrant and cassis with a leafy lift of fresh herbs, black olive, nori and liquorice; cocoa, cedar and violet come with age, over fine, sleek tannin.","ageing":"Firm and fruited young, it opens after about ten years; a strong vintage, the house says, runs to twenty years and more.","serve":"At 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, in a Bordeaux glass; decant a young bottle for an hour or two and an old one slowly, leaving its sediment behind.","label":[{"term":"Cape Mentelle","means":"A headland of the cape's coast, named by a French expedition of 1803 for a French geographer."},{"term":"Margaret River","means":"The cape between Cape Naturaliste and Cape Leeuwin, registered as a Geographical Indication."}]},"table":"Cape Mentelle Cabernet Sauvignon took the Jimmy Watson Trophy, the Melbourne show's prize for a red a year old, in 1983 and again in 1984. From estate vineyards that began with Wallcliffe in 1970, it brings mulberry, cassis and black olive and a sleek, fine tannin.","story":"Three brothers founded Cape Mentelle in 1970 and named it for the nearby headland, which a French expedition had named in 1803 after a French geographer of the day. One of them, who had studied winemaking in California, made the wines. They planted the Wallcliffe Vineyard on lateritic gravel, adding Zinfandel in the early 1970s, a grape he had met in California, and the first Cape Mentelle wines came in the mid 1970s. In 1983 the 1982 Cabernet Sauvignon won the Jimmy Watson Trophy at the Royal Melbourne Wine Show, the award for a dry red a year old, and in 1984 the 1983 won it again: two wins in a row for a region whose first modern commercial vines went in only in 1967. The 1983 came from a hot, dry year, and the house says cool ocean breezes spared it the worst of the heat. In 1985 the founding winemaker went on to found Cloudy Bay in Marlborough, New Zealand. Veuve Clicquot took a stake in Cape Mentelle at the start of the 1990s and full ownership in 2000, and in 2023 it returned to Australian hands. The two trophies mark the years the cape's Cabernet won the notice of the whole country.","others":[{"name":"Cape Mentelle Zinfandel","note":"From vines planted in the 1970s, a grape the founding winemaker met while studying in California."},{"name":"Wallcliffe Sauvignon Blanc Semillon","note":"The house's white blend of the two grapes, in its Wallcliffe range."}],"traps":["The Jimmy Watson wins of 1983 and 1984 were for the 1982 and 1983 vintages: the trophy is judged on reds a year old.","Cape Mentelle's founding winemaker went on to found Cloudy Bay in Marlborough; the two houses are in different countries.","Cape Mentelle is named for a headland of the coast, not for a person of the house."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-7sfvtaud","front":"Which trophy did Cape Mentelle's Cabernet Sauvignon win in 1983 and again in 1984?","back":"The Jimmy Watson Trophy, for a dry red a year old, with the 1982 and the 1983 vintages."},{"id":"fl-bflt46th","front":"Which Marlborough house did the founding winemaker of a Margaret River estate of 1970 go on to found in 1985?","back":"Cloudy Bay, founded by the winemaker of Cape Mentelle."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ef2zyn14","q":"On what soil does the Wallcliffe Vineyard of Cape Mentelle grow its Cabernet Sauvignon?","opts":["Alluvial sand and silt","Volcanic basalt","Terra rossa over limestone","Lateritic ironstone gravel"],"a":3,"exp":"The house describes free draining lateritic gravel from decomposed ironstone over gravelly clay; terra rossa over limestone is Coonawarra's soil, not Margaret River's.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-gj5i09eh","q":"Which vintages of Cape Mentelle Cabernet Sauvignon won the Jimmy Watson Trophy?","opts":["1976 and 1977","1990 and 2000","1982 and 1983","1970 and 1971"],"a":2,"exp":"The 1982 won in 1983 and the 1983 in 1984, since the trophy is judged on reds a year old; 1970 is the founding year and 2000 the year Veuve Clicquot took full ownership.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cape Mentelle: The History of Cape Mentelle","url":"https://www.capementelle.com.au/Cape-Mentelle/History","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cape Mentelle: Cabernet Sauvignon 2018 notes","url":"https://www.capementelle.com.au/assets/images/products/media/CMCabernetSauvignon2018.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Cape Mentelle Cabernet Sauvignon 1983","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-cape-mentelle-cabernet-sauvignon-1983-450885/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Cape Mentelle, the headland","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Mentelle","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Cape Mentelle: Mentelle Notes 2023, Paragon acquires Cape Mentelle","url":"https://www.capementelle.com.au/assets/images/contentblock/files/MentelleNotes2023.pdf","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-5s2taxpe": {"id":"fl-5s2taxpe","key":"tyrrells","group":"hunter","region":"hunter-valley","producer":"Tyrrell's","producerId":"p-tyrrells","say":"","place":"Pokolbin, Hunter Valley, New South Wales","founded":"1858","wine":{"name":"Vat 1 Semillon","appellation":"Hunter Valley","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Semillon alone, from old, dry grown vines.","sites":"Old vine Semillon of the estate at Pokolbin, dry grown and on its own roots, with the Short Flat vineyard at its heart, where Semillon was planted in 1923 and 1927.","making":"Picked by hand and early, at low sugar, the fruit is pressed lightly and only the free run juice is fermented cool in stainless steel tanks. The wine rests about six weeks on its fine lees and is bottled in the winter after the harvest, with no oak at any stage.","glass":"Lemon juice, lemon peel and lime over a waxy, almost paraffin texture when young, light in body and quiet, with high, fresh acidity; with years in bottle come buttered toast, lanolin and honey, while the wine stays dry.","ageing":"Taut and lemony for its first few years, it begins to show toast and lanolin after five to ten years in bottle, and in a strong vintage it goes on for decades.","serve":"7 to 10 degrees Celsius, an older bottle at the warmer end; no need to decant; a white wine glass of medium size.","label":[{"term":"Vat 1","means":"A cellar code of the house, named for a vat, not a vineyard or a legal term."},{"term":"Hunter Valley","means":"The Geographical Indication of the zone north of Sydney that holds the Hunter region and its Pokolbin subregion."},{"term":"Semillon","means":"The grape, said the Australian way; old Hunter labels sold it as Hunter River Riesling, a name no longer allowed."}]},"table":"Steel, an early pick and no oak at all: Tyrrell's Vat 1 is Hunter Valley Semillon made the traditional way, from old, dry grown vines at Pokolbin. Lemony and light when young, it turns to toast and lanolin with years in bottle; an older bottle shows what it becomes.","story":"The founder, an English emigrant who had come out in 1850, took up land at Pokolbin in 1858 and built a slab hut there; the house and the Australian Dictionary of Biography both date his first vintage to 1864. His son Dan Tyrrell (1871 to 1959) made his first wine in 1885, at fourteen, and his last in 1959, running the winery after his father's death in 1909 while a brother helped with the vines. Until then most of the wine was sold to other wineries. Murray Tyrrell (1921 to 2000), a grandson of the founder, took over in 1959 and began to keep the chosen wines for the family's own label. In the 1960s he set them apart as the Winemaker's Selection, each named for the vat or cask that held it, and Vat 1 was the Semillon. He planted Chardonnay in 1968, and its first wine, Vat 47 of 1971, is counted by the house as the first commercial varietal Chardonnay of Australia, a claim others have contested. Vat 1 kept to the old Hunter way: picked early, fermented in steel and never touched by oak, so that its toast and lanolin come from the bottle alone. Vat 1 is the proof that a hot, humid valley can make a light, long lived dry white.","others":[{"name":"Vat 47 Chardonnay","note":"First made in 1971 from vines planted in 1968, and counted by the house as Australia's first commercial varietal Chardonnay."},{"name":"Vat 9 Shiraz","note":"The Shiraz of the Winemaker's Selection, medium bodied and savoury rather than sweet."}],"traps":["Vat 1's toast, honey and lanolin come from years in bottle, never from oak or botrytis; the wine is dry and sees no barrel.","Vat 1 reads like a vineyard or a rank, but it is neither: the number is the house's cellar code.","Vat 47 is Tyrrell's Chardonnay and Vat 1 its Semillon; candidates swap the two numbers."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-mqtm6q27","front":"Where do the toast and lanolin of an older Tyrrell's Vat 1 come from?","back":"From years in bottle alone: the wine is fermented in stainless steel and never sees oak."},{"id":"fl-epbizkw7","front":"Which vineyard lies at the heart of Tyrrell's Vat 1, and when was its Semillon planted?","back":"The Short Flat vineyard at Pokolbin, with Semillon planted in 1923 and 1927."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-21ve98xb","q":"A guest tastes a ten year old Tyrrell's Vat 1 Semillon and asks which barrel gave it the toast. What do you answer?","opts":["None: the toast comes from bottle age","Large old casks for the free run juice","New French oak barriques for six months","Old American oak puncheons for a year"],"a":0,"exp":"Vat 1 is fermented in stainless steel and bottled within months without oak; its toast and lanolin develop in bottle, so no barrel of any size is involved.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-13peuyi3","q":"Tyrrell's Vat 47 of 1971 is counted by the house as Australia's first commercial varietal Chardonnay. Who planted its vines in 1968?","opts":["Dan Tyrrell, the founder's son","Murray Tyrrell, the founder's grandson","Maurice O'Shea of Mount Pleasant","Max Lake of Lake's Folly"],"a":1,"exp":"Murray Tyrrell (1921 to 2000) took over in 1959 and planted Chardonnay in 1968; Dan Tyrrell made his last vintage in 1959, and Max Lake planted Chardonnay at Lake's Folly a year later, in 1969.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Tyrrell's: Our family history","url":"https://www.tyrrells.com.au/family/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tyrrell's: Vat 1 Semillon 2026","url":"https://www.tyrrells.com.au/wine/vat-1-semillon-2026","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Tyrrell's: Vineyards","url":"https://www.tyrrells.com.au/estate/vineyards","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Edward George Young (Dan) Tyrrell","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/tyrrell-edward-george-young-dan-11897","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Murray Tyrrell (winemaker)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murray_Tyrrell_(winemaker)","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-ftxd1eh1": {"id":"fl-ftxd1eh1","key":"mount-pleasant","group":"hunter","region":"hunter-valley","producer":"Mount Pleasant","producerId":"p-mount-pleasant","say":"","place":"Pokolbin, Hunter Valley, New South Wales","founded":"1921","wine":{"name":"Maurice O'Shea Shiraz","appellation":"Hunter Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Shiraz alone, from the estate's old vineyards.","sites":"Chosen parcels of the estate's old Pokolbin vineyards, above all Old Hill, planted in 1880 on volcanic soil beneath the Brokenback Range, Old Paddock, planted in 1921 from its cuttings on red volcanic loam, and Rosehill, planted in 1946.","making":"Drawn from the estate's chosen parcels and barrels, and made only in years the house judges strong enough, the wine is aged for about eighteen months in large French oak barrels before bottling.","glass":"Red cherry and violet when young, gaining the savoury notes of leather and turned earth that Hunter Shiraz takes on in bottle; medium in body and supple, with fine tannin, fresh acidity, a quiet frame of French barrels and a long finish.","ageing":"Ready to enjoy on release, it gains savoury depth over fifteen years and more, and a strong vintage keeps for decades.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one gently off its sediment; a Burgundy glass or a large red wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Maurice O'Shea","means":"The estate's founding winemaker, whose name the flagship Shiraz has carried since its first vintage of 1987."},{"term":"Hunter Valley","means":"The zone named on the label, north of Sydney; the Hunter region and its Pokolbin subregion, where the three vineyards lie, sit within it."},{"term":"Shiraz","means":"The grape, once sold on Hunter labels as Hermitage or Hunter Burgundy, names the law no longer allows."}]},"table":"Mount Pleasant's Maurice O'Shea Shiraz honours the winemaker who made the estate's name between the 1920s and his death in 1956. Drawn from Pokolbin vineyards planted in 1880, 1921 and 1946, it is medium bodied and savoury and made only in strong years.","story":"Shiraz has grown on the hill at Pokolbin since 1880, when an earlier settler planted the Old Hill vineyard on volcanic soil beneath the Brokenback Range. Maurice O'Shea (1897 to 1956) studied wine in France, at Montpellier and in Paris, and came home in 1920 at his mother's request. By the house's account the family bought the property in 1921, and he planted Old Paddock that year from cuttings of Old Hill; the Australian Dictionary of Biography dates the name Mount Pleasant to 1925. In the Depression, in 1932, he sold a half share to McWilliam's and stayed on as manager and winemaker, and in 1941 he sold the rest and went on making the wine. He planted Rosehill in 1946 and remained the estate's winemaker until his death in 1956. In 1987 the estate made the first Maurice O'Shea Shiraz, a selection drawn above all from Old Hill, Old Paddock and Rosehill, released in 1990 and bottled only in strong years. O'Shea's name is thus tied to the medium bodied, long lived Shiraz the Hunter is known for, from vines he planted or tended himself.","others":[{"name":"Lovedale Semillon","note":"Single vineyard Hunter Semillon from the Lovedale vineyard, planted in the 1940s, released with years of bottle age."},{"name":"Old Paddock and Old Hill Shiraz","note":"Shiraz from the estate's vineyards of 1921 and 1880, both on volcanic soil beneath the Brokenback Range."}],"traps":["Mount Pleasant is the estate and Maurice O'Shea its founding winemaker; the Shiraz named for him was first made in 1987, three decades after his death.","Hunter Shiraz is medium bodied and savoury, nearer a cool climate red than a Barossa Shiraz, though the Hunter is warm.","McWilliam's bought half of Mount Pleasant in 1932 and the rest in 1941; O'Shea stayed on as its winemaker until his death in 1956."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-t9re1hya","front":"Maurice O'Shea Shiraz: which three Mount Pleasant vineyards does it draw on, and when were they planted?","back":"Old Hill in 1880, Old Paddock in 1921 and Rosehill in 1946, all at Pokolbin."},{"id":"fl-59f7035a","front":"Where did Maurice O'Shea learn wine before he made it at Mount Pleasant, and when did he come home?","back":"In France, at Montpellier and in Paris; he returned to New South Wales in 1920, at his mother's request."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-pedquxhz","q":"Which vineyard behind Mount Pleasant's Maurice O'Shea Shiraz was planted in 1880?","opts":["Old Paddock","Lovedale","Rosehill","Old Hill"],"a":3,"exp":"Old Hill was planted in 1880 beneath the Brokenback Range; Old Paddock followed in 1921 from its cuttings and Rosehill in 1946, while Lovedale is the estate's Semillon vineyard.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-q1oy1m8s","q":"Who bought the half share in Mount Pleasant that Maurice O'Shea sold in the Depression, in 1932?","opts":["Penfolds","Lindeman's","McWilliam's","Tyrrell's"],"a":2,"exp":"O'Shea sold half to McWilliam's in 1932 and the rest in 1941, staying as manager and winemaker; Tyrrell's was a Pokolbin family house and never owned the estate.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Mount Pleasant: Our History","url":"https://mountpleasantwines.com.au/about-us/our-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Mount Pleasant: Old Hill Vineyard","url":"https://www.mountpleasantwines.com.au/our-story/our-vineyards/old-hill/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Maurice George O'Shea","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/oshea-maurice-george-11317","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Maurice O'Shea (winemaker)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maurice_O'Shea_(winemaker)","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Prince Wine Store: Mount Pleasant Maurice O'Shea Shiraz 1987","url":"https://princewinestore.com.au/mount-pleasant-maurice-o-shea-shiraz-1987.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-9dkbpkul": {"id":"fl-9dkbpkul","key":"brokenwood","group":"hunter","region":"hunter-valley","producer":"Brokenwood","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Pokolbin, Hunter Valley, New South Wales","founded":"1970","wine":{"name":"Graveyard Vineyard Shiraz","appellation":"Hunter Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Shiraz alone, from the one vineyard.","sites":"The Graveyard Vineyard at Pokolbin, beside the house's first block, planted to Shiraz in 1968 on heavy clay, hard to work, which the house credits for the character of its fruit.","making":"Fermented in small open fermenters, the wine is matured for twelve to eighteen months, by vintage, in French oak. New French and American barriques once had a large share; the house has moved towards seasoned French puncheons and larger casks, with no new oak at all in 2022. In weak years the wine is declassified and not released as Graveyard.","glass":"Rose petal over red cherry, raspberry and pomegranate, with a savoury edge in youth and leather and turned earth as it ages; concentrated yet fine, of medium body, with near silky tannin and a long line of acidity.","ageing":"Fragrant enough to drink young, it is built for the cellar: a strong vintage gains leather and savoury depth over decades.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius, in a Burgundy glass or a large red wine glass; an hour in a decanter suits a young bottle, while an old one is decanted gently off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Graveyard Vineyard","means":"The single vineyard, named for the Cemetery Block marked on the Pokolbin parish map, never used as one."},{"term":"Hunter Valley","means":"A zone north of Sydney, registered as a Geographical Indication; within it lie the Hunter region and the Pokolbin subregion, home of the Graveyard."}]},"table":"A Pokolbin block marked on the parish map for a cemetery never used, planted to Shiraz in 1968, is the source of Brokenwood's Graveyard Vineyard Shiraz. Its red cherry and rose petal are fragrant and fine; it ages for decades, and in weak years the house holds it back.","story":"In 1970 three Sydney lawyers, who claimed little knowledge of viticulture, bought a small block at Pokolbin that had once been meant for a cricket ground. They planted Shiraz and Cabernet Sauvignon in 1971 and picked their first vintage in 1973. In 1978 six new partners joined the venture, which made it possible to buy the neighbouring vineyard, planted to Shiraz in 1968. It was called the Graveyard because the Pokolbin parish map marked it as the Cemetery Block, though it was never used as one. Its heavy clay was hard to work, but its fruit stood apart, and in 1983, the first vintage of a new winemaker, the house began to bottle its single vineyard wines, the Graveyard Shiraz among them. The partnership grew, yet the Graveyard stayed a wine of one site, and the house declassifies it in weak years: by the house's count of 2018, six of its first thirty five vintages were not sold under the name. What reaches the glass is Pokolbin Shiraz of one vineyard, fragrant and medium bodied, from a house that began as three lawyers' venture and grew into one of the Hunter's familiar names.","others":[{"name":"ILR Reserve Semillon","note":"The house's aged Semillon, given no oak and released with about six years in bottle."},{"name":"HBA Shiraz","note":"A Shiraz the house lists among its three flagship wines, beside the Graveyard and the ILR."}],"traps":["The Graveyard Vineyard was marked as a cemetery on the parish map but never used as one; nobody lies under the vines.","The Graveyard Shiraz is a single vineyard wine first made in 1983, a decade after the house's first vintage of 1973.","A Graveyard vintage can be missing: the house declassifies the wine in weak years rather than release it under the name."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8m5a1sm2","front":"Brokenwood's Graveyard Vineyard: where does the name come from?","back":"The Pokolbin parish map marked it as the Cemetery Block, though it was never used as one; it was planted to Shiraz in 1968."},{"id":"fl-0we4ouxo","front":"When did Brokenwood buy the Graveyard Vineyard, and when was its first single vineyard Shiraz made?","back":"It was bought in 1978, when six new partners joined; the first Graveyard Shiraz was made in 1983."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-y9gbmh25","q":"A guest asks why some vintages of Brokenwood's Graveyard Vineyard Shiraz were never released. What is the reason?","opts":["The house declassifies it in weak years","The law limits a single vineyard to strong years","The vineyard is rested every third year","Its grapes are sold to other houses in odd years"],"a":0,"exp":"Brokenwood declassifies the Graveyard in weak years, six of its first thirty five vintages; no rotation and no law decides it, only the house's judgement of the year.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-k6encqsi","q":"Brokenwood began in 1970 when three Sydney lawyers bought a small block at Pokolbin. What had that block once been meant for?","opts":["A cemetery","A cricket ground","A school","A racecourse"],"a":1,"exp":"The first block had been meant as a cricket ground; the Cemetery Block, never used, is the neighbouring Graveyard Vineyard, bought in 1978.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Brokenwood: History","url":"https://www.brokenwood.com.au/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Brokenwood: 2018 release wines and the declassification of the 2016 Graveyard Vineyard Shiraz","url":"https://www.brokenwood.com.au/blog/2018-release-wines-and-the-declassification-on-2016-Graveyard-Vineyard-Shiraz","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Brokenwood: Flagship Wines","url":"https://www.brokenwood.com.au/flagship-wines/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Brokenwood, quintessential Hunter Valley","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/brokenwood-quintessential-hunter-valley-409870/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Langton's: Brokenwood Graveyard Vineyard Shiraz 2022","url":"https://www.langtons.com.au/p/brokenwood-wines-graveyard-vineyard-shiraz--hunter-valley-2022-bottle/1618-2022-1.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-rf3x39xe": {"id":"fl-rf3x39xe","key":"lakes-folly","group":"hunter","region":"hunter-valley","producer":"Lake's Folly","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Pokolbin, Hunter Valley, New South Wales","founded":"1963","wine":{"name":"Lake's Folly Cabernets","appellation":"Hunter Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the greater part, with Shiraz, Merlot and Petit Verdot in shares that change with the year.","sites":"The estate's own single vineyard at Pokolbin, hand picked and low yielding; Block 1, the Cabernet planted in 1963, stands in front of the cellar door.","making":"The hand picked estate fruit is fermented in open fermenters, which its sellers credit with soft, fine tannin. The young wine settles for about six months in large old casks, then spends about a year in small French oak barriques, new, one and two years old, before bottling.","glass":"Perfumed, with blackcurrant, violet and a touch of eucalyptus; light to medium in weight, with fine grained tannin, savoury French barrels in the background and a dry, lingering finish: more elegance than power.","ageing":"Open and fragrant in youth, it turns more savoury over ten to twenty years in a good vintage.","serve":"Serve at 15 to 17 degrees Celsius in a Bordeaux glass; decant for an hour when young and gently off the sediment when old.","label":[{"term":"Cabernets","means":"A blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon with other grapes; the plural names the blend, not one variety."},{"term":"Hunter Valley","means":"Zone, region and subregion nest here: the Hunter Valley zone north of Sydney holds the Hunter region, and that holds Pokolbin."}]},"table":"A Sydney surgeon's family planted Cabernet Sauvignon at Pokolbin in 1963, in a valley of Semillon and Shiraz, and Lake's Folly Cabernets is the blend that one vineyard gives. It suits a guest who wants elegance over power: perfumed, light to medium in weight, with blackcurrant and violet.","story":"Max Lake (1924 to 2009) was a Sydney surgeon who specialised in surgery of the hand. In 1963 his family and friends planted Cabernet Sauvignon on a block at Pokolbin, in a valley known for Semillon and Shiraz, and the vines of that first planting, Block 1, stand in front of the cellar door. The house dates its first vintage to 1966, made by hand and foot, and Chardonnay followed in 1969. A small estate making wine from its own vines alone, Lake's Folly is often called the first boutique winery of modern Australia. The founder's son became its winemaker in 1982, with his father consulting, by the house's timeline. In early 2000 the family sold the estate to a Perth businessman, and the Cabernets comes from the same single vineyard. Its blend moves with the year, led by Cabernet Sauvignon, with Shiraz, Merlot and Petit Verdot beside it, and a guest remembers it for perfume and fine tannin rather than weight. Against the guess that the Hunter grows only Semillon and Shiraz, it sets Cabernet of restraint, from a surgeon's folly that lasted.","others":[{"name":"Lake's Folly Chardonnay","note":"The estate's white, from Chardonnay first planted in 1969, known by its label as the Yellow Label."}],"traps":["Read Cabernets as a blend: the plural is no slip for Cabernet Sauvignon alone.","Lake's Folly grows Cabernet in the Hunter, a valley known for Semillon and Shiraz; it is not a Coonawarra or Margaret River wine.","The Cabernets wears the white label and the Chardonnay the yellow, so the trade calls them the White Label and the Yellow Label."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ckhm9969","front":"Who planted Lake's Folly's first Cabernet at Pokolbin, and in which year?","back":"The family and friends of Max Lake (1924 to 2009), a Sydney hand surgeon, in 1963."},{"id":"fl-zmd8z1c6","front":"What does the plural on Lake's Folly Cabernets tell a guest?","back":"That it is a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon, with Shiraz, Merlot and Petit Verdot."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-05ftfb7z","q":"Beside Cabernet Sauvignon, what goes into Lake's Folly Cabernets?","opts":["Grenache, Mourvèdre and Cinsault","Pinot Noir, Gamay and Merlot","Cabernet Franc, Malbec and Carmenère","Shiraz, Merlot and Petit Verdot"],"a":3,"exp":"The blend is led by Cabernet Sauvignon with Shiraz, Merlot and Petit Verdot in shares that change by year; Cabernet Franc and Malbec appear in none of the blends the sources list.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-jxxe6980","q":"What did Max Lake do for a living when his family and friends planted Lake's Folly in 1963?","opts":["He was a Barossa winemaker","He was a Sydney lawyer","He was a Sydney hand surgeon","He was a Hunter cattleman"],"a":2,"exp":"Max Lake was a Sydney surgeon who specialised in surgery of the hand; it was Brokenwood that three Sydney lawyers founded, in 1970.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Lake's Folly: Our History","url":"https://www.lakesfolly.wine/pages/our-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Lake's Folly: Our Wines","url":"https://www.lakesfolly.wine/pages/our-wines","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Max Lake","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Lake","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Dan Murphy's: Lake's Folly Cabernets 2019","url":"https://www.danmurphys.com.au/product/DM_276403/lake-s-folly-cabernets-2019","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Langton's: Lake's Folly White Label Cabernet Blend","url":"https://www.langtons.com.au/lakes-folly-cabernets.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-qeek0fd5": {"id":"fl-qeek0fd5","key":"thomas","group":"hunter","region":"hunter-valley","producer":"Thomas Wines","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Pokolbin, Hunter Valley, New South Wales","founded":"1997","wine":{"name":"Braemore Semillon","appellation":"Hunter Valley","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Semillon alone, from one vineyard.","sites":"The Braemore vineyard on Hermitage Road, Pokolbin, planted in 1969 on a narrow band of deep, free draining sandy loam, alluvial soil laid down by an ancient watercourse.","making":"Hand picked, in some years passed over a sorting table, the fruit is pressed as whole bunches to keep phenolics low and fermented in stainless steel with a neutral yeast. The wine rests four to six weeks, by vintage, on its yeast lees and is bottled in the autumn after the harvest, with no oak.","glass":"Lemongrass, lime zest and chamomile with soft green herbs in youth, light in body, with taut, high acidity and a long, dry finish; bottle age brings toast, honey and lanolin.","ageing":"Bright and drinkable on release, it has a long life ahead: the house holds some back as a Cellar Reserve, released with five to seven years in bottle, when the toast begins to show.","serve":"A medium sized white wine glass at 7 to 10 degrees Celsius, an older bottle a little warmer; it needs no decanting.","label":[{"term":"Braemore","means":"The single vineyard on Hermitage Road, Pokolbin, planted in 1969 and bought by the house in 2017."},{"term":"Cellar Reserve","means":"Printed on the Braemore the house holds back and releases with five to seven years in bottle."},{"term":"Hunter Valley","means":"The Geographical Indication of the zone; Braemore lies at Pokolbin, a subregion of the Hunter region within it."}]},"table":"Braemore, planted in 1969 on deep alluvial sand at Pokolbin, is the one vineyard of Thomas Wines' Braemore Semillon. Whole bunch pressed and made in steel without oak, it is taut with lime and lemongrass on release; the Cellar Reserve shows the toast and honey of bottle age.","story":"The Braemore vineyard was planted to Semillon in 1969 beside Hermitage Road at Pokolbin, on a narrow band of sandy alluvial soil laid down by an ancient watercourse. The house's founder grew up in McLaren Vale, the son of a winemaker there, and came to the Hunter in 1987 to join Tyrrell's, where he made wine for some thirteen years. In 1997 he released the first wine under his own name, choosing to make only the Hunter's two signature grapes, Semillon and Shiraz, and two years later he left Tyrrell's to set up on his own at Pokolbin. For the 2000 vintage the young house first bought Braemore fruit, and the single vineyard Semillon it made became its flagship white. It went on buying the fruit for nearly two decades, and in December 2017, when the vineyard's owners decided to sell, Thomas Wines bought Braemore outright. Its making follows Hunter custom, with early picking, steel and no oak, and the house releases a Cellar Reserve of it with years in bottle. Braemore is the modern face of Hunter Semillon: a single vineyard long in a grower's hands, made by a winemaker trained at Tyrrell's, a sign that the style carries on beyond the old families.","others":[{"name":"Cellar Reserve Braemore Semillon","note":"Braemore held back in the house's cellar and released with five to seven years in bottle."},{"name":"Kiss Shiraz","note":"A Shiraz of the house, from the other of the two grapes it was founded to make."}],"traps":["Braemore is a vineyard on Hermitage Road that Thomas Wines bought in 2017; the house made its Braemore Semillon from bought fruit from the 2000 vintage on.","No barrel touches Braemore: it is made in stainless steel, and the toast and honey of an older bottle come from bottle age."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-9yq2cr01","front":"On what soil is the Braemore vineyard of Thomas Wines planted?","back":"A narrow band of deep, free draining sandy loam, alluvial soil laid down by an ancient watercourse, on Hermitage Road at Pokolbin."},{"id":"fl-3ckr7et3","front":"When did Thomas Wines first make Braemore Semillon, and when did it buy the vineyard?","back":"It first bought Braemore fruit for the 2000 vintage and bought the vineyard in December 2017."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-7hfawbm8","q":"How is Thomas Wines' Braemore Semillon pressed and fermented?","opts":["Whole bunch pressed, fermented in stainless steel","Foot trodden, fermented in old oak puncheons","Left a week on skins, fermented in amphora","Crushed with skin contact, fermented in new oak"],"a":0,"exp":"Braemore is whole bunch pressed to keep phenolics low and fermented in stainless steel with a neutral yeast; skin contact and new oak would blur the lean, lemony style the house is after.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-hg48upqc","q":"Where did the founder of Thomas Wines make wine from 1987 until he set up on his own at Pokolbin in 1999?","opts":["Brokenwood","Tyrrell's","Lake's Folly","Mount Pleasant"],"a":1,"exp":"He joined Tyrrell's in 1987 and made wine there for some thirteen years before leaving in 1999; Brokenwood, Mount Pleasant and Lake's Folly are other Pokolbin houses.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Thomas Wines: Semillon Vineyards","url":"https://www.thomaswines.com.au/pages/semillon-vineyards","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Thomas Wines: 2025 Braemore Semillon","url":"https://www.thomaswines.com.au/products/2025-braemore-semillon","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Companion: Thomas Wines","url":"https://winecompanion.com.au/wineries/new-south-wales/hunter-valley/thomas-wines","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Gourmet Traveller Wine: Betting on Braemore","url":"https://gourmettravellerwine.com/app-sub/editions/feb-mar-22/article/braemore.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"International Fine Wines: Thomas Wines","url":"https://www.internationalfinewines.com.au/pages/thomas-wines","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-jlihumpc": {"id":"fl-jlihumpc","key":"wynns","group":"coonawarra","region":"coonawarra","producer":"Wynns Coonawarra Estate","producerId":"p-wynns-coonawarra","say":"WINZ koo-nuh-WOR-uh","place":"Coonawarra, Limestone Coast, South Australia","founded":"1951","wine":{"name":"Wynns Black Label Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Coonawarra","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the one grape the label names, grown on the estate's own Coonawarra vineyards.","sites":"A selection of the estate's Cabernet from its terra rossa vineyards, red loam over soft limestone on the strip north of Penola; vines planted in the 1950s and 1960s shape the wine.","making":"Drawn from a selection of the estate's Cabernet, the wine is matured for twelve to eighteen months, by vintage, in oak. Older vintages used French and American oak; later ones French alone, mostly hogsheads with some barriques and a small share in large vats, a fifth to a third of it new and the rest seasoned.","glass":"Blackcurrant and mulberry over violet, mint and cedar, at times a black olive note; medium in body, with fine, powdery tannin, fresh acidity and a firm, dry finish.","ageing":"Approachable on release, it keeps for fifteen years and more in a strong vintage, the fruit giving way to cedar, tobacco and dried mint.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Black Label","means":"The house's name for this Cabernet; a brand name, not a grade or a reserve."},{"term":"Coonawarra","means":"The region named on the label; at least 85 per cent of the wine must come from it."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon","means":"The variety named on the label; at least 85 per cent of the wine must be made from it."}]},"table":"Wynns Coonawarra Estate grew around John Riddoch's triple gabled winery of the 1890s and has made Black Label, Cabernet Sauvignon from its own terra rossa, since 1954. There is blackcurrant, mint and cedar here, medium in body with fine tannin, in a wine that drinks young and keeps fifteen years.","story":"On Yallum, a large pastoral run near Penola, John Riddoch (1827 to 1901), a Scottish born pastoralist, settled in 1861. In 1890 he subdivided part of it, a strip of red soil over limestone, as the Penola Fruit Colony, blocks for settlers to plant with fruit and vines, and in the 1890s he built the triple gabled winery around which the estate grew. The colony struggled, far from the large markets, and in the decades after his death in 1901 much of his land, the house says, went back to grazing. In 1951 the Wynn family, Melbourne wine merchants, bought the run down winery and its vineyards and renamed the property Wynns Coonawarra Estate. Their Cabernet of the 1954 vintage, the wine later sold as Black Label, began the line, and the gables of Riddoch's winery came to mark its label. In 1982 the house added John Riddoch Cabernet Sauvignon, a separate selection named for the founder. Black Label has been made in most years since: by the house's count the 2018 was its sixty third vintage. In Black Label the strip's way with Cabernet is plain to taste: cool grown, firm and long lived, from red soil over limestone.","others":[{"name":"John Riddoch Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The house's selection above Black Label, first made from the 1982 vintage and named for the man who planted the district."},{"name":"Michael Shiraz","note":"The house's Shiraz flagship, a reminder that Coonawarra grows more than Cabernet."}],"traps":["Black Label is Wynns' Coonawarra Cabernet; John Riddoch, first made in 1982, is a separate selection, not an older vintage of it.","John Riddoch built the triple gabled winery in the 1890s; the Wynn family bought it only in 1951 and put it on their labels.","Coonawarra is in South Australia, close to the Victorian border, not in Victoria."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-1st1m0wg","front":"From which vintage does the line of Wynns Black Label Cabernet begin?","back":"1954, three years after the Wynn family bought John Riddoch's winery in 1951."},{"id":"fl-pjaivgwg","front":"Who built the triple gabled winery on the Wynns Coonawarra Estate label, and in which decade?","back":"John Riddoch (1827 to 1901), founder of the Penola Fruit Colony, in the 1890s."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-o4wp3s5a","q":"What soil grows the Cabernet in Wynns Black Label?","opts":["Decomposed granite on coastal hills","Deep alluvial gravel beside a river","Black volcanic basalt on steep slopes","Terra rossa, red loam over limestone"],"a":3,"exp":"Black Label comes from the estate's terra rossa, red loam over soft limestone on the flat Coonawarra strip; gravel, basalt and granite are the soils of other regions, not this one.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-8hyoshyt","q":"A family of Melbourne wine merchants bought John Riddoch's run down Coonawarra winery in 1951. Which family?","opts":["The Penfold family","The Redman family","The Wynn family","The Lindeman family"],"a":2,"exp":"The Wynns bought the winery and its vineyards in 1951 and renamed them Wynns Coonawarra Estate; the Redmans were Coonawarra growers whose Rouge Homme winery went to Lindeman's in 1965.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Wynns Coonawarra Estate: Our Heritage timeline","url":"https://www.wynns.com.au/en-au/discover/our-heritage/timeline.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wynns Coonawarra Estate: Black Label Cabernet Sauvignon 2022 tasting note","url":"https://www.wynns.com.au/on/demandware.static/-/Sites-twe-master-catalog/default/dw0b99e56f/tasting-notes/Wynns_Coonawarra_Estate/Wynns%202022%20BL%20CabSauv%20Coonawarra%20TN.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: John Riddoch","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/riddoch-john-8210","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Coonawarra Vignerons: The History of Coonawarra","url":"https://coonawarra.org/2018/02/coonawarra-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Australia: The blending rules","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/news/articles/the-blending-rules","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-w50k5zos": {"id":"fl-w50k5zos","key":"katnook","group":"coonawarra","region":"coonawarra","producer":"Katnook Estate","producerId":"","say":"KAT-nook","place":"Coonawarra, Limestone Coast, South Australia","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Katnook Odyssey Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Coonawarra","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, named alone on the label, from the estate's own vines.","sites":"Specific rows of the estate's own vineyards on Coonawarra's terra rossa, red loam over limestone, on land that was once part of John Riddoch's pastoral holding.","making":"Only vintages the house judges strong enough give an Odyssey. For many years the wine spent thirty months and more in barriques, mostly French with some American, part of it new; vintages of the early 2020s spent fifteen to sixteen months in French oak hogsheads, a third or more of them new.","glass":"Mulberry, cassis and ripe plum with cedar and a touch of mint; full in body, with supple but defined tannin, fresh acidity and a firm, persistent finish.","ageing":"Its first years are tight and firm, and it is made for the cellar: the house's notes give it from ten to twenty years and more, by vintage.","serve":"At 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, in a Bordeaux glass; a young bottle wants an hour or two in a decanter, an old one gentle decanting off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Odyssey","means":"The flagship's name, which the house says stands for the estate's journey from its nineteenth century roots."},{"term":"Katnook Estate","means":"The estate whose woolshed, built for John Riddoch in the 1860s, saw the vintage of 1896."},{"term":"Coonawarra","means":"The Geographical Indication on the label, the source of at least 85 per cent of the wine."}]},"table":"Only in years it judges strong enough does Katnook Estate make Odyssey, from chosen rows of Cabernet Sauvignon on its own terra rossa, aged in French oak. It is built for the cellar, full and firm with cassis, mulberry and cedar, so decant a young bottle.","story":"Katnook stands on land that was part of the pastoral holding of John Riddoch (1827 to 1901), who had made his home near Penola in 1861 and had a woolshed built there in the 1860s. In 1890 he turned part of his land into the Penola Fruit Colony, where vines were planted from 1891, and by the house's account a small first vintage in 1895 was made in a nursery shed and a larger one in 1896 in the Katnook woolshed, with a trained winemaker in charge. When Riddoch died in 1901, the house relates, the property returned to sheep and the woolshed to wool for decades. New vines were planted in 1971, and in 1980, as the house dates it, the estate made its first commercial vintage of the modern era, again in the woolshed. Odyssey began with the 1991 vintage, held back and released in 1996 to mark a hundred years since the woolshed vintage of 1896; the name, the house says, stands for the estate's journey. Made only in years the house judges strong enough, from chosen rows of its own Cabernet, it stands beside a Shiraz, Prodigy, at the head of the range. Pour it as the wine that joins Riddoch's first Coonawarra vintages to the modern strip.","others":[{"name":"Prodigy Shiraz","note":"Odyssey's counterpart in Shiraz, beside it at the head of the range."},{"name":"Katnook Estate Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The estate's Coonawarra Cabernet, from the same terra rossa vineyards as Odyssey."}],"traps":["Odyssey is Katnook's Cabernet Sauvignon flagship; Prodigy is its Shiraz.","The first Odyssey is the 1991 vintage, though it reached the market only in 1996, the centenary of the woolshed vintage.","The woolshed vintage of 1896 was Riddoch's; the estate's first commercial vintage of the modern era came only in 1980."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-0w7il66y","front":"Which vintage was the first Katnook Odyssey, and when did it reach the market?","back":"The 1991, released in 1996 to mark a hundred years since the vintage of 1896 in the estate's woolshed."},{"id":"fl-ih0m2eb0","front":"In which building of John Riddoch's at Katnook, put up in the 1860s, was the vintage of 1896 made?","back":"The woolshed, as the house and a centenary plaque at the estate tell it; the estate's first modern vintage, 1980, was made there too."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-qpblvo4r","q":"A guest asks why some years are missing from a vertical list of Katnook Odyssey. What explains the gaps?","opts":["Made only in years the house judges strong","It is released only in centenary years","Its vineyard is replanted every decade","The Coonawarra GI forbids it in cool years"],"a":0,"exp":"Odyssey is made only in vintages the house judges strong enough, from chosen rows of its terra rossa Cabernet; no rule of the Coonawarra GI governs which years a wine is made.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-2nru3vng","q":"Katnook held back its first Odyssey, the 1991, and released it in 1996. What did the release mark?","opts":["The entry of Coonawarra in the register of GIs","A hundred years since the woolshed vintage of 1896","Twenty five years since the estate's replanting","A hundred years since the Penola Fruit Colony"],"a":1,"exp":"The 1996 release marked the centenary of the 1896 vintage made in Riddoch's woolshed at Katnook; the fruit colony dates from 1890 and the replanting from 1971.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Katnook Estate: Our Story, History","url":"https://club.katnookestate.com.au/Our-Story/History","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Katnook Estate: Odyssey Cabernet Sauvignon 2021","url":"https://club.katnookestate.com.au/product/Odyssey-Cabernet-Sauvignon-2021","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Katnook Estate: Katnook Odyssey 2014","url":"https://club.katnookestate.com.au/product/Katnook-Odyssey-2014","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Berkmann Wine Cellars: Katnook Estate releases the twentieth vintage of Odyssey","url":"https://www.berkmann.co.uk/news/katnook-estate-coonawarra-releases-20th-vintage-odyssey-uk/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Australia: The blending rules","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/news/articles/the-blending-rules","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-6hf9xuuq": {"id":"fl-6hf9xuuq","key":"parker","group":"coonawarra","region":"coonawarra","producer":"Parker Coonawarra Estate","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Coonawarra, Limestone Coast, South Australia","founded":"1985","wine":{"name":"Parker First Growth","appellation":"Coonawarra","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with a small share of Merlot or Petit Verdot in some vintages.","sites":"The Abbey Vineyard, planted in 1985 at the southern end of Coonawarra's terra rossa strip, with an old South Australian selection of Cabernet that the house traces to Bordeaux cuttings brought before phylloxera.","making":"Made only in vintages the house judges able to age fifteen years and more. Fermented in closed static fermenters, the wine stays on its skins for a month to six weeks in all, by vintage; it is then pressed into French oak, mostly new barriques with some hogsheads, and aged sixteen to eighteen months.","glass":"Cigar box, cedar and bay leaf with blackcurrant, mulberry and black olive; full and tightly built, with firm, fine tannin and a long finish.","ageing":"Fifteen years and more is the span the house makes it for, and it is firm and closed when young.","serve":"Allow a young bottle two hours in a decanter and decant an old one gently off its sediment; serve at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius in a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"First Growth","means":"English for premier cru, the rank that heads the 1855 classification of the Médoc; here a brand name, not a rank."},{"term":"Terra Rossa","means":"Italian for red earth, the soil of the strip; older bottles read Terra Rossa First Growth."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon","means":"Named on later labels; the grape makes up the great majority of the blend."}]},"table":"Parker Coonawarra Estate named First Growth for Bordeaux's rank: a Cabernet Sauvignon from the Abbey Vineyard at the southern end of the terra rossa, made only in vintages fit to age. Give it time and a decanter: it is firm and fine, with blackcurrant, cedar and bay leaf.","story":"The Parker family came late to Coonawarra, almost a century after John Riddoch (1827 to 1901) founded the Penola Fruit Colony in 1890. In 1985 the family planted the Abbey Vineyard at the southern end of the terra rossa strip, with Cabernet Sauvignon of an old South Australian selection that the house traces to cuttings brought from Bordeaux before phylloxera. Their aim, the house recalls, was a Coonawarra Cabernet to stand beside Bordeaux, and they named their wine for the Bordeaux rank: in the 1855 classification of the Médoc, the first growths head the list. The first vintage, the 1988, was released under the name Terra Rossa First Growth, and the house has made it only in years it judged fit to age fifteen years and more, mostly Cabernet with a little Merlot or Petit Verdot. Later labels shortened the name to First Growth, and some add Cabernet Sauvignon. The name is a claim, not a rank: Australia has no official classification of growths, and a guest who sees the words on a list is owed that distinction along with the wine, a firm Coonawarra Cabernet from one vineyard, matured in mostly new French barriques.","others":[{"name":"95 Block","note":"Cabernet Sauvignon with a share of Petit Verdot, matured for about sixteen months in barrel."},{"name":"Terra Rossa range","note":"Single varietal wines named for the red soil, Shiraz, Merlot and Cabernet Franc among them."}],"traps":["First Growth is a brand name borrowed from Bordeaux; Australia has no official classification of growths.","Terra Rossa First Growth on an older bottle and First Growth on a newer one are the same wine.","Parker First Growth is made only in vintages the house judges fit to age, so some years have none."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-i0aqawcq","front":"Parker First Growth: which vineyard gives its Cabernet, and where on the strip?","back":"The Abbey Vineyard, planted in 1985 at the southern end of Coonawarra's terra rossa."},{"id":"fl-t7vwq4vm","front":"With which vintage did Parker First Growth begin, and under what name was it sold?","back":"The 1988, as Terra Rossa First Growth, from vines planted in 1985."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-izdq3s8n","q":"Which official classification gave Parker First Growth the rank its name claims?","opts":["The Coonawarra GI's list of growths","The 1855 classification of the Médoc","A ranking set by Wine Australia","None: it is the house's own name"],"a":3,"exp":"Australia has no official classification of growths, so the house borrowed the English for Bordeaux's premier cru as a name; the 1855 list ranks Bordeaux estates, never an Australian wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-7av6wv9p","q":"Parker First Growth comes from the Abbey Vineyard. When did the Parker family plant it?","opts":["1971","1890","1985","1951"],"a":2,"exp":"The family planted the Abbey Vineyard in 1985 and made the wine's opening vintage in 1988; 1890 is Riddoch's fruit colony and 1951 the year the Wynns bought his winery.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Parker Estate: Our Story","url":"https://parkercoonawarraestate.com.au/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Parker Estate: First Growth Cabernet Sauvignon Coonawarra 2022","url":"https://parkercoonawarraestate.com.au/wine/first-growth-cabernet-sauvignon/62224/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Parker Estate: First Growth Coonawarra 2021","url":"https://parkercoonawarraestate.com.au/product/first-growth/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Coonawarra Vignerons: Historical Timeline of the Coonawarra wine region","url":"https://coonawarra.org/timeline/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Nick's Wine Merchants: 2020 Parker Coonawarra Estate First Growth","url":"https://www.nicks.com.au/products/2020-parker-coonawarra-estate-first-growth","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-jshlkmvv": {"id":"fl-jshlkmvv","key":"majella","group":"coonawarra","region":"coonawarra","producer":"Majella","producerId":"","say":"muh-JEL-uh","place":"Coonawarra, Limestone Coast, South Australia","founded":"1991","wine":{"name":"Majella The Malleea","appellation":"Coonawarra","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, a little over half, with Shiraz for the rest.","sites":"The Lynn family's Majella vineyard on the terra rossa of the Coonawarra strip, planted from 1968, mostly to Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz.","making":"The house makes it only in years it judges right. Small parcels ferment for about five days in headed down static fermenters, then move into new French oak, mostly hogsheads, to finish their fermentation; they mature there for sixteen to twenty two months, by vintage, and chosen barrels make the final blend. Vintages of the early 2000s spent as long as twenty nine months.","glass":"Plum, blackcurrant and boysenberry with mocha, cedar and mint; rich and full bodied, with fine, firm tannin and length in the finish.","ageing":"Rich enough to enjoy within a few years of release, it is made to keep: the house gives it fifteen to twenty years.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius in a large Bordeaux glass; give a young bottle an hour or two in the decanter.","label":[{"term":"The Malleea","means":"The house glosses it as an Aboriginal word for a green paddock, for the pastures of the region."},{"term":"Majella","means":"The name of the Lynn family's farm, bought in 1960, which gives the house its name."}]},"table":"Two grapes from the Lynn family's own terra rossa, Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz, go into Majella's The Malleea, finished in new French oak and made only in years that suit it. Rich and firm, with blackcurrant, plum, mocha and mint, it is a wine to decant and keep.","story":"The Lynns had lived around Penola for generations, by the house's account as storekeepers and then graziers, when one of the family bought the property called Majella from his uncle in 1960. In 1968 his sons planted its first vines on the terra rossa, with their father's blessing, and from the 1970s they sold their grapes to the wineries of the district, Wynns among them. For two decades they were growers only. In 1991 they kept back enough Shiraz to make a wine of their own, made at the Brand family's Laira winery nearby, and the first Majella Shiraz was released. The Malleea followed with the 1996 vintage: Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz from the family's own vineyard, finished in new French oak and made only in years when the growing season suits it. The house glosses the name as an Aboriginal word for a green paddock, for the pastures of the Limestone Coast. The family finished its own winery on the farm at the end of 1998, the house records. A region known for Cabernet shows its Shiraz here too, in a blend that turns a grower family's long knowledge of one vineyard into a label of its own.","others":[{"name":"Majella Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The varietal Cabernet, grown on the same terra rossa vineyard as The Malleea."},{"name":"Majella Shiraz","note":"Where the label began, with the 1991 vintage."}],"traps":["The Malleea is a blend of Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz, not a single varietal Cabernet.","Majella is the name of the Lynn family's property, bought in 1960; the family sold its grapes to others until 1991.","The Malleea began with the 1996 vintage, five years after the first Majella wine, a Shiraz of 1991."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-bq25c9x4","front":"Majella The Malleea: which two grapes, and which leads?","back":"Cabernet Sauvignon, a little over half, with Shiraz for the rest."},{"id":"fl-07fpj82d","front":"Until 1991, what did the Lynn family do with the grapes of the Majella vineyard?","back":"Sold them to the district's wineries, Wynns among them; in 1991 they made their own Shiraz."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-xn2dh3i2","q":"What goes into a bottle of Majella The Malleea?","opts":["Shiraz and Grenache","Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz","Cabernet Sauvignon alone","Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot"],"a":1,"exp":"The Malleea blends Cabernet Sauvignon, a little over half, with Shiraz, both from the family's terra rossa; Merlot and Grenache play no part, and Cabernet alone is the house's varietal wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-5cf9r079","q":"In 1991 the Lynn family kept back Shiraz to start their own Majella label. Where was that wine made?","opts":["At the Brand family's Laira winery","In Katnook's nineteenth century woolshed","At Wynns' triple gabled winery","In a new cellar on the Majella farm"],"a":0,"exp":"The first Majella Shiraz was made at the Brand family's Laira winery nearby; the family finished its own winery on the farm only at the end of 1998.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Majella Wines: Our History","url":"https://www.majellawines.com.au/about-us/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Majella Wines: About Us","url":"https://www.majellawines.com.au/about-us","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Majella Wines: The Malleea 2016 tasting note","url":"https://www.majellawines.com.au/files/536_16_malleea.pdf?v=965","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Majella Wines: The Malleea 2001 tasting note","url":"https://www.majellawines.com.au/files/249_2001_malleea_tasting_notes.pdf?v=490","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Coonawarra Vignerons: Majella Wines","url":"https://coonawarra.org/wineries/majella-wines/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-s45m4t04": {"id":"fl-s45m4t04","key":"lindemans","group":"coonawarra","region":"coonawarra","producer":"Lindeman's","producerId":"","say":"LIN-duh-munz","place":"Coonawarra, Limestone Coast, South Australia","founded":"1843","wine":{"name":"Lindeman's Limestone Ridge Shiraz Cabernet","appellation":"Coonawarra","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Shiraz, from a little over half to some four fifths by vintage, with Cabernet Sauvignon for the rest; the label names the larger share first.","sites":"The Limestone Ridge vineyard in central Coonawarra, planted in the late 1960s on terra rossa over the ridge of limestone that gives it its name.","making":"Matured in oak for up to eighteen months, by vintage: for many years in American oak, part of it new and the rest seasoned, and in later vintages in a mix of new and seasoned American and French oak.","glass":"Liquorice, blackberry and blackcurrant with a lift of mint and the vanilla of American oak; medium to full in body, with firm, fine tannin and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"Built to age and firm in youth, it trades blackberry for cedar, leather and dried mint over a decade and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Limestone Ridge","means":"The vineyard, named for the ridge of limestone beneath its terra rossa."},{"term":"Coonawarra Trio","means":"The house's name for three Coonawarra reds: Limestone Ridge, St George and Pyrus."},{"term":"Shiraz Cabernet","means":"Shiraz leads, since Australian labels list the grapes of a blend in descending order of share."}]},"table":"One of the house's Coonawarra Trio, Lindeman's Limestone Ridge is a Shiraz Cabernet from one vineyard, planted in the late 1960s on terra rossa over a ridge of limestone. In the glass it gives blackcurrant, blackberry and liquorice, firm and long lived, and it is worth decanting.","story":"Dr Henry John Lindeman (1811 to 1881), an English surgeon trained at St Bartholomew's Hospital in London, sailed as a ship's surgeon before settling in New South Wales on Cawarra, a property on the Paterson River in the Hunter Valley. He planted his first vines there in 1843, the year the house takes as its founding, and after his death at Cawarra in 1881 his sons carried the business on. The house grew far beyond the Hunter, and in the 1950s it began buying Coonawarra wine for blending. In 1965 it bought the Redman family's Rouge Homme winery, its label and its vineyards, and so arrived in Coonawarra, where it went on to add the St George, Limestone Ridge and Nursery vineyards on the terra rossa of the central strip. Limestone Ridge, planted in the late 1960s and named for the ridge of limestone under its red soil, gave a Shiraz Cabernet; St George gave a Cabernet whose 1980 vintage won the Jimmy Watson Trophy for young reds. With Pyrus, a Cabernet blend, they became the three wines the house calls its Coonawarra Trio. Limestone Ridge keeps the old South Australian pairing of Shiraz with Cabernet, from one vineyard with its name on the label.","others":[{"name":"St George Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Cabernet from the St George vineyard; its 1980 vintage won the Jimmy Watson Trophy for young reds."},{"name":"Pyrus","note":"The third wine of the Coonawarra Trio, a Cabernet blend."}],"traps":["Limestone Ridge is Shiraz led with Cabernet; St George is Cabernet Sauvignon; Pyrus is a Cabernet blend.","Lindeman's was founded in the Hunter Valley in 1843 and came to Coonawarra only in 1965, when it bought Rouge Homme.","Rouge Homme was the Redman family's winery and label, sold to Lindeman's in 1965; it is not one of the Trio's vineyards."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-kwsptt5p","front":"Name the three wines of Lindeman's Coonawarra Trio and the grape that leads each.","back":"Limestone Ridge, Shiraz with Cabernet; St George, Cabernet Sauvignon; Pyrus, a Cabernet blend."},{"id":"fl-m7qvmqh1","front":"What did Lindeman's buy in Coonawarra in 1965, and from whom?","back":"The Rouge Homme winery, its label and its vineyards, from the Redman family."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-xm16j9fx","q":"Why does the label of Lindeman's Limestone Ridge read Shiraz Cabernet rather than Cabernet Shiraz?","opts":["Coonawarra's own rules put Shiraz first","The vineyard grows Shiraz alone","Shiraz is picked before the Cabernet","Shiraz is the larger share of the blend"],"a":3,"exp":"Australian labels list the grapes of a blend in descending order of share, and Limestone Ridge is mostly Shiraz; the rule is national, not Coonawarra's, and the vineyard grows Cabernet too.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-wua2os2o","q":"Dr Henry John Lindeman planted the vines that began the house in 1843. Where?","opts":["At Magill, in the hills east of Adelaide","On the terra rossa strip at Coonawarra","At Cawarra on the Paterson River, in the Hunter","At Rutherglen, in north east Victoria"],"a":2,"exp":"Lindeman planted Cawarra, in the Hunter Valley, in 1843; the house reached Coonawarra only in 1965, and Magill is where Penfolds began.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Lindeman's: Our history","url":"https://www.lindemans.com/en-au/about-us/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Australian Dictionary of Biography: Henry John Lindeman","url":"https://adb.anu.edu.au/biography/lindeman-henry-john-4022","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Coonawarra Vignerons: Lindeman's Coonawarra","url":"https://coonawarra.org/wineries/lindemans-coonawarra/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Nick's Wine Merchants: 2008 Lindemans Limestone Ridge Vineyard Shiraz Cabernet Sauvignon","url":"https://www.nicks.com.au/products/2008-lindemans-limestone-ridge-vineyard-shiraz-cabernet-sauvignon","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Australia: The blending rules","url":"https://www.wineaustralia.com/news/articles/the-blending-rules","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-9a87qmsw": {"id":"fl-9a87qmsw","key":"cloudy-bay","group":"marlborough","region":"marlborough","producer":"Cloudy Bay","producerId":"p-cloudy-bay","say":"","place":"Rapaura, Wairau Valley, Marlborough","founded":"1985","wine":{"name":"Cloudy Bay Sauvignon Blanc","appellation":"Marlborough","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, blended from many separate lots of the Wairau Valley.","sites":"Vineyards in three subregions of the Wairau Valley, Rapaura and Renwick on its stony floor and the Brancott Valley to the south, the house's Estate and Motukawa vineyards among them.","making":"About eighty lots are tasted apart each year before the blend is chosen. Almost all of the wine is fermented cool in stainless steel tanks; a few per cent or less is fermented warmer in old French oak barriques or large oak vats, and in some years a portion with wild yeast, for texture. It is sealed under screwcap.","glass":"Passionfruit, gooseberry and makrut lime over white nectarine and honeydew melon, with a green lift of blackcurrant leaf; bright, cutting acidity and a stony, saline finish.","ageing":"Ready on release, when its aromas are at their most vivid; the house says a good bottle will keep for a decade and more.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius, as the house advises; no need to decant; a tulip shaped white wine glass.","label":[{"term":"Marlborough","means":"A registered New Zealand Geographical Indication: at least 85 per cent of the wine comes from grapes grown there."},{"term":"Sauvignon Blanc","means":"The grape named on the label makes up at least 85 per cent of the wine."},{"term":"Cloudy Bay","means":"The bay into which the Wairau River runs, named by James Cook in 1770; the vineyards lie inland."}]},"table":"From its first vintage of 1985, Cloudy Bay Sauvignon Blanc carried Marlborough's name abroad: passionfruit, gooseberry and makrut lime over a cutting acidity, almost all of it fermented cool in steel. Pour it cold, beside oysters or any dish a squeeze of lemon would suit.","story":"The bay came first: James Cook (1728 to 1779) charted it in 1770 and named it Cloudy Bay, though its Māori name, Te Koko-o-Kupe, the oyster dredge of Kupe, is older. In 1983, by the house's account, the founding winemaker of Cape Mentelle in Margaret River tasted his first Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc and resolved to bottle what he found in it. He founded Cloudy Bay in 1985 with a young winemaker who would direct its first twenty five vintages. That first wine was made from bought Marlborough grapes, trucked north to another company's winery in Gisborne; the house made its first wine in its own winery at Rapaura in 1986, and its passionfruit, gooseberry and cutting acidity carried Marlborough's name abroad within a decade. Pelorus, a sparkling wine, followed from the 1987 harvest, and Te Koko, a Sauvignon Blanc fermented with wild yeast and aged in barrel, from the 1996 vintage, released in 2000. Veuve Clicquot took a stake at the start of the 1990s and full ownership in 2003. The vintage of 1985 is where the story of New Zealand wine abroad begins, and this is the bottle likely to be named at the table.","others":[{"name":"Te Koko","note":"Sauvignon Blanc fermented with wild yeast and aged in oak, first made in 1996 and named for the Māori name of the bay."},{"name":"Pelorus","note":"The house's traditional method sparkling wine of Chardonnay and Pinot Noir, first harvested in 1987."}],"traps":["Cloudy Bay is a house, not a subregion: its vineyards lie inland in the Wairau Valley, and the bay itself grows no grapes.","Te Koko is barrel fermented with wild yeast, so blind it shows texture and little of the pungency of the house's classic Sauvignon Blanc.","Cloudy Bay was founded by Cape Mentelle's founding winemaker, from Margaret River in Western Australia, which candidates rarely expect."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-cjhizd8c","front":"Where and how was Cloudy Bay's first vintage of 1985 made?","back":"From bought Marlborough grapes, trucked north to another company's winery in Gisborne; its own winery followed in 1986."},{"id":"fl-ybk9ufko","front":"Cloudy Bay Sauvignon Blanc: how is almost all of it fermented, and what is the exception?","back":"Cool, in stainless steel; a few per cent or less is fermented warmer in old oak barriques or large vats."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-lfdz3rdn","q":"Where do the grapes for Cloudy Bay Sauvignon Blanc grow?","opts":["In three Wairau Valley subregions: Rapaura, Renwick and Brancott","In the cooler, higher Awatere Valley to the south","In Gisborne, on the east coast of the North Island","On the shores of the bay that gives the house its name"],"a":0,"exp":"The house draws on three Wairau Valley subregions; the bay lies at the river's mouth and grows no vines, and Gisborne is where the first vintage was made, from Marlborough grapes.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-zpcr5qix","q":"Cloudy Bay was founded in 1985 by a Margaret River winemaker. Which estate had he founded there before?","opts":["Moss Wood","Cape Mentelle","Leeuwin Estate","Vasse Felix"],"a":1,"exp":"He was the founding winemaker of Cape Mentelle, founded with his brothers in 1970; Vasse Felix, Leeuwin and Moss Wood are other Margaret River houses with founders of their own.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cloudy Bay: 40 Years in the Making","url":"https://www.cloudybay.com/en-us/journal/40-years-making/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cloudy Bay: Sauvignon Blanc 2021 tasting note","url":"https://www.cloudybay.com/drupal/sites/default/files/2022-12/Cloudy-Bay_Tasting-Note_Sauvignon-Blanc-2021_Landscape.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Cloudy Bay at 40, raising a glass to a game changer","url":"https://www.decanter.com/premium/cloudy-bay-at-40-raising-a-glass-to-a-game-changer-558089","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Cloudy Bay Vineyards","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloudy_Bay_Vineyards","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine Marlborough: Cloudy Bay","url":"https://www.marlboroughwinenz.com/press-releases/cloudy-bay","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-i876hd6c": {"id":"fl-i876hd6c","key":"brancott","group":"marlborough","region":"marlborough","producer":"Brancott Estate","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Brancott, Wairau Plains, Marlborough","founded":"1973","wine":{"name":"Brancott Estate Sauvignon Blanc","appellation":"Marlborough","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, all or nearly all of the blend.","sites":"Vineyards across the company's holdings in Marlborough; the line began with the Brancott Vineyard, the first planting of the modern industry, set out on the Wairau Plains in 1973.","making":"The grapes are crushed and gently pressed in bag presses, and the clarified juice is fermented in temperature controlled stainless steel tanks with as little exposure to air as can be, rather than in oak, so that the bottle keeps the fruit's fresh, green snap.","glass":"Pink grapefruit, gooseberry and honeydew melon, with passionfruit on the palate and a green note of snow pea; light and zesty, with tight, fresh acidity and a clean, quick finish.","ageing":"Made to be drunk young, in the year or two after the vintage, while its fruit is at its freshest.","serve":"7 to 10 degrees Celsius in a white wine glass, with no decanting, beside shellfish, goat's cheese or a green salad.","label":[{"term":"Brancott Estate","means":"The name Montana's wines took worldwide from 2010, after the company's first Marlborough vineyard."},{"term":"Marlborough","means":"Registered as a Geographical Indication; on a label it means at least 85 per cent of the grapes were grown in the region."}]},"table":"Behind Brancott Estate Sauvignon Blanc stands the company, then called Montana, that planted modern Marlborough's first vines at Brancott in 1973 and made the region's first commercial Sauvignon Blanc in 1979. Pink grapefruit, gooseberry and passionfruit make it light and zesty, a bottle to open young and cold.","story":"The company began in West Auckland, where a family from Dalmatia planted a vineyard, and two sons of that family later set up Montana Wines. In the early 1970s Montana sent its viticulturist south to find land and bought farmland on the Wairau Plains of Marlborough. Planting began in the winter of 1973, and on 24 August the company marked it with a ceremony at the Brancott Vineyard: the day, as New Zealand's wine body tells it, that Marlborough's wine industry was born. Sauvignon Blanc went in at Brancott in 1975, and in 1979 came the first commercial Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc, the start of the style the region is known for. Allied Domecq bought Montana in 2001, and Pernod Ricard took over Allied Domecq in 2005. From 2010 the brand took the name Brancott Estate across the world: the name of that first vineyard, and the name its wines already carried in the United States, where Montana is a state. Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc has its root in this bottle.","others":[{"name":"Brancott Estate Letter Series B Sauvignon Blanc","note":"The house's Letter Series Sauvignon Blanc, drawn from chosen Marlborough vineyards."},{"name":"Brancott Estate Flight Song Sauvignon Blanc","note":"Picked earlier than usual for a lighter wine, lower in alcohol than the classic bottling."}],"traps":["Brancott Estate and Montana are one company: the Montana brand took the Brancott Estate name worldwide from 2010.","Brancott's Sauvignon Blanc vines of 1975 were Marlborough's first, not New Zealand's: the grape had been planted in West Auckland in 1969, by New Zealand Winegrowers' account.","1973 is the planting of the Brancott Vineyard and 1979 the first commercial Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc; candidates run the two together."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-riibb8cd","front":"What happened at Brancott on 24 August 1973?","back":"Montana marked the planting of the Brancott Vineyard, the first vines of Marlborough's modern wine industry, with a ceremony."},{"id":"fl-yuqjsnp2","front":"What was planted at Brancott in 1975, and what followed in 1979?","back":"Sauvignon Blanc vines, among Marlborough's first; the first commercial Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc followed in 1979."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-1dbqntc1","q":"A guest reads Marlborough on a bottle of Brancott Estate Sauvignon Blanc. What share of the wine must come from Marlborough?","opts":["At least 75 per cent","All of it, by law","At least half","At least 85 per cent"],"a":3,"exp":"New Zealand law asks at least 85 per cent for a region, a grape or a vintage named on the label; it does not ask for all of it, and 75 per cent or half would fall short.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-dr73235s","q":"Why did Montana's wines take the name Brancott Estate across the world from 2010?","opts":["After the river that drains the valley","After the Dalmatian village its founders left","After its first Marlborough vineyard, planted in 1973","After the Champagne house that bought it"],"a":2,"exp":"Brancott was the vineyard planted in 1973, and the name its wines already carried in the United States, where Montana is a state; the river of the valley is the Wairau.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"New Zealand Winegrowers: Brancott Estate, first vines in Marlborough","url":"https://www.nzwine.com/en/media/our-people/brancott/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Marlborough: Marlborough's wine history","url":"https://www.marlboroughwinenz.com/press-releases/marlboroughs-wine-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Brancott Estate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brancott_Estate","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Brancott Estate: Sauvignon Blanc","url":"https://brancottestate.com/en-us/products/brancott-estate-sauvignon-blanc","kind":"primary"},{"title":"New Zealand Wine Catalogue: Brancott Estate Sauvignon Blanc entry","url":"https://nzwinecatalog.bottlebooks.me/products/by-exhibitor/59ad62d2c5862663cefd6869/6516e3f63eb4fb3963cbd861","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-8vx40zqw": {"id":"fl-8vx40zqw","key":"dog-point","group":"marlborough","region":"marlborough","producer":"Dog Point Vineyard","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Where the Brancott and Omaka valleys meet, Southern Valleys, Marlborough","founded":"2002","wine":{"name":"Dog Point Section 94 Sauvignon Blanc","appellation":"Marlborough","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, from one parcel of the estate.","sites":"A single parcel within the Dog Point Vineyard, planted in 1992 and cropped low, on the free draining silty clay loams that carry the estate's older vines; the vineyard is certified organic.","making":"Picked by hand and pressed as whole bunches, the juice is fermented with natural, wild yeasts and aged in older French oak barrels for eighteen months in all, then bottled without fining.","glass":"Flint, struck match and grapefruit pith over grilled lemon, white peach and honeydew melon, with a mealy, cashew richness; creamy and chalky in texture, with bright acidity and a finish that lasts.","ageing":"Released with more bottle age than most Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc, it is open on release and keeps for several years more, growing deeper and nuttier.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius, warmer than a young Sauvignon from steel, in a white Burgundy glass; it needs no decanting.","label":[{"term":"Section 94","means":"The parcel's title in an early survey of Marlborough, made when the Hawkesbury Run was cut up."},{"term":"Dog Point","means":"The place named for the wild dogs, lost by shepherds, that settlers cleared from the land."},{"term":"Marlborough","means":"A Geographical Indication on the register; at least 85 per cent of the wine must come from grapes grown within it."}]},"table":"Marlborough Sauvignon shows its other face in Dog Point Section 94: one parcel planted in 1992, picked by hand, fermented with wild yeasts and kept eighteen months in older French oak. Expect flint, grapefruit pith and white peach with a creamy texture, and pour it a little warmer than usual.","story":"The place took its name, as the house tells it, from the early sheep runs: boundary dogs lost by shepherds bred into a wild pack that preyed on the flocks they were meant to guard, until settlers cleared them, and the spot became Dog Point. In the late 1970s a Marlborough couple planted their first grapes, among the region's early growers. The husband became Cloudy Bay's viticulturist, and there he worked beside one of its winemakers who, by Cloudy Bay's own history, shared in devising Te Koko: its wild fermented, barrel aged Sauvignon Blanc. A single parcel of the vineyard was planted in 1992, and by the house's account it carries its title from an early survey of Marlborough, made when the vast Hawkesbury Run was cut up: Section 94. The two couples made their first wine of their own from the 2002 vintage and launched the Dog Point label in February 2004, and Section 94 brought the barrel and the wild yeast to a parcel of their own. The vineyard began its turn to organic growing in 2009, the house notes, and is certified organic. When a guest wants Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc with texture rather than snap, Section 94 is the one to offer.","others":[{"name":"Dog Point Sauvignon Blanc","note":"The classic bottling, by the house's account its one wine that is not barrel fermented."},{"name":"Dog Point Chardonnay","note":"Whole bunch pressed and fermented with wild yeasts in French oak barrels, where it stays eighteen months, as Section 94 does."}],"traps":["Tasted blind, Section 94's flint and creamy texture can suggest Chardonnay or a blend, yet it is Sauvignon Blanc fermented in barrel.","Section 94 is a single parcel planted in 1992, not a selection of barrels or a numbered cuvée of the house.","Dog Point's founders came from Cloudy Bay, but Dog Point is a separate house with its own vineyard and label."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-1ir0e69z","front":"How is Dog Point Section 94 fermented and aged?","back":"With wild yeasts in older French oak barrels, where it stays eighteen months before bottling without fining."},{"id":"fl-ukjikbf7","front":"What does the name Section 94 record on a Dog Point label?","back":"The parcel's title in an early survey of Marlborough, when the Hawkesbury Run was cut up; its vines were planted in 1992."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-72009xfu","q":"A guest finds Dog Point Section 94 creamy and flinty, unlike the Sauvignon Blanc she knows. Which making explains it?","opts":["Wild yeasts and eighteen months in older French oak","Skin contact for weeks, as for an orange wine","Cool fermentation in steel and early bottling","A blend with a large share of Semillon"],"a":0,"exp":"Section 94 ferments with wild yeasts and rests eighteen months in older barrels, which builds texture; cool steel and early bottling make the classic, pungent style instead.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-twxxp7jb","q":"How did Dog Point, the place that names Dog Point Vineyard, come by its name?","opts":["From a headland on Cloudy Bay shaped like a dog","From shepherds' dogs gone wild, cleared by settlers","From the Māori name of the valley's river","From the dogs of a gold rush camp"],"a":1,"exp":"As the house tells it, boundary dogs lost by shepherds bred into a pack that attacked the flocks and were cleared by settlers; no headland or river gave the name.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Dog Point Vineyard: Our Place","url":"https://dogpoint.co.nz/pages/the-place","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Dog Point Vineyard: Section 94","url":"https://dogpoint.co.nz/products/section-94-2020","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Dog Point Vineyard: The partners","url":"https://www.dogpoint.co.nz/dog-point/meet-our-team/the-partners","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vintus: Dog Point Section 94 Sauvignon Blanc 2019 technical sheet","url":"https://vintus.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Dog-Point-Section-94-Sauvignon-Blanc-1-1.pdf","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Grape Collective: Dog Point, New Zealand Sauvignon Blanc with a quieter voice","url":"https://www.grapecollective.com/dog-point-new-zealand-sauvignon-blanc-with-a-quieter-voice/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-kc3544z1": {"id":"fl-kc3544z1","key":"greywacke","group":"marlborough","region":"marlborough","producer":"Greywacke","producerId":"p-greywacke","say":"GREY-wack-ee","place":"Southern Valleys and central Wairau Plains, Marlborough","founded":"2009","wine":{"name":"Greywacke Wild Sauvignon","appellation":"Marlborough","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Sauvignon Blanc, from several Marlborough vineyards.","sites":"Several vineyards in the Southern Valleys and on the central Wairau Plains, among them sites at Woodbourne, Renwick and Rapaura, the last two on young alluvial soils.","making":"The juice is cold settled and run into mostly old French oak barriques, where wild yeasts ferment it, the tail of the fermentation running on for months. The lees are stirred from time to time and part of the wine goes through malolactic; it leaves the barrels before the next harvest and rests some six months more on its lees before bottling.","glass":"Ripe peach, elderflower and lemon oil with dried mango, honeycomb and shortbread; savoury and textured, with bright acidity and a salty edge like oyster shell.","ageing":"Bottled about eighteen months after the harvest, it can be poured on release, and its savoury depth holds and grows for some years after.","serve":"10 to 12 degrees Celsius; no need to decant, though it opens in the glass; a white Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Greywacke","means":"A hard grey sandstone, the bedrock under much of New Zealand; said GREY-wack-ee."},{"term":"Wild Sauvignon","means":"Sauvignon Blanc fermented in barrel by wild yeasts rather than cultured ones."},{"term":"Marlborough","means":"The Geographical Indication the label names, which holds at least 85 per cent of the wine to Marlborough fruit."}]},"table":"Cloudy Bay's founding winemaker set up Greywacke in 2009, and its Wild Sauvignon is barrel fermented Marlborough Sauvignon: wild yeasts, mostly old French barriques and long lees ageing give peach, honeycomb and a savoury, salty edge. Treat it as you would a white Burgundy.","story":"Greywacke, said GREY-wack-ee, is the name of the hard grey sandstone that underlies much of New Zealand, whose rounded stones lie in the region's riverbeds. Its founder came to Marlborough as the founding winemaker of Cloudy Bay and was in charge of that house's first twenty five vintages from 1985, the years in which Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc became known across the world. At Cloudy Bay, by that house's account, he and a colleague devised Te Koko, a Sauvignon Blanc fermented with wild yeast and aged in barrel, first made in 1996. In 2009 he and his wife set up a label of their own, buying much of its fruit from the vineyards of one of Dog Point's founding families, his former colleagues at Cloudy Bay. Wild Sauvignon follows the same thinking: wild yeasts in mostly old French barriques, malolactic for part of the wine and long ageing on its lees, so that savour and texture stand where the usual pungency would be. The house makes a classic Sauvignon Blanc beside it, and the two poured side by side show what the barrel changes. Wild Sauvignon is proof that Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc is more than one style.","others":[{"name":"Greywacke Sauvignon Blanc","note":"The classic, tank fermented Marlborough bottling, poured beside Wild Sauvignon to show what the barrel changes."},{"name":"Greywacke Pinot Noir","note":"Mostly from the Yarrum Vineyard on the ridge between the Brancott and Ben Morven valleys."}],"traps":["Blind, the texture and muted pungency that barrel and wild yeast give Wild Sauvignon can pass for a white Bordeaux or an oaked Loire Sauvignon.","Greywacke names a house and a rock; Marlborough has no subregion of that name.","Founded in 2009 by Cloudy Bay's founding winemaker, Greywacke is a house of its own and no second label of Cloudy Bay."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-gl0u3g4r","front":"Greywacke: what does the name mean, and how is it said?","back":"A hard grey sandstone, the bedrock under much of New Zealand; said GREY-wack-ee."},{"id":"fl-nir51jjq","front":"Which wine of a Marlborough house founded in 2009 ferments with wild yeasts in mostly old French barriques?","back":"Greywacke Wild Sauvignon, from the house of Cloudy Bay's founding winemaker."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-wzwvj940","q":"What makes Greywacke Wild Sauvignon less pungent and more savoury than most Marlborough Sauvignon?","opts":["A blend with Chardonnay from the Southern Valleys","Fermentation in new American oak with cultured yeast","Grapes from the cool, high Awatere Valley alone","Wild yeasts in old barrels, malolactic and long lees ageing"],"a":3,"exp":"The house ferments with wild yeasts in mostly old French barriques, puts part of the wine through malolactic and keeps it long on lees; the cooler Awatere gives a greener wine, not a savoury one.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-npx0hzsz","q":"Greywacke's founder directed the first twenty five vintages of another Marlborough house before 2009. Which house?","opts":["Brancott Estate","Fromm Winery","Cloudy Bay","Dog Point Vineyard"],"a":2,"exp":"He was Cloudy Bay's winemaker from its first vintage of 1985; Dog Point, founded by Cloudy Bay colleagues, grows much of Greywacke's fruit.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Greywacke: Our Story","url":"https://greywacke.com/docs/GreywackeAbout.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Greywacke: Wild Sauvignon 2024 tasting notes","url":"https://greywacke.com/docs/GreywackeTastingNotes-WildSauvignon.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Dunells: Greywacke, Marlborough, New Zealand","url":"https://www.dunells.com/advice/featured-producers/greywacke-marlborough-new-zealand/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Stuff, the Marlborough Express: Greywacke emerges from the river bed","url":"https://www.stuff.co.nz/marlborough-express/your-marlborough/3122778/Greywacke-emerges-from-the-river-bed","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Cloudy Bay: 40 Years in the Making","url":"https://www.cloudybay.com/en-us/journal/40-years-making/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-dgenhl0a": {"id":"fl-dgenhl0a","key":"fromm","group":"marlborough","region":"marlborough","producer":"Fromm Winery","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Brancott Valley, Southern Valleys, Marlborough","founded":"1992","wine":{"name":"Fromm Clayvin Vineyard Pinot Noir","appellation":"Marlborough","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir, from one vineyard.","sites":"The Clayvin Vineyard, closely planted on north facing slopes at the end of the Brancott Valley, on the heavy clay that gives it its name, and grown organically.","making":"Fermented with indigenous yeasts after a hand harvest, the wine is matured in seasoned French oak barriques for well over a year, then bottled with neither fining nor filtration.","glass":"Dried strawberry, wild raspberry and blackberry with orange peel, sandalwood and a forest floor note; firm, sappy tannin, fresh acidity and a savoury finish that lingers.","ageing":"The house's own drinking windows open about five years after the vintage; before then it is firm, and a good year keeps for fifteen.","serve":"Unfiltered, so decant an old bottle gently off its sediment and a young one for an hour; 15 to 17 degrees Celsius, in a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Clayvin Vineyard","means":"The hillside vineyard at the end of the Brancott Valley, named for its clay soil."},{"term":"Pinot Noir","means":"Named on the label, the variety must make up at least 85 per cent of the wine."},{"term":"Marlborough","means":"A registered Geographical Indication; naming it on the label calls for at least 85 per cent Marlborough fruit."}]},"table":"Fromm, a Swiss founded house that made red wine its focus in Sauvignon country, draws its Clayvin Vineyard Pinot Noir from organic vines on the clay slopes of the Brancott Valley. Bottled unfined and unfiltered, it brings wild raspberry, orange peel and sappy tannin; give it five years.","story":"At Malans in Graubünden, in eastern Switzerland, the Fromm family makes wine, and in the late 1980s a winegrower of that family and a fellow Swiss winemaker resolved to make wine in Marlborough. In 1992, by the house's account, Fromm Winery began with its first harvest, and its founding vineyard was planted that year; in a region turning to Sauvignon Blanc, the house made red grapes its focus, Pinot Noir, Syrah and Malbec. At the end of the Brancott Valley, meanwhile, a viticulturist and his wife had planted Clayvin in the early 1990s, closely spaced on north facing clay slopes, a vineyard often called Marlborough's first commercial hillside planting, and in 1998 they sold it to Fromm. Its Pinot Noir, grown organically, fermented with indigenous yeasts and bottled unfined and unfiltered, became the wine by which the house is known. The founding family returned to Switzerland in 2008, and the estate passed to partners. In 2015 the Marlborough house of Giesen bought the vineyard, and Fromm went on making Clayvin wines from its fruit. What Marlborough's clay hillsides can give Pinot Noir shows in Clayvin: grip and depth from a region better known for Sauvignon Blanc.","others":[{"name":"Fromm Syrah","note":"The house's Syrah, co-fermented with Viognier, one of the red grapes it made its focus."},{"name":"Fromm Chardonnay Clayvin Vineyard","note":"Chardonnay from the same clay slopes of the Brancott Valley."}],"traps":["Clayvin is easily taken for a subregion, but it is one vineyard at the end of the Brancott Valley, one of Marlborough's Southern Valleys.","Giesen also bottles wines named Clayvin from the same vineyard, so a guest must read the house as well as the site.","Fromm Winery is a Marlborough house with Swiss roots, not the Fromm family's own estate at Malans in Switzerland."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-gpme0c1o","front":"On what soil and aspect does Fromm's Clayvin Vineyard Pinot Noir grow?","back":"Heavy clay, which names the vineyard, on north facing slopes at the end of the Brancott Valley."},{"id":"fl-47lcpuc8","front":"Which Marlborough house, founded by Swiss winegrowers in 1992, bought the Clayvin Vineyard in 1998?","back":"Fromm Winery."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-31b83ox8","q":"How does Fromm make its Clayvin Vineyard Pinot Noir?","opts":["Indigenous yeasts, seasoned barriques, no fining or filtering","Fermented in amphora and aged under flor","Carbonic maceration in steel, bottled within months","Cultured yeasts, new American oak, sterile filtration"],"a":0,"exp":"The house ferments with indigenous yeasts, matures the wine in seasoned French barriques and bottles it unfined and unfiltered; it is no carbonic red bottled within months.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-cj5drcg5","q":"Which year brought the Clayvin Vineyard, on the slopes of the Brancott Valley, into Fromm Winery's hands?","opts":["2008","1998","1992","1973"],"a":1,"exp":"Clayvin, planted in the early 1990s, was sold to Fromm in 1998; 1992 is the house's founding, and 2008 the year the founding family went home to Switzerland.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Fromm Winery: Our Story","url":"https://frommwinery.co.nz/pages/our-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Fromm Winery: The Vineyards","url":"https://frommwinery.co.nz/pages/our-vineyard","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Fromm Winery: 2022 Pinot Noir Clayvin Vineyard","url":"https://frommwinery.co.nz/products/fromm-pinot-noir-clayvin-vineyard-2022","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Stuff, the Marlborough Express: Hill planting pioneers to host premium wine tasting","url":"https://www.stuff.co.nz/marlborough-express/your-marlborough/wine/9011636/Hill-planting-pioneers-to-host-premium-wine-tasting","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Giesen: History of Giesen","url":"https://giesen.co.nz/blogs/our-stories/our-story","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-b27bolvg": {"id":"fl-b27bolvg","key":"felton-road","group":"central-otago","region":"central-otago","producer":"Felton Road","producerId":"p-felton-road","say":"","place":"Felton Road, Bannockburn, Central Otago","founded":"1991","wine":{"name":"Felton Road Block 5 Pinot Noir","appellation":"Central Otago","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir alone, from one block of The Elms Vineyard.","sites":"Block 5 of The Elms Vineyard at Bannockburn, planted in 1993 on a gentle north facing slope at the heart of the vineyard whose soils run from heavy loess and silt to clay and angular schist gravel, with old lake bed sediments reaching into its centre.","making":"Destemmed by gravity into the fermenters, with about a fifth kept as whole bunches in a vintage such as 2017, the fruit soaks for more than a week before a wild fermentation with indigenous yeasts, some three weeks on skins in all. It then spends sixteen to seventeen months in French oak barrels, a quarter to about a third of them new, and goes to bottle unfined and unfiltered.","glass":"Dark cherry, raspberry and crushed berries over rose petal, rhubarb, black tea and white pepper; darker and spicier than the house's other Pinot Noirs, with a stony edge, muscular yet fine tannin and a long finish.","ageing":"Tight and muscular when young, it needs four or five years to open and then drinks well for a decade or more.","serve":"Pour at 15 to 17 degrees Celsius into a wide Burgundy glass; an hour or so in a decanter suits a young bottle.","label":[{"term":"Block 5","means":"One block of The Elms Vineyard, planted in 1993 and bottled on its own."},{"term":"Bannockburn","means":"The sub-region of The Elms, on the south bank of the Kawarau, a district of old gold diggings."},{"term":"Central Otago","means":"The registered Geographical Indication of the inland south of New Zealand's South Island."}]},"table":"Planted in 1993 and farmed biodynamically, one north facing block of The Elms Vineyard at Bannockburn gives Felton Road Block 5 Pinot Noir. Darker and spicier than the house's other Pinots and tight when young, it wants four or five years and then a decanter.","story":"In 1990, by New Zealand Winegrowers' account, Felton Road's founder enrolled in the viticulture course at Lincoln University, and in 1991, the year the house dates its beginning to, he chose and laid out a vineyard just off Felton Road at Bannockburn, a district of old gold workings above the Kawarau. The first vines went in in 1992, and Block 5 followed in 1993 in what became The Elms Vineyard. In 1997 a young winemaker who had studied beside the founder at Lincoln made the first official Felton Road Pinot Noir, and in 2000 the founder sold the estate to a British wine lover. The house came to bottle the blocks of The Elms apart, Block 3 and Block 5 above all, and to farm its vineyards biodynamically, certified by Demeter and by BioGro, with wild yeasts in the cellar and no fining or filtering before bottling. Block 5's soils run from loess and silt to clay and schist gravel, with old lake bed sediments at its heart, and its wine is the darker and spicier of the two. The pair is a lesson in how far soil alone can move Pinot Noir within one vineyard.","others":[{"name":"Felton Road Pinot Noir Bannockburn","note":"The estate blend, drawn from the house's vineyards at Bannockburn, The Elms among them."},{"name":"Felton Road Block 3 Pinot Noir","note":"The neighbouring block of The Elms, bottled apart and studied against Block 5."},{"name":"Felton Road Calvert and Cornish Point Pinot Noirs","note":"Single vineyard wines from two of the house's other Bannockburn sites."}],"traps":["Block 3 and Block 5 are blocks of one vineyard, The Elms, not separate estates, and neither is a reserve or a second wine.","Felton Road Pinot Noir Bannockburn is the house's blend of its vineyards; Block 5 is a single block bottled on its own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-d227kf69","front":"Felton Road Block 5: in which vineyard does it grow, and when was the block planted?","back":"The Elms Vineyard at Bannockburn; Block 5 went in in 1993, on a north facing slope."},{"id":"fl-tm43l42y","front":"Which house made its first official Pinot Noir at Bannockburn in 1997, from a vineyard laid out in 1991?","back":"Felton Road, from The Elms Vineyard just off Felton Road."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-pm0qy8gb","q":"What is the difference between Felton Road's Block 3 and Block 5?","opts":["A reserve wine and the house's second wine","Two clones of Pinot Noir from separate valleys","Two blocks of The Elms Vineyard, bottled apart","Two vintages of the same Bannockburn blend"],"a":2,"exp":"Block 3 and Block 5 are neighbouring blocks of The Elms at Bannockburn, each bottled on its own; neither is a reserve or a second wine, and the blend is the Bannockburn bottling.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-tec092ez","q":"Felton Road's founder sold the estate in 2000. Who had made its first official Pinot Noir, in 1997?","opts":["The buyer of 2000, before the sale","A consultant brought over from Burgundy","A contract winery in Marlborough","A fellow student of his at Lincoln University"],"a":3,"exp":"By New Zealand Winegrowers' account the first Felton Road Pinot Noir was made in 1997 by a young winemaker who had studied beside the founder at Lincoln; the buyer came only in 2000.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Felton Road: The Elms Vineyard, the Pinot Noirs from Block 3 and Block 5","url":"https://feltonroad.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/Block-3-Block-5.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Felton Road: 2017 Pinot Noir Block 5 technical sheet","url":"https://feltonroad.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/2018-Felton-Road-Pinot-Noir-Block-5.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Felton Road: 2023 Pinot Noir Block 5","url":"https://feltonroad.com/product/2023-pinot-noir-block-5/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"New Zealand Winegrowers: Felton Road's bold business plan","url":"https://www.nzwine.com/en/media/our-people/felton-road-milestone/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Otago Daily Times: beautifully crafted Pinot Noirs, each their own characteristics","url":"https://www.odt.co.nz/lifestyle/summer-times/beautifully-crafted-pinot-noirs-each-their-own-characteristics","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-mb6uqugt": {"id":"fl-mb6uqugt","key":"rippon","group":"central-otago","region":"central-otago","producer":"Rippon","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Roy's Bay, Lake Wanaka, Central Otago","founded":"1982","wine":{"name":"Rippon Mature Vine Pinot Noir","appellation":"Central Otago","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Only Pinot Noir, from every fully grown vine of it on the estate.","sites":"The north facing escarpment on the western edge of Roy's Bay, Lake Wanaka, where glacial terminal moraines meet an ancient cone of coarse schist gravels; by the house's account its blocks include some of the oldest commercial vines in Central Otago.","making":"The fruit is picked by hand and fermented with wild yeasts; the wine then spends two winters in barrel, about eighteen months, with only a small share of new oak, and two years in bottle before it is released. It goes to bottle without fining or filtering.","glass":"Wild strawberry, dark cherry and plum over blueberry, mountain herbs and a note of mushroomy undergrowth, with crushed rock beneath; juicy and elegant rather than heavy, with fine, chalky tannin and a long, perfumed finish.","ageing":"It leaves the estate with years in bottle already, open and perfumed, and a strong vintage keeps and deepens for ten years and longer.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; no need to decant, though a young bottle opens in a carafe; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Mature Vine","means":"Drawn from all the estate's fully grown Pinot Noir vines, the younger ones left out."},{"term":"Lake Wanaka","means":"The lake below the estate, which names Wanaka, a sub-region of Central Otago north of Queenstown."},{"term":"Central Otago","means":"One registered Geographical Indication spans every sub-region of the inland south, Wanaka among them."}]},"table":"Above Lake Wanaka, where the family planted trial vines in 1975, every fully grown vine on schist and glacial gravel gives its fruit to Rippon Mature Vine Pinot Noir. It rests two winters in barrel and two years in bottle before release, and pours fine and perfumed.","story":"The land at Roy's Bay on Lake Wanaka, part of the Wanaka Station the founder's grandfather had bought, has been farmed by his family since 1912, by the estate's own account. The founder had thought of growing grapes there since the Second World War, in which he served in submarines, and in 1973 he and his wife moved back to the family farm. In 1975 he planted a trial of some thirty grape varieties beside the lake, among the first vines of modern Central Otago, and in 1982, against the doubts of research bodies and local experts, he planted the first commercial block, the year from which the house dates its winegrowing. He became the founding president of the region's winegrowers' association. After his death his widow carried on the business they had founded, and their son went on to lead the vineyard and the cellar. The estate is farmed biodynamically. Mature Vine gathers all of its fully grown Pinot Noir and Jeunesse the younger vines, while two single blocks, Tinker's Field and Emma's Block, are bottled apart. The modern region's story goes back through this Pinot Noir to its trials of the 1970s, on a lakeside farm where the vines began as an experiment.","others":[{"name":"Rippon Jeunesse Young Vine Pinot Noir","note":"Pinot Noir from the estate's younger vines, not yet held mature enough to go into Mature Vine."},{"name":"Rippon Tinker's Field Mature Vine Pinot Noir","note":"From the block that holds the oldest vines on the property, bottled on its own; Emma's Block is the estate's other single block."},{"name":"Rippon Mature Vine Riesling","note":"Riesling from the same lakeside slope, the estate's other grape of note."}],"traps":["Reading Mature Vine as one block of old vines is a mistake: it takes in all the estate's fully grown Pinot Noir, while Tinker's Field and Emma's Block are the single blocks.","Rippon's trial vines of 1975 came before the region's first commercial vintage of 1987, but its first commercial block was planted only in 1982.","Wanaka is a sub-region of its own, some eighty kilometres north of Queenstown, cooler and slightly wetter than the Cromwell Basin, and no part of Gibbston."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-895n8eba","front":"What did Rippon's founder plant beside Lake Wanaka in 1975?","back":"A trial of some thirty grape varieties; the first commercial block followed in 1982."},{"id":"fl-jhz41xxv","front":"Before release, how long is Rippon Mature Vine Pinot Noir held?","back":"Two winters in barrel, about eighteen months, then two more years in bottle."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-c6xirkwg","q":"What do the words Mature Vine on Rippon's Pinot Noir tell a guest?","opts":["It is drawn from all the estate's fully grown vines","It was bought from older vineyards around Wanaka","It was aged for a decade before release","It comes from one block of the oldest vines"],"a":0,"exp":"Mature Vine gathers every fully grown Pinot Noir vine of the estate, the younger going to Jeunesse; Tinker's Field, the oldest vines' block, is bottled apart, and release comes after some three and a half years, not ten.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-82rt3g8r","q":"Rippon's founder served in the Second World War before he planted beside Lake Wanaka. Which year saw his trial vines go in?","opts":["1912","1975","1987","1982"],"a":1,"exp":"By the house's account the trial of some thirty varieties went in in 1975; 1912 is when the family began farming the land, 1982 the first commercial block and 1987 the region's first commercial vintage.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Rippon: Our History","url":"https://rippon.co.nz/pages/our-history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Rippon: Mature Vine Pinot Noir","url":"https://rippon.co.nz/products/rippon-mature-vine-pinot-noir","kind":"primary"},{"title":"New Zealand Winegrowers: Rippon, our people","url":"https://www.nzwine.com/en/media/our-people/rippon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"World of Fine Wine: Rippon Pinot Noir 1991 to 2021","url":"https://worldoffinewine.com/news-features/rippon-pinot-noir-1991-2021","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Flatiron Wines: Rippon Mature Vine Pinot Noir 2021","url":"https://nyc.flatiron-wines.com/products/rippon-mature-vine-pinot-noir-2021-750ml","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-39e8vj63": {"id":"fl-39e8vj63","key":"gibbston-valley","group":"central-otago","region":"central-otago","producer":"Gibbston Valley","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Gibbston, Kawarau Gorge, Central Otago","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Gibbston Valley Reserve Pinot Noir","appellation":"Central Otago","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir, grown in the house's own vineyards.","sites":"A few low yielding rows chosen in a strong vintage from the house's vineyards: the Home Block at Gibbston in the Kawarau Gorge, planted in 1983, and its sites at Bendigo, among them China Terrace and the higher School House.","making":"As the house tells it, a Reserve takes only a few rows of fruit in a great vintage and is made in small quantities, apart from its other Pinot Noirs. The house ages its Pinot Noir in barrel in the cave blasted into the schist behind the winery in 1995.","glass":"Blackberry, black cherry and dark plum with violets and anise, and cedar and toast from the barrel; deep, layered and long, the fruit dark rather than red.","ageing":"Generous from the start, it gains depth over several years in bottle in a strong vintage.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius in a wide Burgundy glass; decanting a young bottle for an hour is optional.","label":[{"term":"Reserve","means":"The house's selection of a few rows of fruit in a strong vintage, made in small quantities."},{"term":"Gibbston Valley","means":"The house's name, from Gibbston in the Kawarau Gorge, the highest sub-region of Central Otago, where the vines ripen late."},{"term":"Central Otago","means":"The region's registered Geographical Indication; this house gave the region its first commercial vintage, in 1987."}]},"table":"Gibbston Valley released Central Otago's first commercial vintage in 1987, and its Reserve Pinot Noir is the house's pick of a few rows in a strong year. Its 2000 vintage took the Pinot Noir trophy at the International Wine Challenge in London in 2001.","story":"Gibbston lies in the Kawarau Gorge between Queenstown and Cromwell, the highest of Central Otago's sub-regions, where the vines ripen later than in neighbouring districts. The house's founder planted his first vines there in the early 1980s, and in 1983, by the house's account, he planted its first commercial vineyard, the Home Block. In 1987 Gibbston Valley released the region's first commercial vintage, a date New Zealand Winegrowers gives for Gibbston's first commercial bottling. In 1990, in the house's own history, the founder established Gibbston Valley Wines as a boutique winery with a tasting room and a restaurant, and by 1995 the barrels had outgrown their store; rather than build another, he had a cave blasted into the schist mountainside behind the winery, New Zealand's largest wine cave by the house's own account. The house came to grow at Bendigo too, on the warmer terraces by Lake Dunstan, where China Terrace and School House give its single vineyard wines. The Reserve is its selection of a few rows in a strong year, and the Reserve Pinot Noir of 2000 won the Pinot Noir trophy at the International Wine Challenge in London in 2001. The Reserve carries three moments of the house's early history: the region's first commercial vintage, the cave and a London trophy.","others":[{"name":"Gibbston Valley School House Pinot Noir","note":"Single vineyard Pinot Noir from the School House vineyard at Bendigo, some 350 to 420 metres above the sea."},{"name":"Gibbston Valley China Terrace Pinot Noir","note":"From the China Terrace site at Bendigo, about 320 metres above the sea, bottled as a single vineyard Pinot Noir."}],"traps":["Gibbston Valley is a house; Gibbston is the sub-region in the Kawarau Gorge, and other houses make Gibbston wine too.","Gibbston Valley's School House and China Terrace wines come from its vineyards at Bendigo, not from Gibbston, whatever the house's name suggests."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-ebixcbv2","front":"What did Gibbston Valley do in 1995 when its barrels outgrew their store?","back":"It blasted a cave into the schist mountainside behind the winery, New Zealand's largest wine cave by its own account."},{"id":"fl-q2atrbmb","front":"Which Gibbston Valley wine took the Pinot Noir trophy at the International Wine Challenge in London?","back":"The Reserve Pinot Noir of the 2000 vintage, at the Challenge of 2001."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-y4rgnaub","q":"What must fruit be to go into Gibbston Valley's Reserve Pinot Noir, as the house tells it?","opts":["Grapes bought from growers across Otago","Every vine of the original Home Block","A few chosen rows in a strong vintage","The free run juice of every vintage"],"a":2,"exp":"The house says a Reserve takes only a few rows of fruit in a great vintage, made in small quantities; it is neither the whole Home Block nor bought fruit.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-b8u39pn0","q":"Gibbston Valley released Central Otago's first commercial vintage. Which year was it?","opts":["1995","1990","1983","1987"],"a":3,"exp":"The first commercial vintage was 1987; 1983 is the Home Block's planting, 1990 the opening of the boutique winery with its restaurant and 1995 the cave.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Gibbston Valley: Our History","url":"https://www.gibbstonvalley.com/us/our-story/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Gibbston Valley: Wine Ranges","url":"https://www.gibbstonvalley.com/us/our-story/our-wine/wine-ranges/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Gibbston Valley: Vineyards","url":"https://www.gibbstonvalley.com/our-story/our-wine/vineyards/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"New Zealand Winegrowers: New Zealand Wine, a guide to the regions and varieties","url":"https://www.nzwine.com/media/gjcpxwb5/new-zealand-wine-textbook.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Olivers Wines: the London International Wine Challenge results","url":"https://olivers-wines.com.au/?p=63794","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-lsqbqurw": {"id":"fl-lsqbqurw","key":"mt-difficulty","group":"central-otago","region":"central-otago","producer":"Mt Difficulty","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Bannockburn, Central Otago","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Mt Difficulty Bannockburn Pinot Noir","appellation":"Central Otago","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinot Noir only, several clones of it blended across the house's Bannockburn vineyards.","sites":"Estate vineyards and vineyards the winery manages on the south side of the Kawarau at Bannockburn, the earlier plantings above all, on soils that run from open gravels to heavier clays.","making":"Part of the fruit goes into the ferments as whole bunches, from about a tenth to a fifth or more by vintage. The wine then rests ten to twelve months in barrel on its full lees, about a fifth of the barrels new in vintages such as 2021 and 2022, with the malolactic fermentation in the spring; it is racked in late autumn and filtered, but not fined, before bottling.","glass":"Red and black cherry, raspberry and blood plum over blueberry and orange peel; fuller and darker than the cooler sub-regions give, with a silky texture, firm, fine grained tannin and bold acidity.","ageing":"Open it on release, or give a strong vintage some seven years and more to deepen.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a wide Burgundy glass.","label":[{"term":"Bannockburn","means":"A sub-region with its own registered Geographical Indication since 2022, where the wine's vineyards lie."},{"term":"Central Otago","means":"The wider registered Geographical Indication, inside which Bannockburn lies."}]},"table":"In 1998 a group of Bannockburn growers began making wine under one label, and Mt Difficulty Bannockburn Pinot Noir blends the house's vineyards on gravel and clay south of the Kawarau. Bannockburn's warmth shows in its cherry and blood plum and its silky, firm tannin.","story":"Bannockburn's terraces on the south side of the Kawarau were worked for gold in the nineteenth century, and its vineyards came to share the slopes with the old sluicings. In the 1990s the owners of a handful of newly planted vineyards there, who came to be known as the Gang of Four, chose to make wine together under one label rather than each alone. Mt Difficulty's first vintage was 1998. The growers' handshake held until 2004, when the company Mt Difficulty Wines was formed and most of the founding vineyards passed into it. The house went on to bottle some of its vineyards apart: Pipeclay Terrace, from vines planted in 1996 on coarse soils and heavy clay, is released only in strong vintages, and Mansons Farm is made in small quantities. A second label, Roaring Meg, came to stand beside the first. In 2022 Bannockburn became a registered Geographical Indication of its own, within Central Otago. In one bottle the house maps the sub-region: fruit from several vineyards on gravel and clay, blended to show the place rather than one site.","others":[{"name":"Roaring Meg Pinot Noir","note":"Mt Difficulty's second label, drawn from vineyards beyond Bannockburn too, and named for Roaring Meg, a stream of the Kawarau Gorge."},{"name":"Mt Difficulty Pipeclay Terrace Pinot Noir","note":"From vines planted in 1996 on coarse Bannockburn soils and heavy Scotland Point clay, released only in strong vintages."},{"name":"Mt Difficulty Mansons Farm Pinot Noir","note":"A single vineyard Pinot Noir of the house, made in small quantities."}],"traps":["Pipeclay Terrace and Mansons Farm are Mt Difficulty's single vineyard wines; the Bannockburn Pinot Noir blends several Bannockburn vineyards.","Roaring Meg is a second label of the house, not a separate winery or a sub-region of Central Otago."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-jf0riv1e","front":"Mt Difficulty: which vintage was its first, and how did the house begin?","back":"1998, when a group of Bannockburn growers chose to make wine together under one label."},{"id":"fl-4ofxaz77","front":"How long does Mt Difficulty Bannockburn Pinot Noir spend in barrel, and on what?","back":"About a year on its full lees, through the malolactic fermentation in spring; filtered but not fined."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-zfktemct","q":"From what ground does Mt Difficulty draw its Bannockburn Pinot Noir?","opts":["Terraces on the east bank of Lake Dunstan","Vineyards south of the Kawarau, on gravel and clay","High slopes in the Gibbston gorge","A single lakeside slope at Wanaka"],"a":1,"exp":"The house draws on estate and managed vineyards on the south side of the Kawarau at Bannockburn, open gravels to heavier clays; the east bank of Lake Dunstan is Bendigo, a different sub-region.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-kp5hlbov","q":"Mt Difficulty's founding growers made their first wine together from the 1998 vintage. When was the company formally formed?","opts":["2004","1996","2022","2001"],"a":0,"exp":"The growers' handshake held until 2004, when Mt Difficulty Wines was formed; 1996 is the planting of Pipeclay Terrace's vines and 2022 the year Bannockburn became a registered Geographical Indication.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Mt Difficulty: Bannockburn Pinot Noir 2018 fact sheet","url":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1453/4032/files/Mt_Difficulty_Bannockburn_Pinot_Noir_2018_Fact_Sheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Mt Difficulty: Pinot Noir 2016 fact sheet","url":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1453/4032/files/Mt_Difficulty_Pinot_Noir_2016.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Mt Difficulty: Pipeclay Terrace Pinot Noir 2018 fact sheet","url":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/1042/9896/files/Mt_Difficulty_2018_Pipeclay_Terr_PN_Fact_Sheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Otago Daily Times: wine tourism means further expansion","url":"https://www.odt.co.nz/otago/central-otago/wine-tourism-means-further-expansion-l5p4vw6o","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Supermarket News: NWWA Top 50, Roaring Meg","url":"https://supermarketnews.co.nz/features/nwwa-top-50-roaring-meg/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-q8m2s6dt": {"id":"fl-q8m2s6dt","key":"quartz-reef","group":"central-otago","region":"central-otago","producer":"Quartz Reef","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Bendigo Station, Bendigo, Central Otago","founded":"1996","wine":{"name":"Quartz Reef Bendigo Estate Pinot Noir","appellation":"Central Otago","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"All Pinot Noir, grown on the house's own estate at Bendigo.","sites":"The Bendigo Estate vineyard, first planted in 1998 on a north facing slope above the east bank of Lake Dunstan, on arid clay, fine gravel and quartz over the reef that names the house; certified biodynamic by Demeter since 2011.","making":"Hand picked and destemmed, the fruit is cold soaked for some days, fermented with indigenous yeasts and left on its skins after fermentation, about three weeks in all in vintages such as 2014 and 2021. It then spends twelve to fourteen months in French oak barriques, from about a sixth to a half of them new by vintage, and the 2017 went to bottle unfined and unfiltered.","glass":"Black cherry and ripe plum over violet and a hint of cedar, deep in colour and very savoury; weighty and dense, as the warm Bendigo slope makes it, with fine tannin and a long finish.","ageing":"Firm and youthful at first, it opens a few years after the vintage and keeps for a decade in a strong year.","serve":"A wide Burgundy glass, 15 to 17 degrees Celsius, and an hour's decanting for a young bottle.","label":[{"term":"Bendigo Estate","means":"The house's own vineyard at Bendigo Station, first planted in 1998."},{"term":"Bendigo","means":"The sub-region on the east bank of Lake Dunstan, by New Zealand Winegrowers' account perhaps the warmest in Central Otago."},{"term":"Central Otago","means":"A registered Geographical Indication lying at about 45 degrees south, inland in the South Island."}]},"table":"Quartz Reef is named for the reef beneath its warm, north facing slope at Bendigo, first planted in 1998 and farmed biodynamically, where its Bendigo Estate Pinot Noir grows. The house is known as well for its sparkling wine; this is black cherry, plum and violet, dense yet savoury.","story":"By the house's account, an Austrian born winemaker first looked at the slopes of Bendigo, on the east bank of Lake Dunstan, in 1990, and in 1996 founded Quartz Reef with partners, among them the farmer of Bendigo Station. The house took its name from the quartz reef that runs beneath the vineyard, by its own account the largest quartz deposit in New Zealand. The first vines went into Bendigo Station in 1998, and the first harvest came in 2001. Beside its Pinot Noir the house made sparkling wine by the traditional method, with a second fermentation in bottle, and became known for both. In 2007 it began to convert the vineyard to biodynamic farming, and in 2011 it gained Demeter certification, with its winery certified organic by BioGro. The house also bottles one chosen parcel ferment of a vintage on its own, as its Single Ferment Pinot Noir, beside the Bendigo Estate Pinot Noir. For a sommelier it marks the warm end of Central Otago: dark, dense Pinot Noir from a slope that faces the sun.","others":[{"name":"Quartz Reef Méthode Traditionnelle","note":"The house's sparkling wine, made by a second fermentation in bottle, as Champagne is."},{"name":"Quartz Reef Bendigo Estate Single Ferment Pinot Noir","note":"The one parcel ferment of a vintage that the house chooses to bottle on its own, from a single block of the estate."}],"traps":["Bendigo on a Quartz Reef label is the Central Otago sub-region in New Zealand, never the gold town of Bendigo in Victoria, Australia.","Quartz Reef's sparkling wine is made by the traditional method, a second fermentation in bottle, not in tank."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-76ubbjbg","front":"What lies beneath the vineyard at Bendigo Station, and what did it give the house that farms it?","back":"A quartz reef, New Zealand's largest quartz deposit by the house's account; it gave Quartz Reef its name."},{"id":"fl-pzkn26tf","front":"Quartz Reef: when did its biodynamic conversion begin, and when did Demeter certify it?","back":"Conversion began in 2007, and Demeter certification followed in 2011."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-mjcmc63d","q":"Which soils does Quartz Reef name for the north facing slope of its Bendigo Estate vineyard?","opts":["Deep loess over lake bed silt","Glacial moraine and coarse schist gravel","Volcanic ash over limestone","Arid clay, fine gravel and quartz"],"a":3,"exp":"The house describes its Bendigo slope as arid clay, fine gravel and quartz; moraine and coarse schist gravel describe Rippon's slope above Lake Wanaka instead.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-htitthfb","q":"Quartz Reef was founded in 1996. In which year did the first vines go into Bendigo Station?","opts":["1996","2001","1998","2007"],"a":2,"exp":"The first vines went in in 1998 and gave a first harvest in 2001; 1996 is the founding and 2007 the start of the biodynamic conversion.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Quartz Reef: Our History","url":"https://www.quartzreef.co.nz/pages/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quartz Reef: Vineyard","url":"https://www.quartzreef.co.nz/pages/vineyard","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quartz Reef: Pinot Noir","url":"https://www.quartzreef.co.nz/collections/pinot-noir","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Quartz Reef: Bendigo Estate Pinot Noir 2017 review","url":"https://www.quartzreef.co.nz/blogs/wine-reviews/bendigo-estate-pinot-noir-2017-5-stars","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Glengarry Wines: Quartz Reef","url":"https://www.glengarrywines.co.nz/mobile/brands/quartz%20reef","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3hmbfgnu": {"id":"fl-3hmbfgnu","key":"kanonkop","group":"stellenbosch","region":"stellenbosch","producer":"Kanonkop","producerId":"p-kanonkop","say":"kuh-NON-kop, POWL SOW-er","place":"Simonsberg-Stellenbosch ward, Stellenbosch district, Western Cape","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Kanonkop Paul Sauer","appellation":"Simonsberg-Stellenbosch","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, from about three fifths to three quarters by year, with Cabernet Franc and Merlot making up the rest, their parts moving too.","sites":"Kanonkop's own vineyards on the lower slopes of the Simonsberg, in decomposed granite.","making":"Fermented in open concrete fermenters, the cap of skins punched down by hand every two hours through the ferment, as the house describes it. The wine then matures for about two years in new French oak barriques, Nevers oak in the house's recent notes, before it is bottled.","glass":"Cassis, plum and sour cherry over cedar, cigar box and tobacco leaf; full and firm, with a dense, fine tannin that takes years to soften and a long, dry finish.","ageing":"Firm and closed in its youth, it is made to be kept: a strong vintage needs a decade or more in bottle before it opens.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Paul Sauer","means":"Honours the cabinet minister who owned the farm and died in 1976."},{"term":"Simonsberg-Stellenbosch","means":"The ward on the Simonsberg's lower slopes within the Stellenbosch district; every grape must have grown in it."},{"term":"Wine of Origin","means":"A numbered seal on the bottle's neck that certifies origin, vintage and variety."}]},"table":"Named for the cabinet minister who owned the farm, Kanonkop's Paul Sauer is a Cabernet led Bordeaux blend from the Simonsberg, first made in 1981. Open concrete tanks for the ferment and two years in new French oak leave it firm and cedary, so decant it.","story":"The house traces its name to a hillock on the Simonsberg above the farm, where in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries a cannon was fired to announce sailing ships entering Table Bay. After the First World War the farm passed to Paul Sauer (1898 to 1976), son of a Cape cabinet minister and himself a minister in the National Party governments from 1948 into the 1960s. In 1973 his son-in-law and the estate's winemaker bottled the first wines under the Kanonkop label, Cabernet Sauvignon and Pinotage among them; Sauer, who had long sold the farm's grapes in bulk to the Stellenbosch Farmers' Winery, was uneasy at first, by the house's account. He died in 1976, and the farm passed to his daughter, who had married into the Krige family. With the 1981 vintage the estate made its first Bordeaux blend and gave it his name: Paul Sauer, Cabernet Sauvignon with Cabernet Franc and Merlot, fermented in open concrete tanks with the cap punched down by hand and aged in new French oak. Though the house is known above all for Pinotage, Paul Sauer holds none of the grape. On a list it is the Stellenbosch Cabernet blend to name first, and the proof that Kanonkop is more than its Pinotage.","others":[{"name":"Kanonkop Black Label Pinotage","note":"Pinotage from bush vines planted in 1953, the house's case that the grape can age."},{"name":"Kanonkop Pinotage","note":"Pinotage from Kanonkop's own vineyards, among the first wines bottled under its label in 1973."},{"name":"Kadette","note":"The house's everyday range, its red a Cape Blend built on Pinotage."}],"traps":["Paul Sauer holds no Pinotage, so it is a Bordeaux blend and not a Cape Blend, though Kanonkop is known above all for Pinotage.","Paul Sauer the wine was first made in 1981, five years after the death of Paul Sauer the man in 1976.","Simonsberg-Stellenbosch is a ward within the Stellenbosch district, not a district of its own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-3g7ebhqp","front":"In what vessels is Kanonkop Paul Sauer fermented, and how is the cap of skins handled?","back":"Open concrete fermenters, with the cap punched down by hand every two hours."},{"id":"fl-dotk6q4c","front":"Which Stellenbosch Bordeaux blend, first made in 1981, honours a cabinet minister who owned the farm?","back":"Kanonkop Paul Sauer, named for Paul Sauer (1898 to 1976)."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-4b3exj58","q":"A guest asks whether Kanonkop Paul Sauer is a Cape Blend. Which grapes are in it?","opts":["Pinotage alone from old bush vines","Cabernet Sauvignon with Cabernet Franc and Merlot","Shiraz with Cinsaut and Grenache","Pinotage with Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot"],"a":1,"exp":"Paul Sauer is a Bordeaux blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon with no Pinotage, so it is no Cape Blend; Pinotage with Cabernet and Merlot would make one.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ckxb0gsr","q":"What did Kanonkop begin to do in 1973 that changed how its wine reached drinkers?","opts":["Bottle its wine under the Kanonkop label","Buy Pinotage grapes from Swartland growers","Make sparkling wine fermented in bottle","Sell its whole crop in bulk to merchants"],"a":0,"exp":"In 1973 Paul Sauer's son-in-law and the winemaker bottled the first wines under the estate's own label, Cabernet and Pinotage among them; selling in bulk is what the change moved away from.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Kanonkop: Our Story","url":"https://kanonkop.co.za/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Kanonkop: Paul Sauer 2021 factsheet","url":"https://kanonkop.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Kanonkop_Paul_Sauer_2021_Factsheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Paul Sauer","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Sauer","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Club Oenologique: how Kanonkop became one of South Africa's leading wine estates","url":"https://cluboenologique.com/story/how-kanonkop-became-one-of-south-africas-leading-wine-estates/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wines of South Africa: Wine of Origin scheme","url":"https://www.wosa.co.za/The-Industry/Wines-Of-Origin/Wine-Of-Origin-scheme/","kind":"primary"}]},
"fl-u5icqri0": {"id":"fl-u5icqri0","key":"meerlust","group":"stellenbosch","region":"stellenbosch","producer":"Meerlust","producerId":"p-meerlust","say":"MEER-lust","place":"Southern Stellenbosch near False Bay, Stellenbosch district, Western Cape","founded":"1693","wine":{"name":"Meerlust Rubicon","appellation":"Stellenbosch","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon leads, from under half to about seven tenths of the blend by year, with Merlot, Cabernet Franc and, in later vintages, a little Petit Verdot.","sites":"The estate's own vineyards in the cool south of Stellenbosch near False Bay: weathered granite on the warm Compagniesdrift hill, clay of an old estuary on the lowlands, shale at the Quarry, sand and river stones on the terraces.","making":"Each parcel of each variety is fermented apart, with pump overs and délestage, and goes through malolactic fermentation in 300 litre French oak barrels, mostly Nevers and more than half new in the vintages whose sheets give a share. After about eight months the components are blended and returned to barrel, for sixteen to twenty one months in all; the wine then rests in bottle before release.","glass":"Violet, ripe plum, blackberry and cassis over cedar, fennel and liquorice; restrained rather than heavy, with fine, firm tannin and a dry finish that, with age, turns towards tobacco leaf and cigar box.","ageing":"Opens after some years in bottle and develops for a decade and more, longer in a strong vintage.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, in a Bordeaux glass; an hour in a decanter suits a young bottle, while an old one is decanted gently off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Rubicon","means":"Named for crossing the Rubicon, a step with no turning back."},{"term":"Stellenbosch","means":"A district under Wine of Origin, and all the bottle's grapes grew inside it."},{"term":"Wine of Origin","means":"Certification, since 1973, of the origin, vintage and variety the label claims, with a seal on the neck."}]},"table":"The Myburgh family bought Meerlust, near False Bay, in 1756, and its Rubicon, from the 1980 vintage, is a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot and Cabernet Franc. Violet, plum and cedar come with restraint rather than weight, and it rewards years in bottle.","story":"In 1693, the estate records, a German settler took up the farm and named it Meerlust, the pleasure of the sea, since by the estate's account the homestead stands on a granite outcrop five kilometres from False Bay. In 1756 the Myburgh family bought it, eight generations by their own count. The front of the homestead was completed in 1776, and the farmstead has since been declared a national monument. For more than two centuries Meerlust sold its grapes and wine in bulk; the seventh generation of the family turned it towards bottling under the estate's own name, beginning with a Cabernet Sauvignon in the 1970s. Drawn to Bordeaux, he and his winemaker set out to blend Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot and Cabernet Franc in the Médoc manner, and the 1980 vintage became Rubicon, among the first Bordeaux blends of the modern Cape. The poet D. J. Opperman (1914 to 1985), a friend and a professor at the University of Stellenbosch, suggested the name, in the estate's telling: like crossing the Rubicon, the decision could not be undone. At his death in 1988 his son, the eighth generation, took over. Rubicon came in the first generation of Cape Bordeaux blends, and it is a wine made to be kept.","others":[{"name":"Meerlust Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Single variety Cabernet, the wine with which bottling under the estate's own name began in the 1970s."},{"name":"Meerlust Chardonnay","note":"A taut, restrained Chardonnay from the estate's vineyards near False Bay."},{"name":"Meerlust Pinot Noir","note":"Pinot Noir from the estate's cooler blocks near the sea, an unusual grape for Stellenbosch."}],"traps":["Meerlust lies in the cool south of Stellenbosch near False Bay, not on the warm slopes of the Simonsberg.","A Cape Blend needs Pinotage; Rubicon, led by Cabernet Sauvignon, has none and is a Bordeaux blend.","The Myburgh family bought Meerlust in 1756; the farm itself goes back to 1693."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8qe65caf","front":"Meerlust Rubicon: what does the name stand for, and who suggested it?","back":"Crossing the Rubicon, a step with no turning back; the poet D. J. Opperman, a family friend at Stellenbosch University, proposed it."},{"id":"fl-0r5lygzp","front":"A Stellenbosch estate bought by one family in 1756 first made its Bordeaux blend in 1980: which is it?","back":"Meerlust, the Myburgh family's estate near False Bay; the blend is Rubicon."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-94b8hqcq","q":"Meerlust Rubicon, the estate's blend of 1980, is built from which grapes?","opts":["Pinotage with Cabernet Sauvignon and Shiraz from bush vines","Merlot alone from the clay of an old estuary","Shiraz with Grenache and Mourvèdre in the Rhône way","Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot, Cabernet Franc and Petit Verdot"],"a":3,"exp":"Rubicon is a Bordeaux blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot, Cabernet Franc and, in recent years, a little Petit Verdot; with Pinotage it would be a Cape Blend.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-kp3b3jcx","q":"Meerlust's Bordeaux blend, Rubicon, was first made in which vintage?","opts":["1973","1993","1980","1756"],"a":2,"exp":"Rubicon's first vintage is 1980; 1756 is the year the Myburgh family bought the farm, and 1973 the year the Wine of Origin scheme began.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Meerlust: Our Story","url":"https://www.meerlust.co.za/our-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Meerlust: Meerlust Rubicon","url":"https://www.meerlust.co.za/our-wines/meerlust-rubicon/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Meerlust: Rubicon 2023 technical sheet","url":"https://meerlust.co.za/wp-content/uploads/Rubicon-2023.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine.co.za: Meerlust celebrates 50 years of Estate wine production","url":"https://news.wine.co.za/news.aspx?NEWSID=45960","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: D. J. Opperman","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D._J._Opperman","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-wof5shdq": {"id":"fl-wof5shdq","key":"rustenberg","group":"stellenbosch","region":"stellenbosch","producer":"Rustenberg","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Just outside Stellenbosch town, Simonsberg-Stellenbosch ward, Western Cape","founded":"1682","wine":{"name":"Rustenberg John X Merriman","appellation":"Simonsberg-Stellenbosch","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot lead, their balance shifting from year to year, with smaller parts of Petit Verdot and Malbec and, in some earlier years, Cabernet Franc.","sites":"Decomposed granite on the Simonsberg slopes above Stellenbosch, in the estate's own vineyards and no others.","making":"Stainless steel tanks take the ferment, with about a week of extended maceration on the skins. Ageing follows in French oak barriques, around a third of them new and the rest second and third fill, for about twenty months before bottling.","glass":"Sour cherry and blackcurrant with cigar tobacco over fine grained tannin; with age the tannin softens and the palate turns rounder and more savoury.","ageing":"Worth decanting in its youth; the house gives it ten to fifteen years from the vintage in a good cellar.","serve":"Serve at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, decanting a young bottle an hour ahead and an old one off its sediment with care; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"John X Merriman","means":"The blend's name, after the Cape prime minister who farmed Schoongezicht, the other half of the estate, from 1892."},{"term":"Simonsberg-Stellenbosch","means":"A ward of the Stellenbosch district on the Simonsberg's lower slopes, where the estate lies."},{"term":"Rustenberg","means":"The farm granted in 1682 and divided in 1810, whose name the estate carries."}]},"table":"Granted in 1682, the Simonsberg farm of Rustenberg makes John X Merriman, a Bordeaux blend that carries the name of the Cape prime minister who farmed Schoongezicht from 1892. Cabernet and Merlot from decomposed granite give cassis, sour cherry and cigar tobacco.","story":"A grant of 1682 to a German settler from near the Rhine began the farm, by the estate's history. In 1810 an owner divided it into two farms, Rustenberg and Schoongezicht. Phylloxera struck the Cape's vineyards in the late nineteenth century, and in 1892 John X Merriman (1841 to 1926), the English born politician who would be the last prime minister of the Cape Colony, from 1908 to 1910, took on Schoongezicht, while his brother-in-law took Rustenberg; together they replanted on resistant rootstock. Wine has been bottled at the estate without a break since 1892, on its own record. In 1941 the Barlow family bought Rustenberg, and later added Schoongezicht, joining the two farms again after more than a century apart. The estate's Bordeaux blend carries Merriman's name and draws only on its own vineyards; beside it the single vineyard Peter Barlow Cabernet honours the buyer of 1941. A bottle of John X Merriman ties a Simonsberg red to a Cape prime minister, and to an estate that has bottled its own wine for well over a century.","others":[{"name":"Rustenberg Peter Barlow","note":"Cabernet Sauvignon from a single vineyard on the estate, named for the man who bought Rustenberg in 1941."},{"name":"Rustenberg Five Soldiers","note":"A Chardonnay from one Simonsberg block, named for a row of five stone pines on the hill above it."}],"traps":["John X Merriman is a Bordeaux blend with Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot leading, not a single variety Cabernet; Peter Barlow is the estate's Cabernet.","Merriman farmed Schoongezicht, the other half of the divided farm; in 1892 Rustenberg went to his brother-in-law."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-hrg662fs","front":"On which mountain, and in what soil, do the vines of Rustenberg John X Merriman grow?","back":"Decomposed granite on the slopes of the Simonsberg, in the Simonsberg-Stellenbosch ward."},{"id":"fl-p2limtap","front":"Which Cape prime minister of 1908 to 1910 took on the Schoongezicht farm in 1892?","back":"John X Merriman (1841 to 1926), whose name Rustenberg's Bordeaux blend carries."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-sugi7o95","q":"In what, and for how long, does Rustenberg mature John X Merriman before bottling?","opts":["About twenty months in French barriques, a third new","Eight months in stainless steel with no oak","A year in large old Slavonian oak casks","Two years in new American oak barrels"],"a":0,"exp":"The house's notes give about twenty months in French barriques, around a third of them new and the rest second and third fill; American oak and large casks belong to other cellars.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ozxblrnu","q":"Rustenberg and Schoongezicht were joined again under the Barlow family. When had the two farms been divided?","opts":["1941","1810","1892","1682"],"a":1,"exp":"The farm was split into Rustenberg and Schoongezicht in 1810, by the estate's account; 1682 is the first grant, 1892 Merriman's arrival and 1941 the Barlow purchase of Rustenberg.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Rustenberg: Our History","url":"https://www.rustenberg.co.za/our-story/our-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Rustenberg: John X Merriman 2020 factsheet","url":"https://www.rustenberg.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/Rustenberg-John-X-Merriman-2020-Factsheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Rustenberg: John X Merriman 2022 factsheet","url":"https://www.rustenberg.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Rustenberg-John-X-Merriman-2022-Factsheet-New.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: John X. Merriman","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_X._Merriman","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Britannica: John X. Merriman","url":"https://www.britannica.com/print/article/376357","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-hsya5cb2": {"id":"fl-hsya5cb2","key":"simonsig","group":"stellenbosch","region":"stellenbosch","producer":"Simonsig","producerId":"","say":"SEE-muns-sikh, KAHP-suh FON-kul","place":"Stellenbosch district, Coastal Region, Western Cape","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Simonsig Kaapse Vonkel Brut","appellation":"Stellenbosch or Western Cape, by vintage","classification":"","colour":"sparkling","grapes":"Chardonnay and Pinot Noir in roughly equal parts, with a little Pinot Meunier and, in some years, a touch of Pinot Blanc; the first vintage, of 1971, was Chenin Blanc.","sites":"Chardonnay and Pinot Noir from Stellenbosch, and in the vintages labelled Western Cape from vineyards beyond the district as well.","making":"The juice is fermented in stainless steel at a cool 15 to 17 degrees Celsius, part of the Chardonnay in older French oak barrels, and malolactic fermentation is avoided to keep it fresh. The second fermentation takes place in the bottle, and the wine rests at least twenty months on its lees before disgorging.","glass":"Green apple and ripe pear with shortbread from the lees, a fine, lively mousse, fresh, crisp acidity and a delicate, dry finish with a stony edge.","ageing":"Made to be drunk on release, while its fruit is fresh; kept a few years more it softens and gains toasty depth.","serve":"8 to 10 degrees Celsius; no need to decant; a tulip shaped white wine glass shows it better than a narrow flute.","label":[{"term":"Kaapse Vonkel","means":"Afrikaans for Cape sparkle, the name given to the estate's first sparkling wine."},{"term":"Cap Classique","means":"South Africa's name for sparkling wine made by a second fermentation in the bottle, as in Champagne."},{"term":"Brut","means":"The dry style, as on Champagne labels: little sugar is added at disgorging."},{"term":"The vintage year","means":"At least 85 per cent of the wine comes from the harvest of that year."}]},"table":"Simonsig's Kaapse Vonkel, Cape sparkle, is the wine from which South Africa dates Cap Classique, sparkling wine fermented again in the bottle. First made from Chenin Blanc of the 1971 harvest, it has been led by Chardonnay and Pinot Noir since 1987, with twenty months and more on its lees.","story":"In 1953, the family recalls, Simonsig's founder began to farm on De Hoop, which he rented from his father-in-law, and in the early 1960s he bought land of his own. In 1968 he bottled the first wine under the Simonsig name, a Chenin Blanc labelled Steen. Taken with the method of Champagne, he made from the 1971 harvest a sparkling wine fermented a second time in its bottle. The Cape grew little Chardonnay or Pinot Noir then, so the wine was Chenin Blanc. He called it Kaapse Vonkel, Cape sparkle, and released it in 1973. Earlier Cape cellars had tried the method, but the Cap Classique producers count Kaapse Vonkel as the first wine of their category. In 1971, too, Simonsig was among the estates that founded the Stellenbosch Wine Route. In 1987 the blend moved to Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, and Pinot Meunier later joined them. As other cellars took up the method, the style took the name Méthode Cap Classique, and in 1992 its makers formed an association. When a guest asks about Cap Classique, the Cape's own name for the traditional method, Kaapse Vonkel is the bottle to begin with, since the category counts its years from it.","others":[{"name":"Kaapse Vonkel Brut Rosé","note":"The pink version, first made in 2004 by the house's account, from Pinot Noir with Pinotage and Pinot Meunier."},{"name":"Simonsig Chenin Blanc","note":"The grape of the estate's first wine of 1968, labelled Steen, the Cape's old name for Chenin Blanc."}],"traps":["Kaapse Vonkel's first vintage, from the 1971 harvest, was Chenin Blanc; Chardonnay and Pinot Noir came later.","Made from the 1971 harvest, Kaapse Vonkel reached the market only in 1973, after its time on the lees.","Cap Classique is South Africa's name for the traditional method; the name Champagne is reserved for wine from Champagne alone."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-yvjtvxxr","front":"From which grape was the first Simonsig Kaapse Vonkel, of 1971, made?","back":"Chenin Blanc, since the Cape then grew little Chardonnay or Pinot Noir."},{"id":"fl-8nrng3h2","front":"What does Kaapse Vonkel mean, and which Cape category did it begin?","back":"Cape sparkle; the Cap Classique producers date their category, sparkling wine fermented a second time in its bottle, from it."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6wsj6z6n","q":"How long does Simonsig Kaapse Vonkel Brut rest on its lees before disgorging, according to the house?","opts":["At least five years","About three months","About ten years","At least twenty months"],"a":3,"exp":"At least twenty months on the lees, by the house's notes; five years would be far longer than this wine spends, and three months far too short for the method.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-j78w8ept","q":"From which Stellenbosch estate's sparkling wine of the 1971 harvest do the Cap Classique producers date their category?","opts":["Kanonkop","Meerlust","Simonsig","Rustenberg"],"a":2,"exp":"Simonsig made Kaapse Vonkel from the 1971 harvest and released it in 1973, and the producers count it the first Cap Classique; Kanonkop, Meerlust and Rustenberg are known for red wines.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Simonsig: Cap Classique Pioneers","url":"https://www.simonsig.co.za/cap-classique/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Simonsig: The sparkling history of the Malan family and Simonsig","url":"https://www.simonsig.co.za/the-sparkling-history-of-the-malan-family-and-simonsig/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Simonsig: Kaapse Vonkel Brut 2022 tasting note","url":"https://www.simonsig.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Simonsig-Kaapse-Vonkel-Brut-2022-Tasting-Note.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cap Classique Association: 50 years of Cap Classique, 1971 to 2021","url":"https://www.capclassique.co.za/50years/about.html","kind":"primary"},{"title":"winemag: SA wine history, was Simonsig Kaapse Vonkel really our first bottle fermented bubbly","url":"https://winemag.co.za/wine/opinion/sa-wine-history-was-simonsig-kaapse-vonkel-really-our-first-bottle-fermented-bubbly/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-y3s2ew0d": {"id":"fl-y3s2ew0d","key":"lanzerac","group":"stellenbosch","region":"stellenbosch","producer":"Lanzerac","producerId":"","say":"LAN-zuh-rak","place":"Jonkershoek Valley ward, Stellenbosch district, Western Cape","founded":"1692","wine":{"name":"Lanzerac Pinotage","appellation":"Stellenbosch","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Pinotage, unblended: the Cape's own crossing of Pinot Noir and Cinsaut, made at Stellenbosch in 1925.","sites":"Mainly the estate's own blocks in the Jonkershoek Valley on the edge of Stellenbosch town, where mountain soils and a mountain climate keep structure and natural acidity in the grapes, by the house's notes.","making":"After fermentation on the skins, malolactic fermentation follows in barrel, and the wine matures for at least fifteen months in a careful mix of new, second fill and third fill French oak barrels, racked regularly along the way.","glass":"Ripe plum and red and black berries with a hint of sweet oak; a rounded, generous mouthfeel, soft tannin and a lingering finish, with chocolate and caramel as it ages.","ageing":"Approachable from release, by the house's notes; it keeps for three to ten years by vintage, gaining notes of chocolate and caramel.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius; a young bottle needs no decanting, though an hour of air does no harm; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Pinotage","means":"The Cape's own grape, Pinot Noir crossed with Cinsaut, then called Hermitage, in 1925."},{"term":"Lanzerac","means":"The estate's name since 1920, when a new owner renamed the farm once called Schoongezicht."},{"term":"Stellenbosch","means":"The district named on the label, home of the estate and of the university where Pinotage was bred."}]},"table":"The first label to say Pinotage, on the 1959 vintage sold in 1961, carried the name of Lanzerac, an estate of the Jonkershoek Valley. Its Lanzerac Pinotage, mainly from its own valley vineyards and matured in French oak, gives ripe plum and berries with a soft, rounded finish.","story":"Lanzerac's land was granted in 1692 to a settler who named the farm Schoongezicht and, the estate relates, planted its first vines. In 1920 a new owner bought the farm and renamed it Lanzerac; the story goes that the name honours a French general of the First World War, a tale told rather than recorded. In 1925, at the university in Stellenbosch, Abraham Perold (1880 to 1941), its first professor of viticulture, crossed Pinot Noir with Cinsaut, then called Hermitage, and gave the Cape its own grape. The first wine sold as Pinotage came from the 1959 vintage, from grapes grown and made at the Bellevue farm, by most accounts. It was judged the champion of the young wine show that year, and the Stellenbosch Farmers' Winery released it in 1961 under its Lanzerac label, in the Pinotage Association's account. The estate counts that label as its own heritage, and its Lanzerac Pinotage, from its own Jonkershoek vineyards, carries the name forward. The lesson lies in the detail: the first Pinotage label said Lanzerac, but by most accounts the grapes grew at Bellevue.","others":[{"name":"Lanzerac Pionier Pinotage","note":"The estate's other Pinotage, basket pressed and aged up to twenty two months in barriques, up to half of them new."}],"traps":["By most accounts Bellevue grew the grapes of the first wine labelled Pinotage; the Lanzerac name on it was a label of the Stellenbosch Farmers' Winery.","Pinotage was crossed in 1925 from Pinot Noir and Cinsaut; Hermitage was the old Cape name for Cinsaut, not Syrah.","The 1959 is the vintage of the first Pinotage label; 1961 is the year it reached the market."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-o3bgv9uk","front":"Lanzerac Pinotage: which two grapes were crossed to make Pinotage, by whom and in which year?","back":"Pinot Noir and Cinsaut, then called Hermitage at the Cape, crossed by Abraham Perold in 1925."},{"id":"fl-47m39ap9","front":"On the first label to say Pinotage, the 1959 vintage sold in 1961, whose grapes and whose name were they?","back":"By most accounts grapes from the Bellevue farm; the name Lanzerac, a label of the Stellenbosch Farmers' Winery."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-7kwoavz5","q":"Where do the grapes for Lanzerac Pinotage grow, by the house's notes?","opts":["Mainly the estate's own Jonkershoek Valley blocks","Old vines on the Bellevue farm","Bush vines bought from Swartland growers","The Simonsberg slopes above Kanonkop"],"a":0,"exp":"The house's notes say the grapes come mainly from Lanzerac's own Jonkershoek Valley blocks; Bellevue, by most accounts, grew the grapes of the first Pinotage of 1959, not of this wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-yrk39mia","q":"The 1959 that first named Pinotage on its label reached the market in what year?","opts":["1973","1961","1925","1941"],"a":1,"exp":"The Stellenbosch Farmers' Winery released it in 1961 under the Lanzerac label; 1925 is the year of the crossing and 1941 the year the first barrel of Pinotage wine was made.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Lanzerac: Our Heritage","url":"https://lanzerac.co.za/our-heritage/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Lanzerac: Pinotage 2023","url":"https://lanzerac.co.za/product/pinotage-2023/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Pinotage Association: Heritage","url":"https://pinotage.co.za/heritage/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Pinotage Association: The Father of Pinotage","url":"https://pinotage.co.za/?p=373","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Stellenbosch Heritage: Jonkershoek Valley time line","url":"https://www.stellenboschheritage.co.za/wp-content/uploads/Jonkershoek-Timeline.pdf","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3d4xnouf": {"id":"fl-3d4xnouf","key":"sadie","group":"swartland","region":"swartland","producer":"Sadie Family Wines","producerId":"p-sadie-family","say":"kol-oo-MEL-uh","place":"The Paardeberg, near Malmesbury, Swartland","founded":"1999","wine":{"name":"Columella","appellation":"Swartland","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Syrah leads, as it made almost all of the first vintage, with Mourvèdre, Grenache, Carignan, Cinsaut, Tinta Barocca and lately Pinotage in shares that change with the year.","sites":"Old, dry farmed bush vines in vineyards across the Paardeberg, the Kasteelberg, Malmesbury and the Piketberg, from the granite of the Paardeberg to the shale and schist of the Kasteelberg; the house blends vineyards rather than grapes.","making":"The crop is sorted berry by berry, and the wine ferments with its own yeasts in open concrete tanks for about three weeks, with about three weeks more on the skins. It is basket pressed, goes through malolactic fermentation in barrel, spends about a year in French oak, little of it new, then about a year in large old foudres, and is bottled unfined and unfiltered.","glass":"Blackberry, blueberry and black olive over liquorice, tobacco leaf and slate, with a leafy edge; fresh and dense at once, with fine tannin in layers and a lasting, savoury close.","ageing":"Tight in its first five years or so; verticals reaching back to its first vintages show it keeping and gaining savoury depth for twenty years.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle an hour or two ahead, an older one gently off its sediment; a large Syrah or Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Columella","means":"The name of a Roman writer on farming; the house's white, Palladius, bears the name of another."},{"term":"Wine of Origin Swartland","means":"The district of the Coastal Region the grapes come from; the seal sets origin, not style."}]},"table":"Sadie Family Wines first made Columella, its red, in 2000: Syrah with Mourvèdre, Grenache and a few other grapes from old dry farmed bush vines across the Swartland. Fermented in open concrete and rested two years in barrels and old foudres, it pours dense yet fresh.","story":"The founder worked as a winemaker at Spice Route in the Swartland in the late 1990s, and in 1999 he set up the family's own company, whose first wines were made at Spice Route. Columella was first made with the 2000 vintage, almost all Syrah from old bush vines, and by the house's own account it resolved that year to keep its policies in vineyard and cellar for at least ten years before judging them. Over the years the share of Syrah fell as Mourvèdre and Grenache took a larger part and Carignan, Cinsaut, Tinta Barocca and lately Pinotage joined, for the house came to blend vineyards rather than grapes; Palladius, the white, became its counterpart, and an Old Vine Series later gathered single old parcels from across the Cape. In November 2010 the house was one of the four founders of the Swartland Revolution, a festival first held at Riebeek-Kasteel that ran each year until 2015 and drew the wine trade to a district long known for wheat and bulk wine, and in 2011 a group of the district's growers formed the Swartland Independent Producers. In its own review of twenty vintages, in 2020, the house chose to use fewer whole bunches. Columella first showed what the Swartland's old vines could give, and the district's story of old vines starts with it.","others":[{"name":"Palladius","note":"Columella's white companion, a blend of many varieties from old Swartland vines."},{"name":"The Old Vine Series","note":"Single old parcels from across the Cape, among them Skurfberg Chenin Blanc and Pofadder Cinsaut."}],"traps":["Columella is a blend whose share of Syrah has fallen over the years; it is not a varietal Syrah.","Columella is the red and Palladius the white; both carry the names of Roman writers on farming.","Wine of Origin Swartland names the place only; the growers' own style rules come from their association, not from the law."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-d0yoy38k","front":"How has Sadie Family Wines' Columella changed since its first vintage of 2000?","back":"The first was almost all Syrah; the blend has since widened to Mourvèdre, Grenache, Carignan, Cinsaut, Tinta Barocca and lately Pinotage."},{"id":"fl-4p11xn2v","front":"Where does Columella ferment, and for how long?","back":"With its own yeasts in open concrete tanks, for about three weeks, then about three weeks more on the skins."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-mbk7vt51","q":"How does Sadie Family Wines put together its red, Columella?","opts":["One walled vineyard on Paardeberg granite","Grapes bought in from Stellenbosch and Paarl","Several Swartland vineyards on different soils, blended","One parcel of Syrah on schist, bottled alone"],"a":2,"exp":"The house blends vineyards, not grapes, from the Paardeberg, the Kasteelberg, Malmesbury and the Piketberg; one parcel of Syrah on schist is the way of Porseleinberg and Mullineux, not Columella.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-ah1hlqjk","q":"Which event did Sadie Family Wines and three neighbours found in November 2010, one that drew the wine trade to the district each year until 2015?","opts":["The Wine of Origin scheme","The Cape Wine Auction","The Old Vine Project","The Swartland Revolution"],"a":3,"exp":"The Swartland Revolution was first held at Riebeek-Kasteel in November 2010 and ran until 2015; the Old Vine Project certifies old vineyards and is no festival.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"The Sadie Family: Columella 2022 technical details","url":"https://thesadiefamily.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/COLUMELLA-2022-TECHNICAL-DETAILS.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Sadie Family: The Columella 20 Year Review","url":"https://www.thesadiefamily.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/The-Columella-20-Year-Review.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Vinous: Fusion over force, The Sadie Family Columella 2001 to 2020","url":"https://billing.vinous.com/articles/fusion-over-force-the-sadie-family-columella-2001-2020-apr-2026","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: The Sadie Family","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sadie_Family","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Wine Society: Back to the Swartland Revolution","url":"https://www.thewinesociety.com/discover/explore/regional-guides/back-to-the-swartland-revolution/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-2sh51vvs": {"id":"fl-2sh51vvs","key":"mullineux","group":"swartland","region":"swartland","producer":"Mullineux","producerId":"p-mullineux","say":"","place":"Roundstone farm on the Kasteelberg, near Riebeek-Kasteel, Swartland","founded":"2007","wine":{"name":"Mullineux Schist Syrah","appellation":"Swartland","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Only Syrah, from a single parcel of the home farm.","sites":"One parcel on the stony shale and schist of Roundstone, the house's own farm on the slopes of the Kasteelberg near Riebeek-Kasteel, where the rocky soil keeps the vines small.","making":"Whole bunches are crushed by foot into open 500 litre barrels and ferment with their own yeasts for about ten days, with plunging by hand only, then stay about four weeks on the skins. About a year in 500 litre French oak barrels, from a quarter to a half of them new, comes next, then nine or ten months in a 2,000 litre foudre, and the wine is bottled unfined and unfiltered.","glass":"Damson plum and blackberry over black pepper, charcoal, graphite and sage, with a flinty, ashen edge; dense and structured, with fine but plentiful tannin and a firm finish that goes on.","ageing":"Darker and more structured than the house's Granite from the start; by the house's own account it drinks well over fifteen to twenty five years.","serve":"At 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, in a large Syrah or Bordeaux glass; give a young bottle an hour or two in a decanter.","label":[{"term":"Schist","means":"The soil of the parcel, the shale and schist of the Kasteelberg, which names the wine."},{"term":"Roundstone","means":"The house's own farm on the Kasteelberg, printed on the label beside the word Schist."},{"term":"Wine of Origin Swartland","means":"Certified as grown in the Swartland district; it vouches for the place, not for a style."}]},"table":"Of the single soil Syrahs Mullineux bottles, the Schist Syrah comes from one parcel of shale and schist on the house's home farm on the Kasteelberg, made from whole bunches and aged in barrels, then a large foudre. Darker and firmer than the Granite, it should go into a decanter.","story":"The house was founded in 2007 by a winemaking couple who settled at Riebeek-Kasteel in the Swartland. Their first Syrah, of the 2008 vintage, was made at Reyneke in Stellenbosch, where they were then making wine. When the Swartland Revolution first gathered at Riebeek-Kasteel, in November 2010, the couple stood among its four founders, and until 2015 the festival returned each year, bringing buyers and writers to a district that had long been known for bulk wine. The same 2010 vintage began their single soil range: Syrah from schist and Syrah from granite, bottled apart so that the soils could be read side by side. The Schist parcel lies on Roundstone, the house's own farm on the slopes of the Kasteelberg. In 2013 the couple took a partner, and the business became Mullineux and Leeu Family Wines, with a second cellar, Leeu Passant, in Franschhoek. A Syrah from iron rich soil later joined the range, beside Chenin Blanc from quartz, and the Syrahs make the case that Syrah, not Pinotage, is the Swartland's red. Tasted beside the Granite, the Schist lets the district's geology speak: the grape and the making are shared, and the soil names the bottle.","others":[{"name":"Mullineux Granite Syrah","note":"The perfumed, higher toned Syrah from granite, the direct comparison wine."},{"name":"Mullineux Quartz Chenin Blanc","note":"Old vine Chenin Blanc from one soil, taut and stony."},{"name":"Mullineux Straw Wine","note":"Sweet Chenin Blanc from grapes dried before pressing, unfortified."}],"traps":["Schist and Granite are two Syrahs of one house bottled by soil; Granite is the perfumed, higher toned wine, Schist the darker and firmer.","Mullineux Straw Wine is sweet from grapes dried before pressing; it is not fortified.","Swartland Syrah of this kind is perfumed and medium to full bodied; it is no heavy, sun baked blockbuster."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-62uppwq9","front":"Mullineux Schist Syrah comes from a single parcel: on which farm, and on what rock?","back":"On the stony shale and schist of Roundstone, the house's own farm on the Kasteelberg near Riebeek-Kasteel."},{"id":"fl-rpes60in","front":"How does Mullineux begin the fermentation of its Schist Syrah?","back":"Whole bunches are crushed by foot into open 500 litre barrels and ferment with their own yeasts."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-5qgwfrhi","q":"Mullineux Schist Syrah and the house's Granite Syrah differ in which respect?","opts":["A share of white grapes in one","The soil each parcel grows on","Fortification of one of the two","The grape, Syrah against Grenache"],"a":1,"exp":"The house bottles the two apart by soil so that the soil can be read; both are Syrah alone, and neither is fortified.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-a5gbgi1n","q":"Founded in 2007, Mullineux began its single soil Syrahs, Schist and Granite, with which vintage?","opts":["2010","2000","2008","2013"],"a":0,"exp":"The single soil range began with the 2010 vintage; 2008 was the house's first Syrah, made at Reyneke, and 2013 the year of the Leeu partnership.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Liberty Wines: Mullineux Single Terroir Schist Swartland Syrah","url":"https://www.libertywines.co.uk/our-portfolio/MU109-schist-swartland-syrah","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Mullineux and Leeu: 2022 Schist Roundstone Syrah fact sheet","url":"https://mlfwines.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/2022-Mullineux-SCHIST-Roundstone-Syrah-fact-sheet.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Skurnik Wines: Mullineux Syrah Schist Roundstone","url":"https://www.skurnik.com/sku/syrah-schist-roundstone-mullineux-3/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Winemag: Mullineux Schist Syrah vertical","url":"https://winemag.co.za/wine/opinion/mullineux-schist-syrah-vertical/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Wine Society: Back to the Swartland Revolution","url":"https://www.thewinesociety.com/discover/explore/regional-guides/back-to-the-swartland-revolution/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-w8fv6bvz": {"id":"fl-w8fv6bvz","key":"badenhorst","group":"swartland","region":"swartland","producer":"AA Badenhorst Family Wines","producerId":"","say":"BAH-den-horst, KAL-moos-fon-tayn","place":"Kalmoesfontein farm on the Paardeberg, Swartland","founded":"2008","wine":{"name":"Kalmoesfontein White Blend","appellation":"Swartland","classification":"","colour":"white","grapes":"Old bush vine Chenin Blanc leads, with smaller shares of Roussanne, Grenache Blanc, Viognier, Verdelho, Semillon and Palomino, the mix changing from vintage to vintage.","sites":"Small parcels across Kalmoesfontein on the Paardeberg, mostly old, unirrigated bush vines planted from the 1950s onwards on the hill's granite.","making":"The parcels are picked on the same day and pressed together as whole bunches, and the juice ferments as one wine in large old foudres. There it rests on its lees for about a year before bottling.","glass":"Citrus blossom, white peach and pear over tea leaf, fynbos and rose petal; broad, textured and ripe, with gentle grip and a long, savoury finish that turns towards dried honey and undergrowth with age.","ageing":"Open and generous on release; it keeps for some years more, gaining notes of dried honey and undergrowth as it goes.","serve":"10 to 13 degrees Celsius, not too cold, in a wide white wine glass; it needs no decanter.","label":[{"term":"Kalmoesfontein","means":"The home farm on the Paardeberg, whose name the same white blend has carried in place of Family White."},{"term":"Wine of Origin Swartland","means":"Swartland is the district on the seal: a guarantee of where the grapes grew rather than of how the wine tastes."}]},"table":"Once sold as the Family White, AA Badenhorst's Kalmoesfontein White Blend is old bush vine Chenin Blanc pressed and fermented together with Roussanne, Grenache Blanc and other white grapes of the Paardeberg, in large old foudres. It drinks broad, textured and savoury.","story":"Two cousins of a Constantia family, whose grandfather managed the farm at Groot Constantia for decades, founded the house. One of them made wine at Rustenberg in Stellenbosch before, in 2008, the two bought Kalmoesfontein, an old Swartland farm on the Paardeberg. The farm's cellar had last made wine in the 1930s and stood neglected, and the cousins began, by the house's account, by restoring it and planting new vines beside the old bush vines already there, many of them planted in the 1950s and 1960s. The farm's buildings run back further: the house dates the Koringhuis, the old grain store, to the late eighteenth century. The cousins' house and three others founded the Swartland Revolution, which first met at Riebeek-Kasteel in November 2010; held again each year to 2015, the festival put the district before the wine trade. For years its white blend went by the name Family White, and it later took the farm's name, Kalmoesfontein, while some merchants kept the old one. Its grapes, from small parcels across the farm, are picked on one day and fermented together, so the wine is a field blend made as one. It is the Swartland white blend at its plainest: many grapes from one farm, made in a single ferment.","others":[{"name":"Kalmoesfontein Red Blend","note":"The farm's red, also a field blend, its grapes picked on the same day."},{"name":"Secateurs Chenin Blanc","note":"A younger Chenin Blanc, drawn from parcels in and around the Paardeberg."}],"traps":["The Family White Blend and the Kalmoesfontein White Blend are one wine under its old and new names, not two wines.","Its grapes are co-fermented as one wine, not fermented apart and blended afterwards.","Kalmoesfontein is the Badenhorst farm on the Paardeberg; Columella and Palladius are Sadie wines from the same hill."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-czgwt1h0","front":"AA Badenhorst's Kalmoesfontein White Blend is built on which grape?","back":"Old bush vine Chenin Blanc, with Roussanne, Grenache Blanc, Viognier and other white grapes in smaller shares."},{"id":"fl-hu39zmqv","front":"The Badenhorst cousins bought Kalmoesfontein in 2008 on which hill of the Swartland?","back":"The Paardeberg."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-5i2oifd2","q":"In the cellar, what happens to the grapes of AA Badenhorst's Kalmoesfontein White Blend?","opts":["Grapes dried on straw before a sweet ferment","Chenin Blanc alone from one young vineyard","Grapes pressed together and co-fermented in old foudres","Each grape fermented apart in steel, then blended"],"a":2,"exp":"The farm's parcels are picked on one day, pressed together and fermented as one wine in large old foudres; it is many white grapes, not Chenin Blanc alone.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-0zgcl72f","q":"When the Badenhorst cousins bought Kalmoesfontein in 2008, when had its old cellar last made wine?","opts":["In the 1700s","In the 1870s","In the 1990s","In the 1930s"],"a":3,"exp":"The cellar had stood unused since the 1930s, and the cousins restored it first; the Koringhuis, not the cellar, dates to the late eighteenth century.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"AA Badenhorst Family Wines: About us","url":"https://aabadenhorst.com/pages/about-us","kind":"primary"},{"title":"AA Badenhorst Family Wines: Kalmoesfontein Wine Collection","url":"https://aabadenhorst.com/pages/kalmoesfontein-wines","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Liberty Wines: AA Badenhorst Kalmoesfontein White Blend","url":"https://www.libertywines.co.uk/our-portfolio/BF305-kalmoesfontein-white-blend","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Wine Society: A. A. Badenhorst Kalmoesfontein White Blend 2018","url":"https://www.thewinesociety.com/product/a-a-badenhorst-kalmoesfontein-white-blend-swartland-2018-en.aspx","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Broadbent Selections: A. A. Badenhorst Family","url":"https://broadbent.com/?p=542","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-capa7hke": {"id":"fl-capa7hke","key":"porseleinberg","group":"swartland","region":"swartland","producer":"Porseleinberg","producerId":"","say":"por-suh-LAYN-behrkh","place":"A schist ridge near Riebeek-Kasteel, Swartland","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Porseleinberg Syrah","appellation":"Swartland","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"All Syrah, from the farm's own vineyards.","sites":"Steep, rocky slopes of decomposed mica schist on hard bedrock, the schist bluer on the upper slopes and redder, richer in iron, lower down.","making":"The grapes are fermented largely or wholly as whole bunches, and since 2018 the cap of skins has been kept mostly submerged in the juice rather than pumped over. Maturing takes about a year, the great part in large foudres and a small share in concrete eggs, before bottling.","glass":"Red cherry, rose petal and cracked pepper over rosemary, thyme and overturned earth, with an iron, smoky note; wild and savoury, firm yet fine, with a long, peppery finish.","ageing":"Closed for its first five or six years; merchants then give it ten to fifteen years more in bottle, over which it gains complexity.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, with two hours or more in a decanter for a young bottle; a large Syrah or Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Porseleinberg","means":"Porcelain mountain in Afrikaans: the farm's name, and the wine's."},{"term":"Wine of Origin Swartland","means":"The certified origin, the Swartland district: the law fixes where the grapes grew, never the style."}]},"table":"A schist ridge near Riebeek-Kasteel, bought by Boekenhoutskloof in 2009, gives Porseleinberg its single wine: a Syrah of whole bunches, matured mostly in large foudres with a little in concrete eggs. Peppery, wild and savoury, it needs five years of cellaring.","story":"Porseleinberg, porcelain mountain in Afrikaans, is a farm on a rugged schist ridge near Riebeek-Kasteel, in the Swartland district. In 2009 Boekenhoutskloof, the Franschhoek house, bought it, by its own account to secure a reliable source of quality grapes, Syrah above all. Its first wine came with the 2010 vintage, and the farm has made one wine ever since: a Syrah under the farm's own name. That November the farm took its place among the Swartland Revolution's four founders; the festival opened at Riebeek-Kasteel and ran every year until 2015, drawing buyers and writers to the district. The vineyard is decomposed mica schist on hard, rocky bedrock, and the wine is made to show it: whole bunches, large foudres and concrete rather than small new barrels. The label is printed by hand on a vintage Heidelberg letterpress in the farm's own cellar. The Franschhoek house's own Syrah draws largely on the same vineyard, with fruit from Goldmine, a second Swartland farm, but Porseleinberg bottles the ridge alone. In Porseleinberg the Swartland's schist speaks through Syrah and nothing else.","others":[{"name":"Boekenhoutskloof Syrah","note":"Not a Porseleinberg wine but the Franschhoek house's own Syrah, drawn largely from the Porseleinberg vineyard with fruit from its Goldmine site."}],"traps":["Porseleinberg is a single Syrah under the farm's own name; Boekenhoutskloof Syrah, though largely from the same vineyard, is a different wine.","Porseleinberg grows on schist; the granite of the Swartland is the Paardeberg's.","Whole bunches and large foudres make it peppery and savoury, not a ripe, oaky warm climate Shiraz."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-nn81wcuv","front":"On what soil does Porseleinberg Syrah grow?","back":"Decomposed mica schist on hard, rocky bedrock, on a ridge near Riebeek-Kasteel."},{"id":"fl-kthjgl8l","front":"How is the Porseleinberg label printed?","back":"By hand, on a vintage Heidelberg letterpress in the farm's own cellar."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-ec4h18eh","q":"Why does Porseleinberg Syrah taste of pepper and herbs rather than sweet oak?","opts":["Whole bunches and large foudres, not small new barrels","A share of Viognier fermented with the Syrah","Long ageing in small new American oak","Grapes from warm, deep alluvial soils"],"a":0,"exp":"It is fermented largely as whole bunches and matured mostly in large foudres, with a little in concrete eggs, so oak stays in the background; it is Syrah alone, with no Viognier.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-go05eou4","q":"A house bought the Porseleinberg farm in 2009, by its own account to secure a supply of Syrah. Which was it?","opts":["Mullineux of Riebeek-Kasteel","Boekenhoutskloof of Franschhoek","Allesverloren of Riebeek West","Sadie Family Wines of the Paardeberg"],"a":1,"exp":"Boekenhoutskloof bought the farm in 2009, and the wine has been made since the 2010 vintage; Sadie and Mullineux were its fellow founders of the Swartland Revolution, not its owners.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Boekenhoutskloof: Milestones","url":"https://www.boekenhoutskloof.co.za/milestones/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Boekenhoutskloof: Syrah 2021 fact sheet","url":"https://www.boekenhoutskloof.co.za/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Boekenhoutskloof-Syrah-2021.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Berry Bros and Rudd: 2022 Porseleinberg, Swartland","url":"https://www.bbr.com/products-20228030566-2022-porseleinberg-swartland-south-africa","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Farr Vintners: Porseleinberg Syrah 2020","url":"https://www.farrvintners.com/wine.php?wine=84485","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Visi: Porseleinberg letterpress labels","url":"https://visi.co.za/?p=9406","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-f8kghlyz": {"id":"fl-f8kghlyz","key":"allesverloren","group":"swartland","region":"swartland","producer":"Allesverloren","producerId":"","say":"ah-less-fer-LOOR-en","place":"Riebeek West, Swartland","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Allesverloren Cape Vintage","appellation":"Swartland","classification":"","colour":"fortified","grapes":"Led by Tinta Barocca, Touriga Nacional and Souzão; the estate speaks of seven grapes in the wine, with Tinta Roriz and Tinta Francisca among the others.","sites":"Trellised vineyards of the estate at Riebeek West in the Swartland, where the family grew Portuguese grapes for its fortified wine.","making":"Hand picked grapes ferment on their skins, and the wine is fortified with grape spirit before all its sugar has fermented, the method of port. It then spends about twenty months in French oak barrels before bottling, while a Reserve is held six years in oak.","glass":"Deep ruby, with fresh grape and warm cinnamon on the nose; sweet, soft and velvety in the mouth, smooth rather than fiery, with a long finish of rich fruitcake.","ageing":"The estate presents it as soft and smooth enough to drink on release; like other Cape Vintages it can also be kept, softening further in bottle over the years.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant an older bottle off any sediment; a port glass, with aged cheese.","label":[{"term":"Cape Vintage","means":"The name the estate gives this wine from the 2020 vintage: the Cape term for a port style wine of one year."},{"term":"Fine Old Vintage","means":"What the same wine's label said before the estate renamed it Cape Vintage."},{"term":"Allesverloren","means":"All is lost: the farm's name since 1704, after a fire, as the story goes."}]},"table":"Allesverloren, the Riebeek West farm the Malan family took over in 1872, labels its port style red Cape Vintage, replacing Fine Old Vintage from the 2020 vintage. Tinta Barocca, Touriga Nacional and Souzão, fortified and aged about twenty months in French oak, come out sweet, velvety and rich.","story":"The estate's own history places its beginning between 1696 and 1704, when the governor of the Cape, it says, left the land to a widow named Cloete. The story goes that in 1704 the family came home from a journey to Stellenbosch to find the house burnt and the farm in ruins, and the farm took its name, Allesverloren, all is lost. In 1872 the Malan family took over the farm, and on 22 May 1874 Daniel François Malan (1874 to 1959) was born there; he became prime minister of South Africa from 1948 to 1954, and his government brought in apartheid. By the family's own account a Malan of the farm first made the fortified wine that grew into its flagship, and the family were pioneers in bringing Portuguese grapes to South Africa. The wine was long sold as Allesverloren Port. Under a wine agreement signed with the European Union in 2002, South Africa gave up the Portuguese name; the estate's wine became Fine Old Vintage, and from the 2020 vintage it has been called Cape Vintage. Beside it the estate kept Tinta Barocca and Shiraz as dry table wines. It stands for the Swartland's older fortified tradition, made from the grapes of port, beside the district's newer dry reds.","others":[{"name":"Allesverloren Fine Old Vintage Reserve","note":"A reserve of the fortified wine, held six years in older French oak before bottling."},{"name":"Allesverloren Tinta Barocca","note":"A dry red of the Portuguese grape that also leads the fortified wine."},{"name":"Allesverloren Shiraz","note":"A dry Shiraz from the Riebeek West vineyards."}],"traps":["A bottle labelled Fine Old Vintage holds the same wine as Cape Vintage, only from a vintage before 2020.","Allesverloren's fortified wine is from the Swartland, not from Calitzdorp, the Klein Karoo district known for Cape port style wine.","Allesverloren means all is lost; the name comes from a fire of 1704, as the story goes, not from the wine."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-tg54g17j","front":"Allesverloren Cape Vintage: which three grapes lead it?","back":"Tinta Barocca, Touriga Nacional and Souzão, Portuguese grapes of port."},{"id":"fl-1dxv8ldp","front":"What lies behind the name Allesverloren, and what does it mean?","back":"All is lost: as the story goes, the family came home from a journey in 1704 to find the farm burnt."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-3zlg4tkz","q":"What took the word port off the label of Allesverloren's fortified red?","opts":["A move of its vineyards to the Klein Karoo","A switch to a blend of several vintages","A change of style to a dry table wine","A wine agreement with the European Union"],"a":3,"exp":"Under its wine agreement with the European Union, signed in 2002, South Africa gave up the name port; the estate's wine became Fine Old Vintage and, from 2020, Cape Vintage, fortified and of one year as before.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-723m79nl","q":"Allesverloren took its name, all is lost, after an event of 1704. What was it, as the story goes?","opts":["A flood washed away the first vineyards","The governor took the land back","The farm burnt while the family was away","A shipwreck lost the first harvest"],"a":2,"exp":"As the estate tells it, the family came home from a journey to find the house destroyed and the farm in ruins; the governor's part was to leave the land to the widow Cloete.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Allesverloren: The Story of Allesverloren","url":"https://allesverloren.co.za/pages/our-story","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Allesverloren: Cape Vintage 2022","url":"https://allesverloren.co.za/products/allesverloren-fine-old-vintage","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine.co.za: Allesverloren's Port now called Fine Old Vintage","url":"https://news.wine.co.za/news.aspx?NEWSID=19780","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Aspire Lifestyle: Allesverloren Cape Vintage 2020","url":"https://aspirelifestyle.co.za/allesverlorens-latest-port-style-wine-reaffirms-farms-icon-status/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: D. F. Malan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D._F._Malan","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-1yrrhvj1": {"id":"fl-1yrrhvj1","key":"catena-zapata","group":"mendoza","region":"mendoza","producer":"Catena Zapata","producerId":"p-catena-zapata","say":"kah-TEH-nah sah-PAH-tah","place":"Luján de Cuyo, with the Adrianna Vineyard in Gualtallary, Uco Valley, Mendoza","founded":"1902","wine":{"name":"Catena Zapata Adrianna Vineyard River Stones Malbec","appellation":"Mendoza","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Malbec, from the family's own selection of old vines taken from its Angélica vineyard in Lunlunta.","sites":"River Stones, a small parcel of the Adrianna Vineyard in Gualtallary, Tupungato, at about 1,370 metres by the house's sheets, on the bed of an ancient river whose stones are coated white with calcium carbonate.","making":"Cold soaked for some days, then fermented over two to three weeks, with part of the vat in whole bunches in some later vintages. Élevage runs well over a year in French oak, in barrels in the early vintages and in large, old foudres in later ones; the months and the vessels change with the vintage.","glass":"Violet and blackberry over black cherry, with a stony, faintly metallic edge, fresh acidity and long, smooth tannin; taut and lifted rather than plush.","ageing":"It opens slowly in bottle after a tight first five years or so, and has the acidity and tannin to keep for well over a decade.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two; a large Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Adrianna Vineyard","means":"The family's vineyard in Gualtallary, planted in 1992 and named for the youngest daughter of the founder's grandson."},{"term":"River Stones","means":"The parcel's name, for the old riverbed of white stones it grows on; recent labels shorten it to River."},{"term":"Mendoza","means":"The province of origin; every grape comes from the one parcel in Gualtallary, in the Uco Valley."}]},"table":"High in Gualtallary, one parcel of Catena Zapata's Adrianna Vineyard lies on an old riverbed whose stones are coated white with calcium carbonate, and its Malbec is bottled as River Stones. Taut rather than plush, with a violet scent, it repays a few years in bottle and a decanter.","story":"Italian by birth, the Catena family's founder reached Argentina in 1898 and planted his first Malbec vineyard in Mendoza in 1902, by the family's own account; the house counts four generations since. His son, in the family's telling, held that Malbec could stand beside the wines of France, and his grandson, back from a visiting professorship at the University of California, Berkeley, in the early 1980s, turned the house towards fine wine, first with Cabernet Sauvignon and Chardonnay and then with Malbec. In 1992 the family planted the Adrianna Vineyard at about 1,450 metres in Gualtallary, in the Tupungato department of the Uco Valley, and named it for the grandson's youngest daughter. Its Malbec came from the family's own selection, taken from Lot 18 of its old Angélica vineyard in Lunlunta, Maipú. The new vineyard was divided into parcels and studied closely, and the Catena Institute of Wine has published research from it. River Stones is the parcel on the bed of an ancient river, its stones coated white with calcium carbonate, bottled apart from its neighbours Fortuna Terrae and Mundus Bacillus Terrae. Recent labels shorten its name to Adrianna Vineyard River. Set beside a Luján de Cuyo Malbec, it shows how far altitude and stone have carried the grape from that plush style.","others":[{"name":"Nicolás Catena Zapata","note":"The house's blend of Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec, a different wine from the single parcel Adrianna bottlings."},{"name":"Adrianna Vineyard Fortuna Terrae","note":"Malbec from another named parcel of the same vineyard, bottled on its own beside River Stones."},{"name":"Adrianna Vineyard White Bones Chardonnay","note":"Chardonnay from a parcel named for the calcareous deposits in its soil, bottled apart from its neighbour White Stones."}],"traps":["River Stones is a parcel of the Adrianna Vineyard, not a separate estate, and recent labels call it simply River.","Gualtallary lies in Tupungato in the Uco Valley, not in Luján de Cuyo, so its Malbec is tauter and more perfumed than the plush Luján style.","Nicolás Catena Zapata is the house's Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec blend; the Adrianna wines are single parcel bottlings."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-6rjpeer5","front":"What covers the ground of Catena Zapata's River Stones parcel?","back":"The stones of an ancient riverbed, coated white with calcium carbonate, in the Adrianna Vineyard in Gualtallary."},{"id":"fl-1xuexkcq","front":"In which year was Catena Zapata's Adrianna Vineyard planted, and how high?","back":"In 1992, at about 1,450 metres in Gualtallary, Tupungato, in the Uco Valley."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-er4ofzum","q":"What sets Catena Zapata's River Stones apart from the other Malbecs of the Adrianna Vineyard?","opts":["It is aged for years in new American oak","Its parcel is an old riverbed of white stones","It blends fruit from several Uco Valley departments","It comes from old vines in Luján de Cuyo"],"a":1,"exp":"River Stones is named for its parcel, the bed of an ancient river whose stones are coated in calcium carbonate; its fruit is all from Gualtallary, not from Luján de Cuyo.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-nfm1mk39","q":"When, by the family's account, did the Catena founder plant his first Malbec vineyard in Mendoza, having reached Argentina in 1898?","opts":["1902","1853","1992","1930"],"a":0,"exp":"The house dates itself to 1902, when the founder planted his first Malbec; 1853 is the year Malbec reached Mendoza, and 1992 the planting of the Adrianna Vineyard.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Catena Zapata: Since 1902, a journey through four generations","url":"https://catenazapata.com/since-1902/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Catena Zapata: Adrianna Vineyard press kit","url":"https://catenazapata.com/wp-content/uploads/downloads/Press-Kit-Adrianna-Vineyard.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Catena Zapata: River Stones 2022 technical sheet","url":"https://www.catenawines.com/es/river-stones-es.php","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Winebow: Catena Zapata Adrianna Vineyard River","url":"https://winebow.com/node/431532","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bodega Catena Zapata","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodega_Catena_Zapata","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-f7e8vq5e": {"id":"fl-f7e8vq5e","key":"achaval-ferrer","group":"mendoza","region":"mendoza","producer":"Achaval-Ferrer","producerId":"p-achaval-ferrer","say":"ah-CHAH-vahl feh-REHR","place":"Perdriel, Luján de Cuyo, with Finca Altamira in La Consulta, Uco Valley, Mendoza","founded":"1998","wine":{"name":"Achaval-Ferrer Finca Altamira Malbec","appellation":"Mendoza","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Old vine Malbec, with no other grape.","sites":"Finca Altamira, an old Malbec vineyard in La Consulta, within Paraje Altamira in the San Carlos department, at about 1,050 to 1,100 metres on alluvial ground with a shallow surface of silt, sand and volcanic ash.","making":"Cement vats hold the ferment, worked by open pump overs; by the house's own sheet there is no cold soak, no added enzyme and no correction of acidity. The wine then ages for a little over a year in French oak barrels, most of them new, and goes into bottle without filtration.","glass":"Black cherry, blackberry and violet, with graphite and wet slate beneath; vigorous acidity and fine, chalky tannin give it tension and grip rather than sweetness.","ageing":"After a firm, closed youth it needs five years or more, and a strong vintage keeps well beyond a decade.","serve":"Pour at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius into a large Bordeaux glass, a young bottle after an hour or two in a decanter.","label":[{"term":"Finca Altamira","means":"The named old vineyard in La Consulta; finca means farm or estate and carries no legal rank."},{"term":"Mendoza","means":"Only the province is named, though the grapes all grow in one Uco Valley vineyard."},{"term":"Malbec","means":"The one grape in the bottle."}]},"table":"From the vintage of 1999, Finca Altamira was the first wine Achaval-Ferrer released: Malbec from one old vineyard in La Consulta, in the Uco Valley. Expect violet, black cherry and a chalky grip; decant it and pour it beside red meat.","story":"By its own story, Achaval-Ferrer began with a group of Argentine and Italian friends who travelled thousands of kilometres in search of old vineyards before they came upon Finca Altamira in 1998. Its old Malbec vines in La Consulta, in the south of the Uco Valley, gave the house its founding idea: to bottle each old vineyard on its own rather than blend Mendoza into one wine. Finca Altamira 1999 was the first wine the house released, made from very low yields. Single vineyard Malbecs from Finca Bella Vista in Luján de Cuyo and Finca Mirador in Medrano followed beside it, with Quimera as the blend across sites. In 2011 the SPI group, owner of the Stolichnaya vodka brand, bought into the house. In 2013 Paraje Altamira, the alluvial fan of the Tunuyán river around the vineyard, became one of Argentina's geographical indications, its boundary drawn by soil survey: official recognition of the case for site that single vineyard bottlings such as this one had made. Finca Altamira teaches Mendoza Malbec by place, its chalky grip coming from the ground rather than from the cellar.","others":[{"name":"Finca Bella Vista","note":"Single vineyard Malbec from old vines in Luján de Cuyo, denser than Finca Altamira."},{"name":"Finca Mirador","note":"An old vineyard in Medrano, bottled as a single vineyard Malbec."},{"name":"Quimera","note":"A blend across the house's sites, led by Malbec."}],"traps":["The house's winery and Finca Bella Vista are in Luján de Cuyo, but Finca Altamira grows farther south, in Paraje Altamira in the Uco Valley.","Finca on a label means farm or estate and carries no legal rank; it is not a classification.","Paraje Altamira became a geographical indication in 2013, fourteen years after the vintage of 1999 that began Finca Altamira."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-bsygwlar","front":"Where is Achaval-Ferrer's Finca Altamira vineyard, and on what ground?","back":"In La Consulta, within Paraje Altamira in the Uco Valley, at about 1,050 to 1,100 metres on alluvial silt, sand and volcanic ash."},{"id":"fl-v3mnh5dl","front":"Which wine was the first that Achaval-Ferrer released, and from which vintage?","back":"Finca Altamira 1999, from the old vineyard the founders came upon in 1998."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6bwz9kh4","q":"Unlike the house's Quimera, Achaval-Ferrer's Finca Altamira is what kind of wine?","opts":["It comes from young vines on the valley floor of Maipú","It is aged for years in large old foudres","It blends several grapes from across the house's sites","It is Malbec from one old vineyard in La Consulta"],"a":3,"exp":"Finca Altamira is single vineyard Malbec from La Consulta in the Uco Valley; Quimera is the house's blend across its sites, so the blending answer describes the other wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-qduhk3b7","q":"Which group bought into Achaval-Ferrer, the house of Finca Altamira, in 2011?","opts":["A cooperative of Chilean growers","The Moët Hennessy group of France","The SPI group, owner of Stolichnaya vodka","The Catena family of Mendoza"],"a":2,"exp":"The SPI group, behind the Stolichnaya vodka brand, bought into Achaval-Ferrer in 2011; Moët Hennessy is the group behind Terrazas de los Andes and its share of Cheval des Andes.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Achaval-Ferrer: Finca Altamira vineyard","url":"https://achaval-ferrer.com/vineyards/finca-altamira","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Achaval-Ferrer: Finca Altamira 2019 technical sheet","url":"https://achaval-ferrer.com/pdf/Ficha-Altamira-2019_en.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Achaval-Ferrer: Altamira 15","url":"https://achaval-ferrer.com/altamira15/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Stolichnaya group buys major stake in Achaval-Ferrer","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/stolichnaya-group-buys-major-stake-in-achaval-ferrer-40013","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Just Drinks: SPI Group makes Achaval-Ferrer majority share buy","url":"https://www.just-drinks.com/news/argentina-spi-group-makes-achaval-ferrer-majority-share-buy","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-772xgysy": {"id":"fl-772xgysy","key":"zuccardi","group":"mendoza","region":"mendoza","producer":"Familia Zuccardi","producerId":"p-zuccardi","say":"pee-EH-drah een-fee-NEE-tah","place":"Paraje Altamira, San Carlos, Uco Valley, Mendoza","founded":"1963","wine":{"name":"Zuccardi Finca Piedra Infinita","appellation":"Paraje Altamira","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Malbec alone, drawn from several plots of the one vineyard.","sites":"The Piedra Infinita vineyard at about 1,100 metres in Paraje Altamira, on the alluvial fan of the Tunuyán river: a sandy surface over granite stones and boulders coated in calcium carbonate.","making":"Its ferment runs in concrete vats without an epoxy lining, on the grapes' own yeasts. The early vintages were aged partly in used 500 litre French oak barrels and partly in concrete; the house's sheets of 2019 and 2022 give concrete alone for the ageing, with no oak at all.","glass":"Wet stone and dried herbs under violet and black pepper, with a chalky, finely grained grip and fresh acidity; grain and tension count for more in it than ripe sweetness.","ageing":"Stony and reserved on release, it gains depth over its first decade, and its acidity carries it beyond.","serve":"15 to 17 degrees Celsius, a little cooler than a plush Malbec, in a large Bordeaux glass; a young bottle takes an hour of decanting.","label":[{"term":"Paraje Altamira","means":"The geographical indication of the Tunuyán river's alluvial fan in San Carlos, recognised in 2013 and drawn by soil."},{"term":"Finca Piedra Infinita","means":"The vineyard's name, infinite stone in Spanish, for the stones that fill its ground."}]},"table":"Granite stones fill the Paraje Altamira vineyard behind Familia Zuccardi's Finca Piedra Infinita, a Malbec fermented and aged in concrete rather than new oak. Pour it a little cool: violet, pepper and wet stone sit over a chalky grip.","story":"The house dates itself to 1963, when its founder, an engineer, planted the family's first vineyard in Mendoza to show off an irrigation system he had devised; the vineyard drew the family into wine. In 2007 it planted its first vineyard in the Uco Valley, Finca Canal Uco in Paraje Altamira, and then the Piedra Infinita and Los Membrillos vineyards nearby. Piedra Infinita lies on the alluvial fan of the Tunuyán river, where granite stones and boulders coated in calcium carbonate lie under a sandy surface. Paraje Altamira was recognised as a geographical indication in 2013, the year building began on the Zuccardi Valle de Uco winery. It opened in 2016, raised in part from the stones of the Piedra Infinita vineyard itself. The wine named for that vineyard is fermented there in concrete with native yeasts; its early vintages also saw used 500 litre barrels, and the house's sheets of 2019 and 2022 have it aged in concrete alone. Single plots of the same vineyard, such as Gravascal, are bottled beside it, and so is Concreto, which puts the vessel on its label. In this wine Mendoza Malbec turns away from ripeness and new oak, towards stone, freshness and a named place.","others":[{"name":"Zuccardi Concreto","note":"Paraje Altamira Malbec fermented and aged in concrete, the wine that put the vessel on its label."},{"name":"Zuccardi Aluvional","note":"A series of Malbecs from single places, Gualtallary, Paraje Altamira, Los Chacayes and San Pablo among them, bottled side by side."},{"name":"Finca Piedra Infinita Gravascal","note":"One small plot of the same vineyard, its Malbec made in concrete from ferment to bottling."}],"traps":["Zuccardi Valle de Uco is the winery's name; Piedra Infinita is the vineyard and the wine, though the two are often run together.","Aluvional is a range of separate single place bottlings, not one blend.","The calcareous coat on Paraje Altamira's stones is patchy within an alluvial fan, not a continuous bed of limestone."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-udq0fzhw","front":"Zuccardi Finca Piedra Infinita: in what is it fermented, and in what did the vintages of 2019 and 2022 age?","back":"Concrete for both: fermentation with native yeasts, and by the house's sheets the ageing too, with no oak."},{"id":"fl-omdlhkwb","front":"What did the founder of Familia Zuccardi plant in 1963, and why?","back":"The family's first vineyard, to show off an irrigation system he had devised."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-qcvnqh8x","q":"A guest wants a Mendoza Malbec made without new oak. Why does Zuccardi's Finca Piedra Infinita fit?","opts":["It rests in large new foudres before bottling","It is fermented and aged in concrete vats","It is aged for two years in new French barrels","It is fermented in new American oak casks"],"a":1,"exp":"Finca Piedra Infinita is fermented in concrete, and the house's sheets of 2019 and 2022 age it there too; earlier sheets name used 500 litre barrels beside the concrete, not new oak.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-xvzbo39i","q":"Long before its Paraje Altamira winery of 2016, when did Familia Zuccardi plant its first vineyard?","opts":["1963","2007","1998","1902"],"a":0,"exp":"The founder planted the family's first vineyard in 1963 to show an irrigation system; 2007 is the year of its first Uco Valley vineyard, Finca Canal Uco.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Zuccardi Valle de Uco: History","url":"https://zuccardiwines.com/en/historia/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Zuccardi Valle de Uco: Fincas","url":"https://zuccardiwines.com/en/fincas/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Zuccardi: Finca Piedra Infinita 2022 technical sheet","url":"https://zuccardiwines.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TS-ENG-FINCA-PIEDRA-INFINITA-2022.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Zuccardi: Finca Piedra Infinita 2017 technical sheet","url":"https://zuccardiwines.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/TS-ENG-FINCA-PIEDRA-INFINITA-2017.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Enthusiast: Zuccardi 2022 Finca Piedra Infinita Malbec, Paraje Altamira","url":"https://www.wineenthusiast.com/buying-guide/zuccardi-2022-finca-piedra-infinita-malbec-paraje-altamira/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-z9dpi0r9": {"id":"fl-z9dpi0r9","key":"cheval-des-andes","group":"mendoza","region":"mendoza","producer":"Cheval des Andes","producerId":"w-cheval-des-andes","say":"sheh-VAHL day ZAHND","place":"Las Compuertas, Luján de Cuyo, Mendoza","founded":"1999","wine":{"name":"Cheval des Andes","appellation":"Mendoza","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec, their proportions changing markedly with the vintage, with a little Petit Verdot in some years.","sites":"Old Malbec at Las Compuertas in Luján de Cuyo, ungrafted and partly planted in 1929, long watered by flooding from snowmelt channels, with higher fruit from the house's vineyard in Paraje Altamira in the Uco Valley.","making":"Picked by hand and fermented parcel by parcel in small vats of 30 to 80 hectolitres. After the malolactic fermentation the teams of Cheval des Andes and Château Cheval Blanc build the first blend together, and the wine then ages for thirteen to eighteen months, by vintage, in barrels and larger casks, new and used, mostly of French oak.","glass":"Cedar and tobacco beneath blackberry, cassis and violet, with polished, fine grained tannin and a fresh, long finish; it reads more like Bordeaux than like plush Mendoza.","ageing":"Polished enough to drink within a few years of release, it has the structure to develop over fifteen years or more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; a young bottle goes into a decanter an hour or so ahead; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Cheval des Andes","means":"French for horse of the Andes, after Château Cheval Blanc, the Bordeaux partner in the wine."},{"term":"Mendoza","means":"Origin at the level of the province, the grapes drawn from Luján de Cuyo and the Uco Valley."}]},"table":"Château Cheval Blanc and Terrazas de los Andes make Cheval des Andes together in Mendoza, blending Cabernet Sauvignon with old, ungrafted Malbec from Las Compuertas. Cedar and violet come before plush fruit; serve it as you would a red Bordeaux.","story":"The Malbec at Las Compuertas, in Luján de Cuyo, was planted in 1929 and long watered in the old way, by flooding from the snowmelt channels whose floodgates give the place its name. Flooding drowns phylloxera, so the vines could grow on their own roots, as Bordeaux's vines did before the louse reached France in the nineteenth century. As the house tells it, the head of Château Cheval Blanc saw in these vines a road back to the Bordeaux of that time, and in 1999 the Saint-Émilion estate joined Terrazas de los Andes, the Mendoza house founded by Moët Hennessy in 1996, to make one wine. The first vintage was 1999. Each parcel is fermented apart, and after the malolactic fermentation the teams of both houses meet to build the first blend, as a Bordeaux château assembles its grand vin from many lots. Higher fruit from the house's vineyard in Paraje Altamira, in the Uco Valley, joins the old vines. For a sommelier, Cheval des Andes is Argentina's place in the set of French joint ventures abroad, beside Opus One in Napa and Almaviva in Chile, and a lesson in Malbec given a Bordeaux frame.","others":[{"name":"Château Cheval Blanc","note":"The Saint-Émilion partner, whose own wine comes from other grapes in another place, though the two teams blend together."},{"name":"Terrazas de los Andes Grand Malbec","note":"The Argentine partner's own high altitude Malbec, made apart from the joint venture."}],"traps":["Cheval Blanc is built on Cabernet Franc and Merlot, but Cheval des Andes blends Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec.","The Argentine partner is Terrazas de los Andes, Moët Hennessy's Mendoza house, not Catena.","It is a Mendoza wine from Luján de Cuyo and the Uco Valley, not a Patagonian one."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-9xmiz3hu","front":"Which two houses made Cheval des Andes, and in what year did they begin?","back":"Château Cheval Blanc of Saint-Émilion and Terrazas de los Andes of Mendoza, in 1999, the year of the first vintage."},{"id":"fl-61vy14uu","front":"Why could the old Las Compuertas Malbec of Cheval des Andes grow on its own roots?","back":"It was flood irrigated from snowmelt channels, and the flooding drowns phylloxera."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-qxfrn1wt","q":"A guest who loves Château Cheval Blanc orders Cheval des Andes expecting the same grapes. What is in the glass?","opts":["Cabernet Franc and Merlot, as in Saint-Émilion","Malbec alone from high Uco Valley vineyards","Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec, with a little Petit Verdot","Bonarda and Malbec from the east of Mendoza"],"a":2,"exp":"Cheval des Andes blends Cabernet Sauvignon and Malbec, with Petit Verdot in some years; Cabernet Franc and Merlot are Cheval Blanc's grapes in Saint-Émilion.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-yizwc8gs","q":"Which Mendoza house did Château Cheval Blanc join in 1999 to make Cheval des Andes?","opts":["Familia Zuccardi","Bodega Norton","Catena Zapata","Terrazas de los Andes"],"a":3,"exp":"Terrazas de los Andes, founded by Moët Hennessy in 1996, is the Argentine partner; Catena Zapata, the likeliest wrong answer, has no part in the wine.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cheval des Andes: The Story","url":"https://www.chevaldesandes.com/the-story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cheval des Andes: The Savoir-Faire","url":"https://www.chevaldesandes.com/the-savoir-faire/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cheval des Andes: 2021 technical sheet","url":"https://www.chevaldesandes.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Cheval-Des-Andes-2021-FT-Eng-3.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Lay and Wheeler: Cheval des Andes producer page","url":"https://laywheelertrading.com/magazine/producer/cheval-des-andes/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Terrazas de los Andes","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terrazas_de_los_Andes","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-tthr6ha9": {"id":"fl-tthr6ha9","key":"norton","group":"mendoza","region":"mendoza","producer":"Bodega Norton","producerId":"","say":"","place":"Perdriel, Luján de Cuyo, Mendoza","founded":"1895","wine":{"name":"Norton Privada","appellation":"Luján de Cuyo","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Malbec in the lead, with Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon, in a blend that shifts with the vintage.","sites":"Old vines, fifty to ninety years old by the house's own sheet, from its vineyards at La Colonia, Lunlunta and Agrelo.","making":"Three days of cold soak come before a ferment of about ten days. A year to sixteen months in French oak barrels follows, by vintage, then six months in bottle before release.","glass":"Dark plum, blackberry and kirsch over cassis and dark chocolate, with a touch of cola and toasty barrel; full, ripe and supple, in the plush style of Luján de Cuyo.","ageing":"Ready within a few years of the vintage, it keeps for ten years and more by the house's own reckoning.","serve":"A Bordeaux glass at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, the bottle decanted an hour ahead when young.","label":[{"term":"Privada","means":"Spanish for private: by the house's account, a blend first made for the owning family to share with friends."},{"term":"Norton","means":"The founder's surname, kept by the house since 1895."},{"term":"Luján de Cuyo","means":"The appellation the house gives the wine: the historic first zone just south of Mendoza city."}]},"table":"Bodega Norton, the Luján de Cuyo house of 1895, calls its old vine blend Privada, private: Malbec with Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon, aged in French oak. Plum, kirsch and dark chocolate make it a ripe, supple partner for grilled beef.","story":"Edmund James Palmer Norton (1865 to 1944), an English engineer, came to Argentina to help build the railway across the Andes and settled in Mendoza. In 1895 he founded his own house at Perdriel in Luján de Cuyo, planting vines brought from France in what the house calls the first vineyard south of the Mendoza river. The house kept his name, and in 1989 it was bought by a member of the Austrian Swarovski crystal family. In 1997 it bought Finca La Colonia, and by its own account it was among the first four wineries to take the controlled appellation of Luján de Cuyo. Privada, the house says, began as a request from the Swarovski family to the house's winemaker: a wine to drink among intimates and to welcome friends, and the Spanish name, private, says so. It blends Malbec with Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon from vines of fifty to ninety years, aged in French oak. Norton Privada is the classic Luján de Cuyo blend: Malbec in the lead, two Bordeaux grapes beside it and the plush frame of the province's historic first zone, from a house whose story runs back to the railway age.","others":[{"name":"Norton D.O.C. Malbec","note":"Malbec under the controlled appellation of Luján de Cuyo, the D.O.C. its label names."},{"name":"Privada Family Malbec","note":"Malbec alone, from the same family cellar range as Privada."}],"traps":["Bodega Norton has nothing to do with Norton, the American grape of Virginia; its wines are made from French varieties.","Privada is a blend of Malbec, Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon, sold lately as Privada Family Blend; the house's Privada Family Malbec is the varietal wine.","The founder was an English railway engineer; the Austrian Swarovski family bought the house only in 1989."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-8cy3xro9","front":"Who founded Bodega Norton in 1895, and what had brought him to Argentina?","back":"Edmund James Palmer Norton, an English engineer who came to help build the railway across the Andes."},{"id":"fl-dpi7q41r","front":"Norton Privada: which three grapes make the blend?","back":"Malbec in the lead, with Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon beside it."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-is556ux5","q":"What does a bottle of Norton Privada hold, grape by grape?","opts":["A blend of Cabernet Franc and Malbec from the east","A blend led by Malbec, with Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon","A Malbec and Syrah blend from San Rafael","A Malbec alone from one vineyard in the Uco Valley"],"a":1,"exp":"Privada blends Malbec with Merlot and Cabernet Sauvignon from the house's old vineyards; the house's Privada Family Malbec is the single variety wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-m6olf7ft","q":"A member of the Swarovski family bought Bodega Norton, the Perdriel house of 1895, in which year?","opts":["1989","1944","2001","1963"],"a":0,"exp":"A member of the Austrian Swarovski crystal family bought Norton in 1989; 1944 is the year its founder died.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Bodega Norton: History","url":"https://www.norton.com.ar/en/history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Bodega Norton: Privada Family Blend","url":"https://www.norton.com.ar/en/our-wines/family-cellar-wines/privada-family-blend-2/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Edmund James Palmer Norton","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_James_Palmer_Norton","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wein-Plus Glossary: Norton, Bodega Norton","url":"https://glossary.wein-plus.eu/norton-bodega-norton","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wine.com: Bodega Norton Privada 2014","url":"https://wine.com/product/norton-privada-2014/169815","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-x8io18zc": {"id":"fl-x8io18zc","key":"concha-y-toro","group":"maipo","region":"maipo-valley","producer":"Concha y Toro","producerId":"p-concha-y-toro","say":"KON-chah ee TOH-roh, dohn mel-CHOR","place":"Pirque, with the Don Melchor vineyard at Puente Alto, Maipo Valley, Chile","founded":"1883","wine":{"name":"Don Melchor Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"DO Puente Alto, Maipo Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, the great majority, with small parts of Cabernet Franc, Merlot and Petit Verdot, each from parcels of its own.","sites":"The Don Melchor vineyard at Puente Alto, on stony alluvial ground on the north bank of the Maipo at about 650 metres, at the foot of the Andes: seven parcels of Cabernet Sauvignon, Parcel 1 among them, planted in 1979, beside smaller parcels of the other three grapes.","making":"Each parcel, and each smaller lot within it, is picked and fermented on its own, and the blend is chosen each year from well over a hundred lots with a consultant from the Médoc. Some fifteen months in French oak barrels follow, about seven in ten of them new, before bottling.","glass":"Blackcurrant and blackberry with red cherry, violet and rose, a note of pencil lead and the fresh mint of the Alto Maipo; fine grained, firm tannin and a long, fresh finish. Age brings cedar, coffee and dark chocolate.","ageing":"Tight for its first eight to ten years in a strong vintage, it then keeps and develops for a quarter of a century and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two, an old one gently off its sediment; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Don Melchor","means":"The founder's name, Melchor Concha y Toro, given to the wine with its first vintage, 1987."},{"term":"Puente Alto","means":"The denomination of origin: the Alto Maipo area on the north bank of the river, at the foot of the Andes."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon","means":"The grape named on the label, the great majority of the blend, with small parts of three others."}]},"table":"First made in 1987 and named for the house's founder, Don Melchor is Concha y Toro's Cabernet from stony ground at Puente Alto, at the foot of the Andes. Blackcurrant, violet and Andean mint ride on fine, firm tannin; decant it young.","story":"Melchor Concha y Toro (1833 to 1892), a Santiago lawyer, deputy and minister of finance, founded the house in 1883 at Pirque, on land owned by his wife, of the Subercaseaux family. As the company tells it, he planted varieties brought from Bordeaux, and a French oenologist came to oversee the first harvest. In 1921 the family made the house a company, its shares held among themselves, and those shares were first traded on the Santiago exchange in 1933. Its fine wine began across the river, at Puente Alto. In 1984, as Viña Don Melchor's own timeline records, Émile Peynaud (1912 to 2004) tasted Cabernet from its Puente Alto vineyard, saw the promise of the ground and proposed his collaborator in the Médoc to lead the work. The first vintage, 1987, was named Don Melchor for the founder. From 1997 the vineyard was studied parcel by parcel, and each parcel has since been picked and fermented apart. In 2019 Don Melchor left the Concha y Toro range to stand as a winery of its own, Viña Don Melchor, within the group. Of the three Puente Alto Cabernets on a Chilean list, Don Melchor is the eldest, begun nine years before Almaviva and twelve before Viñedo Chadwick.","others":[{"name":"Don Melchor DM/01","note":"A single parcel bottling of Viña Don Melchor, drawn mainly from Parcel 1, planted in 1979, first released with the 2022 vintage."},{"name":"Carmín de Peumo","note":"Carmenère from Peumo in the Cachapoal valley, the house's serious case for the grape."},{"name":"Casillero del Diablo","note":"The house's worldwide everyday brand, worth knowing for its scale."}],"traps":["Don Melchor grows at Puente Alto, across the river from the house's historic seat at Pirque, not at Pirque itself.","Almaviva is Concha y Toro's joint venture with the Mouton company, a different wine from Don Melchor though grown on the same Puente Alto ground.","Since 2019 Don Melchor has stood as its own winery, Viña Don Melchor, within the Concha y Toro group: the same wine from the same vineyard."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-b9b91ihe","front":"Whose name does Concha y Toro's Don Melchor carry, and what was its first vintage?","back":"Melchor Concha y Toro, who founded the house at Pirque in 1883; its first vintage was 1987."},{"id":"fl-3dz011jc","front":"Which Bordeaux oenologist tasted Puente Alto Cabernet in 1984 and set Concha y Toro on the road to Don Melchor?","back":"Émile Peynaud (1912 to 2004), who proposed his collaborator in the Médoc to lead the work."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-e1taqjph","q":"Where are the grapes for Concha y Toro's Don Melchor grown?","opts":["Peumo, in the Cachapoal valley","Isla de Maipo, on the warm valley floor","Puente Alto, on the north bank of the Maipo","Pirque, around the house's historic cellars"],"a":2,"exp":"Don Melchor comes from the house's vineyard at Puente Alto, on stony ground at the foot of the Andes; Pirque, across the river, is the house's historic seat, not the vineyard.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-fhs1jmmo","q":"What became of Don Melchor's place within the Concha y Toro group in 2019?","opts":["It moved its vineyard from Puente Alto to Pirque","It was merged into the Almaviva joint venture","It was sold to a Bordeaux first growth","It became a winery of its own, Viña Don Melchor"],"a":3,"exp":"In 2019 Don Melchor left the Concha y Toro range to stand as its own winery within the group; Almaviva remains a separate venture with the Mouton company.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Concha y Toro: From a man's vision, the history of Don Melchor","url":"https://conchaytoro.com/en/about-us/our-history/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Viña Don Melchor: Timeline","url":"https://donmelchor.com/en/story/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Viña Don Melchor: Don Melchor 2020 technical sheet","url":"https://donmelchor.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/TS-Don-Melchor-2020.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Viña Concha y Toro: Annual report on Form 20-F for 2019","url":"https://vinacyt.com/content/uploads/2021/12/vina-concha-y-toro-20f-2019.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Biblioteca del Congreso Nacional de Chile: Melchor Concha y Toro","url":"https://www.bcn.cl/historiapolitica/resenas_parlamentarias/wiki/Melchor_Concha_y_Toro","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-y4jtlog2": {"id":"fl-y4jtlog2","key":"almaviva","group":"maipo","region":"maipo-valley","producer":"Almaviva","producerId":"w-almaviva","say":"al-mah-VEE-vah","place":"Puente Alto, Maipo Valley, Chile","founded":"1997","wine":{"name":"Almaviva","appellation":"DO Puente Alto, Maipo Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, by far the larger part, with Carménère next and, in most vintages, small parts of Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot or Merlot.","sites":"Puente Alto vineyards set aside for Almaviva in the Alto Maipo, on poor, stony alluvial soils that drain freely, cooled each night by cold air from the Andes.","making":"The grapes are cooled and left on their skins, then warmed for fermentation in stainless steel tanks sized to each parcel, with a final maceration; the malolactic follows on its own. The wine spends about ten months in the Grand Chai, then moves to a second year cellar, for sixteen to twenty months in all in French oak barrels, most of them new.","glass":"Cassis and black cherry over graphite and cedar, with a lift of roasted red pepper and paprika from the Carménère and a touch of dark chocolate; full and polished, with fine grained tannin and fresh acidity.","ageing":"Approachable young for its build, it opens fully after about ten years, and a strong year keeps well beyond twenty.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius in a Bordeaux glass; give a young bottle an hour or more in a decanter, and take an old one gently off its sediment.","label":[{"term":"Almaviva","means":"Count Almaviva, hero of The Marriage of Figaro, his name printed in the playwright's own handwriting."},{"term":"The Mapuche design","means":"Three reproductions of a stylised Mapuche design, a vision of the earth and the cosmos, for Chile."},{"term":"Puente Alto","means":"The origin on the label: an Alto Maipo area at the foot of the Andes, shared with Don Melchor."}]},"table":"Concha y Toro and the Mouton Rothschild company first made Almaviva in 1996 from Puente Alto: Cabernet Sauvignon led, with Carménère adding a lift of roasted red pepper. Its label pairs a count from The Marriage of Figaro with a Mapuche design.","story":"The wine came before the company. Its first vintage, 1996, was made from Concha y Toro's Puente Alto vineyards under the joint direction of the Chilean house and the Bordeaux company that Baron Philippe de Rothschild (1902 to 1988) had built around Château Mouton Rothschild, which had already made Opus One in Napa with the Mondavi family. In 1997 his daughter, Baroness Philippine de Rothschild (1933 to 2014), who had succeeded him at Mouton in 1988, and the chairman of Concha y Toro signed the agreement that created Almaviva, one Franco Chilean wine from one estate. Its international launch came in 1998, and the estate built a winery designed by a Chilean architect, its curved roof meant to echo the Andes. The name and the label tell of the two cultures: Count Almaviva is the hero of The Marriage of Figaro by Beaumarchais (1732 to 1799), and the label prints the name in the playwright's own handwriting beside three reproductions of a stylised Mapuche design, a vision of the earth and the cosmos. A second wine, Epu, the Mapuche word for two, followed in later years. For a sommelier, Almaviva completes the set of French ventures abroad with Opus One in Napa and Cheval des Andes in Mendoza, and it is the one of the three that carries Carménère.","others":[{"name":"Epu","note":"The second wine, its name the Mapuche word for two, for the two houses and cultures behind it."}],"traps":["Almaviva's French partner is the Mouton Rothschild company; the Lafite company's Chilean estate is Los Vascos in Colchagua.","Seña is the Errázuriz family's Aconcagua wine, first made with Napa's Mondavi winery; Almaviva is Puente Alto, in the Maipo.","Almaviva carries Carménère in its blend, which Opus One does not."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-uo4g7zva","front":"What do the name and the design on Almaviva's label stand for?","back":"The count of The Marriage of Figaro, in Beaumarchais's own handwriting, for France; three reproductions of a Mapuche design of the earth and the cosmos, for Chile."},{"id":"fl-arq84582","front":"At Almaviva, which came first, the wine or the signed partnership, and in which years?","back":"The 1996 vintage came first; Concha y Toro and the Mouton company signed the agreement in 1997."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-hcici75v","q":"A guest notices roasted red pepper in a glass of Almaviva. Which grape of the blend explains it?","opts":["Syrah","Carménère","Malbec","Petit Verdot"],"a":1,"exp":"Carménère, the Bordeaux grape Chile kept, brings roasted red pepper and paprika to the Cabernet led blend; Petit Verdot joins only some vintages in small parts, and Malbec and Syrah are not in the wine.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-vrkpmdu5","q":"In 1997 Concha y Toro signed the agreement that created Almaviva. Which house signed for the French side?","opts":["The Mouton Rothschild company","Château Cheval Blanc of Saint-Émilion","The Bordeaux merchant house of Ginestet","The Lafite Rothschild company"],"a":0,"exp":"The Mouton company, led by Baroness Philippine de Rothschild (1933 to 2014), signed in 1997; the Lafite company went instead to Colchagua, where its estate is Los Vascos.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Almaviva: History","url":"https://www.almavivawinery.com/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Almaviva: The wine","url":"https://www.almavivawinery.com/the-wine","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Almaviva: Winemaking","url":"https://www.almavivawinery.com/winemaking","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Lea and Sandeman: Almaviva, Maipo, Chile","url":"https://www.leaandsandeman.co.uk/wine-producer/Almaviva-1109-04.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"The Rothschild Archive: Philippine Mathilde Camille de Rothschild","url":"https://family.rothschildarchive.org/people/161-philippine-mathilde-camille-de-rothschild-1933-2014","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-zaqdvkmr": {"id":"fl-zaqdvkmr","key":"santa-rita","group":"maipo","region":"maipo-valley","producer":"Santa Rita","producerId":"p-santa-rita","say":"SAHN-tah REE-tah, KAH-sah reh-AHL","place":"Alto Jahuel, Buin, Maipo Valley, Chile","founded":"1880","wine":{"name":"Casa Real Cabernet Sauvignon","appellation":"Maipo Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Only Cabernet Sauvignon, from old massal selections of the grape on their own roots.","sites":"Old, ungrafted Cabernet at Alto Jahuel on the Andean side of the Maipo, on alluvial soils over a clay subsoil: the Carneros Viejo block, which gave the first vintage, and in later accounts a second old sector nearby.","making":"The first vintage, 1989, was fermented in large wooden casks, and later vintages in stainless steel. Ageing is in French oak barriques, for about fourteen months in the vintages of the 2000s and for eighteen to twenty months, most of the barrels new, from 2018 onwards.","glass":"Red plum and cassis with cedar, tobacco leaf and a faint roasted green pepper; medium bodied for Maipo Cabernet, its dusty, fine tannin lifted by fresh acidity. Old bottles turn to leather, prune and cigar box.","ageing":"Reserved for its first decade in a strong vintage; the house gives it twenty years and more, and the 1989 showed well when tasted at twenty four years old.","serve":"A Bordeaux glass at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour in a decanter for a young bottle, and a gentle decanting off the sediment for an old one.","label":[{"term":"Casa Real","means":"The name of the wine, the house's old vine Cabernet Sauvignon from Alto Jahuel, first made with the 1989 vintage."},{"term":"Cabernet Sauvignon","means":"The one grape of the wine, from old, ungrafted vines at Alto Jahuel."},{"term":"Maipo Valley","means":"Valle del Maipo in Spanish, the origin named on the label; here its Andean side, at Alto Jahuel."}]},"table":"Since 1989 Santa Rita has bottled Casa Real, its Cabernet Sauvignon from old, ungrafted vines at Alto Jahuel, first from the Carneros Viejo block. Cedar, tobacco leaf and red plum over dusty tannin make it the old school face of Maipo Cabernet, and it ages for decades.","story":"The house tells a story older than its vineyard. In 1814, by its account, 120 soldiers of the independence army took refuge at Alto Jahuel in the house of Paula Jaraquemada (1768 to 1851), which stands on the estate; Chile's council of national monuments tells it of 1818, after the patriot defeat at Cancha Rayada. Viña Santa Rita itself was founded on the estate in 1880 by a Santiago businessman, and from the late nineteenth century until the middle of the 1970s it belonged to the family of his son in law, the García Huidobros. In 1972 the estate's park, houses, chapel and cellars were declared a historic monument, and in 1980 the Claro group bought into the house. Its familiar 120 range is named for the soldiers of the legend. Casa Real came with the 1989 vintage, when Cabernet from one old block, Carneros Viejo, was first bottled on its own. Decanter later named the 1989 one of its Wine Legends. Casa Real is the classic Cabernet of a nineteenth century Maipo house, a counterweight to the view that Chilean quality began in the 1990s.","others":[{"name":"120","note":"The familiar range, its number taken from the soldiers who, in the estate's story, sheltered at Alto Jahuel."},{"name":"Medalla Real","note":"A steady Maipo Cabernet, a step below Casa Real."},{"name":"Floresta","note":"The single vineyard range, drawn from several Chilean valleys."}],"traps":["Casa Real is old vine Cabernet from Alto Jahuel; 120 is Santa Rita's everyday range, a different wine altogether.","Older labels and lists call it Casa Real Reserva Especial; it is the same wine, not a separate bottling.","Alto Jahuel names a place at Buin inside the Maipo Valley, not a valley apart."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-o75172fb","front":"In which vintage, and from which old block, did Santa Rita first bottle Casa Real?","back":"1989, from the Carneros Viejo block of old, ungrafted Cabernet at Alto Jahuel."},{"id":"fl-48t0cdxz","front":"Which family owned Santa Rita until the mid 1970s, and which group bought into it in 1980?","back":"The García Huidobro family, kin of the founder by marriage; then the Claro group."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6gta05ry","q":"How was Santa Rita's first Casa Real, the 1989, fermented, and how has the wine been fermented since?","opts":["In stainless steel, later replaced by large wooden casks","In concrete eggs, later replaced by oak barrels","In clay amphorae, later replaced by concrete vats","In large wooden casks, later replaced by stainless steel"],"a":3,"exp":"Decanter's Wine Legend notes that the 1989 was fermented in large wooden casks and that the wine has since been fermented in stainless steel; the reverse order is the trap.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-y7i4n8yj","q":"Santa Rita's 120 range takes its name from a story the house tells of its Alto Jahuel estate. Who were the 120?","opts":["French workers who planted the first vines","Families who farmed the estate for its founder","Patriot soldiers who took refuge on the estate","Barrels in the house's first Bordeaux shipment"],"a":2,"exp":"The house tells of 120 soldiers of the independence army sheltered in Paula Jaraquemada's house on the estate, in 1814 by its account and 1818 by Chile's monuments council; the name honours soldiers, not the estate's workers.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Consejo de Monumentos Nacionales: Parque de la Viña Santa Rita","url":"https://www.monumentos.gob.cl/monumentos/monumentos-historicos/parque-de-la-vina-santa-rita","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Viña Santa Rita: Memoria anual 2019","url":"https://www.santarita.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/MEMORIA-S.A.-VIN%CC%83A-SANTA-RITA-2019-OK-VSR-2.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Santa Rita Casa Real 1989","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-santa-rita-casa-real-1989-428837-428837/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Millesima: Santa Rita Casa Real Cabernet Sauvignon 2022","url":"https://www.millesima.com/santa-rita-casa-real-cabernet-sauvignon-2022.html","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Memoria Chilena: Paula Jaraquemada (1768 to 1851)","url":"https://www.memoriachilena.gob.cl/602/w3-article-100721.html","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-s5qlvbz8": {"id":"fl-s5qlvbz8","key":"cousino-macul","group":"maipo","region":"maipo-valley","producer":"Cousiño Macul","producerId":"","say":"koo-SEE-nyoh mah-KOOL, LOH-tah","place":"Macul, Peñalolén, on the edge of Santiago, Maipo Valley, Chile","founded":"1856","wine":{"name":"Lota","appellation":"Maipo Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"A larger part of Cabernet Sauvignon with Merlot, the share moving with the vintage.","sites":"The old blocks of the Macul estate at the foot of the Andes, which Santiago has grown around: Cabernet Sauvignon planted in 1932 and Merlot from about 1980. The importer's sheet for 2020 also names the family's Buin vineyard.","making":"Fermented in stainless steel tanks, with the malolactic fermentation to follow; the wine is then aged about fifteen months in French oak barriques, most of them new, before bottling.","glass":"Blackberry, cassis and black cherry with cedar, tobacco leaf and a touch of dark chocolate, the Merlot rounding the middle; full bodied, with ripe, firm tannin and a long, savoury finish.","ageing":"It stays firm for five to eight years, then keeps and gains for fifteen years and more in a strong year.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour or two; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Lota","means":"The wine's name, shared with the coal port near Concepción whose mines the founder worked."},{"term":"Cousiño Macul","means":"The family's name joined to that of the hacienda on the edge of Santiago it bought in 1856."},{"term":"Maipo Valley","means":"The denomination of origin, Valle del Maipo in Spanish: Santiago's own valley."}]},"table":"For its hundred and fiftieth year, in 2006, Cousiño Macul launched Lota, a blend of old vine Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot from the Macul estate that Santiago has spread around. Expect blackberry, cedar and a touch of dark chocolate, with ripe, firm tannin and a long, savoury finish.","story":"The Hacienda Macul, at the foot of the Andes on the edge of Santiago, was bought in 1856 by Matías Cousiño (1810 to 1863), whose fortune grew with the coal mines he worked at Lota, on the coast near Concepción; the house counts that year as its founding. His son and his son's wife renewed its vines in the 1860s, bringing, as the house records, Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot from Pauillac, Chardonnay from Burgundy and Sauvignon from Martillac. A Cabernet harvest of 1927, in the house's telling, gave rise to Antiguas Reservas, the line it calls its emblem. By the 1990s Santiago had spread around the estate, and in 1996 the family bought land at Buin, further south in the Maipo, while the historic cellars and the old vines stayed at Macul. Lota was launched in 2006 to mark the house's hundred and fiftieth year: a blend of Cabernet Sauvignon from vines planted in 1932 and Merlot from about 1980, both from the old estate. It shares its name with the coal port where the founder worked the mines. Against the Puente Alto blends, Lota is the old Santiago house's answer, grown on a vineyard the city has surrounded.","others":[{"name":"Antiguas Reservas Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Classic Maipo Cabernet in a line the house dates to the Cabernet harvest of 1927."},{"name":"Antiguas Reservas Chardonnay","note":"The same line in white, from Chardonnay the house says the family brought from Burgundy in the 1860s."}],"traps":["Lota is the house's old vine blend, launched in 2006; Antiguas Reservas is its classic Cabernet line, dated by the house to 1927.","Lota the wine grows at Macul in Santiago; Lota the town is the coal port near Concepción where the family mined coal, not a vineyard site.","Cousiño Macul's founding year is 1856, older than Santa Rita's 1880 and Concha y Toro's 1883."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-5c05c7wz","front":"When did Cousiño Macul launch Lota, and what did the year mark?","back":"In 2006, to mark the house's hundred and fiftieth year since 1856."},{"id":"fl-5m1n97ub","front":"Lota is a place as well as a wine of Cousiño Macul: what was it to the founding family?","back":"The coal port near Concepción where Matías Cousiño (1810 to 1863) worked the mines."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6yp48ouq","q":"What goes into Cousiño Macul's Lota, and from where?","opts":["Cabernet Franc and Malbec from Pirque","Old vine Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot from Macul","Carménère and Syrah from Colchagua","Cabernet Sauvignon alone from Puente Alto"],"a":1,"exp":"Lota blends Cabernet Sauvignon from Macul vines of 1932 with Merlot of about 1980; the Puente Alto Cabernets are Don Melchor, Almaviva and Viñedo Chadwick.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-jzmzc0zi","q":"Santiago's spread around the Macul estate led Cousiño Macul to buy land elsewhere in the Maipo. Where, and when?","opts":["Buin, in 1996","Pirque, in 1883","Puente Alto, in 1987","Alto Jahuel, in 1880"],"a":0,"exp":"The family bought land at Buin in 1996, keeping its cellars and old vines at Macul; 1883 at Pirque is Concha y Toro's founding and 1880 at Alto Jahuel Santa Rita's.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Cousiño Macul: History","url":"https://cousinomacul.com/en/pages/historia","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Cousiño Macul: Antiguas Reservas, 90 years of the line","url":"https://cousinomacul.com/en/blogs/noticias/antiguas-reservas-90-anos-de-la-linea-mas-emblematica-de-cousino-macul","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Winebow: Cousiño Macul","url":"https://www.winebow.com/our-brands/cousino-macul","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Winebow: Cousiño-Macul Lota 2020","url":"https://www.winebow.com/our-brands/cousino-macul/cousino-macul-lota/2020","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Biblioteca del Congreso Nacional de Chile: Matías Cousiño Jorquera","url":"https://www.bcn.cl/historiapolitica/resenas_parlamentarias/wiki/Mat%C3%ADas_Cousi%C3%B1o_Jorquera","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-bxqwnm8t": {"id":"fl-bxqwnm8t","key":"vinedo-chadwick","group":"maipo","region":"maipo-valley","producer":"Viñedo Chadwick","producerId":"w-vinedo-chadwick","say":"vee-NYEH-doh CHAD-wick","place":"Puente Alto, Maipo Valley, Chile","founded":"1999","wine":{"name":"Viñedo Chadwick","appellation":"Maipo Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, alone in many vintages, with a few per cent of Petit Verdot in some since 2016.","sites":"The family's old polo field at Puente Alto, a single vineyard of stony alluvial ground on the Maipo's north bank, at about 650 metres where the Andes begin; planted from 1992.","making":"Lot by lot, the fruit ferments in small tanks, of stainless steel and in some vintages concrete, and stays on its skins for eighteen to forty days by lot and year. The malolactic follows in oak, and the wine is aged about twenty two months, most of it in new French oak barrels and a share in large oak foudres.","glass":"Blueberry, cassis and dark cherry with liquorice, fresh mint and a touch of bitter chocolate; silky and tightly knit rather than broad, its fine, gravelly tannin set against fresh acidity.","ageing":"Firm in youth, it opens after about a decade when the vintage is strong and keeps for twenty years and more.","serve":"Pour at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius into a Bordeaux glass, a young bottle after an hour or two in a decanter.","label":[{"term":"Viñedo Chadwick","means":"Spanish for the Chadwick vineyard: the wine takes the family's name and comes from one vineyard."},{"term":"Maipo Valley","means":"The wider origin this label gives; the vineyard itself lies at Puente Alto, on the valley's Andean side."}]},"table":"Viñedo Chadwick's 2000, from the family's old polo field at Puente Alto, planted in 1992, placed first at the Berlin Tasting of 2004, ahead of Château Lafite. It is the Cabernet of the family behind Viña Errázuriz, its blueberry and liquorice carried on silky, fine tannin.","story":"The Errázuriz house was founded in 1870 in the Aconcagua valley, north of Santiago, but its Maipo wine began as a sports ground. The family's patriarch, a polo player, had built his home and a polo field on an estate he bought at Puente Alto, the old Viña San José de Tocornal; by the family's account, the land reform of the 1960s led him to sell most of it, but he kept the house and the field. In 1992, at his son's suggestion, the field was planted with Cabernet Sauvignon; he did not live to taste its first vintage, and the wine is named in his memory. The first vintage was 1999. On 23 January 2004, in Berlin, the family set Chilean wines blind beside Bordeaux first growths and Italian reds before thirty six European critics and writers: sixteen wines of 2000 and 2001 in all. Viñedo Chadwick 2000, only its second vintage, placed first and Seña 2001 second, with Château Lafite 2000 third. Petit Verdot joined the blend in small measure with the 2016 vintage, while the warm 2020 went back to Cabernet alone. The Berlin result is the date to know: Chile's own blind tasting coup, held in Berlin and not in Paris, and won by a Puente Alto Cabernet from a former polo field.","others":[{"name":"Seña","note":"The family's Aconcagua blend, begun with the Mondavi winery of Napa, which placed second at Berlin with its 2001."},{"name":"Don Maximiano Founder's Reserve","note":"The Errázuriz house's historic Aconcagua red, named for its founder."},{"name":"Las Pizarras","note":"Chardonnay and Pinot Noir from schist soils on the cool Aconcagua coast."}],"traps":["The Berlin Tasting was held in Berlin in January 2004, not in Paris and not in the 1970s; Viñedo Chadwick 2000 placed first.","Viñedo Chadwick is Puente Alto, in the Maipo; Seña, from the same family, is Aconcagua.","Don Melchor and Almaviva share the Puente Alto subzone but are Concha y Toro's wine and its venture's, not the Chadwick family's."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-41wczmza","front":"What was the ground of Viñedo Chadwick before it was planted in 1992?","back":"The polo field of the family's patriarch at Puente Alto; he did not live to taste its first vintage, and the wine is named in his memory."},{"id":"fl-f9ygt5ix","front":"What grape makes Viñedo Chadwick, and what joined it in small measure from 2016?","back":"Cabernet Sauvignon, alone in many vintages; a few per cent of Petit Verdot in some years since."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-9nlqr3nl","q":"In what vessels is Viñedo Chadwick raised before bottling, by the family's sheets?","opts":["Mostly clay amphorae, with a share in old chestnut vats","Mostly old American casks, with a share in concrete","Mostly new French barrels, with a share in large foudres","Mostly stainless steel, with a share in used barrels"],"a":2,"exp":"The family's sheets give about twenty two months, mostly in new French oak barrels, with a share in large oak foudres; the sheets read name only French oak.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-pr02277i","q":"At the Berlin Tasting of 2004, Viñedo Chadwick 2000 placed first among sixteen wines tasted blind. Which wine came third?","opts":["Sassicaia 2000","Château Margaux 2001","Château Latour 2000","Château Lafite 2000"],"a":3,"exp":"Lafite 2000 came third, behind Viñedo Chadwick 2000 and Seña 2001; the placings of Margaux and Latour differ between the published lists.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Viñedo Chadwick: Past vintages","url":"https://vinedochadwick.cl/past-vintage","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Viñedo Chadwick 2000","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-vinedo-chadwick-2000-375867/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Viñedo Chadwick: Legacy of excellence","url":"https://vinedochadwick.cl/legacy-of-excellence","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Surprise result for Margaux and Lafite against New World","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/surprise-result-for-margaux-and-lafite-against-new-world-71333","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Hatch Mansfield: Viñedo Chadwick 2013","url":"https://www.hatchmansfield.com/our-winemakers/vinedo-chadwick/wine/vinedo-chadwick-2013-3x75cl/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-kaicy97f": {"id":"fl-kaicy97f","key":"montes","group":"colchagua","region":"colchagua","producer":"Montes","producerId":"p-montes","say":"MOHN-tess AHL-fah EM","place":"Apalta, near Santa Cruz, Colchagua Valley, Chile","founded":"1988","wine":{"name":"Montes Alpha M","appellation":"Colchagua Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon, about four fifths, with Cabernet Franc and smaller shares of Merlot and Petit Verdot.","sites":"The house's Finca de Apalta, on the granite hillsides of the Apalta horseshoe near Santa Cruz; by the house's sheets the fruit comes from the estate's steepest slopes, cropped at low yields.","making":"Picked into small boxes in the cool of the day and sorted by bunch and by berry, the fruit falls uncrushed by gravity into stainless steel, soaks cold for about five days and ferments for about ten. The wine then spends eighteen months in French oak barriques, all of them new in the early vintages and most of them in later ones.","glass":"Ripe cherry, plum and cassis with a faint note of mint, over cedar, vanilla, tobacco and toast from the oak; concentrated, with dense, silky tannin and a long finish.","ageing":"Ready a few years after release, it goes on developing in the cellar for ten years and more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant a young bottle for an hour; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Alpha M","means":"The house's Bordeaux blend from its Apalta estate, made from the 1996 vintage onwards."},{"term":"Apalta","means":"The horseshoe of granite hills near Santa Cruz, an area of the Colchagua Valley under Chile's origin decree since 2018."},{"term":"Colchagua Valley","means":"The southern zone of the Rapel Valley in Chile's origin decree, warm and inland."}]},"table":"On granite hillsides at Apalta, near Santa Cruz, Viña Montes grows the Cabernet Sauvignon that leads Montes Alpha M, its Bordeaux blend, given eighteen months in French barriques. The oak shows in cedar and toast over ripe cherry and cassis, the whole concentrated and long.","story":"The house's own timeline starts with a harvest: in 1987 its founding winemaker and a partner picked the grapes of the first Montes Alpha Cabernet Sauvignon, and in 1988 four partners founded the company under the name Discover Wines; it later took the name Viña Montes, after the winemaker. The partners looked for hillside ground in Colchagua, and the timeline records the purchase of their Apalta estate in 1995: a horseshoe of granite slopes near Santa Cruz, where the vines were cropped low. Alpha M followed with the 1996 vintage, a Bordeaux blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon from the estate's steepest slopes and aged in new French barriques, the first of the flagship wines in the house's timeline. The same hill went on to give the house's Syrah, Montes Folly, from 2000, and part of its Carmenère, Purple Angel, from 2003. In December 2004 Montes opened a new winery on the estate, its design based, the house says, on the principles of feng shui, and the fruit falls by gravity from sorting table to tank. Apalta became an area of Chile's origin decree in 2018. Alpha M marks the moment a Chilean house set out to make a Cabernet blend for the cellar from one hillside.","others":[{"name":"Montes Alpha Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"The wine of the house's first harvest in 1987, by its timeline, and the root of the Alpha range."},{"name":"Montes Purple Angel","note":"Carmenère with a little Petit Verdot, picked late enough to lose the grape's green edge."},{"name":"Montes Folly","note":"Syrah from the steepest slopes of the Apalta estate."}],"traps":["Cabernet Sauvignon leads Montes Alpha M; the house's Carmenère is Purple Angel, and its Syrah is Folly.","Montes Alpha M and Montes Alpha Cabernet Sauvignon are different wines: the M is the Apalta estate blend, made from 1996.","Apalta is an area within the Colchagua Valley, itself a zone of the Rapel Valley, not a valley of its own."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-r68io1dl","front":"Montes Alpha M: which grape leads the blend, and how long is it aged in oak?","back":"Cabernet Sauvignon, about four fifths, with Cabernet Franc, Merlot and Petit Verdot; eighteen months in French barriques."},{"id":"fl-n2z94d9s","front":"Which vintage gave the first Montes Alpha M, and from which estate?","back":"The 1996, from the house's Finca de Apalta on the granite hillsides near Santa Cruz."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-f5ph3dmi","q":"A guest expects Montes Alpha M to be a Carmenère. Which grape in fact leads its blend?","opts":["Carmenère","Cabernet Sauvignon","Cabernet Franc","Petit Verdot"],"a":1,"exp":"Alpha M is about four fifths Cabernet Sauvignon, with Cabernet Franc, Merlot and Petit Verdot in smaller shares; Carmenère leads the house's Purple Angel instead.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-iptx6bfk","q":"Under what name did the four partners behind Viña Montes found their company in 1988?","opts":["Discover Wines","Viña Apalta","Santa Cruz Vintners","Bodegas Alpha"],"a":0,"exp":"The house's timeline gives Discover Wines in 1988, a year after the first Montes Alpha harvest; the Apalta estate was bought in 1995.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Montes: Our History","url":"https://www.monteswines.com/EN/historia","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Montes: Our Cellar","url":"https://monteswines.com/EN/bodega","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Montes: Alpha M 2020 technical sheet","url":"https://www.monteswines.com/fichas_tecnicas/ES/ft/TDAlphaM2020ESP.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Montes: Alpha M in the house's shop","url":"https://tienda.monteswines.com/montes-alpha-m-premium-cabernet-sauvignon-62/p","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Wine Legend, Montes Alpha M 1999","url":"https://www.decanter.com/learn/wine-legend-montes-alpha-m-1999-422869/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-gvl0i0ck": {"id":"fl-gvl0i0ck","key":"lapostolle","group":"colchagua","region":"colchagua","producer":"Lapostolle","producerId":"p-lapostolle","say":"lah-poss-TOLL, klohss ah-PAHL-tah","place":"Apalta, near Santa Cruz, Colchagua Valley, Chile","founded":"1994","wine":{"name":"Clos Apalta","appellation":"Apalta, Colchagua Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Carmenère, from about half to two thirds, with Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot, and in some years a little Cabernet Franc or Petit Verdot.","sites":"Old vines on the estate's Apalta hillside, planted in the early twentieth century from a French massal selection and never grafted, on colluvial granite soils with layers of clay.","making":"In the later sheets read, every grape is destemmed by hand at the estate's gravity winery; the 2003 was destemmed by hand only in part. Most of the wine ferments in French oak vats of 75 hectolitres, punched down by hand over four to five weeks on the skins, and goes through malolactic in new barrels. It then ages about two years in French barriques, most of them new, and is bottled without filtration.","glass":"Blackberry, blueberry and cassis with clove, black pepper and dried fig, then graphite, cedar, cocoa, leather and tobacco; full and velvety, with fine, polished tannin and a long, smoky finish.","ageing":"Made for a long life, by the house's sheets: decant a young bottle or give it several years in the cellar, after which it keeps developing for many more.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius, 17 by the house's sheets, in a Bordeaux glass; a young bottle wants at least an hour in a decanter.","label":[{"term":"Clos Apalta","means":"A proprietary name for the estate's grand vin; clos here carries no French appellation meaning."},{"term":"Apalta","means":"An area of the Colchagua Valley since the decree of 2018: the horseshoe of hills near Santa Cruz."}]},"table":"Carmenère leads Clos Apalta, Lapostolle's blend from old, ungrafted vines on the Apalta hill, destemmed by hand and aged about two years in French oak, most of it new. In the glass come blackberry, dried fig and tobacco on a velvety texture.","story":"The family behind Lapostolle made its name in liqueur: an ancestor, Louis-Alexandre Marnier Lapostolle (1857 to 1930), created Grand Marnier, cognac with bitter orange, in 1880. More than a century later his descendants looked to Chile, and in 1994 a couple of the family founded Lapostolle in the Colchagua Valley, with an old vineyard on the Apalta hill at its heart. Its vines had been planted early in the twentieth century from a French massal selection and never grafted. The first Clos Apalta followed with the 1997 vintage, a blend of Merlot, Carmenère and Cabernet Sauvignon. Carmenère, which Chile had long grown as Merlot until the two were told apart in 1994, came to lead the blend. A winery on the slope moves the wine by gravity, and in its later vintages every grape is destemmed by hand before it ferments in large oak vats. The estate's vineyards are farmed organically and biodynamically. When Chile's flagships are listed, Clos Apalta is the wine most often set beside Almaviva, and the reference for Carmenère made to age.","others":[{"name":"Le Petit Clos","note":"A second blend from the estate's Apalta vines, in the Bordeaux manner by the house's sheets."},{"name":"Lapostolle Cuvée Alexandre","note":"The range below, bottled by grape: Cabernet Sauvignon, Carmenère or Chardonnay."}],"traps":["Clos Apalta is led by Carmenère, while Almaviva and Don Melchor in the Maipo are led by Cabernet Sauvignon.","Clos here is a proprietary name with no French appellation meaning; Clos Apalta is a Chilean wine from the Colchagua Valley.","Lapostolle was founded by the family of Grand Marnier, not by a Bordeaux château; Los Vascos is the Lafite venture in Colchagua."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xe440dz3","front":"How are Clos Apalta's grapes destemmed, and in what vessels does most of it ferment?","back":"By hand at the estate, every grape in the later vintages; then mostly in French oak vats of 75 hectolitres."},{"id":"fl-kly05xuh","front":"Which liqueur did an ancestor of the family that founded Lapostolle create in 1880?","back":"Grand Marnier, cognac with bitter orange."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-l0r1wula","q":"Which grape leads the blend in Lapostolle's Clos Apalta?","opts":["Malbec","Syrah","Carmenère","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":2,"exp":"Carmenère, about half to two thirds of the blend, leads Clos Apalta; Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot join it in smaller shares.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-7rg4rxrd","q":"Lapostolle's first Clos Apalta came a few years after the house's founding in 1994. Which vintage was it?","opts":["1994","1987","2005","1997"],"a":3,"exp":"The first Clos Apalta was the 1997, three years after the founding of 1994, the year Carmenère was also told apart from Merlot in Chile; 1987 is the first Montes Alpha harvest.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Clos Apalta: History of the domaine","url":"https://closapalta.com/domaine/history","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Clos Apalta: technical sheet for 2015","url":"https://www.closapalta.com/ClosApalta/media/pdfs/technicalsheets/closapalta/CLOS-APALTA-2015-ENG.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Clos Apalta: the 2003 vintage","url":"https://www.closapalta.com/wine/clos-apalta/2003","kind":"primary"},{"title":"BK Wine Magazine: Lapostolle and Clos Apalta","url":"https://www.bkwine.com/features/wine-producer-profiles/lapostolle-clos-apalta/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Alexandre Marnier-Lapostolle","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandre_Marnier-Lapostolle","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3rjd3zr5": {"id":"fl-3rjd3zr5","key":"casa-silva","group":"colchagua","region":"colchagua","producer":"Casa Silva","producerId":"","say":"KAH-sah SEEL-vah, lohss LEEN-gess","place":"Angostura, San Fernando, with the vineyard at Los Lingues, Colchagua Valley","founded":"1997","wine":{"name":"Casa Silva Microterroir de Los Lingues Carmenère","appellation":"Colchagua Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Carmenère alone, from plants selected in each block of the Los Lingues vineyard.","sites":"The Los Lingues vineyard on the eastern edge of the Colchagua Valley, in the Andean foothills near San Fernando: clay loam of alluvial and colluvial origin, low in organic matter and fertility, from fine sand to angular granite stones.","making":"By the house's sheet for 2021, the berries were sorted a second time and soaked cold for ten days, then fermented in French oak, in barrels of 500 litres and foudres of 1,000 litres, with a long maceration after. Malolactic followed in barrel, and the wine aged fifteen months in French oak.","glass":"Ripe red plum and blackberry with a hint of coffee and a soft touch of black pepper; deep ruby with a violet rim, supple, with good structure and soft tannin.","ageing":"Drinks well a few years after the vintage and, by the house's account, keeps for about fifteen years.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; an hour's decanting suits a young bottle; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Microterroir","means":"The house's name for the vines its soil study picked out, block by block, at Los Lingues."},{"term":"Los Lingues","means":"Part of the wine's name, for the Andean foothill vineyard; an area by the decree of 2018, though the sheet's origin is Colchagua Valley."},{"term":"Carmenère","means":"The Bordeaux grape Chile long grew as Merlot, bottled here on its own."}]},"table":"A soil study begun in 2005 with the University of Talca singled out, in each block, the vines of Casa Silva's Microterroir de Los Lingues: Carmenère alone, from the Andean foothills. The grape comes fully ripe here, with red plum, blackberry and a hint of coffee.","story":"The Silva family's vines in Colchagua go back to a French ancestor from near Saint-Émilion, who reached Chile in the late nineteenth century and, in the family's own history, bought the Angostura estate near San Fernando early in the twentieth. For decades his descendants grew grapes and sold their fruit and wine in bulk to larger houses. In the late 1970s the family began restoring the old vineyards and cellar, and in April 1997, on the proposal of the eldest son of the fifth generation, the family founded Viña Casa Silva to bottle the estate's wine under its own name. The house's vineyard at Los Lingues lies on the eastern edge of the valley at the foot of the Andes, and in 2005 the house began a study of its soils and vines with the University of Talca, which the house describes as the first microterroir study in Chile. Plot by plot, the work showed which ground gave which fruit and guided the replanting, and the Microterroir Carmenère gathers the vines it picked out in each block. In 2018 Chile's origin decree named Los Lingues an area of the valley. The wine's Carmenère comes from a cooler foothill site, picked ripe without the green edge, by a family that measured its ground before it named it.","others":[{"name":"Casa Silva S7 Carmenère","note":"Carmenère from a single block of the Los Lingues vineyard."},{"name":"Casa Silva 1912 Vines Sauvignon Gris","note":"A white from old Sauvignon Gris vines, named for the year the house gives for their planting."}],"traps":["Casa Silva's Los Lingues lies at the Andean edge of Colchagua, not on the Apalta hill and not at Paredones by the sea.","Microterroir is Carmenère alone, not a blend in the way of Clos Apalta, which adds Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot.","Viña Casa Silva bottled its first wines in 1997, though the family's vineyards in Colchagua are far older."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-xc669d2b","front":"What work lies behind the choice of vines for Casa Silva's Microterroir?","back":"A study of the soils and vines of Los Lingues, begun in 2005 with the University of Talca, plot by plot."},{"id":"fl-rj1szkgt","front":"Where is Los Lingues, the source of Casa Silva's Microterroir?","back":"On the eastern edge of the Colchagua Valley, in the Andean foothills near San Fernando; an area of the decree since 2018."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-cb0jmtfh","q":"A guest orders Casa Silva's Microterroir from Los Lingues. What is in the glass?","opts":["Malbec from old vines","Carmenère alone","A blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon","Syrah with a little Viognier"],"a":1,"exp":"Only Carmenère goes into Microterroir, from vines its soil study picked out at Los Lingues; it is not a blend led by Cabernet Sauvignon.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-h3747tln","q":"When did the Silva family found Viña Casa Silva to bottle the estate's wines under its own name?","opts":["1997","2005","1892","1978"],"a":0,"exp":"The house dates its own label to April 1997; the family's vines are older, the restoration began in the late 1970s and the soil study in 2005.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Casa Silva: Our vineyard, tradition and innovation","url":"https://www.casasilva.cl/en/our-vineyard/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Casa Silva: Microterroir Carmenère","url":"https://www.casasilva.cl/en/producto/casa-silva-microterroir-carmenere/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Casa Silva: Microterroir technical sheet for 2021","url":"https://www.casasilva.cl/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/MICROTERROIR-2021-ES.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Casa Silva: the Microterroir and Genome research projects","url":"https://www.casasilva.cl/en/revolutionizing-chiles-wine-industry-casa-silvas-groundbreaking-microterroir-and-genome-research-projects/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"The Drinks Business: research highlights Chile's potential","url":"https://www.thedrinksbusiness.com/2009/10/research-highlights-chiles-potential/","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-3vcoi8ib": {"id":"fl-3vcoi8ib","key":"viu-manent","group":"colchagua","region":"colchagua","producer":"Viu Manent","producerId":"","say":"VEE-oo mah-NENT","place":"Cunaco, Colchagua Valley, Chile","founded":"1935","wine":{"name":"Viu 1","appellation":"Colchagua Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Malbec, the great majority, with a little Cabernet Sauvignon and in some years a touch of Petit Verdot.","sites":"Block 4 of the house's San Carlos vineyard near Cunaco, an old planting of Malbec on alluvial, sandy clay soil with good drainage; the house dates the vines to more than a century ago.","making":"After a cold soak of about six days, the must ferments in small stainless steel tanks with short pump overs, about three weeks on the skins in all. Its fifteen months or so in French oak are spent, in recent editions, partly in large untoasted foudres and partly in barriques.","glass":"Violet and fresh plum with blueberry and blackberry and a hint of graphite; balanced and concentrated, with fine, velvety tannin and a clear finish that persists.","ageing":"It can be drunk on release, after its time in oak, and the house's own pages give it about a decade to develop.","serve":"16 to 18 degrees Celsius; decant before serving, as the house advises; a Bordeaux glass.","label":[{"term":"Viu 1","means":"The house's flagship Malbec from Block 4, released in numbered editions from the 1999 vintage."},{"term":"Colchagua Valley","means":"The origin printed on the label, home to the San Carlos vineyard: the Rapel Valley's southern zone."}]},"table":"Block 4 of the San Carlos vineyard near Cunaco, old Malbec on sandy clay, gives Viu Manent its Viu 1, scented with violet and plum and fine in tannin. By its own account, the house was first in Chile to bottle Malbec alone under the grape's name, in 1993.","story":"In 1935 a Catalan immigrant and his two sons founded Bodegas Viu in Santiago, bottling wine for Chilean tables under the Vinos Viu brand. The family wanted vineyards of its own, and in 1966 one of the sons bought the Hacienda San Carlos de Cunaco in the Colchagua Valley, an estate whose old vines, the house says, came from France before phylloxera. Among them were plots of Malbec. In 1993, by the house's own account, Viu Manent became the first Chilean winery to bottle, label and sell a wine of Malbec alone under the grape's name. The house then set its old Malbec block, Block 4 of the San Carlos vineyard, apart, and Viu 1 was first made from the 1999 vintage, as a tribute to the son who had bought the estate. Each vintage made is counted as an edition, the 2021 being the twentieth on the house's sheet, and the bottles are numbered. A little Cabernet Sauvignon joins the Malbec, and recent editions age partly in large, untoasted foudres. Viu 1 answers a guest who thinks of Malbec as Argentina's alone: an old Colchagua block of Malbec, bottled under the grape's name.","others":[{"name":"Viu Manent Secreto Malbec","note":"Malbec of the Secreto range, which adds about fifteen per cent of other grapes whose names the house keeps secret."}],"traps":["Malbec, not Carmenère or Cabernet Sauvignon, makes up most of Viu 1, though a little Cabernet often joins it.","Viu Manent was founded in Santiago in 1935 as a bottler; its Colchagua estate at Cunaco came only in 1966.","Viu 1 counts its vintages as editions, the first from 1999; the number on the label is part of the name, not a vintage."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-eu72qblz","front":"From which block and vineyard does Viu 1 draw its Malbec?","back":"Block 4 of the San Carlos vineyard near Cunaco, an old planting the house dates to more than a century ago."},{"id":"fl-tbjlq3tv","front":"What did Viu Manent bottle in 1993, by its own account?","back":"The first Chilean wine of Malbec alone, labelled and sold under the grape's name."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-6l3qt42c","q":"What is the main grape of Viu Manent's Viu 1?","opts":["Carmenère","Syrah","Malbec","Cabernet Franc"],"a":2,"exp":"Malbec makes up most of Viu 1, from Block 4 of the San Carlos vineyard, with a little Cabernet Sauvignon; Carmenère plays no part in it.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-adv4vsp1","q":"Viu Manent began as a Santiago bottler in 1935. Which year brought the family the San Carlos de Cunaco estate in Colchagua?","opts":["1935","1993","1999","1966"],"a":3,"exp":"The Hacienda San Carlos de Cunaco was bought in 1966; 1993 brought the first bottled Malbec and 1999 the first Viu 1.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"Viu Manent: Great milestones","url":"https://viumanent.cl/en/quienes-somos/historia/grandes-hitos/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Viu Manent: Icon wines","url":"https://viumanent.cl/en/our-wines/iconos/","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Viu Manent: Viu 1 technical sheet for 2021","url":"https://viumanent.cl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/2021-en-ficha-tecnica-viu1-1.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Viu Manent: Viu 1 technical sheet for 2020","url":"https://viumanent.cl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/2020-en-ficha-tecnica-viu1-1.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Viña Viu Manent","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vi%C3%B1a_Viu_Manent","kind":"secondary"}]},
"fl-u00su5aq": {"id":"fl-u00su5aq","key":"neyen","group":"colchagua","region":"colchagua","producer":"Neyen","producerId":"","say":"neh-YEN, ess-PEE-ree-too deh ah-PAHL-tah","place":"Apalta, near Santa Cruz, Colchagua Valley, Chile","founded":"","wine":{"name":"Neyen Espíritu de Apalta","appellation":"Colchagua Valley","classification":"","colour":"red","grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon and Carmenère, in near equal parts in the vintages read; early vintages leaned towards Carmenère and some carried Petit Verdot as well.","sites":"Old ungrafted vines on the Apalta estate, Cabernet Sauvignon planted around 1890 and Carmenère in 1936 by the house's account, on granite soils washed down from the hills and clay and silt laid by the river.","making":"Hand picking early in the morning and two sortings come first; the grapes then ferment with native yeasts in tanks of 5,000 litres over three to four weeks. On the sheets read, each wine spent about fourteen months in French barriques before blending and some six months more in a large foudre.","glass":"Raspberry and blackcurrant with cedar, vanilla, leather and a subtle pepper; fresh and composed rather than heavy, with smooth tannin and a finish that lingers.","ageing":"It opens a few years after the vintage; merchants' notes give a strong year ten years and more in the cellar.","serve":"In a Bordeaux glass at 16 to 18 degrees Celsius, after an hour in a decanter if the bottle is young.","label":[{"term":"Espíritu de Apalta","means":"The spirit of Apalta: the meaning of Neyen, the name the house says locals gave the hill's energy."},{"term":"Colchagua Valley","means":"The valley named as the origin, a zone of the Rapel Valley that takes in the Apalta hill."}]},"table":"Neyen Espíritu de Apalta joins Cabernet Sauvignon and Carmenère in near equal parts from ungrafted Apalta vines planted around 1890 and in 1936. Fresh rather than heavy, it shows raspberry and blackcurrant with cedar and a subtle pepper.","story":"Around 1890, by the house's account, Cabernet Sauvignon was planted on the estate's corner of Apalta and the walls of its old winery went up, the oldest in Apalta as the house tells it. Carmenère followed in 1936, and the vines were never grafted. In the early 1970s the Rojas family bought the property and, for decades, sold most of its fruit to others. In 2002 the family created Neyen, which the house glosses as the spirit of Apalta, the energy locals feel on the hill, to bottle a small share of the old vines' fruit under its own name, and the first vintage was the 2003. The blend of Cabernet Sauvignon and Carmenère from the old vines has been Neyen's flagship ever since, its shares moving from vintage to vintage. In 2016 the Spanish sherry house González Byass bought a majority of Veramonte, the Chilean company that had taken in Neyen, and with it the Apalta estate. In one glass, then, Neyen pours what old ungrafted vines can give: Cabernet Sauvignon of about 1890 and Carmenère of 1936 from one hill.","others":[{"name":"Neyen Ancestral de Apalta Cabernet Sauvignon","note":"Cabernet Sauvignon alone, which the house presents as the rebirth of old vines in its historic Apalta vineyard."}],"traps":["Near equal parts of Cabernet Sauvignon and Carmenère make Neyen Espíritu de Apalta; its neighbour Clos Apalta gives Carmenère the lead.","Not every old vine at Neyen is of one age: the house dates its Cabernet Sauvignon to about 1890 and its Carmenère to 1936."],"cards":[{"id":"fl-o9x07nvh","front":"Neyen Espíritu de Apalta: which two grapes, and from vines planted in which years?","back":"Cabernet Sauvignon of about 1890 and Carmenère of 1936, both ungrafted, by the house's account."},{"id":"fl-wj6y120z","front":"What does the name Neyen mean, as the Apalta house tells it?","back":"The spirit of Apalta: the name locals gave the energy of the hill."}],"questions":[{"id":"fl-8vqzba2p","q":"What sets apart the vines behind Neyen Espíritu de Apalta?","opts":["High vineyards on the Andean slopes","Old ungrafted vines, the eldest planted around 1890","Young vines grafted onto American rootstock","Vines on pergolas close to the sea"],"a":1,"exp":"Neyen draws on ungrafted Cabernet Sauvignon of about 1890 and Carmenère of 1936 on the Apalta hill, inland near Santa Cruz, not young grafted vines.","tag":"wine"},{"id":"fl-kwxo2pip","q":"Which Spanish house bought a majority of Veramonte, and with it the Neyen estate in Apalta, in 2016?","opts":["González Byass","Bodegas Torres","Vega Sicilia","Freixenet"],"a":0,"exp":"González Byass of Jerez bought the majority in 2016; Torres came to Chile much earlier, at Curicó in 1979.","tag":"story"}],"sources":[{"title":"González Byass: Bodega Neyen de Apalta","url":"https://www.gonzalezbyass.com/es/bodegas-marcas/neyen-de-apalta-1","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Wine Industry Advisor: Viña Neyen de Apalta marks twenty years of winemaking","url":"https://wineindustryadvisor.com/2023/07/20/vina-neyen-de-apalta-celebrates-20th-anniversary-of-winemaking/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Viñedos Veramonte: Neyen technical sheet","url":"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0471/0881/7051/files/FT_Veramonte_Neyen.pdf","kind":"primary"},{"title":"Decanter: Colchagua in focus","url":"https://www.decanter.com/features/colchagua-in-focus-247627/","kind":"secondary"},{"title":"Decanter: Spain's Gonzalez Byass buys Chilean winery","url":"https://www.decanter.com/wine-news/gonzalez-byass-chile-winery-307476/","kind":"secondary"}]}
};
